window.PU_PAPER_TEXT["appendices--appendix_q_planck_scale"] = "# Appendix Q: Derivation of the Planck-MPU Scale Ratio\n\n## Q.0 Conditional Action-Ledger Calibration\n\nThis section separates a dimensionless predictive ledger from a mechanical action. Their proportionality scale $\\kappa_A$ is independent; identifying $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$ requires calibration against quantum phase data.\n\n### Q.0.1 The Puzzle of Least Action\n\nThe Principle of Least Action stands as one of the most powerful organizing principles in physics. From it, classical mechanics, field theory, and the path integral formulation of quantum mechanics can be derived. Yet the standard presentation offers no explanation for *why* nature should extremize this particular quantity—the integral of the Lagrangian over time.\n\n<a id=\"principle-q03a-finite-to-continuum-variational-closure\"></a>**Principle Q.0.3a (Finite-to-Continuum Variational Closure).** On every fixed-boundary and fixed-holonomy admissible class used for a fundamental PU continuum limit, the discrete dimensionless ledgers $\\mathcal L_h$ are equicoercive and $\\Gamma$-converge to a lower-semicontinuous ledger $\\mathcal L$. Each $\\mathcal L_h$ has a minimizer $\\phi_h$, every minimizing sequence has a convergent subsequence, and the continuum action is\n$$\n\\mathcal S=\\kappa_A\\mathcal L\n$$\nfor one registered constant $\\kappa_A>0$. When an Euler–Lagrange statement is made, the admissible limit has a common tangent domain and $\\mathcal L$ is Gâteaux differentiable on that domain. This is a physical continuum-closure principle and is independently testable by convergence of the retained discrete response functionals.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q03a-discrete-minimizers-give-the-continuum-action-principle\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.3a (Discrete Minimizers Give the Continuum Action Principle).** Under Principle Q.0.3a, every cluster point $\\phi$ of discrete minimizers is a minimizer of $\\mathcal L$ and of $\\mathcal S$. If $\\phi$ lies in the differentiable tangent domain, then\n$$\nD\\mathcal L(\\phi)[v]=0,\n\\qquad\nD\\mathcal S(\\phi)[v]=0\n$$\nfor every admissible two-sided variation $v$.\n\n*Proof.* Equicoercivity gives a convergent subsequence $\\phi_{h_j}\\to\\phi$. The fundamental theorem of $\\Gamma$-convergence gives\n$$\n\\mathcal L(\\phi)=\\min\\mathcal L\n=\\lim_j\\min\\mathcal L_{h_j}.\n$$\nMultiplication by the positive constant $\\kappa_A$ preserves minimizers. For every admissible two-sided variation, the scalar function $t\\mapsto\\mathcal L(\\phi+tv)$ has a minimum at $t=0$ and is differentiable there, so its derivative vanishes. The action identity gives $D\\mathcal S=\\kappa_A D\\mathcal L$. ∎\n\nNonminimizing discrete saddles require a separate first-variation or slope-convergence certificate; $\\Gamma$-convergence alone does not transfer arbitrary stationary points.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q03b-quadratic-finite-ledger-mosco-and-stationarity-limit\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.3b (Quadratic Finite-Ledger Mosco and Stationarity Limit).** Let $H=L^2(0,1)$, fix $\\lambda>0$ and $f\\in L^2(0,1)$, and take the nested dyadic meshes $h_n=2^{-n}$. Let $V_n\\subset H_0^1(0,1)$ be the continuous piecewise-affine functions on the $n$th mesh. Extend the finite ledger\n$$\n\\mathcal L_n(u)\n:=\n\\frac12\\int_0^1|u'|^2dx\n+\\frac\\lambda2\\int_0^1|u|^2dx\n-\\int_0^1fu\\,dx,\n\\qquad u\\in V_n,\n\\tag{Q.0.3b.1}\n$$\nby $+\\infty$ on $H\\setminus V_n$, and define $\\mathcal L$ by the same formula on $H_0^1(0,1)$ and by $+\\infty$ elsewhere. Then:\n\n1. $(\\mathcal L_n)$ is equicoercive in the strong $L^2$ topology and Mosco-converges, hence $\\Gamma$-converges, to $\\mathcal L$ on $H$.\n2. The unique stationary point $u_n\\in V_n$ satisfies\n   $$\n   \\int_0^1u_n'v_n'\\,dx+\\lambda\\int_0^1u_nv_n\\,dx\n   =\\int_0^1fv_n\\,dx\n   \\quad(v_n\\in V_n),\n   \\tag{Q.0.3b.2}\n   $$\n   and $u_n\\to u$ strongly in $H_0^1(0,1)$, where $u$ is the unique weak solution of $-u''+\\lambda u=f$ with zero boundary data.\n3. Whenever $w_n\\to w$ and $v_n\\to v$ strongly in $H_0^1$ with $w_n,v_n\\in V_n$,\n   $$\n   D\\mathcal L_n(w_n)[v_n]\\longrightarrow D\\mathcal L(w)[v].\n   \\tag{Q.0.3b.3}\n   $$\nMultiplication by a fixed registered $\\kappa_A>0$ gives the same statements for $\\mathcal S_n=\\kappa_A\\mathcal L_n$ in action units.\n\n*Proof.* Young's inequality and Poincaré's inequality give constants $c,C>0$, independent of $n$, such that $\\mathcal L_n(u)\\ge c\\|u\\|_{H^1}^2-C$. Rellich compactness makes every common sublevel precompact in $L^2$, proving equicoercivity. If $u_n\\rightharpoonup u$ in $L^2$ and $\\sup_n\\mathcal L_n(u_n)<\\infty$, the coercive bound supplies a subsequence weakly convergent in $H_0^1$ to the same $u$; weak lower semicontinuity of the two quadratic terms and weak continuity of the $f$ term give the Mosco liminf inequality. For $u\\in H_0^1$, its nodal interpolants on the dyadic meshes converge strongly in $H_0^1$ after the standard density approximation by smooth functions, giving a strong $L^2$ recovery sequence and convergence of all three terms. If $u\\notin H_0^1$, the recovery requirement is vacuous because $\\mathcal L(u)=+\\infty$. This proves Mosco convergence.\n\nThe bilinear form in (Q.0.3b.2) is uniformly coercive, so Lax--Milgram gives unique $u_n$ and $u$. Galerkin orthogonality and Céa's lemma give\n$$\n\\|u-u_n\\|_{H^1}\n\\le C_\\lambda\\inf_{v_n\\in V_n}\\|u-v_n\\|_{H^1}\\longrightarrow0.\n$$\nFinally, the derivative is the bilinear left side of (Q.0.3b.2) minus the $f$ pairing, so strong $H^1$ convergence of both arguments proves (Q.0.3b.3). Positive scalar multiplication preserves every assertion and supplies the declared units. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q03c-semilinear-finite-ledger-mosco-limit-saddle-transfer-and-explicit-galerkin-error\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.3c (Semilinear Finite-Ledger Mosco Limit, Saddle Transfer and Explicit Galerkin Error).** Keep $H=L^2(0,1)$, the dyadic meshes $h_n=2^{-n}$ and the spaces $V_n\\subset H_0^1(0,1)$ of Theorem Q.0.3b, and norm $H_0^1(0,1)$ by $\\|v\\|:=\\|v'\\|_{L^2}$. Fix $f\\in L^2(0,1)$ and $G\\in C^1(\\mathbb R)$ with $g:=G'$. Extend the finite semilinear ledger\n$$\n\\mathcal L_n^G(u)\n:=\n\\int_0^1\\left(\\frac12|u'|^2+G(u)-fu\\right)dx,\n\\qquad u\\in V_n,\n\\tag{Q.0.3c.1}\n$$\nby $+\\infty$ on $H\\setminus V_n$, and define $\\mathcal L^G$ by the same formula on $H_0^1(0,1)$ and by $+\\infty$ elsewhere. A discrete stationary point is a $u_n\\in V_n$ with\n$$\n\\int_0^1u_n'v'\\,dx+\\int_0^1\\bigl(g(u_n)-f\\bigr)v\\,dx=0\n\\qquad(v\\in V_n),\n\\tag{Q.0.3c.2}\n$$\nand a continuum stationary point is a $u\\in H_0^1(0,1)$ satisfying the same identity for every $v\\in H_0^1(0,1)$, that is, a weak solution of $-u''+g(u)=f$ with zero boundary data. Let $P_n$ be the $H_0^1$-orthogonal projection onto $V_n$. Then:\n\n1. If $G$ is bounded below, $(\\mathcal L_n^G)$ is equicoercive in the strong $L^2$ topology and Mosco-converges, hence $\\Gamma$-converges, to $\\mathcal L^G$ on $H$. Each $\\mathcal L_n^G$ has a minimizer, $\\min\\mathcal L_n^G\\to\\min\\mathcal L^G$, and every sequence of discrete minimizers has a subsequence converging strongly in $H_0^1$ to a minimizer of $\\mathcal L^G$.\n2. For every $G\\in C^1(\\mathbb R)$, with no convexity or lower bound, every $H_0^1$-bounded sequence of discrete stationary points has a subsequence converging strongly in $H_0^1$ to a continuum stationary point. Whenever $w_n\\to w$ and $v_n\\to v$ strongly in $H_0^1$ with $w_n,v_n\\in V_n$, $D\\mathcal L_n^G(w_n)[v_n]\\to D\\mathcal L^G(w)[v]$.\n3. Let $G\\in C^2(\\mathbb R)$ and let $u$ be a nondegenerate continuum stationary point: $v=0$ is the only $v\\in H_0^1$ with $\\int_0^1(v'w'+g'(u)vw)\\,dx=0$ for every $w\\in H_0^1$. The Morse index of $u$ is unrestricted, so $u$ may be a nonminimizing saddle. Then there exist $n_0$, $\\rho>0$ and $\\gamma>0$ such that, for every $n\\ge n_0$, (Q.0.3c.2) has exactly one solution $u_n$ with $\\|u_n-P_nu\\|\\le\\rho$, and\n   $$\n   \\|u-u_n\\|\n   \\le\\left(1+\\frac{2L}{\\pi^2\\gamma}\\right)\\|u-P_nu\\|\n   \\le\\left(1+\\frac{2L}{\\pi^2\\gamma}\\right)\\frac{2^{-n}}{\\pi}\\|f-g(u)\\|_{L^2},\n   \\tag{Q.0.3c.3}\n   $$\n   where $L:=\\max\\{|g'(s)|:|s|\\le\\|u\\|_{L^\\infty}+1\\}$ and $\\gamma$ is a uniform discrete inf-sup constant of the linearization at $u$. If $g'(u(x))\\ge-\\lambda_0$ on $[0,1]$ with $0\\le\\lambda_0<\\pi^2$, one may take $\\gamma=1-\\lambda_0/\\pi^2$.\n\nMultiplication by a fixed registered $\\kappa_A>0$ gives the same statements for $\\kappa_A\\mathcal L_n^G$ in action units. The quadratic ledger of Theorem Q.0.3b is the case $G(s)=\\lambda s^2/2$, for which (Q.0.3c.3) holds with $\\gamma=1$ and $L=\\lambda$.\n\n*Proof.* On $H_0^1(0,1)$, the two one-sided estimates $|v(x)|^2\\le x\\|v'\\|_{L^2(0,x)}^2$ and $|v(x)|^2\\le(1-x)\\|v'\\|_{L^2(x,1)}^2$ give $|v(x)|^2\\le x(1-x)\\|v\\|^2$, hence $\\|v\\|_{L^\\infty}\\le\\frac12\\|v\\|$, and Poincaré's inequality gives $\\|v\\|_{L^2}\\le\\pi^{-1}\\|v\\|$. The Hölder bound $|v(x)-v(y)|\\le|x-y|^{1/2}\\|v\\|$ and the Arzelà--Ascoli theorem make $H_0^1(0,1)\\hookrightarrow C[0,1]$ compact. In one dimension $P_n$ is nodal interpolation, since for $v\\in V_n$ and each mesh cell $K$ the integral $\\int_K(u-I_nu)'v'\\,dx$ is $v'|_K$ times the difference of the vanishing nodal errors of the nodal interpolant $I_nu$. On a cell $K$ of length $h_n$ the error $e=u-P_nu$ vanishes at both ends, so $e'$ has zero mean and Wirtinger's inequality gives $\\|e'\\|_{L^2(K)}\\le h_n\\pi^{-1}\\|u''\\|_{L^2(K)}$ whenever $u\\in H^2$. Density of smooth functions and $\\|P_n\\|\\le1$ then give $\\|u-P_nu\\|\\to0$ for every $u\\in H_0^1$.\n\nItem 1. If $G\\ge-g_0$, then $\\mathcal L_n^G(v)\\ge\\frac12\\|v\\|^2-g_0-\\pi^{-1}\\|f\\|_{L^2}\\|v\\|\\ge\\frac14\\|v\\|^2-g_0-\\pi^{-2}\\|f\\|_{L^2}^2$ with constants independent of $n$, so common sublevel sets are bounded in $H_0^1$ and precompact in $L^2$. If $u_n\\rightharpoonup u$ weakly in $L^2$ and $\\liminf\\mathcal L_n^G(u_n)<\\infty$, pass to a subsequence realizing the liminf; it is bounded in $H_0^1$, so a further subsequence converges weakly in $H_0^1$ and uniformly, necessarily to $u$. Weak lower semicontinuity of the Dirichlet term, uniform convergence of $G(u_n)$ and weak convergence of the $f$ term give the Mosco liminf inequality. For $u\\in H_0^1$, the sequence $P_nu$ converges to $u$ in $H_0^1$ and uniformly, so all three terms converge; for $u\\notin H_0^1$ the recovery requirement is vacuous. Each $\\mathcal L_n^G$ is continuous and coercive on the finite-dimensional space $V_n$ and therefore has a minimizer, and the fundamental theorem of $\\Gamma$-convergence gives convergence of minima and of minimizing cluster points. Discrete minimizers satisfy $\\mathcal L_n^G(u_n)\\le\\mathcal L_n^G(0)=G(0)$ and are therefore bounded in $H_0^1$; they satisfy (Q.0.3c.2) because $\\mathcal L_n^G$ is differentiable on $V_n$, and item 2 upgrades their convergence to strong $H_0^1$ convergence.\n\nItem 2. Let $u_n$ satisfy (Q.0.3c.2) with $\\|u_n\\|\\le C$. A subsequence converges weakly in $H_0^1$ and uniformly to some $u$. For $w\\in H_0^1$, test (Q.0.3c.2) with $P_nw$: since $(P_nw)'\\to w'$ in $L^2$, $u_n'\\rightharpoonup u'$, $g(u_n)\\to g(u)$ uniformly and $P_nw\\to w$ in $L^2$, the limit is the continuum identity for $w$. Testing (Q.0.3c.2) with $u_n$ and the continuum identity with $u$ gives\n$$\n\\|u_n\\|^2=\\int_0^1\\bigl(f-g(u_n)\\bigr)u_n\\,dx\\longrightarrow\\int_0^1\\bigl(f-g(u)\\bigr)u\\,dx=\\|u\\|^2,\n$$\nand weak convergence with convergence of norms is strong convergence. Since $D\\mathcal L_n^G(w_n)[v_n]=\\int_0^1w_n'v_n'\\,dx+\\int_0^1(g(w_n)-f)v_n\\,dx$ and strong $H_0^1$ convergence implies uniform convergence, the derivative limit follows.\n\nItem 3. Let $\\langle Av,w\\rangle:=\\int_0^1(v'w'+g'(u)vw)\\,dx$ and $\\gamma_n:=\\inf_{v\\in V_n,\\|v\\|=1}\\sup_{w\\in V_n,\\|w\\|=1}\\langle Av,w\\rangle$. Suppose $\\liminf\\gamma_n=0$. Along a subsequence there are $v_n\\in V_n$ with $\\|v_n\\|=1$ and $\\epsilon_n:=\\sup_{w\\in V_n,\\|w\\|=1}\\langle Av_n,w\\rangle\\to0$, and after extraction $v_n\\rightharpoonup v$ in $H_0^1$ and uniformly. For $w\\in H_0^1$, $|\\langle Av_n,P_nw\\rangle|\\le\\epsilon_n\\|w\\|$ and $|\\langle Av_n,w-P_nw\\rangle|\\le\\|A\\|\\,\\|w-P_nw\\|$, while $\\langle Av_n,w\\rangle\\to\\langle Av,w\\rangle$; hence $\\langle Av,w\\rangle=0$ for all $w$, and nondegeneracy gives $v=0$. Then $\\langle Av_n,v_n\\rangle=1+\\int_0^1g'(u)v_n^2\\,dx\\to1$, contradicting $\\langle Av_n,v_n\\rangle\\le\\epsilon_n$. Therefore there are $n_1$ and $\\gamma>0$ with $\\gamma_n\\ge\\gamma$ for $n\\ge n_1$. If $g'(u)\\ge-\\lambda_0$, then $\\langle Av,v\\rangle\\ge(1-\\lambda_0/\\pi^2)\\|v\\|^2$ for every $v$, which gives the explicit constant for every $n$.\n\nFor $n\\ge n_1$ the restriction $A_n:V_n\\to V_n^{*}$ is invertible with $\\|A_n^{-1}\\|\\le\\gamma^{-1}$. Put $F:=D\\mathcal L^G$ and $\\Phi_n(v):=v-A_n^{-1}\\bigl(F(v)|_{V_n}\\bigr)$ on $V_n$; its fixed points are exactly the solutions of (Q.0.3c.2). For $v_1,v_2\\in V_n$ with $\\|v_i-u\\|\\le r\\le2$,\n$$\n\\bigl\\langle A(v_1-v_2)-F(v_1)+F(v_2),w\\bigr\\rangle\n=-\\int_0^1\\int_0^1\\bigl[g'(v_2+s(v_1-v_2))-g'(u)\\bigr]ds\\,(v_1-v_2)w\\,dx,\n$$\nso $\\|\\Phi_n(v_1)-\\Phi_n(v_2)\\|\\le\\omega(r/2)(\\pi^2\\gamma)^{-1}\\|v_1-v_2\\|$, where $\\omega$ is the modulus of continuity of $g'$ on $[-\\|u\\|_{L^\\infty}-1,\\|u\\|_{L^\\infty}+1]$. Fix $r\\in(0,2]$ with $\\omega(r/2)\\le\\pi^2\\gamma/2$ and put $\\rho:=r/2$. Because $F(u)=0$ and $\\int_0^1(P_nu-u)'w'\\,dx=0$ for $w\\in V_n$, as soon as $\\|u-P_nu\\|\\le\\rho$,\n$$\n\\|\\Phi_n(P_nu)-P_nu\\|\n\\le\\gamma^{-1}\\sup_{w\\in V_n,\\|w\\|=1}\\int_0^1\\bigl(g(P_nu)-g(u)\\bigr)w\\,dx\n\\le\\frac{L}{\\pi^2\\gamma}\\|u-P_nu\\|=:\\epsilon_n\\longrightarrow0.\n$$\nChoose $n_0\\ge n_1$ with $\\|u-P_nu\\|\\le\\rho$ and $\\epsilon_n\\le\\rho/2$ for $n\\ge n_0$. Then $\\Phi_n$ maps the closed ball $\\{v\\in V_n:\\|v-P_nu\\|\\le\\rho\\}$, which lies in $\\{\\|v-u\\|\\le r\\}$, into itself and is a $\\frac12$-contraction there, so Banach's fixed-point theorem gives exactly one solution $u_n$ in that ball and $\\|u_n-P_nu\\|\\le2\\epsilon_n$. The triangle inequality gives the first bound in (Q.0.3c.3). The continuum identity gives $u''=g(u)-f\\in L^2$, and the cellwise Wirtinger bound gives the second. Positive scalar multiplication preserves every assertion. ∎\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q03d-stationary-point-transfer-nonentailment-under-equicoercive-gamma-convergence\"></a>**Proposition Q.0.3d (Stationary-Point Transfer Nonentailment under Equicoercive $\\Gamma$-Convergence).** On the one-dimensional ledger space $\\mathbb R$ let\n$$\n\\mathcal L(x):=\\frac{x^2}2,\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal L_n(x):=\\frac{x^2}2+\\frac1n\\sin(nx).\n\\tag{Q.0.3d.1}\n$$\nThen $\\sup_x|\\mathcal L_n(x)-\\mathcal L(x)|\\le1/n$, so $(\\mathcal L_n)$ is equicoercive and $\\Gamma$-converges to $\\mathcal L$, and minimizers of $\\mathcal L_n$ converge to the unique minimizer $0$. Every stationary point of $\\mathcal L_n$ lies in $[-1,1]$, and every point of $(-1,1)$ is a limit of strict local maxima of $\\mathcal L_n$, whereas $\\mathcal L$ has the single stationary point $0$. The first variations satisfy $\\mathcal L_n'(x)-\\mathcal L'(x)=\\cos(nx)$, which converges to $0$ at no $x$. Transfer of nonminimizing stationary points therefore uses data beyond equicoercive $\\Gamma$-convergence, such as the first-variation convergence (Q.0.3b.3), item 2 of Theorem Q.0.3c or an equivalent slope certificate.\n\n*Proof.* The uniform bound gives $\\Gamma$-convergence of these continuous functions and the common lower bound $\\mathcal L_n(x)\\ge x^2/2-1$ gives equicoercivity; the fundamental theorem of $\\Gamma$-convergence gives convergence of minimizers to $0$. Stationarity reads $x+\\cos(nx)=0$, which forces $|x|\\le1$. Fix $x_0\\in(-1,1)$ and $c<1$ with $|x_0|<c$. For large $n$ the interval $[x_0-2\\pi/n,x_0+2\\pi/n]\\subset(-c,c)$ contains a half-period $[2\\pi j/n,(2j+1)\\pi/n]$ on which $\\cos(nx)$ decreases from $1$ to $-1$, so $x+\\cos(nx)$ changes sign from positive to negative there and has a zero $\\xi_n$. At such a zero $\\cos(n\\xi_n)=-\\xi_n$ and $\\sin(n\\xi_n)=\\sqrt{1-\\xi_n^2}\\ge\\sqrt{1-c^2}$, so $\\mathcal L_n''(\\xi_n)=1-n\\sin(n\\xi_n)<0$ once $n>(1-c^2)^{-1/2}$; thus $\\xi_n$ is a strict local maximum and $\\xi_n\\to x_0$. If $\\cos(nx)\\to0$, then $\\cos(2nx)=2\\cos^2(nx)-1\\to-1$, contradicting convergence of its subsequence to $0$. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q03e-all-dimensional-quasilinear-ledger-limit-and-complete-stationary-classification\"></a>\n\n**Theorem Q.0.3e (All-Dimensional Quasilinear Ledger Limit and Complete Stationary Classification).** Let $d\\ge1$, give the connected flat torus $\\mathbb T^d=(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z)^d$ volume one, and set $H=L^2(\\mathbb T^d;\\mathbb R)$ and $X=W^{1,4}(\\mathbb T^d;\\mathbb R)$. For $n\\ge1$, subdivide the torus into cubes of side $h_n=2^{-n}$ and each cube into its $d!$ Freudenthal simplices, with the same ordering of coordinate directions. Let $V_n\\subset X$ be the periodic continuous piecewise-affine space on this mesh. These spaces contain constants, are nested, and have dense union in $X$. For $a\\ge0$ define\n$$\nE_a(u)=\\int_{\\mathbb T^d}\\bigl(|\\nabla u|^4+|\\nabla u|^2+u^4+u^3+au\\bigr)\\,dx\\quad(u\\in X),\n\\qquad E_a=+\\infty\\quad\\hbox{on }H\\setminus X.\n\\tag{Q.0.3e.1}\n$$\nLet $a_n\\ge0$ converge to $a$, and let $E_n$ be $E_{a_n}$ restricted to $V_n$, extended by $+\\infty$ on its complement in $H$. Then:\n\n1. The sequence is uniformly lower bounded, equicoercive in strong $L^2$, and Mosco-converges to $E_a$.\n2. Put $r(0)=-3/4$. For $a>0$, let $r(a)<-3/4$ be the unique root of $4r^3+3r^2+a=0$. The complete continuum stationary set is the constant fields $\\{0,-3/4\\}$ when $a=0$ and $\\{r(a)\\}$ when $a>0$. The complete stationary set on each real space $V_n$ is the same list with $a_n$ in place of $a$.\n3. The unique global minimizers are the constants $r(a)$ and $r(a_n)$, respectively, and $r(a_n)\\to r(a)$. Writing $W_a(t)=t^4+t^3+at$, their minimum values are $W_a(r(a))$ and $W_{a_n}(r(a_n))$; in particular $\\min E_0=-27/256$.\n4. At $a=0$, the field $0$ is degenerate and nonminimizing. Its Hessian is\n   $$\n   D^2E_0(0)[v,w]=2\\int_{\\mathbb T^d}\\nabla v\\cdot\\nabla w\\,dx,\n   \\tag{Q.0.3e.2}\n   $$\n   whose kernel consists exactly of constants. Every $X$-neighborhood of $0$ contains fields with energy above and below $E_0(0)$.\n5. Suppose $a=0$. There exists a sequence of stationary fields, defined at every sufficiently large finite level and converging to $0$ in $L^2$, exactly when $a_n=0$ eventually. There exists an infinite subsequence of finite stationary fields converging to $0$ exactly when $a_n=0$ infinitely often. The field $-3/4$ is always recovered by the minimizers.\n6. If $w_n,v_n\\in V_n$ converge strongly in $X$ to $w,v$, then $DE_n(w_n)[v_n]\\to DE_a(w)[v]$. On the common domain $X$, the unrestricted functionals $E_{a_n}$ converge to $E_a$, together with all their derivatives, uniformly on bounded sets. Multiplication of every ledger by one fixed registered $\\kappa_A>0$ preserves all these assertions.\n\n*Proof.* Put $A=\\max(a,\\sup_n a_n)<\\infty$. Young's inequality gives $|t|^3+A|t|\\le|t|^4/2+C_A$, hence\n$$\nE_n(u)\\ge\\|\\nabla u\\|_4^4+\\tfrac12\\|u\\|_4^4-C_A.\n$$\nCommon sublevels are bounded in $X$. The embedding $X\\hookrightarrow L^q$ is compact for $q=2,3$ in every finite dimension: for $d>4$ the critical exponent $4d/(d-4)$ exceeds $4$, for $d=4$ every finite $q$ is subcritical, and for $d<4$ the Morrey embedding gives a positive Hölder exponent. This proves equicoercivity and the lower bound.\n\nFor the Mosco lower bound, take $u_n\\rightharpoonup u$ in $L^2$ and a subsequence realizing a finite lower energy limit. The coercive bound gives a further subsequence weakly convergent in $X$ and strongly convergent in $L^2$ and $L^3$, necessarily to $u\\in X$. The gradient quartic, gradient quadratic and field quartic terms are weakly lower semicontinuous. Moreover,\n$$\n\\|u_n^3-u^3\\|_1\\le\\|u_n-u\\|_3\\bigl(\\|u_n\\|_3^2+\\|u_n\\|_3\\|u\\|_3+\\|u\\|_3^2\\bigr)\\longrightarrow0,\n$$\nand $a_n\\int u_n\\to a\\int u$. Summing gives the lower bound.\n\nPeriodic convolution approximates every $u\\in X$ strongly in $X$, because translations of $u$ and its weak derivatives are continuous in $L^4$. For a smooth periodic field, its nodal interpolants on the stated shape-regular meshes converge in $W^{1,\\infty}$: Taylor's formula gives function error at most $C_dh_n^2\\|D^2u\\|_\\infty$ and gradient error at most $C_dh_n\\|D^2u\\|_\\infty$ on each simplex. A diagonal choice therefore gives $v_n\\in V_n$ with $v_n\\to u$ strongly in $X$. All polynomial integrals converge, proving strong recovery. At $E_a(u)=+\\infty$ the recovery upper bound is automatic. This proves Mosco convergence and also the asserted density of the FE spaces.\n\nWrite\n$$\nq_a(t)=W_a'(t)=t^2(4t+3)+a.\n$$\nFor $a=0$ its roots are $-3/4,0$; it is negative below $-3/4$ and positive above $-3/4$ except at $0$. For $a>0$ it is positive on $[-3/4,\\infty)$, while $q_a'(t)=6t(2t+1)>0$ for $t\\le-3/4$ and $q_a(t)\\to-\\infty$ as $t\\to-\\infty$. Thus $r(a)$ exists uniquely and\n$$\nq_a(t)(t-r(a))\\ge0\\quad(t\\in\\mathbb R).\n$$\nEvery continuum stationary field satisfies\n$$\n\\int_{\\mathbb T^d}\\bigl((4|\\nabla u|^2+2)\\nabla u\\cdot\\nabla v+q_a(u)v\\bigr)\\,dx=0\\quad(v\\in X).\n\\tag{Q.0.3e.3}\n$$\nHölder's inequality makes every term integrable. The discrete equation has $a_n$ and tests $v\\in V_n$. Test by $u-r(a)$, or $u-r(a_n)$ at a finite level. The identity becomes\n$$\n0=\\int_{\\mathbb T^d}\\bigl(4|\\nabla u|^4+2|\\nabla u|^2+q_a(u)(u-r(a))\\bigr)\\,dx.\n$$\nAll summands are nonnegative, so $\\nabla u=0$ almost everywhere. Connectedness makes $u$ constant, and the test $v=1$ forces $q_a(u)=0$. Conversely each listed constant satisfies every test. This proves the complete lists, including exclusion of every nonconstant stationary solution at finite and continuum level.\n\nThe sign of $q_a$ makes $W_a$ strictly decreasing up to $r(a)$ and strictly increasing after it, apart from its isolated zero derivative at $0$ for $a=0$. Hence $W_a(t)\\ge W_a(r(a))$, with equality exactly at $r(a)$. Nonnegativity of the gradient terms proves uniqueness and the minimum formulas. The roots on $(-\\infty,-3/4]$ are bounded when the coefficients are bounded; any cluster point of $r(a_n)$ is the unique root there for coefficient $a$, proving their convergence.\n\nAt $0$ the second derivatives of the cubic and quartic terms vanish, giving (Q.0.3e.2); testing a kernel vector against itself proves that it is constant. On constant fields $u=t$, the energy $t^4+t^3$ is positive for small positive $t$ and negative for $-1<t<0$. If $a_n>0$, every stationary field is $r(a_n)<-3/4$, at $L^2$ distance at least $3/4$ from $0$; if $a_n=0$, the field $0$ is exactly stationary. This proves both recovery criteria.\n\nStrong gradient convergence in $L^4$ gives convergence of $|\\nabla w_n|^2\\nabla w_n$ in $L^{4/3}$; strong field convergence in $L^4$ gives cubic convergence in $L^{4/3}$ and quadratic convergence in $L^2$. Pairing with $v_n\\to v$ proves item 6. On $X$, the common-domain difference is exactly $(a_n-a)\\int u$, so all higher derivative differences vanish. These integral polynomials are continuous polynomials on $X$ and therefore possess all the derivatives asserted here. A fixed positive scalar preserves minimizers, stationary sets and convergence and supplies the registered action units. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q03e1-rational-degenerate-recovery-and-fixed-limit-effectivity-boundary\"></a>\n\n**Corollary Q.0.3e.1 (Rational Degenerate Recovery and Fixed-Limit Effectivity Boundary).** Set $\\delta_n=4^{-n}$. For any subset $A\\subset\\mathbb N_{\\ge1}$ let\n$$\na_n=\\begin{cases}0,&n\\in A,\\\\4\\delta_n(3/4+\\delta_n)^2,&n\\notin A.\\end{cases}\n\\qquad r_n=-3/4-\\delta_n.\n\\tag{Q.0.3e.4}\n$$\nThen $0\\le a_n\\le4\\delta_n\\le1$, all coefficients tend to zero with the displayed modulus, and the complete finite stationary set is $\\{0,-3/4\\}$ for $n\\in A$ and $\\{r_n\\}$ otherwise. All its fields, including the unique minimizer, have nodal values in $\\delta_n\\mathbb Z\\cap[-n,n]$. Full-tail recovery of the same degenerate continuum field $0$ is equivalent to $A$ being cofinite; infinite-subsequence recovery is equivalent to $A$ being infinite.\n\nThere is no total algorithm which decides either recovery predicate for all uniformly supplied rational coefficient sequences in this family, even when all sequences have the identical continuum functional $E_0$ and the same known convergence modulus.\n\n*Proof.* Since $4r_n+3=-4\\delta_n$, substitution gives $4r_n^3+3r_n^2+a_n=0$ on every positive-coefficient level. For $n\\ge1$, $\\delta_n\\le1/4$, $r_n\\in[-1,-3/4)$ and $a_n\\le4\\delta_n$. The numbers $0,-3/4,r_n$ lie on the grid because $(3/4)/\\delta_n=3\\cdot4^{n-1}$ is an integer. Theorem Q.0.3e gives all stationary and recovery assertions.\n\nGiven a finite program $e$, simulate it for $n$ steps and set $a_{e,n}=0$ if it has not halted by then and $a_{e,n}=4\\delta_n(3/4+\\delta_n)^2$ otherwise. Each coefficient is a computable rational obtained by a bounded simulation, and every sequence converges to zero with bound $4\\delta_n$. If $e$ never halts, the field $0$ is stationary at every level. If it halts, every sufficiently late finite stationary field is $r_n$, at distance at least $3/4$ from $0$. Thus either recovery predicate decides nonhalting. A total nonhalting decider would also decide halting. To recall the diagonal contradiction, from a total halting decider $H$ form the program $D(x)$ which loops when $H(x,x)$ reports halting and halts otherwise; $H(D,D)$ is wrong in either case. This proves the algorithmic assertion. ∎\n\nThe sequence in (Q.0.3e.4) consumes its supplied rational $a_n$ at each level. Arbitrary $A$ need not admit one finite membership program; the bounded-simulation family in the proof does admit a uniform levelwise generator. The corollary gives an exact mathematical recovery criterion and an algorithmic obstruction for that predicate. Construction Q.0.3h supplies its finite source response; a total decision algorithm for across-level recovery is a different operation.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q03f-arbitrary-compact-degenerate-stationary-spectra\"></a>\n\n**Theorem Q.0.3f (Arbitrary Compact Degenerate Stationary Spectra).** Fix $d\\ge1$ and the torus, spaces $H,X,V_n$ of Theorem Q.0.3e. Let $K\\subset\\mathbb R$ be nonempty and compact, choose $r\\in K$, and let $L_n\\subset K$ be nonempty closed sets containing $r$. There are scalar potentials $P,P_n\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R)$ with uniform quadratic upper and coercive lower bounds, all nonnegative, such that\n$$\n0\\le P_n(s)-P(s)\\le2^{-n-1}(s-r)^2,\n\\qquad P_n\\longrightarrow P\\quad\\hbox{in }C^j_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\mathbb R)\\quad(j\\ge0).\n\\tag{Q.0.3f.1}\n$$\nThe functionals\n$$\nF(u)=\\int_{\\mathbb T^d}(|\\nabla u|^4+|\\nabla u|^2+P(u))\\,dx\\quad(u\\in X),\n\\qquad\nF_n(u)=\\int_{\\mathbb T^d}(|\\nabla u|^4+|\\nabla u|^2+P_n(u))\\,dx\\quad(u\\in V_n),\n\\tag{Q.0.3f.2}\n$$\nextended by $+\\infty$ off their respective domains, are equicoercive and Mosco-convergent on $H$. Their complete stationary sets are precisely the constant fields with values in $K$ and $L_n$, respectively. Every such point is degenerate, with Hessian kernel exactly the constant fields. The constant $r$ is a zero-energy global minimizer at every level and in the limit. For $k\\in K$, full-tail stationary recovery holds exactly when $\\operatorname{dist}(k,L_n)\\to0$; subsequential recovery holds exactly when $\\liminf_n\\operatorname{dist}(k,L_n)=0$.\n\n*Proof.* First construct a smooth flat zero-set function for each nonempty compact $C\\subset K$. Enumerate the rational intervals $I_j=(a_j,b_j)$ whose closed intervals lie in $\\mathbb R\\setminus C$; their interiors cover that complement. Define\n$$\n\\phi_j(s)=\\begin{cases}\\exp\\!\\bigl(-1/((s-a_j)(b_j-s))\\bigr),&a_j<s<b_j,\\\\0,&\\text{otherwise},\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\nc_j=\\frac{2^{-j}}{1+\\max_{0\\le k\\le j}\\|D^k\\phi_j\\|_\\infty},\n\\qquad z_C=\\sum_{j\\ge1}c_j\\phi_j.\n$$\nEach bump is smooth, positive on its interior and flat at its endpoints. For any fixed derivative order $k$, the tail with $j\\ge k$ is uniformly bounded termwise by $2^{-j}$. Therefore every derivative series converges uniformly and may be differentiated termwise. Every derivative vanishes on $C$, whereas each point outside $C$ lies in the interior of a positive summand. Thus $z_C\\ge0$, its zero set is exactly $C$, and it is flat on $C$.\n\nChoose $R$ with $K\\subset(-R,R)$ and one smooth cutoff $0\\le\\chi\\le1$, equal to one on a neighborhood of $[-R,R]$ and zero outside $[-R-1,R+1]$. Set\n$$\nh_C=\\chi z_C+(1-\\chi).\n$$\nIt is nonnegative, flat and zero exactly on $C$, and equal to one outside the fixed compact interval. All its derivatives are bounded. Put $h=h_K$, $u_n=h_{L_n}$ and\n$$\n\\varepsilon_n=\\frac{2^{-n}}{1+\\max_{0\\le j\\le n}\\|D^ju_n\\|_\\infty},\\qquad\nh_n=h+\\varepsilon_nu_n,\\qquad\ng(s)=(s-r)h(s),\\qquad g_n(s)=(s-r)h_n(s),\n$$\n$$\nP(s)=\\int_r^s g(t)\\,dt,\\qquad P_n(s)=\\int_r^s g_n(t)\\,dt.\n\\tag{Q.0.3f.3}\n$$\nBecause $\\varepsilon_n>0$ and both summands are nonnegative, the zero set of $h_n$ is $K\\cap L_n=L_n$. Since $r\\in L_n$, the zero sets of $g$ and $g_n$ are $K$ and $L_n$. Flatness gives $g'(k)=0$ at every $k\\in K$ and $g_n'(l)=0$ at every $l\\in L_n$.\n\nFor fixed $j$ and $n\\ge j$, $\\varepsilon_n\\|D^ju_n\\|_\\infty\\le2^{-n}$. Multiplication by $s-r$ and integration prove local convergence in every derivative order. The oriented integral of $(t-r)u_n(t)$ from $r$ to $s$ is nonnegative, and its absolute value is at most $\\|u_n\\|_\\infty(s-r)^2/2$, proving (Q.0.3f.1). The sign of $g(s)$ is the sign of $s-r$ wherever it is nonzero, so $P\\ge0$. Outside the fixed interval $h=1$, making $P$ a quadratic plus an affine function on each tail. Hence $cs^2-C\\le P(s)\\le C(1+s^2)$ for some $c,C>0$. Equation (Q.0.3f.1) gives the same lower and a common quadratic upper bound for all $P_n$.\n\nAn energy bound controls $\\|\\nabla u\\|_4$ and $\\|u\\|_2$. Periodic Poincaré and volume one give\n$$\n\\|u\\|_4\\le C_d\\|\\nabla u\\|_4+\\left|\\int_{\\mathbb T^d}u\\,dx\\right|\\le C_d\\|\\nabla u\\|_4+\\|u\\|_2.\n$$\nThus common sublevels are bounded in $X$ and relatively compact in $L^2$. For weak $L^2$ convergence with bounded energies, extract a subsequence weak in $X$ and strong in $L^2$. Convexity gives the weak lower bound for both gradient terms. The at-most-linear growth of $g$ gives\n$$\n|P(s)-P(t)|\\le C(1+|s|+|t|)|s-t|,\n$$\nso the potential integrals converge under strong $L^2$ convergence. Since $P_n\\ge P$, this proves the Mosco lower bound. For recovery use the strong $X$ FE approximants from Theorem Q.0.3e. Their $L^2$ norms remain bounded; the same estimate gives convergence of the $P$ integrals and (Q.0.3f.1) makes the extra $P_n-P$ integrals vanish. The gradient integrals converge strongly, proving Mosco convergence. All energy summands are nonnegative and vanish at the constant $r$.\n\nThe first variation is well-defined on $X$ because $g,g_n$ have at-most-linear growth. At any stationary field, the admissible test $u-r$ gives\n$$\n0=\\int_{\\mathbb T^d}\\bigl(4|\\nabla u|^4+2|\\nabla u|^2+(u-r)^2h(u)\\bigr)\\,dx,\n$$\nor the same identity with $h_n$. Its nonnegative terms force $\\nabla u=0$, hence $u$ is constant. Testing by $1$ forces $g(u)=0$ or $g_n(u)=0$, giving exactly the claimed complete stationary sets. Every listed constant conversely satisfies every test. At any such constant the quartic-gradient Hessian vanishes and the reaction derivative is zero, leaving $2\\int\\nabla v\\cdot\\nabla w$ and exactly the constant kernel.\n\nThe $L^2$ distance between constant fields is the scalar distance because the torus has volume one. Each nonempty compact $L_n$ attains its distance to $k$. Choosing a closest value proves sufficiency of each distance criterion; the distance lower bound for every chosen stationary value proves necessity. ∎\n\nThe scalar potentials are $C^\\infty$; the integral functionals on $X$ have the $C^2$ regularity used for the first variation and Hessian. No all-order Fréchet regularity of these general integral functionals is required. Taking $K$ to be a Cantor set and $L_n=\\{r\\}$ removes every other prescribed degenerate stationary value at every finite level while preserving the common minimizer and the stated convergence. The construction accepts arbitrary compact sets as mathematical data; such data need not have finite effective descriptions. Theorem Q.0.3f therefore holds for compact data without effective descriptions, while Construction Q.0.3h supplies a finite source for the rational family of Theorem Q.0.3e.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q03g-finite-spectrum-classification-of-good-variational-limits\"></a>\n\n**Theorem Q.0.3g (Finite-Spectrum Classification of Good Variational Limits).** Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space and let $F:X\\to\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}$ be proper and lower semicontinuous, with every sublevel $\\{F\\le c\\}$ compact; call such a functional good. Let $D\\subset X$ be a countable set whose closure contains $\\operatorname{dom}F:=\\{x:F(x)<+\\infty\\}$, and let $A\\subset\\mathbb R$ be dense. Then there are extended-real functionals $F_n$ such that:\n\n1. Each $F_n$ has finite nonempty effective domain contained in $D$, and all its finite values belong to $A$.\n2. The sequence $(F_n)$ is equicoercive in the metric topology and $\\Gamma$-converges to $F$.\n3. Each $F_n$ attains its minimum, $\\min F_n\\to\\min F$, and every sequence of discrete minimizers has a convergent subsequence; every such cluster point minimizes $F$.\n4. If $X$ is a real or complex Hilbert space with its norm metric, the same sequence Mosco-converges to $F$: the liminf inequality holds for weakly convergent sequences, and recovery is strong.\n\nSuch a set $D$ always exists, because $\\operatorname{dom}F$ is a countable union of compact metric sublevels. Neither completeness nor global separability of $X$ is required. Conversely, every proper $\\Gamma$-limit of an equicoercive sequence of finite-support extended-real functionals on a metric space is lower semicontinuous and has compact sublevels. Thus the proper functionals obtainable in this way are exactly the proper lower-semicontinuous functionals with compact sublevels.\n\nThe two coded arithmetic choices\n$$\nA_{23}=\\mathbb Z\\ln2+\\mathbb Z\\ln3,\n\\qquad\nA_2=\\mathbb Q\\ln2\n$$\nare dense in $\\mathbb R$. Their elements have finite integer or rational energy codes. For a nonnegative $F$, the same construction can use upward-rounded values in $\\mathbb Q_{\\ge0}\\ln2$ and therefore keep every finite discrete energy nonnegative.\n\n*Proof.* First $F$ has a finite attained minimum. Choose $x_0\\in\\operatorname{dom}F$. If $\\inf F=-\\infty$, choose a sequence whose energies tend to $-\\infty$. Its tail lies in the compact set $\\{F\\le F(x_0)\\}$, so a subsequence converges to a point of $X$. Lower semicontinuity would give value $-\\infty$ at that point, contrary to the codomain of $F$. Hence $m:=\\inf F$ is finite. A minimizing sequence lies eventually in a fixed compact sublevel; a convergent subsequence and lower semicontinuity show that $m$ is attained.\n\nFor every sufficiently large positive integer $n$, define the truncated epigraph\n$$\nE_n:=\\{(x,t)\\in X\\times\\mathbb R:m\\le t\\le n,\\ F(x)\\le t\\}.\n$$\nEquip the product with the maximum metric. The set $E_n$ is nonempty and closed, and it lies in the compact product $\\{F\\le n\\}\\times[m,n]$, so it is compact. Put $\\delta_n=1/n$ and choose a finite $\\delta_n$-net\n$$\n\\{(x_{n,i},t_{n,i}):1\\le i\\le k_n\\}\\subset E_n.\n$$\nEvery $x_{n,i}$ belongs to $\\operatorname{dom}F$. Choose $q_{n,i}\\in D$ with $d(q_{n,i},x_{n,i})<\\delta_n$, and use density of $A$ to choose\n$$\nt_{n,i}<a_{n,i}<t_{n,i}+\\delta_n,\n\\qquad a_{n,i}\\in A.\n$$\nOn the finite set of selected support points define\n$$\nF_n(q):=\\min\\{a_{n,i}:q_{n,i}=q\\},\n$$\nand set $F_n=+\\infty$ elsewhere. For the finitely many early indices with empty $E_n$, use any one fixed point of $D$ and one fixed value of $A$. Properness of $F$ ensures that $D$ is nonempty. This supplies finite nonempty domains. All sufficiently late finite energies exceed $m$, so the entire sequence has a uniform finite lower bound after including the finitely many early values.\n\n**Liminf.** Let $u_n\\to u$. If $\\liminf_nF_n(u_n)=+\\infty$, the required inequality is automatic. Otherwise the uniform lower bound permits passage to a subsequence realizing a finite lower limit, with all its energies finite. At each of those indices choose an index $i_n$ attaining the defining minimum at $u_n=q_{n,i_n}$. Then $x_{n,i_n}\\to u$ and\n$$\nF(x_{n,i_n})\\le t_{n,i_n}<F_n(u_n).\n$$\nLower semicontinuity gives\n$$\nF(u)\\le\\liminf_{n\\to\\infty}F_n(u_n).\n$$\n\n**Recovery.** Fix $u\\in\\operatorname{dom}F$. For all large $n$, the point $(u,F(u))$ belongs to $E_n$. Choose a net point satisfying\n$$\nd(x_{n,i_n},u)\\le\\delta_n,\n\\qquad\n|t_{n,i_n}-F(u)|\\le\\delta_n.\n$$\nThen $q_{n,i_n}\\to u$ and\n$$\nF_n(q_{n,i_n})\\le a_{n,i_n}\n<t_{n,i_n}+\\delta_n\n\\le F(u)+2\\delta_n.\n$$\nThe liminf inequality already proved shows that these recovery energies converge to $F(u)$. When $F(u)=+\\infty$, the recovery upper bound is vacuous, and the constant sequence $u_n=u$ suffices. Thus $F_n\\xrightarrow{\\Gamma}F$.\n\n**Equicoercivity without completeness.** Fix $c\\in\\mathbb R$ and put $K_c=\\{F\\le c\\}$. Every sufficiently late support point $q$ with $F_n(q)\\le c$ has a minimizing witness $x_{n,i}\\in K_c$ with $d(q,x_{n,i})<\\delta_n$. If $K_c$ is empty, there are no such late support points. Let\n$$\nS_c:=\\bigcup_{n\\ge1}\\{F_n\\le c\\}.\n$$\nTake any sequence in $S_c$, and choose a source index for each point. If these indices have a bounded subsequence, its points lie in a fixed finite set, so it has a constant subsequence. Otherwise pass to source indices tending to infinity. The associated witnesses lie in the compact set $K_c$, and a subsequence of them converges to a point of $K_c\\subset X$. The support points converge to that same point because their distances to their witnesses tend to zero. Hence every sequence in $S_c$ has a subsequence converging in $X$. For a sequence in $\\overline{S_c}$, choose its $k$th approximating point in $S_c$ within distance $1/k$ and apply the preceding argument. Its corresponding subsequence converges to the same limit, which belongs to $\\overline{S_c}$. Therefore $\\overline{S_c}$ is sequentially compact and hence compact in the metric space. It is the common compact sublevel container required for equicoercivity.\n\n**Minimizers.** Every $F_n$ attains its minimum on its finite nonempty domain. Recovery at a minimizer of $F$ gives\n$$\n\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}\\min F_n\\le m.\n$$\nChoose discrete minimizers along a subsequence realizing the lower limit of the minimum values. Their energies are bounded above, and equicoercivity supplies a convergent further subsequence. The liminf inequality gives a limiting value at least $m$. Hence $\\min F_n\\to m$. Every sequence of discrete minimizers is eventually contained in a common compact sublevel container, and the same liminf argument shows that every cluster point minimizes $F$.\n\n**Mosco convergence.** Suppose now that $X$ is a Hilbert space and $u_n\\rightharpoonup u$. A finite lower energy limit can be realized on a subsequence whose energies are bounded above. Its common sublevel container is norm-compact, so a further subsequence converges strongly to some $v$. Strong convergence implies weak convergence, and uniqueness of the weak limit gives $v=u$. Applying the already established strong liminf inequality on this subsequence proves the weak liminf inequality. The strong recovery sequences are unchanged. This argument uses neither convexity nor a restriction to real scalars.\n\n**Converse.** Let $(G_n)$ be equicoercive and suppose that its $\\Gamma$-limit $G:X\\to\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}$ is proper. To prove lower semicontinuity, let $x_k\\to x$. If $\\liminf_kG(x_k)=+\\infty$, there is nothing to prove. Otherwise pass to a subsequence realizing that lower limit with finite values; the lower limit may initially be $-\\infty$. For each $k$, use recovery at $x_k$ to choose a strictly increasing source index $n_k$ and a point $y_k$ such that\n$$\nd(y_k,x_k)<1/k,\n\\qquad\nG_{n_k}(y_k)\\le G(x_k)+1/k.\n$$\nThen $y_k\\to x$, and the $\\Gamma$-liminf inequality along this subsequence gives\n$$\nG(x)\\le\\liminf_{k\\to\\infty}G_{n_k}(y_k)\n\\le\\liminf_{k\\to\\infty}G(x_k).\n$$\nIn the case of a lower limit $-\\infty$, this contradicts properness; otherwise it is the required lower-semicontinuity inequality. For any $c$, recovery at every point of $\\{G\\le c\\}$ is eventually contained in the common compact container for the discrete sublevels $\\{G_n\\le c+1\\}$. Its limit therefore lies in that compact container. The sublevel $\\{G\\le c\\}$ is closed by lower semicontinuity, and is consequently compact. This proves the converse.\n\nFinally, every compact metric sublevel of $F$ is separable. Since\n$$\n\\operatorname{dom}F=\\bigcup_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}\\{F\\le k\\},\n$$\nthe union of countable dense subsets of those sublevels provides the required countable $D$ even when $X$ itself is not separable.\n\n**Arithmetic alphabets.** The ratio $\\alpha=\\ln2/\\ln3$ is irrational: a rational equality $\\alpha=p/q$ would imply $2^q=3^p$ for positive integers $p,q$, contrary to prime factorization. For every positive integer $N$, apply the pigeonhole principle to the $N+1$ fractional parts of $0,\\alpha,\\ldots,N\\alpha$. Two differ by less than or equal to $1/N$, giving integers $a,b$, not both zero, with\n$$\n0<|a\\ln2+b\\ln3|\\le\\frac{\\ln3}{N}.\n$$\nChanging sign gives arbitrarily small positive elements of $A_{23}$. Integer multiples of any such element approximate every real number to within that element. Thus $A_{23}$ is dense. The alphabet $A_2$ is dense because multiplication by the positive constant $\\ln2$ is a homeomorphism of $\\mathbb R$ and $\\mathbb Q$ is dense. If $F\\ge0$, every truncated-epigraph height is nonnegative, so the upward choices can all be made in $\\mathbb Q_{\\ge0}\\ln2$; the early finite values may also be chosen there. This proves the last assertion. ∎\n\n**Arithmetic, source and effective presentations.** An element $a\\ln2+b\\ln3$ has the finite code $(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2$. Its signed coefficients are essential to the density argument. The nonnegative event-count semigroup $\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\ln2+\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\ln3$ has only finitely many elements in each bounded interval, so that semigroup cannot replace $A_{23}$ in the theorem. A physical use of a negative coefficient requires a registered signed likelihood contrast or another realized signed source operation in addition to the arithmetic code.\n\nFor a rational open interval, an element of $A_{23}$ inside it can be found effectively by dovetailing integer pairs and precision levels. For $m\\in\\{2,3\\}$ and $z=(m-1)/(m+1)$, the rational bounds\n$$\n\\ln m\n=2\\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}\\frac{z^{2k+1}}{2k+1}+R_K,\n\\qquad\n0<R_K\\le\\frac{2z^{2K+1}}{(2K+1)(1-z^2)}\n$$\ncertify interval membership once the error is small enough. Density supplies a pair lying strictly inside the requested interval, and dovetailing eventually certifies that pair. The same logarithm bounds and an enumeration of rationals implement interval rounding in $A_2$.\n\nFor $r=a/b\\in\\mathbb Q_{\\ge0}$, with integers $a\\ge0$ and $b\\ge1$, the probability $p=2^{-r}$ is the unique positive solution of $p^b=2^{-a}$. It therefore has a finite algebraic descriptor. The nonnegative stochastic-instrument branch based on this probability realizes values $r\\ln2$, with its supplied finite preparation, phase, clock and record services. Its energy alphabet is $\\mathbb Q_{\\ge0}\\ln2$, which is dense in $[0,\\infty)$ rather than in all of $\\mathbb R$. For a signed $F$, an independently registered fixed lower bound permits a fixed offset $C$ with $F+C\\ge0$; one may choose $C=c\\ln2$ for a sufficiently large nonnegative integer $c$. Applying the nonnegative construction then realizes $F+C$. The offset remains in the physical ledger and action unless a separate signed-reference implementation realizes its subtraction. A level-dependent subtraction or a change of the action scale is not part of this argument.\n\nThe epigraph-net construction is an existence theorem for arbitrary $F$. For an arbitrary dense set $A$, it need not give finite descriptions of the selected energy values. For $A_{23}$ and $A_2$, the selected energies do have finite codes, but the sequence of selected support and energy tables need not be generated by one finite program. A uniform compiler requires an effective presentation of the finite epigraph nets, effective approximation by the supplied support dictionary, effective energy rounding and certified approximation bounds. An arbitrary good functional is not supplied with those data by the theorem; finitely coded programs without an additional oracle produce only countably many sequences, whereas good functionals already form an uncountable class on any nonempty metric space.\n\nFor a separable real Hilbert function space, or the separable closed real linear span of $\\operatorname{dom}F$ inside an arbitrary real Hilbert carrier, a supplied countable orthonormal basis gives a countable dense dictionary of finite linear combinations with coefficients in $\\mathbb Q$. In the complex case, use the separable closed complex linear span and coefficients in $\\mathbb Q+i\\mathbb Q$. These finite coefficient records provide a mathematical field decoder once the basis is specified. A physical field preparation, spatial embedding or all-input field response implementing that decoder requires its own source map.\n\nThe theorem classifies variational limits and their minimizers. Finite-support extended-real functionals have no nontrivial tangent domain, so first-variation responses and the persistence of degenerate stationary points lie outside this classification; Theorems Q.0.3e and Q.0.3f treat them on their families, and Construction Q.0.3h and Theorem Q.0.3l supply the source realization.\n\n<a id=\"construction-q03h-qualified-finite-rational-field-ledger-and-calibrated-response-source\"></a>\n\n**Construction Q.0.3h (Qualified Finite Rational-Field Ledger and Calibrated Response Source).** On the finite ideal primitive-service branch of Definition C.6c.4a, the quasilinear family of Theorem Q.0.3e has an exact finite rational-input likelihood/action realization coupled to continuing occurrences of the qualified cell of Theorem C.6c.P17a. The construction supplies its complete finite-input response map, exact symbolic stationary residual and resource record as follows.\n\n**1. Field table and variational limit.** At level $n\\ge1$ let $N_n=2^n$, $h_n=N_n^{-1}$ and $\\delta_n=h_n^2$, and let $Q_n\\subset V_n$ consist of the fields whose nodal values lie in $\\delta_n\\mathbb Z\\cap[-n,n]$. Its cardinality is\n$$\nM_n=(2nN_n^2+1)^{N_n^d}.\n$$\nLexicographic order of the nodal integer vectors fixes a complete index table. Take supplied rational coefficients $a_n\\in[0,1]$ converging to $a\\in[0,1]$, and define\n$$\nR_n(q)=1+E_{a_n}(q),\\qquad \\mathcal L_n(q)=(\\ln2)R_n(q)\\quad(q\\in Q_n),\n\\tag{Q.0.3h.1}\n$$\nwith $+\\infty$ off $Q_n$ as functionals on $H$. These values are nonnegative rationals times $\\ln2$. Indeed the negative scalar minimizer lies in $[-1,-3/4]$, increasing $a$ lowers $W_a(t)$ for negative $t$, and $W_a(t)\\ge0$ for $t\\ge0$. Thus the minimum over $a\\in[0,1]$ is $W_1(-1)=-1$. The offset $1$ is fixed at every level and is retained in the physical action.\n\nFor a simplex $T$ with rational nodal values $z_0,\\ldots,z_d$ and barycentric coordinates $\\lambda_i$, the exact identities\n$$\n\\int_T\\prod_i\\lambda_i^{\\alpha_i}\\,dx\n=|T|\\frac{d!\\prod_i\\alpha_i!}{(d+k)!},\\qquad\n\\int_Tq^k\\,dx\n=|T|\\frac{d!k!}{(d+k)!}\\sum_{\\alpha_0+\\cdots+\\alpha_d=k}\\prod_i z_i^{\\alpha_i}\n\\tag{Q.0.3h.2}\n$$\nhold for $|\\alpha|=k$. The second follows by the multinomial expansion. Rational linear algebra gives the affine gradient and rational volume; (Q.0.3h.2) for $k=1,3,4$ and the constant gradient terms therefore computes every entry of the complete table exactly. Enumeration of the input vectors, simplices, rational solves and finite sums terminates at every supplied $(d,n,a_n)$.\n\nThe sequence in (Q.0.3h.1) is equicoercive and Mosco-converges to $(\\ln2)(1+E_a)$. The lower bound and weak lower-limit proof of Theorem Q.0.3e apply to the subset $Q_n\\subset V_n$. For recovery, interpolate a fixed smooth periodic field and round each nodal value to the nearest multiple of $\\delta_n$. For large $n$ the amplitude bound $n$ is inactive. The rounding error is at most $\\delta_n/2$ in value and at most $C_d\\delta_n/h_n=C_dh_n$ in gradient. It therefore preserves strong $W^{1,4}$ convergence. Smooth approximation and a diagonal choice give recovery for every $u\\in X$. All polynomial integrals converge. Finite domains give attainment; equicoercivity and recovery give convergence of minimum values and of minimizing cluster points. The unique continuum minimizer is the constant $r(a)$, so every sequence of finite minimizers converges to it in $L^2$.\n\n**2. Exact stationary labels.** Let $\\varphi_1,\\ldots,\\varphi_{N_n^d}$ be the real nodal hat basis of $V_n$. Retain the rational vector\n$$\nB_n(q)_j=\\int_{\\mathbb T^d}\\bigl((4|\\nabla q|^2+2)\\nabla q\\cdot\\nabla\\varphi_j+(4q^3+3q^2+a_n)\\varphi_j\\bigr)\\,dx\n\\tag{Q.0.3h.3}\n$$\nand the bit $Z_n(q)$ which is one exactly when all reduced numerators vanish. Formula (Q.0.3h.2) computes this vector by finite rational arithmetic; exhaustive enumeration fixes a finite worst-case output width before the input arrives. A finite XOR-into-fresh lookup on the retained input and fresh output bank gives a reversible total decoder. Linearity in the test vector proves\n$$\nZ_n(q)=1\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad DE_{a_n}(q)[v]=0\\quad\\hbox{for every real }v\\in V_n.\n$$\nFor the coefficients of Corollary Q.0.3e.1, every real-$V_n$ stationary solution and the unique real-$V_n$ minimizer lie in $Q_n$. Thus the finite decoder represents their complete sets exactly, and minimization over $Q_n$ has the same exact minimum and unique minimizer as minimization over $V_n$. This output is a symbolic response to a finite classical field label. The all-real tangent domain in (Q.0.3e.3) remains a separate domain from the physical input set $Q_n$.\n\n**3. Primitive admission and qualified occurrences.** Definition C.6c.4a supplies fresh finite pure registers, finite local unitary matrices with explicit unitarity certificates, complete projective record services, finite classical circuits, and registered finite clock/profile services with the stated tariff. Use these services at one local cabinet port. This construction specifies a new completed ledger/phase macro on a separate appliance. The heat endpoint macro interface of Definition C.6c.4a and its protected passive payload are unchanged. General finite algebraic appliance matrices act on that appliance as primitive pulses of Definition C.6c.4a, while the fixed basis gates of Theorem C.6c.P17a act on its original cells.\n\nPut $b=\\max(1,\\lceil\\log_2M_n\\rceil)$ and use $N_{\\mathrm{cell}}=\\max(2,b)$ unchanged ready cells. Each has six data bits $(m,y,d,w,f_0,f_1)$, its 66-bit six-CNOT ROM and three-bit cursor. The original ready-state minimum $C_P(\\mu_0)=C_{\\mathrm{op}}=1$, attained by program 0, is supplied by Theorem C.6c.P17a. Copy the $b$ declared classical input bits to the exposed $w$ bits with $b$ CNOTs, then copy those values to fresh appliance controls with $b$ further CNOTs. Invalid binary labels have a recorded default path with the same reserved stock and time and admit no unknown phase input. All other cell factors and ports remain as in their owner.\n\nPrepare four independent fair bits $A_1,B_1,A_2,B_2$ with four charged Hadamards, retaining all factors and measurement records. Define the source circuit\n$$\nF_1=A_1\\wedge B_1,\\quad F_2=A_2\\wedge B_2,\\quad X_1=F_1,\\quad X_2=X_1\\mathbin{\\oplus}F_2.\n$$\nEach AND is a Toffoli into a fresh target and the $X_2$ XOR uses two CNOTs. Each cell executes its first ROM CNOT $m\\to f_0$ and cursor update before the first pair is consumed. Measure $A_1,B_1$, compute $F_1$ and deliver $X_1$ to the existing basis port 6. The remaining five core CNOTs are ingress, discrepancy, verification feedback, model write and second forecast, with their original cursor updates. After all second forecasts are frozen, measure $A_2,B_2$, compute $F_2,X_2$, and deliver $X_2$ to the appliance and terminal scoring registers. Compute a fresh appliance enable $e=X_1\\oplus X_2$ by two CNOTs from those delivered messages. The identity $e=F_2$ is a consequence of this circuit; the control wire is the delivered-message XOR.\n\nThe six ordered core address pairs are $(0,4),(6,1),(0,2),(1,2),(2,0),(0,5)$, with data addresses $(m,y,d,w,f_0,f_1)$ numbered $0$ through $5$. They give $m=f_1=X_1$ and forecast $(1+2X_1)/4=\\Pr(X_2=1\\mid X_1)$. The Brier risk is $3/16$ versus the matched fair-forecast risk $1/4$, so the gain $1/16$ exceeds the frozen margin $1/32$. The discrepancy $d=m_{\\mathrm{old}}\\oplus X_1$ and update $m\\leftarrow m\\oplus d=X_1$ are retained operations.\n\nUse precisely the equal-slot identity nulls of Theorem C.6c.6: remove the model write $d\\to m$, the second forecast $m\\to f_1$, or the verification feedback $y\\to d$. Each leaves $f_1=0$, so the forecast is $1/4$ while $\\Pr(X_2=1)=3/8$. Each null risk is therefore $(5/8)(1/16)+(3/8)(9/16)=1/4$. The complete original and null laws are the sixteen fair source strings of weight $1/16$ executed through the respective circuit, retaining every source, core, terminal and scoring factor. Their later comparator laws are specified next. These are continuing qualified occurrences of the original ready cells; subsequent payload loading and evolution do not reassign program 0 to the evolved states or to the entire cabinet.\n\n**4. Raw instrument and calibrated phase.** Fix a valid label $q$, write $R=R_n(q)$, $\\ell=R\\ln2$ and $p=2^{-R}>0$. If $R=A/B$ with integers $A\\ge0,B\\ge1$, then $p$ is the unique positive root of $p^B=2^{-A}$, so $p,\\sqrt p,\\sqrt{1-p}$ have finite algebraic descriptions. Define\n$$\n\\mathsf R_p=\\begin{pmatrix}\\sqrt p&\\sqrt{1-p}\\\\\\sqrt{1-p}&-\\sqrt p\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad \\mathsf R_p^*=\\mathsf R_p,\\qquad \\mathsf R_p^2=I.\n$$\nInstall the complete direct sum over all valid and invalid $b$-bit labels before any input; invalid blocks have $R=0$ and are rejected by their validity flag. Use four fresh qubits $B,C,D,E$ in $|0000\\rangle$. In forward mode act by identity on $B$; in reverse comparison mode act by $\\mathsf R_p$ when valid and enabled. Apply the three CNOTs $B\\to C\\to D\\to E$, then the complete computational record instrument on $E$. Conditional on enable, the probabilities for $z=0,1$ are $(1,0)$ in forward mode and $(p,1-p)$ in reverse mode; the complete retained poststate is $|zzzz\\rangle\\langle zzzz|$. These modes are the two registered raw comparison preparations.\n\nSince $\\Pr(e=1)=1/4$, the marked event $\\mathcal A=\\{\\mathrm{valid}=1,e=1,z=0\\}$ obeys\n$$\nP_F(\\mathcal A\\mid q)=\\tfrac14,\\qquad P_R(\\mathcal A\\mid q)=\\tfrac14p,\\qquad\n\\ln\\frac{P_F(\\mathcal A\\mid q)}{P_R(\\mathcal A\\mid q)}=R\\ln2=\\ell.\n\\tag{Q.0.3h.4}\n$$\nThe disabled probability $3/4$ and reverse enabled complement $(1-p)/4$ remain in the full instrument. For each enabled fair-source string its common factor $1/16$ cancels in the same raw ratio. Conditioning both event laws to probability one would destroy that ratio; (Q.0.3h.4) uses the unconditioned weights.\n\nFix independent calibrations $\\tau>0$ and $\\hbar>0$, and put $g(t)=1-\\cos(2\\pi t/\\tau)$ on $[0,\\tau]$, zero outside. Then $g\\ge0$, its endpoints vanish, $\\max g=2$ and $\\int g=\\tau$. Every self-adjoint involution $W$ is implemented by the admitted positive drive\n$$\nH_W(t)=\\frac{\\pi\\hbar}{2\\tau}g(t)(I-W),\\qquad\nU_W(\\tau)=\\exp\\bigl(-i\\pi(I-W)/2\\bigr)=W.\n\\tag{Q.0.3h.5}\n$$\nIts peak norm is at most $2\\pi\\hbar/\\tau$ and norm-action at most $\\pi\\hbar$. Controlled involutions, CNOT, Hadamard and XOR-into-fresh lookup matrices have the displayed certificate. The appliance coupling is a supplied primitive pulse of the source.\n\nInstall the diagonal all-label phase generator on the separate probe $Q$, with declared dummy branches on $Q_{\\mathrm{dummy}}$. On a valid ready forward input its restriction is\n$$\nH_q(t)=\\frac{\\hbar\\ell}{\\tau}g(t)|0\\rangle\\langle0|_Q,\\qquad\nU_q=\\operatorname{diag}(e^{-i\\ell},1),\\qquad\n\\int_0^\\tau(E_0-E_1)\\,dt=\\hbar\\ell.\n\\tag{Q.0.3h.6}\n$$\nThe channel equals that of $U_{\\mathrm{ref}}(\\ell)=\\operatorname{diag}(1,e^{i\\ell})$. The physical splitting integral fixes the real action lift, including the offset in (Q.0.3h.1), independently of a phase representative modulo $2\\pi$. The same $\\hbar,\\tau$ are used at every level.\n\nAccept an unknown $\\rho_{Q R_{\\mathrm{ext}}}$, possibly entangled with any external reference, only after valid forward readiness and immediately before the hold. All setup records are then independent of this input. During the hold all other supplier operations act on separate factors. Thus for every supplier-history instrument $\\mathcal I_{z,\\mathrm{sup}}$,\n$$\n\\mathcal I_z(\\rho\\otimes\\sigma_{\\mathrm{sup}})\n=\\bigl(\\operatorname{Ad}(U_q)\\otimes\\operatorname{Id}_{R_{\\mathrm{ext}}}\\bigr)(\\rho)\n \\otimes\\mathcal I_{z,\\mathrm{sup}}(\\sigma_{\\mathrm{sup}}).\n\\tag{Q.0.3h.7}\n$$\nSumming histories gives the normalized channel at that accepted port; the weight $1/4$ of the successful forward branch belongs to the preceding readiness stage. The external reference belongs to the input supplier and is never copied.\n\nFor diagnostics, prepare $Q$ as $|+x\\rangle$ in the preceding Hadamard slot. For arbitrary-input mode this preparation acts on the dummy instead and the unknown $Q$ arrives at the hold cut. With $\\Pi_{b,\\pm}=(I\\pm\\sigma_b)/2$, $b=x,y$, use fresh pointer $P=|0\\rangle$ and the involution\n$$\nC_b=\\Pi_{b,+}\\otimes I_P+\\Pi_{b,-}\\otimes X_P.\n$$\nAfter its pulse, copy $P$ into two fresh records and apply the complete projective pointer instrument. The conditional state on $Q R_{\\mathrm{ext}}$ is $(\\Pi_{b,s}\\otimes I)\\rho_{\\mathrm{hold}}(\\Pi_{b,s}\\otimes I)$, with three matching retained pointer records and the supplier record. Both outcomes are retained and sum to a normalized instrument. The diagnostic probabilities are $(1+\\cos\\ell)/2$ and $(1+\\sin\\ell)/2$ for the positive $x,y$ outcomes. Zero idle Hamiltonians remove intervening free phases. These measurements follow the hold output cut.\n\n**5. Finite word and all-input ownership.** The appliance has consecutive paid slots for input/control dispatch; the Bernoulli pulse; three Bernoulli copies; ready dispatch and complete Bernoulli record; diagnostic preparation or dummy; phase hold; detector or dummy; two pointer copies; and terminal dispatch and complete pointer record. A dispatch is the full finite XOR-into-fresh involution, with all controls and targets owned. Equivalently it can be expanded into its finite basis-gate word, with the resulting larger counts. The source includes every original cell, fresh source and comparator bit, field/control copy, phase/dummy/pointer factor, validity/failure flag, controller, clock, immutable coefficient/programme ROM and receipt bank. Every occupied interval is retained through the specified horizon. No register is reset or reused.\n\nAt each supplied level the table, algebraic coefficients, supports, complete controlled sums, padded word, clock and peak reserve are installed before the classical input. Loading always uses the same fixed CNOT lists. Hence the source-support and full-operator tariff are independent of $q$. An external classical input is explicitly owned as an input factor. If preparation is included instead, an equal-load two-rail encoding with one occupied rail per logical bit gives a fixed preparation schedule; these are distinct invoice conventions.\n\nProgramme/coefficient ROM may be supplied as preloaded pure-register stock under Definition C.6c.4a, charging its complete carrier and commissioning service. The original cell-ROM writes remain basis calls. The exact mathematical enumeration specifies the delivered finite data; physical execution of an onsite compiler additionally requires its input/output/workspace/trace and preparation time to be retained and charged.\n\nFor stock $B$, bit-time $I$ in units of $\\tau_*$, pulse invocations $G$, complete record services $J$ and charged time slots $K$, the one-port tariff is\n$$\n\\frac{V}{u_*}=\\alpha_BB+\\alpha_II+\\alpha_GG+\\alpha_JJ+\\alpha_KK\n+\\alpha_\\Omega\\frac{\\tau_*}{\\hbar}\\max_t\\|H_{\\mathrm{total}}(t)\\|,\n\\tag{Q.0.3h.8}\n$$\nwith fixed positive coefficients. Interport lane and link terms vanish because this cabinet uses no interport service. Slots are serialized; commuting drives within a slot contribute their simultaneous sum. For a smooth drive $g(t)K_0$, absolute switching throughput is bounded by $4\\|K_0\\|$, and its integrated Heisenberg work is $U^*H(\\tau)U-H(0)=0$. These are drive-operator records. During the normalized hold the supplier records obey (Q.0.3h.7).\n\nKeeping the full input word, $B_n(q),Z_n(q)$ and all source records gives a total projection to the rational field, symbolic residual, raw ledger and calibrated phase/action for every admitted input, even when phases coincide. The all-real stationary completeness theorem and Corollary Q.0.3e.1 then identify exactly which finite labels represent stationary solutions. The selected likelihood ledger in (Q.0.3h.4), relative action in (Q.0.3h.6) and complete source-resource objective in (Q.0.3h.8) are separately registered quantities. An identification with heat or total-resource minimization requires its own owner. This source responds to finite field labels through its decoded-residual table; Theorem Q.0.3l supplies the canonical field, action and tangent interface with finite kick instruments and controlled normal-state limits.\n\n<a id=\"example-q03h1-two-node-field-table\"></a>\n\n**Example Q.0.3h.1 (Two-Node Field Table).** Take $d=1$, $n=1$, $N_1=2$, $a_1=1$ and nodal values $z_0/4,z_1/4$ with $z_i\\in\\{-4,\\ldots,4\\}$. The 81 valid labels are $j=9(z_0+4)+(z_1+4)$, and the other 47 seven-bit labels have explicit invalid defaults. The exact two-interval periodic energy is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE(z_0,z_1)={}&\\frac{(z_1-z_0)^4}{16}+\\frac{(z_1-z_0)^2}{4}\n+\\frac{z_0^4+z_0^3z_1+z_0^2z_1^2+z_0z_1^3+z_1^4}{1280}\\\\\n&+\\frac{z_0^3+z_0^2z_1+z_0z_1^2+z_1^3}{256}+\\frac{z_0+z_1}{8}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{Q.0.3h.9}\n$$\nThe gradient has magnitude $|z_1-z_0|/2$ on both half-intervals; (Q.0.3h.2) gives the displayed potential terms. Thus (Q.0.3h.9) is the exact geometric integral. Put $R=1+E$. The unique stationary/minimizing field is $(-1,-1)$, label 0, with $R=0$; direct rational comparison of the 81 entries gives $R_{\\max}=1366/5$. The true nodal residuals are $4\\partial E/\\partial z_i$. Their common denominator is 320 and each signed numerator fits 19 bits. A direct XOR lookup emits their two's-complement codes, a valid bit and a stationary bit; an additional done bit completes the 41-bit residual bank. Invalid inputs emit zero numerator codes with both flags zero and admit no unknown phase input.\n\nSeven unchanged cells of Theorem C.6c.P17a carry the seven label bits. At the phase hold the installed all-label generator of (Q.0.3h.6) has peak norm $(2732/5)\\ln2\\,\\hbar/\\tau$ and norm-action $(1366/5)\\ln2\\,\\hbar$, both attained at the two labels $(z_0,z_1)=(\\mp4,\\pm4)$ with $R=R_{\\max}$.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q03i-exact-all-real-finite-bank-mean-ledger-and-tangent-responses\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.3i (Exact All-Real Finite-Bank Mean Ledger and Tangent Responses).** For a polynomial ledger with rational coefficients on a finite element space, a finite count-score instrument on independent Bernoulli input banks has mean ledger, force and tangent responses equal to $L(q)$, $\\partial_jL(q)$ and $DL(q)[v]$ at every real input of the amplitude window. Sections 2 and 6 characterize all instruments on this input interface: no finite bank processor outputs a nonconstant deterministic value or applies a nonconstant unitary phase channel.\n\n**1. Source contract.**\n\nFix a finite FE space with rational basis data, $N$ nodal coordinates, an amplitude window $R>0$ rational, and a polynomial ledger $L(q)$ with rational coefficients. The quasilinear ledger of Theorem Q.0.3e is one instance in every dimension. Set $q_j=R(2p_j-1)$, $p_j\\in[0,1]$. For an integer $k$, the **input** is the bank\n\n$$\\rho_p=\\bigotimes_{j=1}^N[\\operatorname{diag}(1-p_j,p_j)]^{\\otimes k}.$$ \n\nEach of the $Nk$ physical input qubits is independently supplied. The service neither knows $p$, receives a name for $p$, prepares this bank from a name, nor clones a single unknown qubit. The product-input promise is indispensable. Correlations among the input copies can change the stated response. The implemented channel remains defined and normalized for arbitrary correlated inputs and arbitrary external reference extensions, but their mean need not equal $L(q)$.\n\nThe bank is an input manifest on the finite Hilbert/instrument branch in addition to the finite-field-table interface of Construction Q.0.3h. Conditional on delivery of this input bank, all remaining source data below have finite effective descriptions independent of p. Rational p can be prepared by the finitely described primitive services; arbitrary p is external physical input, exactly as an unknown reference is external input in Construction Q.0.3h.\n\n**2. Exhaustive characterization for this interface.**\n\nWrite $S_{j}$ for the number of ones in the $j$-th bank. For $s=(s_1,\\dots,s_N)$, define\n\n$$B_{k,s}(p)=\\prod_j\\binom{k}{s_j}p_j^{s_j}(1-p_j)^{k-s_j}.$$ \n\nEvery normalized quantum instrument acting on this input, with any fixed finite ancilla, controller, adaptation and finite stopping horizon, has outcome probabilities\n\n$$P_p(y)=\\sum_s B_{k,s}(p)c_{y,s},$$\n\nwhere $c_{y,s}\\ge 0$ and $\\sum_y c_{y,s}=1$. Conversely, every such finite coefficient family is realized by measuring the bank in the computational basis, counting $s$ and applying the finite classical kernel $c_{.,s}$, provided its coefficients are admitted finitely described source data. This is an iff for the mathematical finite instruments; effectivity of the coefficients is an additional source condition.\n\nProof: expand $\\rho_{p}$ as a convex combination of computational basis projectors. The conditional outcome probability on a basis string lies in [0,1]. Average these probabilities over strings with the same count s. Each such string has the same product weight. This gives c and its normalization. The converse is the stated normalized measure-and-process instrument. Thus arbitrary quantum processing offers no larger probability class on this commuting input family. The same argument with positive output operators gives an exact Bernstein expansion of every output-state family; with an additional arbitrary quantum payload, it gives a convex combination of CPTP channels, one per s.\n\nFor a signed bounded readout $Y$, its mean is precisely a polynomial of coordinatewise degree $\\le k$, with Bernstein coefficients $y_s=\\mathbb{E}[Y\\mid S=s]$. Conversely every polynomial of coordinatewise degree $\\le k$ has a unique such Bernstein expansion and is realized by assigning the finite output label $s$ the score $y_{s}$. For every real $p$ the physical law of the input bank and output records has the displayed mean, without learning $p$.\n\nAn event probability requires Bernstein coefficients in [0,1]; a polynomial with values in [0,1] need not have them. A signed force observable is therefore a score mean, typed separately from a raw likelihood. Degree elevation may enlarge the probability class, but no finite bank admits a polynomial that takes the value 0 or 1 at an interior point of the cube or of one of its faces unless the polynomial is constant on that face; at a vertex the value is the Bernstein coefficient of that vertex, which may be 0 or 1.\n\n**3. Joint ledger, force and tangent response.**\n\nLet $F_j(q)=\\partial_j L(q)$. Choose $k$ large enough to represent $L$ and every $F_{j}$ in the Bernstein basis after $q=R(2p-1)$. Compute their rational coefficient tables $a_{s}$ and $b_{j,s}$. One physical count record $s$ then has scores\n\n$$A(s)=a_s,\\qquad F_j^{\\mathrm{out}}(s)=b_{j,s}.$$ \n\nTheir exact expectations are $L(q)$ and $F_j(q)$ for every real $q\\in[-R,R]^N$. Signed rational scores are fixed readout calibrations. An exact test for a zero force is excluded by section 6 whenever the window contains inputs with zero and with nonzero force.\n\nFor an **unknown real tangent** $v_j=T(2t_j-1)$, supply a separate independent bank of $N$ Bernoulli qubits with parameters $t_{j}$. If $Z_{j}$ is its recorded bit, output\n\n$$D(s,Z)=\\sum_j b_{j,s}T(2Z_j-1).$$ \n\nIndependence gives $\\mathbb{E}D=\\sum_j F_j(q)v_j=DL(q)[v]$. This is an all-real tangent intervention. Alternatively, the full force vector is a retained response, and a finitely supplied rational tangent is a deterministic classical readout; the two interfaces are distinct.\n\nThe complete instrument can retain the original measured input basis strings, count registers, score labels, source/qualification records, invalid-code flags and clock records. For a basis-string projector $P_{x}$ the Kraus map is $(P_x\\otimes I_{\\mathrm{ext}})\\rho(P_x\\otimes I_{\\mathrm{ext}})$, followed by its finite record isometry. The sum is TP. It preserves every external-reference marginal; the measurement removes correlations with the measured input branch by branch, while classical correlations with the recorded string remain. Computational-basis copying copies the measured classical record only. All outcome branches, including extreme and low-probability strings, are retained.\n\n**4. Finite service and costs.**\n\nThe coefficient tables are obtained by finite rational polynomial arithmetic. They are fixed before the input arrives and never depend on its unknown p. The physical word consists of a computational-basis record service on each input qubit, reversible finite counting/XOR record circuits, optional finite score-label writes, and retained archives. Its generators can use the same finitely described involution pulses as Construction Q.0.3h. Tensoring an independently qualified cell of Theorem C.6c.P17a supplies the same qualification component used there; a new feedback role requires its own qualification.\n\nEvery input qubit, fresh pointer, controller, programme/table ROM, comparator/work register, count/score record and retained archive is an owned factor. At the external-input convention the bank's preparation is outside the appliance boundary and explicitly invoiced to its input owner. Repeated trials consume newly supplied independent banks. A preparation-inclusive source must add its preparation supplier, work, time and stock.\n\nUse the fixed positive tariff and units of (Q.0.3h.8). The finite circuit fixes B,I,G,J,K and the full controlled-generator peak before any p arrives. A one-port arrangement has zero link contribution; input stock and maintenance are charged, and no register is reset or reused. Each pulse's physical duration, generator norm and integrated norm are retained; finite service expansion and all additional record supports are charged. This is a finite constructive descriptor for each (N,k,R,L); the bounds are not uniform in the refinement N or the accuracy.\n\n**5. Sampling, readout and instrument errors.**\n\nLet $M=\\max_s\\max(|a_s|,\\max_j|b_{j,s}|)$. For $m$ independently supplied trials, empirical means satisfy\n\n$$\\mathbb P\\!\\left(\\max_{0\\le j\\le N}|\\overline Y_j-\\mathbb EY_j|>\\epsilon\\right)\\le 2(N+1)\\exp[-m\\epsilon^2/(2M^2)].$$\n\nThis follows by applying the bounded-variable exponential estimate to scores in $[-M,M]$ and taking a union bound. The observables may be correlated within a trial; no independence between score coordinates is used. For a supplied norm on $V_{n}$, define the finite constant $C_n=\\sup_{\\lVert v\\rVert\\le 1}\\sum_j|v_j|$. Then a maximum coordinate error $\\epsilon$ gives dual-force error $\\le C_n\\epsilon$. $C_n$ and coefficient range $M$ must be retained and need not be uniform in $n$. Tangent scores are bounded by $NTM$, giving the corresponding scalar sampling bound.\n\nIf a real implemented full instrument differs from the ideal in reference-stable trace norm by at most $\\delta$ (diamond norm with the convention $\\lVert\\rho-\\sigma\\rVert_1$, no factor $1/2$), any score of absolute value $\\le M$ has mean error $\\le M\\delta$. A score-calibration error $\\eta$ contributes $\\le\\eta$. If an input bank differs from the specified product state in trace norm by $\\delta_{\\mathrm{in}}$, contractivity gives another $M\\delta_{\\mathrm{in}}$; an arbitrary correlated bank is outside this small-error bound. These errors are separate from FE/continuum error and from resource-comparison tolerances.\n\nMultiplication of all ledger/force scores by a fixed independently calibrated $\\kappa_{A}$ gives action and action-derivative units. It is an operational mean-score calibration; by section 6 it cannot be promoted to the coherent relative-action channel with unitary diag(1,exp(i $\\kappa_{A}$ L(q)/hbar)).\n\n**6. Exact no-go for deterministic all-real outputs and coherent action promotion.**\n\nFor $p\\in(0,1)^N$ every computational basis string has strictly positive weight. If a fixed finite instrument outputs a deterministic value $g(p)$, all its conditional distributions on those strings must be the same point mass at $g(p)$. Applying this to any second interior $p'$ forces $g(p')=g(p)$. Therefore no nonconstant exact force or field value can be emitted deterministically from one such finite bank. This remains true with arbitrary finite quantum ancillas/adaptation: Section 2 already exhausts their outcome laws. Confidence-controlled approximate estimation is not contradicted.\n\nA stronger channel statement holds. Suppose an arbitrary unknown reference payload is to undergo the unitary channel $U_p(\\cdot)U_p^*$, with no postselection, after processing the bank. The output channel is $\\sum_x w_x(p)\\Phi_x$, with every $w_x(p)>0$ in the interior. If this convex combination is a unitary channel, every $\\Phi_{x}$ is that same unitary channel: in a Choi representation the sum is rank one and positive, so each positive summand has support in that one-dimensional space; TP fixes its coefficient. Thus $U_p$'s channel is constant on the interior. In particular no nonconstant relative-phase channel with unitary $\\operatorname{diag}(1,\\exp(i\\kappa_{A}L(q)/\\hbar))$ can be applied exactly to an arbitrary reference payload by a finite bank processor. Keeping failure/postselection flags changes the specified task and cannot be removed from the invoice.\n\nA diagonal score-controlled hold produces the mixture of relative-phase channels with unitaries $\\operatorname{diag}(1,\\exp(i\\kappa_A a_s/\\hbar))$, not the relative-phase channel of the average $L(q)$. Replacing this mixture by the unitary $\\operatorname{diag}(1,\\exp(i\\kappa_{A}\\,\\mathbb{E}A/\\hbar))$ is invalid. A scalar global phase alone has a constant quantum channel and is not the no-go target. This separates the all-real mean-force interface from the coherent action channel, which Theorem Q.0.3l supplies on canonical field inputs.\n\n**7. Continuum force.**\n\nFor Theorem Q.0.3e let $X=W^{1,4}(\\mathbb{T}^d)$, and use its usual norm. Its derivative $F=DE_a:X\\to X^*$ is locally Lipschitz: on $\\lVert u\\rVert_X,\\lVert w\\rVert_X\\le R$,\n\n$$\\|F(u)-F(w)\\|_{X^*}\\le C_d(1+R+R^2)\\|u-w\\|_X.$$ \n\nProof: $\\bigl||z|^2z-|y|^2y\\bigr|\\le C(|z|^2+|y|^2)|z-y|$, Holder with exponents $2,4$ and $4/3$, and the same cubic/quadratic identities for the scalar potential. The linear $a$-term cancels. Hence any supplied field approximants $u_{n}$ with certified $X$-error $\\eta_{n}$ give a full continuum dual-force bound $C_d(1+R+R^2)\\eta_n$ for $F(u_n)$. Such a certificate is additional to $L^2$ Mosco convergence and to finite nodal observations of an arbitrary $W^{1,4}$ field, and the physical finite output is the restriction $F(u_n)|_{V_n}$ of the full $X^*$ functional.\n\nThere can be no universal upgrading rule that reconstructs the full continuum force uniformly on an infinite-dimensional field ball from finitely many linear field observations, even for a quadratic ledger. On an infinite-dimensional real Hilbert space $H$ take $E(u)=\\lVert u\\rVert^2/2$ and $F(u)=u$ under Riesz identification. Any finite-rank linear observation $T$ has a unit vector $h$ in its kernel. Inputs $h$ and $-h$ give identical observations, while their forces are distance 2 apart. Any reconstruction from that shared observation has error at least 1 on one input. The same conclusion holds for randomized output in worst-case expected norm by the triangle inequality. Thus convergence in a weaker variational topology supplies no uniform full-force source. This obstruction covers all processing of the specified finite observation interface; richer input contracts are outside it.\n\n**8. Summary.**\n\nThe construction supplies exact all-real finite-dimensional mean ledger, force and tangent responses with normalized spectator-safe instruments and finite-sample and source budgets, and it classifies every instrument on this input interface. Preparation of an arbitrary real bank is external input, and by section 6 the mean scores cannot replace a coherent calibrated action channel.\n\n<a id=\"example-q03i1-nonconstant-two-node-all-real-input\"></a>**Example Q.0.3i.1 (Nonconstant Two-Node All-Real Input).** Write the nodal values as $q_0,q_1\\in[-1,1]$, rather than the integer labels $z_j=4q_j$ of Example Q.0.3h.1. The geometric energy is\n$$\nE(q_0,q_1)=16(q_1-q_0)^4+4(q_1-q_0)^2\n+\\frac{q_0^4+q_0^3q_1+q_0^2q_1^2+q_0q_1^3+q_1^4}{5}\n+\\frac{q_0^3+q_0^2q_1+q_0q_1^2+q_1^3}{4}\n+\\frac{q_0+q_1}{2}.\n\\tag{Q.0.3i.1}\n$$\nSet $q_j=2p_j-1$. Supply four independent Bernoulli qubits for each of the two real input parameters, eight physical input qubits in total. Retain every one of the 256 possible raw bit strings. The following matrices give the complete 25 count-score entries for the energy and its two nodal derivatives. Rows are $s_0=0,\\ldots,4$ and columns $s_1=0,\\ldots,4$; no count label is a label for the unknown real field.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{pmatrix}\n-1 & -1 & \\frac{7}{3} & \\frac{15}{2} & \\frac{1361}{5}\\\\\n-1 & -\\frac{11}{4} & -\\frac{17}{12} & -\\frac{623}{10} & \\frac{17}{2}\\\\\n\\frac{7}{3} & -\\frac{17}{12} & \\frac{1819}{45} & -\\frac{5}{4} & \\frac{11}{3}\\\\\n\\frac{15}{2} & -\\frac{623}{10} & -\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} & 1\\\\\n\\frac{1361}{5} & \\frac{17}{2} & \\frac{11}{3} & 1 & 3\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$$\nF_0^{\\mathrm{out}}=\\begin{pmatrix}\n0 & -\\frac{7}{2} & -\\frac{15}{2} & -\\frac{698}{5} & -\\frac{2637}{5}\\\\\n5 & \\frac{9}{8} & \\frac{3653}{60} & \\frac{2267}{40} & -\\frac{1391}{10}\\\\\n\\frac{17}{2} & -\\frac{1191}{20} & \\frac{1}{6} & \\frac{1201}{20} & -\\frac{15}{2}\\\\\n\\frac{1401}{10} & -\\frac{2237}{40} & -\\frac{1201}{20} & \\frac{1}{8} & -3\\\\\n\\frac{2647}{5} & \\frac{708}{5} & \\frac{59}{6} & \\frac{13}{2} & 4\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$$\nF_1^{\\mathrm{out}}=\\begin{pmatrix}\n0 & 5 & \\frac{17}{2} & \\frac{1401}{10} & \\frac{2647}{5}\\\\\n-\\frac{7}{2} & \\frac{9}{8} & -\\frac{1191}{20} & -\\frac{2237}{40} & \\frac{708}{5}\\\\\n-\\frac{15}{2} & \\frac{3653}{60} & \\frac{1}{6} & -\\frac{1201}{20} & \\frac{59}{6}\\\\\n-\\frac{698}{5} & \\frac{2267}{40} & \\frac{1201}{20} & \\frac{1}{8} & \\frac{13}{2}\\\\\n-\\frac{2637}{5} & -\\frac{1391}{10} & -\\frac{15}{2} & -3 & 4\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor completeness, the finite coefficient construction itself verifies every displayed entry. If\n$$\nf(2p_0-1,2p_1-1)=\\sum_{a,b=0}^4 c_{ab}p_0^a p_1^b,\n$$\nits score at $(s_0,s_1)$ is\n$$\ny_{s_0s_1}=\\sum_{a\\le s_0,\\ b\\le s_1}c_{ab}\n\\frac{\\binom{s_0}{a}}{\\binom4a}\n\\frac{\\binom{s_1}{b}}{\\binom4b}.\n\\tag{Q.0.3i.2}\n$$\nIndeed, for $S\\sim\\operatorname{Bin}(4,p)$, counting ordered distinct successful positions gives $\\mathbb E\\binom Sa=\\binom4a p^a$; independence gives the two-coordinate identity. Substitution of $f=E,\\partial_{q_0}E,\\partial_{q_1}E$ proves the three exact expectation identities for every real pair. The absolute score bounds are $1361/5$ for $E$ and $2647/5$ for either derivative. These finitely supplied rational scores are readout calibrations of the count record. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q03j-exact-nonpolynomial-mean-responses-with-conditional-finite-expected-source-tariff\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.3j (Exact Nonpolynomial Mean Responses with Conditional Finite Expected Source Tariff).** This is a callable-source extension of Theorem Q.0.3i: the number of requested input samples is random with finite mean and has no fixed bound. The on-demand supplier of independent Bernoulli inputs is a source contract of the theorem.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-and-finite-descriptor\"></a>**Theorem and finite descriptor.**\n\nLet $f:[0,1]^N\\to\\mathbb{R}^D$ have rational-function components with rational coefficients and denominator nonzero on the closed cube. The denominators come with finite positive lower-bound certificates. Thus $f$ is $C^\\infty$, its values on rational grids are rational, its first two derivative bounds are finite computably bounded, and its exact grid evaluation has bit complexity polynomial in the grid bit length for fixed $f,N,D$. The field energy, its gradient, or finitely many derivative responses can be chosen together as $f$. This includes nonpolynomial functions such as $1/(1+\\sum_j p_j^2)$.\n\nRegister $r=3/8$. Draw $L\\ge 1$ with $P(L=l)=w_l=(1-r)r^{l-1}$: at each level use exactly three fresh fair bits, designate three of the eight words as continue and the remaining five as stop. Retain all raw selector words and the STOP record. Conditional on $L=l$ exactly $3l$ selector bits have been consumed. Select $L$ before commissioning the large input bank; no large bank is held during level selection. Request $2^L$ independent samples of each of the $N$ Bernoulli inputs and one independent $N$-bit baseline sample $Z$. All consumed input banks and raw outcomes are retained, with the supplier's product/independence contract explicitly recorded.\n\nSplit the $2^L$ samples into halves and write $A,B\\in[0,1]^N$ for their empirical means. Return the rational score\n\n$$Y=f(Z)+\\frac{f((A+B)/2)-(f(A)+f(B))/2}{w_L}.$$ \n\nThis is a finite effective algorithm on each finite branch $L$, with only finitely described rational coins, counts, arithmetic and score calibration. It never queries the real $p$. $L$ is finite almost surely. A truncation $L\\le L_{\\max}$ is a different source with a calculable bias.\n\n**Proof of exactness and variance.**\n\nLet $B_m f(p)=\\mathbb{E}f(\\text{mean of } m \\text{ i.i.d. Bernoulli vectors})$. For $\\Delta_{l}$ equal to the numerator above,\n\n$$\\mathbb E\\Delta_l=B_{2^l}f(p)-B_{2^{l-1}}f(p).$$ \n\nThe baseline mean is $B_1 f(p)$. Uniform continuity and the elementary mean-square bound $\\mathbb{E}\\lVert\\text{mean}-p\\rVert^2\\le N/(4m)$ give $B_m f(p)\\to f(p)$ uniformly.\n\nFor a scalar component with Hessian operator norm $\\le H$, Taylor's theorem about $(A+B)/2$ yields\n\n$$|\\Delta_l|\\le(H/8)\\|A-B\\|^2.$$ \n\nFor $m=2^{l-1}$, each coordinate of $A-B$ is the mean of $m$ independent centered differences of two Bernoulli variables, each bounded by 1. Independence and expansion of the fourth moment give $\\mathbb{E}(A_j-B_j)^4\\le C/m^2$. Since $(\\sum_j x_j^2)^2\\le N\\sum_j x_j^4$,\n\n$$\\mathbb E\\|A-B\\|^4\\le CN^2/m^2.$$ \n\nTherefore $\\mathbb{E}|\\Delta_l|\\le CHN\\,2^{-l}$ and $\\mathbb{E}|\\Delta_l|^2\\le CH^2N^2\\,4^{-l}$, uniformly over every real $p$ in the cube. Absolute summability justifies the telescoping expectation:\n\n$$\\mathbb EY=B_1f+\\sum_{l\\ge1}\\mathbb E\\Delta_l=f(p).$$ \n\nAlso $\\mathbb{E}|\\Delta_L/w_L|^2=\\sum_l\\mathbb{E}|\\Delta_l|^2/w_l<\\infty$ because $4r>1$. The vector version follows by summing component estimates. This gives a uniform finite second-moment bound $V_{f}$ with computable constants. Averaging $M$ independent complete runs yields $\\mathbb{E}\\lVert\\operatorname{mean}(Y)-f(p)\\rVert^2\\le V_f/M$. Markov's inequality gives confidence $\\le V_f/(M\\epsilon^2)$ for Euclidean error $>\\epsilon$. A tangent bank independent of the entire source gives the same exact all-real bilinear derivative response as in Theorem Q.0.3i; its bounded score factor preserves finite variance.\n\n**Expected budget and the essential schedule.**\n\nFor $r=3/8$, $\\mathbb{E}[2^L]=5$, $\\mathbb{E}[L]=8/5$, and the mean raw selector consumption is $24/5$ fair bits. The bank and independent baseline together consume mean $6N$ Bernoulli input qubits (plus $N$ for a tangent bank when used). Also $\\sum_l 4^{-l}/w_l=6/5$. Expected input consumption is finite because $\\sum_l w_l 2^l<\\infty$ when $2r<1$. Finite expected input consumption leaves the expected complete tariff open: serially retaining $2^l$ bits for $2^l$ slots would charge order $4^l$ bit-time and diverge at $r=3/8$.\n\nUse instead a callable finite-stage factory that, after $L$ is selected, delivers the specified finite bank and a parallel finite circuit. Read the bank in one scheduled parallel slot (full support and summed generator norm charged). Count by a balanced adder tree, then use finite rational arithmetic and archive collection. The retained selector bits, their preparation/record pulses and their pre-bank holding intervals add at most $\\operatorname{polynomial}(l)$ support and work, including $O(l^2)$ pre-bank selector bit-time. The circuit has $\\operatorname{polynomial}(l)$ depth and $O(2^l\\operatorname{polynomial}(l))$ support/bit-time/gate costs for fixed $N,f$. Fresh records and arithmetic workspace are retained without resets. Full simultaneous Hamiltonian norm is bounded by the corresponding sum over active primitive supports, $O(2^l\\operatorname{polynomial}(l))$. Include local supplier commissioning, coefficient ROM, input-bank delivery, qualification, clock distribution and all retained intervals. Under a callable-source tariff bounded by $C_f\\,2^l\\operatorname{polynomial}(l)$, the total expected tariff is finite since $2r<1$.\n\nThis is a sufficient source contract. If the factory's commissioning or stock-delivery costs exceed that bound, the exact expectation theorem remains true and the finiteness of the expected total tariff must be rechecked. The rational encoded score labels have $O(l)$ bit length for fixed $f$; they are digital signed scores, and an analog force actuator or action reserve proportional to the score magnitude has its own magnitude-dependent source cost. Each realization has finite support and finite duration, while the source family has no fixed maximum support, peak strength or worst-case latency. The input supplier owns the requested independent samples and their preparation costs.\n\nEvery conditional-L processing map is normalized and CP on all input/reference extensions; the direct sum over the normalized stopping law is normalized. No failing branches are discarded. The response is a signed score with an unbounded range across L. Robustness under perturbations in total variation or diamond distance requires in addition the weighted-score moment or tail control appropriate to this unbounded readout.\n\n**Boundary.**\n\nThe construction extends the mean-response source from polynomial ledgers to rational-function responses with certified denominators. The positive-mixture argument of Theorem Q.0.3i, section 6, applies on every fully supported finite raw branch and on their countable positive sum, so a nonconstant coherent relative-phase channel with unitary $\\operatorname{diag}(1,\\exp(i\\kappa_A f_j(p)/\\hbar))$ is excluded for this interface as well; the obstruction of section 7 to uniform continuum force recovery is unchanged.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q03k-exact-mean-logarithmic-likelihood-ledger-and-accessible-generator-derivative\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.3k (Exact Mean Logarithmic Likelihood Ledger and Accessible Generator Derivative).** For a repeatable instrument source with success probability $p(u)>0$, the harmonic score of the geometric waiting time has mean $-\\log p(u)$; on a source whose nominated control gate has a two-eigenvalue generator, paired shifted invocations give a bounded score with mean $p'(u)$ and a logarithmic-force score with mean $\\frac{d}{du}[-\\log p(u)]$. Repeatable preparation and the shifted interventions are premises of the source contract.\n\n**1. Log ledger from repeated events.**\n\nFix a normalized source instrument with success probability $p(u)>0$ at its all-real externally supplied control $u$. Each invocation prepares a fresh instance through the registered supplier and retains both success/failure outcomes. Invoke it independently until the first success. Let $N\\ge 1$ count all invocations and output the rational signed-score label $H_{N-1}=\\sum_{j=1}^{N-1}1/j$, with $H_{0}=0$. Retain every source invocation, all FAIL records, the SUCCESS/STOP record, controller, arithmetic and archive state. No trial is postselected away from the invoice.\n\nThen $P(N>j)=(1-p)^j$ and, by summing nonnegative terms,\n\n$$\\mathbb EH_{N-1}=\\sum_{j\\ge1}\\frac{\\mathbb P(N>j)}j=\\sum_{j\\ge1}\\frac{(1-p)^j}j=-\\log p.$$ \n\nThe power series identity follows by integrating the geometric series from $0$ to $1-p$. Hence this is an exact all-real mean likelihood ledger. $\\mathbb{E}N=1/p$ and $\\mathbb{E}N^2=(2-p)/p^2$. Since $H_{N-1}\\le N$, its second moment is finite. At $p=1$ the ledger is identically zero. At $p=0$ the protocol never stops, and that point is excluded from the finite-a.s. source contract.\n\nA uniform $p\\ge p_{\\min}>0$ gives uniform bounds on invocation count, second moment and expected serial archive bit-time. Without it, finiteness is pointwise and the source budget is not uniform. Under a specified polynomial-in-$N$ total-source tariff, geometric tails give finite expected total tariff at each $p>0$, uniformly when $p\\ge p_{\\min}$. The preparation/control/maintenance tariff of the supplier must be included and tested against the polynomial bound.\n\nThe rational $H_{N-1}$ has a finite exact description. Its numerator/denominator bit lengths are at most polynomial in N (the common denominator N! suffices); finite arithmetic and retained intermediates therefore admit polynomial-in-N source bounds. The value is an encoded score, and $F_{\\mathrm{out}}$ below is an encoded signed response calibrated in derivative units. An actuator of strength $H_{N-1}$ or $F_{\\mathrm{out}}$ must own its generator, dynamic range, energy/current reserves and interactions, with their magnitude-dependent costs.\n\n**2. The value port carries no derivative.**\n\nThe instrument of section 1 determines $p(u)$ at the control value in use only: two families can agree in $p$ at one control and differ in derivative. A force response therefore requires a separately admitted derivative-source intervention. The following one is explicit and finite.\n\n**3. Finite-generator derivative service.**\n\nSuppose a source word contains a nominated control gate $U(u)=\\exp(-iuG)$, with fixed known finite Hermitian $G$ having precisely two eigenvalues $+r$ and $-r$, $r>0$. Preparation and all other gates/instruments in that invocation are independent of this occurrence's $u$. The source manifest explicitly admits repeating the same fresh preparation and shifting this gate control by $+s$ or $-s$, $s=\\pi/(4r)$, while retaining the corresponding source costs. This is physical intervention access to the gate control; the Bernoulli-state inputs of Theorem Q.0.3i need not admit these shifts.\n\nFor any retained event, its probability is $p(u)=A+B\\cos(2ru)+C\\sin(2ru)$: expand $U(u)$ by its two spectral projections and take the event expectation. Thus exactly\n\n$$p'(u)=r[p(u+s)-p(u-s)].$$ \n\nUse independent shifted invocations to obtain event bits $B_+,B_-$ and the bounded signed response $Y=r(B_+-B_-)$. Then $\\mathbb{E}Y=p'(u)$, $|Y|\\le r$. Irrational shift angles/generator constants require their admitted finite descriptions and calibrated physical service, exactly as the source gate does.\n\nIf a common control occurs in finitely many gates, differentiate the finite product: the derivative is the sum of the partial derivatives obtained by shifting one occurrence at a time while holding all other occurrences at $u$. This requires separately addressing those occurrences in the source interface. Performing all such paired calls supplies a signed $Y$ with $\\mathbb{E}Y=p'(u)$ and $|Y|\\le\\sum_a r_a$. The identity holds with arbitrary finite intermediate instruments after classical outcome branches are retained/summed, since composition remains linear in each nominated unitary channel. The two-eigenvalue hypothesis concerns the total generator on the full gate support. It cannot be checked only on an active block: a coherently controlled $\\exp(-iuG)$ generally has total-generator spectrum $\\{0,+r,-r\\}$, so the displayed two-shift formula is not justified for that gate. Such a coherent-control service is excluded here unless its full response independently has the stated single-frequency form; a general finite-spectrum shift rule requires its own derivation and additional admitted interventions. Classical branch conditioning does not authorize coherent-control substitution. Parameter-dependent preparations, readout effects or hidden controls have additional derivative terms and cannot be omitted.\n\n**4. Logarithmic force.**\n\nIndependently of the shifted calls, run the unshifted geometric stopping experiment from Section 1 and form\n\n$$F_{\\mathrm{out}}=-YN.$$ \n\nBy independence, $\\mathbb{E}F_{\\mathrm{out}}=-p'(u)/p(u)=d[-\\log p(u)]/du$. If $|Y|\\le C$, then $\\mathbb{E}F_{\\mathrm{out}}^2\\le C^2(2-p)/p^2$. Hence the exact logarithmic force has finite variance and pointwise finite expected source cost under the stated supplier tariff; $p_{\\min}$ makes the bounds uniform. No finite trial determines the exact real derivative, but the complete physical response has the exact required mean.\n\nFor several control coordinates repeat the separately registered derivative service for each. An unknown real tangent v needs its own input representation and independent response source. If bounded tangent coordinates are supplied as independent Bernoulli qubits with mean scores $v_{j}$, the product construction gives an exact mean directional derivative, with the input-bank promise and source charges retained.\n\nFor an additive finite likelihood ledger $\\sum_i[-\\log p_i(u)]$, independent invocations and summation give exact mean value and derivative. Multiplying the readout scores by the independently calibrated $\\kappa_{A}$ gives action units for the mean scores.\n\n**5. Reference and cost boundary.**\n\nEach invocation is its normalized finite instrument tensored with identity on an external spectator. Composing all STOP histories gives a trace-preserving countable instrument on the admitted product-preparation family because N is finite a.s. All failures, input preparations, clocks, shifts, supplier stocks and growing records remain owned. The on-demand repetition contract is stronger than receiving one unknown state; no cloning operation is used. If the source is correlated between trials, its waiting law need not be geometric and the identities above need not hold. Errors in such a source require explicit waiting-time/moment control: a small per-call trace distance bounds the error of the unbounded score uniformly only together with a success floor or another tail certificate.\n\nThe construction gives exact mean value and derivative responses for stochastic-rate ledgers $-\\log p(u)$ whose parameter derivatives are accessible through the shift interventions of section 3. Structural and thermodynamic ledgers of Definition Q.0.1 are outside this class.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q03l-canonical-field-observable-source-for-the-all-dimensional-quasilinear-ledger\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.3l (Canonical Field-Observable Source for the All-Dimensional Quasilinear Ledger).** The complete finite-source family below supplies qualified-PU action and tangent-response instruments for the all-dimensional quasilinear ledger of Theorem Q.0.3e, including its degenerate stationary and recovery cases. It joins the same raw marked likelihood ledger to an independently calibrated positive action coupling, exact finite kick instruments, and a controlled canonical-field limit with normal inputs. Finite devices, ideal identities and supplied approximation/tail errors remain distinct. Together with Theorem Q.0.3e and Corollary Q.0.3e.1 it is the calibrated realization of that higher-dimensional, non-semilinear family and of its degenerate stationary field.\n\n*Proof and complete source record.*\n\n**Ledger and source family.**\n\nThe construction uses the family of Theorem Q.0.3e and the coefficient sequences of Corollary Q.0.3e.1. It supplies their field-observable action and first-variation response as coherent instruments on the primitive-service branch of Definition C.6c.4a.\n\nFix spatial dimension $d$, FE level $n$, $N=2^{nd}$, and rational $a\\in[0,A]$, where $A\\ge 0$ is one supplied rational bound for the entire coefficient sequence. Let $\\phi_{j}$ be the periodic nodal basis of Theorem Q.0.3e. Define the scalar polynomial\n\n$$\nE(q)=\\int\\bigl[|\\sum q_{j}\\nabla\\phi_j|^4+|\\sum q_{j}\\nabla\\phi_j|^2+(\\sum q_j\\phi_j)^4+(\\sum q_j\\phi_j)^3+a\\sum q_j\\phi_j\\bigr]\\,dx.\n$$\n\nEvery coefficient is an explicitly computable rational from the simplex formula (Q.0.3h.2). Put $C_A=7+A+A^2$ and $L(q)=\\ln(2)[C_A+E(q)]$. The elementary inequalities $|t|^3\\le t^4/4+27/4$ and $A|t|\\le t^4/4+3A^{4/3}/4$, together with $A^{4/3}\\le A+A^2$, prove $E(q)\\ge -27/4-3(A+A^2)/4$. Thus $C_A+E(q)>0$ uniformly over this whole source family. $C_{A}$ is fixed before the level or input; its physical phase/action is retained. The source owns this constant offset; the offset-one source of Construction Q.0.3h is a separate source. The partial derivatives of $L$ are exactly the nodal residuals $B_j(q)$ of (Q.0.3h.3), multiplied by $\\ln(2)$, and the mixed and all higher derivatives are derivatives of this same polynomial.\n\n**Ideal canonical input and action.**\n\nThe ideal field-coordinate Hilbert carrier is $K=L^2(\\mathbb{R}^N,dq)$. $Q_j$ is multiplication by $q_{j}$, and $P_j=-i\\hbar\\,\\partial_j$ on its standard self-adjoint domain. The field-input domain is normal states, including states entangled with any external reference. No delta-position eigenvector is a supplied input. Schwartz vectors form the specified common core for all polynomial multiplication and momentum expressions below.\n\n$L(Q)$ is the nonnegative self-adjoint maximal multiplication operator, with domain $\\{\\psi:L\\psi\\in L^2\\}$. It is defined by its spectral measure. Fix the source clock and physical $\\hbar$ independently of the polynomial coefficients. For this explicitly nominated source choose and independently calibrate the action unit $\\kappa_A=2\\pi\\hbar/\\ln(2)$, once for every level and field input. This value is fixed before the field, mesh, coefficient or tangent inputs. It is a calibration choice of this source: by Convention Q.0.2x the action scale is independent input, and Construction Q.0.3h uses the scale $\\hbar$. It makes the finite phase $\\exp[-i\\kappa_A L(q_z)/\\hbar]=\\exp[-2\\pi i(C_A+E(q_z))]$ a root of unity, while the real, unwrapped splitting integral $\\kappa_A L(q_z)$ remains a separately retained physical response. Different rational ledger values may yield the same root-of-unity phase; the installed generator, its unwrapped energy splitting, raw comparison probabilities and retained coefficient record distinguish them. Action and ledger equality refer to these unwrapped data. The canonical identities below hold for every positive $\\kappa_{A}$; the exact algebraic spectral compiler uses this one registered value. Choose the pulse duration $\\tau>0$ independently as well. Let $g\\ge 0$ be a registered $C^1$ pulse, supported on $[0,\\tau]$, with $\\int g\\,dt=1$. $C^1$ regularity suffices for the exact commuting propagator and retained switching integral, and the raised-cosine profile used below is $C^1$. On a real two-state phase probe with projector $\\Pi=|0\\rangle\\langle 0|$ supply the physical coupling\n\n$$\nH(t)=\\kappa_A g(t) L(Q) \\otimes \\Pi.\n$$\n\nFor $g(t)>0$ its self-adjoint domain is $D(L)$ on the active probe summand and all $K$ on the inactive summand; at $g(t)=0$ the operator is zero on all $K\\otimes\\mathbb{C}^2$. The common Schwartz core suffices for every differentiated identity below. This ideal multiplication Hamiltonian is the limit of the finite zero-idle matrix sources below and contains no kinetic term. Its exact propagator is\n\n$$\nU=\\exp[-i \\kappa_A L(Q) \\otimes \\Pi / \\hbar].\n$$\n\nThis is a controlled action on the input field. Its two probe branches retain the real splitting integral $\\kappa_A L(Q)$, including the fixed offset, and a calibrated measurement of the generator fixes this lift of the phase.\n\nEvery derivative of exp[-i $\\kappa_{A}$ L(q)/hbar] is a polynomial times that unit-modulus function. Therefore U and U* preserve Schwartz space. On that common invariant core, direct differentiation proves\n\n$$\nU^{*} (P_j \\otimes I) U = P_j \\otimes I - \\kappa_A (\\partial_j L)(Q) \\otimes \\Pi.\n$$\n\nThis is a Heisenberg momentum-kick identity. For every real FE tangent $v=\\sum v_j\\phi_j$, the corresponding linear combination of physical momenta satisfies\n\n$$\nU^{*} P_v U - P_v = -\\kappa_A \\ln(2)\\, DE_a(q)[v],\n$$\n\nas a multiplication operator on the active probe branch. Thus the force observable is exactly the first variation of the finite-dimensional ledger whose continuum and stationary properties Theorem Q.0.3e proves. On a normal field wavepacket it is the operator-valued, distribution-sensitive response; its variance and field/probe correlations are retained.\n\nFurther commutators with $P_{k}$ give the mixed derivatives on Schwartz space. This supplies a common differentiable physical tangent response. The joint action channel of the source on field, probe and arbitrary external reference is $\\operatorname{Ad}(U)\\otimes\\mathrm{Id}$, without field copying or postselection.\n\n**Finite sources.**\n\nThe ideal source is the explicit limit of the following finite physical cabinets. At grade $m$ take a rational box $[-R_m,R_m)^N$ with $R_m=2^m$ and a uniform rational grid step $h_m=2^{-m}$, subdividing the box exactly. Let $D_m=(2R_m/h_m)^N$ and $b_m=N(2m+1)$, so $D_m=2^{b_m}$ exactly. Every $b_m$-bit word labels one cube and its center $q_{z}$. The finite source exactly supplies rational $a_{n}$ within its fixed rational bound $A$. The family includes $a_{n}=0$ and the rational degeneracy and recovery sequences of Corollary Q.0.3e.1; coefficients enter as rational inputs. If computable real coefficients are requested, use certified rational approximants: on a box with $|q_j|\\le R$, coefficient error $\\delta$ changes $E$ by at most $\\delta R$ and each directional derivative by $\\delta|\\int v|$, since only the linear $a\\int u$ term changes. These errors are retained separately, and a nonzero coefficient error leaves the exact zero pattern of the sequence undetermined. The source supplies the finite diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nL_m = \\sum_z L(q_z)|z\\rangle\\langle z|,\n$$\n\nwith its entire all-input coefficient table installed before the unknown input. Polynomial evaluation gives rational $E(q_z)$, so $L_{m}$ has rational-times-$\\ln 2$ eigenvalues, finite descriptors and a nonnegative bound. The physical Hamiltonian is $H_m(t)=\\kappa_A g(t)L_m\\otimes\\Pi$; it is supplied as a finite matrix coupling on the whole register, a primitive pulse of Definition C.6c.4a. Its maximum energy is $\\kappa_{A}\\lVert g\\rVert_\\infty\\max_z L(q_z)$, action norm is $\\kappa_A\\max_z L(q_z)$, and switching variation is $\\kappa_A\\max_z L(q_z)\\int|g'|\\,dt$. Each is finite and fully charged, and each grows without bound in $m$ and in $n$.\n\nUse $b_{m}$ payload factors $w$ of $c_m=\\max(2,b_m)$ otherwise unchanged 75-bit cells of Theorem C.6c.P17a. Their original core gates never touch $w$. Accept the unknown finite field register by paid basis SWAPs into these payloads at a declared ready cut, then transfer it by a second paid basis-SWAP bank to a separate $b_m$-bit field appliance. Each SWAP is three explicit basis CNOTs. Only these admitted basis gates touch $w$; all general finite phase, reflection, spectral-readout and preparation matrices below act on the separate appliance. The field is transported and never copied to classical records. The source supplies one complete qualification prefix on the original zero-ready cells, using the original fresh fair-source circuit, exact ROM gates, feedback and forecast instruments. Additional requested copies of that task use fresh full ready cores, their complete 75-bit stock, ROM writes and source/record calls with a new invoice; evolved cores are not reused. The coherent action port accepts its input after the complete prefix on every source history. The source-message bit $e$ gates the raw comparison instrument only, and readiness involves no test or postselection of the field. These are completed local field macros on the primitive-service branch of Definition C.6c.4a; the heat-endpoint access manifest and its passive-payload convention are unchanged. The qualification instruments retain gain $1/16$ and null gain zero because they act as the identity on every $w$ factor, also for unknown $w$ states entangled with an external reference. The paid transfer macros act on $w$, and the ready-state complexity certificate of Theorem C.6c.P17a refers to the initial ready state.\n\nInstall the physical diagonal action coupling on the separate field appliance and phase probe. The transported field is the same input state, including its external-reference correlations; $w$ now holds the former ready zero state of the appliance. For a classical basis-field calibration run, the comparator circuit of Construction Q.0.3h has reverse probability $2^{-[C_A+E(q_z)]}$ and forward probability one, on the same enabled event. Its unconditioned log-likelihood ratio is $L(q_z)$, with the qualification weights cancelling as in (Q.0.3h.4). This is a separate declared raw calibration run. In coherent field-input action mode, the comparator is not applied to that input: otherwise its records would dephase the field. Mode choice, dummy factors and both complete histories are retained. The shared coefficient table and same physical splitting link the raw scalar ledger and the coherent action; the two modes run on separately supplied inputs.\n\nFor momentum readout the cabinet supplies the cyclic shift $S_{j}$ of its field-grid basis and the Hermitian central-difference momentum matrix\n\n$$\nP_{j,m} = \\hbar(S_j-S_j^{*})/(2 i h_m).\n$$\n\nChoose the orientation $S_j\\psi(q)=\\psi(q+h_m e_j)$. The source may measure this matrix by its discrete Fourier instrument. For a tangent-response measurement, supply instead the finite Hermitian matrix $K_{v,m}=U_m^*P_{v,m}U_m-P_{v,m}$, where $P_{v,m}=\\sum_j v_jP_{j,m}$ for the declared real tangent $v$. To measure this input-picture kick after the action pulse, the terminal observable is $K_{\\mathrm{out}}=U_mK_{v,m}U_m^*$, with projectors $U_mE_\\lambda U_m^*$ whenever $E_{\\lambda}$ resolves $K_{v,m}$. Pullback through the action gives exactly the $E_{\\lambda}$ law on the original input. The terminal basis unitary is $V_KU_m^*$, where $V_{K}$ diagonalizes $K_{v,m}$; its full matrix and its unitarity certificate are supplied and charged as one complete local service. Its spectral resolution is a supplied full finite local projective instrument: its matrix entries are explicitly determined by the already installed phase and shift matrices, and the retained spectral certificate states orthogonal projectors summing to $I$ and $K_{v,m}=\\sum_\\lambda\\lambda E_\\lambda$. For rational tangents $v$, the registered calibration makes every entry of $K_{v,m}/\\hbar$ algebraic: $L(q_z)/\\ln 2$ is rational, $U_{m}$ has root-of-unity entries and $P_{j,m}/\\hbar$ has rational imaginary shift entries. Exact algebraic root isolation, nullspace solving and Gram–Schmidt within each eigenspace therefore give a terminating finite spectral certificate, with repeated eigenvalues grouped and no division by a presumed nonzero eigenvalue gap. Every algebraic number is retained by its finite defining polynomial and isolating data. All individual coordinate tangent instruments $v=e_j$ are included. An arbitrary real finite-dimensional tangent is their mathematical linear combination; a requested effective nonrational tangent is implemented with certified rational approximants and retained error bounded by $\\sum_j|v_j-\\hat{v}_j|\\,\\lVert K_{j,m}\\rVert$ at finite grade, or by the supplied polynomial moment bound in the ideal limit. The diagonalizing finite local unitary and complete basis records are charged on the whole field-appliance/probe support. These exact algebraic source matrices use the ideal primitive-service boundary of Definition C.6c.4a, whose principal-logarithm construction supplies their pulse generators. A numerical implementation retains its operator/instrument error in addition to the grid error. A sequence of projective momentum measurements before and after the pulse on one unknown state disturbs the input and measures a different observable; the source can estimate separate momentum expectations on independently supplied copies, with that protocol and its costs recorded. The diagonal residual matrix is a further readable observable, distinct from $K_{v,m}$. The finite matrices satisfy no exact canonical commutation relation.\n\n**Explicit finite cabinet and resource compiler.**\n\nOne fixed-stock implementation is specified completely as follows. Set $b=b_m$, $c=\\max(2,b)$, $D=2^b$. At one local port allocate: $c$ complete 75-bit cells; an external $b$-bit field input bank and its one-bit phase probe; a separate $b$-bit field appliance; a $b$-bit dummy field and one dummy probe; the eight qualification source/work bits A1,B1,A2,B2,F1,F2,X2,e; four comparator bits B,C,D,E; four immutable records for the fair-source measurements; one comparator record; and $b+1$ terminal field/probe records. All cell $w$ bits used as the field register are already in the $75c$ count. Thus the data/input/readout allocation before programme and controller stock is\n\n$$\nB_{\\mathrm{data}} = 75c + 4b + 20.\n$$\n\nInputs are not included among fresh pure preparations. All other data bits start at the specified basis zero, except declared preloaded programme descriptors. The dummy field is a separate physical register. A raw-comparison run applies its table-controlled reflection to the field and the phase pulse to the dummy. A coherent-action run applies the reflection to the dummy and its phase pulse to the field. Mode is a fixed supplied classical programme bit, set before input; controlled-routing blocks are explicitly completed on both settings and their full support is charged. Neither mode reuses the other mode's state or records.\n\nSerialize the following finite list of logical service calls, each of duration $\\tau_*$: one commissioning preparation of all pure noninput stock; $16c$ cell-ROM X writes; 4 Hadamards on fair-source bits; 2 Toffoli source ANDs; 4 CNOT source XORs (two to form X2, two to form e); $6c$ core CNOTs and $6c$ cursor X updates in the exact forecast-before-outcome order of Theorem C.6c.P17a; $6b$ basis CNOTs implementing the input-to-$w$ and $w$-to-appliance SWAP banks; one full table-controlled comparator reflection and 3 comparator copies; one positive action pulse; one complete finite basis unitary $V_KU_m^*$ for the requested $K_{v,m}$ on field plus phase probe, and its inverse $U_mV_K^*$ after the complete terminal basis records; alternatively the registered diagnostic mode uses a field Fourier basis and phase-probe basis before its records. In raw-comparison mode both reserved terminal basis calls are identities, so the $b$ field records are exactly the coordinate-grid labels $z$ and their active comparator probabilities are $\\mu_m(z)$, $p_z\\mu_m(z)$, and $(1-p_z)\\mu_m(z)$; the marked $e=1$ weights include the common $1/4$ and the separate $e=0$ weight $3/4$ as stated below, and the Radon–Nikodym law of this mode is read in the coordinate basis. The mode is fixed before input, with identical reserved full support and slot counts. The reflection uses the delivered-message XOR $e$ in raw mode; the coherent phase pulse does not. Four fair-source record services occur only at their designated consumption times; one comparator record and $b+1$ terminal field/probe records complete the word. The force mode records the complete spectral outcome and its full conditional state using the transported $K_{\\mathrm{out}}$ spectral certificate just defined. Its input-picture response is exactly $K_{v,m}$. Its $b+1$ basis records distinguish every output eigenvector even within a degenerate eigenspace. After those records, the charged inverse-basis call $U_mV_K^*$ returns the field/probe to its original physical basis. Thus its conditional poststate is the fine-grained projector state on that basis. Coarse-graining produces the spectral value law, and the retained eigenvector records refine degeneracies: on a degenerate eigenspace the instrument is the fine-grained projective measurement in the retained eigenbasis and differs from the Lüders instrument of the coarse spectral projector. The diagnostic field Fourier matrix on one grid coordinate diagonalizes its shift and hence $P_{j,m}$; the phase basis is a supplied two-by-two matrix.\n\nConsequently the logical unitary/preparation count and record-service count are exactly\n\n$$\nu = 28c + 6b + 18,\\quad j = b + 6,\\quad L_{\\mathrm{calls}} = u+j.\n$$\n\nThe 16c ROM writes, 6c core gates and 6c cursor updates give 28c; the remaining 18 are commissioning 1, fair Hadamards 4, source Toffolis 2, source CNOTs 4, comparator reflection and copies 4, action 1, and terminal bases 2. Each operation has a full matrix/instrument descriptor and its owned support. Initial/source wires and measured records persist, and no register is reset or reused.\n\nTo make programme progression explicit, allocate $r=\\lceil\\log_2(L_{\\mathrm{calls}}+1)\\rceil$ controller bits, initialized zero, and $r$ fresh trace bits per logical call. After each logical call append a finite cyclic increment of the controller (on all $2^r$ states), followed by a finite XOR-into-fresh copy of its $r$-bit value to the next trace bank. These are two charged finite unitary calls. Their source clock reserves the same duration $\\tau_*$. Programme ROM contains an explicit prefix-coded serialization of this complete matrix/record word, the fixed rational FE coefficients, algebraic comparator entries, pulse parameters, input-domain bounds and its support table. Let $R$ be the exact bit length of that serialization, computed by concatenating the stated finite encodings before input. This logical serialization refers to named data supports and logical calls only, so its length is defined without self-reference. After its length is computed, the separate finite installation descriptor assigns the ROM/controller/trace addresses and the compiler supplies their physical supports. The source uses the preloaded pure-ROM service boundary of Construction Q.0.3h. Thus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB &= B_{\\mathrm{data}} + R + r(1+L_{\\mathrm{calls}}), \\\\\nG &= u+2L_{\\mathrm{calls}},\\quad J=j,\\quad K=3L_{\\mathrm{calls}},\\quad I=BK\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhen all stock, including the external input reserve, is charged for the full $K$-slot horizon. This conservative full-horizon retention convention is the nominated allocation, so $I=BK$ holds with equality. The external field waits in its input bank until the SWAP cut; its original supplied state and any outside reference remain owned by their input supplier. All ancillary stock commissioning is one admitted finite pure-register service with its declared full support and preparation pulse. A different manufacturing boundary adds its own costs.\n\nFor each nonphase finite unitary choose a Hermitian logarithm $A$ with spectrum in $[0,2\\pi]$, supplying its full spectral/matrix certificate; $H_A(t)=\\hbar g_*(t)A$, with $g_*=(1-\\cos(2\\pi t/\\tau_*))/\\tau_*$, is positive and has peak at most $4\\pi\\hbar/\\tau_*$, action at most $2\\pi\\hbar$ and switching variation at most $8\\pi\\hbar/\\tau_*$. The phase call uses $H_{\\mathrm{phase}}=\\kappa_A g_*L_{m}\\otimes\\Pi$, with peak $2\\kappa_A\\ell_{\\max}/\\tau_*$, action $\\kappa_A\\ell_{\\max}$ and variation $4\\kappa_A\\ell_{\\max}/\\tau_*$, where $\\ell_{\\max}=\\max_z L(q_z)$. Record and pure-stock preparation services carry their explicitly supplied finite primitive profiles and peak/action records in the same word; if their peaks exceed these bounds, use their recorded maximum as well. Zero baseline Hamiltonians apply outside these registered pulses. The profile integrals determine the total action and switching records.\n\nThere are no interport lanes or links. With $\\Omega$ the maximum of every installed service's full-support peak and $A_{\\mathrm{word}}$ the sum of its norm-action integrals, the full nominated tariff is exactly\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV/u_{*} &= \\alpha_B B + \\alpha_I I + \\alpha_G G + \\alpha_J J \\\\\n&\\quad+ \\alpha_K K + \\alpha_\\Omega (\\tau_{*}/\\hbar)\\, \\Omega.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll $\\alpha$ values are independently fixed positive source prices. $A_{\\mathrm{word}}$ and the sum of switching variations are separate retained responses. This exhausts acquisition/representation via full stock and ROM, processing via calls, maintenance via I, controller/update via explicit counter and trace calls, and readout/custody via record services and retained factors. Ready pure-register energy, clock preparation and any external work/holding buffers are separately described service stock: their carrier, occupied intervals and preparation/control calls are concatenated into this same compiler before B,G,J,K,I and $\\Omega$ are evaluated. The displayed formula describes the nominated externally driven primitive boundary; stock of a microscopic manufacturer or an external buffer inside the declared boundary extends these counts by its serialized calls and bit-time. The source-resource total, the selected raw ledger and heat are distinct quantities.\n\n**Normal-state raw-ledger owner.**\n\nNormal field states have an ideal raw instrument. Put $p(Q)=\\exp[-L(Q)]$, a bounded multiplication effect with $0<p\\le 1$, and supply the bounded controlled reflection $R_p$ on the comparator bit, with entries $\\sqrt{p}$, $\\sqrt{1-p}$ as in Construction Q.0.3h. Its finite cabinets are the table-controlled reflections of that construction. In a forward comparison run the success flag is certain; in a reverse run its success effect is $p(Q)$, and failure effect is $I-p(Q)$. If the incoming normal field state has position law $\\mu$, joint measurement of the field position spectral measure and the success flag gives forward measure $\\mu(dq)$, reverse success measure $p(q)\\mu(dq)$, and retained reverse failure measure $(1-p(q))\\mu(dq)$. Since $p>0$, the Radon–Nikodym log ratio is exactly $L(q)$, $\\mu$-almost everywhere. The preceding display describes the active comparator law. In the qualified source the marked event includes $e=1$, whose input-independent probability is $1/4$. Its complete marked measures are $P_F(dq,e=1,\\text{success})=\\mu(dq)/4$, $P_R(dq,e=1,\\text{success})=p(q)\\mu(dq)/4$ and $P_R(dq,e=1,\\text{failure})=(1-p(q))\\mu(dq)/4$. The disabled $e=0$ branch has measure $3\\mu(dq)/4$ and remains separately recorded. Thus the common factor cancels on the marked success event exactly as in (Q.0.3h.4). The log ratio $L$ belongs to the marked success event pointwise in $q$; the unmarked success probability includes disabled outcomes, and the integrated ratio $-\\log\\int p\\,d\\mu$ is a different quantity.\n\nPosition spectral measures here are ideal limiting response observables. Each finite cabinet has the corresponding active laws $\\mu_m(z)$, $p(q_z)\\mu_m(z)$, $[1-p(q_z)]\\mu_m(z)$. Its marked weights are $P_F(z,e=1,\\text{success})=\\mu_m(z)/4$, $P_R(z,e=1,\\text{success})=p(q_z)\\mu_m(z)/4$ and $P_R(z,e=1,\\text{failure})=[1-p(q_z)]\\mu_m(z)/4$; the disabled weight $3\\mu_m(z)/4$ is retained. The marked successful log ratio is exactly $L(q_z)$ wherever $\\mu_m(z)>0$. Binned ideal continuous outputs have the log ratio $-\\log\\mathbb{E}_\\mu[p(Q)\\mid\\text{bin}]$; on a bin where $|L-L(q_z)|\\le\\varepsilon$ this differs from $L(q_z)$ by at most $\\varepsilon$, directly from $\\exp(-\\varepsilon)\\le p(q)/p(q_z)\\le\\exp(\\varepsilon)$. Input and spatial tails are retained separately. A normal external-reference correlation does not alter these marginal probabilities. The bounded comparator instrument itself has normal poststates from the Kraus operators $\\sqrt{p(Q)}$ and $\\sqrt{1-p(Q)}$. Finite-bin and finite-cabinet records retain their complete postinstrument states. Coherent action and raw-ledger comparison are separate modes run on separately supplied inputs. The same explicit function $L$ controls the reflection and the positive phase coupling, so their action-ledger link is physical and testable.\n\n**Declared input preparation and access.**\n\nUnknown-input mode accepts one finite field register from the user of the cabinet, together with a registered state-domain/tail certificate. A source-prepared calibration mode supplies a finite rational-complex normalized amplitude vector (with its algebraic normalization), a complete unitary taking a fresh standard basis state to that vector, and its positive finite-matrix pulse under Definition C.6c.4a. Finite linear algebra completes the first column to a unitary. Its Hermitian logarithm, full matrix stock, precision, peak and duration are charged. Smooth compact-support continuum test vectors can be approximated by these vectors with supplied integration/quadrature bounds.\n\nFor physical continuum convergence use the explicit input domain consisting of a normal state together with a decomposition/approximation certificate by compact smooth vectors, bounds on the requisite derivatives and weighted moments, and certified remainder tails. Pure states may supply a $C_c^3$ vector with bounding box and explicit derivative bounds; finite mixtures and purified finite sums follow by linearity, with all preparation/reference costs. Schwartz vectors with effective norm, momentum and polynomial-weighted tails are another admitted class. For these inputs choose the truncation radius and grid grade before input arrival using their certificates; report the finite error. These operational domains are nonempty. The strong extension to all normal states is an analytic limit without a uniform rate.\n\n**Strong canonical limit and physical force convergence.**\n\nEmbed the valid finite field space isometrically into $K$ by sending $|z\\rangle$ to $h_m^{-N/2}$ times the indicator of its cube. Let $J_{m}$ be this embedding, $P_m=J_mJ_m^*$, the cube-average projection extended by zero outside the box. As $R_{m}$ tends to infinity and $h_{m}$ tends to zero, $P_{m}$ tends strongly to $I$: prove it first for continuous compactly supported functions by uniform continuity and then use density and $\\lVert P_m\\rVert\\le 1$.\n\nLet $L_m(q)$ denote the piecewise-constant center value. For compactly supported smooth $\\psi$, eventually its support lies strictly inside the box, and the mean-value theorem gives $\\sup_{\\mathrm{support}}|L_m-L|\\le\\sqrt{N}h_m\\sup|\\nabla L|/2$ on an enlarged compact set. Hence the embedded phase matrices converge strongly to $U$ on that dense class, and thus on all $L^2$ by their norm-one bound. The same proof works jointly with the probe and every external reference, first on finite sums of simple tensors and then by density. $J_{m}$ and $J_m^*$ are analytic comparison embeddings. At finite $m$ the embedded map $T_m=J_mU_mJ_m^*$ is a contraction; $T_m\\rho T_m^*$ has trace $\\operatorname{Tr}(P_m\\rho)$, and the missing weight $\\operatorname{Tr}((I-P_m)\\rho)$ remains an explicit cutoff deficit. For every trace-class normal state this positive embedded output converges in trace norm to $U\\rho U^*$; prove it first for finite-rank states by strong convergence, then use contraction bounds and trace-norm approximation. The deficit tends to zero and is retained at every finite $m$. The devices accept supplied normalized finite states with their approximation certificates. The convergence is strong, state by state, and is not uniform over inputs of unbounded energy.\n\nFor $\\psi$ smooth compactly supported in the interior, central differences converge in $L^2$ to $\\partial_j\\psi$, with error bounded by $h_m^2\\lVert\\partial_j^3\\psi\\rVert_2/6$ for the continuous translation expression. Cube averaging commutes with translations by integer grid steps away from the boundary. Therefore\n\n$$\nJ_m P_{j,m} J_m^{*} \\psi \\to P_j \\psi,\n$$\n\nand, using the direct conjugated-shift expression below,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ_m [U_m^{*} P_{j,m} U_m-P_{j,m}] J_m^{*} \\psi \\\\\n&\\to -\\kappa_A (\\partial_j L)(Q) \\otimes \\Pi \\psi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStrong convergence of the unitaries leaves this limit of unbounded observables open; a quantitative direct proof follows. Write $f_{z}$ for the cube average of $\\psi$, and $\\Delta_\\pm=L(q_z\\pm he_j)-L(q_z)$. On a fixed enlarged compact let $M_1$ bound $|\\partial_j L|$ and $M_2$ bound the Euclidean norm of $\\nabla(\\partial_j L)$. Then $|\\Delta_\\pm|\\le hM_1$ and $|\\exp(ix)-1-ix|\\le x^2/2$. The exact conjugated-shift expression has leading term $-\\kappa_A[(D_+L)f_{z+}+(D_-L)f_{z-}]/2$, with $D_+L=[L(q_z+h)-L(q_z)]/h$ and $D_-L=[L(q_z)-L(q_z-h)]/h$. The exponential remainder has $L^2$ norm at most $\\kappa_{A}^{2}hM_1^2\\lVert\\psi\\rVert_2/(2\\hbar)$. Taylor's theorem bounds the difference of either $D_\\pm L$ from $\\partial_j L(q)$ anywhere in its center cube by $h(1+\\sqrt{N})M_2/2$. Translations obey $\\lVert\\psi(\\cdot\\pm he_j)-\\psi\\rVert_2\\le h\\lVert\\partial_j\\psi\\rVert_2$, and cube averaging obeys $\\lVert P_m\\psi-\\psi\\rVert_2\\le\\sqrt{N}h\\lVert\\nabla\\psi\\rVert_2$ by the cube Poincaré estimate. Consequently a conservative explicit total error bound is\n\n$$\nh\\{[\\kappa_A^2M_1^2/(2\\hbar)+\\kappa_A(1+\\sqrt{N})M_2]\\lVert\\psi\\rVert_2+\\kappa_{A}M_1(1+\\sqrt{N})\\lVert\\nabla\\psi\\rVert_2\\}.\n$$\n\nProjection contractions and the triangle inequality prove this bound componentwise on each probe branch. All constants are finite polynomial derivative bounds on the supplied compact box. Boundary wraparound terms vanish once the support and its two-cell enlargement lie inside the source box. The extension to supplied Schwartz inputs also needs a uniform tail bound; the estimate $\\lVert K_{j,m}\\rVert=O(1/h)$ alone would not suffice. Let $C_{L}$ be the sum of the absolute coefficients of $\\partial_j L$, increased if necessary so $|\\partial_j L(q)|\\le C_L(1+|q|^3)$ globally. On an interior neighbor edge, $|\\exp(ix)-1|\\le|x|$ bounds the kick coefficient by $\\kappa_A C_L(1+\\sup_{\\mathrm{segment}}|q|^3)/2$. On a wraparound edge the trivial bound gives $\\hbar/h=\\hbar R_m$, but both endpoint cubes have a coordinate of modulus at least $R_m-h$ and hence their weight $1+|q|^3$ dominates $R_{m}$ uniformly. Neighbor weights, including wraparound neighbors, are comparable by a constant depending only on $N$; cube averaging is a contraction and Jensen's inequality bounds its weighted norm by the corresponding continuum weighted norm with a fixed dimension factor. These explicit estimates give, for example, the conservative uniform bound\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lVert J_m K_{j,m} J_m^{*} \\psi\\rVert_2 \\\\\n&\\le C_{\\mathrm{tail}} \\lVert(1+|Q|^{3})\\psi\\rVert_2, \\\\\nC_{\\mathrm{tail}}&=128(1+\\sqrt{N})^{6}(\\kappa_A C_L+\\hbar),\\quad m\\ge 1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe same weighted bound holds for the limiting multiplication kick, with a smaller constant. To verify the displayed choice, use $|q_z-q|\\le\\sqrt{N}/2$, $(1+x+y)^3\\le 4[(1+x)^3+y^3]$, the two neighboring shift contractions, and $R_m\\ge 2$; each conversion costs less than the displayed dimension factor. A tangent combination multiplies the bound by $\\sum_j|v_j|$ and uses the corresponding maximum $C_{L}$. Thus split $\\psi=\\chi_R\\psi+(1-\\chi_R)\\psi$ with a fixed smooth compact cutoff: the two tail contributions are at most the uniform constant times the supplied weighted tail, independently of $m$. The compact part has the direct $O(h)$ estimate. Choose $R$ first and $m$ second. This proves the Schwartz kick extension without a tail times $1/h$ error. The response certificate is the sum of these weighted tails and the finite compact derivative estimate, and it depends on the supplied weighted norm of the input. The momentum convergence above is needed on compact smooth test vectors only, where it was proved directly.\n\nThe ideal kick identity is exact; a finite cabinet has its exact finite momentum matrix and a nonzero controlled error relative to that ideal. Finite-shot readout errors are separately bounded and charged.\n\n**Degenerate solutions and continuum spatial limit.**\n\nUse any supplied rational coefficient sequence of Corollary Q.0.3e.1 with the fixed bound $A$, retaining the exact zero/nonzero pattern as source data. At every spatial level, the polynomial generating the physical action/kick is the exact $E_{a_n}$ whose complete real stationary set Theorem Q.0.3e classifies. The physical kick vanishes as a multiplication response precisely at the polynomial zero set, which normal states approach by concentration. For an explicit concentration family choose one fixed normalized $C_c^\\infty$ bump $\\eta$ supported in $[-1,1]^N$ with supplied derivative/integration bounds, and $\\psi_{\\hat{q},\\delta}(q)=\\delta^{-N/2}\\eta((q-\\hat{q})/\\delta)$, where $\\hat{q}$ is a supplied rational center and $\\delta>0$ rational. This is a normal state. On its support $|q-\\hat{q}|\\le\\delta\\sqrt{N}$. If $M_2$ bounds $\\lVert\\nabla(\\partial_j L)\\rVert$ there, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\langle\\partial_j L(Q)\\rangle-\\partial_j L(\\hat{q})|&\\le\\delta \\sqrt{N}\\, M_2, \\\\\n\\langle|\\partial_j L(Q)-\\partial_j L(\\hat{q})|^{2}\\rangle&\\le\\delta^{2} N M_2^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second inequality bounds the variance as well. For a tangent combination use the analogous gradient bound for $\\sum_j v_j\\partial_j L$. Rational $\\hat{q}$ approaching a chosen real FE field adds the corresponding center-approximation error by the same mean-value bound. If that field is stationary, its exact derivative vanishes, so both force bias and variance tend to zero under these explicit bounds. Finite cabinets approximate these bump states by supplied normalized rational-complex vectors with controlled quadrature/norm and weighted errors. The state-preparation unitary and its full descriptor/pulse are additional registered service calls. The stationary solutions and their full-tail and subsequence recovery criteria are those of Theorem Q.0.3e and Corollary Q.0.3e.1, including loss of the degenerate zero field when $a_{n}>0$.\n\nThe scalar source ledgers have the following spatial recovery schedule. At spatial mesh size $h_{\\mathrm{FE}}=2^{-n}$, take $m(n)\\ge 2n$, so the source coordinate grid step is at most $h_{\\mathrm{FE}}^{2}$ and its amplitude box tends to infinity. Rounding each smooth interpolant nodal coefficient to a cube center changes the field by at most $h_m/2$ and its gradient by at most $C_dh_m/h_{\\mathrm{FE}}$; both vanish. Smooth approximation and diagonal recovery therefore give the same Mosco limit $\\ln(2)[C_A+E_a]$ for the finite-grid ledger functions, with the coercive lower bound inherited from $E_{a}$. Every finite grid attains its minimum; its minimizers converge to the unique continuum minimizer by equicoercivity and recovery. This is the rational-rounding argument of Construction Q.0.3h, with the present fixed offset and grid boxes. The grid need not contain an exact stationary coordinate; the stationary classification is that of the real space $V_{n}$.\n\nThe effective source family includes $a_n=1/(n+1)$, which approaches zero but never recovers the degenerate stationary zero field; a sequence that is identically zero beyond a supplied cutoff, which recovers it on the full tail; and $a_{n}=0$ for even $n$ and $1/(n+1)$ for odd $n$, which recovers it on a subsequence but not the full tail. These are explicit rational descriptors within $A=1$; by Corollary Q.0.3e.1 no total algorithm decides the eventual-zero property of uniformly supplied sequences. All source wavepacket localization and finite-grid force errors remain separately controlled.\n\nSpatial FE convergence and source-digitization convergence are separate limits. Given a particular bounded spatial field/tangent sequence and a requested error $\\epsilon_{n}$, choose the physical grid grade $m(n)$ using the above finite polynomial/tail bounds so its force/action error is $\\le\\epsilon_n$. Strong $W^{1,4}$ first-variation convergence in Theorem Q.0.3e then gives the same limiting continuum variation. The grade $m(n)$ depends on the input and tail domain, and the two limits are taken in this order.\n\n**Summary.**\n\nThe construction gives a calibrated PU field-observable realization of an all-dimensional, non-semilinear family with an exactly classified degenerate stationary field and explicit recovery criteria. It adds a coherent field action and a momentum-response interface to the finite-label source of Construction Q.0.3h. The compact stationary spectra of Theorem Q.0.3f and the functionals of Theorem Q.0.3g have no source realization here. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q01r-full-state-dependent-singular-entrance-and-accessibility-coercivity-classification\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.1r (Full State-Dependent Singular-Entrance and Accessibility-Coercivity Classification).**\n\n#### 1. Exact claim and comparison with the previous work\n\nThis is a new, proved source class. It is not a claim that every degenerate generator has an integrated local-conjugate rate. It extends the previous everywhere-available rare-edge/DAG construction in two directions that matter: hazards can vanish on arbitrary closed state-dependent sets, and positive-cost directed cycles are allowed. Zero-toll edges are allowed, subject to the exact no-zero-toll-cycle condition below. Boundary accessibility is computed from the support of the actual joint history density, not from the graph alone or the limiting generator. No diffusion or uniformly positive jump theorem is reproved.\n\nThe theorem has no unpopulated LDP or accessibility certificate among its premises. Its effective rational piecewise-affine subclass has a finite exact accessibility and constrained-recovery algorithm at every action bound. The source construction in Theorem Q.0.1s gives the same law to superexponential precision on an actual finite ND–RID carrier.\n\n#### 2. Source class\n\nFix a finite directed graph $G=(V,E)$, a starting vertex $i_0$, a horizon $T>0$ and $x_0\\in\\mathbb{R}^p$. Multiple labelled edges are permitted. In vertex $i$ the continuous coordinate solves $x'=b_i(t,x)$, where the finitely many drifts are uniformly bounded by $B$ and jointly Lipschitz on the compact finite-horizon reachable set $K$. They may be extended with the same bounds to a surrounding compact set. An edge changes the vertex and leaves $x$ continuous. For each edge $e:i\\to j$ fix:\n\n* a constant dimensionless toll $a_{e}\\ge 0$;\n* a bounded nonnegative Lipschitz function $k_e(t,x)$, with $0\\le k_e\\le K_e$.\n\nThe rate at refinement $n$ is $r_e^n(t,x)=k_e(t,x)\\exp(-na_e)$. Zero means exactly zero. Let $E_+=\\{e:a_e>0\\}$, $E_0=\\{e:a_e=0\\}$. Assume the graph $(V,E_0)$ is acyclic. If $E_+$ is empty, the entire graph is acyclic and all statements reduce to a finite set of words. Otherwise let $a_*=\\min_{E_+}a_e>0$. Set $d=|V|$. Let $\\Lambda$ be any certified uniform upper bound on the total outgoing rate before exponential suppression; $\\sum_e K_e$ is always sufficient. Likewise choose $\\Lambda_+$ bounding the total outgoing prefactor on $E_+$; $\\sum_{E_+}K_e$ is sufficient. Tighter maximum-outgoing-row bounds are permitted and are used in the witness.\n\nThe process exists nonexplosively by thinning a Poisson process of rate $\\Lambda$, integrating the current drift between proposals and selecting the actual outgoing edges with their rates. Thus this definition supplies a law, rather than presupposing one. The retained state set V times K is compact. The generator, on differentiable f, is\n\n$$\nG_n f(i,x,t)=\\partial_t f+b_i(t,x)\\cdot\\nabla_x f+\\sum_{e:i\\to j} k_e(t,x)e^{-na_e}[f(j,x,t)-f(i,x,t)].\n$$\n\nIt is a genuinely state-dependent jump generator with deterministic (fully degenerate) within-sector transport. For positive tolls its ordinary limiting generator loses entrance information.\n\n#### 3. The actual common carrier and accessible histories\n\nA word $w=(e_1,\\dots,e_m)$ is any directed word from $i_0$. Let $\\Delta_w=\\{0\\le t_1\\le\\dots\\le t_m\\le T\\}$. Include the empty word as a point. Given $t\\in\\Delta_w$, concatenate the drift flows with switches at $t_1,\\dots,t_m$; denote the resulting continuous path $x_w(t;\\cdot)$ and sector word by $i_0,\\dots,i_m$. Zero-duration intermediate sectors are kept in the word. Continuous dependence of ODE solutions and Gronwall give continuity of $(t,s)\\mapsto x_w(t;s)$, with the evident one-sided interpretation at a switch; $x$ itself is continuous there.\n\nLet\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\nO_w=\\{t:0<t_1<\\dots<t_m<T\\text{ and }k_{e_l}(t_l,x_w(t;t_l))>0\\text{ for every }l\\}, \\\\\nS_w=\\operatorname{closure}_{\\Delta_w}(O_w),\\quad A(w)=\\sum_l a_{e_l}.\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nThe closure is joint. Replacing it by separate closures of the edge-positive sets is generally wrong. If $O_{w}$ is empty, $S_{w}$ is empty even if every edge of $w$ is individually present in the graph. The empty word has $S_{\\emptyset}=\\{\\emptyset\\}$, $A=0$.\n\nUse $H=\\bigsqcup_w\\Delta_w$, with distance 1 between distinct words and $\\min(1,\\max_l|t_l-s_l|)$ within a word. This is a countable disjoint union of compact metric spaces, hence a complete separable metric space. Its compact subsets meet only finitely many words. This is the marked-history topology. It is not the discrete-sector Skorokhod $J_1$ topology. In particular collapsed entrance clusters are represented without discarding their order or cost.\n\nDefine $I(w,t)=A(w)$ for $t\\in S_{w}$ and $\\infty$ otherwise.\n\n#### 4. Exact density identity\n\nOn a nonempty word with strictly ordered times the law has Lebesgue density\n\n$$\nf_{n,w}(t)=\\exp(-n A(w)) [\\prod_l k_{e_l}(t_l,x_w(t;t_l))] \\exp(- \\int_0^T R_n(i_w(s),x_w(t;s),s)\\, ds),\n$$\n\nwhere $R_{n}$ is the total outgoing rate. The empty-word probability is its survival factor. This follows directly by multiplying successive survival probabilities and hazard densities; deterministic inter-event flows introduce no Jacobian in the jump-time coordinates. The survival factor lies between exp(-Lambda T) and 1, uniformly in n,w,t.\n\nLet $\\mu_{w}$ be the finite measure on $\\Delta_{w}$ with density $\\prod_l k_{e_l}$ on strictly ordered times and zero on the faces. Then $\\operatorname{support}(\\mu_w)=S_w$. Indeed the product is continuous and positive on $O_{w}$. Every neighborhood of a point in its closure contains a positive-volume ball in $O_{w}$. Off that closure it vanishes in a neighborhood. Consequently, for every measurable $F$ in $\\Delta_{w}$,\n\n$$\n\\exp(-\\Lambda T) \\exp(-n A(w)) \\mu_w(F) \\le P_n(\\{w\\} \\times F) \\le \\exp(-n A(w)) \\mu_w(F).\n\\tag{1}\n$$\n\nThis comparison is the entire finite-word LDP proof. It also makes clear why an exactly absent gate and a gate approached through positive histories are different.\n\n#### 5. Count tail and good full LDP\n\nA word with $r$ positive-toll edges has at most $d-1$ consecutive zero-toll edges before, between, and after them. Thus $m\\le d(r+1)-1$, so $r\\ge\\lfloor m/d\\rfloor$. The number $N_+$ of positive-toll jumps is stochastically dominated by a Poisson variable with mean $\\Lambda_+T\\exp(-na_{*})$, by a separate thinning of the positive-edge proposal clock. Therefore, for $q\\ge 1$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_n(N\\ge dq) &\\le P(\\mathrm{Pois}(\\Lambda_+ T e^{-na^*})\\ge q) \\\\\n&\\le \\exp(\\Lambda_+ T e^{-na^*}) (\\Lambda_+ T)^q e^{-n a^*q}/q! .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n$$\n\nThe last bound follows by $\\mathbb{E}[(1+z)^P]=\\exp(\\lambda z)$, or directly by factoring the Poisson tail; the slightly looser $\\exp(\\lambda)\\lambda^q/q!$ bound suffices. In particular $\\limsup_n n^{-1}\\log P_n(N\\ge dq)\\le -a_*q$.\n\nThe sets $K_m=\\bigcup_{|w|\\le m}\\Delta_w$ are compact. Equation (2) proves exponential tightness at every prescribed speed-$n$ level by choosing $m$ large. If $E_+$ is empty, $H$ itself is a finite union and is compact.\n\nFor an open $O$ in $H$ and any $h=(w,t)$ in $O$ with finite $I$, the relative-open set $O_w'=O\\cap\\Delta_{w}$ has $\\mu_w(O_w')>0$. Equation (1) gives $\\liminf n^{-1}\\log P_n(O)\\ge -A(w)$; taking the infimum proves the lower bound.\n\nFor a closed $F$, split $F$ into $F\\cap K_{m}$ and $K_m^c$. The first part has finitely many words. A component disjoint from $S_{w}$ has $\\mu_{w}$ mass zero. Every remaining component is bounded using (1) by a finite constant times $\\exp[-n\\inf_{F\\cap K_m}I]$. The tail has exponent at most $-a_*\\lfloor(m+1)/d\\rfloor$. The inequality $\\limsup n^{-1}\\log(u_n+v_n)\\le\\max(\\limsup n^{-1}\\log u_n,\\limsup n^{-1}\\log v_n)$, followed by $m\\to\\infty$, proves the closed-set upper bound.\n\nFinally $\\{I\\le L\\}$ is a finite union of the closed $S_{w}$: the word-length bound gives $|w|\\le d(\\lfloor L/a_*\\rfloor+1)-1$. It is compact. Hence $I$ is a good rate function and $P_{n}$ satisfies a full good speed-$n$ LDP on $H$.\n\nThis proves the theorem for every bounded Lipschitz gate in the declared class, including nonalgebraic zero sets. The proof covers arbitrary entrance times, repeated positive-cost cycles, zero-toll acyclic cascades, limiting endpoint entrances, and simultaneous limiting cascades. No inaccessible path receives a finite rate merely because a local conjugate happens to be finite.\n\n#### 6. A fully computed hard-gate witness\n\nTake $T=1$, $x_0=0$, vertices $\\{0,1,2,3\\}$, and $b_1=1$, $b_0=b_2=b_3=0$. The five labelled edges are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n01&:\\ a=2,\\ k=(t-1/4)_+; \\\\\n12&:\\ a=0,\\ k=(x-1/4)_+; \\\\\n02&:\\ a=5,\\ k=1; \\\\\n10&:\\ a=1,\\ k=1; \\\\\n13&:\\ a=0,\\ k=(1/4-t)_+.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly the reachable range $0\\le x\\le 1$ is needed. $\\Lambda=3$ is a valid total-rate upper bound. The zero-toll graph contains 12 and 13 and is acyclic. The cycle 01,10 costs 3.\n\nFor $w=(01,12)$, $x(t_2)=t_2-t_1$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nO_w&=\\{1/4<t_1,\\ t_1+1/4<t_2<1\\}, \\\\\nS_w&=\\{1/4\\le t_1,\\ t_1+1/4\\le t_2\\le 1\\},\\quad I=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $h=(t_1,t_2)=(1/4,1/2)$, BOTH hazards are exactly zero. Nevertheless $I(h)=2$ because the joint positive set approaches $h$. This is a concrete singular boundary entrance, not a positive-rate special case. For example $(1/4+\\epsilon,1/2+3\\epsilon)$ lies in $O_{w}$ for sufficiently small $\\epsilon$.\n\nFor $w=(01,13)$, positivity would require $t_1>1/4$ and $t_2<1/4$ with $t_1<t_2$, an impossibility. $S_{w}$ is empty. The same argument after any number of 10 cycles shows that state 3 is unreachable. Individual graph reachability would give the wrong answer.\n\nWith $a=t_1-1/4$, $b=t_2-t_1-1/4$, the gate product is $ab$ on $a,b>0$, $a+b<1/2$. Its integral is $(1/2)^4/24=1/384$. Equation (1) gives the rigorous all-$n$ bounds\n\n$$\ne^{-3}e^{-2n}/384 \\le P_n(\\text{word}=01,12) \\le e^{-2n}/384.\n$$\n\nThe direct word 02 has toll 5. Every word ending in 2 either has toll $\\ge 5$ through 02 or at least 2 through 01,12; cycles only add 3. The terminal-2 event is a union of clopen word components. Both its interior and closure are itself, and its rate infimum is exactly 2. Thus\n\n$$\n\\lim_n -n^{-1}\\log P_n(\\text{final sector}=2)=2.\n$$\n\nThe same equality holds for the finite source constructed below, by superexponential equivalence. State 3 has probability exactly zero in both sources.\n\n#### 7. Necessary scope distinction\n\nAn integral depending only on the ordinary limiting local generator cannot encode these costs. Change $a_{01}$ from 2 to 4, leaving all positive-toll edges positive. The limiting generator, drift, all exact gate zero sets, and the accessible support are unchanged, but the terminal-2 exponent changes from 2 to 4 (the competing direct cost remains 5). Therefore no function of those limiting data alone determines the transition rate.\n\nNor is the rate a universal absolutely-continuous path integral on the discrete sector coordinate: a finite-cost retained history has sector jumps and explicit entrance tolls. It is a singular-boundary extension of the original local-action branch, with its own stated carrier. This extension is mathematically complete; it is not a silent redefinition of Definition Q.0.1a.\n\n#### 8. Exact accessibility classification when zero-toll cycles are allowed\n\nThe acyclic-zero-edge hypothesis above is a convenient finite graph certificate. The density argument yields the following sharper necessary-and-sufficient result for EVERY finite graph with the same bounded Lipschitz gates and constant nonnegative tolls, including zero-toll cycles. This states an intrinsic mathematical classification; it does not postulate an LDP and it does not claim an algorithm for arbitrary noncomputable gate functions.\n\nFor $q\\ge 0$ let $W_{q}$ be the set of words w such that $O_{w}$ is nonempty and w contains at most q positive-toll edges. Call the source accessibility-coercive if $W_{q}$ is finite for every q. When E+ is empty this means the complete accessible word language is finite. Then:\n\n**Classification theorem.** The laws admit a good speed-$n$ LDP in the declared marked-history topology if and only if the source is accessibility-coercive. In the affirmative case its rate is exactly $I(w,t)=A(w)$ on $S_{w}$ and $\\infty$ otherwise. If accessibility-coercivity fails, no alternative good rate on this same topology can repair the claim.\n\nProof of sufficiency. The finite-word density identity (1) never used the no-zero-cycle hypothesis. For each $q$ take the compact set $K_q=\\bigcup_{w\\in W_q}\\Delta_w$. A realized history outside $K_{q}$ has at least $q+1$ positive-toll edges, almost surely. The positive-edge Poisson domination remains valid on arbitrary finite graphs, because its total rate is at most $\\Lambda_+e^{-na_*}$. Thus $P(K_q^c)$ has speed-$n$ upper exponent at most $-(q+1)a_*$. If $E_+$ is empty, all probability lies in the finite compact language. The previous finite-component upper/lower proof now applies with $K_{q}$ in place of the word-length truncation. $A(w)\\le L$ implies at most $\\lfloor L/a_*\\rfloor$ positive edges, so rate sublevels meet finitely many words and are compact. This proves the full good LDP.\n\nProof of necessity and uniqueness of the local rate. Suppose some $W_{q}$ is infinite. Since $a_{\\max}=\\max_{E_+}a_e$ is finite, infinitely many nonempty support components have $A(w)\\le q\\,a_{\\max}$ (use zero when $E_+$ is empty). Their union contains one point per component with pairwise distance 1, so the claimed sublevel cannot be compact.\n\nIt remains to exclude a different good rate $J$. Every point outside $S_{w}$ has an open neighborhood in its word component of zero probability; the LDP lower bound forces $J=\\infty$ there. For $h\\in S_{w}$, every sufficiently small relatively open neighborhood $O$ of $h$ has exact exponent $-A(w)$ by (1). Its LDP lower bound forces $\\inf_O J\\ge A(w)$. In particular $J(h)\\ge A(w)$. On the other hand every closed component-ball $F_{r}$ around $h$ has positive $\\mu_{w}$ mass, so its probability has exact exponent $-A(w)$. The closed-set upper bound forces $\\inf_{F_r}J\\le A(w)$. Choose points $h_r\\in F_r$ with $J(h_r)\\le A(w)+r$. Since $h_r\\to h$, lower semicontinuity gives $J(h)\\le A(w)$. Hence $J(h)=A(w)$ at every supported point. Every candidate rate is therefore $I$, whose bounded-action sublevel above is noncompact. A good rate is impossible. QED.\n\nThis also gives an exact failure of exponential tightness: for any compact $K$ only finitely many word components meet $K$, so choose $w\\in W_{q}$ outside them. Equation (1) gives $\\limsup n^{-1}\\log P(K^c)\\ge -A(w)\\ge -q\\,a_{\\max}$. No compact family achieves levels above $q\\,a_{\\max}$. A zero-toll cycle that can actually be repeated arbitrarily often in $[0,T]$ is an immediate failure witness; a graph-theoretic zero cycle that gates render impossible is not. Thus the classification explicitly distinguishes graph cycles from actual accessible boundary dynamics.\n\n**Nonempty examples on both sides.** An everywhere-open zero-toll two-state cycle has positive density for every alternating word and fails the criterion already at $q=0$. Its n-independent law may have the trivial nongood rate zero on support, but it has no good marked-history rate. Conversely a graph may contain a zero-toll return edge whose gate closes permanently before the outward edge first opens. Its return words have empty $O_{w}$; if all remaining accessible cycles have positive toll, the accessible language meets the criterion despite the graph-theoretic zero cycle. The hard-gate witness already demonstrates the basic chronological exclusion mechanism.\n\nThis theorem completes the good-versus-nongood classification for the entire declared finite-graph, constant-toll, deterministic-transport class on its actual marked carrier. It does not classify density-dependent small jumps, infinitely many sectors, singular jump sizes, or an unannounced replacement topology.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q01s-finite-same-history-nd-rid-source-and-exact-constrained-recovery\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.1s (Finite Same-History ND-RID Source and Exact Constrained Recovery).**\n\n#### 1. Effective source family, formally declared here\n\nFor the finite construction specialize the theorem to rational T,x0, constant rational drift vectors $b_{i}$, rational nonnegative tolls $a_{e}$, and rational bounded continuous piecewise-affine gates $k_{e}$ supplied as finite min/max expressions of affine functions. A global rational Lipschitz bound L and drift bound B on the reachable box are obtained directly from their coefficients. This is an effective, genuinely state-dependent subfamily of the proved theorem. It contains the hard-gate witness verbatim.\n\nTheorem Q.0.1s declares a mathematical finite driven-register source family. The source consists of the explicitly prescribed finite pulse list, supplied ready registers and maximally mixed coin registers below. Its actuator is the prescribed external Hamiltonian; it is not asserted to be an autonomous finite clock/battery. No bounded-power, bounded-stock, or refinement-uniform efficiency claim is made. All ideal source resources within this declared externally driven model are populated below, without symbolic unspecified cost functions.\n\nChoose a nonnegative integer $c$ with $T\\Lambda 2^{-(1+c)}\\le 1/2$. Let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_n&=n!\\, 2^{n^2+c},\\quad \\delta_n=T/M_n, \\\\\nb_n&=4\\lceil \\log_2 M_n\\rceil+n+\\lceil \\log_2(2|E|+2)\\rceil+4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factorial is important: it permits exact rational fixed-boundary recovery, which a purely dyadic clock can obstruct. The physical observation horizon is T at every n. There is no rescaling to a second unpaid clock.\n\nAt a tick beginning at t and coordinate x define\n\n$$\nk_e^{\\mathrm{safe}}(t,x)=[k_e(t,x)-L(1+B)\\delta_n]_+.\n$$\n\nCompute a rational $p_{e,n}$ with $0\\le p_{e,n}\\le\\exp(-na_e)$ and error $\\le 2^{-b_n}$; set $p_{e,n}=1$ exactly if $a_{e}=0$. Define the dyadic row\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nq_e(t,x) &= 2^{-b_n} \\lfloor 2^{b_n}\\delta_n k_e^{\\mathrm{safe}}(t,x)\\,p_{e,n}\\rfloor, \\\\\nq_0(i,t,x) &= 1-\\sum_{e\\ \\text{leaving}\\ i}q_e(t,x).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the final tick $k=M_n-1$ set every event probability to zero and $q_{0}=1$. This harmless terminal guard ensures all recorded events occur strictly before $T$, which is used by the exact constrained-recovery theorem. Its total comparison error is at most $\\Lambda\\delta_n$. All other $q$ are exact rational numbers with denominator $2^{b_n}$. The total event probability is at most $\\Lambda\\delta_n\\le 1/2$. Exactly zero gates stay exactly zero. A $b_n$-bit uniformly random integer, partitioned by the cumulative integer row, generates precisely this law. After the outcome, advance $x$ by $\\delta_nb_i$ using the old sector $i$, advance $t$ by $\\delta_{n}$, and replace $i$ by the edge head (or retain it on outcome 0). Record the event at the tick's right endpoint. There is at most one event per tick.\n\nThe safety subtraction ensures that every event in the digital source is allowed at its recorded endpoint in the exact continuous concatenated flow: $k_e(t+\\delta,x+\\delta b_i)\\ge k_e(t,x)-L(1+B)\\delta>0$ whenever $q_{e}>0$. Thus every digital word/time point belongs to the actual $O_{w}$. This prevents small discretization errors from inventing inaccessible boundary words.\n\n#### 2. A terminating certified exponential computation\n\nNo exact transcendental floor oracle is used. For $a_{e}>0$ put $z=na_e$, $m=\\max(1,\\lceil z\\rceil)$, $u=z/m\\in(0,1]$. Choose odd $J\\ge b_n+\\lceil\\log_2 m\\rceil+4$. The alternating Taylor partial sum\n\n$$\ns_J=\\sum_{j=0}^J(-u)^j/j!\n$$\n\nsatisfies $0<s_J\\le e^{-u}$ and $0\\le e^{-u}-s_J\\le 1/(J+1)!\\le 2^{-J}$. Therefore $p_{e,n}=s_J^m$ is rational, below $e^{-z}$, and\n\n$$\n0\\le e^{-z}-p_{e,n}\\le m 2^{-J}\\le 2^{-b_n}.\n$$\n\nAll floors defining $q$ now concern rational numbers and terminate exactly. To make arithmetic size explicit, write $u=A/D$ in lowest terms and use the common denominator $D^J\\,J!$. Numerator and denominator of $s_{J}$ require at most\n\n$$\nQ_0=\\lceil \\log_2(J+1)\\rceil+J \\lceil \\log_2 \\max(2,D)\\rceil+J \\lceil \\log_2 \\max(2,J)\\rceil+3\n$$\n\nbits. The unreduced rational power $s_{J}^{m}$ needs at most $mQ_0+1$ bits in each part. Factoring, summing and multiplying these explicit integers gives a finite construction using, conservatively, $O((J^2+m)(mQ_0+1)^2)$ bit operations with school arithmetic. This is an algorithmic size bound, not a claimed hardware clock rate. No numerical approximation is silently exponentiated without its error ledger.\n\nFor each edge on a nonterminal tick,\n\n$$\n0\\le \\delta k_e^{\\mathrm{safe}} e^{-na_e}-q_e\\le (1+\\delta K_e)2^{-b_n}\\le 2 \\times 2^{-b_n}\n$$\n\nafter enlarging $c$ if necessary so $\\delta\\max K_{e}\\le 1$. Hence a whole row's total-variation error is at most $2|E|2^{-b_n}$.\n\n#### 3. Superexponential same-history comparison\n\nLet $X_{n}$ be the continuous process of the theorem and $Y_{n}$ this digital source. Couple both through a common rate-$\\Lambda$ marked proposal clock, then couple its first-order tick transition to the dyadic row. A tick with at least two proposals has probability $\\le(\\Lambda\\delta)^2/2$; summed over $M$ ticks its contribution is $\\le\\Lambda^2T\\delta/2$.\n\nBefore any mismatch, corresponding accepted jumps have the same labels and times differing by at most $\\delta$. With constant drifts, after $N$ proposals the continuous coordinates differ by at most $2BN\\delta$. Inside a tick add $B\\delta$ for elapsed flow. The gates are Lipschitz, their exponential factors are $\\le 1$, and $\\mathbb{E}N\\le\\Lambda T$. Thus the expected integrated rate discrepancy is bounded by\n\n$$\n\\lvert E\\rvert LT[1+B+2B\\Lambda T]\\delta.\n$$\n\nThe safety subtraction contributes at most $|E|L(1+B)T\\delta$. The at-most-one-proposal law and its first-order row differ by at most another $2\\Lambda^2T\\delta$. The terminal guard contributes at most $\\Lambda\\delta$. Maximal row coupling and the rounding estimate therefore give the explicit safe bound\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(\\text{coupling failure})&\\le C \\delta+2|E|M 2^{-b_n}, \\\\\nC&=\\Lambda+3\\Lambda^2T+|E|LT[2(1+B)+2B \\Lambda T].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{S1}\n$$\n\n$L$ may be enlarged to a common bound including all time and state arguments; this only makes $C$ conservative. On success the word is identical and every time differs by $\\le\\delta$, hence $d_H(X_n,Y_n)\\le\\delta$. Since $\\log M_n/n\\to\\infty$,\n\n$$\n\\limsup_n n^{-1}\\log P(d_H(X_n,Y_n)>\\epsilon)=-\\infty\n$$\n\nfor every $\\epsilon>0$. Consequently the good LDP, exact accessible support rate, and all proved tube exponents transfer to this actual finite source. Superexponential absolute error also implies vanishing relative error against every fixed finite-action tube having probability exp[-n(A+o(1))]. This is stronger than weak approximation.\n\n#### 4. Exact ND–RID realization and predictive update\n\nRepresent the digital state by the current sector $i$, tick $k$, and occupation counts $c_1,\\dots,c_d$; each $c_{j}$ counts elapsed ticks spent in sector $j$ and $\\sum c_j=k$. Then\n\n$$\nt=k\\delta,\\quad x=x_0+\\delta \\sum_j b_j c_j.\n$$\n\nAlso retain the previous outcome. All entries have finite ranges. Let $X_{n}$ be this finite state set, $Y=\\{\\text{the fixed source program at }n\\}$, and $O=\\{0\\}\\cup E$. Define\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\nV_{\\mathrm{prob}}(x,y)(\\alpha)=q_\\alpha(x), \\\\\nT_{\\mathrm{prob}}(x,y,\\alpha)=\\text{point mass at }F_\\alpha(x),\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nwhere F advances the old-sector occupation count, tick, outcome, and vertex as just specified. Define arbitrary normalized updates on zero-probability outcomes. These are exactly the two kernels of Definition 6, not a simulation of unspecified kernels. The outcome changes the next state and hence its next forecast row. The source-to-marked-history map records nonzero outcomes and their physical tick endpoints; its continuous-coordinate readout is the exact affine concatenation. The diagram between the finite register, this ND–RID tuple, and the digital history law commutes exactly.\n\nA predictor can read the finite row $q$ before verification. The realized one-hot outcome supplies the Brier discrepancy; its conditional mean is $1-\\lVert q\\rVert_2^2$. The row for the updated state is the next prediction. This populates predict/verify/update. No PCE global-optimality or minimal-MPU irreducibility theorem is inferred from this elementary source.\n\n#### 5. Completely finite driven implementation and stock ledger\n\nPut $l=\\lceil\\log_2(M+1)\\rceil$, $r=\\lceil\\log_2\\max(2,d)\\rceil$, $o=\\lceil\\log_2(|E|+1)\\rceil$. A state snapshot fits in\n\n$$\ns=(d+1)l+r+o\n$$\n\nbits. Every tick is a deterministic function $F$ of the current $s$-bit snapshot and $b=b_n$ fair bits, with $s$-bit output. Its complete truth table has at most $D=s\\,2^{s+b}$ output bits; values on invalid input strings are fixed arbitrarily. This truth table is computed from the exact rational program above, before the run. It is the finite control description, not an unknown implementation constant.\n\nUse a fresh zero $s$-bit output register at each tick. The reversible map $|z\\rangle|v\\rangle\\to|z\\rangle|v\\oplus F(z)\\rangle$ retains the complete old input and therefore needs no erasure. One explicit circuit applies, for each input pattern and every output bit equal to 1, a multi-controlled NOT with complements for zero controls. With $c_{\\mathrm{in}}=s+b$ controls, the standard clean-ancilla AND/uncompute construction uses at most $2c_{\\mathrm{in}}-3$ Toffolis, plus at most $2c_{\\mathrm{in}}$ input complements. Thus\n\n$$\nR=s\\, 2^{s+b}(4(s+b)+1)\n$$\n\nis a conservative finite upper bound on primitive gates per tick; s+b-2 clean reusable AND ancillas suffice. Pad unused gate slots by identity. All old snapshots and all coin bits remain owned by the source after the run. The archive is lossless without a hidden reset.\n\nAt idle take $H_0=0$ on every register and fix a bath inverse temperature $\\beta$. Supply $Mb$ maximally mixed independent coin bits, exactly thermal for $H_0$. Supply all snapshots, reusable work ancillas, and the $D$-bit control description as ready pure stock. A conservative ready-bit count is\n\n$$\nB_{\\mathrm{ready}}=D+(M+1)s+(s+b)+1.\n$$\n\nThe last bit is the action readout below. The total Hilbert carrier uses $B_{\\mathrm{total}}=B_{\\mathrm{ready}}+Mb$ qubits. The exact initial nonequilibrium free-energy excess is $\\beta^{-1}B_{\\mathrm{ready}}\\ln 2$. Coins contribute zero. This is supplied stock, not an assertion that a finite-time reset attains the Landauer lower bound. There is no reset during the experiment; repeating it requires new stock or a separately paid reset. Neither the archive nor seeds are discarded.\n\nEvery classical primitive is a Hermitian involution U. In a slot of length $\\tau$ implement\n\n$$\nG_U=(\\pi \\hbar/(2\\tau))(I-U).\n$$\n\nThen $\\exp(-iG_U\\tau/\\hbar)=U$ exactly and $\\lVert G_U\\rVert\\le\\pi\\hbar/\\tau$. Include one additional controlled phase slot per tick as described next, so $\\tau=T/[M(R+1)]$. The gate list, pulse widths, finite stock, and control description completely specify this ideal driven source. At a pulse beginning and ending with idle Hamiltonian zero, the two switching-work expectations are $\\operatorname{tr}(G\\rho_{\\mathrm{in}})$ and $-\\operatorname{tr}(G\\rho_{\\mathrm{out}})$; they cancel exactly because the pulse unitary commutes with $G$. Their absolute sum is bounded by $2\\lVert G\\rVert$. Thus there is no unaccounted net ideal quench work, but the large peak-energy and gross-throughput bounds below are retained. This finite prescribed-drive convention does not include a claim about the cost of an autonomous controller or about uniform physical resources in the continuum limit.\n\n#### 6. Independent action calibration on the SAME source carrier\n\nIndependently fix energy and time standards $E_{\\mathrm{ref}}>0$, $\\tau_{\\mathrm{ref}}>0$ and $\\kappa_0=E_{\\mathrm{ref}}\\tau_{\\mathrm{ref}}$. Add a Ramsey bit initially $|+\\rangle$. After outcome $e$ in a tick, apply for one slot\n\n$$\nG_e=-(\\kappa_0 a_e/\\tau)|1\\rangle\\langle 1|,\n$$\n\ncontrolled by that tick's outcome; for no event apply zero. The gate is an actual independently energy/time-calibrated Hamiltonian. It is not a relabelling of log probability. The reference pulse $-E_{\\mathrm{ref}}|1\\rangle\\langle 1|$ for $\\tau_{\\mathrm{ref}}$ has phase $\\kappa_0/\\hbar$, fixing the action conversion on the same readout standard.\n\nFor a digital history h with edge word w, the conditional Ramsey state is\n\n$$\n(|0\\rangle+\\exp[i \\kappa_0 A(w)/\\hbar]|1\\rangle)/\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThe relative dynamical action of this specified retained readout is exactly $S_{\\mathrm{phase}}(h)=\\kappa_0A(w)$. Thus $S_{\\mathrm{phase}}/\\kappa_0=I$ on every supported finite history, and on all finite-rate limit histories by continuity within each word. Both normalized X/Y Ramsey quadratures are specified, so this is a response-level, historywise equality. Choosing $\\kappa_0=\\hbar$ is an explicit independent calibration choice; it is not derived from $\\ln 2$ or from the stochastic law.\n\nThis identifies the action of the retained Ramsey response. It does NOT identify A with the total actuator action, bath heat, or the Appendix-U field action. Those quantities have separately specified carriers and ledgers. Removing the Ramsey gates leaves the entire stochastic law unchanged; their addition is explicit source data. The construction supplies a real calibrated source action, while also showing why it cannot be inferred from stochastic probabilities alone.\n\nLet $a_{\\max}=\\max_e a_e$ and $C_G=\\max(\\pi\\hbar,\\kappa_0a_{\\max})$. Every pulse obeys $\\lVert G\\rVert\\le C_G/\\tau$. There are $M(R+1)$ slots, so the actual registered worst-case bounds are\n\npeak control norm $\\le C_GM(R+1)/T$,\ngross switching-work throughput $\\le 2C_G[M(R+1)]^2/T$,\nmaximum retained relative action $\\le\\kappa_0a_{\\max}M$.\n\nThese are explicit finite quantities determined by the source inputs and $n$. The exact per-history action is the smaller sum $\\kappa_0A(w)$. No finite source resource is equated with that rare-history rate.\n\n#### 7. What has and has not been realized\n\nThe new family has an exact finite ND–RID source, a complete finite driven implementation, a populated stock/clock/energy description, certified LDP-scale precision, exact state-dependent gates, and an independently calibrated same-carrier action response. These are completed constructions, not proposed certificate slots.\n\nThis bare zero-idle driven-register family is not asserted to be the qualified P17/native Appendix-B MPU source used in other source joins, nor does it establish MPU minimality. It does not identify the Ramsey response with the original U Euclidean bounce/Hessian/contour or with Y's electroweak/APS/transport observables. Renaming an edge U or Y, or setting its toll numerically to 284 or 77/4+ln2/3, would not establish those maps. A separate original-domain source map is required for those identifications.\n\n#### Exact constrained recovery on the same source\n#### 1. Rational polyhedral histories and constraints\n\nWith rational constant drifts, $x_w(t;s)$ is piecewise affine in the switch times. Suppose gate-positive regions are finite unions of rational open polyhedra in $(t,x)$, as holds for the supplied rational piecewise-affine gates. At a fixed word, its positive-history set $O_{w}$ is therefore a finite union of relatively open rational polyhedral cells in the ordered time simplex. A fixed endpoint $x(T)=y$ with rational $y$ gives rational affine equalities in the switch times. A rational convex polyhedral obstacle $x(s)\\in P$ for all $s$ is equivalent to membership of $x$ at the endpoints of each affine segment, hence a finite list of rational linear inequalities. Finite intersections and finite unions of such declared constraints are allowed. Call the resulting closed history constraint $C_{w}$.\n\nFor a given word the exact necessary and sufficient inward-recovery restriction is\n\n$$\nS_{w}\\cap C_w=\\operatorname{closure}(O_w\\cap C_w).\n\\tag{R}\n$$\n\nThe closure is relative to $\\Delta_{w}$. The right side is always contained in the left. Equality can fail when the exact constraint pins a gate to its zero boundary.\n\n#### 2. A finite exact checker for (R) below every action bound\n\nFix a finite rational action bound L0. The positive-cycle argument bounds word length by d(floor(L0/a*)+1)-1, or by d-1 if there are no positive edges. Enumerate those finitely many words. Decompose $O_{w}$ into its supplied rational polyhedral gate cells; intersect each with each polyhedral constraint component.\n\nFor a cell with strict inequalities $a_j\\cdot t>b_j$ and weak/equality constraints, solve the bounded rational linear program maximizing $\\rho$ subject to $0\\le\\rho\\le 1$, $a_j\\cdot t\\ge b_j+\\rho$, and all weak/equality constraints. Its optimum is positive exactly when the strict cell is nonempty. If positive, the closure of that cell is exactly the polyhedron formed by replacing all its strict inequalities by weak ones: for any point $z$ in that polyhedron and one strict feasible point $y$, $(1-\\epsilon)z+\\epsilon y$ is strict feasible and converges to $z$. If the optimum is zero or infeasible, the cell contributes nothing.\n\nApply this construction once without $C_{w}$ to compute $S_{w}$ and once with $C_{w}$ to compute $\\operatorname{closure}(O_w\\cap C_w)$. Their equality after intersection is a finite equality of rational polyhedral unions. It can be decided by enumerating sign cells of all boundary hyperplanes and rational feasibility, or ordinary exact polyhedral set-difference checks. This terminates for every supplied bound and input. There is no claim of deciding arbitrary noncomputable real zero sets.\n\n#### 3. Source-supported exact discrete recovery\n\nLet $H_{n}$ be the actual positive-probability digital histories, at times $jT/M_n$. Every $h_{n}\\in H_{n}$ lies in $O_{w}$, by the safety-margin proof. Define the finite digital variational ledger $I_n(h)=A(w)$ on $H_{n}$ and $\\infty$ elsewhere. This is a rate-labelled support functional; it is not $-n^{-1}\\log$ of an individual clock cell. A clock cell contains discretization entropy and must not be mistaken for a tube probability.\n\nAssume (R) for the declared constraint on every finite-action word. Then $I_n+\\mathrm{indicator}_C$ $\\Gamma$-converges to $I+\\mathrm{indicator}_C$ on $H$.\n\nLiminf: if $h_n\\to h$ in $H$ and $\\liminf I_n(h_n)<\\infty$, convergence eventually fixes the same word $w$. Each $h_{n}$ lies in $O_{w}\\cap C_{w}$; closedness of $C_{w}$ and the definition of $S_{w}$ give $h\\in S_{w}\\cap C_{w}$. Since the toll is constant on the word, the liminf is $A(w)=I(h)$.\n\nRecovery: fix $h\\in S_{w}\\cap C_{w}$. By (R), find points of $O_{w}\\cap C_{w}$ approaching $h$. A nonempty rational relatively open polyhedral cell has rational points dense in it, so choose such points $h^{(j)}$ with rational time coordinates $t_l/T$. Let $D_{j}$ be a common positive integer denominator. Because $M_{n}$ contains $n!$, every coordinate of $h^{(j)}$ is on the actual source clock for all $n\\ge D_j$. At each fixed $h^{(j)}$, all strict order and gate margins are positive. As $\\delta_n\\to 0$ the safety margin is smaller than these margins; and because $b_{n}$ grows like $4\\log_2 M_n$ while $-\\log(\\delta_n e^{-na_e})=\\log M_n+O(n)$, every required rounded probability $q_{e}$ is positive for all sufficiently large $n$. The no-event probability is $\\ge 1/2$. Therefore that exact constrained history has positive probability in $H_{n}$ for all sufficiently large $n$. Choose an increasing diagonal schedule $j(n)\\to\\infty$ slowly enough to meet the denominator, margin, and precision conditions. Then $h_n=h^{(j(n))}$ is exactly endpoint/obstacle admissible, converges to $h$, and $I_n(h_n)=A(w)=I(h)$.\n\nThis proves the claim. It is actual source-supported recovery on the same clock and carrier, with exact endpoints and obstacles, not a generic fixed-start approximation.\n\nNecessity: if $h$ is in $S_{w}\\cap C_{w}$ but outside $\\operatorname{closure}(O_w\\cap C_w)$, no sequence from $H_{n}\\cap C$ can converge to $h$ because $H_{n}$ is a subset of $O_{w}$. Hence no recovery theorem for these source ledgers is possible there. Condition (R) is exact, not an arbitrary stronger gate.\n\n#### 4. Two explicit tests\n\n1. In the hard-gate witness, for word (01,12), impose endpoint $x(1)=1/3$. Once sector 2 is reached, $x$ is constant, so this means $t_2-t_1=1/3$. The positive set has $1/4<t_1<2/3$ and $t_2=t_1+1/3$. Its closure includes the endpoint times $(t_1,t_2)=(1/4,7/12)$. Thus (R) holds for the full constrained support. Taking $t_1=1/4+1/j$ and $t_2=t_1+1/3$ gives exact rational constrained recovery for $j$ large. A purely dyadic grid could never represent $t_2-t_1=1/3$; $n!\\,2^{n^2+c}$ does so for every $n\\ge 3$.\n\n2. Instead impose endpoint $x(1)=1/4$. This forces $t_2-t_1=1/4$, so $k_{12}=0$ at every such exact history. The constrained support $S_{w}\\cap C_{w}$ is nonempty, but $O_{w}\\cap C_{w}$ is empty. Recovery fails, exactly as (R) predicts. The ambient LDP rate at that boundary remains 2; conditioning on an exact measure-zero endpoint is a different problem. Neither the ambient LDP nor the source construction claims a positive probability for that exact endpoint event.\n\nThe finite witness/checker implements both tests using exact rational arithmetic, along with the graph, accessibility, source normalization, and calibrated action checks. It does not treat finite testing as proof of the all-input theorem.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q01t-common-clock-native-jump-limit-and-complete-whole-cell-compiler\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.1t (Common-Clock Native Jump Limit and Complete Whole-Cell Compiler).**\n\n#### 1. Exact sequence\n\nUse the full original matrix $N=72I+16A+5J$ on the 276 pairs of a 24-set. Let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_0&=J/276,\\quad P_1=(6A+12I-J)/132,\\quad P_2=(460I-23A+2J)/506, \\\\\n\\lambda&=(2156,392,40),\\quad d_s=1/\\lambda_s,\\quad v_{sj}=P_s e_j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese are rational mutually orthogonal projectors of ranks 1,23,252, and $N=\\sum\\lambda_sP_s$. Every original cross term is retained. For integer $r\\ge 1$, on time [0,1] take independent signed Poisson channels of rates $r^3d_s/2$ and jumps $\\pm v_{sj}/r^2$. This is $m_r=r^2$ in the requested moderate-deviation sequence. There are $K=1656$ marked signed channels. Drift is zero, covariance per unit time is exactly $N^{-1}/r$, and total event rate is\n\n$$\n\\Lambda_r=r^3 D,\\quad D=276(1/2156+1/392+1/40).\n$$\n\nAll rates and increments are rational. Endpoint $e_{p}$ is attainable for every $r$: take exactly $r^{2}$ positive jumps in each of channels $(s,p)$, $s=0,1,2$, and no others. The observed physical horizon is always one; $r$ is the LDP speed, not a clock conversion.\n\nThe speed-r Hamiltonian is\n\n$$\nH_r(p)=r^2 \\sum_{sj} d_s[\\cosh(p\\cdot v_{sj}/r)-1].\n$$\n\nPut $H_0(p)=p^TN^{-1}p/2$. The Taylor series with nonnegative even terms gives\n\n$$\n0\\le H_r(p)-H_0(p)\\le [\\sum_{sj} d_s(p\\cdot v_{sj})^4 \\cosh(|p\\cdot v_{sj}|/r)]/(24r^2).\n\\tag{1}\n$$\n\nSince $\\lVert v_{sj}\\rVert\\le1$ and $\\sum_{sj}d_s=D$, on $\\lVert p\\rVert\\le R$ this is at most $DR^{4}\\cosh(R)/(24r^{2})$. Thus convergence is uniform on compact dual sets with a populated remainder. Also $H_r(p)\\le H_1(p)$ for all $r\\ge1$, by the same series.\n\n#### 2. Path LDP on the common unit clock\n\nWe give the argument, including the compactness step. For a partition $0=t_0<\\dots<t_k=1$, independence and the Poisson exponential formula give exactly\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{-1}\\log \\mathbb{E} \\exp[r \\sum_i p_i\\cdot (X_r(t_i)-X_r(t_{i-1}))] \\\\\n&=\\sum_i (t_i-t_{i-1}) H_r(p_i).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy (1) its limit is the everywhere finite differentiable strictly convex quadratic sum. Exponential tilting proves the finite-dimensional upper/lower bounds: at a prescribed increment $a$ over duration $h$, tilt by $p=Na/h$; under this tilt its mean tends to $a$ and its covariance tends to zero, and the log likelihood has limit $p\\cdot a-hH_0(p)=a^TNa/(2h)$. Exponential Markov bounds and compact covering give the upper bound. Gaussian coercivity makes the finite-dimensional rate good.\n\nFor path compactness, apply the exponential martingale separately to each signed coordinate on a time window of length h. For each fixed $a>0$ its maximal-increment bound is at most\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&2\\cdot 276 \\exp[-r(a \\epsilon/\\sqrt{276}-h C_a)], \\\\\nC_a &= \\max_{j,\\text{sign}} H_1(\\text{sign}\\, a e_j)<\\infty.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCover $[0,1]$ by at most $2/h$ overlapping windows; oscillation over any interval of length at most $h$ is controlled by one of the windows of length at most $2h$, with a harmless factor two in $\\epsilon$. Therefore for fixed $\\epsilon$, sending $h$ to zero and then $a$ to infinity gives arbitrarily negative exponential rates for the modulus violation. The same bound with $h=1$ controls the supremum norm. The deterministic jump bound is $\\max\\lVert v_{sj}\\rVert/r^2\\le 1/r^2$ and tends to zero.\n\nTo state the uniform-topology conclusion on continuous paths without asserting that discontinuous paths belong to $C$, replace $X_{r}$ by the polygonal interpolation of its values on the deterministic grid of size at most $r^{-4}$. The preceding martingale bound and a polynomial union bound show that its uniform distance from $X_{r}$ is superexponentially small: for every $\\epsilon>0$ the $\\limsup$ of $r^{-1}\\log$ of that probability is $-\\infty$, since $a$ is arbitrary. The interpolated process is exponentially tight in $C([0,1],\\mathbb{R}^{276})$. Finite-dimensional bounds, compact covering, and this exponential tightness give the full good path LDP; the original cadlag process has the corresponding LDP in the usual Skorokhod topology and the same continuous finite-rate paths.\n\nIts rate is the supremum over partitions of\n\n$\\sum_i(x(t_i)-x(t_{i-1}))^TN(x(t_i)-x(t_{i-1}))/(2(t_i-t_{i-1}))$.\n\nThis equals $I_0[x]=(1/2)\\int\\dot{x}^{T}N\\dot{x}$ when $x(0)=0$ and $x$ is $H^1$, and $\\infty$ otherwise. Proof: Cauchy-Schwarz bounds each partition energy by the integral for $H^1$ paths; conditional averages of the derivative on refining partitions converge in $L^2$. Conversely, bounded partition energies give weakly compact piecewise-constant derivatives whose integrals converge to $x$, so $x$ is $H^1$ and the same lower bound holds. This identifies precisely the original native Definition-T.13 action, not just its endpoint number.\n\n#### 3. Equicoercive action Gamma-limit and exact boundary recovery\n\nLet $L_r=H_r^*$ and $F_r[x]=\\int L_r(\\dot{x})$ for absolutely continuous $x$ starting at 0, $\\infty$ otherwise. These are the deterministic local actions of the $r$-dependent jump shapes; the actual speed-$r$ triangular-array LDP has limit $I_0$ as proved above.\n\nFor every $a>0$ and every $v$, choose $p=av/\\lVert v\\rVert$ (or $p=0$ if $v=0$). Since $H_r\\le H_1$,\n\n$$\nL_r(v)\\ge a\\lVert v\\rVert-C(a),\\quad C(a)=\\max_{\\lVert p\\rVert=a}H_1(p)<\\infty.\n$$\n\nThe supremum of these lower bounds over $a$ is a nonnegative superlinear function $\\Psi(\\lVert v\\rVert)$. Thus bounded $F_{r}$ implies uniform integrability of derivatives, uniform boundedness of paths and uniform equicontinuity, hence relative compactness in the uniform topology. This proves equicoercivity, including on the closed fixed-endpoint class $x(1)=e_p$.\n\nFor uniform $x_r\\to x$ and any $C^1$ dual test $p(t)$, integration by parts and (1) yield\n\n$$\n\\liminf F_r[x_r]\\ge \\int p\\, dx-\\int H_0(p)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTaking the supremum over such $p$ gives $I_0[x]$, including $\\infty$ when the distributional derivative is not $L^2$. This proves $\\Gamma$-liminf without assuming convergence of velocities. Since $H_r\\ge H_0$, one has $L_r\\le L_0$. Local uniform convergence of $H_r$ and Gaussian coercivity of $H_0$ imply $L_r(v)\\to L_0(v)$ for each $v$: maximizers stay bounded because $H_r\\ge H_0$, and (1) controls that compact dual set. Therefore the constant recovery sequence $x_r=x$ works for every finite-energy path by dominated convergence, as $0\\le L_r(\\dot{x})\\le L_0(\\dot{x})$. It preserves both endpoints EXACTLY. Infinite-energy points impose no recovery upper-bound obligation. Hence $F_{r}$ $\\Gamma$-converges equicoercively to $I_0$ on the full starting-point class and on the original fixed-endpoint class.\n\nFor endpoint $e_{p}$, Jensen gives $\\min F_r=L_r(e_p)$, with the linear witness $te_{p}$. The limiting minimum is $e_{p}^{T}Ne_p/2=77/2$. There is also an explicit bound, using $p=Ne_p$ in the dual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n77/2-E_r &\\le L_r(e_p)\\le 77/2, \\\\\nE_r&=[\\sum_{sj} d_s u_{sj}^4 \\cosh(|u_{sj}|/r)]/(24r^2),\\quad u_{sj}=v_{sj}^{\\mathsf T} N e_p.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPut $U_4=\\sum_{sj}d_su_{sj}^4$, an exact rational number computed by the checker. Since $|u_{sj}|\\le\\lambda_s\\le 2156$, for $r\\ge 2156$, $\\cosh(|u|/r)<2$ and $E_r\\le U_4/(12r^2)$. The half-history on $[0,1/2]$ has action $L_r(e_p)/2$, converging to $77/4$ without any clock renormalization.\n\n#### Exact endpoint and linear-witness recovery in the actual laws\n\nAn integer-count argument proves the stronger local result here, rather than inferring it from the LDP. Set $u_{sj}=v_{sj}^TNe_{p}$ and prescribe signed count means\n\n$$\n\\mu_{sj,\\pm}(r)=(r^3 d_s/2)[\\sqrt{1+(u_{sj}/r)^2} \\pm u_{sj}/r].\n$$\n\nTheir endpoint mean is exactly $e_{p}$, because their signed differences are $r^2d_su_{sj}$. Round each mean to an integer. Multiply all rational $v$ by a fixed common denominator, giving a fixed integer lattice $G$. The rounded endpoint-count discrepancy from $r^2e_p$, in these integer units, lies in $G$ and remains in a fixed bounded set. Membership follows from the exact three-channel endpoint representation. For each possible discrepancy choose once an integer channel correction. There are only finitely many such discrepancies; the maximum correction is $O(1)$, independent of $r$. All means grow proportionally to $r^{3}$, so corrected counts are nonnegative for large $r$ and give endpoint EXACTLY $e_{p}$.\n\nThe independent Poisson probability of this count vector has log probability equal to minus its count-relative entropy plus $O(\\log r)$ per channel, by Stirling. Bounded count corrections change entropy by $o(r)$. The unrounded entropy divided by $r$ is\n\n$$\nr^2 \\sum_{sj} d_s\\, \\ell(u_{sj}/r),\\quad \\ell(z)=z \\operatorname{asinh}(z)-\\sqrt{1+z^2}+1,\n$$\n\nwhich tends to $(1/2)\\sum d_su_{sj}^2=77/2$. Thus $\\liminf r^{-1}\\log P(X_r(1)=e_p)\\ge -77/2$. The endpoint closed-ball LDP upper bound, followed by ball radius tending to zero, gives the matching limsup. Therefore the exact-point exponent is $77/2$.\n\nConditionally on these count vectors, each channel's event times are independent uniform points. Centered bridge fluctuations after multiplication by $v/r^2$ have uniformly vanishing size in probability: the expected squared maximal empirical-count deviation is bounded by a constant times its count $O(r^3)$, hence the scaled bound is $O(1/r)$; the finite channel sum preserves convergence. The mean path differs from $te_{p}$ only by the bounded count correction divided by $r^{2}$. Hence every fixed uniform tube around $te_{p}$ together with the exact endpoint has the same limiting exponent. The dyadic compilation couples the exact channel word, so (S1)'s superexponential total-variation bound transfers this exact-endpoint result, as well as the witness-tube lower bound, without subtracting a conditioning normalization. Exact digital fixed-boundary recovery is therefore proved for this original native endpoint. Arbitrary obstacles or U/Y boundary data are not inferred.\n\n#### 4. What action transport is now actually proved\n\nTake the limiting source response class to be $H^1$ paths from 0 to $e_{p}$, with identity quotient (distinct paths are distinct registered native responses), source action $I_0$, map $\\Phi(x)=x$, and observable $\\rho(x)=B\\dot{x}$. Then every native T.13a-type mathematical diagram commutes: endpoints and unit clock are unchanged, domination is equality historywise, $x^*=te_p$ is saturating, and $B^TB=N>0$ makes $\\rho$ separate paths with fixed start. All this is derived as the rate/action limit of the explicit source sequence and its finite implementation, rather than by assigning a Ramsey action toll or declaring a desired exponent.\n\nThe qualifier “native” is essential. Identity quotient is the specified coordinate-source quotient; it has not been proved equal to the original electroweak gauge quotient. B dot x is its original incidence readout, not an independently derived physical electroweak observable. No CP-odd nonzero midpoint readout, Berry/APS operator family, chiral transport, or S3 single-budget allocation is supplied by a symmetric Poisson law. In particular an odd observable of an invariant unconditioned symmetric ensemble has zero mean; adding a physical CP source needs its original certificate. This supplies the common-sequence jump/native-action component; the sector-action realization is supplied separately below.\n\n#### Exact same-sequence whole-cell digital supplier\n#### 1. Finite digital law and comparison\n\nUse the exact rational projector channels of Theorem Q.0.1s, $K=1656$, $D=276\\sum_s 1/\\lambda_s$. At stage $r$ set\n\n$$\nl=r^2+20,\\quad M=2^l,\\quad \\delta=1/M,\\quad b=4l+r+20.\n$$\n\nAt each of the first M-1 ticks give signed channel (s,j,sign) the probability\n\n$$\nq_{sj,\\mathrm{sign}}=2^{-b} \\lfloor 2^b \\delta r^3/(2 \\lambda_s)\\rfloor.\n$$\n\nThe remaining probability is “no event.” At the last tick set every event probability to zero. This preserves the prior no-terminal-event convention. Record an event at its tick's right endpoint, adding sign $v_{sj}/r^2$. Each row is an exact integer partition of $b$ fair bits; no transcendental or real oracle is used. All $q$ are strictly positive, and the event sum is below $1/2$. Indeed $D<8$ and $8r^3/2^{r^2+20}<1/2$ for integer $r\\ge 1$. The last tick's guard does not affect endpoint accessibility: $M-1>3r^2$ and every needed channel has positive $q$.\n\nCouple the original independent Poisson source and this law by total marked proposals at rate $\\Lambda_r=r^3D$. The probability of any tick with two proposals is at most $\\Lambda_r^2/(2M)$. The difference between an at-most-one Poisson row and its first-order row is bounded conservatively by $2\\Lambda_r^2/M$ after summing ticks. Rounding costs at most $KM2^{-b}$; suppressing the last tick costs $\\Lambda_r/M$. Thus\n\n$$\nP(\\text{any channel-word mismatch})\\le 3 \\Lambda_r^2/M+K M 2^{-b}+\\Lambda_r/M.\n\\tag{S1}\n$$\n\nThe logarithm of its right side divided by $r$ tends to $-\\infty$. On successful coupling events have the same marks and times differ by at most $\\delta$. Their uniform coordinate-path difference is at most $1/r^2$, because there is at most one proposal per tick. Polygonal interpolation of the digital grid values changes the step path by at most $1/r^2$. Therefore the implemented digital response is exponentially equivalent at speed $r$ to the source of Theorem Q.0.1s, and has the SAME full path LDP and native action. No absolute-probability error is mistaken for relative rare-event control; (S1) is superexponential and hence is negligible against every fixed finite-action open tube with a finite exponent.\n\nAll events stay on the same unit observation clock. There is no state-dependent gate in this specific constant-rate source; the old state-dependent gated source and its support guards are retained unchanged as a separate construction. This compiler does not claim to transfer arbitrary U/Y boundary constraints.\n\n#### 2. Explicit finite reversible construction\n\nStore each channel's count, the tick, and last outcome. A snapshot fits in\n\n$$\ns=(K+1)(l+1)+\\lceil \\log_2(K+1)\\rceil\n$$\n\nbits. (The l+1 count bits cover M.) The next snapshot is the deterministic function F of the old snapshot and a b-bit seed that selects a channel from the integer probability row. The update increments that channel's count and the tick. The endpoint and path coordinates are exact rational functions of the count record. Invalid snapshots receive a fixed default update, making F a total finite Boolean function.\n\nA complete truth table has at most $D_F=s\\,2^{s+b}$ output bits. A truth-table XOR circuit using fresh output bits, multi-controlled NOTs and clean AND/uncompute ancillas computes $F$ without erasure. A conservative primitive X/CNOT/Toffoli count is\n\n$$\nR=s\\, 2^{s+b}[4(s+b)+1],\n$$\n\nwith at most s+b clean workspace bits. Each input pattern is tested by complementing zero-controls, computing its conjunction, toggling the requested outputs, and uncomputing. Every old snapshot and seed is retained. Nothing is reset or discarded. Generate each seed by b Hadamards on fresh zero payloads; because seeds remain orthogonal recorded labels, the final computational response has exactly the same law as independent fair bits even if the full state remains coherent. Its ordinary computational instrument, with fresh owned pointer payloads, is the one already specified by the source leaves.\n\nLet $o=\\lceil\\log_2(K+1)\\rceil$ and $S=M(b+R+o+1)$. This bounds the raw payload computation, seed generation, outcome-pointer copying and padding slots. ROM, a one-hot cursor and readout records are retained. A controlled execution slot (ROM enable and cursor enable), including clean-control conjunctions, a controlled primitive and cursor advance, uses at most 64 of the already admitted X, H, CNOT, Toffoli and controlled-H pulses. Controlled-Toffoli is synthesized by clean AND/uncompute; no new arbitrary-matrix gate leaf is needed. Write $Q=64S$ for a padded pulse count during observation.\n\nFor an explicit noncircular upper bound on all storage, put\n\n$$\nZ=s+b+S+D_F+M+K+100,\\quad N_{\\mathrm{cells}}=2^{Z^2}.\n$$\n\nThis enormously conservative value exceeds snapshots $(M+1)s$, seed bits $Mb$, workspace, $D_{F}$ truth-table bits, $S$ cursor bits, pointer and record bits, and a full gate-address ROM of at most $S[6\\lceil\\log_2 N_{\\mathrm{cells}}\\rceil+100]$ bits, plus a finite binary description of the integers and formulas above. Unused cells are padding and remain owned and charged. This is a terminating constructive family, not a practical or uniform resource estimate. Enumerating $F$, outputting its circuit, assigning consecutive payload addresses and writing the padded ROM is a concrete finite constructor at every $r$; all integer arithmetic is exact.\n\n#### 3. Join to the admitted small-cell source leaves\n\nThe independently supplied Theorem C.6c.Q02a declares C8 modules with two predictive-core qubits and one independently addressable payload, the ready core and zero payload, admitted finite X/H/CNOT/Toffoli/controlled-H pulses on named supports, computational instruments, owned ROM/cursors and finitely driven control. The compilation above uses only those leaves. Allocate EVERY seed, snapshot, work, ROM, cursor and pointer bit to the payload of a distinct such cell; count its full three-qubit carrier and its baseline, not just the payload. The core is untouched by payload computation. The cell's demonstrated qualifying two-CNOT core protocol remains available on its independent core; it need not be rerun during every payload tick. Programme-loaded/runtime cells are evolved admitted cells, as expressly allowed in that source record.\n\nThus the compiler uses assemblies of the declared C8 primitive family. It is not the old numerical packing experiment, nor a recovery of the original structural universal presentation; its source-relative domain is the displayed finite supplier grammar, and the global comparison with the original MPU is a separate question. Geometry is an ordinary finite cabinet assembly with one cubic-centimetre cell volume, not a source-derived electroweak geometry or a new emergent-manifold theorem. No unlisted current sheet or spatial sensor is used here.\n\n#### 4. Clock, energy, stock and retention\n\nUse $\\tau_0=1$ second for the native baseline $e_b=\\hbar/\\tau_0$ per whole cell. At the observation stage pad the prescribed pulse list to $Q$ slots and set $\\tau=1\\,\\text{second}/Q$. Every involution pulse has the admitted generator $\\pi\\hbar(I-U)/(2\\tau)$, norm at most $\\pi\\hbar/\\tau$. All core and payload idles carry the declared baseline. No bounded-power claim is made.\n\nThe owned carrier is $3N_{\\mathrm{cells}}$ qubits, baseline energy $N_{\\mathrm{cells}}e_b$ and cabinet volume $N_{\\mathrm{cells}}$ cubic centimetres. The ready source has two pure bits per cell (core-high and payload) and one degenerate maximally mixed core-low bit. Its supplied excess ready-stock free energy is $2N_{\\mathrm{cells}}\\ln 2/\\beta$. This is supplied preparation stock, not a claim of finite-time exact thermal reset. The baseline and its native cost are separate from this entropy excess.\n\nInstallation of ROM and initial cursor needs at most $N_{\\mathrm{cells}}$ X pulses of duration $\\tau_0$ each, leaving all records owned; denote the actual count $W\\le N_{\\mathrm{cells}}$. It is preparatory time, explicitly outside the fixed one-second observed history. Total owned duration is $W\\tau_0+1$ second, with full carrier qubit-time $3N_{\\mathrm{cells}}(W\\tau_0+1\\,\\text{second})$. A constructor producing the finite ready assembly and its ROM uses the admitted delivery/installation service; no microscopic manufacturing efficiency or autonomous battery is inferred.\n\nObservation peak control norm is at most $\\pi\\hbar Q/(1\\,\\text{second})$. The sum of absolute on/off switching-work expectations is bounded by $2\\pi\\hbar Q^2/(1\\,\\text{second})$. Installation contributes at most $2\\pi\\hbar W/\\tau_0$. Each ideal pulse begins and ends at idle; its two switching expectations cancel because its propagator commutes with its generator, so no net ideal quench work is hidden by this gross-throughput bound. Ready stock, baseline, programme, clock and archive remain charged even when this ideal net work is zero. If an autonomous waveform supplier or finite-power refinement limit is required, that is not supplied by this finitely driven branch and is not claimed here.\n\nThe physical unit clock is therefore paid with stage-dependent pulse strength and very large retained stock. There is no second unpaid continuum clock, silent seed disposal, cyclic reset, dropped purifier, or partial-cell resource count. These resources establish a finite implementation of the native stochastic response. They do not establish an original electroweak action/observable, APS family or baryon transport interpretation.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q01u-spatial-su2-interval-index-gauge-faithful-coordinate-and-scalar-contraction\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.1u (Spatial SU2 Interval Index, Gauge-Faithful Coordinate and Scalar Contraction).**\n\n#### 1 Geometry and operator with no connection collars\n\nFix $R>0$ and $X=[0,1]\\times SU(2)$, oriented by $dt$ followed by the standard left-invariant spatial volume. The metric is $dt^2+R^2\\,ds_{\\mathrm{unit}}^2$. For the degree-one identity map $g:SU(2)\\to SU(2)$, write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\theta&=g^{-1}dg=(i/2) \\tau_a e_L^a, \\\\\nds_{\\mathrm{unit}}^2&=(1/4) \\sum_a(e_L^a)^2, \\\\\n\\int \\operatorname{tr}(\\theta^3)&=24 \\pi^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Pauli matrices $\\tau$ act on the fundamental rank-two isospin bundle and $\\operatorname{tr}((\\tau_a/2)(\\tau_b/2))=\\delta_{ab}/2$. Let\n\n$$\na_f=f(t)\\theta,\\qquad a_f(\\partial_t)=0,\n\\tag{1}\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is smooth on the closed unit interval, $0\\le f\\le 1$, $f(0)=0$ and $f(1)=1$. There is no collar-flat requirement. The distinguished history is $f_*(t)=t$. In particular its normal curvature component is nonzero at each endpoint, so it cannot be turned into a product connection near the boundary by a smooth gauge change.\n\nThe curvature and its primitive topological number are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_f&=f'\\, dt \\wedge \\theta+(f^2-f)\\theta^2, \\\\\nQ_W&=-(1/(8\\pi^2)) \\int_X \\operatorname{tr}(F_f \\wedge F_f) \\\\\n&=[3f^2-2f^3]_0^1=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n$$\n\nThe gravitational degree-four form is zero for the fixed product metric. This computation is a direct curvature calculation; no collar-dependent geometric index formula is used here.\n\nLet $J_a=-iE_a^L$, where $E_a^L$ is dual to $e_{L}^{a}$, and let $\\sigma$ act on the two spatial spin components. The actual spatial operator in the fixed left frame is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_f&=R^{-1}(2 \\sigma\\cdot J+3/2+f \\sigma\\cdot \\tau), \\\\\nA_f&=-H_f.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n$$\n\nThe standard fixed-left-frame spatial normalization is given by Gibbons and Steif (1994), equations (4.10), (4.12) and (4.13).\n\nHere is the exact four-dimensional identification, which does not require f' to vanish. In the product frame take skew Clifford matrices\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc(dt)&=\\begin{bmatrix}0&-I\\\\ I&0\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\\nc(e^a)&=\\begin{bmatrix}0&i \\sigma_a\\\\ i \\sigma_a&0\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\\n-c(dt)c(e^1)c(e^2)c(e^3)&=\\operatorname{diag}(I,-I).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe upper two spinor components are positive chirality. Product temporal spin transport, temporal gauge in (1), and a time-independent volume density identify both chiral L2 spaces with L2([0,1],H), where\n\n$$\nH=L^2(S^3_R,\\mathbb{C}^2_{\\mathrm{spin}} \\otimes \\mathbb{C}^2_{\\mathrm{iso}}).\n$$\n\nThe identifications are unitary; no time reparametrization, density multiplier depending on t, omitted temporal gauge term or normalization change occurs. The lower-left block of the covariant Dirac expression is exactly\n\n$$\nU_- D_X^+ U_+^{-1}=\\partial_t+A_f=\\partial_t-H_f.\n\\tag{4}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of f enters curvature but not an additional term in this first-order minimally coupled Dirac expression. Equation (4) holds on smooth sections and then on the closed boundary realization described next.\n\n#### 2 Boundary projectors and the interval index theorem\n\nWrite $B_f=H_f$ for the intrinsic spatial operator with the final-boundary convention $c_{\\mathrm{boundary}}(v)=c(dt)c(v)$. On the positive chiral bundle $c_{\\mathrm{boundary}}(e^a)=-i\\sigma_a$, whereas the spatial coefficient in (4) uses $+i\\sigma_{a}$. Consequently\n\n$$\nB_f=-A_f.\n\\tag{5}\n$$\n\nThis is a derived Clifford sign, not a relabeling of an abstract matrix path. With the initial and final endpoints still named 0 and 1, impose\n\n$$\nu(0) \\in H_{\\ge 0}(B_0),\\qquad u(1) \\in H_{<0}(B_1).\n\\tag{6}\n$$\n\nThe free spectrum has smallest absolute eigenvalue 3/(2R), and $B_{1}$ is gauge-unitarily equivalent to $B_{0}$. Thus both endpoint kernels vanish. Under (5), (6) is precisely\n\n$$\nu(0) \\in H_{<0}(A_0),\\qquad u(1) \\in H_{\\ge 0}(A_1).\n\\tag{7}\n$$\n\nThe inequalities in (6) reproduce original Y.4.3a's written trace projectors on this invertible-endpoint example. The absence of product connection collars is still a real change of analytic branch, so this index is denoted $I_{\\mathrm{lin}}$ rather than claimed as a witness to that original definition.\n\nLet\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW&=\\{u: \\sum_j(1+j)^2 \\lVert u_j\\rVert^2<\\infty\\}, \\\\\nE&=H^1([0,1];H) \\cap L^2([0,1];W), \\\\\nE_{\\mathrm{APS}}&=\\{u \\in E \\text{ satisfying (7)}\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{8}\n$$\n\nThe endpoint traces in (8) retain their natural half-Sobolev regularity; (7) does not replace it by an arbitrary $L^2$ trace domain. Finite spatial harmonic sums form a core. The eigenvalues of $A_{0}$ grow linearly in $j$, so its graph norm is equivalent to the $W$ norm. Projection to $j\\le J$ is finite rank and its tail $H$ norm is at most $(1+J)^{-1}$ times the $W$ norm. Therefore $W$ embeds compactly in $H$. Moreover\n\n$$\n\\lVert A_f-A_h\\rVert_{B(H)}\\le 3\\lvert f-h\\rvert/R.\n$$\n\nBounded self-adjoint perturbation gives self-adjointness on the same $W$ and equivalent graph norms. Hence $t\\mapsto A_{f(t)}$ is norm-continuous in $B(W,H)$. These statements apply in particular to $f=t$ and require no endpoint flattening.\n\nThe needed analytic theorem states that a norm-continuous family of self-adjoint operators on a common compactly embedded graph domain has a Fredholm interval realization of $\\partial_t+A$ with initial-negative and final-nonnegative spectral traces, whose index is the spectral flow. Its closed domain is (8), and its adjoint has complementary traces. No constant endpoint collar is among those assumptions. See van den Dungen and Ronge (2021, Assumption 4.1, Definition 4.2, Proposition 4.3 and Theorem 4.9).\n\nApplying this theorem to the literal operator in (4) proves a closed Fredholm geometric boundary problem. The smooth product spinor maps U_+,U_- preserve (8) and carry (6) to (7); equality on the smooth core therefore extends to equality of the closed realizations. In particular this is an actual domain-and-codomain unitary identification, with both endpoint projectors transported.\n\nThe paper's constant extension of A outside the interval is an auxiliary analytic proof construction. This result neither extends the physical clock nor claims that such exterior intervals are resource-free physical collars.\n\n#### 3 Full spatial spectral flow and gauge endpoints\n\nFor completeness the spatial decomposition, including all noncrossing modes, is as follows. Scalar Peter-Weyl spin $j$ has right multiplicity $2j+1$. Coupling $J$ with $S=\\sigma/2$ and $T=\\tau/2$ gives grand-spin branches:\n\n- $G=j+1$: $RE=2j+3/2+f$, grand multiplicity $2j+3$;\n- $G=j-1$, $j\\ge 1$: $RE=-2j-1/2+f$, grand multiplicity $2j-1$;\n- $G=j$, $j\\ge 1/2$: the two reduced eigenvalues of\n\n  [[3/2-3f, 2sqrt(j(j+1))],\n   [2sqrt(j(j+1)), -1/2+f]],\n\n  each with grand multiplicity 2j+1;\n- $j=0$: singlet 3/2-3f and triplet 3/2+f.\n\nThese are $H_{f}$ eigenvalues; $A_{f}$ has their negatives. The mixed determinant is\n\n$$\n-3(f-1/2)^2-4j(j+1)<0,\n$$\n\nso one mixed eigenvalue remains positive and the other negative. All unmixed non-singlet branches retain their signs. For $f=t$ only the $j=0$ singlet crosses, with $A_{t}$ eigenvalue $(3t-3/2)/R$ and positive derivative $3/R$. Therefore\n\n$$\nI_{\\mathrm{lin}}=\\operatorname{ind}(D_X^+ \\text{ with (6)})=\\mathrm{SF}(A_t)=+1=Q_W.\n\\tag{9}\n$$\n\nThis equality is established by the interval index theorem, the full spatial spectrum and the direct curvature calculation separately. It does not invoke original Y.4.3b outside that theorem's collar hypotheses. More generally all smooth f in Section 1 have the same index: the straight homotopy between f and t fixes the invertible endpoints and retains the common domain. Backtracking can create paired crossings but cannot change the spectral flow.\n\nLet U be multiplication by g on isospin wavefunctions. The exact identity\n\n$$\nU^{-1}J_a U=J_a+\\tau_a/2\n$$\n\ngives $A_1=U^{-1}A_0U$ on $W$. Multiplication by $g$ changes a scalar harmonic $j$ only to $j\\pm 1/2$, so $U$ and $U^{-1}$ preserve $W$. This is continuum endpoint gauge equivalence. **Finite-cutoff endpoint defect and full-CAR realization.** Retain every scalar Peter–Weyl shell $j=0,1/2,\\dots,J$, with $L=2J$, including the right multiplicity $2j+1$. The full one-particle dimension is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_J&=4 \\sum_{k=1}^{L+1} k^{2} \\\\\n&=(2/3)(L+1)(L+2)(2L+3).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe spectral table above proves the finite interval index directly. Every mixed determinant is negative, every non-singlet branch keeps its sign, and the $j=0$ singlet $A_{t}$ eigenvalue (3t-3/2)/R has its unique positive crossing. Thus the cutoff still has index +1 for every $J\\ge 0$; no omitted high shell contributes another crossing.\n\nEndpoint gauge equivalence nevertheless fails at each finite cutoff. For the intrinsic $H_f=-A_f$, the displayed unmixed and mixed eigenvalues give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lambda_{\\max}(H_0^J)&=(2J+3/2)/R, \\\\\n\\lambda_{\\max}(H_1^J)&=(2J+5/2)/R.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $f=0$ the mixed eigenvalues are $(1/2\\pm(2j+1))/R$; for $f=1$ they are $(-1/2\\pm(2j+1))/R$. These, together with the unmixed branches and the $j=0$ singlet/triplet, give the stated maxima also at $J=0$. Therefore $\\lambda_{\\min}(A_0^J)=-(2J+3/2)/R$ and $\\lambda_{\\min}(A_1^J)=-(2J+5/2)/R$. The variational bound $|\\lambda_{\\min}(B)-\\lambda_{\\min}(C)|\\le\\lVert B-C\\rVert$ now yields, for every unitary $V$ on the full cutoff,\n\n$$\n\\lVert A_1^J-V^{*} A_0^J V\\rVert \\ge 1/R.\n$$\n\nIn particular no finite-cutoff endpoint unitary implements the continuum identity $A_1=U^{-1}A_0U$. Multiplication by $g$ couples $j$ to $j\\pm 1/2$, so its continuum action does not preserve the top cutoff shell. A finite spectral-flow index and continuum endpoint gauge equivalence do not supply finite endpoint equivalence.\n\nThe actual full-CAR carrier, preparation and paid resource construction are Theorem Q.0.1v, parts 3–6. Part 3 retains all $m_{J}$ occupation modes with explicit Jordan–Wigner matrices on $m_{J}$ occupation qubits and one addressed payload per whole C8 cell in its conservative realization; it uses neither a reduced two-by-two block nor a free packing argument. Part 4 prepares the filled negative $A_0^J$ sea with\n\n$$\nq_J=n_-(A_0^J)=(2/3)(L+1)(L+2)(L+3)\n$$\n\noccupied modes. The endpoint count is $q_J-1$. The ideal quadratic Hamiltonian commutes with total bare occupation $N$, so $N-(q_J-1)=+1$ on that initially filled $q_{J}$ sector is an endpoint reference-vacuum comparison, not creation of bare CAR number. On $k$-particle antisymmetric tensors $d\\Gamma(B)$ is a sum of $k$ copies of $B$, giving $\\lVert d\\Gamma(B)\\rVert\\le m_J\\lVert B\\rVert$ over the full Fock carrier. The complete Fock factor cannot be replaced by the one-particle norm.\n\nThe same theorem retains the qualification for a compiled checkpoint unitary $W$: if $\\lVert W-U\\rVert\\le\\epsilon$, leakage out of the initial number sector is at most $\\epsilon^{2}$ and the normal-ordered expectation differs from $+1$ by at most $m_J\\epsilon^2$. Exact number conservation is a property of the ideal quadratic channel, not automatically of intermediate native pulses or arbitrary entrywise approximations. Part 6 supplies the actual finite inventory, source/ROM/archive preparation, retained cells, physical durations, pulse peaks and gross work, and the continuous-drive bound (10). These resources grow with cutoff and accuracy; no uniform finite stock, free filled sea, exact finite semigroup or finite endpoint-equivalence claim is made.\n\nIf one instead inserts the intrinsic family $B_t=H_t$ into $\\partial_t+B_t$ with its initial-negative/final-nonnegative traces, its index is $-1$. It is not unitarily equivalent to (4),(6). Removing collars has not cured the original same-family sign issue. The present branch explicitly keeps $A=-B$ and transports the projectors.\n\n#### 4 A field-defined quotient coordinate rather than a free scalar label\n\nThere is a precise gauge-invariant way to recover f on this limited spatial branch. Fix the trivialization used above and let\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{CS}(a)=-1/(8\\pi^2) \\int_{S^3} \\operatorname{tr}(a \\wedge d_S a+(2/3)a^3).\n$$\n\nFor $a_{f}$, direct substitution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nq_f(t)&:=\\mathrm{CS}(a_f(t))-\\mathrm{CS}(a_f(0))=h(f(t)), \\\\\nh(u)&=3u^2-2u^3.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{10}\n$$\n\nFor a smooth gauge map $k:[0,1]\\times S^3\\to SU(2)$, the change of CS on each slice is the same integer: its degree is constant along $t$. The spatial exact-form term integrates to zero on $S^3$. Consequently the relative quantity $q$ in (10) is invariant even when the gauge map has nonzero degree. Temporal connection components created by $k$ are retained, not discarded; they do not change this slice-relative invariant.\n\nBecause $h$ is strictly increasing on $[0,1]$, with $h(0)=0$ and $h(1)=1$, the entire function $f=h^{-1}(q_f)$ is recovered from the field history modulo gauge. Let $\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{hom}}$ be the gauge saturation of all histories (1) in Section 1. The quotient $\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{hom}}/\\text{gauge}$ is exactly the set of those functions $f$: gauge-equivalent histories have the same $q$ and hence $f$, and histories with the same $f$ are gauge equivalent by the definition of saturation. This statement includes the topology of a full based history. It does not assert that the pure-gauge endpoint configurations 0 and $\\theta$ are inequivalent under large spatial gauge transformations.\n\nDefine the candidate field-to-native map and response by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi_{\\mathrm{hom}}([a])(t)&=h^{-1}(q_a(t))\\, e=f(t)e, \\\\\n\\rho_{\\mathrm{hom}}([a])(t)&=Be\\, f'(t).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{11}\n$$\n\nIt preserves the original endpoints and unit clock, is gauge invariant, and its response separates this homogeneous quotient: equality of $\\rho$ gives equality of f' because Be is nonzero, and the common initial value fixes f. In particular it is not merely an arbitrary matrix coordinate called a gauge field. The inverse of h has a singular derivative at 0 and 1; no uniformly Lipschitz extension to unrestricted field histories is claimed. On $\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{hom}}$ the composite in (11) is the smooth f already specified.\n\nMost importantly, (11) is a **constructed candidate observable on this restricted field class**, not a proof that the original registered electroweak active-response observable is this functional. No transverse gauge, Higgs, charge, family or source-preparation degrees of freedom have been identified with it. Its faithfulness does not extend beyond the explicitly gauge-saturated homogeneous class. Original T.13a still requires that physical registration and its action.\n\n#### 5 Exact native witness and relation to the finite source\n\nKeep the original incidence matrix $B$, marked pair $e$, $N=B^TB$, coefficient $1/2$ and unit clock. The native jump-source rate in Theorem Q.0.1t is\n\n$$\nI_{\\mathrm{St}}[x]=(1/2) \\int \\lVert B x'\\rVert^2.\n$$\n\nThis is the action from the actual full-incidence generating functional, not an action derived from the spatial field. Evaluating that proven functional on (11) gives\n\n$$\nI_{\\mathrm{St}}[\\Phi_{\\mathrm{hom}}(a_f)]=(77/2) \\int_0^1(f')^2\\, dt.\n$$\n\nFor the actual linear connection $a_*=t\\theta$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi_{\\mathrm{hom}}(a_{*})&=te,\\qquad \\rho_{\\mathrm{hom}}(a_{*})=Be, \\\\\nI_{\\mathrm{St}}[\\Phi_{\\mathrm{hom}}(a_{*})]&=77/2, \\\\\n(1/2) \\int_0^{1/2}\\lVert \\rho_{\\mathrm{hom}}(a_{*})\\rVert^2&=77/4.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{12}\n$$\n\nEquations (9) and (12) hold on exactly the same interval and smooth field history. There is neither an infimum without attainment nor a shrinking-collar limit in this additive branch. The native endpoint probability remains unnormalized and costs 77/2; conditioning a native bridge on that endpoint still subtracts this cost. None of these probability statements comes from the spatial index.\n\nThe finite native source realizes all 276 directions. It is not a probability measure supported exactly on the line R e: its signed microscopic jump channels are not line-parallel. Thus (11),(12) are a common-witness and restricted quotient-coordinate result, **not** a lift of every finite source path to $\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{hom}}$ or a new physical path-space LDP on that class. In particular the old exact-line obstacle obstruction is unchanged. Deriving the actual joint field/source law requires a separate source-to-field construction or contraction and boundary proof; simply placing the spatial field on the limiting witness does not provide one.\n\n#### 6 A stronger homogeneous Yang Mills action obstruction\n\nLet $C=12\\pi^2/g_{\\mathrm{YM}}^2>0$ with the physical coupling fixed independently, and retain the actual Euclidean action on this spatial connection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{\\mathrm{YM}}[f]&=C \\int_0^1 [R(f')^2+(4/R)V(f)]\\,dt, \\\\\nV(f)&=f^2(1-f)^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{13}\n$$\n\nThis follows from the electric and magnetic field strengths in (2), the fundamental trace and volume $2\\pi^2R^3$. The second term is the magnetic action; it is nonzero for $f=t$. In particular\n\n$$\nS_{\\mathrm{YM}}[t]=C(R+2/(15R)).\n\\tag{14}\n$$\n\nThere is a stronger failure than the existence of an extra term. Define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_\\epsilon(t)&=t-\\epsilon V'(t), \\\\\nV'(t)&=2t(1-t)(1-2t),\\qquad 0<\\epsilon<1/2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{15}\n$$\n\nSince $-1\\le V''(t)\\le2$ on $[0,1]$, $f_\\epsilon'>0$; it has the same endpoints, stays in $[0,1]$, and obeys\n\n$$\nf_\\epsilon(1-t)=1-f_\\epsilon(t),\\qquad f_\\epsilon(1/2)=1/2.\n$$\n\nHence (15) remains in the smooth monotone complement-symmetric history class, has the same spatial boundary data and the same unique Dirac crossing at the original midpoint. Direct differentiation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd/d\\epsilon\\, S_{\\mathrm{YM}}[f_\\epsilon] \\text{ at } 0 \\\\\n&=-(4C/R) \\int_0^1(V')^2\\,dt \\\\\n&=-8C/(105R)<0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{16}\n$$\n\nThe electric first variation is zero because the perturbation vanishes at both endpoints. Exact polynomial integrals give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int(V')^2&=2/105,\\qquad \\int(V'')^2=4/5, \\\\\nI_{\\mathrm{St}}[\\Phi_{\\mathrm{hom}}(a_{f_\\epsilon})]&=77/2+(154/5)\\epsilon^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{17}\n$$\n\nThis also yields a constructive strict decrease, without an unspecified small-o remainder. Taylor's theorem and $V''\\le 2$ on the full admissible range give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{\\mathrm{YM}}[f_\\epsilon]-S_{\\mathrm{YM}}[t] \\\\\n&\\le C/(105R)[-8\\epsilon+(84R^2+8)\\epsilon^2].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{18}\n$$\n\nFor example $\\epsilon=1/(21R^2+4)$ lies in $(0,1/2)$ and makes the right side strictly negative for every $R>0$.\n\nTherefore the following precise no-go holds. On any proposed physical class containing $t$ and the deformations (15), with proposed response map (11) and physical action exactly (13), no positive coupling and finite radius can satisfy both T.13a's witness equation at the field $t\\theta$ and its all-path domination. If (14) is not 77/2, the witness fails. If it is 77/2, (18) gives $S_{\\mathrm{YM}}[f_\\epsilon]<77/2$ while (17) gives $I_{\\mathrm{St}}[\\Phi_{\\mathrm{hom}}(a_{f_\\epsilon})]>77/2$, contradicting domination. Even adjusting the overall coefficient after the fact cannot fix this functional failure.\n\nThis is a no-go for the stated homogeneous Yang-Mills map and neighborhood, not for every original electroweak action or transport. Higgs and other physical terms might change the first variation; another physically registered response map might send a different minimizer to te; an independently justified class could exclude these deformations. None of those repairs is supplied or ruled out here. Restricting to the singleton linear history after discovering the descending direction would not establish the original physical class or its source law.\n\n#### Actual scalar contraction and exact endpoint law\n#### 1 Exact finite-source observable and its current pullback\n\nLet $X_{r}$ be the already admitted 276-coordinate native source, starting at zero on $[0,1]$, and $R_r=BX_r$ its actual 759-component incidence record. Keep its complete finite word, counters, controller, clock and archive. Write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb&=Be,\\qquad b^{\\mathsf T} b=e^{\\mathsf T}Ne=77, \\\\\n\\ell&=Ne/77, \\\\\nY_r&=\\ell^{\\mathsf T} X_r=b^{\\mathsf T} R_r/77, \\\\\nR_r^{\\mathrm{red}}&=bY_r=\\Pi_b R_r,\\qquad \\Pi_b=bb^{\\mathsf T}/77.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{1}\n$$\n\nThe map is fixed independently of the target action. $\\Pi_{b}$ is the orthogonal rank-one projector onto the marked incidence vector, and $P\\Pi_b=\\Pi_bP=\\Pi_b$ for $P=BN^{-1}B^T$. It is applied to the actual current, not to a new arbitrary scalar source. In the native pair basis, $Ne$ has integer entries 77 at $e$, 21 at pairs sharing one endpoint, and 5 at disjoint pairs. Thus (1) is an exact rational function of the existing finite counters.\n\nAt each finite stage there are finitely many native records. Grouping their existing pointer-instrument branches by the value of (1) gives a legitimate coarse-grained instrument: a sum of completely positive instrument branches is completely positive, and the sum over all grouped outcomes is the original trace-preserving channel. The full record is retained. This does not erase or declare response-null the other source coordinates, controllers or archives. If an additional online arithmetic pointer or field driver is physically requested, its circuitry, positive durations and work must also be counted; no such new physical supplier is inferred from mathematical outcome grouping.\n\nFor finite-rate paths define the corresponding response as\n\n$$\n\\rho_{\\mathrm{red}}=b y'=\\Pi_b \\rho_{\\mathrm{full}}.\n\\tag{2}\n$$\n\nEquation (2) is a **successor readout declaration**, not equality of reduced and original full active responses on every native path.\n\n#### 2 Full path contraction with the exact coefficient\n\nThe established full native LDP has good rate\n\n$$\nI[x]=(1/2) \\int x'^{\\mathsf T} N x',\n$$\n\non absolutely continuous fixed-start paths, and $\\infty$ otherwise. Its path topology is uniform. The linear map $x\\mapsto\\ell^Tx$ is continuous. For an admissible scalar $y$, every lift has the unique decomposition\n\n$$\nx=ye+z,\\quad \\ell^{\\mathsf T} z=0,\\quad z(0)=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $e^{T}Nz'=77\\ell^Tz'=0$ almost everywhere,\n\n$$\nI[x]=(77/2) \\int (y')^2+(1/2) \\int z'^{\\mathsf T} N z'.\n\\tag{3}\n$$\n\n$N$ is positive definite. Therefore the infimum over all lifts is attained uniquely at $z=0$. If a finite-rate lift exists then $y$ is $H^1$; conversely $x=ye$ is a finite-rate lift for every fixed-start $H^1$ $y$. The contraction principle gives the full good scalar path LDP\n\n$$\nJ[y]=(77/2) \\int (y')^2,\\qquad y(0)=0,\n\\tag{4}\n$$\n\nwith value $\\infty$ off that class. Equivalently $\\ell^TN^{-1}\\ell=1/77$. There is no conditioning on a line and no use of an empty-interior event.\n\nThe same coefficient follows from the actual source's connected current functional. Substituting $J_{\\mathrm{full}}=bj/77$ into the established full-current generating functional $W(J_{\\mathrm{full}})=(1/2)\\int J_{\\mathrm{full}}^TPJ_{\\mathrm{full}}$ gives\n\n$$\nW_{\\mathrm{scalar}}(j)=1/(2\\cdot 77) \\int j^2.\n$$\n\nIts Legendre transform is (4). This checks the normalization by both path contraction and the original measured-current source map.\n\nFor endpoint $y(1)=1$, (4) has unique minimizer $y=t$ and value $77/2$. The full native minimizing lift is exactly $X=te$. The exact finite-source scalar endpoint subprobability also has this rate: its upper bound follows from the closed scalar endpoint fiber, while its lower bound follows from the already proved exact native $X_r(1)=e$ subprobability and its pinned LDP, which is a subset of $Y_r(1)=1$. The lower argument works for every relative open scalar-path neighborhood by lifting $y$ to $ye$. It does not require all microscopic paths to equal their minimizing lift.\n\nThus the full scalar pinned LDP has rate $J$ restricted to $y(1)=1$. Normalizing that bridge subtracts $77/2$, as usual. No physical rate or exact fluctuation prefactor is supplied by this subtraction.\n\n#### 3 Optional compact coefficient readout and an actual field reconstruction\n\nThe linear $Y_{r}$ can leave [0,1]. To obtain a coefficient that remains in the homogeneous field interval without killing paths at its boundary, define a further explicitly declared continuous readout\n\n$$\nc(u)=\\min(1,\\max(0,u)),\\qquad Z_r(t)=c(Y_r(t)).\n\\tag{5}\n$$\n\nThis retains the original $Y_{r}$ and all source records. In particular it does not replace the source dynamics by a reflected or absorbed Markov process. The clipped process need not itself be Markov; overshoots of $Y_{r}$ remain retained data. At each finite stage its paths are piecewise linear, with rational additional breakpoints where a segment meets 0 or 1.\n\nThe pointwise map c is 1-Lipschitz for uniform path norms. On H1 paths, its Sobolev chain rule gives\n\n$$\n\\int |(c \\circ y)'|^2 \\le \\int |y'|^2.\n$$\n\nIf $z$ takes values in $[0,1]$, the lift $y=z$ gives equality and $c(y)=z$. If a finite-rate lift exists then $z$ is $H^1$. Consequently a second contraction proves a full good LDP on $C([0,1],[0,1])$ with rate\n\n$$\nJ_{\\mathrm{clip}}[z]=(77/2) \\int (z')^2,\\qquad z(0)=0,\n\\tag{6}\n$$\n\nand infinity otherwise. Clipping is an observation map, not an all-time obstacle event, so the original affine/polyhedral obstacle obstruction does not apply to it.\n\nDefine the reconstructed spatial connection and its operator by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_r(t)&=Z_r(t)\\theta, \\\\\nA_r(t)&=-R^{-1}(2 \\sigma\\cdot J+3/2+Z_r(t) \\sigma\\cdot \\tau).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient-to-connection and coefficient-to-$B(W,H)$-operator maps are continuous in the uniform topology. Therefore their response laws inherit (6). For every finite source record, (7) is a genuine spatial $SU(2)$ connection with piecewise-Lipschitz time coefficients and the same actual spatial Dirac expression. This is an explicitly declared weaker temporal regularity than the smooth spatial-connection class fixed at the start of this theorem. The interval Fredholm theorem needs only the continuous common-domain family, so it applies whenever the endpoint event below holds. At finite rate, coefficients are $H^1$, curvature is $L^2$ and the identity $Q_W=h(z(1))-h(z(0))$ follows from the Sobolev chain rule. The product spinor identification remains exact because the gauge coefficient is a bounded zero-order term.\n\nOn the gauge saturation of these homogeneous H1 histories, the relative Chern-Simons coordinate and its inverse constructed in this theorem recover z. Hence the constructed response b z' separates that declared field quotient. The field's topology here is the one transported by this quotient coordinate. No assertion is made about faithfulness on an unrestricted electroweak or full-source quotient.\n\nThis proves an exact mathematical source-to-spatial-response reconstruction rather than only associating a field to the minimizing path. It does not prove that the finite source's physical electromagnetic or nonabelian output ports generate the continuum connection (7), or that it drives every finite CAR cutoff Hamiltonian on the fixed clock. Those concrete field/operator supplier and resource records are still missing.\n\nThe LDP here is for coefficient paths in the uniform topology. The response b z' is specified on finite-rate paths; no strong L2 tightness of the microscopic polygon derivatives or separate L2-current LDP is asserted.\n\n#### 4 Exact clipped endpoint branch and the tube distinction\n\nLet $E=\\{Z_r(1)=1\\}=\\{Y_r(1)\\ge 1\\}$. The unnormalized endpoint subprobability has a full relative LDP on the closed endpoint fiber $z(1)=1$ with rate (6) restricted to that fiber.\n\nFor the lower bound at a finite-rate $z$ with $z(1)=1$, take\n\n$$\ny_\\delta=z+\\delta t,\\qquad \\delta>0.\n$$\n\nThen $y_{\\delta}(1)>1$, $c(y_{\\delta})\\to z$ uniformly and $J[y_{\\delta}]\\to J_{\\mathrm{clip}}[z]$. Every relative open neighborhood of $z$ therefore contains $c(y_{\\delta})$ for small $\\delta$; continuity of $c$ gives an open scalar-path neighborhood of $y_{\\delta}$ whose images stay in that neighborhood and whose endpoints exceed 1. Apply the full scalar LDP lower bound there and let $\\delta$ decrease to zero. The upper bound follows by the full clipped LDP on the closed fiber, together with exponential tightness for the standard relative closed-set version. The unique endpoint minimizer is $z=t$, so\n\n$$\n\\lim (1/r) \\log \\Pr(E)=-77/2.\n\\tag{8}\n$$\n\nConditioned on $E$, the rate is $J_{\\mathrm{clip}}-77/2$. On $E$, all reconstructed endpoint operators in (7) are the same pure-gauge pair $A_0,A_1$; the interval spatial index is $+1$. This again uses the independently proved no-collar interval branch, not original Y.4.3a.\n\nSelecting E declares an oriented positive endpoint protocol. It does not derive a physical CP-breaking preparation or a nonzero baryon yield from a CP-symmetric source ensemble; averaging opposite protocols can still cancel their signed observables.\n\nThere is an important boundary distinction. The output fiber $z(1)=1$ has empty interior in the uniform topology of $C([0,1],[0,1])$. Its exponent (8) was proved by the explicit open preimages in Y, not by claiming that it meets Q.0.1b's output-tube interior condition. A Q.0.1c application still needs the actual requested transition class and its correct tube proof. The old unconditioned compact native tube result is unchanged.\n\nFor an unconditioned scalar tube before clipping, the usual construction is available: a small uniform tube around t, with terminal basin [1,1+delta], has interior and closure rate infimum 77/2. The interior paths (1+epsilon)t approach the minimizer, while endpoint displacement gives the matching lower bound. This is a scalar-source tube statement; its endpoint basin is not the exact pure-gauge field endpoint class, so it is not silently substituted for that class.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q01v-same-word-full-car-checkpoint-channel-with-admitted-pulse-compilation\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.1v (Same-Word Full-CAR Checkpoint Channel with Admitted Pulse Compilation).**\n\n#### 1 Original admissibility audit\n\nThe original Section 3.2.1 Definition 6 permits both deterministic RID and ND-RID. Its probabilistic outcome and transformation maps take values in probability simplices, so point masses are allowed. The next state depends on the registered outcome. No restriction requires the initial state to contain no latent random record.\n\nOriginal Definition Q.0.1a asks for finite ND-RID/PCE stochastic dynamics on the declared compact retained state space, a good local path LDP and the independent physical-action calibration. It does not add a fresh-noise-during-observation rule, a universal intervention grammar, or autonomous time-independent Hamiltonian evolution. The source replay below therefore supplies a valid finite ND-RID realization of the **same declared response process**. It does not by itself prove a local path rate for a larger response in which the entire future ROM is visible at time zero. Physical ownership of a register and inclusion in the chosen mathematical response are different statements.\n\nOriginal MPU Definition 26 permits a self-adjoint $H(t)$ on the driven internal unitary branch. Its energy calibration remains separate. Appendix B Definition B.3 specifically requires no explicit time dependence when claiming its finite closed autonomous energy-density construction. Theorem Q.0.1v uses the admitted finitely driven source category and does not claim B.3 autonomy. Adding a finite positive Fourier-clock regulator is possible under its separately proved error and resource ledger, but no such additional autonomy hypothesis is imposed on Q.0.1a here.\n\nThe small-C8 admission fixes named $X$, $H$, CNOT, Toffoli, controlled-$H$ and quarter-phase $Q=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-i)$ pulses, fresh preparation, computational instruments, and owned finite drive/ROM services. An arbitrary spatial quadratic Hamiltonian is not already one of those pulse leaves. Section 5 supplies an approximate checkpoint compiler from the actual listed gates, while Section 3 labels the continuous quadratic supplier separately.\n\nThese statements inspect the original definitions; they do not infer the physical action-scale equality in Q.0.1a or a PCE minimum from an arbitrary kernel. The accepted native source's existing qualification and ownership record is retained, not strengthened by the word \"ND-RID.\"\n\n#### 2 Exact preparation and replay law\n\nAt native stage $r$, retain the previously fixed $M=2^{r^{2}+20}$, seed length $b$, integer probability rows, no-last-event guard and rational signed increments. Let $\\omega$ denote all $b(M-1)$ fair seed bits, plus the deterministically computed outcome/counter record. Use the existing finite reversible source compiler to prepare this record before $t=0$, using fresh zero payloads, Hadamards, computational pointer copies and the same integer row decisions. All seeds, outcomes and old snapshots are retained. The mixed classical law is the exact product law; keeping a measurement purification does not alter its declared computational instrument.\n\nDuring [0,1], read the immutable record in its original order at the same registered tick times. Let the full finite RID state be (omega,k,c), where k is the cursor and c the accumulated record. At the next tick,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{\\mathrm{prob}}(\\omega,k,c)&=\\delta_{o_k(\\omega)}, \\\\\nT_{\\mathrm{prob}}(\\omega,k,c,o)&=\\delta_{(\\omega,k+1,c+\\Delta(o))}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{1}\n$$\n\nDefine the total update on off-support inputs as well, by the same increment rule or an explicit invalid-state flag. Thus (1) is a finite genuine ND-RID kernel whose transformation uses the outcome. Averaging the initial product preparation gives exactly the previous joint law of every finite sequence of outcomes. The future seed bits remain independent conditional on the earlier declared outcome record. Conditional on the full latent $\\omega$, of course, the law is deterministic; these are different conditioning statements.\n\nPolygonal interpolation, the fixed scalar projection and optional clipping give exactly the previous $X_r,Y_r,Z_r$ processes. The replay introduces no new probability error, conditioning, rare-event factor or time change. All preparation time is recorded outside the unchanged observed unit interval. If an allowed observer reads the entire future ROM before $t=0$, that is a larger read grammar and the response law must be stated with that information; no universal invisibility claim is made.\n\n#### 3 The explicit conditional spatial CAR Hamiltonian\n\nPut $L=2J$, and let\n\n$$\nm=m_J=(2/3)(L+1)(L+2)(2L+3)\n$$\n\nbe the one-particle dimension of the full spatial cutoff. Use the fixed free spatial eigenmode basis, with its exact angular-momentum coefficients. On m occupation qubits define the standard Jordan-Wigner CAR matrices $c_{a}$. This is an actual finite representation of the full matrix CAR algebra. A conservative physical implementation places each occupation qubit in the independently addressable payload of one whole admitted C8 cell, leaving its two qualifying core qubits untouched. This uses m whole cells rather than claiming the smaller algebraic packing ceil(m/3) as a free physical control realization.\n\nFor a retained coefficient f, the one-body matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_f^J&=A_0^J+f V^J, \\\\\nV^J&=-(\\sigma\\cdot \\tau)/R \\text{ on each retained spatial block}, \\\\\n\\lVert V^J\\rVert&=3/R, \\\\\n\\lVert A_f^J\\rVert &\\le (2J+9/2)/R,\\qquad 0\\le f\\le 1.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n$$\n\nSet\n\n$$\nK_f=d\\Gamma(A_f^J)=\\sum_{ab} (A_f^J)_{ab} c_a^{\\dagger} c_b.\n\\tag{3}\n$$\n\nIts diagonal entries and the real/imaginary parts of every off-diagonal coupling are fixed algebraic numbers times 1/R. Equation (3), the CAR representation and the declared basis specify every matrix entry and support. In particular\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\lVert K_f\\rVert\\le m(2J+9/2)/R, \\\\\n\\lVert K_f-K_g\\rVert\\le 3m\\lvert f-g\\rvert/R.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{4}\n$$\n\nR is registered in the same natural-unit time/length convention as the spatial Dirac calculation; interpreting the Hamiltonian in joules uses $\\hbar$ K and the independently fixed length-to-time conversion. The physical second of the observation window is unchanged.\n\nOn the full ROM/matter carrier define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_{\\mathrm{ROM}}(t)&=\\sum_\\omega f_\\omega(t)|\\omega\\rangle\\langle \\omega|, \\\\\nH_{\\mathrm{drv}}(t)&=\\hbar[I_{\\mathrm{ROM}} \\otimes K_0+F_{\\mathrm{ROM}}(t) \\otimes d\\Gamma(V^J)] \\\\\n&\\quad +E_{\\mathrm{ref}} I.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n$$\n\nHere $f_{\\omega}$ is the exact clipped polygon from that record, or an explicitly registered smooth approximation. The ROM projectors commute with (5), so the ROM is never overwritten. All controls and their generally global supports are displayed; no spatial edge-local compiler is asserted. Choosing\n\n$$\nE_{\\mathrm{ref}}=\\hbar m(2J+9/2)/R\n$$\n\nmakes (5) nonnegative; the source's ordinary cell baseline is added and charged separately. The scalar shift does not change any channel but remains a genuine energy-reference entry. Its matrix is finite and Hermitian, and its continuous piecewise-linear coefficients give a unique unitary propagator. This proves the **specified driven extension** mathematically. Installing the couplings (5) directly is additional source hardware, not inferred merely from the existing gate menu.\n\nFor a classical ROM preparation, the exact endpoint channel is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(\\rho)&=\\sum_\\omega p_\\omega |\\omega\\rangle\\langle \\omega| \\otimes \\\\\n&\\quad U_\\omega \\rho U_\\omega^{\\dagger},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n$$\n\nwith $U_{\\omega}$ generated by (3) on the unit interval. Equation (6) is CPTP for every matter input, including one entangled with an arbitrary reference. It retains the original word distribution exactly. The ideal one-body propagator obeys\n\n$$\n\\lVert U_f^{(1)}(t)-U_g^{(1)}(t)\\rVert\\le (3/R)\\int_0^t\\lvert f-g\\rvert.\n$$\n\nOn the full Fock space the correct bound is instead\n\n$$\n\\lVert U_f(t)-U_g(t)\\rVert\\le (3m/R)\\int_0^t\\lvert f-g\\rvert,\n\\tag{7}\n$$\n\nand the unitary-channel diamond error is at most twice this. These follow by differentiating $U_f(t,s)U_g(s,0)$, integrating, and using unitarity. The factor $m$ cannot be omitted when claiming a full-CAR channel bound. On a fixed $k$-particle sector it can be replaced by $k$.\n\nEvery actual finite $f_{\\omega}$ is Lipschitz with a known stage-uniform bound, for example $M/r^{2}$ since every projected jump magnitude is less than $1/r^{2}$. Its degree-$n$ Bernstein polynomial $g_{\\omega}$ preserves $[0,1]$ and both endpoint values, with\n\n$$\n\\lVert g_{\\omega}-f_{\\omega}\\rVert_{\\infty}\\le (M/r^{2})/(2\\sqrt{n}).\n\\tag{8}\n$$\n\nIndeed the Bernstein polynomial is $\\mathbb{E}[f_{\\omega}(\\mathrm{Bin}(n,t)/n)]$ and Cauchy-Schwarz bounds the expected displacement by $1/(2\\sqrt{n})$. This supplies a constructive smooth coefficient approximation and, through (7), a same-clock propagator error. It does not flatten endpoint collars, and no such flattening is needed by the interval theorem. The original recorded $f$ process can remain the exact readout while the approximate drive and its error are separately recorded.\n\n#### 4 Vacuum and normal-ordering record\n\nChoose the matter preparation to fill precisely the negative $A_0^J$ modes in the fixed free-mode basis. Their number is\n\n$$\nq_J=n_-(A_0^J)=(2/3)(L+1)(L+2)(L+3).\n\\tag{9}\n$$\n\nIt is an ordinary fixed occupation bit word in that basis; $q_{J}$ payload X preparations suffice. The basis and Hamiltonian matrices are declared before selecting a random word. This is the filled negative sea, not the empty CAR vacuum. Its occupation, preparation stock and energy remain owned.\n\nThe ideal quadratic channel (3) commutes with total occupation $N$. On the endpoint event $f(1)=1$, the already proved cutoff spectral count gives $n_-(A_1^J)=q_J-1$. Hence the final normal-ordered number $N-n_-(A_1^J)$ equals $+1$ exactly on the initial $q_{J}$ number sector, without an adiabatic limit. This is a reference-vacuum comparison, not creation of bare CAR number or a complete Standard-Model anomaly/CP transport claim.\n\nThe native endpoint event selects an oriented branch. A CP-symmetric mixture with its conjugate protocol may cancel the signed result. The microscopic gauge-endpoint cutoff defect and all filled-sea resources are still retained.\n\n#### 5 A terminating compiler using only admitted pulses\n\nThere is a genuine complex-unitary universal set already inside the source leaves. Let $S=Q^{3}=\\operatorname{diag}(1,i)$. Controlled-$S$ is implemented exactly by\n\nToffoli(control,target,clean scratch),\n$S$(scratch),\nToffoli(control,target,clean scratch).\n\nThe scratch returns exactly to zero on all inputs. This is five named primitive pulses if S is implemented by three Q pulses; its inverse uses one Q and the same two Toffolis. The set consisting of H and controlled-S is strictly universal for complex unitaries on at least two qubits, in operator norm. This is Theorem 1 of Aharonov (2003), which explicitly distinguishes strict universality from real-amplitude computational simulation.\n\nHere $m\\ge 4$, so the finite CAR carrier is within that theorem's dimensional range. To compile a target propagator, proceed as follows.\n\n1. Fix $r,J$, a finite rational checkpoint list $0=t_0<\\dots<t_s=1$, and tolerance $\\epsilon>0$ before running the source. For every finitely many $\\omega$ and each interval compute the target finite propagator to certified precision. Its piecewise-affine coefficient has rational breakpoints and algebraic matrix coefficients. A truncated Dyson/Picard expansion on each piece has an explicit norm remainder bounded by $\\exp(BT)(BT)^{p+1}/(p+1)!$, where $B$ bounds $\\lVert K\\rVert$. Its entries are therefore computable with certified errors. A separately supplied computable radius $R$ is sufficient; no target physical observable is an input to the search.\n2. Remove only the common irrelevant scalar phase to obtain an $SU(2^m)$ target. This phase is independent of $\\omega$: $\\operatorname{tr}(A_f^J)=-3m/(2R)$, and $\\operatorname{tr}(d\\Gamma(A_f^J))/2^m=\\operatorname{tr}(A_f^J)/2=-3m/(4R)$. Thus the scalar phase is the same on all ROM branches and checkpoint intervals of the same duration.\n3. Enumerate finite words in $H$, controlled-$S$ and their inverses, with all addressed supports, while dovetailing rational precision. Accept the first word for which a certified Frobenius-norm upper bound on the error is less than $\\epsilon/s$. This is a decidable strict acceptance test. Strict operator-norm universality implies arbitrarily small Frobenius error in the fixed finite dimension, so a word with positive certificate margin exists and the search terminates. No claim of practical complexity is needed.\n4. Replace every controlled-$S$ by its explicit admitted-pulse realization. Store the complete finite word catalogue in owned ROM. Copy the ROM label into fresh work payloads with ROM-controlled CNOTs. Perform all complements and exact reversible $X$/Toffoli equality tests on those work payloads, never on the original ROM, then execute that label's word under its computed enable bit. Uncompute the work test and uncopy it with the original ROM as control. Every primitive generator commutes with the original ROM word projectors, including during its positive-duration pulse. Controlled-$H$ is already a leaf. Controlled phase and higher-controlled classical gates use clean AND/uncompute with the admitted $Q$ and Toffoli pulses. Preserve every original record and clean scratch. Pad words to one stage-dependent finite common length.\n5. Execute each padded interval programme inside its unchanged physical interval $[t_i,t_{i+1}]$. If $Q_{i}$ primitive slots are assigned to an interval of length $\\Delta_{i}$, give each slot the positive duration $\\Delta_{i}/Q_{i}$. The admitted pulse generators have the corresponding finite strength; no minimum-duration or bounded-power premise is invented. All extra controller and programme slots count toward $Q_{i}$.\n\nTelescoping at the completed checkpoints gives operator error at most $\\epsilon$ and complete conditional-channel diamond error at most 2epsilon. The same bound holds with arbitrary matter/reference input. For a fixed finite registered history of read instruments, include their owned dilations and pointer records in the compiled sequence; telescoping then bounds the complete transcript channel, rather than inferring histories from a final marginal. An arbitrary unregistered intervention between primitive pulses is outside this result.\n\nImportantly the **actual physical Hamiltonian of this compiled realization** is the admitted finite sequence of named pulse generators. During a word it need not approximate (5), conserve N, or follow a Gaussian state path. At the selected completed checkpoints it approximates the specified Dirac channel with the above bound. Thus this is a physical finite-CPTP approximation, not an identity of instantaneous generators or of classical actions.\n\nFor the filled $q_{J}$ initial sector, a final operator error $\\epsilon$ implies probability at most $\\epsilon^{2}$ of leaving that number sector, and normal-ordered-number expectation differs from $+1$ by at most $m\\epsilon^{2}$ on the endpoint branch. Exact number conservation and exact $+1$ apply to (3), not automatically to the approximating pulse word. One can choose $\\epsilon_r=\\exp(-r^{2})$ before comparison; the resulting uniform channel error is superexponential at native speed $r$. The classical word and scalar response laws remain exact at every stage because the ROM is never modified.\n\n#### 6 A finite paid inventory and clock\n\nLet B0 be the number of logical bits in the complete original source preparation, immutable seed/outcome/counter record, m occupation bits, clean arithmetic/workspace and all compiled word labels before programme addresses are installed. Let $Q_{p}$ be the primitive count for preparing seeds and reversible records, excluding the $q_{J}$ occupation X pulses counted separately. Let Q be the complete padded observation primitive count, including enable tests, control synthesis, cursor, read pointers and uncomputations. Let $L_{\\mathrm{cat}}$ be the total stored target-word catalogue length. Each is a finite integer produced by the terminating constructor above.\n\nFor an explicit conservative carrier bound put\n\n$$\nZ=B_0+Q_p+Q+L_{\\mathrm{cat}}+100,\\qquad N_{\\mathrm{cells}}=2^{Z^2}.\n$$\n\nOne payload bit per whole C8 cell accommodates every listed register and a full addressed programme of at most $(Q_p+q_J+Q)[6Z^{2}+100]$ bits ($q_J\\le m\\le B_0$). The exponential bound dominates these terms and resolves the address-width bookkeeping without a circular hidden programme. Padding cells remain real owned cells. There are $3N_{\\mathrm{cells}}$ physical qubits and $N_{\\mathrm{cells}}$ cubic-centimetre cell volumes on the admitted ideal cabinet branch. This is not a physical $S^3$ spatial embedding theorem.\n\nUse the admitted per-cell baseline $e_b=\\hbar/(\\text{one second})$, and count it throughout preparation and observation. Install programme bits and perform the $Q_{p}$ preparation pulses at explicitly positive durations, for example one second each. A conservative preparation window is $(N_{\\mathrm{cells}}+Q_p+q_J)$ seconds. The entire carrier is retained through that window and the one-second observation; no random seed, filled mode, cursor or used pointer is erased or reused without charge. Initial ready stock is the source's two pure bits and one maximally mixed degenerate core bit per cell, with the same $2N_{\\mathrm{cells}}\\ln 2/\\beta$ supplied free-energy inventory. This is preparation stock, not a finite-bath exact-reset claim.\n\nIf the observation slots have durations $\\tau_{a}>0$, their admitted pulse norms are at most $\\pi\\hbar/\\tau_a$ and their total gross on/off work is bounded by $2\\pi\\hbar\\sum_a 1/\\tau_a$. For equal padding to $Q$ slots on the unit interval these become $\\pi\\hbar Q$ and $2\\pi\\hbar Q^{2}$. Checkpoint intervals can be padded to equal rational slots after a common refinement. Installation/preparation pulses and their gross work are added separately. Large amplitudes and diverging refinement resources are explicit, not claimed bounded.\n\nIf the continuous extension (5) is used instead of the pulse compiler, its baseline $E_{\\mathrm{ref}}$, every quadratic support, ROM-controlled coupling and drive are charged. Its coefficient variation gives the finite bound\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int \\lVert \\partial_t H_{\\mathrm{drv}}\\rVert\\,dt \\\\\n&\\le (3\\hbar m/R) \\int \\max_\\omega |\\partial_t f_\\omega|\\,dt \\\\\n&\\le 3\\hbar m M/(R r^2).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{10}\n$$\n\nwith any external switching and preparation added. For a fixed conditional word its corresponding block has the smaller variation bound $(3\\hbar m/R)\\operatorname{TV}(f_{\\omega})$; the maximum must stay inside the integral for the full controlled Hamiltonian. Equation (10) is a bound on that registered drive, not a deduction of physical work from the probability rate. The full Fourier-clock regulator can make an autonomous approximation with its additional positive baseline, clock stock and channel error; it cannot be inserted for free or called an exact finite semigroup.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q01w-owned-coefficient-input-yang-mills-selector-and-full-normalized-boundary-law\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.1w (Owned Coefficient-Input Yang-Mills Selector and Full Normalized Boundary Law).**\n\n#### 1 One proposal law, one observed second, one retained word\n\nUse precisely the full native proposal in Theorems Q.0.1t and Q.0.1u: $N=B^{T}B$, $e$ the marked pair, $b=Be$, $b^{T}b=77$, $\\ell=Ne/77$, and the rational projector channels $v_{sj}=P_s e_j$, $\\lambda_s=(2156,392,40)$. At integer stage $r$, signed jumps are $\\pm v_{sj}/r^{2}$ and the ideal comparison rates are $r^{3}/(2\\lambda_s)$. Its admitted finite law uses $M=2^{r^{2}+20}$ ticks on $[0,1]$, $b_{\\mathrm{src}}=4\\log_2(M)+r+20$ fair seed bits per row, dyadic channel probabilities, and a last-tick no-event guard. Every original record is retained.\n\nThe complete source word $\\omega$ is prepared before observation by the existing exact finite fair-bit/reversible compiler. Its proposal law $p_r(\\omega)$ is exact. It supplies polygonal $X_r$, $Y_r=\\ell^{T}X_r$, and $f_r=c(Y_r)$, where $c(u)=\\min(1,\\max(0,u))$. There are no interior pins in this protocol. In particular, it is not the separately conditioned midpoint/multipin law. Endpoint selection is performed by the new comparator below on this same unconditioned word. The actual clock remains one second. If the original small-noise parameter is required at every real $\\eta>0$ rather than only $\\eta=1/r$, use $r(\\eta)=\\lceil 1/\\eta\\rceil$; $\\eta\\,r(\\eta)\\to 1$, so the same laws have the same rate at speed $1/\\eta$ without any physical clock change.\n\nThe proved proposal LDP is good in the uniform topology, with scalar clipped rate\n\n$$\nJ_a[f]=a \\int |f'|^{2},\\quad f \\in H^{1},\\quad 0\\le f\\le 1,\\quad f(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $a=a_0$ for the native source. Its exact endpoint subprobability on $E=\\{f(1)=1\\}$ has the full relative LDP with $J_{a}$ restricted to $E$. Its endpoint lower proof uses raw paths $y_{\\delta}=f+\\delta t$: $y_{\\delta}(1)>1$, $c(y_{\\delta})\\to f$ uniformly, and $J_a[y_{\\delta}]\\to J_a[f]$. No midpoint pin is invoked or violated. The projector construction, boundary arithmetic and no-last-event guard are unchanged.\n\n#### 2 Physical coefficients are inputs, not fitted normalizations\n\nFix positive computable physical radius $R$ (numerically expressed in the already registered one-second length/time units), coupling $g$, and an independently supplied action unit/calibration $\\kappa$. Fix them before selecting source rates. In the normalization of the spatial calculation, put $C=12\\pi^{2}/g^{2}$; if that displayed action is already dimensionless, $\\kappa$ is the numerical value of the supplied action unit in the same convention. The desired homogeneous physical functional is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{\\mathrm{YM}}[f]/\\kappa &= a \\int |f'|^{2} + \\beta \\int V(f), \\\\\na &= C R/\\kappa,\\quad \\beta = 4 C/(\\kappa R).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe exact connection is $A_f=f\\,g_{\\mathrm{map}}^{-1}\\,dg_{\\mathrm{map}}$ on $[0,1]\\times S^3_R$, with $g_{\\mathrm{map}}$ the degree-one identity map. Its physical field strength, fundamental trace and spatial volume produce this action; the potential is not introduced as a new definition of Yang–Mills.\n\nThere are two explicitly different, internally coherent source choices:\n\n1. **Native proposal retained:** $a=a_0$. Literal physical equality holds only if the independently supplied data already obey $CR/\\kappa=a_0$; $\\beta$ is then $4C/(\\kappa R)$. A mismatch is reported, not repaired by changing $R$, $g$, $\\kappa$, the endpoint or the clock.\n2. **Physically parameterized changed proposal:** set $\\alpha=a_0/a=77\\kappa/(2CR)$, and multiply every native signed proposal rate by $\\alpha$. This is a changed source intensity, not a time change and not a different source for a later proof step. The same one-word preparation, filter, endpoint test, vacuum fallback and Dirac drive all use this $\\alpha$-source. Its scalar rate is exactly $J_{a}$.\n\nFor completeness the second construction needs no real-probability oracle. Choose fixed rational bounds $0<\\alpha_-<\\alpha<\\alpha_+$ and positive rational $\\alpha_r$ in $[\\alpha_-,\\alpha_+]$ with $|\\alpha_r-\\alpha|\\le\\exp(-r^{2})$. Choose a fixed integer $s_{\\alpha}$ so $8\\alpha_+r^{3}/2^{r^{2}+20+s_{\\alpha}}<1/2$ for all $r\\ge 1$; for example a sufficiently large $s_{\\alpha}$ with $2^{s_{\\alpha}}\\ge\\max(1,\\alpha_+)$ works with the original slack. Use $M=2^{r^{2}+20+s_{\\alpha}}$. For this changed proposal add a fixed integer precision offset $c_{\\alpha}\\ge 0$ with $2^{c_{\\alpha}}\\alpha_-\\ge 2\\max_s\\lambda_s$, and use $b_{\\mathrm{src}}=4\\log_2(M)+r+20+c_{\\alpha}$ fair bits per row before dyadically rounding $\\delta r^{3}\\alpha_r/(2\\lambda_s)$. Every nonzero channel then has a positive dyadic probability at every stage, including arbitrarily small $\\alpha$. This offset does not change the native $\\alpha=1$ packet; it is part of the explicitly changed coefficient-input source. All rows and the entire finite word remain literal binary data. A Poisson coupling bounds the additional source-intensity mismatch by $r^{3}D|\\alpha_r-\\alpha|$, which is superexponential at speed $r$. The old rounding/multiple-proposal/no-last-event estimates hold with their constants multiplied by fixed bounds on $\\alpha$. Hence the implemented law is exponentially equivalent to the $\\alpha$-source.\n\nIts speed-$r$ Hamiltonian converges to $\\alpha p^{T}N^{-1}p/2$. Legendre transformation and the same scalar contraction give the full rate $(1/(2\\alpha))\\int x'^{T}Nx'$ and $J_{a}$ for the scalar, since $77/(2\\alpha)=a$. Exact endpoint accessibility is unaffected because all the changed finite channel rows remain positive. More directly, the clipped endpoint relative LDP used here needs no new exact lattice-entropy argument: its upper bound follows from the closed clipped endpoint fiber, and its lower bound uses the same open raw overshoots $y_{\\delta}=f+\\delta t$ in the full $\\alpha$-source scalar LDP. Their kinetic rates converge to $J_a[f]$. Thus the entire endpoint-filter proof uses this one $\\alpha$-source law.\n\nChoosing $\\alpha$ from known physical kinetic data engineers a source representation of that specified action. It does not derive those physical data, $\\kappa=\\hbar$, a preferred radius, or the value $77/2$ from the source. No parameter is solved backwards from a desired transition minimum.\n\n#### 3 A literal fair-bit success instrument\n\nFor each finite word $\\omega$ calculate\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh(\\omega)&=\\int_0^1 V(c(Y_\\omega(t)))\\, dt, \\\\\nH(\\omega)&=\\beta h(\\omega),\\quad w_r(\\omega)=\\exp[-r H(\\omega)].\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe clipped polygon has rational vertices and rational added clipping breakpoints. Integrating a quartic polynomial on each affine segment computes $h$ exactly as a rational. It satisfies $0\\le h\\le 1/16$. This calculation is on the actual complete native word, not on its minimizing path. For computable $\\beta$ the desired $w$ is computable to any certified finite precision.\n\nFix a rational $B\\ge\\beta/16$ and set\n\n$$\nn_r = r^{2} + \\lceil 2 r B\\rceil + 20,\\quad d_r=2^{-n_r}.\n$$\n\nCompute a rational approximation to $w$ within $d_r/4$ and round it to a dyadic multiple $q_r(\\omega)=k_r(\\omega)/2^{n_r}$ in $[0,1]$, with absolute error at most $2d_r$. If $h=0$ set $k=2^{n_r}$ exactly. Clamping a rounded value to the unit interval does not increase the error. Since $w\\ge\\exp(-rB)$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|q_r/w_r - 1| &\\le \\theta_r := 2 d_r \\exp(rB) \\\\\n&\\le 2^{-r^{2}-19}, \\\\\n|\\log q_r + r H| &\\le 2 \\theta_r.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final logarithmic estimate uses $\\theta_{r}<1/2$. Every q is positive. This is uniform over all finite source words, including rare endpoint words. The error is superexponential at speed r. No additive-error estimate is divided by an unknown exponentially small event.\n\nPrepare $n_{r}$ independent fair bits $u$ in fresh C8 payloads. Reversibly compare their integer value with $k_r(\\omega)$, and write the result in a fresh success pointer $S$. Computational measurement is the admitted source instrument. Its conditional success probability is exactly $q_r(\\omega)$; its failure probability is exactly $1-q_r(\\omega)$. The two CP branches sum to a trace-preserving instrument on the complete source, seeds and pointers. The original $\\omega$ is never overwritten.\n\nThis is an actual Feynman–Kac selector: its ideal weight is the product of $\\exp[-r\\int_{\\mathrm{segment}}\\beta V(f)]$ over the same physical interval. It is implemented as one owned preparatory comparison of the complete word. It is not falsely represented as unconditional matter evolution or fresh online noise. A segmentwise fair-bit survival implementation could instead use additional independent seeds and an AND of the survival flags, with precision increased by $\\log_2(M)+O(1)$, but is unnecessary here and is not substituted into the one-word proof.\n\n#### 4 Endpoint test, vacuum fallback, and full normalized law\n\nCompute the exact additional bit $E_r=[Y_r(1)\\ge 1]$, equivalently $[f_r(1)=1]$, from the original rational counters. Put $A_r=S\\text{ AND }E_{r}$. Both bits and every failure record are retained.\n\nBefore $t=0$ the supplier prepares the entire word, computes the selector and endpoint bits, and fixes the field drive to\n\n$$\nF_r(t)=\\begin{cases} f_r(t) & \\text{if } A_r=1, \\\\ 0 & \\text{if } A_r=0. \\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThus a failed attempt actually drives the vacuum throughout the observed second. It is not a retrospectively erased portion of a partly executed non-vacuum history. A registered field-output instrument may also report $A_{r}$ at its final read; the theorem below uses just the continuous field coordinate. All raw ROM coordinates remain physically owned but are not asserted to be coordinates of this deliberately coarse-grained field output. Different raw histories with the same F are not declared gauge-equivalent raw sources.\n\nFor any field set D the exact output probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nu_r(D)&=\\sum_\\omega p_r(\\omega) q_r(\\omega) 1_E(\\omega) 1_D(f_\\omega) \\\\\n&\\quad+ (1-Q_r) 1_D(0), \\\\\nQ_r&=\\sum_\\omega p_r(\\omega) q_r(\\omega) 1_E(\\omega).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a normalized probability measure. The complementary term includes both selector rejection and endpoint rejection. There is no q-dependent state reset and no denominator.\n\nLet $C_0=C([0,1],[0,1])$ with initial value 0 and uniform topology. Define the closed admitted history domain\n\n$$\n\\Gamma=\\{\\text{the constant vacuum path } 0\\} \\cup E.\n$$\n\nThis is a source protocol boundary restriction fixed before any asymptotics. The two components are separated by uniform distance at least 1. The same definition applies at every finite stage. The full good LDP on the ordinary ambient C0 has rate\n\n$$\nK[f]=\\begin{cases} 0, & f \\text{ identically } 0; \\\\ J_a[f]+\\beta \\int V(f), & f \\in E \\cap H^{1}; \\\\ +\\infty, & \\text{otherwise}. \\end{cases}\n$$\n\n**Proof.** The known exact-endpoint subprobability has its relative good LDP with rate $J_a$. $H(f)=\\beta\\int V(f)$ is bounded and uniformly continuous. Local upper/lower LDP bounds multiplied by $\\exp(-rH)$, followed by finite compact covers and exponential tightness, give the weighted subprobability rate $J_a+H$ on $E$. The uniform multiplicative comparator bound preserves it. In particular $Q_{r}$ has exponent $-m$, where $m=\\inf_E(J_a+H)\\ge a>0$. Hence $1-Q_r$ tends to 1 and has rate zero. Adding its atom at 0 gives the stated LDP by the finite union bound for closed sets and by considering whichever component meets a given open set. $E$ is closed in $C_0$, so its relative closed subsets are ambient closed; exponential tightness is inherited from the proposal because weights are at most 1, together with the one added compact atom. The rate is good because $J_{a}$ is good and $H$ is nonnegative continuous. The rate is infinity at every other path because a sufficiently small uniform neighborhood separates it from both support components; $\\Gamma$ is closed. This proves the total normalized law, not a law conditioned on $A_{r}=1$. QED.\n\nOn $\\Gamma$ this is exactly the local homogeneous action: $K[f]=\\int[a|f'|^{2}+\\beta V(f)]$, including the vacuum where it vanishes. Its extension outside the **declared** admissible boundary domain is infinity. This is not a claim that the scalar observation is an unconstrained Markov diffusion whose finite local Lagrangian assigns this rate to every scalar path. The latent finite ROM/cursor update is the actual finite ND-RID dynamics; the retained field is the specified output. The prescribed boundary-domain restriction must be retained in every Q.0.1c use.\n\n#### 5 Minimum, exact endpoint tube, and symmetry\n\nThe direct method gives an attained minimum $m(a,\\beta)$ on $E$: bounded action gives an $H^1$ bound, uniform compactness, weak $H^1$ lower semicontinuity of kinetic energy, and uniform continuity of the bounded potential. Every admissible path joins 0 to 1.\n\nA quantitative strict gap is elementary. Write $f(t)=t+g(t)$, $s^{2}=\\int|g'|^{2}$, with $g(0)=g(1)=0$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int |f'|^{2}&=1+s^{2}, \\\\\n|g(t)|&\\le\\sqrt{t(1-t)}\\, s\\le s/2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn $[0,1]$, $V$ has Lipschitz constant $L=1/(3\\sqrt{3})$, and $\\int_0^1 V(t)\\,dt=1/30$. Thus $\\int V(f)\\ge 1/30-Ls/2$. If $s^{2}\\le 3/100=1/(900L^{2})$, the potential integral is at least $1/60$. Otherwise the kinetic excess is at least $3a/100$. Therefore\n\n$$\nm(a,\\beta)\\ge a+\\min(\\beta/60,3a/100)>a.\n$$\n\nThere is also a complete global characterization, without numerical shooting. There is a unique $E_0>0$ solving\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{a} \\int_0^1 [E_0+\\beta V(u)]^{-1/2}\\,du=1.\n$$\n\nThe left side decreases strictly from infinity to zero, using $V(u)\\sim u^{2}$ at the endpoint. Define $f_{*}$ implicitly by\n\n$$\nt=\\sqrt{a} \\int_0^{f_{*}(t)} [E_0+\\beta V(u)]^{-1/2}\\,du.\n$$\n\nIt is smooth, strictly increasing, and has exact endpoints. For any absolutely continuous competitor,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na f'^{2}+\\beta V(f) \\\\\n&=a \\left(|f'|-\\sqrt{(E_0+\\beta V(f))/a}\\right)^{2} \\\\\n&\\quad+2 \\sqrt{a} \\sqrt{E_0+\\beta V(f)}\\, |f'|-E_0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integrated weighted total variation is at least its endpoint integral. Equality holds precisely on this increasing solution, proving uniqueness and\n\n$$\nm=2 \\sqrt{a} \\int_0^1 \\sqrt{E_0+\\beta V(u)}\\,du-E_0.\n$$\n\nThe potential symmetry proves $f_{*}(1-t)=1-f_{*}(t)$, hence $f_{*}(1/2)=1/2$ and equal half-actions $m/2$. The latter is an action symmetry, not a physical CP or exact baryon prefactor theorem. The minimizer has nonzero endpoint velocities $\\sqrt{E_0/a}$, so it does not acquire forbidden connection collars. The linear path is not stationary when $\\beta>0$; the previously displayed deformation lowers its action, giving $m<a+\\beta/30$.\n\nAn actual **ambient** transition tube is, for example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT_\\delta&=\\{f \\in C_0: \\lVert f-f_{*}\\rVert_\\infty<\\delta \\text{ and } f(1)>1/2\\}, \\\\\n&0<\\delta<1/2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is open in C0. Its closure excludes the vacuum. Every finite-stage output in this tube has endpoint exactly 1, because all non-vacuum outputs come from the endpoint-success branch. Every finite-rate path in its closure belongs to E and has action at least m; f_* lies in its interior and attains m. For the closed initial basin {0} and closed terminal basin [1/2,1],\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\inf_{T_\\delta^{\\mathrm{interior}}} K &= \\inf_{\\operatorname{closure}(T_\\delta)} K \\\\\n&= \\inf_{f(0)=0,\\, f(1) \\in [1/2,1]} K = m, \\\\\n\\lim_r (1/r) \\log \\nu_r(T_\\delta)&=-m.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is exactly the interior/closure/minimum condition, while successful physical gauge endpoints remain 0 and 1. We do not claim that E itself has ambient interior. The larger tube contains off-support intermediate-endpoint paths, but those have infinite source rate and zero finite-stage probability. This is why the explicit vacuum-fallback source change matters.\n\n#### 6 Normalization, repeat attempts, and what the source law does not say\n\nThere are three distinct normalizations:\n\n- Filter alone: $Z_r=\\mathbb{E}[q_r(\\omega)]$ has rate 0, since $\\inf(J_a+H)=0$ at the unpinned vacuum path. Its normalized unpinned selected law has the same rate $J_a+H$, although its exact finite normalization is not generally 1.\n- Filter plus endpoint: $Q_{r}$ has rate $m$. Its normalized successful bridge has rate $I_{\\beta}-m$. That subtraction is never called the absolute Yang–Mills action.\n- Full output with vacuum fallback: $\\nu_{r}$ is already normalized and its transition probability has absolute rate $m$, as proved above. The failure probability has rate 0.\n\nIf one demands a successful transition on demand, independent fresh attempts require mean attempt count $Q_r^{-1}=\\exp[rm+o(r)]$. A finite $L$-attempt bank fails with probability $(1-Q_r)^L$. It cannot guarantee success. To make this at most $\\delta$, take $L\\ge\\log(\\delta)/\\log(1-Q_r)$, rounded up, using the exactly computable finite dyadic $Q_{r}$. Parallel attempts multiply the owned carrier, pure stock, installation and record budget; serial attempts multiply preparatory time and use fresh owned banks unless a separately supplied reset protocol is admitted. Retain the all-failed output. Amplification changes the unconditional transition probability and therefore cannot preserve the old rare-transition exponent while claiming near-certain delivery. No repetition or reset is used in the present one-shot construction.\n\n#### 7 Same field and finite Dirac supplier, with all resources paid\n\nFor each actual accepted word use its own $f_{\\omega}$ in the existing no-collar connection and operator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_f&=-R^{-1}(2 \\sigma \\cdot J+3/2+f\\, \\sigma \\cdot \\tau), \\\\\nD_X^{+} &\\text{ identified with } \\partial_t+A_f, \\\\\n&\\text{initial trace in } H_{<0}(A_0),\\ \\text{final trace in } H_{\\ge 0}(A_1).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese are precisely the previously proved transported projectors and Clifford sign; they are not replaced by the intrinsic $B$ family. Successful endpoints are the same gauge-equivalent $A_0,A_1$, and the entire endpoint fiber has primitive interval index $+1$ and Chern–Weil number 1. The vacuum fallback has constant $A_{0}$ and index 0. At finite CAR cutoff $J$ use the same full $m_J$-mode CAR target, filled negative $A_{0}$ sea, and physical C8 checkpoint compiler. The programme is now controlled by the single complete record $(\\omega,u,A_r)$; its target coefficient is $f_{\\omega}$ or 0 as specified in Section 4. No independently prepared word is used for the action, index or driver.\n\nFor every registered finite transcript choose the existing uniform per-word channel error $\\epsilon_r=\\exp(-r^{2})$, accounting for the full Fock norm and all readout dilations. The classical pointer law and $F_{r}$ readout remain exact. The uniform conditional quantum bound survives rare selection without division by $Q_{r}$. The old autonomous Fourier-clock supplier may also be used only with its actual enlarged-support, positive-baseline and clock-stock ledger; it is not needed for this finite driven construction. Neither compiler makes its instantaneous microscopic Hamiltonian or switching work equal to Yang–Mills action.\n\nThe new selector uses only fresh Hadamard bits, exact Boolean comparisons built from $X$/CNOT/Toffoli, AND, pointer copies, and the admitted computational instrument. For a completely explicit terminating implementation, tabulate $k_r(\\omega)$ and $E_{r}$ over the finite original seed words. A truth-table XOR circuit for the total Boolean function $(\\omega,u)\\to A_r$ uses at most $2^d(4d+1)$ elementary comparison/uncompute slots before controlled-slot padding, with $d$ the finite input bit count, and $O(d)$ clean workspace. No unknown real gate is required. A more efficient arithmetic circuit is optional. Failed inputs and invalid states receive specified total default updates.\n\nLet $B_{\\mathrm{log}}$ count every original seed, snapshot, outcome and raw counter, the $n_{r}$ selector seeds, selector and endpoint pointers, old and new ROM tables, every arithmetic/workspace bit, all matter occupations and driver labels. Let $Q_{\\mathrm{pre}}$ and $Q_{\\mathrm{obs}}$ be the complete finite padded preparation and observation gate counts, and $L_{\\mathrm{cat}}$ the total programme catalogue length before physical addresses. Put\n\n$$\nZ=B_{\\mathrm{log}}+Q_{\\mathrm{pre}}+Q_{\\mathrm{obs}}+L_{\\mathrm{cat}}+100,\\quad N_{\\mathrm{cells}}=2^{Z^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe earlier whole-cell address bound applies after these additions, not with the old numerical cell count. All $d$-bit truth tables, gate addresses, one-hot cursors, clean scratch, failed-seed archives, pointers and padding fit in this conservative finite bound. Each bit uses the payload of one whole three-qubit C8 cell. The two qualifying core bits remain intact. The physical inventory is $3N_{\\mathrm{cells}}$ qubits and $N_{\\mathrm{cells}}$ cubic-centimetre cabinet volumes, with $N_{\\mathrm{cells}}\\,\\hbar/(\\text{one second})$ baseline throughout.\n\nInstallation has $W\\le N_{\\mathrm{cells}}$ $X$ pulses. Give every installation and preparation pulse one second, so $T_{\\mathrm{pre}}=(W+Q_{\\mathrm{pre}})$ seconds, followed by the unchanged one-second observation. Retain the entire carrier for $T_{\\mathrm{pre}}+1$; its qubit-time is $3N_{\\mathrm{cells}}(T_{\\mathrm{pre}}+1)$. Ready stock is two pure bits per cell and one maximally mixed zero-Hamiltonian core bit, with the admitted $2N_{\\mathrm{cells}}\\ln 2/\\beta_{\\mathrm{thermal}}$ supplied free-energy inventory. This is fresh initial stock, not a finite-bath exact reset theorem. Nothing is erased or cyclically reused.\n\nFor observation slots $\\tau_{j}>0$ summing to one second, admitted pulse norms are bounded by $\\pi\\hbar/\\tau_j$ and gross switching work by $2\\pi\\hbar\\sum_j(1/\\tau_j)$. Equal padding gives peak $\\pi\\hbar Q_{\\mathrm{obs}}$ and gross $2\\pi\\hbar Q_{\\mathrm{obs}}^{2}$ in the chosen second units. Installation/preparation add their actual pulses and gross work, bounded by $2\\pi\\hbar(W+Q_{\\mathrm{pre}})$ in those units. Catalogue fabrication and drive service are the explicit original ideal source leaves, not free manufacturing. Growing power, stock, geometry, preparation time and archive are stated rather than claimed bounded.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q01ac-one-finite-coherent-source-for-rare-entry-histories-and-no-jump-response\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.1ac (One Finite Coherent Source for Rare-Entry Histories and No-Jump Response).** Fix a finite directed acyclic graph $(V,E)$, initial vertex $v_0$, horizon $T>0$, nonnegative dimensionless tolls $a_e$, positive attempt rates $k_e$ in inverse seconds, a diagonal phase generator $H=\\operatorname{diag}(E_i)$, and $\\hbar>0$. These data are chosen before the requested response. Put\n$$\nr_e(n)=k_ee^{-na_e},\\qquad R_i(n)=\\sum_{e:i\\to j}r_e(n),\\qquad\n\\Lambda=\\max_i\\sum_{e:i\\to j}k_e.\n\\tag{Q.0.1ac.1}\n$$\nEvery missing edge has rate zero at every refinement. The finite instrument below has the same diagonal rare-entry history law, its coherent no-jump extension and a quantitative reduced-system GKSL limit. It supplies an explicit finite driven realization for each $n$, with its outcome stocks and control scales counted. It does not impose a bounded-resource continuum realization.\n\n*Proof and construction.*\n\n##### I. One clock, exact instrument and superexponential digital approximation\n\nChoose a time unit $t_{\\rm ref}>0$, let $M_n=\\lceil(T/t_{\\rm ref})e^{n^2}\\rceil$, increasing it if necessary so $\\delta_n=T/M_n$ and $\\Lambda\\delta_n\\le1/2$. On $\\mathbb C^{|V|}$ use\n$$\nK_0=\\operatorname{diag}\\left(e^{-iE_i\\delta_n/\\hbar}\\sqrt{1-\\delta_nR_i}\\right),\\qquad\nK_e=\\sqrt{\\delta_nr_e}\\,|j\\rangle\\langle i|\\quad(e:i\\to j).\n\\tag{Q.0.1ac.2}\n$$\nTheir squared Kraus sum is exactly $I$. Each tick has a fresh outcome register with alphabet $\\{0\\}\\cup E$. For history $h=(\\alpha_1,\\ldots,\\alpha_{M_n})$, put $C_h=K_{\\alpha_{M_n}}\\cdots K_{\\alpha_1}$ and $p_n(h)=\\operatorname{Tr}(C_h\\rho C_h^*)$. Classical rare-entry statements take $\\rho=|v_0\\rangle\\langle v_0|$; coherent statements allow every density matrix and arbitrary spectators.\n\nFix $b_0\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}$ and choose $b_n=\\lceil6n^2/\\log2\\rceil+b_0$. Round each $\\delta_nr_e$ downward to a multiple $q_e$ of $2^{-b_n}$ and set $q_0(i)=1-\\sum_{e:i\\to j}q_e$. The rounded instrument has Kraus factors $\\sqrt{q_e}|j\\rangle\\langle i|$ and diagonal $e^{-iE_i\\delta_n/\\hbar}\\sqrt{q_0(i)}$. It is exactly complete. Its coherent isometry is\n$$\nV_q|i\\rangle=e^{-iE_i\\delta_n/\\hbar}\\sqrt{q_0(i)}|i,0\\rangle\n+\\sum_{e:i\\to j}\\sqrt{q_e}|j,e\\rangle.\n\\tag{Q.0.1ac.3}\n$$\nDistinct columns are orthonormal: the common no-jump label retains distinct vertex states, and each edge label has one fixed initial vertex. Thus the isometry has a finite unitary completion. A $b_n$-bit uniform integer and the row's cumulative dyadic table implement exactly the same population/outcome transition on diagonal inputs. The measured random seed implements this classical restriction; the coherent isometry supplies the quantum channel without replacing an unknown quantum input by a sampled classical label.\n\nCouple the continuous-time chain of rates (Q.0.1ac.1) and the rounded tick chain using one rate-$\\Lambda$ proposal clock and maximal coupling on each tick. Two or more proposals in a tick have probability at most $(\\Lambda\\delta_n)^2/2$. The first-order table differs from the exact one-tick transition by at most $C\\Lambda^2\\delta_n^2$, uniformly in states and $n$, and rounding adds at most $|E|2^{-b_n}$. Therefore\n$$\n\\Pr(\\text{coupling fails on }[0,T])\n\\le C\\Lambda^2T\\delta_n+M_n|E|2^{-b_n}.\n\\tag{Q.0.1ac.4}\n$$\nOn success the complete ordered edge word agrees and every jump time moves by at most $\\delta_n$. Consequently, in the marked-history topology of Theorem Q.0.1r,\n$$\n\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}\\frac1n\\log\\Pr(d_{\\mathcal H}(X_n,Y_n)>\\varepsilon)=-\\infty\n\\quad(\\varepsilon>0).\n\\tag{Q.0.1ac.5}\n$$\nFor this DAG, words have length at most $|V|-1$, so its marked carrier is a finite union of closed time simplices. The exact continuous density is\n$$\nf_{n,w}(t)=\\left(\\prod_{e\\in w}k_e\\right)e^{-nA(w)}\n\\exp\\left[-\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{m}R_{i_\\ell}(n)(t_{\\ell+1}-t_\\ell)\\right],\n\\quad A(w)=\\sum_{e\\in w}a_e.\n\\tag{Q.0.1ac.6}\n$$\nHere $t_0=0,t_{m+1}=T$, and the empty-word probability is its survival factor. This factor is between $e^{-\\Lambda T}$ and one. Theorem Q.0.1r, or its direct finite-word upper/lower proof applied to this density, gives rate $A(w)$ on each simplex, including limiting simultaneous cascades. Equation (Q.0.1ac.5) transfers it to the actual finite digital source. No independent microscopic-action normalization is inferred.\n\n##### II. Reduced-system quantum approximation on the same clock\n\nThe continuous comparison generator is\n$$\n\\mathcal L_n(\\rho)=-\\frac i\\hbar[H,\\rho]\n+\\sum_e\\left(L_e\\rho L_e^*-\\frac12\\{L_e^*L_e,\\rho\\}\\right),\\qquad\nL_e=\\sqrt{r_e}|j\\rangle\\langle i|.\n\\tag{Q.0.1ac.7}\n$$\nScalar Taylor expansion of $\\sqrt{1-x}$ for $0\\le x\\le1/2$ and of the phase in (Q.0.1ac.2), including spectators, gives one-step diamond expansion $\\Phi_\\delta=I+\\delta\\mathcal L_n+O(\\delta^2)$ with a constant depending only on the fixed finite menu, $\\Lambda$ and $\\|H\\|/\\hbar$. Telescoping CPTP contractions yields\n$$\n\\|\\Phi_{\\delta_n}^{M_n}-e^{T\\mathcal L_n}\\|_\\diamond\\le CT\\delta_n.\n\\tag{Q.0.1ac.8}\n$$\nFor rounding, each jump-amplitude change is at most $\\sqrt{2^{-b_n}}$, and the no-jump change is at most $C|E|2^{-b_n}$ because its survival is at least $1/2$. Thus the isometries differ in norm by at most $C\\sqrt{|E|2^{-b_n}}$; their reduced channels differ in diamond norm by at most twice this. The conservative accumulated bound is\n$$\nC M_n\\sqrt{|E|2^{-b_n}}=O(e^{-2n^2}).\n\\tag{Q.0.1ac.9}\n$$\nHence the same finite source supplies classical marked-record approximation and reduced-system quantum convergence. A joint coherent continuous-record limit would additionally require a common record-Hilbert embedding; it is not asserted by these two estimates. Finite-history traces remain exact as in Proposition X.1c.\n\n##### III. Selective no-jump conditioning and off-diagonal response\n\nFor the continuum instrument the no-jump operator and probability are\n$$\nK_\\varnothing(t)=\\operatorname{diag}(e^{-iE_it/\\hbar-R_it/2}),\\qquad\ns_t(\\rho)=\\sum_i\\rho_{ii}e^{-R_it}.\n$$\nIts conditional state is $K_\\varnothing\\rho K_\\varnothing^*/s_t(\\rho)$. On a diagonal law $p$,\n$$\n\\mathscr F_t(p)_i=\\frac{p_ie^{-R_it}}{\\sum_jp_je^{-R_jt}},\n$$\n$$\n\\mathscr F_t(\\lambda p+(1-\\lambda)q)\n=\\frac{\\lambda s_t(p)\\mathscr F_t(p)+(1-\\lambda)s_t(q)\\mathscr F_t(q)}\n{\\lambda s_t(p)+(1-\\lambda)s_t(q)}.\n\\tag{Q.0.1ac.10}\n$$\nThe normalized selection is generally nonlinear, while its unnormalized instrument and full unconditional evolution are affine and spectator compatible. Every jump gain $L_e\\rho L_e^*$ is diagonal, so the generator gives exactly\n$$\n\\rho_{ij}(t)=e^{-i(E_i-E_j)t/\\hbar-(R_i+R_j)t/2}\\rho_{ij}(0),\\qquad i\\ne j.\n\\tag{Q.0.1ac.11}\n$$\nOn edges $01,12,02$ with tolls $a>0,0,b>0$ and prefactors $u,v,w>0$, the terminal-two exponent is $\\min(a,b)$ by (Q.0.1ac.6). In contrast, as $n\\to\\infty$, $R_0\\to0$, $R_1=v$, $R_2=0$. Thus the $01$ and $12$ coherences decay at limiting rate $v/2$, while $02$ has finite-$n$ decay rate $(ue^{-na}+we^{-nb})/2$ tending to zero. Its phase remains unless $E_0=E_2$. The zero-toll downstream edge can destroy coherence while adding no rare entrance toll. The two jump operators on $01,12$ do not commute: only $L_{12}L_{01}$ is nonzero.\n\nThe marked compactification cannot be replaced by a good discrete-sector $J_1$ principle. In this three-state example every compact $J_1$ set of paths starting at zero has a uniform positive initial interval $\\varepsilon_K$ on which its paths stay zero. Otherwise a convergent subsequence with departure times tending to zero would violate right continuity at zero after time changes tending uniformly to the identity. A direct $02$ jump before $\\varepsilon_K$ lies outside the compact set and has probability\n$$\nr_{02}\\frac{1-e^{-R_0\\varepsilon_K}}{R_0},\\qquad\n-\\frac1n\\log\\left[r_{02}\\frac{1-e^{-R_0\\varepsilon_K}}{R_0}\\right]\\longrightarrow b.\n\\tag{Q.0.1ac.12}\n$$\nNo compact family achieves exponential-tightness levels greater than $b$ on that $J_1$ space. The good marked-history result therefore has substantive topology and endpoint data.\n\n##### IV. Exact finite driven source and normalized operational maps\n\nComplete the columns of (Q.0.1ac.3) to a unitary $U_n$ by lexicographic Gram–Schmidt on the remaining computational basis vectors, skipping zero residuals. The finite construction depends only on its declared table, phases and clock. On the joint system and fresh outcome register choose\n$$\nG_n=\\frac{i\\hbar}{\\delta_n}\\operatorname{Log}U_n,\\qquad\n\\|G_n\\|\\le\\frac{\\pi\\hbar}{\\delta_n},\\qquad\n e^{-i\\delta_nG_n/\\hbar}=U_n,\n\\tag{Q.0.1ac.13}\n$$\nwith unitary-log eigenphases in $(-\\pi,\\pi]$. This is a finite self-adjoint controlled Hamiltonian with the actual phase response. Padding to eight-level tensor factors is a Hilbert-carrier encoding, not an MPU minimality or functional-qualification proof. If a gate list is required, finite two-level elimination supplies its actual length $L_n$; subwindows $\\delta_n/L_n$ have individual norm bound $\\pi\\hbar L_n/\\delta_n$. No nearest-neighbor geometry is supplied by this factorization.\n\nFor the classical operational map, let the retained state be current vertex, tick and previous outcome, with fixed context containing the table, $\\delta_n$ and tick arming. Verification probabilities are $q_\\alpha(i)$, and the normalized update sends it to $(j(\\alpha,i),k+1,\\alpha)$. Define a fixed normalized update on zero-probability outcomes. For the quantum map use probabilities $\\operatorname{Tr}\\mathcal I_\\alpha(\\rho)$ and normalized state $\\mathcal I_\\alpha(\\rho)/\\operatorname{Tr}\\mathcal I_\\alpha(\\rho)$ on nonzero branches, with tick advance, fixed perspective kernel $\\delta_{s_0}$, and supplied arming at every tick. These populate the normalized verification/update and registered instrument interfaces of Definition 6 and Definition 27 on this source.\n\nOn the diagonal branch a predictive register may write the known row $q(i)$ before verification and update to $q(j(\\alpha,i))$ after the outcome. Its Brier discrepancy is $\\|q(i)-\\operatorname{onehot}(\\alpha)\\|^2$, of mean $1-\\|q(i)\\|^2$. Relative to the uniform row on $r=|E|+1$ outcomes, the improvement is $\\|q(i)-\\operatorname{uniform}\\|^2$, strictly positive for a nonuniform row. This is a concrete forecast/verify/update response. Coherent inputs are acted on by the instrument without copying an unknown state into that classical predictor.\n\n##### V. Finite-horizon stocks, clock and control account\n\nFor this driven realization take idle Hamiltonian zero on the entire pulsed carrier, including system, work, records and ancillas. The $H$ in (Q.0.1ac.2) is an externally pulsed phase generator included in $U_n$, rather than a nonzero idle Hamiltonian. At inverse temperature $\\beta$, each pure supplied ready register of dimension $D$ has excess free energy $\\beta^{-1}\\log D$ relative to its zero-Hamiltonian Gibbs state. Each fair Gibbs bit has zero excess. The coherent realization supplies $M_n$ pure outcome registers of dimension $r$, costing ready-stock free energy $M_n\\beta^{-1}\\log r$, together with the system preparation and every declared work/control/prediction register. Each additional pure dimension-$D_j$ factor contributes $\\beta^{-1}\\log D_j$; a supplied mixed input has its actual zero-idle excess free energy. All outcome registers and garbage are retained throughout the finite horizon; no reset is invoked.\n\nA raw classical realization can use $M_n\\lceil\\log_2r\\rceil$ archive bits, $b_n$ random-work bits, $\\lceil\\log_2|V|\\rceil$ state bits, $\\lceil\\log_2(M_n+1)\\rceil$ tick bits and a table of at most $|V|rb_n$ bits. Store the dyadic jump numerators and infer $q_0$ by the row remainder. Optional prediction/scoring workspace is additional and must be counted for that extension. The $M_nb_n$ independent fair-bit draws are preparation data; if retained rather than consumed, their full seed storage is counted separately. Lossless DAG compression can retain at most $|V|-1$ edge labels and tick indices plus length, with its actual compression/control cost.\n\nEach constant gate Hamiltonian $G$ on a subwindow $\\tau$ has two separately owned quench works $\\operatorname{Tr}(G\\rho_{\\rm in})$ and $-\\operatorname{Tr}(G\\rho_{\\rm out})$. Its expectation is conserved during its isolated pulse, so they cancel in net, while the absolute sum is at most $2\\|G\\|$. For $M_nL_n$ gates of duration $\\delta_n/L_n$, a gross throughput bound is\n$$\n\\mathcal W_{\\rm gross}\\le2\\pi\\hbar M_nL_n^2/\\delta_n.\n\\tag{Q.0.1ac.14}\n$$\nThis does not price construction or maintenance of the external clock/controller at zero. If a nonzero idle $H_0$ is retained instead, net switching work is $\\operatorname{Tr}[H_0(\\rho_{\\rm out}-\\rho_{\\rm in})]$ and stock free energy uses $F(\\rho,H_0)-F(\\gamma_\\beta,H_0)$; the zero-idle formulas no longer apply.\n\nFor a repeated cycle, any reset is charged by actual conditional entropy given the retained side information. Erasing all independent uniform seeds with no side copy has ideal heat lower bound $k_BT_{\\rm bath}M_nb_n\\log2$. Saturation requires a separate quasistatic realization; quantum ready-stock renewal has its own reset protocol. Source preparation, gate execution, clock, archive/compression, reset and calibration remain disjoint ledger entries. The explicit finite counts and norm bounds do not provide their physical prices or a bounded-power refinement.\n\nFinally the toll ledger has reference-measure dependence. In dimensionless times $t/t_{\\rm ref}$,\n$$\n-\\log f^s_{n,w}=nA(w)-\\sum_{e\\in w}\\log(k_et_{\\rm ref})+\\int_0^TR_{i(t)}dt.\n$$\nAn individual digital jump-cell surprise contains $-\\log\\delta_n\\asymp n^2$; integration over its time cells or use of this density reference removes that discretization entropy. The LDP uses tube probabilities. An independent factor $\\kappa_A$ can give $\\kappa_AA(w)$ action units, but neither the source nor its record dual identifies it with $\\hbar$ or a mechanical action. This is a specified finite ordinary quantum instrument and controlled source, with the topology, resources and response limits stated above. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q01l-full-stopped-birth-path-ldp-with-an-entrance-correction\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.1l (Full Stopped Birth Path LDP with an Entrance Correction).** Fix $a,T>0$. Let $\\lambda:[0,a]\\to[0,\\infty)$ be continuous and nondecreasing, with\n\n$$\n\\lambda(0)=0,\\quad \\lambda(x)>0\\ (x>0),\\qquad\n\\int_0^a|\\log\\lambda(x)|dx<\\infty,\\qquad\n\\int_0^a\\frac{dx}{\\lambda(x)}=\\infty.\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.1}\n$$\n\nLet $m_n\\ge1$ be positive integers, $a_n=m_n/n\\le a$, $a_n\\to a$, and $\\delta_n>0\\to0$ with $-n^{-1}\\log\\delta_n\\to\\kappa\\in[0,\\infty]$. The chain $X_n(0)=0$ jumps from $k/n$ to $(k+1)/n$ at rate $n[\\lambda(k/n)+\\delta_n]$ for $k<m_n$, and is absorbed at $a_n$.\n\nOn $D([0,T],[0,a])$ with the Skorokhod $J_1$ topology, its laws satisfy a good LDP of speed $n$. Define\n\n$$\n\\tau_a(x)=\\inf\\{t\\in[0,T]:x(t)=a\\},\\qquad \\inf\\varnothing=+\\infty,\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.2}\n$$\n\nand let $\\mathcal A_a$ be the nondecreasing absolutely continuous paths $x:[0,T]\\to[0,a]$ with $x(0)=0$. For $\\kappa<\\infty$, the rate is\n\n$$\nI^a_\\kappa(x)=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\displaystyle \\kappa\\mathbf1_{\\{x(T)>0\\}}+\n\\int_0^{T\\wedge\\tau_a(x)} L(x(t),\\dot x(t))dt,&x\\in\\mathcal A_a,\\\\\n+\\infty,&\\text{otherwise},\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.3}\n$$\n\nwhere for $0<x\\le a$ and $v\\ge0$,\n\n$$\nL(x,v)=v\\log\\frac v{\\lambda(x)}-v+\\lambda(x),\\qquad\nL(0,0)=0,\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.4}\n$$\n\nand $L=+\\infty$ for other velocities (using $0\\log0=0$). The value at the single first hitting time does not affect the integral. In particular $I^a_\\kappa(0)=0$. For $\\kappa=\\infty$, the rate is zero at the identically zero path and $+\\infty$ elsewhere. All finite sublevels are compact in the uniform topology as well as in $J_1$.\n\nThe same good LDP and rate hold on $C([0,T],[0,a])$ with uniform topology for the following continuous interpolation: join $(0,0)$ and every actual jump vertex $(t_j,j/n)$ by line segments, then hold the last value through $T$. Equally, any measurable continuous interpolation staying within $o(1)$, deterministically, of $X_n$ has this conclusion.\n\nThe entrance charge occurs once whenever the limiting path is nonzero. Waiting at zero before departure costs zero; holding still at a positive interior state costs $\\lambda(x)$ per unit time; holding at the absorbing endpoint costs zero. The theorem does not charge the interior value $L(a,0)=\\lambda(a)$ after absorption.\n\n**Proof.**\n\n**A. A constant-rate Poisson path reference, with its hypotheses checked.**\n\nLet $Q_n$ be the law of $Y_n(t)=N(nt)/n$, where $N$ is a rate-one Poisson process, on $D([0,T],[0,\\infty))$ with $J_1$. Its good rate is\n\n$$\nJ(x)=\\int_0^T\\ell(\\dot x(t))dt,\\qquad \\ell(v)=v\\log v-v+1,\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.5}\n$$\n\nfor nondecreasing absolutely continuous $x$ starting at zero, and $+\\infty$ otherwise. Here is a direct proof of this reference assertion, so a theorem about a weaker Skorokhod topology is not being substituted.\n\nFor a finite time partition, increments are independent Poisson random variables with means $n\\Delta t_i$. The normalized vector has log-mgf\n\n$$\n\\frac1n\\log E\\exp\\{n\\sum_i\\theta_i\\Delta Y_{n,i}\\}\n=\\sum_i\\Delta t_i(e^{\\theta_i}-1).\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.6}\n$$\n\nIts finite-dimensional LDP has rate $\\sum_i\\Delta t_i\\ell(u_i/\\Delta t_i)$ on nonnegative increment vectors. This follows directly from Stirling's formula for lattice probabilities, including the exact probability $e^{-n\\Delta t_i}$ of zero increments, and exponential Chernoff tails; finite products preserve these bounds. Thus no boundary steepness claim at a zero increment is needed.\n\nTake nested uniform dyadic partitions of mesh $h=T/2^r$. Let $P_hY_n$ linearly join their sampled values. Its finite-dimensional image LDP is good and its rate is\n\n$$\nJ_h(z)=\\sum_i h\\ell\\!\\left(\\frac{z((i+1)h)-z(ih)}h\\right)\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.7}\n$$\n\non the corresponding nondecreasing polygons, infinity otherwise. Monotonicity gives\n\n$$\n\\|Y_n-P_hY_n\\|_\\infty\\le\\max_i\\{Y_n((i+1)h)-Y_n(ih)\\}.\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.8}\n$$\n\nFor $\\varepsilon>h$, the union bound and the Poisson Chernoff bound give\n\n$$\n\\limsup_n\\frac1n\\log Q_n\\{\\|Y_n-P_hY_n\\|_\\infty>\\varepsilon\\}\n\\le-\\varepsilon\\log(\\varepsilon/h)+\\varepsilon-h,\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.9}\n$$\n\nwhich tends to $-\\infty$ as $h\\downarrow0$. The finite partition count contributes zero on the speed-$n$ scale. Uniform distance bounds $J_1$ distance, so (Q.0.1l.9) applies to that topology too.\n\nFor completeness the needed limiting-rate compactness is elementary. If $J_h(z)\\le R$, its nonnegative slope $v_h$ has $\\int\\ell(v_h)\\le R$. Superlinear growth of $\\ell$ makes the slopes uniformly integrable with bounded total mass, hence the polygons are uniformly bounded and equicontinuous. Every sequence with $h\\downarrow0$ and bounded cost therefore has a uniformly convergent subsequence with absolutely continuous limit $x$. Weak lower semicontinuity of the convex integral gives $J(x)\\le\\liminf J_h(z_h)$. This lower semicontinuity can also be read directly from\n\n$$\n\\int\\ell(v)dt=\\sup_{f\\in C([0,T])}\\left\\{\\int fv\\,dt-\\int(e^f-1)dt\\right\\}.\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.10}\n$$\n\nConversely, if $J(x)<\\infty$, then $P_hx\\to x$ uniformly and Jensen gives $J_h(P_hx)\\le J(x)$. These compactness, lower-bound and recovery statements identify the limiting rate exactly as (Q.0.1l.5); they also prove it is good, with uniformly compact sublevels.\n\nHere is explicitly how they give the path LDP. For an open neighborhood of a finite-cost $x$, choose $h$ so that $P_hx$ lies well inside it and the error exponent in (Q.0.1l.9) is more negative than $-J(x)-1$. The finite-dimensional lower bound, minus the error probability, gives the lower bound $-J(x)$. For a closed $F$,\n\n$$\nQ_n(F)\\le Q_n(P_hY_n\\in F^{\\varepsilon})+\nQ_n(d_{J_1}(Y_n,P_hY_n)>\\varepsilon),\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.11}\n$$\n\nwhere $F^{\\varepsilon}$ denotes the closed metric enlargement. Apply the polygon upper bound. If the resulting infima of $J_h$ stay bounded along $h\\downarrow0,\\varepsilon\\downarrow0$, the preceding equicoercivity produces a limit in $F$ with no greater cost. If they do not stay bounded, their exponent tends to $-\\infty$. Taking first suitably small $h$ for each fixed $\\varepsilon$, then $\\varepsilon\\downarrow0$, proves the closed upper bound. This proves the full good $J_1$ reference LDP, including exponential control beyond compact sets.\n\n**B. Bounded extension and the exact finite-(n) likelihood.**\n\nExtend the rate by $b(x)=\\lambda(x)$ on $[0,a]$ and $b(x)=\\lambda(a)$ for $x>a$. Then $b$ is bounded, continuous, nondecreasing, zero only at zero, and log-integrable on every bounded interval. Write $M=\\lambda(a)>0$. Let $Z_n$ be the unstopped pure-birth chain with rates $n[b(k/n)+\\delta_n]$ for every $k\\ge0$. For large $n$, all rates are bounded by $n(M+1)$, which proves nonexplosion by Poisson domination.\n\nOn a count history $z$ with $j=nz(T)$ jumps, multiplication of the successive holding-time densities, including survival from the last jump through $T$, gives the exact likelihood\n\n$$\n\\frac1n\\log\\frac{dP_n}{dQ_n}(z)\n=\\frac1n\\sum_{k=0}^{j-1}\\log(b(k/n)+\\delta_n)\n-\\int_0^T[b(z(t))+\\delta_n-1]dt.\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.12}\n$$\n\nJump-time values and left limits have the same time integral. Formula (Q.0.1l.12) is a true density, not an unnormalized tilt: both sides come from nonexplosive birth-process path densities, the rates are positive at every finite $n$, and these densities integrate to one. The $j=0$ case is included, with empty sum. No separately chosen normalizing constant is allowed.\n\nFor $j\\ge1$, separate the $k=0$ term and define\n\n$$\n\\kappa_n=-\\frac1n\\log\\delta_n,\\quad\nB(y)=\\int_0^y\\log b(u)du,\\quad\n\\Phi(z)=B(z(T)) -\\int_0^T[b(z(t))-1]dt.\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.13}\n$$\n\nThe primitive $B$ is finite and continuous, including at zero.\n\n**C. Uniform singular Riemann convergence.**\n\nFor every finite $K$,\n\n$$\n\\sup_{1\\le j\\le nK}\\left|\n\\frac1n\\sum_{k=1}^{j-1}\\log(b(k/n)+\\delta_n)-B(j/n)\n\\right|\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.14}\n$$\n\nAn explicit justification handles arbitrary decay schedules $\\delta_n$. On $[\\epsilon,K+1]$, positivity and continuity imply uniform convergence of $\\log(b+\\delta_n)$ to $\\log b$, and ordinary Riemann sums converge uniformly in their partial endpoint. Near zero choose $\\epsilon$ so that $b(2\\epsilon)<1$. For large $n$,\n\n$$\n|\\log(b(k/n)+\\delta_n)|\n\\le |\\log b(k/n)|+\\log(M+1)^{+}.\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.15}\n$$\n\nThe function $-\\log b$ is nonnegative and nonincreasing on $(0,2\\epsilon]$. Thus for $l\\le n\\epsilon$,\n\n$$\n\\frac1n\\sum_{k=1}^{l}|\\log b(k/n)|\n\\le\\int_0^{l/n}|\\log b(u)|du\\le\\int_0^\\epsilon|\\log b(u)|du.\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.16}\n$$\n\nThe latter tends to zero with $\\epsilon$. A possible final cell and the omission of the endpoint cell are controlled by absolute continuity of the integral of $|\\log b|$, uniformly in their positions on a compact interval. This proves (Q.0.1l.14), not only convergence at one fixed terminal point. In particular no hidden uniform lower bound on $b(1/n)$ or polynomial floor assumption is used.\n\nConsequently, on $1\\le nz(T)\\le nK$, (Q.0.1l.12) equals\n\n$$\n-\\kappa_n+\\Phi(z)+r_{n,K}(z),\\qquad\n\\sup|r_{n,K}|\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.17}\n$$\n\nLet $E$ be the closed subspace of nonnegative nondecreasing càdlàg paths starting at zero. The map $\\Phi:E\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous in $J_1$: endpoints converge in $J_1$, $B$ is continuous, and $z_n(t)\\to z(t)$ at every continuity point of $z$. A càdlàg path has at most countably many discontinuities, so boundedness and continuity of $b$ give convergence of its time integral by dominated convergence. On $\\{z(T)\\le K\\}$, $\\Phi$ is bounded above and below.\n\n**D. The unstopped good LDP and the exceptional zero path.**\n\nSuppose first $\\kappa<\\infty$. The candidate unstopped rate on $E$ is\n\n$$\nI_\\kappa(z)=J(z)-\\Phi(z)+\\kappa\\mathbf1_{\\{z(T)>0\\}},\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.18}\n$$\n\nand it is infinity outside $E$. Since $J(0)=T$ and $\\Phi(0)=T$, the zero path has rate zero.\n\nAt every nonzero finite-rate path, take a sufficiently small open neighborhood whose endpoints stay in $[\\epsilon,K]$. Formula (Q.0.1l.17), continuity of $\\Phi$, and the reference LDP lower bound give the lower bound $-I_\\kappa(z)$. If an open set contains zero, use the exact probability\n\n$$\nP_n(Z_n\\equiv0)=\\exp(-n\\delta_n T),\\qquad\n\\frac1n\\log P_n(Z_n\\equiv0)=-\\delta_nT\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.19}\n$$\n\nThis supplies the missing lower bound without falsely applying a continuous-tilt theorem at zero.\n\nFor a closed set $F\\subset E$ excluding zero, there exists $\\epsilon>0$ such that $z(T)\\ge\\epsilon$ on $F$. Indeed otherwise monotonicity would give $\\|z\\|_\\infty=z(T)\\to0$, contradicting closedness. On the closed truncation $F\\cap\\{z(T)\\le K\\}$, (Q.0.1l.17) and the reference LDP yield\n\n$$\n\\limsup_n n^{-1}\\log P_n(F\\cap\\{Z_n(T)\\le K\\})\n\\le -\\inf_{F\\cap\\{z(T)\\le K\\}}[J(z)-\\Phi(z)+\\kappa].\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.20}\n$$\n\nTo see this bounded-tilt upper step without a hidden compactness premise, partition the bounded range of $\\Phi$ into finitely many closed intervals of length $\\eta$; each intersection with the closed truncation is closed. Apply the reference closed upper bound on each piece and then let $\\eta\\downarrow0$. Uniformity of (Q.0.1l.17) absorbs its error. The finite union changes no speed-$n$ exponent.\n\nThere is also the direct source tail bound, by thinning domination with intensity $nC$, where $C=M+1$: for $K>CT$,\n\n$$\n\\limsup_n n^{-1}\\log P_n(Z_n(T)>K)\n\\le-[K\\log(K/(CT))-K+CT]\\longrightarrow-\\infty.\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.21}\n$$\n\nSplitting $F$ at $K$ in (Q.0.1l.20) and sending $K\\to\\infty$ proves its full closed upper bound. If $F$ contains zero, the bound is the trivial probability bound, because the rate is nonnegative, as shown next. Sets outside $E$ cause no issue: the laws are supported on the closed set $E$.\n\nFor an absolutely continuous $z\\in E$, the monotone change of variables, first with bounded truncations of $\\log b$, gives\n\n$$\n\\int_0^T\\dot z(t)\\log b(z(t))dt=B(z(T)).\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.22}\n$$\n\nThe integral over the initial zero interval is defined as zero, since $\\dot z=0$ almost everywhere there. Integrability follows from $\\int_0^{z(T)}|\\log b(u)|du<\\infty$; the positive part is bounded by a constant times $z(T)$. Thus (Q.0.1l.22) is a finite real number and no $\\infty-\\infty$ cancellation occurs. Since the negative part of $v\\log v$ is bounded, (Q.0.1l.18) becomes\n\n$$\nI_\\kappa(z)=\\kappa\\mathbf1_{\\{z(T)>0\\}}+\n\\int_0^T\\left[\\dot z\\log\\frac{\\dot z}{b(z)}-\\dot z+b(z)\\right]dt.\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.23}\n$$\n\nThis identity remains valid when the entropy integral is infinite. It proves nonnegativity and the claimed local-action expression before stopping.\n\nThe rate is lower semicontinuous: $J$ is lower semicontinuous, $\\Phi$ continuous, and $\\kappa\\mathbf1_{\\{z(T)>0\\}}$ lower semicontinuous on $E$, since $\\kappa\\ge0$. For goodness put $c=\\max(0,\\log M)$, so $B(y)\\le cy$. Then\n\n$$\nI_\\kappa(z)\\ge J(z)-cz(T)-T.\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.24}\n$$\n\nThe entropy dual bound with $\\theta=c+1$ gives $J(z)\\ge(c+1)z(T)-T(e^{c+1}-1)$, hence\n\n$$\nI_\\kappa(z)\\ge z(T)-Te^{c+1}.\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.25}\n$$\n\nA common $I_\\kappa$-sublevel has bounded endpoints and, by the previous inequality, bounded $J$. It is a closed subset of a uniformly compact $J$-sublevel, proving goodness in both topologies.\n\nWhen $\\kappa=\\infty$,\n\n$$\nP_n(Z_n\\not\\equiv0)=1-e^{-n\\delta_nT}\\le n\\delta_nT,\n\\qquad \\limsup_n n^{-1}\\log P_n(Z_n\\not\\equiv0)=-\\infty.\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.26}\n$$\n\nTogether with (Q.0.1l.19), this directly proves the good LDP with only zero of finite rate, for every open and closed set. There is no need to subtract an infinite coefficient in (Q.0.1l.17).\n\n**E. Clipping, the moving lattice endpoint, and the stopped rate.**\n\nThe pointwise clipping map $C_a(z)(t)=\\min(z(t),a)$ is $J_1$-continuous: any time changes witnessing $z_n\\to z$ also witness $C_a(z_n)\\to C_a(z)$, because clipping is 1-Lipschitz. The contraction principle therefore gives a good LDP for $C_aZ_n$, with rate\n\n$$\n\\widehat I^a_\\kappa(x)=\\inf\\{I_\\kappa(z): C_a z=x\\}.\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.27}\n$$\n\nOn the same birth-history construction, $X_n=C_{a_n}Z_n$ exactly, since clipping stops the first $m_n$ births and ignores all subsequent births. Moreover\n\n$$\n\\|C_{a_n}Z_n-C_aZ_n\\|_\\infty\\le|a_n-a|\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.28}\n$$\n\nThey are deterministically exponentially equivalent at speed $n$. This handles arbitrary convergence of $a_n$; no $O(1/n)$ endpoint approximation is assumed. All chains take values in $[0,a]$ under the stated $a_n\\le a$ convention.\n\nIt remains to identify (Q.0.1l.27), rather than just state a contraction formula. A finite-rate preimage is absolutely continuous and nondecreasing, so its clip is in $\\mathcal A_a$. If $x(T)<a$, the only preimage in $E$ is $z=x$, and (Q.0.1l.23) gives (Q.0.1l.3). If $x$ reaches $a$ at $s\\le T$, every finite-rate preimage agrees with $x$ on $[0,s]$. It pays the same entrance charge and the same action up to $s$, followed by a nonnegative action. This gives the lower bound in (Q.0.1l.3). It is attained by the absolutely continuous preimage\n\n$$\nz(t)=x(t)\\quad(t\\le s),\\qquad\nz(t)=a+M(t-s)\\quad(t\\ge s).\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.29}\n$$\n\nFor $z\\ge a$, the extended rate equals $M$, so the post-hit path has velocity $M=b(z)$, and its local cost is exactly zero. Thus (Q.0.1l.27) is precisely (Q.0.1l.3). This argument also covers $s=T$, and the zero preimage is unique for the zero output. For $\\kappa=\\infty$, contraction leaves only zero of finite rate. The compact sublevels obtained in the contraction are continuous and uniformly compact, since clipping is uniformly continuous on the uniformly compact unstopped sublevels.\n\n**F. Continuous interpolation and normalization.**\n\nWith the interpolation in the theorem, between successive jump vertices the step path is constant and the interpolated path lies between that value and its next value. After the last jump both are constant. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\|\\overline X_n-X_n\\|_\\infty\\le 1/n\n\\tag{Q.0.1l.30}\n$$\n\nalmost surely (jump times exactly at zero or $T$ have probability zero and may be defined arbitrarily). Exponential equivalence transfers the same $J_1$ LDP to $\\overline X_n$. The subspace $C([0,T],[0,a])$ is $J_1$-closed, and its induced $J_1$ topology is exactly its uniform topology. Because the interpolated laws are supported there, the restricted LDP is the asserted good uniform-topology LDP. The same reasoning applies to any stated deterministic $o(1)$ continuous interpolation.\n\nFinally, all rates are nonnegative and $I^a_\\kappa(0)=0$, so their infimum is zero, consistent with probability normalization. Under the assumed divergent $\\int_0^a1/\\lambda$, zero is the only zero-action path even for $\\kappa=0$: equality in the entropy density would force $\\dot x=\\lambda(x)$ before stopping. A nondecreasing solution departing from zero would require finite elapsed time at least $\\int_\\epsilon^bdu/\\lambda(u)$ to reach any $b>0$, which diverges as $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$. This is impossible. No local Lipschitz assumption or unproved ODE uniqueness at zero is used. ∎\n\n<a id=\"remark-q01l1-stopped-source-scope-and-independent-hitting-probabilities\"></a>**Remark Q.0.1l.1 (Stopped-source scope and independent hitting probabilities).**\n\n1. This is a full path-space LDP, not merely an endpoint or hitting-time contraction, for precisely the one-way monotone boundary-degenerate source above. The proof uses that each positive level is visited once. It does not extend to repeated visits, opposite jump directions, interior zero sets, or nonmonotone rate functions without new estimates.\n2. At a fixed positive endpoint $a$, minimizing (Q.0.1l.3) over arrival by $T$ recovers the variational value $\\kappa+J_\\lambda(T)$ of Theorem Q.0.1g. An exact event such as endpoint equality need not have nonempty interior, so the probability equality in Theorem Q.0.1g should retain its independent holding-time proof; the present LDP is not used to infer a boundary-event exponent from an unverified continuity-set condition.\n3. The infinite deterministic travel-time assumption is needed here to retain the accepted source class and to prove uniqueness of the zero-cost path. The path-LDP proof itself only uses positivity away from zero, bounded monotone extension, and log-integrability. No relaxation is part of this registered theorem.\n4. Definition Q.0.1a currently requires a local integral rate. When $\\kappa>0$, the additional entrance charge is not the displayed limiting local Hamiltonian's integral conjugate. This theorem therefore discharges the exact good path-law subpredicate, but does not by itself instantiate all of Definition Q.0.1a unchanged. At $\\kappa=0$, the theorem supplies the purely local stopped action rate if the nominated source contract is separately met; the local density must still use the absorbing-boundary convention. For $\\kappa>0$, it supplies a checked entrance correction and obstruction to the unchanged purely local form, not a redefinition or silent extension of PU's action notion. For every $\\kappa$, Theorem Q.0.1b requires its own interior/closure infimum condition, and Corollary Q.0.1c requires the historywise reconstruction.\n5. The theorem is a dimensionless path-law result for this registered source. An additive finite-ledger $\\Gamma$ limit, physical ND–RID realization, Appendix-U/Y boundary map, structural-operation identity and charged-bath implementation retain their separate source requirements. The dimensionless entrance coefficient $\\kappa$ is unrelated to the action-calibration scale $\\kappa_A$. The zero-floor accessibility obstruction remains valid.\n\n### Q.0.2 The Discrete Predictive Cost Functional\n\nThe MPU network executes cyclical predictive operations, each cycle implementing the Fundamental Predictive Loop (Definition 4):\n\n$$\\text{Predict} \\to \\text{Verify} \\to \\text{Update}$$\n\nOn the registered binary-support branch, the structural ledger value is exactly\n$$\n\\varepsilon_0=\\ln|\\{0,1\\}|=\\ln2\n$$\nby Proposition 5, Definition 28, Definition J.1, and Theorem J.1. This is a log-cardinality theorem and is independent of the event distribution. If a reusable implementation additionally resets the prediction register under Theorem 31's thermodynamic hypotheses, its separate physical ledger obeys\n$$\n\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{reset}}\n=H_q(P\\mid R)+\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{diss}}\n\\ge H_q(P\\mid R),\n\\qquad\n\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{diss}}\\ge0.\n$$\nA positive uniform heat floor inferred from this entropy bound requires an independent positive lower bound on $H_q(P\\mid R)$; structural log-cardinality alone implies neither heat nor mechanical action.\n\n<a id=\"definition-q01-dimensionless-discrete-predictive-ledger\"></a>**Definition Q.0.1 (Dimensionless Discrete Predictive Ledger).** For a trajectory with finitely many registered steps, let\n$$\n\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{disc}}:=\\sum_{i=1}^N\\ell_i\n$$\nbe a declared dimensionless additive ledger. The values $\\ell_i$ must be supplied by the relevant structural, stochastic-rate, or thermodynamic implementation record; they are not all identified with bath heat or total entropy production. Given an independent action scale $\\kappa_A>0$, define\n$$\n\\mathcal S_{\\mathrm{disc}}:=\\kappa_A\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{disc}}.\n$$\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q01-conditional-structural-count\"></a>**Proposition Q.0.1 (Conditional Structural Count).** If a branch assigns exactly one binary structural label to each of $N_{\\mathrm{ops}}$ registered steps and no other contribution to this ledger, then\n$$\n\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{disc}}=N_{\\mathrm{ops}}\\ln2.\n$$\nThis is an alphabet-counting identity, not a universal physical-action, heat, or entropy-production lower bound. ∎\n\n<a id=\"remark-q01a-ledger-separation\"></a>**Remark Q.0.1a (Ledger Separation).** Registered reset heat obeys Theorem J.1 and depends on $H_q(P\\mid R)$. A large-deviation rate functional and a mechanical action require their own bridge. The calibration $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$ is never inferred from $\\ln2$.\n\n### Q.0.3 The Continuum Limit via Γ-Convergence\n\nA continuum action can be assigned to the discrete predictive ledger on a branch with an independently established $\\Gamma$-limit and action-scale calibration. Theorem D.6 of Appendix D gives convergence of minima and minimizing subsequences under its liminf, recovery, equicoercivity, and properness hypotheses; Section O.7.1 of Appendix O treats the spatial sector under its additional continuum-identification hypotheses.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q01-calibrated-continuum-action-limit\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.1 (Calibrated Continuum-Action Limit).** Let $\\kappa_A>0$ be an independently supplied action scale. Assume the declared topology, equicoercivity, liminf inequality, and recovery sequences establish\n$$\n\\mathcal L_h\\xrightarrow{\\Gamma}\\mathcal L\n$$\nfor a dimensionless discrete ledger. Then\n$$\n\\mathcal S_h:=\\kappa_A\\mathcal L_h\n\\xrightarrow{\\Gamma}\n\\mathcal S:=\\kappa_A\\mathcal L.\n$$\nThe $\\Gamma$-limit fixes the functional only relative to $\\kappa_A$; it does not derive $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q01-conditional-action-entropy-calibration\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.1 (Conditional Action--Entropy Calibration).** If $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$ is independently calibrated and a recovery sequence carries the additive history ledger, then\n$$\n\\mathcal S[\\phi]\n=\\hbar\\lim_{h\\to0}\\sum_{i\\in\\phi_h}\\varepsilon_i^{(h)}.\n$$\nThis is a calibrated representation, not a derivation of $\\hbar$. ∎\n\n<a id=\"definition-q01a-predictive-large-deviation-branch\"></a>**Definition Q.0.1a (Predictive Large-Deviation Branch).** A predictive large-deviation branch is a family of finite ND-RID/PCE stochastic dynamics\n$$\nX^\\eta_t,\n\\qquad\n\\eta>0,\n$$\non a compact retained state space, with small effective noise parameter $\\eta$, satisfying a good path-space large-deviation principle: for every admissible path set $\\mathcal A$,\n$$\n-\\inf_{\\gamma\\in\\mathcal A^\\circ} I_{\\mathrm{PU}}[\\gamma]\n\\le\n\\liminf_{\\eta\\downarrow0}\n\\eta\\log\\mathbb P_\\eta(X^\\eta_{[0,T]}\\in\\mathcal A)\n\\le\n\\limsup_{\\eta\\downarrow0}\n\\eta\\log\\mathbb P_\\eta(X^\\eta_{[0,T]}\\in\\mathcal A)\n\\le\n-\\inf_{\\gamma\\in\\overline{\\mathcal A}} I_{\\mathrm{PU}}[\\gamma].\n\\tag{Q.0.1a.1}\n$$\nThe rate functional has local form\n$$\nI_{\\mathrm{PU}}[\\gamma]\n=\n\\int_0^T\nL_{\\mathrm{PU}}(\\gamma(t),\\dot\\gamma(t))\\,dt\n\\tag{Q.0.1a.2}\n$$\nfor absolutely continuous retained paths and is $+\\infty$ otherwise. The physical action of the same retained path is\n$$\n\\mathcal S_{\\mathrm{PU}}[\\gamma]\n=\n\\kappa_A I_{\\mathrm{PU}}[\\gamma],\n\\tag{Q.0.1a.3}\n$$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q01b-predictive-large-deviation-variational-principle\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.1b (Predictive Large-Deviation Variational Principle).** On a predictive large-deviation branch, the dominant rare transition from a closed retained basin $A$ to a closed retained basin $B$ over the time window $[0,T]$ is governed by the finite-time transition cost\n$$\n\\mathcal I_{A\\to B}\n=\n\\inf_{\\gamma(0)\\in A,\\ \\gamma(T)\\in B}\nI_{\\mathrm{PU}}[\\gamma].\n\\tag{Q.0.1b.1}\n$$\nLet $\\mathcal T_{A\\to B}$ be a transition tube satisfying\n$$\n\\inf_{\\gamma\\in\\mathcal T_{A\\to B}^{\\circ}}I_{\\mathrm{PU}}[\\gamma]\n=\n\\inf_{\\gamma\\in\\overline{\\mathcal T}_{A\\to B}}I_{\\mathrm{PU}}[\\gamma]\n=\n\\mathcal I_{A\\to B}.\n$$\nThen\n$$\n\\lim_{\\eta\\downarrow0}\n\\eta\\log\n\\mathbb P_\\eta(X^\\eta_{[0,T]}\\in\\mathcal T_{A\\to B})\n=\n-\\mathcal I_{A\\to B}.\n\\tag{Q.0.1b.2}\n$$\nOn the accepted action-calibration branch,\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathcal S_{A\\to B}^{\\min}}{\\kappa_A}\n=\n\\mathcal I_{A\\to B}.\n\\tag{Q.0.1b.3}\n$$\n\n*Proof.* Apply the path-space large-deviation lower bound to the tube interior and the upper bound to its closure. The three-way infimum hypothesis makes the two exponential bounds equal to $-\\mathcal I_{A\\to B}$, proving (Q.0.1b.2). Multiplication of the minimizing rate functional by the accepted action scale $\\kappa_A$ proves (Q.0.1b.3). ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q01c-conditional-branch-action-reconstruction\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.1c (Conditional Branch-Action Reconstruction).** Let a retained branch satisfy Definition Q.0.1a, and suppose an accepted reconstruction identifies the branch's dimensionless action $\\mathcal S_{\\mathrm{br}}/\\kappa_A$ historywise with $I_{\\mathrm{PU}}$ on the same admissible transition class and boundary data. Their global infima then agree. If a transition tube also satisfies the interior-and-closure infimum condition of Theorem Q.0.1b, its probability exponent equals that common infimum with a minus sign. In particular, an Appendix-U or Appendix-Y transition exponent belongs to this common large-deviation ledger only when both the reconstruction and the transition-tube condition are certified for its retained paths. Stationarity alone does not identify a dominant transition exponent.\n\n*Proof.* Definition Q.0.1a supplies the path-space large-deviation principle. Historywise equality on the same admissible class gives equality of the global infima. The transition-tube hypothesis then supplies the matching lower and upper bounds in Theorem Q.0.1b. A stationary history that does not attain the global infimum is not selected by that conclusion. Without the historywise reconstruction, two action functionals need not be the same rate functional and no consolidation follows. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q01d-uniformly-elliptic-nd-rid-diffusion-rate-and-reconstruction-residual\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.1d (Uniformly Elliptic ND--RID Diffusion Rate and Reconstruction Residual).** Let the retained state space be the flat torus $\\mathbb T^d$, let $b:\\mathbb T^d\\to\\mathbb R^d$ be Lipschitz, and let $\\sigma$ be an invertible constant matrix with $a=\\sigma\\sigma^T$. Fix $x_0\\in\\mathbb T^d$. For $\\eta>0$, define the retained stochastic dynamics\n$$\ndX_t^\\eta=b(X_t^\\eta)dt+\\sqrt{2\\eta}\\,\\sigma\\,dW_t,\n\\qquad X_0^\\eta=x_0,\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal G_\\eta f=b\\cdot\\nabla f+\\eta\\,a:D^2f.\n\\tag{Q.0.1d.1}\n$$\nIts nonlinear generator satisfies\n$$\n\\eta e^{-f/\\eta}\\mathcal G_\\eta e^{f/\\eta}\n=b\\cdot\\nabla f+(\\nabla f)^Ta\\nabla f+\\eta\\,a:D^2f\n\\longrightarrow\nH(x,\\nabla f),\n\\tag{Q.0.1d.2}\n$$\nwhere $H(x,p)=b(x)\\cdot p+p^Tap$. The path laws obey a good large-deviation principle on $C([0,T],\\mathbb T^d)$ with rate\n$$\nI_{\\mathrm{PU}}[\\gamma]\n=\\frac14\\int_0^T\n(\\dot\\gamma-b(\\gamma))^Ta^{-1}(\\dot\\gamma-b(\\gamma))\\,dt\n\\tag{Q.0.1d.3}\n$$\nfor absolutely continuous paths with $\\gamma(0)=x_0$ and $+\\infty$ otherwise.\n\nFor any continuous claimed branch density $L_{\\mathrm{br}}$ on a compact path domain\n$$\nK_V:=\\{(x,v):x\\in\\mathbb T^d, |v|\\le V\\},\n$$\ndefine the registered reconstruction residual\n$$\nr_{\\mathrm{br}}(x,v)\n:=L_{\\mathrm{br}}(x,v)\n-\\frac14(v-b(x))^Ta^{-1}(v-b(x)).\n\\tag{Q.0.1d.4}\n$$\nIf $\\|r_{\\mathrm{br}}\\|_{L^\\infty(K_V)}\\le\\epsilon$, then every admissible path with $|\\dot\\gamma|\\le V$ satisfies\n$$\n\\left|\n\\int_0^TL_{\\mathrm{br}}(\\gamma,\\dot\\gamma)dt\n-I_{\\mathrm{PU}}[\\gamma]\n\\right|\n\\le T\\epsilon,\n\\tag{Q.0.1d.5}\n$$\nand, on every nonempty fixed-boundary admissible class for which both infima are finite, the corresponding infima differ by at most $T\\epsilon$. In particular, $r_{\\mathrm{br}}=0$ on $K_V$ is a sufficient certificate for exact historywise reconstruction on the displayed admissible path class. No converse from equality of the fixed-$x_0$ path functionals to pointwise equality of densities is asserted.\n\n*Proof.* Direct differentiation of $e^{f/\\eta}$ gives (Q.0.1d.2). The Legendre transform of $H(x,p)$ is\n$$\n\\sup_p\\{p\\cdot v-H(x,p)\\}\n=\\frac14(v-b(x))^Ta^{-1}(v-b(x)),\n$$\nbecause the maximizer is $p=\\tfrac12a^{-1}(v-b)$. To justify the path-law claim, periodically lift $b$ to $\\mathbb R^d$ and choose one lift of $x_0$. The lifted drift is bounded and globally Lipschitz; the constant matrix $\\sqrt2\\,\\sigma$ is Lipschitz and uniformly nondegenerate. Chiarini and Fischer (2014, Theorem 2 and Example 1) give a good finite-time path LDP for the Euclidean lifted SDE with speed $1/\\eta$ and controlled rate $\\tfrac12\\int_0^T|u(t)|^2dt$ under $\\dot\\phi=b(\\phi)+\\sqrt2\\,\\sigma u$. Invertibility of $\\sigma$ gives $u=(\\sqrt2\\,\\sigma)^{-1}(\\dot\\phi-b(\\phi))$ and the $1/4$ factor in (Q.0.1d.3). Projection of Euclidean paths to $C([0,T],\\mathbb T^d)$ is continuous in the uniform topology, so the contraction principle gives a good torus-path LDP. Every torus path beginning at $x_0$ has a unique continuous lift beginning at the chosen Euclidean lift, making the contracted rate exactly (Q.0.1d.3), with $+\\infty$ off the absolutely continuous class. Integrating the pointwise residual bound proves (Q.0.1d.5); a vanishing pointwise residual makes the two path functionals equal. Taking infima preserves the same uniform error. ∎\n\n<a id=\"lemma-q01x-poisson-path-reference-in-j_1-and-uniform-interpolation\"></a>**Lemma Q.0.1x (Poisson Path Reference in $J_1$ and Uniform Interpolation).** Fix $T>0$. Let $Q_n$ be the law of $Y_n(t)=N(nt)/n$, with $N$ a rate-one Poisson process. Put\n$$\n\\ell_1(v)=v\\log v-v+1\\quad(v\\ge0),\\qquad 0\\log0=0,\n$$\nand let $\\mathcal A$ consist of nondecreasing absolutely continuous paths $\\phi:[0,T]\\to[0,\\infty)$ with $\\phi(0)=0$. Then $Q_n$ has a good speed-$n$ large-deviation principle on $D([0,T],[0,\\infty))$ with the $J_1$ topology and rate\n$$\nJ(\\phi)=\\begin{cases}\\displaystyle\\int_0^T\\ell_1(\\dot\\phi(t))\\,dt,&\\phi\\in\\mathcal A,\\\\+\\infty,&\\text{otherwise.}\\end{cases}\n\\tag{Q.0.1x.1}\n$$\nIts sublevels are uniformly compact. Join $(0,0)$ and the actual post-jump vertices $(t_j,j/n)$ by straight segments, then hold the last value through $T$; the no-jump interpolation is zero. These continuous interpolations have the same good principle on $C([0,T],[0,\\infty))$ in the uniform topology.\n\n*Proof.* On a finite time partition the increments are independent, with Poisson means $n\\Delta t_i$, and\n$$\n\\frac1n\\log\\mathbb E\\exp\\!\\left(n\\sum_i\\theta_i\\Delta Y_{n,i}\\right)\n=\\sum_i\\Delta t_i(e^{\\theta_i}-1).\n\\tag{Q.0.1x.2}\n$$\nStirling's formula for lattice probabilities, the exact zero-increment probability $e^{-n\\Delta t_i}$, and exponential Chernoff tails give the finite-dimensional rate $\\sum_i\\Delta t_i\\ell_1(u_i/\\Delta t_i)$, including zero increments. For nested dyadic partitions of mesh $h=T/2^r$, let $P_hY_n$ be the polygon through the sampled values. Its rate on nondecreasing mesh polygons is\n$$\nJ_h(z)=\\sum_i h\\ell_1\\!\\left(\\frac{z((i+1)h)-z(ih)}h\\right).\n$$\nMonotonicity gives $\\|Y_n-P_hY_n\\|_\\infty\\le\\max_i\\Delta_iY_n$. For $\\varepsilon>h$, the union and Poisson Chernoff bounds therefore give\n$$\n\\limsup_n\\frac1n\\log Q_n\\{\\|Y_n-P_hY_n\\|_\\infty>\\varepsilon\\}\n\\le-\\varepsilon\\log(\\varepsilon/h)+\\varepsilon-h\\longrightarrow-\\infty\n\\quad(h\\downarrow0).\n\\tag{Q.0.1x.3}\n$$\nThe finite partition count has zero speed-$n$ contribution, and uniform distance bounds $J_1$ distance.\n\nIf $J_h(z_h)\\le R$, superlinear growth of $\\ell_1$ makes the nonnegative polygon slopes uniformly integrable and bounds their total mass. The polygons are thus uniformly bounded and equicontinuous. A uniformly convergent subsequence has an absolutely continuous limit, and the convex integral is lower semicontinuous. Explicitly,\n$$\n\\int_0^T\\ell_1(v)\\,dt\n=\\sup_{f\\in C([0,T])}\\left\\{\\int_0^Tfv\\,dt-\\int_0^T(e^f-1)\\,dt\\right\\}.\n\\tag{Q.0.1x.4}\n$$\nConversely $P_h\\phi\\to\\phi$ uniformly and Jensen's inequality gives $J_h(P_h\\phi)\\le J(\\phi)$. These facts identify the limiting rate and its uniformly compact sublevels. For an open neighborhood of a finite-cost path, choose a sufficiently fine polygon in that neighborhood and make the error exponent in (Q.0.1x.3) smaller than $-J(\\phi)-1$; the finite-dimensional lower bound minus the error probability gives the path lower bound. For closed $F$, use\n$$\nQ_n(F)\\le Q_n(P_hY_n\\in F^\\varepsilon)\n +Q_n(d_{J_1}(Y_n,P_hY_n)>\\varepsilon),\n$$\nwhere $F^\\varepsilon$ is the closed metric enlargement. Bounded approximate polygon costs have a convergent subsequence whose limit lies in $F$ as $h,\\varepsilon\\downarrow0$; unbounded costs give exponent $-\\infty$. This proves the full closed upper bound. The vertex interpolation preserves the endpoint and differs from $Y_n$ by at most $1/n$. Its distance to $P_hY_n$ is bounded by the preceding polygon error plus $1/n$. The identical approximation, compactness and recovery argument in the uniform topology proves its $C$-space assertion. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q01y-finite-real-traps-arithmetic-charges-and-complete-subsequential-classification\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.1y (Finite Real Traps, Arithmetic Charges and Complete Subsequential Classification).** Fix $\\kappa,T>0$, a finite set $Z=\\{z_1<\\cdots<z_r\\}\\subset[0,\\infty)$, exponents $\\alpha_i>0$ and a positive continuous function $g$. Assume\n$$\n\\lambda(x)=g(x)\\prod_{i=1}^r|x-z_i|^{\\alpha_i}\n\\quad\\text{is bounded on }[0,\\infty).\n\\tag{Q.0.1y.1}\n$$\nThe empty product is allowed. Start $X_n$ at zero and give the jump $k/n\\to(k+1)/n$ rate $na_n(k/n)$, where $a_n=\\lambda+e^{-\\kappa n}$. These chains are nonexplosive. Define the nearest nonnegative lattice sites, with upward ties, and their charges by\n$$\nq_{n,i}=\\frac{\\lfloor nz_i+1/2\\rfloor}{n},\\qquad\nc_{n,i}=\\max\\left\\{0,-\\frac1n\\log a_n(q_{n,i})\\right\\}\\in[0,\\kappa].\n\\tag{Q.0.1y.2}\n$$\nThe sites are distinct for all sufficiently large $n$. For $a>0,v\\ge0$, write $\\ell(v,a)=v\\log(v/a)-v+a$, with $\\ell(0,a)=a$, $\\ell(0,0)=0$, $\\ell(v,0)=+\\infty$ for $v>0$, and $\\ell(v,a)=+\\infty$ for $v<0$. On $\\mathcal A$ of Lemma Q.0.1x put\n$$\nS(\\phi)=\\int_0^T\\ell(\\dot\\phi(t),\\lambda(\\phi(t)))\\,dt.\n$$\nAlong every infinite subsequence on which $c_n\\to c$, the laws of $X_n$ have a good speed-$n$ $J_1$ path principle with rate\n$$\nI_c(\\phi)=\\begin{cases}\nS(\\phi)+\\displaystyle\\sum_i c_i\\mathbf1_{\\{\\phi(T)>z_i\\}},&\\phi\\in\\mathcal A,\\\\\n+\\infty,&\\text{otherwise.}\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{Q.0.1y.3}\n$$\nSublevels are uniformly compact. The endpoint-preserving vertex interpolation has the same principle in uniform $C$ space. An infinite subsequence has a speed-$n$ path principle with a lower semicontinuous rate **if and only if** its charge vectors converge. Consequently the full sequence has a principle exactly when $c_n$ converges, and its subsequential rates are exactly $I_c$ for the cluster vectors $c$ of $c_n$. Distinct vectors give distinct rates.\n\nFor $b>0$ with $b\\notin Z$ and $\\tau_{n,b}=\\inf\\{t:X_n(t)\\ge b\\}$, every convergent-charge subsequence also satisfies\n$$\n\\lim-\\frac1n\\log\\mathbb P(\\tau_{n,b}\\le T)\n=\\sum_{z_i<b}c_i+\\sup_{\\eta\\ge0}\\{B_b(\\eta)-\\eta T\\},\\qquad\nB_b(\\eta)=\\int_0^b\\log\\!\\left(1+\\frac\\eta{\\lambda(x)}\\right)dx,\n\\quad B_b(0)=0.\n\\tag{Q.0.1y.4}\n$$\n\n*Proof: uniform moving-prefix estimate.* Set $C(y)=\\int_0^y\\log\\lambda(x)\\,dx$. It is finite and continuous on bounded intervals. For every finite $K$,\n$$\n\\sup_{0\\le j\\le\\lfloor nK\\rfloor}\n\\left|\\frac1n\\sum_{k<j}\\log a_n(k/n)-C(j/n)\n +\\sum_i c_{n,i}\\mathbf1_{\\{j/n>q_{n,i}\\}}\\right|\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{Q.0.1y.5}\n$$\nTo prove this, work on a compact interval containing the endpoint cutoff and the needed zero neighborhoods. Remove the one nearest site for each zero. Every retained site is at distance at least $1/(2n)$ from every zero, including an unchosen tied site. With $A_0=\\sum_i\\alpha_i$ and the compact minimum $g_{\\min}>0$,\n$$\n\\lambda(k/n)\\ge g_{\\min}(2n)^{-A_0},\\qquad\n0\\le\\log a_n(k/n)-\\log\\lambda(k/n)\n\\le g_{\\min}^{-1}(2n)^{A_0}e^{-\\kappa n}.\n$$\nThus the floor contributes a uniformly vanishing normalized prefix error on retained sites. The continuous term $\\log g$ has uniform partial Riemann convergence; removing finitely many samples costs $O(1/n)$. For $\\log|x-z_i|$, the cell attached to the removed site lies within $3/(2n)$ of $z_i$ and has absolute integral $O(\\log n/n)$. The finitely many adjacent cells have that same bound, since their retained sample distances are between $1/(2n)$ and $O(1/n)$. On remaining cells monotone integral comparisons telescope on each side of the zero, again giving $O(\\log n/n)$ uniformly over every prefix. Removing sites chosen for other zeros costs $O(1/n)$ because distinct zeros are separated. Truncated neighborhoods obey the same estimate. Each removed sample present in a prefix contributes exactly $-d_{n,i}$, where $d_{n,i}=-n^{-1}\\log a_n(q_{n,i})$. Its difference from $c_{n,i}$ is at most $n^{-1}\\log\\max\\{1,\\|\\lambda\\|_\\infty+1\\}$, proving (Q.0.1y.5). No Diophantine lower bound on a removed distance was used. Its indicator is at the moving $q_{n,i}$: arrival at that site does not yet include its outgoing rate.\n\n*Proof: exact likelihood and lower bound.* On the closed space $\\mathcal E$ of nonnegative nondecreasing càdlàg paths starting at zero, let\n$$\n\\Phi(x)=C(x(T))-\\int_0^T[\\lambda(x(t))-1]dt.\n$$\nIt is $J_1$-continuous and bounded on endpoint cutoffs: endpoints converge, path values converge at continuity times, and bounded continuous $\\lambda$ permits dominated convergence. Relative to the Poisson law in Lemma Q.0.1x, a history with $j=nx(T)$ has the normalized likelihood, including final survival,\n$$\n\\frac1n\\log\\frac{dP_n}{dQ_n}(x)\n=\\frac1n\\sum_{k<j}\\log a_n(k/n)\n -\\int_0^T[\\lambda(x(t))+e^{-\\kappa n}-1]dt\n=\\Phi(x)-\\sum_i c_{n,i}\\mathbf1_{\\{x(T)>q_{n,i}\\}}+o_K(1)\n\\tag{Q.0.1y.6}\n$$\nuniformly when $x(T)\\le K$. A positive endpoint away from $Z$ has a neighborhood on which its charge converges uniformly to the constant strict-terminal sum. Continuity of $\\Phi$ and the reference lower bound give the lower bound there. The zero path uses its exact no-jump probability $\\exp[-n(\\lambda(0)+e^{-\\kappa n})T]$, with exponent $T\\lambda(0)$. At a positive terminal zero, use $a\\phi$ with $a\\uparrow1$, its endpoint below that zero and above all smaller zeros. It has exactly the required strict-terminal charge, and\n$$\nJ(a\\phi)=a\\int_0^T\\dot\\phi\\log\\dot\\phi\\,dt\n +a\\log(a)\\phi(T)-a\\phi(T)+T\\longrightarrow J(\\phi),\n\\tag{Q.0.1y.7}\n$$\nwhile $\\Phi(a\\phi)\\to\\Phi(\\phi)$. This supplies recovery even when the path waits at its terminal zero.\n\n*Proof: full closed upper bound.* For fixed $\\varepsilon>0$ and large $n$, $q_{n,i}<z_i+\\varepsilon$, so positivity of the charges gives\n$$\n\\sum_i c_{n,i}\\mathbf1_{\\{x(T)>q_{n,i}\\}}\n\\ge P_\\varepsilon(x(T))-\\sum_i|c_{n,i}-c_i|,\n\\qquad P_\\varepsilon(y)=\\sum_i c_i\\mathbf1_{\\{y>z_i+\\varepsilon\\}}.\n\\tag{Q.0.1y.8}\n$$\nThe shifted penalty is lower semicontinuous. For closed $F\\subset\\mathcal E$, divide endpoint space into its finitely many constant-penalty regions and the bounded range of $\\Phi$ into arbitrarily narrow closed strips. Take the closure of each piece and apply the reference closed upper bound. The penalty can only decrease on the closure boundary, giving\n$$\n\\limsup_n\\frac1n\\log P_n(F\\cap\\{X_n(T)\\le K\\})\n\\le-\\inf_{\\substack{\\phi\\in F\\\\\\phi(T)\\le K}}\n [J(\\phi)-\\Phi(\\phi)+P_\\varepsilon(\\phi(T))].\n\\tag{Q.0.1y.9}\n$$\nThe infima converge as $\\varepsilon\\downarrow0$ to the infimum with $P(y)=\\sum_i c_i\\mathbf1_{\\{y>z_i\\}}$. Indeed bounded approximate costs imply bounded $J$ on the cutoff; reference compactness gives a limiting path in $F$. Every strictly crossed limiting zero is eventually crossed with the $\\varepsilon$ margin. Lower semicontinuity of $J$ and continuity of $\\Phi$ give the required liminf. The reverse comparison follows from $P_\\varepsilon\\le P$; the same compactness argument excludes bounded approximate costs when the limiting infimum is infinite. With $M=\\|\\lambda\\|_\\infty+1$, Poisson domination gives, for $K>MT$,\n$$\n\\limsup_n\\frac1n\\log P_n(X_n(T)>K)\n\\le-K\\log\\frac K{MT}+K-MT\\longrightarrow-\\infty.\n\\tag{Q.0.1y.10}\n$$\nThis removes the cutoff. Reference exponential tightness transfers on cutoffs by the upper likelihood bound, and the same endpoint tail handles their complement.\n\n*Proof: action, goodness and topology.* For $\\phi\\in\\mathcal A$, bounded truncation and local absolute integrability of $\\log\\lambda$ give the monotone change of variables\n$$\n\\int_0^T\\dot\\phi(t)\\log\\lambda(\\phi(t))dt=C(\\phi(T)).\n\\tag{Q.0.1y.11}\n$$\nThe derivative vanishes almost everywhere on every fixed zero-level set. Thus $J-\\Phi=S$ as extended integrals; $\\Phi$ is finite at a finite endpoint, so there is no undefined cancellation. Finite $J$ and finite $S$ are equivalent on $\\mathcal A$. Put $M_0=\\max(1,\\|\\lambda\\|_\\infty)$. The estimates\n$$\n\\Phi(\\phi)\\le\\phi(T)\\log M_0+T,\\qquad\nJ(\\phi)\\ge\\phi(T)\\log\\frac{\\phi(T)}T-\\phi(T)+T\n$$\nforce bounded endpoints, then bounded $J$, on $I_c$ sublevels. Lower semicontinuity and reference compactness prove uniform compactness. The vertex interpolation in Lemma Q.0.1x preserves the terminal count and is within $1/n$ of the step path. On cutoffs its $\\Phi$ changes by $o(1)$ while its charges agree exactly; the uniform reference argument therefore proves the stated $C$-space principle.\n\n*Proof: complete subsequence criterion.* The charge vectors lie in the compact cube $[0,\\kappa]^r$. Distinct vectors $c,d$ have a first differing coordinate $j$. Take a positive $b$ after $z_j$ and before the next zero, or beyond the last zero. The linear path $bt/T$ has finite action and\n$$\nI_c(bt/T)-I_d(bt/T)=\\sum_{i\\le j}(c_i-d_i)=c_j-d_j\\ne0.\n$$\nIf charge vectors on a subsequence do not converge, two different cluster vectors give two further subsequences with these distinct good rates. A hypothetical path principle on the original subsequence would be inherited by both; uniqueness of a lower semicontinuous rate on the metric path space contradicts the difference. Convergence suffices by the preceding proof. The empty-vector case is automatic.\n\n*Proof: direct hitting law.* Put $L_n=\\lceil nb\\rceil$. Independent exponential holding times give, for $\\eta>0$,\n$$\nZ_n(\\eta)=\\mathbb E e^{-n\\eta\\tau_{n,b}}\n=\\prod_{k<L_n}\\frac{a_n(k/n)}{a_n(k/n)+\\eta},\\qquad\n-\\frac1n\\log Z_n(\\eta)\\longrightarrow\\sum_{z_i<b}c_i+B_b(\\eta).\n\\tag{Q.0.1y.12}\n$$\nHere $b\\notin Z$ fixes every prefix indicator eventually; the denominator has ordinary continuous Riemann convergence. Local logarithmic integrability makes $B_b$ finite and continuous at zero. Its derivative $t_b(\\eta)=\\int_0^b(\\lambda+\\eta)^{-1}dx$ decreases continuously and strictly from $t_0\\in(0,\\infty]$ to zero. When $T<t_0$, the unique maximizer solves $t_b(\\eta_T)=T$; for $T\\ge t_0$ the supremum is zero. Chernoff gives one exponent bound. For the other, change the holding rates to $n(a_n+\\eta)$. Their sum has mean tending to $t_b(\\eta)$, variance at most $L_n/(n^2\\eta^2)=O(1/n)$, and original-to-tilted density $Z_n(\\eta)e^{n\\eta\\tau}$. For $T<t_0$ choose $t_b(\\eta)=s<T$, localize near $s$, then take $s\\uparrow T$. For $T\\ge t_0<\\infty$, every positive tilt has mean strictly below $T$; take $n$ first, then $\\eta\\downarrow0$, using $B_b(\\eta)\\to0$ and $\\eta t_b(\\eta)\\le\\eta t_0\\to0$. This includes $T=t_0$ and proves (Q.0.1y.4) directly. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q01y1-algebraic-admission-parity-and-an-irrational-counterexample\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.1y.1 (Algebraic Admission, Parity and an Irrational Counterexample).** For rational $z=p/q$ in lowest terms, indices divisible by $q$ have charge $\\kappa$; on nonadmitted indices $|k/n-z|\\ge1/(qn)$, so the charge tends to zero. For an algebraic irrational zero with integer minimal polynomial $P$ of degree $d$, $n^dP(k/n)$ is a nonzero integer. On a compact interval, the mean-value bound $|P(k/n)|\\le C|k/n-z|$ therefore gives a polynomial lower separation and charge tending to zero. The finite congruence classes modulo the least common multiple of rational denominators give the exact admitted-zero rates. The same argument works with polynomial lattice separation in place of algebraicity.\n\nIn particular, take $\\lambda(x)=x^2(x-1/2)^2$ for $x\\le1$ and $\\lambda(x)=1/4$ for $x>1$. The even and odd subsequences have respectively\n$$\nI_{\\mathrm{even}}=S+\\kappa\\mathbf1_{\\{\\phi(T)>0\\}}\n +\\kappa\\mathbf1_{\\{\\phi(T)>1/2\\}},\\qquad\nI_{\\mathrm{odd}}=S+\\kappa\\mathbf1_{\\{\\phi(T)>0\\}}.\n\\tag{Q.0.1y.13}\n$$\nFor $b\\in(1/2,1)$ their hitting exponents are $2\\kappa+J_b(T)$ and $\\kappa+J_b(T)$. Here $t_0=\\infty$, so the positive optimizer exists uniquely for every $T>0$ and $J_b(T)>0$. Nonzero grid rates on $[0,1]$ are at least $1/(64n^2)$, and the prefix error is $O(\\log n/n)+O(n^2e^{-\\kappa n})$. Arrival at $1/2$ on an even grid does not incur the second charge; departure does.\n\nFor genuinely different irrational charges, set $a_1=1$, $a_{j+1}=10^{2a_j}$, $Q_j=10^{a_j}$ and\n$$\nz=\\sum_{j\\ge1}10^{-a_j},\\qquad p_j=Q_j\\sum_{i\\le j}10^{-a_i}\\in\\mathbb Z.\n$$\nThen $0<z<1/2$ and\n$$\n10^{-Q_j^2}\\le z-p_j/Q_j\\le2\\,10^{-Q_j^2}.\n\\tag{Q.0.1y.14}\n$$\nThe infinitely many nonzero decimal digits with unbounded intervening gaps make $z$ irrational; the bound beats every fixed rational-power approximation. Take $\\lambda=x^2(x-z)^2$ on $[0,1]$, continued constantly as $(1-z)^2$ after $1$. It satisfies (Q.0.1y.1), with $g=1$ up to $1$ and $g=(1-z)^2/[x^2(x-z)^2]$ thereafter. On $n=Q_j$ the nearest interior rate is at most $C10^{-2Q_j^2}$, negligible against the floor, so its charge tends to $\\kappa$. On $n=Q_j+1$,\n$$\nnz=p_j+z+Q_j(z-p_j/Q_j)=p_j+z+o(1),\n\\qquad n|z-p_j/n|\\longrightarrow z.\n$$\nIts nearest rate is bounded above and below by positive multiples of $n^{-2}$, so that charge tends to zero. The initial charge remains $\\kappa$. Thus the two subsequences have rates $S+\\kappa\\mathbf1_{\\{\\phi(T)>0\\}}+\\kappa\\mathbf1_{\\{\\phi(T)>z\\}}$ and $S+\\kappa\\mathbf1_{\\{\\phi(T)>0\\}}$, and their hitting exponents beyond $z$ differ by $\\kappa$. Both have the same limiting Hamiltonian $H(x,p)=\\lambda(x)(e^p-1)$, since the floor vanishes in the smooth-test nonlinear generator limit. They have no common full-sequence path principle by Theorem Q.0.1y. This construction concerns asymptotic laws, with no finite-size numerical premise. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q01z-finite-logarithmic-defect-measures-and-non-power-traps\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.1z (Finite Logarithmic Defect Measures and Non-Power Traps).** Let $\\lambda:[0,\\infty)\\to[0,\\infty)$ be bounded and continuous with a finite zero set $Z=\\{z_1<\\cdots<z_r\\}$, possibly empty, and locally integrable $|\\log\\lambda|$. Define $a_n$, $X_n$, $\\mathcal A$ and $S$ as in Theorem Q.0.1y, without assuming its power factorization. Fix $M\\ge\\max(1,\\|\\lambda\\|_\\infty+1)$ and suppose, on an infinite subsequence, that the positive measures\n$$\n\\mu_n=\\frac1n\\sum_{k\\ge0}\\log\\frac M{a_n(k/n)}\\,\\delta_{k/n}\n\\quad\\text{converge vaguely to a locally finite }\\mu.\n\\tag{Q.0.1z.1}\n$$\nIt suffices to have weak convergence of restrictions to one $[0,L]$ with $L>\\max Z$ and $\\lambda(L)>0$; for empty $Z$, any $L>0$ suffices. A bound on $\\mu_n([0,L])$ gives a further weakly convergent subsequence, but such a bound is an additional hypothesis. Necessarily, with $f=\\log(M/\\lambda)$ off $Z$,\n$$\n\\mu=f(x)\\,dx+\\sum_i c_i\\delta_{z_i},\\qquad 0\\le c_i<\\infty.\n\\tag{Q.0.1z.2}\n$$\nThese atoms are independent of $M$ and may exceed $\\kappa$. Along the given subsequence, the good $J_1$ path rate, its uniform-interpolation version and the direct hitting law for $b>0$, $b\\notin Z$, are exactly (Q.0.1y.3) and (Q.0.1y.4) with these $c_i$.\n\n*Proof: defect and moving cumulative endpoints.* Away from $Z$, the log weights converge locally uniformly to $f$, so the limit equals $f\\,dx$ there. For $R>0$, the cap $f_R=\\min(f,R)$, extended by $R$ at each zero, is continuous and\n$$\n\\log\\frac M{a_n}\\ge f_R-\\log\\!\\left(1+\\frac{e^{-\\kappa n}e^R}{M}\\right).\n\\tag{Q.0.1z.3}\n$$\nThis follows by factoring out $\\lambda$ when $\\lambda\\ge Me^{-R}$ and comparing with $Me^{-R}+e^{-\\kappa n}$ otherwise. Test against nonnegative compactly supported continuous functions and pass through Riemann and vague convergence, then $R\\uparrow\\infty$. Thus $\\mu\\ge f\\,dx$. Its difference is a nonnegative measure on finite $Z$, giving (Q.0.1z.2). Changing $M$ adds the same constant multiple of Lebesgue measure to the limit and its density, leaving the atoms fixed. Positivity outside the cutoff gives ordinary local Riemann convergence and no boundary atom there.\n\nSet $F(y)=\\int_0^y f(x)dx$ and $C(y)=y\\log M-F(y)$. At a lattice endpoint $y=j/n$,\n$$\n\\frac1n\\sum_{k<j}\\log a_n(k/n)=y\\log M-\\mu_n([0,y)).\n\\tag{Q.0.1z.4}\n$$\nThis half-open interval counts departures. On a compact interval of positive endpoints avoiding $Z$, monotonicity and a finite continuity mesh make the cumulative convergence uniform:\n$$\n\\mu_n([0,y))\\longrightarrow F(y)+\\sum_{z_i<y}c_i.\n\\tag{Q.0.1z.5}\n$$\nIn the relative half-line, $0$ is not a boundary obstruction for $[0,y]$ at a positive continuity endpoint; an origin atom is retained. At arbitrary endpoints, the needed substitute is the one-sided bound: for fixed $K,\\varepsilon>0$ there is $e_n\\to0$ such that\n$$\n\\mu_n([0,y))\\ge F(y)+\\sum_i c_i\\mathbf1_{\\{y>z_i+\\varepsilon\\}}\n -\\omega_F(\\varepsilon)-e_n\\quad(0\\le y\\le K),\n\\tag{Q.0.1z.6}\n$$\nwhere $\\omega_F$ is the modulus of continuity on $[0,K]$. Choose finitely many continuity points, with sufficiently small gaps, so that for each $y\\ge\\varepsilon$ one lies in $[y-\\varepsilon,y-\\varepsilon/2]$. Refine near zero if necessary. Then $[0,s]\\subset[0,y)$, all shifted atoms lie below $s$, and $F(s)\\ge F(y)-\\omega_F(\\varepsilon)$. Weak convergence on this finite mesh and positivity prove the bound. For $y<\\varepsilon$, no shifted atom is active and positivity with $F(y)\\le\\omega_F(\\varepsilon)$ suffices.\n\n*Proof: path and hitting estimates.* Lemma Q.0.1x supplies the reference. Use the same $\\Phi$ and exact normalized likelihood in (Q.0.1y.6), with the exact prefix (Q.0.1z.4). Uniform convergence (Q.0.1z.5) gives its local lower bound at positive endpoints outside $Z$. The zero path again has exponent $T\\lambda(0)$ even if its origin atom is larger than $\\kappa$. Scaling $a\\phi$ from below, with (Q.0.1y.7), recovers positive terminal zeros and their strict-terminal charge. By (Q.0.1z.6), on the endpoint cutoff the log likelihood is at most\n$$\n\\Phi(x)-\\sum_i c_i\\mathbf1_{\\{x(T)>z_i+\\varepsilon\\}}\n +\\omega_F(\\varepsilon)+o(1).\n\\tag{Q.0.1z.7}\n$$\nThe finite endpoint-region and closed-$\\Phi$-strip argument in the proof of (Q.0.1y.9) gives, for closed $H\\subset\\mathcal E$,\n$$\n\\limsup_n\\frac1n\\log P_n(H\\cap\\{X_n(T)\\le K\\})\n\\le\\omega_F(\\varepsilon)-\\inf_{\\substack{\\phi\\in H\\\\\\phi(T)\\le K}}\n \\left[J(\\phi)-\\Phi(\\phi)+\\sum_i c_i\\mathbf1_{\\{\\phi(T)>z_i+\\varepsilon\\}}\\right].\n$$\nBounded approximate costs imply bounded $J$, hence a compact limiting path. Every strictly crossed zero is eventually charged; letting $\\varepsilon\\downarrow0$ gives the strict-terminal infimum. The endpoint tail (Q.0.1y.10) removes $K$ and transfers exponential tightness. Local absolute log-integrability gives (Q.0.1y.11) by truncation, so $J-\\Phi=S$ without infinite cancellation. The same Jensen bounds prove goodness and uniform compactness. Exact endpoint preservation and a cutoff $o(1)$ change of $\\Phi$ give the interpolation assertion.\n\nFor hitting, the exact product (Q.0.1y.12) still applies. Equation (Q.0.1z.5) at $\\lceil nb\\rceil/n\\to b$ identifies its numerator, and the positive-tilt denominator has ordinary Riemann convergence. Thus its limit is $\\sum_{z_i<b}c_i+B_b(\\eta)$. The tilted mean, variance and density in the proof of Theorem Q.0.1y apply unchanged. Taking positive tilts first also handles $T=t_0<\\infty$. This proves the direct exponent, without a hitting-map contraction. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"example-q01z1-a-finite-defect-larger-than-the-exponential-floor\"></a>**Example Q.0.1z.1 (A Finite Defect Larger Than the Exponential Floor).** Fix $n_1\\ge10$ and $n_{j+1}=2^{n_j^2}$. Put $a_j=1/n_j$, $w_j=n_j^{-4}$, $H_j=2\\kappa n_j$, $\\psi(u)=\\max(1-|u|,0)$, and\n$$\nB(x)=\\sum_{j\\ge1}H_j\\psi\\!\\left(\\frac{x-a_j}{w_j}\\right),\\qquad\n\\lambda(x)=\\begin{cases}0,&x=0,\\\\x^2e^{-B(x)},&0<x\\le1,\\\\1,&x>1.\\end{cases}\n\\tag{Q.0.1z.8}\n$$\nThe supports are disjoint, contained in $(0,1)$ and locally finite away from zero. Since $0\\le\\lambda(x)\\le x^2$, the rate is bounded and continuous, with sole zero $0$, and\n$$\n\\int_0^1|\\log\\lambda(x)|dx=2+2\\kappa\\sum_j n_j^{-3}<\\infty.\n\\tag{Q.0.1z.9}\n$$\nOutside the supports $\\lambda=x^2$, forcing exponent $2$ in any putative positive continuous power factorization at zero; at their centers $\\lambda(a_j)/a_j^2=e^{-2\\kappa n_j}\\to0$, contradicting such a factor.\n\nIts log measures converge to $f\\,dx+2\\kappa\\delta_0$ on $n=n_j$, and to $f\\,dx+\\kappa\\delta_0$ on $n=n_j+1$. To verify this, compare with the base rate $\\lambda_0=x^2$ on $[0,1]$, continued as $1$. Removing the exact zero sample and using monotone log Riemann sums gives its limit $f_0\\,dx+\\kappa\\delta_0$; nonzero samples have rate at least $n^{-2}$ and negligible floor error. On notch supports the log difference is\n$$\nD_n(x)=\\log\\frac{x^2+e^{-\\kappa n}}{x^2e^{-B(x)}+e^{-\\kappa n}},\\qquad0\\le D_n\\le B.\n$$\nFor $j=1$ the previous-notch family is empty. For $j\\ge2$ and previous notches $i<j$, $x\\ge1/(2n_{j-1})$ and $B\\le2\\kappa n_{j-1}$, giving\n$$\n|D_n-B|\\le4n_{j-1}^2e^{2\\kappa n_{j-1}-\\kappa n}\\longrightarrow0\n$$\non both schedules. The variation of the previous-notch sum is $2\\sum_{i<j}H_i$; its prefix Riemann discrepancy is at most that variation divided by $n$, tending to zero. Its integrals converge in $L^1$ to $B$, so it contributes $B\\,dx$ without an atom. At $n=n_j$ the current support contains exactly $a_j$, and\n$$\n\\frac1{n_j}D_{n_j}(a_j)\n=\\frac1{n_j}\\log\\frac{n_j^{-2}+e^{-\\kappa n_j}}\n {n_j^{-2}e^{-2\\kappa n_j}+e^{-\\kappa n_j}}\\longrightarrow\\kappa.\n$$\nThis site tends to zero. At $n=n_j+1$, its closest positive site is separated from $a_j$ by $1/[n_j(n_j+1)]>w_j$, so the current notch is missed. All future supports lie below $1/(2n_j)$ and exclude zero, hence contain no sampled site on either schedule; their continuum integrals tend to zero. These previous/current/future comparisons also hold against compactly supported continuous tests and prove the two measure limits. Theorem Q.0.1z therefore gives distinct good path rates, differing by $\\kappa$ on every nonzero finite-action path, and hitting exponents to any $b>0$ differing by $\\kappa$. Uniqueness rules out a common full-sequence path principle. The atom $2\\kappa$ comes from two outgoing sites collapsing onto one continuum zero, and exceeds the single-site charge in Theorem Q.0.1y. $\\square$\n\nTheorems Q.0.1y–z concern finite-zero one-direction pure-birth sources. Their departure charges and logarithmic-measure hypothesis identify the microscopic information needed in addition to a limiting local Hamiltonian. Theorem Q.0.1r treats its separate fixed-sector marked-history accessibility class; neither theorem identifies an arbitrary multidirectional physical source, an infinite barrier or a fixed-endpoint obstacle recovery.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q01e-degenerate-torus-diffusions-and-jump-generator-hamiltonians\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.1e (Degenerate Torus Diffusions and Jump-Generator Hamiltonians).**\n\n1. *Degenerate constant noise.* Keep the torus $\\mathbb T^d$, the Lipschitz drift $b$ and the starting point $x_0$ of Theorem Q.0.1d, let $\\sigma\\in\\mathbb R^{d\\times m}$ be an arbitrary constant matrix, possibly of rank below $d$, let $W$ be an $m$-dimensional Brownian motion, and put $a:=\\sigma\\sigma^T$ with Moore--Penrose inverse $a^+$. The laws of\n   $$\n   dX_t^\\eta=b(X_t^\\eta)dt+\\sqrt{2\\eta}\\,\\sigma\\,dW_t,\n   \\qquad X_0^\\eta=x_0,\n   \\tag{Q.0.1e.1}\n   $$\n   satisfy a good large-deviation principle on $C([0,T],\\mathbb T^d)$ with speed $1/\\eta$ and rate\n   $$\n   I_{\\mathrm{PU}}[\\gamma]\n   =\\frac14\\int_0^T\n   (\\dot\\gamma-b(\\gamma))^Ta^+(\\dot\\gamma-b(\\gamma))\\,dt\n   \\tag{Q.0.1e.2}\n   $$\n   when $\\gamma$ is absolutely continuous, $\\gamma(0)=x_0$ and $\\dot\\gamma(t)-b(\\gamma(t))\\in\\operatorname{ran}a$ for almost every $t$, and $I_{\\mathrm{PU}}[\\gamma]=+\\infty$ otherwise. The nonlinear-generator identity (Q.0.1d.2) holds unchanged, and the Legendre transform of $H(x,p)=b(x)\\cdot p+p^Tap$ equals $\\frac14(v-b(x))^Ta^+(v-b(x))$ where $v-b(x)\\in\\operatorname{ran}a$ and $+\\infty$ elsewhere. Along every finite-cost path the velocity component in $\\ker a$ equals that of the drift, and (Q.0.1d.3) is the invertible case $a^+=a^{-1}$.\n2. *Jump generators.* Let $Z\\subset\\mathbb R^d\\setminus\\{0\\}$ be finite and let $\\lambda_\\zeta:\\mathbb T^d\\to[0,\\infty)$, $\\zeta\\in Z$, be continuous. For\n   $$\n   \\mathcal G_\\eta^Jf(x)\n   :=\\frac1\\eta\\sum_{\\zeta\\in Z}\\lambda_\\zeta(x)\\bigl[f(x+\\eta\\zeta)-f(x)\\bigr]\n   \\tag{Q.0.1e.3}\n   $$\n   and every $f\\in C^1(\\mathbb T^d)$, uniformly in $x$,\n   $$\n   \\eta e^{-f/\\eta}\\mathcal G_\\eta^Je^{f/\\eta}\n   \\longrightarrow H_J(x,\\nabla f),\n   \\qquad\n   H_J(x,p):=\\sum_{\\zeta\\in Z}\\lambda_\\zeta(x)\\bigl(e^{p\\cdot\\zeta}-1\\bigr).\n   \\tag{Q.0.1e.4}\n   $$\n   The limiting Hamiltonian $H_J(x,\\cdot)$ determines the rates $(\\lambda_\\zeta(x))_{\\zeta\\in Z}$ uniquely. Its Legendre transform $L_J(x,v):=\\sup_p\\{p\\cdot v-H_J(x,p)\\}$ is convex and nonnegative, vanishes exactly at the mean drift $v=\\sum_\\zeta\\lambda_\\zeta(x)\\zeta$, and is finite exactly on the convex cone generated by $Z_x:=\\{\\zeta:\\lambda_\\zeta(x)>0\\}$, where\n   $$\n   L_J(x,v)\\le\\sum_{\\zeta\\in Z_x}\\lambda_\\zeta(x)\\,\\theta\\!\\left(\\frac{\\mu_\\zeta}{\\lambda_\\zeta(x)}\\right),\n   \\qquad\n   \\theta(r):=r\\ln r-r+1,\\quad\\theta(0):=1,\n   \\tag{Q.0.1e.5}\n   $$\n   for every decomposition $v=\\sum_{\\zeta\\in Z_x}\\mu_\\zeta\\zeta$ with $\\mu_\\zeta\\ge0$.\n3. *Second-order generator data do not fix the rate.* For $d=1$, $Z=\\{1,-1\\}$ and constant $\\lambda_{\\pm1}=\\lambda>0$, the generator (Q.0.1e.3) satisfies $\\mathcal G_\\eta^Jf=\\eta\\lambda f''+O(\\eta^3)$ for smooth $f$, which has the drift and diffusion coefficients of (Q.0.1d.1) with $b=0$ and $a=\\lambda$. The lifted endpoint laws on $\\mathbb R$ started at $0$ satisfy large-deviation principles with speed $1/\\eta$ and rates $TL_J(x/T)$ for the jump process and $TL_D(x/T)$ for the diffusion, where\n   $$\n   L_J(v)=v\\operatorname{arsinh}\\frac{v}{2\\lambda}-\\sqrt{v^2+4\\lambda^2}+2\\lambda\n   <L_D(v)=\\frac{v^2}{4\\lambda}\n   \\qquad(v\\ne0).\n   \\tag{Q.0.1e.6}\n   $$\n   Hence the two branches assign different exponents to $\\{X_T^\\eta\\ge x\\}$ for every $x>0$.\n\n*Proof.* Item 1. Periodically lift $b$ to a bounded globally Lipschitz field $\\tilde b$ with constant $L_b$, lift $x_0$ to $\\tilde x_0$, and let $\\Psi(w)$ be the unique solution of $\\phi(t)=\\tilde x_0+\\int_0^t\\tilde b(\\phi(s))\\,ds+\\sigma w(t)$ for $w\\in C_0([0,T];\\mathbb R^m)$. Gronwall's inequality gives $\\|\\Psi(w_1)-\\Psi(w_2)\\|_\\infty\\le\\|\\sigma\\|e^{L_bT}\\|w_1-w_2\\|_\\infty$, and the lifted solution of (Q.0.1e.1) is $\\Psi(\\sqrt{2\\eta}W)$ pathwise. Schilder's theorem (Dembo and Zeitouni 1998, Theorem 5.2.3) gives the laws of $\\sqrt{2\\eta}W$ a good principle with speed $1/(2\\eta)$ and rate $\\frac12\\int_0^T|\\dot w|^2dt$ on absolutely continuous $w$ with square-integrable derivative, and $+\\infty$ otherwise; at speed $1/\\eta$ the rate is $\\frac14\\int_0^T|\\dot w|^2dt$. The contraction principle (Dembo and Zeitouni 1998, Theorem 4.2.1) for the continuous map $\\pi\\circ\\Psi$, with $\\pi$ the torus projection, gives a good principle with rate $\\inf\\{\\frac14\\int_0^T|\\dot w|^2dt:\\pi\\Psi(w)=\\gamma\\}$. A torus path from $x_0$ has a unique continuous lift $\\tilde\\gamma$ from $\\tilde x_0$, and $\\pi\\Psi(w)=\\gamma$ exactly when $\\sigma w(t)=\\tilde\\gamma(t)-\\tilde x_0-\\int_0^t\\tilde b(\\tilde\\gamma)\\,ds$. If this holds for a finite-energy $w$, then $\\tilde\\gamma$ is absolutely continuous and $r:=\\dot{\\tilde\\gamma}-\\tilde b(\\tilde\\gamma)=\\sigma\\dot w\\in\\operatorname{ran}\\sigma=\\operatorname{ran}a$ almost everywhere; the least-norm solution of $\\sigma u=r$ is $\\sigma^+r$ and $(\\sigma^+)^T\\sigma^+=(\\sigma\\sigma^T)^+=a^+$, so $|\\dot w|^2\\ge r^Ta^+r$. Conversely, if $r\\in\\operatorname{ran}a$ almost everywhere with $\\int_0^Tr^Ta^+r\\,dt<\\infty$, then $\\dot w:=\\sigma^+r$ is square integrable and $\\sigma\\sigma^+r=r$, because $\\sigma\\sigma^+$ is the orthogonal projection onto $\\operatorname{ran}\\sigma$, so the bound is attained. If $\\tilde\\gamma$ is not absolutely continuous or $r\\notin\\operatorname{ran}a$ on a set of positive measure, no finite-energy $w$ exists. This proves (Q.0.1e.2). The computation of (Q.0.1d.2) does not use invertibility. If $v-b\\notin\\operatorname{ran}a$, its nonzero component $q\\in\\ker a=(\\operatorname{ran}a)^\\perp$ gives $p\\cdot(v-b)-p^Tap=t|q|^2\\to\\infty$ along $p=tq$; if $v-b=r\\in\\operatorname{ran}a$, the concave quadratic is maximized at $p=\\frac12a^+r$, where $aa^+r=r$ and $a^+aa^+=a^+$ give the value $\\frac14r^Ta^+r$.\n\nItem 2. By the mean-value theorem $(f(x+\\eta\\zeta)-f(x))/\\eta=\\zeta\\cdot\\nabla f(x+\\vartheta\\eta\\zeta)$ for some $\\vartheta\\in(0,1)$, which converges uniformly to $\\zeta\\cdot\\nabla f(x)$ by uniform continuity of $\\nabla f$ on the compact torus; the rates are bounded and the exponential is uniformly continuous on bounded sets, which proves (Q.0.1e.4). Choose $q$ with the numbers $c_\\zeta:=q\\cdot\\zeta$ nonzero and pairwise distinct; then $H_J(x,tq)=\\sum_\\zeta\\lambda_\\zeta(x)e^{tc_\\zeta}-\\sum_\\zeta\\lambda_\\zeta(x)$, and linear independence of $1$ and the exponentials $e^{tc_\\zeta}$ determines every $\\lambda_\\zeta(x)$. The Legendre transform is a supremum of affine functions and hence convex, and $p=0$ gives $L_J\\ge0$. Since $H_J(x,\\cdot)$ is convex, differentiable and vanishes at $0$, $L_J(x,v)=0$ exactly when $v$ is its gradient at $0$, namely $\\sum_\\zeta\\lambda_\\zeta(x)\\zeta$. If $v=\\sum_{\\zeta\\in Z_x}\\mu_\\zeta\\zeta$ with $\\mu_\\zeta\\ge0$, the termwise bound $\\mu_\\zeta s-\\lambda_\\zeta(e^s-1)\\le\\lambda_\\zeta\\theta(\\mu_\\zeta/\\lambda_\\zeta)$ for all real $s$ gives (Q.0.1e.5). If $v$ lies outside the finitely generated, hence closed, cone, Farkas' lemma gives $q$ with $q\\cdot\\zeta\\le0$ on $Z_x$ and $q\\cdot v>0$, and $p=tq$ gives $L_J(x,v)\\ge tq\\cdot v\\to\\infty$.\n\nItem 3. Taylor expansion of $f(x\\pm\\eta)$ to fourth order gives the generator expansion. The lifted jump endpoint is $\\eta(N_+-N_-)$ with independent Poisson counts of mean $\\lambda T/\\eta$, so $\\eta\\ln\\mathbb E\\exp(pX_T^\\eta/\\eta)=\\lambda T(e^p+e^{-p}-2)$ for every $\\eta$; the diffusion endpoint is centered Gaussian with variance $2\\eta\\lambda T$, giving $\\lambda Tp^2$. Both limits are finite and differentiable, so the Gärtner--Ellis theorem (Dembo and Zeitouni 1998, Theorem 2.3.6) gives the two principles with the Legendre transforms $TL_J(x/T)$ and $TL_D(x/T)$ of $TH_J$ and $TH_D$, where $H_J(p)=2\\lambda(\\cosh p-1)$ and $H_D(p)=\\lambda p^2$. Solving $v=2\\lambda\\sinh p$ gives $p=\\operatorname{arsinh}(v/(2\\lambda))$ and the closed form of $L_J$. Since $\\cosh p-1>p^2/2$ for $p\\ne0$, $H_J>H_D$ off $0$; for $v\\ne0$ the maximizer $p_v$ of $pv-H_J(p)$ is nonzero, so $L_J(v)=p_vv-H_J(p_v)<p_vv-H_D(p_v)\\le L_D(v)$. Both rates are continuous and strictly increasing on $[0,\\infty)$, so the infima over $(x,\\infty)$ and $[x,\\infty)$ coincide and the half-line exponent is $-TL(x/T)$ in each case. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q01g-exact-boundary-entrance-cost-for-vanishing-floor-birth-sources\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.1g (Exact Boundary-Entrance Cost for Vanishing-Floor Birth Sources).** Let $a>0$ and let $\\lambda:[0,a]\\to[0,\\infty)$ be continuous and nondecreasing, with\n$$\n\\lambda(0)=0,\\qquad \\lambda(x)>0\\quad(0<x\\le a),\\qquad\n\\int_0^a|\\log\\lambda(x)|\\,dx<\\infty,\\qquad\n\\int_0^a\\frac{dx}{\\lambda(x)}=\\infty.\n\\tag{Q.0.1g.1}\n$$\nLet $m_n\\ge1$ be integers with $m_n/n\\le a$ and $m_n/n\\to a$, and let $\\delta_n>0$ tend to zero with $-n^{-1}\\log\\delta_n\\to\\kappa\\in[0,\\infty]$. Start the finite pure-birth chain $X_n$ at zero, give its jump from $k/n$ to $(k+1)/n$ rate $n[\\lambda(k/n)+\\delta_n]$ for $0\\le k<m_n$, and absorb it at $m_n/n$. Write $\\tau_n$ for its absorption time. Then, for every fixed $T>0$,\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}-\\frac1n\\log\\mathbb P(\\tau_n\\le T)\n=\\kappa+J_\\lambda(T),\\qquad\nJ_\\lambda(T):=\\sup_{\\theta\\ge0}\\left\\{A_\\lambda(\\theta)-\\theta T\\right\\},\n\\quad\nA_\\lambda(\\theta):=\\int_0^a\\log\\left(1+\\frac\\theta{\\lambda(x)}\\right)dx.\n\\tag{Q.0.1g.2}\n$$\nHere $A_\\lambda(0):=0$ and the right side is $+\\infty$ when $\\kappa=\\infty$. There is a unique maximizer $\\theta_T>0$, characterized by\n$$\n\\int_0^a\\frac{dx}{\\lambda(x)+\\theta_T}=T,\n\\qquad J_\\lambda(T)=A_\\lambda(\\theta_T)-\\theta_T T>0.\n\\tag{Q.0.1g.3}\n$$\nNo monotonicity or prescribed rate of decay of the sequence $\\delta_n$ is required. In particular the theorem includes $m_n=\\lfloor an\\rfloor$, after discarding any initial zero values.\n\nThe local Hamiltonian $H(x,p)=\\lambda(x)(e^p-1)$ has conjugate\n$$\nL(x,v)=v\\log\\frac v{\\lambda(x)}-v+\\lambda(x)\\quad(x>0,v\\ge0),\n\\qquad L(x,v)=+\\infty\\quad(x>0,v<0),\n\\tag{Q.0.1g.4}\n$$\nwith $0\\log0=0$, and $L(0,0)=0$, $L(0,v)=+\\infty$ for $v\\ne0$. Among absolutely continuous paths in $[0,a]$ from zero to $a$ in time $T$, the minimum of $\\int_0^T L(x,\\dot x)dt$ is exactly $J_\\lambda(T)$. Its unique minimizer is the inverse of the strictly increasing function\n$$\nF_{\\theta_T}(u):=\\int_0^u\\frac{dx}{\\lambda(x)+\\theta_T},\n\\qquad x(t)=F_{\\theta_T}^{-1}(t),\\qquad\n\\dot x=\\lambda(x)+\\theta_T.\n\\tag{Q.0.1g.5}\n$$\nThus the transition exponent has the additional initial entrance cost $\\kappa$, which is absent from the limiting local Hamiltonian and its integrated conjugate. This is an exact hitting-probability theorem, not a full path-space large-deviation principle.\n\n*Proof.* Put $\\lambda_{n,k}:=\\lambda(k/n)+\\delta_n$. The holding times $E_{n,k}$ are independent exponentials of rates $n\\lambda_{n,k}$ and $\\tau_n=\\sum_{k=0}^{m_n-1}E_{n,k}$. Consequently, for $\\theta>0$,\n$$\nZ_n(\\theta):=\\mathbb E e^{-n\\theta\\tau_n}\n=\\prod_{k=0}^{m_n-1}\\frac{\\lambda_{n,k}}{\\lambda_{n,k}+\\theta},\n\\qquad A_n(\\theta):=-\\frac1n\\log Z_n(\\theta)\n=\\frac1n\\sum_{k=0}^{m_n-1}\\log\\left(1+\\frac\\theta{\\lambda_{n,k}}\\right).\n\\tag{Q.0.1g.6}\n$$\nThe zero-state term is $n^{-1}\\log(1+\\theta/\\delta_n)=-n^{-1}\\log\\delta_n+n^{-1}\\log(\\delta_n+\\theta)\\to\\kappa$.\n\nFor the remaining terms put $f_\\delta(x):=\\log(1+\\theta/(\\lambda(x)+\\delta))$. The function $f_0$ is decreasing and integrable: where $\\lambda\\le1$, it is bounded by $\\log(1+\\theta)+|\\log\\lambda|$, and where $\\lambda\\ge1$, by $\\log(1+\\theta)$. On $[\\varepsilon,a]$, $f_{\\delta_n}\\to f_0$ uniformly. Near zero, comparison of right endpoints with preceding cells gives\n$$\n\\frac1n\\sum_{1\\le k\\le n\\varepsilon} f_{\\delta_n}(k/n)\n\\le\\frac1n\\sum_{1\\le k\\le n\\varepsilon} f_0(k/n)\n\\le\\int_0^\\varepsilon f_0(x)dx\\longrightarrow0\n\\quad(\\varepsilon\\downarrow0).\n\\tag{Q.0.1g.7}\n$$\nOrdinary Riemann convergence away from zero, including the moving endpoint $m_n/n\\to a$, therefore proves $A_n(\\theta)\\to\\kappa+A_\\lambda(\\theta)$ when $\\kappa<\\infty$.\n\nFor $\\theta>0$, bounded differentiation on compact positive $\\theta$ intervals gives $A_\\lambda'(\\theta)=t(\\theta):=\\int_0^a(\\lambda(x)+\\theta)^{-1}dx$. This is continuous and strictly decreasing, tending to infinity at zero by (Q.0.1g.1) and monotone convergence, and to zero at infinity since $t(\\theta)\\le a/\\theta$. Also $A_\\lambda(\\theta)\\to0$ as $\\theta\\downarrow0$, by domination by the integrable integrand at $\\theta=1$. Thus $A_\\lambda(\\theta)-\\theta T$ has the unique positive maximizer (Q.0.1g.3), and its maximum is positive.\n\nOn $\\{\\tau_n\\le T\\}$, $e^{-n\\theta\\tau_n}\\ge e^{-n\\theta T}$, so\n$$\n\\mathbb P(\\tau_n\\le T)\\le e^{-n[A_n(\\theta)-\\theta T]}.\n\\tag{Q.0.1g.8}\n$$\nFor finite $\\kappa$, taking a liminf of the negative normalized logarithm and optimizing over $\\theta>0$ gives the lower bound $\\kappa+J_\\lambda(T)$ on that exponent.\n\nFor the reverse bound, define $Q_{n,\\theta}$ by making the independent holding times exponential with rates $n(\\lambda_{n,k}+\\theta)$. Direct comparison of their densities gives\n$$\n\\frac{d\\mathbb P}{dQ_{n,\\theta}}\n=Z_n(\\theta)e^{n\\theta\\tau_n},\\qquad\n\\mu_n(\\theta):=\\mathbb E_Q\\tau_n\n=\\frac1n\\sum_{k=0}^{m_n-1}\\frac1{\\lambda_{n,k}+\\theta}\\longrightarrow t(\\theta),\n\\qquad\nv_n(\\theta):=\\operatorname{Var}_Q\\tau_n\n=\\frac1{n^2}\\sum_{k=0}^{m_n-1}\\frac1{(\\lambda_{n,k}+\\theta)^2}\n\\le\\frac{m_n}{n^2\\theta^2}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{Q.0.1g.9}\n$$\nThe mean convergence is a bounded Riemann sum. Fix $s\\in(0,T)$, choose $\\theta_s$ with $t(\\theta_s)=s$, and fix $0<\\varepsilon<\\min\\{s,T-s\\}$. Chebyshev's inequality gives $Q_{n,\\theta_s}(s-\\varepsilon\\le\\tau_n\\le s+\\varepsilon)\\to1$. Restricting the density integral to this event yields\n$$\n\\mathbb P(\\tau_n\\le T)\n\\ge e^{-nA_n(\\theta_s)+n\\theta_s(s-\\varepsilon)}\nQ_{n,\\theta_s}(s-\\varepsilon\\le\\tau_n\\le s+\\varepsilon).\n\\tag{Q.0.1g.10}\n$$\nFirst take $n\\to\\infty$ with $s,\\varepsilon$ fixed, then $\\varepsilon\\downarrow0$, then $s\\uparrow T$. Continuity of the inverse of $t$ gives the upper exponent bound $\\kappa+J_\\lambda(T)$. This proves (Q.0.1g.2) for finite $\\kappa$ without exchanging the noise-floor and small-jump limits. If $\\kappa=\\infty$, the first holding time alone gives $\\mathbb P(\\tau_n\\le T)\\le1-e^{-n\\delta_nT}\\le n\\delta_nT$, proving divergence of the exponent. Although $Z_n(0)=1$, the positive-$\\theta$ limit of $-n^{-1}\\log Z_n$ includes $\\kappa$; no differentiability theorem at $\\theta=0$ is being invoked.\n\nFor the action assertion, finite action forces $\\dot x\\ge0$ almost everywhere. At fixed $\\theta=\\theta_T$, let $p_M(x):=\\min\\{M,\\log(1+\\theta/\\lambda(x))\\}$, with $p_M(0):=M$. This is bounded and continuous and $H(x,p_M(x))\\le\\theta$. Fenchel's inequality and the chain rule for its continuously differentiable primitive give\n$$\n\\int_0^T L(x,\\dot x)dt\\ge\\int_0^a p_M(u)du-\\theta T.\n\\tag{Q.0.1g.11}\n$$\nMonotone convergence proves the lower bound $J_\\lambda(T)$. The inverse in (Q.0.1g.5) exists and is $C^1$ because $F_\\theta'=1/(\\lambda+\\theta)>0$. For its path, Fenchel equality holds for $t>0$, and substitution $dt=dx/(\\lambda+\\theta)$ gives\n$$\n\\int_0^T L(x,\\dot x)dt\n=\\int_0^a\\left[\\log\\left(1+\\frac\\theta{\\lambda(x)}\\right)-\\frac\\theta{\\lambda(x)+\\theta}\\right]dx\n=A_\\lambda(\\theta)-\\theta T.\n\\tag{Q.0.1g.12}\n$$\nThis is finite by log-integrability; an infinite density at the single initial time does not affect the integral.\n\nFor uniqueness, a nondecreasing continuous path has an initial zero interval $[0,w]$ and is positive afterward. On $[w+\\varepsilon,T]$ put $p(x)=\\log(1+\\theta/\\lambda(x))$ and $D=L-p\\dot x+\\theta\\ge0$. This gap vanishes exactly when $\\dot x=\\lambda(x)+\\theta$. The bounded-primitive chain rule on that interval, followed by $\\varepsilon\\downarrow0$, gives\n$$\nI(x)=J_\\lambda(T)+\\theta w+\\int_{(w,T)}D\\,dt.\n\\tag{Q.0.1g.13}\n$$\nHere the initial zero segment has zero action, and monotone convergence handles $D$. Equality forces $w=0$ and $D=0$ almost everywhere. Differentiating $F_\\theta(x(t))$ then proves (Q.0.1g.5) uniquely. This separated strictly positive scalar equation needs only the stated continuity, not local Lipschitz regularity. Finally $J_\\lambda'(s)=-\\theta_s<0$, so permitting first arrival at any $s\\le T$ has minimum $J_\\lambda(T)$. The post-arrival cost for the stopped source is zero; the interior density $L(a,0)=\\lambda(a)$ must not be charged after absorption. ∎\n\n<a id=\"example-q01g1-quadratic-boundary-rate-and-closed-form-cost\"></a>**Example Q.0.1g.1 (Quadratic Boundary Rate and Closed-Form Cost).** For $\\lambda(x)=x^2$, all hypotheses of (Q.0.1g.1) hold. The conclusion also holds for arbitrary positive integers $m_n$ with $m_n/n\\to a$, without the restriction $m_n/n\\le a$, using the same quadratic rate beyond $a$: the moving-endpoint Riemann argument has only a vanishing bounded terminal contribution. Direct integration gives\n$$\nA_a(\\theta)=a\\log\\left(1+\\frac\\theta{a^2}\\right)+2\\sqrt\\theta\\arctan\\frac a{\\sqrt\\theta},\n\\qquad\nT=\\frac1{\\sqrt\\theta}\\arctan\\frac a{\\sqrt\\theta},\n\\qquad\nJ_a(T)=a\\log\\left(1+\\frac\\theta{a^2}\\right)+\\theta T.\n\\tag{Q.0.1g.14}\n$$\nThe optimizer is $x(t)=\\sqrt\\theta\\tan(\\sqrt\\theta t)$, with no pole on $[0,T]$ by the endpoint equation. At $a=T=1$, $\\theta\\simeq0.7401738843949671$ and $J_a(T)\\simeq1.2941589261887052$. The entrance contribution remains exactly $\\kappa$ for every allowed schedule.\n\nThe source can be embedded before stopping in a smooth periodic nominated generator: choose a circle coordinate chart containing a neighborhood of $[0,a]$, a smooth nonnegative periodic $\\lambda$ equal to $x^2$ there, and rates $n[\\lambda(x)+\\delta_n]$ for jumps of size $1/n$. For all sufficiently large $n$, stopping at the $m_n$-th jump gives precisely the finite chain above. Smoothness is asserted for the unstopped source, not for its absorbing boundary. ∎\n\n<a id=\"example-q01g2-the-same-sine-squared-source-as-the-accessibility-obstruction\"></a>**Example Q.0.1g.2 (The Same Sine-Squared Source as the Accessibility Obstruction).** For $\\lambda(x)=\\sin^2x$ on $[0,a]$ with $0<a<\\pi/2$, the rate is increasing, $\\log\\lambda(x)=2\\log x+O(x^2)$ is integrable, and $\\int_0^a\\csc^2x\\,dx=\\infty$. Thus Theorem Q.0.1g applies to the smooth periodic one-direction birth source with jump size $1/n$ and rate $n[\\sin^2x+\\delta_n]$, stopped at $\\lfloor an\\rfloor/n$. Substitution $u=\\tan x$ makes its exact time equation\n$$\nT=\\frac1{\\sqrt{\\theta(1+\\theta)}}\n\\arctan\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\theta}{\\theta}}\\tan a\\right).\n\\tag{Q.0.1g.15}\n$$\nThe exponent is $\\kappa+J_{\\sin^2}(T)$ with $J$ given by (Q.0.1g.2). At exactly zero floor the source is absorbed at zero and its hitting probability is identically zero, while the displayed formal action minimum is finite. Thus the new theorem quantitatively extends, rather than removes, the initial-accessibility obstruction. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q01g3-order-of-limits-and-explicit-finite-size-certificates\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.1g.3 (Order of Limits and Explicit Finite-Size Certificates).** For a fixed positive floor $\\delta$, let\n$$\nJ_{\\lambda,\\delta}(T):=\\sup_{\\theta\\ge0}\n\\left\\{\\int_0^a\\log\\left(1+\\frac\\theta{\\lambda(x)+\\delta}\\right)dx-\\theta T\\right\\}.\n\\tag{Q.0.1g.16}\n$$\nThen $\\lim_n-n^{-1}\\log\\mathbb P_\\delta(\\tau_n\\le T)=J_{\\lambda,\\delta}(T)$ and $J_{\\lambda,\\delta}(T)\\uparrow J_\\lambda(T)$ as $\\delta\\downarrow0$. Taking $n\\to\\infty$ before removing the floor therefore yields $J_\\lambda(T)$; joint schedules can instead yield $\\kappa+J_\\lambda(T)$. Removing the floor first at fixed $n$ gives zero hitting probability and infinite exponent.\n\nFor every finite $n$ and $\\theta>0$, the quantities in (Q.0.1g.6) and (Q.0.1g.9) give the exact bounds\n$$\nA_n(\\theta)-\\theta T\\le-\\frac1n\\log\\mathbb P(\\tau_n\\le T)\n\\le A_n(\\theta)-\\theta(\\mu_n(\\theta)-\\varepsilon)\n-\\frac1n\\log\\left(1-\\frac{v_n(\\theta)}{\\varepsilon^2}\\right),\n\\tag{Q.0.1g.17}\n$$\nwhere the upper bound requires $0<\\varepsilon<\\mu_n(\\theta)$, $\\mu_n(\\theta)+\\varepsilon\\le T$, and $v_n(\\theta)<\\varepsilon^2$.\n\n*Proof.* With fixed $\\delta$, the product/tilt proof above applies using the nonsingular rate $\\lambda+\\delta$. If $T<\\int_0^a(\\lambda+\\delta)^{-1}dx$, the optimizer is positive and the same proof applies directly. If $T$ exceeds that travel time, un-tilted mean/variance concentration makes the exponent zero. At equality, tilt to means strictly below $T$ and send the tilt parameter to zero; its bound tends to zero, which is also the trivial lower bound on a negative probability logarithm. As $\\delta\\downarrow0$ the integrands increase, giving $J_{\\lambda,\\delta}\\le J_\\lambda$ and monotonicity; evaluation at the fixed optimizer $\\theta_T$ and dominated convergence give the matching limiting lower bound. At fixed $n$, $\\mathbb P_\\delta(\\tau_n\\le T)\\le n\\delta T\\to0$. Finally (Q.0.1g.8) proves the left inequality of (Q.0.1g.17); restricting the exact tilted density ratio to $|\\tau_n-\\mu_n|\\le\\varepsilon$ and applying Chebyshev proves the right one. ∎\n\nAll costs in this theorem are dimensionless. The entrance coefficient $\\kappa$ is distinct from the independently calibrated action scale $\\kappa_A$. The result proves this exact stopped hitting exponent and formal local-action minimum. It does not itself prove a full path-space large-deviation principle, a historywise action reconstruction, an ND–RID physical source or an Appendix-U/Y map. Uses in Definition Q.0.1a, Theorem Q.0.1b or Corollary Q.0.1c retain their stated path-law, transition-tube and reconstruction hypotheses. No identification with reset heat, mechanical action or $\\hbar$ follows.\n\n<a id=\"remark-q01c1-conditional-reset-power-viability-inequality\"></a>**Remark Q.0.1c.1 (Conditional Reset-Power Viability Inequality).** Choose a finite window of duration $\\tau>0$ and one bath temperature $T>0$. For each of finitely many reset classes $j$, prescribe a deterministic count $n_j\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}$ of completed resets and require the complete Definition 28 contract on every admitted reset history, finite mean heats, and an additive conditional-expectation ledger. For reset $l$ in class $j$, put\n$$\nh_{jl}:=\\mathbb E\\!\\left[H_{q_{jl}}(P_{jl}\\mid R_{jl})\\right],\n\\qquad\nr_j:=\\frac{n_j}{\\tau},\n\\qquad\nH_j:=\\frac1{n_j}\\sum_{l=1}^{n_j}h_{jl}\n$$\nwhen $n_j>0$, and set $H_j=0$ when $n_j=0$. The expectation averages the actual conditional reset laws over their registered histories. Define\n$$\n\\nu:=\\sum_jr_j,\\qquad\n\\overline{H_q(P\\mid R)}:=\\nu^{-1}\\sum_jr_jH_j\n$$\nwhen $\\nu>0$, and define the normalized mean bath-heat rate by\n$$\n\\dot{\\mathcal L}_{\\mathrm{reset}}\n:=\\frac1{k_BT\\tau}\\sum_j\\sum_{l=1}^{n_j}\\mathbb E Q_{\\mathrm{bath},jl}.\n$$\nAll entropies are in nats. Sequential conditional Landauer gives\n$$\n\\dot{\\mathcal L}_{\\mathrm{reset}}\\ge\\sum_jr_jH_j\n=\\nu\\,\\overline{H_q(P\\mid R)}\n$$\nfor $\\nu>0$. For the input-power comparison, independently require a complete, nonoverlapping mean-energy balance on the same window,\n$$\nE_{\\mathrm{in}}\n=\\sum_{j,l}\\mathbb E Q_{\\mathrm{bath},jl}\n+E_{\\mathrm{loss}}+\\Delta E_{\\mathrm{store}},\n\\qquad E_{\\mathrm{loss}}\\ge0,\\quad\\Delta E_{\\mathrm{store}}\\ge0,\n$$\nwhere $E_{\\mathrm{loss}}$ counts the other exports and $\\Delta E_{\\mathrm{store}}$ is the retained energy change, excluding energy already assigned to the bath or other exports. Set $R_{\\mathrm{in}}:=E_{\\mathrm{in}}/(k_BT\\tau)$ and $R_{\\mathrm{loss}}:=E_{\\mathrm{loss}}/(k_BT\\tau)$. These rates have units of inverse time, and\n$$\nR_{\\mathrm{in}}\\ge\\dot{\\mathcal L}_{\\mathrm{reset}}+R_{\\mathrm{loss}}.\n$$\nThis comparison uses the additional no-drawdown energy premise; it does not follow from conditional Landauer alone.\n\n*Proof.* Conditional Landauer and the tower property give $\\mathbb E Q_{\\mathrm{bath},jl}\\ge k_BT h_{jl}$. Finite summation and division by $k_BT\\tau>0$ prove the reset-rate bound. Dividing the separate energy balance by the same factor and using $\\Delta E_{\\mathrm{store}}\\ge0$ proves the input-rate inequality. If $\\nu=0$, every $n_j=0$ and the completed-reset sum is empty, so $\\dot{\\mathcal L}_{\\mathrm{reset}}=0$; apparatus overhead still belongs to its own energy entry. Any limiting rate statement additionally requires convergence of these window averages and preservation of their heat and energy balances. ∎\n\nThe specialization $\\nu\\ln2$ requires every registered binary record to be conditionally uniform on its admitted histories. The structural value $\\varepsilon_0=\\ln2$ alone determines no maintenance power.\n\nThis is a necessary finite-resource viability condition for code-maintaining aggregates.\n\n### Q.0.4 Action-Scale Calibration\n\n<a id=\"convention-q02x-action-scale-calibration\"></a>**Convention Q.0.2x (Action-Scale Calibration).** The continuum action carries an independent positive scale $\\kappa_A$ with units of action per dimensionless ledger unit. The identification $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$ is a calibration condition against quantum phase data, not a consequence of counting or dimensional analysis.\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q02-dimensional-role\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.2 (Dimensional Role).** On a calibrated branch,\n$$\n\\mathcal S=\\kappa_A\\mathcal L.\n$$\nIf comparison with experiment selects $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$, then the familiar phase factor $e^{i\\mathcal S/\\hbar}$ is recovered. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q02b-conditional-per-cycle-action\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.2b (Conditional Per-Cycle Action).** If one registered structural cycle contributes $\\ln2$ to $\\mathcal L$, then its calibrated action contribution is $\\kappa_A\\ln2$. This is not a universal minimum physical action. ∎\n\n<a id=\"remark-q01-status\"></a>**Remark Q.0.1 (Status).** Neither $\\ln2$ nor the variational limit fixes the empirical value of $\\kappa_A$.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q02e-local-coherent-phase-calibration-and-geometric-phase-separation\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.2e (Local Coherent-Phase Calibration and Geometric-Phase Separation).** On the independent reference carrier $\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{ref}}=\\operatorname{span}\\{|0\\rangle,|1\\rangle\\}$, implement, for every registered control value $\\ell\\in\\mathbb R$,\n$$\nU_{\\mathrm{ref}}(\\ell)\n=|0\\rangle\\!\\langle0|+e^{i\\ell}|1\\rangle\\!\\langle1|.\n\\tag{Q.0.2e.1}\n$$\nPrepare $|+_x\\rangle=(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)/\\sqrt2$ and use the normalized projective readouts in the $x$ and $y$ bases, where $|+_y\\rangle=(|0\\rangle+i|1\\rangle)/\\sqrt2$. Their positive-outcome probabilities are\n$$\np_x^+(\\ell)=\\frac{1+\\cos\\ell}{2},\n\\qquad\np_y^+(\\ell)=\\frac{1+\\sin\\ell}{2}.\n\\tag{Q.0.2e.1a}\n$$\nThus the pair of normalized responses identifies $e^{i\\ell}$ exactly and fixes the reference phase without using the PU ledger.\n\nSuppose the same control family has a registered dimensionless PU ledger $\\mathcal L(\\ell)=\\ell$ and candidate action $\\mathcal S(\\ell)=\\kappa_A\\ell$ with $\\kappa_A>0$, and that its quantum action rule represents the relative phase by\n$$\nU_A(\\ell)\n=|0\\rangle\\!\\langle0|\n+e^{i\\kappa_A\\ell/\\hbar}|1\\rangle\\!\\langle1|.\n\\tag{Q.0.2e.2}\n$$\nEquality of the two coherent channels on any open interval about zero holds exactly when\n$$\n\\kappa_A=\\hbar.\n\\tag{Q.0.2e.3}\n$$\n\nIn particular, after (Q.0.2e.3), the registered event value $\\ell=N\\ln2$ has relative phase $e^{iN\\ln2}$ for every $N\\in\\mathbb Z$. For a registered closed scalar transport branch at leading adiabatic and semiclassical order, assume that the phase factors are $e^{i\\mathcal S/\\hbar}$, $e^{i\\gamma_{\\mathrm B}}$, $e^{-i\\pi\\mu/2}$ and $e^{i\\Theta_{\\partial}}$, with a common gauge and endpoint convention and each contribution counted once. Their product has leading transport phase\n$$\n\\Theta_{\\mathrm{sc}}\n=\\frac{\\mathcal S}{\\hbar}\n+\\gamma_{\\mathrm B}\n-\\frac\\pi2\\mu\n+\\Theta_{\\partial}\n\\pmod{2\\pi},\n\\tag{Q.0.2e.4}\n$$\nwhere $\\gamma_{\\mathrm B}\\in\\mathbb R/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)$ is the Berry phase of a smooth isolated nondegenerate eigenline, with holonomy $e^{i\\gamma_{\\mathrm B}}$, $\\mu\\in\\mathbb Z$ is the oriented Maslov index in the $e^{i\\mathcal S/\\hbar}$ convention, and $\\Theta_{\\partial}$ is the registered scalar boundary/spin phase on the same branch. On variations of $\\ell$ that preserve the eigenline loop and connection, caustic class and boundary/spin data, the last three leading terms are constant. This does not establish constancy or vanishing of omitted finite-speed or higher semiclassical phase corrections; any claim about the exact transported phase requires a separate bound or a certificate that those corrections vanish. The exact calibration (Q.0.2e.3) follows from the independent coherent-channel equality, without using this approximation.\n\n*Proof.* Direct Born-rule evaluation gives (Q.0.2e.1a); its two centered probabilities are $(\\cos\\ell,\\sin\\ell)$ and therefore recover the reference phase modulo $2\\pi$. Both unitaries fix $|0\\rangle$ and hence cannot differ by a nontrivial global phase. Equality gives $e^{i\\ell}=e^{i(\\kappa_A/\\hbar)\\ell}$ on an open interval. Differentiation at $\\ell=0$ gives $1=\\kappa_A/\\hbar$. Conversely, (Q.0.2e.3) makes (Q.0.2e.1)--(Q.0.2e.2) identical. On the stated adiabatic eigenline branch, the Berry holonomy is $e^{i\\gamma_{\\mathrm B}}$ (Berry 1984; Simon 1983). For the scalar semiclassical convention, passage through a caustic of oriented multiplicity $m$ multiplies the leading amplitude by $e^{-i\\pi m/2}$; reversing the crossing reverses $m$ (Keller 1958, equations 14–15). Multiplication of the registered scalar factors yields (Q.0.2e.4) modulo $2\\pi$. Preserving their defining data makes the three nonaction terms locally constant at that order. This scalar product identity supplies no estimate for the omitted phase corrections. ∎\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q02f-finite-calibration-records-that-identify-kappa_a\"></a>**Proposition Q.0.2f (Finite Calibration Records That Identify $\\kappa_A$).** In the setting of Theorem Q.0.2e, let $\\Lambda\\subset\\mathbb R$ be a registered set of control values, let $\\langle\\Lambda\\rangle$ be the additive subgroup of $\\mathbb R$ that it generates, and put\n$$\nK(\\Lambda)\n:=\\{\\kappa_A>0:\\ U_A(\\ell)=U_{\\mathrm{ref}}(\\ell)\\ \\text{for every }\\ell\\in\\Lambda\\}.\n\\tag{Q.0.2f.1}\n$$\nBy (Q.0.2e.1a), $\\kappa_A\\in K(\\Lambda)$ exactly when the two channels have equal normalized $x/y$ response pairs at every $\\ell\\in\\Lambda$. Then:\n\n1. If $\\langle\\Lambda\\rangle=\\{0\\}$, then $K(\\Lambda)=(0,\\infty)$.\n2. If $\\langle\\Lambda\\rangle=\\ell_0\\mathbb Z$ with $\\ell_0>0$, then $K(\\Lambda)=\\{\\hbar(1+2\\pi m/\\ell_0):m\\in\\mathbb Z,\\ m>-\\ell_0/(2\\pi)\\}$, an infinite discrete set.\n3. If $\\langle\\Lambda\\rangle$ is not cyclic, then $K(\\Lambda)=\\{\\hbar\\}$. For finite $\\Lambda$ this holds exactly when $\\Lambda$ contains two nonzero values with irrational ratio.\n\nConsequently no single control value identifies $\\kappa_A$, while any two values with irrational ratio do. On the event lattice $\\Lambda=\\{N\\ln2:N\\ge1\\}$, item 2 gives\n$$\nK(\\Lambda)=\\{\\hbar(1+m\\beta_L):m\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\},\n\\qquad\n\\beta_L=\\frac{2\\pi}{\\ln2},\n\\tag{Q.0.2f.2}\n$$\nwith $\\beta_L$ the reciprocal Landauer angle of Section Q.0.7.1, so event-count responses leave infinitely many action scales. Adjoining the single control value $2\\pi$ gives the two-value record $\\{\\ln2,2\\pi\\}$, for which $K=\\{\\hbar\\}$.\n\n*Proof.* Both unitaries fix $|0\\rangle$, so they agree at $\\ell$ exactly when $e^{i\\ell}=e^{i\\kappa_A\\ell/\\hbar}$, that is, $c\\ell\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z$ with $c:=\\kappa_A/\\hbar-1$. The set $\\{\\ell:c\\ell\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z\\}$ is an additive subgroup of $\\mathbb R$, so the condition on $\\Lambda$ is equivalent to the condition on $\\langle\\Lambda\\rangle$. Item 1 is immediate. In item 2 the condition reads $c\\ell_0\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z$, and positivity of $\\kappa_A$ is $1+2\\pi m/\\ell_0>0$. Every additive subgroup of $\\mathbb R$ is cyclic or dense; in item 3, $c\\ne0$ would make $c\\langle\\Lambda\\rangle$ a dense subset of the discrete set $2\\pi\\mathbb Z$, so $c=0$. A finitely generated subgroup whose nonzero generators have pairwise rational ratios lies in $\\ell_1\\mathbb Q$ for one generator $\\ell_1$ and is cyclic, while two values with irrational ratio cannot lie in one cyclic group. For the event lattice $\\langle\\Lambda\\rangle=\\ln2\\,\\mathbb Z$, and $m>-\\ln2/(2\\pi)$ means $m\\ge0$. Finally $2\\pi/\\ln2$ is irrational by Lemma Q.0.7b. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q02g\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q02g-canonical-free-source-action-likelihood-phase-bridge\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.2g (Canonical Free-Source Action–Likelihood–Phase Bridge).** The registered finite-source family below gives a channel-identification bridge beyond the finite logarithmic module of Theorem M.6.6c. Its complete normalized Gaussian comparison laws, quadratic momentum dynamics and paid positive finite inverse-free echo implement the full field/probe/reference phase channel with calibrated relative action equal to $\\hbar$ times the raw ledger. It supplies the canonical normal limit, shear and Gaussian geometry, exact endpoint/Gouy and typed Berry/Maslov records, independently normalized observables and complete finite source/control/invoice/error contracts. The mass, duration, Gaussian widths and reference-arm energy are calibrations chosen for this source, and $\\hbar$ enters as an independently calibrated unit.\n\n*Proof and complete source record.*\n\n#### 1. Source contract and finite canonical family\n\nFix independently calibrated mass $m>0$, action unit $\\hbar>0$ and positive duration $t$. Put\n\n$$\na=t/(2m\\hbar),\\quad p_{*} = \\sqrt{\\ln(2)/a}.\n$$\n\nThese quantities carry physical units of mass, momentum, action and time. At source grade $n\\ge 0$ let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\delta_n=2^{-n},\\quad J_n=2^{2n},\\quad h_n=p_{*} \\delta_n, \\\\\n&p_j=j h_n,\\quad -J_n\\le j\\le J_n, \\\\\n&d_n=2J_n+1,\\quad b_n=\\lceil\\log_2 d_n\\rceil,\\quad D_n=2^{b_n}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe declared physical momentum register has the $d_{n}$ active levels $|j\\rangle$, embedded in a $D_n$-level binary appliance. Its remaining padding levels are supplied, retained and priced; all matrices below are completed on them. Admitted input states are arbitrary normal states on the active field and two-state probe, including arbitrary external-reference entanglement. A finite $|j\\rangle$ is an ordinary normalized finite state.\n\nInstall the self-adjoint momentum observable\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_n&= \\sum_j p_j |j\\rangle\\langle j|, \\\\\nH_n&=P_n^{2}/(2m),\\quad B_n=\\max_j p_j^{2}/(2m).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn padding assign $P_{n}=0$ and $H_{n}=0$. The installed kinetic Hamiltonian is the quadratic expression in this momentum matrix. A supplied finite position comparison matrix is the cyclic central difference\n\n$$\nX_n=i\\hbar(S_n-S_n^{*})/(2h_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $(S_n v)_j=v_{j+1}$ on the active cyclic index. It is zero on padding. It is a finite comparison observable: $X_{n}$ and $P_{n}$ satisfy no exact canonical commutation relation, and their canonical limit on a common core is proved in part 5. Its measurement, if requested, has a separately supplied finite Fourier-basis instrument.\n\nThe field/probe carrier has exactly zero idle Hamiltonian outside the two explicitly installed mechanical pulses. All preparation, transport, controller gaps and readout schedules retain this zero-idle convention.\n\nThe apparatus uses the primitive services of Definition C.6c.4a, the finite local driven matrix and record service boundary of the cabinet of Theorem Q.0.3l, with its own matrices, word and invoice supplied in part 8. All entries used here have finite algebraic, logarithmic or elementary-computable descriptors. At the ideal native boundary their calibrated finite coefficients and waveforms are supplied as couplings. A numerical realization carries the operator and instrument errors of part 9.\n\nThe kinetic path-action comparison starts at the appliance entrance after the paid transfers; transfer/preparation/control actions remain separate in the complete source invoice. The kinetic, raw-comparison and readout services share this one installed $P_{n}$ spectrum, the independently fixed $m,t,\\hbar$ and one finite descriptor. Raw comparison, coherent unknown-input, and prepared diagnostic modes are separately registered before input. The raw mode measures its own prepared states; the coherent mode acts on the unknown input without measuring it.\n\n#### 2. Two complete normalized raw Gaussian sources\n\nDefine\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\alpha_j&=a p_j^{2}=\\ln(2)\\, \\delta_n^{2} j^{2}, \\\\\nZ_{F,n}&=\\sum_j \\exp(-\\alpha_j),\\quad Z_{R,n}=\\sum_j \\exp(-2\\alpha_j), \\\\\nf_n(j)&=\\exp(-\\alpha_j)/Z_{F,n}, \\\\\nr_n(j)&=\\exp(-2\\alpha_j)/Z_{R,n}, \\\\\nc_n&=\\ln(Z_{F,n}/Z_{R,n})\\ge 0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSupply two pure normal finite preparations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|F_n\\rangle&=\\sum_j \\sqrt{f_n(j)}\\, |j\\rangle, \\\\\n|R_n\\rangle&=\\sum_j \\sqrt{r_n(j)}\\, |j\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvery amplitude is algebraic: $\\exp(-\\alpha_j)=2^{-j^{2}/4^{n}}$. Finite algebraic normalization and a full algebraic unitary completion give a concrete preparation matrix from a fresh basis state. A Householder preparation may be used when its denominator is nonzero, with the identity used for an already equal target; finite Gram–Schmidt is another terminating construction. The matrix, complete support, positive-duration implementation and source stock are paid. These are coherent Gaussian preparations of pure finite states.\n\nThe raw experiment measures the momentum basis with all $d_{n}$ outcomes retained. Both laws are normalized exactly. Their registered full-output likelihood ratio is\n\n$$\nL_n(j)=\\ln[f_n(j)/r_n(j)]=\\alpha_j-c_n.\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.1}\n$$\n\nThis ledger can be negative. It is a raw comparison ledger in the sense of Definition Q.0.1, supplied by this stochastic implementation record; its values are log-likelihood ratios of either sign. The constant $c_{n}$ is retained as part of the ledger.\n\nEach preparation follows one complete unchanged qualification prefix of the cells of Theorem C.6c.P17a, transported through payload SWAPs as specified in part 8 of this proof. If $h$ denotes any positive-probability complete qualification history, its law $q(h)$ is identical in the two experiments and independent of the fresh Gaussian preparation. The complete paired raw law on the declared comparison carrier is\n\n$$\nP_F(h,j)=q(h) f_n(j),\\quad P_R(h,j)=q(h) r_n(j).\n$$\n\nThus (Q.0.2g.1) holds also with every qualification history retained. Preparation choice and apparatus descriptor are retained; the registered comparison maps the F experimental choice to the R choice. All supplier services in the declared driven boundary are deterministic conditional on their predeclared choice, or have the same retained independent law; a stochastic arm-dependent supplier requires a new full law. The finite Gaussian preparations and basis instruments are trace-preserving native services on their declared fresh-state domain, so both laws contain every outcome.\n\nFor a registered finite sequence of independently supplied raw trials the product likelihood has additive sum of these $L_{n}$ values. Each repetition needs a fresh full preparation/record word and invoice.\n\n#### 3. Positive kinetic, reference and echo Hamiltonians\n\nSet the physical reference-arm energy\n\n$$\nE_{\\mathrm{ref},n}=\\hbar c_n/t.\n$$\n\nIt is registered from the normalized source probabilities and the fixed energy and time units before any coherent response is requested; it is a normalization calibration chosen for this source. Its finite logarithmic coefficient is supplied and priced as a native coupling.\n\nLet $\\Pi_0=|0\\rangle\\langle0|$ and $\\Pi_1=|1\\rangle\\langle1|$ on the probe. The first positive-duration pulse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_{\\mathrm{first},n}(s)&=g(s)[H_n\\otimes\\Pi_0+E_{\\mathrm{ref},n} I\\otimes\\Pi_1], \\\\\ng(s)&=1-\\cos(2\\pi s/t),\\quad 0\\le s\\le t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is positive, continuous and zero at endpoints, and $\\int g\\,ds=t$. Its exact endpoint is\n\n$$\nW_n=\\operatorname{diag}(\\exp(-itH_n/\\hbar),\\ \\exp(-ic_n)I).\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.2}\n$$\n\nThe physically retained common free factor is field-dependent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW_n&=(\\exp(-itH_n/\\hbar)\\otimes I)\\, R_n, \\\\\nR_n&=\\operatorname{diag}(I,\\exp[i(\\alpha(P_n)-c_n)]).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.3}\n$$\n\nThe common factor in (Q.0.2g.3) is an operator on the field and stays in the channel. The un-echoed $W_{n}$ is one separately nominated transport menu.\n\nFor the primary channel comparison supply a second positive-duration pulse, acting on the field and identically on both probe arms:\n\n$$\nH_{\\mathrm{echo},n}(s)=g(s)[B_n I-H_n]\\otimes I.\n$$\n\nBecause the finite spectrum of $H_{n}$ lies in $[0,B_n]$, this Hamiltonian is positive: the inverse-free operation is implemented with its positive baseline. Its endpoint is $\\exp(-itB_n/\\hbar)\\exp(+itH_n/\\hbar)\\otimes I$. The complete two-pulse endpoint is\n\n$$\nV_n=\\exp(-itB_n/\\hbar)\\, R_n.\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.4}\n$$\n\nThe scalar $\\exp(-itB_n/\\hbar)$, the baseline action $tB_n$, every intermediate kinetic motion and the Hamiltonians are retained in the path and source records. Only this scalar factor cancels from the channel. The baseline $B_{n}$ grows without bound as $n\\to\\infty$.\n\nConsequently, for every admitted field/probe/reference state $\\rho$,\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{Ad}(V_n)\\otimes \\mathrm{Id}_R)(\\rho)=(\\operatorname{Ad}(R_n)\\otimes \\mathrm{Id}_R)(\\rho).\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.5}\n$$\n\nEquation (Q.0.2g.5) holds without selection or postselection. The external accepted-input cut and the canonical mechanical comparison cut are distinct. At the latter, both paid payload SWAP banks as well as all qualification/readiness operations have ended. Their exact action is $|\\psi\\rangle_{\\mathrm{in}}|0\\rangle_w|0\\rangle_{\\mathrm{app}}\\to|0\\rangle_{\\mathrm{in}}|0\\rangle_w|\\psi\\rangle_{\\mathrm{app}}$, extended linearly to every mixed input and spectator. The input and payload factors are then fixed and independent of the unknown state. Thus the canonical appliance input is exactly the external logical input under the registered basis identification. From this mechanical cut every remaining supplier service before the end of the echo acts as identity on the unknown field/probe/reference except for the two displayed deterministic Hamiltonians; its retained histories therefore factor as the fixed channel (Q.0.2g.5) tensored with that supplier's instrument. This is a direct tensor-generator property of this declared driven source boundary; a physical driver that exports momentum or arm information requires its own proof.\n\nThe independently specified reference $R_{n}$ is the spectral/direct-sum extension of the phase reference of Theorem Q.0.2e: on each finite $|j\\rangle$ sector it is exactly $\\operatorname{diag}(1,\\exp[iL_n(j)])$, using (Q.0.2g.1), and on superpositions it is its fixed linear coherent extension on the same $P_{n}$ spectral decomposition, a unitary channel. Equations (Q.0.2g.2)–(Q.0.2g.5) prove that the mechanical source implements that complete extension, including all off-diagonal field coherence and all reference entanglement. Because of the echo, the equality holds for the physical channel in the laboratory frame.\n\n#### 4. Exact identifying scalar records inside the broader family\n\nAt $n=0$ the three momenta are $-p_*,0,p_*$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&Z_{F,0}=2,\\quad Z_{R,0}=3/2,\\quad c_0=\\ln(4/3), \\\\\n&L_0(0)=\\ln(3/4),\\quad L_0(1)=L_0(-1)=\\ln(3/2).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth scalar sectors are ordinary admissible finite physical states. Their two nonzero ledger values have irrational ratio. Indeed if a rational relation exists, clearing denominators gives an integer relation between $\\ln 3-2\\ln 2$ and $\\ln 3-\\ln 2$; unique prime factorization forces both coefficients to vanish. Hence Proposition Q.0.2f identifies $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$ uniquely among positive action scales compatible with both scalar channel records.\n\nEquation (Q.0.2g.5) holds for every field/probe/reference input and every grade; these two records provide the independent finite identifiability check. The higher grades, canonical dynamics and geometric records follow in parts 5 to 7.\n\nPrepare $|j\\rangle\\otimes|+x\\rangle$ by the paid source word, apply both pulses, and use independently specified finite $x$/$y$ projective probe detectors. Their complete outcome pairs are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np_{x,\\pm}(j)&=[1\\pm\\cos L_n(j)]/2, \\\\\np_{y,\\pm}(j)&=[1\\pm\\sin L_n(j)]/2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.6}\n$$\n\nBoth outcomes are retained and sum to one. The registered spectral controls of Theorem Q.0.2e are obtained on these finite sectors.\n\n#### 5. Canonical normal limit, quadratic dynamics and errors\n\nUse the momentum representation $K=L^{2}(\\mathbb{R},dp)$, $P\\psi(p)=p\\psi(p)$, $X\\psi(p)=i\\hbar\\psi'(p)$. The ideal kinetic energy $H=P^{2}/(2m)$ has its usual maximal multiplication domain. $C_c^{\\infty}(\\mathbb{R})$ is a common core for the polynomial expressions and is preserved by all phase multipliers below. Embed the finite active register by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\iota_n|j\\rangle&=h_n^{-1/2}\\, 1_{[p_j-h_n/2,\\,p_j+h_n/2)}, \\\\\nQ_n&=\\iota_n \\iota_n^{*}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $Q_{n}$ denotes this projection; the position observable is $X$. Its retained box has radius $R_n^{\\mathrm{box}}=(J_n+1/2)h_n\\to\\infty$. Cell-average projections $Q_{n}$ tend strongly to $I$: uniform continuity proves the assertion on compact continuous functions, then density and contractivity prove it on $L^{2}$. These embeddings are analytic comparison maps.\n\n##### Normalization and raw-law convergence\n\nFor $s=1,2$ put $I_s=\\int\\exp(-sa p^{2})\\,dp=\\sqrt{\\pi/(sa)}$ and $A_{s,n}=h_n\\sum_j\\exp(-sa p_j^{2})$. Elementary cellwise variation and the Gaussian tail bound give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|A_{s,n}-I_s| &\\le \\varepsilon_{s,n}, \\\\\n\\varepsilon_{s,n}&=2h_n+\\exp[-sa(R_n^{\\mathrm{box}})^{2}]/(sa R_n^{\\mathrm{box}}).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.7}\n$$\n\nIndeed the total variation integral of $\\exp(-sa p^{2})$ is 2, and its two outside tails are at most $\\exp(-saR^{2})/(saR)$. Thus $c_n=\\ln(A_{1,n}/A_{2,n})\\to c=(\\ln 2)/2$. Whenever $\\varepsilon_{s,n}\\le I_s/2$,\n\n$$\n|c_n-c| \\le 2\\varepsilon_{1,n}/I_1+2\\varepsilon_{2,n}/I_2.\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.8}\n$$\n\nThe embedded pure preparations converge in norm to the normal Gaussian wavepackets\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi_F(p)&=(a/\\pi)^{1/4} \\exp(-a p^{2}/2), \\\\\n\\Phi_R(p)&=(2a/\\pi)^{1/4} \\exp(-a p^{2}).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $u_s(p)=\\exp(-sa p^{2}/2)$, piecewise sampling has $L^{2}$ error bounded by\n\n$$\nh_n \\lVert u_s'\\rVert_2 + \\lVert 1_{|p|>R_n^{\\mathrm{box}}}\\, u_s\\rVert_2.\n$$\n\nThis follows by the fundamental theorem of calculus on each cell and Cauchy–Schwarz, with a conservative constant one. Normalization increases the bound by at most the factor $2/\\lVert u_s\\rVert_2$. It gives explicit preparation-state errors in norm. Their momentum measurements have densities\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(p)&=\\sqrt{a/\\pi}\\, \\exp(-a p^{2}), \\\\\nr(p)&=\\sqrt{2a/\\pi}\\, \\exp(-2a p^{2}), \\\\\nL(p)&=\\ln[f(p)/r(p)]=a p^{2}-(\\ln 2)/2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.9}\n$$\n\nThese are complete normalized spectral response laws on Lebesgue momentum measure, and the Radon–Nikodym identity holds almost everywhere for these normal-state measurements. Complete normalized poststates for individual momentum outcomes exist for the finite cabinets only.\n\n##### Full coherent channel convergence\n\nDefine the ideal reference/action channel by the explicit unitary\n\n$$\nR=\\operatorname{diag}(I,\\exp[i(aP^{2}-c)]),\\quad c=(\\ln 2)/2.\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.10}\n$$\n\nThe finite physical endpoint is $V_{n}$ from (Q.0.2g.4); its common scalar cancels only at channel level. Thus it suffices to compare $T_n=\\iota_n R_n\\iota_n^*$ with $R$. For a normalized field/probe/reference vector $\\psi$ with momentum derivative and moment in $L^{2}$, set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne_n(\\psi)&=h_n \\lVert\\partial_p\\psi\\rVert_2/\\pi \\\\\n&\\quad+\\lVert 1_{|p|>R_n^{\\mathrm{box}}}\\psi\\rVert_2 \\\\\n&\\quad+a[h_n \\lVert P\\psi\\rVert_2+h_n^{2}/4] \\\\\n&\\quad+|c_n-c|.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.11}\n$$\n\nThen $\\lVert(T_n-R)\\psi\\rVert_2\\le e_n(\\psi)$. To see this, the cell Poincaré inequality bounds $\\lVert Q_n\\psi-\\psi\\rVert$ by the first two terms. On a cell, $|p_j^{2}-p^{2}|\\le h_n|p|+h_n^{2}/4$ and $|\\exp(iu)-\\exp(iv)|\\le|u-v|$. Extend the approximate phase outside the box by the exact phase for this proof only; projection still removes that outside component. This proves (Q.0.2g.11), including Hilbert-valued spectator vectors. The phase bound and projection strong convergence also prove strong convergence on every normal vector by compact approximation, without assuming its unbounded moments are finite.\n\nFor a normal joint density $\\rho$, the positive embedded output $T_n\\rho T_n^*$ has trace $\\operatorname{Tr}(Q_n\\rho)$; its missing weight is explicitly $\\operatorname{Tr}((I-Q_n)\\rho)$. It is retained without renormalization. Strong convergence and finite-rank approximation give trace-norm convergence to $R\\rho R^*$. Quantitatively,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lVert T_n\\rho T_n^{*}-R\\rho R^{*}\\rVert_1 \\\\\n&\\le 2 [\\operatorname{Tr}((T_n-R)\\rho(T_n-R)^{*})]^{1/2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.12}\n$$\n\nThe factorization proof uses Hilbert–Schmidt Cauchy–Schwarz and $\\lVert T_n\\rVert\\le 1$, $\\lVert R\\rVert=1$. Apply (Q.0.2g.11) to a purification or a supplied pure-state decomposition to obtain an explicit bound. The finite devices accept supplied normalized finite states, with their own approximation error added. The convergence is strong, state by state; it is not uniform in diamond norm over inputs of unbounded energy.\n\n##### Canonical observables and motion\n\nOn $\\psi\\in C_c^{\\infty}$ strictly inside the growing boxes, cube averaging commutes with integer-cell translations. Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\iota_n P_n \\iota_n^{*} \\psi &\\to P\\psi, \\\\\n\\iota_n X_n \\iota_n^{*} \\psi &\\to X\\psi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe central-difference derivative error is at most $\\hbar h_n^{2}\\lVert\\psi'''\\rVert_2/6$, plus the cell projection error for $i\\hbar\\psi'$. Wraparound is absent once the support and two-cell margin lie inside the box. Thus these finite matrices have the canonical limit on the common core.\n\nThe ideal echo channel obeys the exact common-core Heisenberg identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR^{*} (X\\otimes I) R &= X\\otimes I -(t/m)P\\otimes\\Pi_1, \\\\\nR^{*} (P\\otimes I) R &= P\\otimes I.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.13}\n$$\n\nThis gives the backward-free shear on arm 1 and the identity on arm 0. Strong convergence of the unitaries leaves the limit of the finite unbounded responses open; it is proved from the exact conjugated-shift expression. If $\\alpha(p)=ap^{2}$, $\\Delta_{+}=\\alpha(p_j+h_n)-\\alpha(p_j)$, $\\Delta_{-}=\\alpha(p_j)-\\alpha(p_j-h_n)$, its active-arm position change is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(i\\hbar/(2h_n))[(\\exp(i\\Delta_{+})-1)S_n \\\\\n&\\qquad-(\\exp(-i\\Delta_{-})-1)S_n^{*}].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.14}\n$$\n\nOn a fixed compact enlargement let $M_1=\\max|\\alpha'|$ and $M_2=\\max|\\alpha''|=2a$. The exponential remainder $|\\exp(iu)-1-iu|\\le u^{2}/2$, the mean-value theorem and translation/Poincaré bounds give the conservative direct error\n\n$$\nh_n \\hbar\\{(M_1^{2}/2+2M_2)\\lVert\\psi\\rVert_2+2M_1\\lVert\\psi'\\rVert_2\\}\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.15}\n$$\n\nagainst $-(t/m)P\\psi$ on that compact domain. The calculation is the explicit centered-shift expansion: the leading term is $-\\hbar[(D_+\\alpha)\\psi_{j+1}+(D_-\\alpha)\\psi_{j-1}]/2$; each difference quotient differs from $\\alpha'(p)$ by at most $h_{n}M_2$ on its cell, and each shift/average differs from $\\psi$ by at most a conservative $2h_n\\lVert\\psi'\\rVert$.\n\nThere is also a uniform weighted-tail bound. Every interior coefficient in (Q.0.2g.14) has magnitude at most $\\hbar a(|p_j|+h_n/2)$. On the sole cyclic wrap edge the endpoint energies are equal, so its phase difference and position-change coefficient vanish exactly. Shift, cell-average and triangle inequalities therefore give, for example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lVert\\iota_n(R_n^{*}X_n R_n-X_n)\\iota_n^{*}\\psi\\rVert_2 \\\\\n&\\le 4\\hbar a\\, \\lVert(|P|+2p_{*})\\psi\\rVert_2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.16}\n$$\n\nThis extends (Q.0.2g.13) from compact tests to supplied Gaussian/Schwartz inputs by choosing a compact cutoff first and then $n$. The bound (Q.0.2g.16) contains no factor $1/h_n$. The finite measurement of $X_{n}$, its conjugate or their difference, if requested, uses its supplied finite spectral instrument and its own precision record on separately prepared trials.\n\nEquations (Q.0.2g.11)–(Q.0.2g.16) separate bounded-channel convergence, common-core canonical geometry and unbounded-observable response. Gaussian tails, derivatives and moments are explicit and finite. The baseline $B_{n}$ grows with n and remains in every finite invoice.\n\n#### 6. Path records, endpoint polarization, Maslov and Berry typing\n\nThe physical finite sources have their exact matrices and channel records; their canonical phase-space comparison is supplied by the common-core and normal limits of part 5. Register the canonical coordinates $(x,p)$, the momentum wavefunction convention $\\exp(+ipx/\\hbar)$ in the inverse Fourier transform, and the Hamilton-principal spatial action $S_x=\\int(p\\,dx-H\\,ds)$. Momentum-polarized action is\n\n$$\nS_p=S_x-[px]_{\\mathrm{initial}}^{\\mathrm{final}}.\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.17}\n$$\n\nThis endpoint correction is explicit and is kept separate from $\\kappa_{A}$.\n\nDuring a raised-cosine pulse the mechanical flow parameter is $u(s)=\\int_0^s g(v)\\,dv$, with $u(t)=t$. The physical laboratory duration remains $t$; $u$ is the flow parameter of the time-dependent Hamiltonian. Momentum is constant. In the first pulse arm 0 has $x=x_0+p\\,u(s)/m$, while arm 1 stays at $x_0$. During the echo both arms move by $-p\\,u(s)/m$. Write $E=p^{2}/(2m)$, and retain the grade's $B_n,c_n$. The complete two-pulse path ledger is:\n\n| arm | spatial displacement | $S_x$ | $[px]$ | $S_p$ |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 0: free, then inverse-free | 0 | $-tB_n$ | 0 | $-tB_n$ |\n| 1: constant reference, then inverse-free | $-pt/m$ | $-\\hbar c_n-Et-tB_n$ | $-2Et$ | $Et-\\hbar c_n-tB_n$ |\n\nThese values follow by integration. On the free segment, $p\\,dx=2E\\,du$ and $H\\,ds=E\\,du$, so $S_x=Et$. On the inverse segment $H\\,ds=(B_n-E)\\,du$ and $p\\,dx=-2E\\,du$, so $S_x=-Et-tB_n$. The constant first segment on arm 1 contributes $-\\hbar c_n$. Equation (Q.0.2g.17) yields the last column.\n\nThe relative momentum-polarized action is therefore\n\n$$\nS_{p,1}-S_{p,0}=Et-\\hbar c_n=\\hbar L_n(p).\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.18}\n$$\n\nIn the spatial-polarized record it is instead\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{x,1}-S_{x,0}&=-Et-\\hbar c_n, \\\\\n-\\Delta[px]&=+2Et,\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhose sum is (Q.0.2g.18). Both records are retained; the term $Et$ enters the momentum-polarized and the spatial relative action with opposite signs. The common $-tB_n$ is present in both absolute actions; it cancels in their difference and in the scalar channel quotient of (Q.0.2g.4), and it remains in the apparatus invoice.\n\n##### Geometric contributions and domains\n\nThe probe projectors and the momentum frame are fixed. The construction transports no Hamiltonian eigenline and contains no adiabatic parameter loop, so the registered fixed-frame connection contribution, the Berry term of this family, is zero.\n\nThe classical transformations are free symplectic shears. The echo path of arm 0 retraces the shear and is a closed path with identity metaplectic endpoint; its oriented Maslov index is zero in $\\exp(-i\\pi\\mu/2)$ convention. Arm 1 is an open negative shear. It has no interior focal crossing in the stated free coordinate-kernel chart; its interior Maslov index is zero. The kernel's initial endpoint polarization and square-root branch are fixed separately below. Thus the closed-branch specialization applies to arm 0 only, and exact open-path formulas govern arm 1.\n\nFor $u>0$ the exact free and inverse-free coordinate kernels, in the declared Fourier convention, are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nK_u(x,y)&=\\sqrt{m/(2\\pi\\hbar u)}\\, \\exp(-i\\pi/4) \\\\\n&\\quad\\exp[i m(x-y)^{2}/(2\\hbar u)], \\\\\nK_{-u}(x,y)&=\\sqrt{m/(2\\pi\\hbar u)}\\, \\exp(+i\\pi/4) \\\\\n&\\quad\\exp[-i m(x-y)^{2}/(2\\hbar u)].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.19}\n$$\n\nThese distributional kernels are comparison formulas. Their normalization and endpoint square-root phases follow by completing the Fourier Gaussian integral, with the branch continuous from positive real Gaussian regularization. Convolution $K_{-u}K_{u}$ is the identity as an operator on normal states. Thus the endpoint phases of the retraced echo cancel. The $\\pm\\pi/4$ Fourier endpoint factors are retained in the boundary/determinant convention and are distinct from an interior integer Maslov crossing.\n\nEvery propagation and shear above is exactly quadratic. The commuting time profile only reparametrizes u, so the finite-speed and higher semiclassical phase corrections vanish identically. The nonzero finite-grade errors are the separately bounded canonical discretization, state-preparation and service-precision errors.\n\n#### 7. Normal Gaussian paths and complete observable records\n\nTake the normal spatial packet\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\psi_0(x)&=(2\\pi\\sigma^{2})^{-1/4} \\\\\n&\\quad\\exp[-(x-x_0)^{2}/(4\\sigma^{2})+ip_0(x-x_0)/\\hbar],\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith $\\sigma>0$ and finite $x_0,p_0$. Its momentum variance is $v=\\hbar^{2}/(4\\sigma^{2})$. Let $E_0=p_0^{2}/(2m)$, $\\eta=\\hbar t/(2m\\sigma^{2})$, $b=\\eta/2$. The packet and its finite approximants are supplied preparations with errors and costs.\n\nBefore the echo, the free arm's exact spatial packet is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\psi_t(x)&=(2\\pi\\sigma^{2})^{-1/4}(1+i\\eta)^{-1/2} \\\\\n&\\quad\\exp[-(x-x_0-p_0t/m)^{2}/(4\\sigma^{2}(1+i\\eta)) \\\\\n&\\qquad+ip_0(x-x_0)/\\hbar-iE_0t/\\hbar].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.20}\n$$\n\nThe square root is continuous from $t=0$ and equals one there. Its phase is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&p_0(x-x_0)/\\hbar-E_0t/\\hbar \\\\\n&\\quad+\\eta(x-x_0-p_0t/m)^{2}/[4\\sigma^{2}(1+\\eta^{2})] \\\\\n&\\quad-(1/2) \\arctan \\eta.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.21}\n$$\n\nAt the moving center it is $+E_0t/\\hbar-(1/2)\\arctan\\eta$. The first term there is the spatial principal action, the Gouy/determinant boundary phase is $-(1/2)\\arctan\\eta$, and the momentum-polarized phase is $-E_0t/\\hbar$.\n\nThe following formulas are the canonical comparison wavepackets associated with a grade’s retained scalar baseline; the finite-grid wavefunctions approximate them with the errors of parts 5 and 9 of this proof. On the full real-line canonical space the operator $B_n-H$ is not positive, so the positive inverse-free pulse exists at finite grade only. After the full canonical comparison echo, arm 0 is $\\exp(-itB_n/\\hbar)\\psi_0$. Arm 1 is $\\exp[-i(tB_n/\\hbar+c_n)]\\psi_{-t}$, with\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\psi_{-t}(x)&=(2\\pi\\sigma^{2})^{-1/4}(1-i\\eta)^{-1/2} \\\\\n&\\quad\\exp[-(x-x_0+p_0t/m)^{2}/(4\\sigma^{2}(1-i\\eta)) \\\\\n&\\qquad+ip_0(x-x_0)/\\hbar+iE_0t/\\hbar].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.22}\n$$\n\nIts phase, excluding the retained constant $-tB_n/\\hbar-c_n$, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&p_0(x-x_0)/\\hbar+E_0t/\\hbar \\\\\n&\\quad-\\eta(x-x_0+p_0t/m)^{2}/[4\\sigma^{2}(1+\\eta^{2})] \\\\\n&\\quad+(1/2) \\arctan \\eta.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.23}\n$$\n\nAt its moving center $x=x_0-p_0t/m$ this is $-E_0t/\\hbar+(1/2)\\arctan\\eta$, agreeing with the spatial-action row in part 6 of this proof and its exact boundary determinant. The phase-front term in (Q.0.2g.23) is retained away from that center.\n\nThe full Gaussian boundary correction is explicit. Its Gouy part has $|\\Theta_G|<\\pi/4$ for finite $t>0$; on a nominated $|x-x_{\\mathrm{center}}|\\le A\\sigma\\sqrt{1+\\eta^{2}}$ window, the extra wavefront term has magnitude at most $\\eta A^{2}/4$. Outside that window the exact formula still holds, and the wavefront term is unbounded in $x$. The probability of the excluded window is the known Gaussian tail, bounded for example by $2\\exp(-A^{2}/2)$ for $A>0$. The Fourier endpoint convention in (Q.0.2g.19) and the complex Gaussian determinant in (Q.0.2g.20)/(Q.0.2g.22) are two descriptions of the same convolution calculation and are counted once.\n\n##### Probe readout: normalized, possibly reduced-visibility, and correctly typed\n\nWith prepared probe $|+x\\rangle$, the full ideal echo state, apart from the retained common scalar, is\n\n$$\n(\\psi_0\\otimes|0\\rangle + \\exp(-ic) \\exp(+itH/\\hbar)\\psi_0\\otimes|1\\rangle)/\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nPut $\\chi=\\langle\\psi_0|\\exp(-ic)\\exp(+itH/\\hbar)|\\psi_0\\rangle$. Direct integration of its normalized momentum Gaussian gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\chi&=\\exp(-ic)(1-ib)^{-1/2} \\\\\n&\\quad\\exp[i(E_0t/\\hbar)/(1-ib)],\\quad c=(\\ln 2)/2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.24}\n$$\n\nThe root is continuous from $b=0$. The independent x/y detector records are exactly\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np_{x,\\pm}&=(1\\pm\\operatorname{Re} \\chi)/2, \\\\\np_{y,\\pm}&=(1\\pm\\operatorname{Im} \\chi)/2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.25}\n$$\n\nThe sign agrees with Theorem Q.0.2e: on a scalar reference $\\exp(i\\ell)$, $\\chi=\\exp(i\\ell)$ and $p_{y,+}=(1+\\sin\\ell)/2$. These two full normalized response pairs include the field/probe overlap. Explicitly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\chi|&=(1+b^{2})^{-1/4} \\\\\n&\\quad\\exp[-(E_0t/\\hbar)b/(1+b^{2})], \\\\\n\\arg\\chi&=-c+(1/2)\\arctan b+(E_0t/\\hbar)/(1+b^{2}) \\bmod 2\\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn particular $|\\chi|<1$ for a finite-width packet and $t>0$, so the reduced probe map is not a unitary phase channel: the probe is entangled with the momentum. The unitary equality (Q.0.2g.5)/(Q.0.2g.10) holds on the full field and probe carrier with every spectator. The exact scalar-control unitary responses are those of finite $|j\\rangle$ inputs in (Q.0.2g.6).\n\nAt finite grade the exact diagnostic record is\n\n$$\n\\chi_n=\\sum_j |\\psi_n(j)|^{2} \\exp[iL_n(j)],\n$$\n\nand the same normalized formulas (Q.0.2g.25) hold with $\\chi_{n}$. A joint terminal momentum/probe readout has probabilities\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(j,\\pm|x)&=|\\psi_n(j)|^{2}[1\\pm\\cos L_n(j)]/2, \\\\\np(j,\\pm|y)&=|\\psi_n(j)|^{2}[1\\pm\\sin L_n(j)]/2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.26}\n$$\n\nAll outcomes and poststates are retained. Summing j yields the reduced response; conditioning on a recorded j is a separately typed postmeasurement diagnostic.\n\n##### Additional geometric observable check\n\nThe ideal output means on the two branches obey\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\langle X\\rangle_0=x_0,\\quad \\langle X\\rangle_1=x_0-p_0t/m, \\\\\n&\\operatorname{Var}(X)_0=\\sigma^{2},\\quad \\operatorname{Var}(X)_1=\\sigma^{2}(1+\\eta^{2}), \\\\\n&\\langle P\\rangle_0=\\langle P\\rangle_1=p_0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.27}\n$$\n\nThe un-echoed free menu instead has +p0t/m on arm 0; the two menus have different geometry. Finite $X_{n}$ is measured by its cyclic Fourier spectral instrument on separately prepared diagnostic trials. Its normalized spectral law and complete conditional state are retained. Before/after means use separate supplied trials. Equations (Q.0.2g.14)–(Q.0.2g.16) control the displacement response on Gaussian inputs.\n\nFor effective Gaussian parameters, sample the momentum wavefunction on the finite grid and normalize; a finite rational-complex approximation can be supplied with norm error $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{prep},n}$. Its full preparation matrix and arithmetic precision are charged. For unbounded $X_{n}$ mean comparisons add the conservative error $2\\lVert X_n\\rVert\\,\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{prep},n}\\le 2\\hbar\\,\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{prep},n}/h_n$. For $X_n^{2}$ moments add $2\\hbar^{2}\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{prep},n}/h_n^{2}$. Choose, for example, $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{prep},n}\\le\\delta_n^{3}$; both added terms tend to zero. Smooth Gaussian sampling, centered differences and exponentially small box tails give convergence of the unapproximated sampled means by the same derivative estimates in part 5 of this proof. The position estimates depend on the supplied derivative and moment bounds of the input. For the exact base raw Gaussians, the finite amplitudes are algebraic.\n\n#### 8. Complete qualified-source word, full stock and independent invoice\n\nThis part supplies the physical cabinet. Let $b=b_n$, $k=b+1$ (field plus probe bits), and $c_{\\mathrm{cells}}=\\max(2,k)$. Purchase $c_{\\mathrm{cells}}$ complete unchanged 75-bit cells of Theorem C.6c.P17a, their supplied-ready stock, their ROMs and one complete qualification prefix. Its $6c_{\\mathrm{cells}}$ core CNOTs, $6c_{\\mathrm{cells}}$ cursor updates, $16c_{\\mathrm{cells}}$ ROM $X$ writes and forecast-before-outcome order are preserved. The four fresh fair-source bits, two ANDs and four source XORs are the source circuit of Construction Q.0.3h. Hence the predictive gain and null comparisons of the cells remain, with all sixteen qualification histories retained. Only the basis gates of the cells act on their core.\n\nExactly k of the cells' existing payload factors w receive the field+probe through basis SWAPs; a second SWAP bank transfers them to the separate kinetic appliance. Each SWAP is three basis CNOTs. Thus 6k admitted basis CNOTs perform these two banks. Unknown states and all their external-reference correlations are transported by these SWAPs. The core acts as identity on w during qualification; the paid transport macros act on w afterward, through the payload interface of the qualified cells.\n\n##### Exact digital and active stock\n\nAllocate, in addition to the complete cells:\n\n- one k-bit external input bank;\n- one k-bit separate kinetic appliance;\n- one k-bit dummy bank;\n- the eight source/work bits A1,B1,A2,B2,F1,F2,X2,e;\n- four fresh fair-source record cells;\n- k fresh terminal field/probe record cells;\n- four fixed mode/guard cells (three mode bits and one guard).\n\nEvery $w$ bit is already counted in the $75c_{\\mathrm{cells}}$. Thus\n\n$$\nB_{\\mathrm{data}}=75c_{\\mathrm{cells}}+4b+20.\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.28}\n$$\n\nAll carrier cells, including binary padding, unused modes/dummies and unused cell payloads, remain acquired and retained. The input bank is an external supplied-state reserve in unknown-input mode; its state and reference belong to the input supplier. In raw and prepared diagnostic modes that same bank is supplied fresh zero and its preparation is charged. Pure noninput stock and programme stock have explicit supplied-ready services.\n\nThe Gaussian field preparation acts on the input bank in a raw/prepared-diagnostic mode and on the separate dummy in unknown-input mode. The probe preparation likewise prepares the declared basis or $|+x\\rangle$ state in a diagnostic mode, and a dummy in arbitrary-input mode. These are complete finite mode-controlled matrices, with the fixed classical mode chosen before input. Raw mode uses probe $|0\\rangle$; its later computational probe outcome is deterministic and does not alter the $f$/$r$ law. Coherent unknown-input mode accepts its arbitrary field/probe only after qualification and before the two payload SWAP banks. Commissioning explicitly excludes the unknown external input in that mode; the separately prepared dummy and both complete SWAP banks preserve its state and reference correlations. Acceptance is independent of every qualification result and raw Gaussian outcome, and the Gaussian preparation leaves the unknown state untouched.\n\nAfter transport, the two physical kinetic/echo pulses act on the appliance. The coherent channel output cut is the end of the echo, before intentional diagnostics. In unknown-input mode all later reserved readout calls act on the dummy bank and the unknown appliance output is untouched. In raw mode the terminal basis is the identity and its field records are exactly j. In probe diagnostic modes it is the x or y basis on the probe, with identity on the field; in geometric mode it includes the declared cyclic field Fourier basis and a computational probe branch. The requested basis and its inverse are installed on their full supports.\n\nA terminal record service copies/reads each chosen finite basis bit into its fresh record with both outcomes retained. The subsequent paid inverse-basis call returns the measured carrier to its physical basis. Thus the full conditional poststate is the fine-grained projector state in that basis. Degenerate coarse $X_{n}$ eigenvalues can be reported by forgetting labels in a declared classical output map, while the underlying finer Fourier labels remain; the instrument is the fine-grained one and differs from the Lüders instrument of the coarse eigenprojectors.\n\n##### Explicit logical word and controller\n\nSerialize the following exact calls, preserving the inherited prefix's causal order:\n\n1. one commissioning supplied-ready preparation service for all nominated pure noninput stock;\n2. $16c_{\\mathrm{cells}}$ cell-ROM $X$ writes;\n3. four fair Hadamards, two source Toffoli ANDs and four source CNOT XORs;\n4. $6c_{\\mathrm{cells}}$ core CNOTs and $6c_{\\mathrm{cells}}$ cursor $X$ updates, interleaved with their source/forecast records;\n5. one full Gaussian/prepared-field unitary and one probe-preparation unitary, or their declared dummy versions;\n6. $6k$ basis CNOTs for the two complete payload SWAP banks;\n7. the first physical kinetic/reference pulse and the physical inverse-free echo;\n8. one terminal basis unitary and one inverse-basis unitary.\n\nFour fair-source record services occur at their consumption times, and k terminal record services after the terminal basis. Therefore the exact logical unitary/preparation and record-service counts are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=28c_{\\mathrm{cells}}+6k+17, \\\\\nj&=k+4=b+5, \\\\\nL_{\\mathrm{calls}}&=u+j.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.29}\n$$\n\nThe constant 17 is commissioning 1, fair Hadamards 4, source Toffolis 2, source CNOTs 4, field/probe preparations 2, kinetic/echo 2 and terminal bases 2. Unknown-input, raw and diagnostic modes use the same reserved support/slot census; unused calls are paid dummies.\n\nAllocate $r=\\lceil\\log_2(L_{\\mathrm{calls}}+1)\\rceil$ physical controller bits and $r$ fresh trace bits for each logical call. After every logical call append a cyclic controller increment and an XOR-into-fresh copy of its $r$-bit value. These are two supplied and charged finite unitaries on their full supports. In particular the two controller slots after the first pulse precede the echo. The appliance field/probe has zero idle Hamiltonian in those slots and every other nonmechanical comparison gap, so $x$ and $p$ remain stationary and the field acquires no further principal action or free phase. The controller/supplier actions remain in their own full invoice. The active mechanical duration is $2t$, whereas the full physical laboratory horizon is $Kt$. All earlier controller inputs and traces persist.\n\nConstruct the programme in the following nonrecursive order. First form the finite logical word, which includes one fixed commissioning-service token and excludes that package’s state-preparation matrix and full installation archive. Its length $L_{\\mathrm{calls}}$ determines r independently of any programme byte count. Next serialize the runtime calls, their full coefficient/matrix tables, algebraic Gaussian preparation data, logarithmic normalization coefficients, profiles, units and error contracts, and the controller/trace matrices on their known r-bit supports. Support names at this stage are symbolic logical addresses, independent of the final ROM length. The resulting finite prefix-coded bit string has its exactly computed length $R_{\\mathrm{bits}}$; it contains neither the commissioning full-support matrix nor a copy of itself, so its length is defined without a fixed point.\n\nAfter $R_{\\mathrm{bits}}$ is frozen compute B below, assign numeric programme/controller/trace addresses, and supply one external preloaded-programme/ready-stock commissioning package on that completed finite support. This package delivers the specified programme basis state and the nominated other ready pure factors while excluding the unknown input as stated above. Its full support, finite profile, supplier reservation and preparation-response record are independent installation data. Its coefficient-port count is evaluated after B is known and included in $C_{\\mathrm{mat}}$, outside the runtime ROM and $R_{\\mathrm{bits}}$. The native supplied-state package is one primitive service. Its full-support readiness/port/peak/response reservations are charged below. The construction order is runtime word, then $R_{\\mathrm{bits}}$, then B and addresses, then one supplied-ready package, with no dependence backwards. A different microscopic manufacturing boundary adds its own supplier records. All runtime installed programme/coupling tables and the separate package are included in their respective invoice rows.\n\nWith every stock factor charged for the entire padded horizon, define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB&=B_{\\mathrm{data}}+R_{\\mathrm{bits}}+r(1+L_{\\mathrm{calls}}), \\\\\nG&=u+2L_{\\mathrm{calls}},\\quad J=j,\\quad K=3L_{\\mathrm{calls}}, \\\\\nT_{\\mathrm{phys}}&=Kt,\\quad I_{\\text{bit-slot}}=BK.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.30}\n$$\n\nThese are exact counts for this nominated conservative allocation, which is not optimized. The initial controller is zero, its trace destinations are fresh, and its finite word retains mode, source and all timing labels. The cabinet has one port and no interport lanes.\n\n##### Delivered matrix ports and positive waveforms\n\nEvery physical finite matrix call includes its full support, delivered coupling and pulse. For a nonphase finite unitary $U$ choose the supplied Hermitian logarithm $A$ with spectrum in $[0,2\\pi]$, $\\exp(-iA)=U$, and implement it by\n\n$$\nH_A(s)=\\hbar g(s) A/t,\\quad 0\\le s\\le t.\n$$\n\nIt is positive, has endpoint $U$, peak at most $4\\pi\\hbar/t$, norm-action at most $2\\pi\\hbar$ and total switching variation at most $8\\pi\\hbar/t$. Its finite matrix/spectral certificate is part of the delivered service, the finite ideal native matrix service of Definition C.6c.4a.\n\nThe two mechanical pulses have peak at most $2B_n$, norm-action exactly $tB_n$ each, and switching variation at most $4B_n$ each. Indeed $0\\le c_n\\le\\max_j\\alpha_j$ follows from $Z_F/Z_R$ being an $r_n$-weighted average of $\\exp(\\alpha_j)$, so $E_{\\mathrm{ref},n}\\le B_n$. These bounds include the full positive echo baseline. Both pulses vanish at their endpoints. Their isolated net work relative to the zero idle Hamiltonian is zero, while their positive peak, energy expectation, variation, waveform service and drive price remain in the invoice; net work, preparation and control cost, and heat are separate rows.\n\nCommissioning and record services are separately declared native supplied-state/normalized-instrument services with finite supports, profiles, peaks, norm-action or work/heat records as appropriate. Their provided response bounds are used directly if larger than the unitary bound above. All pure stock, outcomes and garbage remain retained after the word. A repeated experiment purchases a fresh complete word or supplies an explicit separately charged reset protocol.\n\nThe dense-matrix apparatus is charged through a separate delivered matrix-port count $C_{\\mathrm{mat}}$. For each installed Hamiltonian/unitary on a support of dimension $d_{s}$ charge $d_{s}^{2}$ coefficient ports; for each normalized instrument with $q_{s}$ retained Kraus blocks charge $q_s d_s^{2}$ ports, conservatively retaining all blocks and supports. Sum these entries over the runtime installed calls, including the controller/trace matrices and mode blocks, even when a cheaper reuse would be possible. Then add the commissioning package’s declared full-support preparation ports (conservatively its support-dimension squared), computed after $B$ is frozen; this final $C_{\\mathrm{mat}}$ calculation and the external installation archive stay outside $R_{\\mathrm{bits}}$. The resulting finite $C_{\\mathrm{mat}}$ is computed from the completed support list before input. Padding ports are counted. Pure-ready and clock service ports in this admitted external native boundary carry their explicit declared support/price packages.\n\n##### Full independently calibrated resource tariff\n\nChoose independent strictly positive prices $\\alpha_B,\\alpha_I,\\alpha_G,\\alpha_J,\\alpha_K,\\alpha_{\\Omega},\\alpha_C$ and resource unit $u_*$. Let $\\Omega$ be the maximum of every installed service's full-support peak and retain $A_{\\mathrm{word}}$, the sum of operator norm-action integrals for unitary services, together with all preparation/record work and heat rows. The complete nominated resource invoice is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV/u_{*}&=\\alpha_B B+\\alpha_I I_{\\text{bit-slot}}+\\alpha_G G+\\alpha_J J+\\alpha_K K \\\\\n&\\quad+\\alpha_\\Omega(t/\\hbar)\\Omega+\\alpha_C C_{\\mathrm{mat}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.31}\n$$\n\nFull acquisition/representation, processing, holding, explicit clock/controller calls, readout/custody and delivered matrix ports are present. The externally programmed timing/waveform/pure-ready service boundary is the source type of Theorem Q.0.3l, with these support, duration and peak reservations; the clock is an external service. If an additional physical clock, battery, work buffer or supplier is included in a different source boundary, its stock, occupancy, calls, coefficient ports and responses are concatenated before (Q.0.2g.28)–(Q.0.2g.31) are evaluated.\n\nFor the specified unitary calls, the action bound is\n\n$$\nA_{\\mathrm{unitary}} \\le 2tB_n+2\\pi\\hbar(G-3),\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.32}\n$$\n\nwhere the three excluded calls are the two mechanical pulses and commissioning. The commissioning and instrument response rows are added separately, since (Q.0.2g.32) covers the unitary calls only. The exact per-call action and switching data are retained together with these upper bounds. Source-relative principal action (Q.0.2g.18), norm-action (Q.0.2g.32), positive resource invoice (Q.0.2g.31), signed drive work, preparation free energy and heat are different typed quantities; $\\hbar L$ is the relative principal action (Q.0.2g.18).\n\nEach finite grade is fully priced and has finite duration, stock and bounded couplings. The total resource, peak and baseline action are not uniform in the grade: $B_n$, $C_{\\mathrm{mat}}$ and the absolute echo action grow without bound, while the normalized channel and relative geometric responses have the normal limits of part 5.\n\n#### 9. Precision and finite observations\n\nThe exact equations above are statements at the declared ideal finite native-service boundary. All coefficients of the family are computable: its raw states are algebraic, $c_{n}$ is the logarithm of a positive explicitly given algebraic number, and the remaining scalar controls have finite computable descriptors in independently calibrated physical units. The matrix and logarithmic coefficient ports are delivered and calibrated native services, a source premise with a positive price. A digital or microscopic replacement meets the following retained errors.\n\nFor two Hamiltonian profiles on the same finite support, Duhamel's integral identity gives\n\n$$\n\\lVert U_{\\mathrm{actual}}-U_{\\mathrm{ideal}}\\rVert \\le (1/\\hbar)\\int\\lVert H_{\\mathrm{actual}}-H_{\\mathrm{ideal}}\\rVert\\, ds.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding unitary-channel diamond distance is at most twice this bound, capped at 2. Composing finite channels/instruments telescopes their diamond errors. Add the supplied preparation trace error, normalized record-instrument errors, both mechanical pulse errors and all relevant control/coupling errors. Equation (Q.0.2g.12) then adds the separate canonical grid/tail error. A finite approximate implementation satisfies the finite-law identities within those margins.\n\nFor raw laws, if $|\\tilde{f}(j)-f_n(j)|\\le\\varepsilon_F$ and $|\\tilde{r}(j)-r_n(j)|\\le\\varepsilon_R$, with $\\varepsilon_F\\le\\min f_n/2$ and $\\varepsilon_R\\le\\min r_n/2$, the mean-value theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\ln[\\tilde{f}(j)/\\tilde{r}(j)]-L_n(j)| \\\\\n&\\le 2\\varepsilon_F/\\min f_n+2\\varepsilon_R/\\min r_n.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{Q.0.2g.33}\n$$\n\nAll minima are strictly positive at each finite grade and tend to zero with n, so the precision and the number of observations required for a fixed ledger accuracy, and their prices, grow without bound in n. The ledger is the pointwise raw log ratio (Q.0.2g.1).\n\nThe finite coefficient/service precision and ROM/port descriptions are included before the compiler counts are evaluated. The finite Fourier and raw-preparation routines are exact algebraic computations. The mandatory channel and x/y detector use explicitly given matrices. Optional nonalgebraic observable spectra are implemented by a separately supplied validated spectral certificate or by a finite numerical instrument with its own operator/outcome error.\n\nEvery binary $x$/$y$ response is a normalized observed outcome. To estimate both positive probabilities with additive error $\\varepsilon$ and joint failure probability at most $\\alpha$, $N\\ge\\ln(4/\\alpha)/(2\\varepsilon^{2})$ independent trials per basis suffice by the elementary Bernoulli Hoeffding bound. Each trial uses the complete paid word and fresh records. The exact identification of Proposition Q.0.2f applies to the ideal complete response laws; a finite experiment determines the calibration within its confidence and error region.\n\n##### Relation of the raw ledger to the coherent channel\n\nThe normalized raw stochastic experiment and the coherent channel are distinct experiments on one source. They are related by the common physical momentum spectrum and normalized source ledger (Q.0.2g.1)/(Q.0.2g.9), the mechanical relative momentum-polarized action (Q.0.2g.18), and the physically implemented complete channel (Q.0.2g.5)/(Q.0.2g.10). The mass, duration, Gaussian widths and reference normalization are calibrations chosen for this source; with them the relative action equals $\\hbar$ times the raw ledger. The path conventions of part 6 and the full-field channel are part of the construction.\n\n∎\n\nExact domain: every stated finite symmetric momentum cabinet, every admitted normal field/probe input and external reference, its normalized pure Gaussian raw comparison preparations and complete finite instruments, and the proved canonical normal-state limit with the supplied derivative/moment/tail domains for unbounded observable responses. Premises: independently calibrated physical mass, duration and action units; the unchanged qualified-cell prefix and payload SWAPs; the separately delivered finite kinetic/reference/echo, matrix, clock, preparation and readout services; the exact zero-idle schedule and explicit nonrecursive programme/commissioning boundary. Every physical finite coefficient/service and approximation error is retained.\n\nEquivalence: equality of complete raw comparison laws with their partition normalizers, exact arbitrary-input/spectator channel equality after the physically implemented echo, and the full registered relative-action/path/observable maps. Only the scalar echo factor cancels in the channel; its absolute baseline action stays in the invoice. The external acceptance and mechanical appliance cuts are distinct and joined by the proved SWAP transport. The two finite incommensurate controls identify $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$ under Proposition Q.0.2f; the full coherent identity is derived independently in (Q.0.2g.2)–(Q.0.2g.5).\n\nBudget and verifier: all finite grades, installed padding/modes/dummies, source histories, controllers, traces, matrix ports and full-horizon custody; exact Gaussian normalization and raw RN ratio; positive pulse algebra and source factorization; scalar calibration; cell/tail/strong-channel estimates and direct finite conjugated-shift response; spatial and momentum-polarized path integrals; fixed Fourier and endpoint conventions; exact Gaussian phase/overlap laws; the full acyclic source compiler and independent resource/work/action/heat rows; and retained service, preparation, raw-log and sampling errors. The closed retraced arm and open shear arm are distinguished. The fixed-frame Berry contribution and interior Maslov indices are zero on their stated branches; the nonzero polarization and Gouy/wavefront boundary terms are kept and bounded. For the exact quadratic dynamics the finite-speed and higher semiclassical phase remainders vanish.\n\nFalsifiers include an unowned source or normalization, discarded field-dependent evolution, nonunitary reduced probe response mislabeled as the full channel, a missing supplier/input-cut operation, a wrong polarization-action sign, double-counted endpoint/Maslov phase, unpriced controller or commissioning self-reference, lost failure/padding/record data, or convergence beyond the stated input/error domain.\n\n### Q.0.5 Conditional Stationarity Transfer\n\n<a id=\"definition-q02a-registered-phase-sector\"></a>**Definition Q.0.2a (Registered Phase Sector).** A phase sector is an equivalence class of admissible paths on which the independently registered differential-character/connection data and boundary data are fixed. The integer $k$ in Theorem Q.0.4 is the lift of an exact trivial-phase condition; it is a topological winding number only when a separate integral homotopy or cohomology certificate identifies it as such. Variations within a sector preserve the registered data.\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q03-conditional-stationarity-transfer\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.3 (Conditional Stationarity Transfer).** On a fixed calibrated sector with constant $\\kappa_A>0$ and $\\mathcal S=\\kappa_A\\mathcal L$,\n$$\n\\delta\\mathcal S=0\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n\\delta\\mathcal L=0.\n$$\nThis equivalence is a scalar-rescaling identity; it neither selects $\\kappa_A$ nor proves the existence of a continuum action. ∎\n\n**Physical Interpretation:** Principle Q.0.3a and Theorem Q.0.3a make the continuum Euler–Lagrange equation the stationarity condition of the $\\Gamma$-limit of the finite predictive ledger on the declared prescribed-boundary and prescribed-holonomy class. Every sequence of discrete PCE minimizers has a convergent subsequence, every cluster point minimizes the continuum ledger, and differentiability on the admissible tangent domain then gives $\\delta\\mathcal S=0$. The independent constant $\\kappa_A$ calibrates ledger units to action units. Nonminimizing saddles remain governed by the separately stated first-variation gate.\n\n### Q.0.6 Connection to the Path Integral\n\nOn a branch carrying an independently defined quantum path amplitude and a historywise action--ledger calibration, its phase factor can be rewritten in ledger variables.\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q02-conditional-ledger-representation-of-a-path-amplitude\"></a>**Proposition Q.0.2 (Conditional Ledger Representation of a Path Amplitude).** Suppose an independently defined quantum path amplitude has phase $e^{i\\mathcal S[\\phi]/\\hbar}$ and a certified branch identifies\n$$\n\\mathcal S[\\phi]=\\kappa_A\\mathcal L[\\phi].\n$$\nThen\n$$\ne^{i\\mathcal S[\\phi]/\\hbar}\n=e^{i(\\kappa_A/\\hbar)\\mathcal L[\\phi]}.\n$$\nOnly on the additional calibration $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$ does this reduce to $e^{i\\mathcal L[\\phi]}$. This algebraic substitution neither derives the Feynman measure nor identifies thermodynamic entropy with quantum phase. ∎\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q02c-finite-entropy-transform-duality\"></a>**Proposition Q.0.2c (Finite Entropy Transform Duality).** Let $\\mathcal H_\\Lambda$ be a finite cutoff set of admissible histories and define\n$$\nE[\\phi]:=\\sum_{i\\in\\phi}\\varepsilon_i,\n\\qquad\nZ_\\Lambda(z):=\\sum_{\\phi\\in\\mathcal H_\\Lambda}w_\\phi e^{-zE[\\phi]},\n$$\nwhere $w_\\phi\\ge0$ are branch weights independent of $z$. Assume in addition that the accepted calibration certificate gives $E[\\phi]=\\mathcal S[\\phi]/\\hbar$ for every history in $\\mathcal H_\\Lambda$. Then $Z_\\Lambda(z)$ is an entire function of $z$, and its two distinguished finite-cutoff evaluations are\n$$\nZ_\\Lambda(1)=\\sum_{\\phi\\in\\mathcal H_\\Lambda}w_\\phi e^{-E[\\phi]},\n$$\nthe finite Gibbs/Laplace weight of the registered additive event ledger, and\n$$\nZ_\\Lambda(-i)=\\sum_{\\phi\\in\\mathcal H_\\Lambda}w_\\phi e^{iE[\\phi]}\n=\n\\sum_{\\phi\\in\\mathcal H_\\Lambda}w_\\phi e^{i\\mathcal S[\\phi]/\\hbar},\n$$\nthe finite path-amplitude weight of Proposition Q.0.2. For a countable history set $\\mathcal H$, assume the same certificate gives $E[\\phi]=\\mathcal S[\\phi]/\\hbar$ for every $\\phi\\in\\mathcal H$. The same analytic statement then holds on any domain on which\n$$\nZ(z)=\\sum_{\\phi\\in\\mathcal H}w_\\phi e^{-zE[\\phi]}\n$$\nconverges normally on compact subsets. In particular, the Gibbs value at $z=1$ is defined whenever $1$ lies in such a domain, and the phase value at $z=-i$ is defined either when $-i$ lies in such a domain or as the limit of finite-cutoff oscillatory amplitudes whenever that limit exists.\n\n*Proof.* For finite $\\mathcal H_\\Lambda$, each term $w_\\phi e^{-zE[\\phi]}$ is entire in $z$, and a finite sum of entire functions is entire. Substituting $z=1$ gives the Gibbs/Laplace weight. Substituting $z=-i$ gives $e^{iE[\\phi]}$, and by the historywise calibration hypothesis of Proposition Q.0.2c,\n$$\nE[\\phi]=\\mathcal S[\\phi]/\\hbar\\qquad\\text{on the calibrated branch}.\n$$\nThis gives the path-amplitude form. For countable $\\mathcal H$, normal convergence on compact subsets gives analyticity by the Weierstrass theorem on the stated domain. Boundary or off-domain phase evaluations are not automatic; they are exactly the finite-cutoff oscillatory limits stated in the hypothesis. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q02d-single-entropy-transform-ledger\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.2d (Single Entropy-Transform Ledger).** Under Proposition Q.0.2c, including its historywise calibration $E[\\phi]=\\mathcal S[\\phi]/\\hbar$, the finite Gibbs/Laplace weight and the finite path-amplitude weight are two evaluations of the same finite entropy transform:\n$$\nZ_\\Lambda(z)=\\sum_{\\phi\\in\\mathcal H_\\Lambda}w_\\phi e^{-zE[\\phi]},\n\\qquad\nE[\\phi]=\\sum_{i\\in\\phi}\\varepsilon_i.\n$$\nIn particular,\n$$\nZ_\\Lambda(1)=\\sum_{\\phi\\in\\mathcal H_\\Lambda}w_\\phi e^{-E[\\phi]},\n\\qquad\nZ_\\Lambda(-i)=\\sum_{\\phi\\in\\mathcal H_\\Lambda}w_\\phi e^{iE[\\phi]}\n=\\sum_{\\phi\\in\\mathcal H_\\Lambda}w_\\phi e^{i\\mathcal S[\\phi]/\\hbar}.\n$$\nThus, at finite cutoff, the two displayed weights are evaluations at $z=1$ and $z=-i$ of the same registered transform. This analytic-continuation identity does not identify a physical Gibbs ensemble with a quantum path integral or derive either ledger from SPAP; those interpretations require the independent weight, action-calibration, and quantum-phase hypotheses. For a countable history set, the statement holds only on domains of normal convergence or through the explicitly stated finite-cutoff oscillatory limit.\n\n*Proof.* Proposition Q.0.2c defines the finite transform $Z_\\Lambda(z)$ and proves it is entire. Evaluation at $z=1$ gives the Gibbs/Laplace weight. Evaluation at $z=-i$ gives $e^{iE[\\phi]}$, and the historywise calibration hypothesis of Proposition Q.0.2c gives $E[\\phi]=\\mathcal S[\\phi]/\\hbar$. The countable case inherits exactly the normal-convergence and finite-cutoff-limit qualifications already stated in Proposition Q.0.2c. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q04-conditional-ledger-phase-matching\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.4 (Conditional Ledger Phase Matching).** Under Proposition Q.0.2 and the calibration $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$, relative phases are differences of the certified dimensionless ledger modulo $2\\pi$. Without that bridge, equal structural or thermodynamic costs do not imply constructive quantum interference. ∎\n\n### Q.0.6.1 Selected Update-Count Character Duality\n\n<a id=\"convention-q06a0-character-status\"></a>**Convention Q.0.6a.0 (Character Status).** The character $N\\mapsto e^{iN\\ln2}$ is a mathematically selected character of the update-count group. It is a physical phase only after the independent action and quantum-phase calibrations above. Density of this character in $U(1)$ does not derive a gauge symmetry or select a gauge dynamics.\n\n<a id=\"definition-q06a1-update-count-group-completion\"></a>**Definition Q.0.6a.1 (Update-Count Group Completion).** Let $\\mathbb N$ be the monoid of registered event counts under addition. Its Grothendieck group completion is $\\mathbb Z$. A character of $\\mathbb Z$ is a homomorphism\n$$\n\\chi:\\mathbb Z\\to U(1).\n$$\nThe Landauer character is\n$$\n\\chi_L(N)=e^{iN\\ln 2}.\n\\tag{Q.0.6a}\n$$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q06a2-pontryagin-dual-of-update-count\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.6a.2 (Pontryagin Dual of Update Count).** The character group of the update-count completion is\n$$\n\\widehat{\\mathbb Z}\\cong U(1),\n\\tag{Q.0.6b}\n$$\nwith isomorphism determined by\n$$\n\\theta\\in\\mathbb R/2\\pi\\mathbb Z\n\\quad\\longmapsto\\quad\n\\chi_\\theta(N)=e^{iN\\theta}.\n$$\nUnder this identification, the Landauer character is the point $\\theta=\\ln2\\pmod{2\\pi}$, and its image\n$$\nG_L=\\{\\chi_L(N):N\\in\\mathbb Z\\}\n$$\nis dense in $U(1)$.\n\n*Proof.* A homomorphism $\\chi:\\mathbb Z\\to U(1)$ is determined by $\\chi(1)$. Conversely, every $z\\in U(1)$ defines a homomorphism by $\\chi_z(N)=z^N$. Thus $\\widehat{\\mathbb Z}\\cong U(1)$. Writing $z=e^{i\\theta}$ gives the displayed parameterization. The Landauer character has $z=e^{i\\ln2}$. Density of its image is Theorem Q.0.7d, equivalently irrationality of $\\ln2/(2\\pi)$ from Lemma Q.0.7b. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q06a3-selected-character-fourier-sums-of-update-histories\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.6a.3 (Selected-Character Fourier Sums of Update Histories).** Let $\\mathcal H_\\Lambda$ be a finite cutoff set of histories, with update count $N[\\gamma]\\in\\mathbb Z$ and weights $w_\\gamma\\ge0$. Define the finite update-count measure\n$$\n\\mu_\\Lambda\n=\n\\sum_{\\gamma\\in\\mathcal H_\\Lambda}\nw_\\gamma\\,\\delta_{N[\\gamma]}.\n\\tag{Q.0.6c}\n$$\nIts Fourier transform at the selected Landauer character is the exact finite sum\n$$\n\\widehat\\mu_\\Lambda(\\chi_L)\n=\n\\sum_{\\gamma\\in\\mathcal H_\\Lambda}\nw_\\gamma\\,\\chi_L(N[\\gamma])\n=\n\\sum_{\\gamma\\in\\mathcal H_\\Lambda}\nw_\\gamma e^{iN[\\gamma]\\ln2}.\n\\tag{Q.0.6d}\n$$\nEquation (Q.0.6d) is a selected-character Fourier sum. It is a physical path amplitude only on a branch satisfying Convention Q.0.6a.0's independent action and quantum-phase calibrations. If, in addition, every retained history has a certified equality\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathcal S[\\gamma]}{\\hbar}\n=N[\\gamma]\\ln2+\\Delta[\\gamma],\n$$\nthen its calibrated finite amplitude is\n$$\n\\sum_{\\gamma\\in\\mathcal H_\\Lambda}\nw_\\gamma e^{i\\Delta[\\gamma]}\\chi_L(N[\\gamma]).\n\\tag{Q.0.6e}\n$$\n\n*Proof.* The Fourier transform of a finite measure on $\\mathbb Z$ at a character $\\chi$ is\n$$\n\\widehat\\mu(\\chi)=\\sum_N\\mu(N)\\chi(N).\n$$\nSubstitution of (Q.0.6c) and $\\chi=\\chi_L$ gives (Q.0.6d). Under the separately certified historywise action equality, multiplication by $e^{i\\Delta[\\gamma]}$ gives (Q.0.6e). No physical-amplitude statement follows from the Fourier identity without those calibrations. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q06a4-finite-cyclic-character-orthogonality\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.6a.4 (Finite Cyclic-Character Orthogonality).** On the cyclic quotient $\\mathbb Z/q\\mathbb Z$, the characters\n$$\n\\chi_j(n)=e^{2\\pi ijn/q},\n\\qquad j=0,\\ldots,q-1,\n$$\nsatisfy\n$$\n\\frac1q\\sum_{n=0}^{q-1}\n\\chi_j(n)\\overline{\\chi_k(n)}\n=\n\\delta_{jk}.\n\\tag{Q.0.6f}\n$$\n\n*Proof.* If $j=k$, every summand is $1$. If $j\\ne k$, the sum is a geometric series with ratio $e^{2\\pi i(j-k)/q}\\ne1$ and therefore vanishes. Because $q\\ln2\\notin2\\pi\\mathbb Z$ for every $q\\ge1$, $\\chi_L$ does not descend to a nontrivial character of $\\mathbb Z/q\\mathbb Z$. Thus (Q.0.6f) is generic finite Fourier orthogonality; a physical interference rule additionally requires a separately realized coherent phase branch. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q01aa-rounded-diffusion-path-laws-and-a-finite-exact-fair-bit-source\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.1aa (Rounded Diffusion Path Laws and a Finite Exact-Fair-Bit Source).** Fix integers $d,m\\ge1$, $T>0$, and the unit torus $\\mathbb R^d/\\mathbb Z^d$. Let $b:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R^d$ and $\\sigma:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R^{d\\times m}$ be bounded, $\\mathbb Z^d$-periodic, globally Lipschitz coefficients. Set $a=\\sigma\\sigma^{\\mathsf T}$. Rank changes and zeros are allowed. Start at 0. The zero start is deliberate: it belongs to every grid and needs no computable-real rounding decision. A fixed other start can be handled by supplied grid approximants converging to it, with their initial error included below.\n\nFor $n\\ge1$ put\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\eta_n=h_n=\\delta_n=1/n,\\qquad r_n=1/n^2,\\\\\nK_n=\\lceil Tn\\rceil,\\quad t_k=\\min(k/n,T),\\quad \\Delta_k=t_{k+1}-t_k,\\\\\nG_n=(r_n\\mathbb Z^d)/\\mathbb Z^d,\\qquad N_n=|G_n|=n^{2d}.\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nThere are at most two distinct positive $\\Delta_k$. Let $R_n$ be coordinatewise nearest-grid rounding on $\\mathbb R^d$, using $R_n(y)_i=r_n\\lfloor y_i/r_n+1/2\\rfloor$. This Borel convention satisfies $R_n(y+\\ell)=R_n(y)+\\ell$ for $\\ell\\in\\mathbb Z^d$ and $|R_n(y)-y|\\le c_dr_n$, $c_d=\\sqrt d/2$.\n\nDefine a reference lifted chain with independent standard Gaussian vectors $\\xi_k\\in\\mathbb R^m$ and $\\zeta_k\\in\\mathbb R^d$ by\n\n$$\nZ_0=0,\\qquad Z_{k+1}=R_n\\!\\left(Z_k+\\Delta_k b(Z_k)+\\sqrt{2\\eta_n\\Delta_k}\\,[\\sigma(Z_k)\\xi_k+\\sqrt{\\delta_n}\\,\\zeta_k]\\right).\\tag{Q.0.1aa.1}\n$$\n\nThe projected nodes form a genuine finite-state time-inhomogeneous Markov chain on $G_n$. Its transition probabilities $p_{k,n}(x,y)$ integrate the fully normalized Gaussian with mean $x+\\Delta_kb(x)$ and covariance\n\n$$\nC_{k,n}(x)=2\\eta_n\\Delta_k[a(x)+\\delta_n I_d].\\tag{Q.0.1aa.2}\n$$\n\nover all lattice translates of the half-open rounding cell centered at y. The positive $\\delta_n$ makes boundaries probability zero. The infinite sum is part of the mathematical definition, not an online Gaussian sampling requirement.\n\nDefine $J_n$ to interpolate any node sequence in $G_n$ by the straight torus geodesic with coordinate displacement in $[-1/2,1/2)$, resolving antipodal ties by that convention. This defines a continuous path from each finite node sequence; continuity of $J_n$ as a map between varying spaces is neither asserted nor needed.\n\nPut\n\n$$\nB_n=n^2+\\lceil\\log_2(K_nN_n)\\rceil,\\qquad M_n=2^{B_n}.\\tag{Q.0.1aa.3}\n$$\n\nFor each distinct pair $(\\Delta,x)$ at fixed n choose one stochastic row q of denominator $M_n$ with row total variation at most $N_n/M_n$, and reuse it at every time step with that $\\Delta$ and state x. A constructive choice appears in Part III. Let $Q_n$ be the finite-state Markov law with these q rows, start 0, and path output $J_n$.\n\n**Path-law and finite-source conclusion.** The laws $Q_n$ obey a good LDP on $C([0,T],\\mathbb{R}^d/\\mathbb{Z}^d)$, in the uniform topology, at speed $n$. The rate is\n\n$$\nI_0(\\gamma)=\\frac14\\int_0^T[\\dot z-b(z)]^T a(z)^+[\\dot z-b(z)]\\,dt.\\tag{Q.0.1aa.4}\n$$\n\nif $\\gamma$ starts at 0, its lift z from 0 is absolutely continuous, and $\\dot z-b(z)\\in\\operatorname{ran}a(z)$ almost everywhere; otherwise the rate is infinity. The integral is extended-valued: its finiteness remains a separate condition. This theorem gives the full path LDP, not an arbitrary closed-event lower bound or singleton-endpoint exponent.\n\nEach n has an exact sampler of its node sequence using $K_nB_n$ independent fair bits, finite integer registers, and a finite transition table. A finite rational description of the path clock and effective generation of the tables are asserted under the effective hypotheses below. It does not use real-coordinate Gaussian sampling online. Effective compilation is asserted only when T is a supplied positive rational and $b,\\sigma$ have computable evaluation procedures with supplied effective magnitude and Lipschitz bounds. Arbitrary abstract Lipschitz coefficients are not automatically computable. No uniform polynomial preprocessing bound follows merely from these inputs.\n\n**Proof.**\n\n#### I. Rounded lifted polygons and the full path rate\n\nThe following proof controls the rounded Brownian functional itself, including multiplicative noise and the short final time cell.\n\nWork on canonical Wiener space for the independent Brownian coordinates (W,B), with their usual completed, augmented natural filtration. All controls in the variational formula are progressively measurable with respect to this filtration; anticipating controls are excluded. Let $u=(q,v)$ be such a control with $\\int_0^T|u|^2\\le A$ almost surely. Insert its cell integrals into the Brownian-shifted recurrence. At every step perform the same rounding after adding the controlled increment. If $e_{k+1}$ is that rounding error, then $|e_{k+1}|\\le c_dr_n$ for every realization and every control, without a continuity assumption on rounding.\n\nUse the auxiliary adapted càdlàg frozen process Y, which evolves during each open cell by frozen drift, controlled drift, and Brownian noise and jumps by $e_{k+1}$ at its right endpoint. Its node values are the rounded nodes. Let $\\bar Y_s$ be the most recent node value, and $R_t=\\sum_{t_{k+1}\\le t}e_{k+1}$. Then exactly\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nY_t&=\\int_0^t b(\\bar Y_s)\\,ds+\\sqrt2\\int_0^t[\\sigma(\\bar Y_s)q_s+\\sqrt{\\delta_n}\\,v_s]\\,ds+M_t+R_t,\\\\\nM_t&=\\sqrt{2\\eta_n}\\int_0^t[\\sigma(\\bar Y_s)\\,dW_s+\\sqrt{\\delta_n}\\,dB_s].\n\\end{aligned}\\tag{Q.0.1aa.5}\n$$\n\nEndpoint choices for integrands do not matter. The rounding errors can depend on the current Brownian increment; R is adapted, and its deterministic variation bound, rather than any martingale property, is used:\n\n$$\n\\sup_t|R_t|\\le\\operatorname{Var}(R)\\le c_dK_nr_n\\le c_d(T+h_n)\\frac{r_n}{h_n}=O(r_n/h_n)=O(1/n).\\tag{Q.0.1aa.6}\n$$\n\nThis is the precise $O(r/h)$ rounding perturbation: the accumulated forcing in the integral equation. It is not a pathwise bound on the difference of two arbitrary multiplicative-noise trajectories under a common Gaussian coupling.\n\nWrite $B_0=\\sup|b|$, $S=\\sup\\|\\sigma\\|_{\\rm op}$ and $S_F=\\sup\\|\\sigma\\|_F$. Doob's inequality and Itô isometry give\n\n$$\n\\mathbb E\\sup_t|M_t|^2\\le8\\eta_nT(S_F^2+d\\delta_n).\\tag{Q.0.1aa.7}\n$$\n\nFor every within-cell time and also its pre-rounding right limit, the increment from the left node is bounded by\n\n$$\nD_n=B_0h_n+\\sqrt2(S+1)\\sqrt{Ah_n}+2\\sup_t|M_t|+c_dr_n.\n$$\n\nThus $\\sup|Y-\\bar Y|\\le D_n$ (with the appropriate left-cell interpretation at a node); if $P_n^u$ is the continuous Euclidean polygon through the rounded nodes, then $\\sup|P_n^u-Y|\\le2D_n$. The latter follows by comparing both to the same left node within a cell. The jump at a right node has already been included in $D_n$.\n\nLet $\\Phi(u)=z$ solve $\\dot z=b(z)+\\sqrt2\\sigma(z)q,\\ z(0)=0$. Put $L_A=L_bT+\\sqrt2L_\\sigma\\sqrt{TA}$. Subtract its integral equation from (Q.0.1aa.5). Gronwall with integrable coefficient $L_b+\\sqrt2L_\\sigma|q_s|$ yields\n\n$$\n\\|Y-\\Phi(u)\\|_\\infty\\le e^{L_A}\\left[L_AD_n+\\sqrt{2\\delta_nTA}+\\|M\\|_\\infty+c_dK_nr_n\\right].\\tag{Q.0.1aa.8}\n$$\n\nConsequently, uniformly over these adapted bounded-energy controls,\n\n$$\n\\mathbb E\\|P_n^u-\\Phi(u)\\|_\\infty^2\\le C_A[h_n+\\eta_n+\\delta_n+(K_nr_n)^2]\\longrightarrow0.\\tag{Q.0.1aa.9}\n$$\n\nThe harmless $r_n^2$ term is absorbed in $(K_nr_n)^2$, and $h_n^2$ in $h_n$. This proof includes the possibly short last cell using its actual $\\Delta_k$. A supplied initial grid error adds its squared magnitude to (Q.0.1aa.9).\n\nFor completeness, the remaining probability argument is stated explicitly on this canonical Brownian space. Weak $L^2$ control balls are compact metrizable. $\\Phi$ is continuous from their weak topology into uniform paths: bounded-energy solutions have a common 1/2-Hölder modulus; in any uniformly convergent subsequence, Lipschitz coefficient convergence and weak testing against $\\sigma(z)$ identify the integral equation and its unique solution. Thus random controls $u_n\\Rightarrow u$ on a weak ball satisfy $(u_n,P_n^{u_n})\\Rightarrow(u,\\Phi(u))$ by (Q.0.1aa.9).\n\nThe finite rounded recurrence is a Borel Brownian functional, despite its discontinuities. The Boué--Dupuis bounded-measurable Brownian variational identity applies (Chiarini and Fischer 2014, Appendix A (A.1)). For bounded continuous F,\n\n$$\n-\\eta_n\\log\\mathbb E e^{-F(P_n)/\\eta_n}=\\inf_u\\mathbb E\\left[\\frac12\\|u\\|_2^2+F(P_n^u)\\right].\\tag{Q.0.1aa.10}\n$$\n\nDeterministic controls and (Q.0.1aa.9) give the upper Laplace bound. For the lower bound, with $L=\\|F\\|_\\infty$ choose nearly minimizing controls whose expected squared energies are $\\le4L+2$. Stop each when its energy reaches A. Stopping decreases energy, changes the output only on an event of probability $\\le(4L+2)/A$, and so incurs at most $2L(4L+2)/A$ in the expected F term. On the weakly compact ball, (Q.0.1aa.9), weak lower semicontinuity of energy and Portmanteau give the lower bound by $\\inf_u\\{\\tfrac12\\|u\\|_2^2+F(\\Phi(u))\\}$, less that stopping error. Send $A\\to\\infty$. This proves the Laplace principle.\n\nIts control rate is good because $\\Phi$ maps weak energy balls compactly and a minimizing sequence has a weak limit realizing the minimum. The least-norm solution of $\\sqrt2\\sigma(z)q=\\dot z-b(z)$ is $q=\\sigma(z)^+(\\dot z-b(z))/\\sqrt2$. The pseudoinverse is Borel even at rank changes; its squared norm is half the integrand quadratic in (Q.0.1aa.4). The unused v is optimally zero. This identifies the rate. Projection onto the torus is continuous, and each full continuous projected path has a unique lift anchored at 0, so contraction gives exactly (Q.0.1aa.4) for the projected lifted polygon.\n\n#### II. Shortest-coordinate decoding\n\nThe lifted reference polygon uses potentially unbounded winding increments and is not itself a finite-register source. The finite node chain with $J_n$ discards that information. We now prove that this loss does not change its LDP under the stated schedules.\n\nIf every rounded lifted increment has all its coordinate magnitudes strictly below 1/2, projection of the lifted polygon equals $J_n$ of its projected nodes. For n sufficiently large, $B_0/n+r_n/2\\le1/4$. A failure therefore entails a Gaussian noise coordinate with magnitude at least 1/4 in at least one cell. Conditionally on the past each such coordinate is centered Gaussian of variance at most\n\n$$\nV_n=\\frac{2(S^2+1)}{n^2}.\n$$\n\nIts two-sided tail is at most $2\\exp[-1/(32V_n)]$. A union bound, requiring no independence of these state-dependent increments, yields\n\n$$\n\\mathbb P\\{\\text{projected lifted polygon differs from }J_n\\}\\le2dK_n\\exp\\!\\left[-\\frac{n^2}{64(S^2+1)}\\right].\\tag{Q.0.1aa.11}\n$$\n\nThe logarithm divided by n tends to minus infinity. Hence the two coupled path laws are exponentially equivalent at speed n, for every positive uniform-distance tolerance, and have the same good LDP. Possible exact antipodal ties do not need a zero-probability argument: any tie belongs to the same long-increment event and is covered by (Q.0.1aa.11). Nearest-grid rounding ties have probability zero in the uncontrolled positive-floor reference law; under controls they may occur, but (Q.0.1aa.6) is valid under the fixed convention anyway.\n\nThis argument is schedule-specific. It does not assert that shortest-geodesic interpolation is harmless for arbitrary mesh/noise schedules or arbitrary winding processes.\n\n#### III. Certified dyadic rows and complete path-law transfer\n\nUse the convention $\\operatorname{TV}(p,q)=\\tfrac12\\sum_j|p_j-q_j|$. Here $N=N_n,\\ B=B_n,\\ M=2^B$. Since $K\\ge1\\text{ and }n\\ge1,\\ M\\ge2N$. Given any probability row p, obtain rational numbers $v_j$ with $|v_j-p_j|\\le1/(4M)$. Set $z_j=\\max(v_j,0),\\ s=\\sum_jz_j\\text{ and }w_j=z_j/s$. Because $\\|z-p\\|_1\\le N/(4M)<1,\\ s>0$, and\n\n$$\n\\|w-p\\|_1\\le\\|w-z\\|_1+\\|z-p\\|_1=|1-s|+\\|z-p\\|_1\\le\\frac{N}{2M}.\n$$\n\nCompute integer floors $a_j=\\lfloor Mw_j\\rfloor$. Distribute the remaining $M-\\sum_ja_j$ units to entries with largest fractional remainders, breaking ties by index. All calculations here are on rational numbers, so no undecidable equality test on true probabilities occurs. Each resulting integer $c_j$ differs from $Mw_j$ by at most 1, $c_j\\ge0,\\text{ and }\\sum_jc_j=M$. For $q_j=c_j/M$,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{TV}(q,p)\\le\\frac12\\left[\\frac NM+\\frac{N}{2M}\\right]=\\frac{3N}{4M}\\le\\frac NM.\\tag{Q.0.1aa.12}\n$$\n\nThis proves mathematical existence for any row; its effective version requires the certified $v_j$ and thus the computability hypotheses of Part IV. The q law is exactly dyadic; it is deliberately an approximation to p, not a claim of exact finite-bit Gaussian-cell sampling.\n\nBy sequential maximal coupling, two chains whose rows differ by at most $\\varepsilon$ can be coupled to keep all K transitions equal with failure probability $\\le K\\epsilon$. Alternatively the same bound follows by telescoping products of Markov kernels in total variation. Deterministic interpolation cannot increase TV. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\operatorname{TV}\\bigl(\\operatorname{law}(J_n\\text{ under }p),Q_n\\bigr)\\le\\frac{K_nN_n}{2^{B_n}}\\le2^{-n^2},\\\\\n\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}\\frac1n\\log\\operatorname{TV}=-\\infty.\n\\end{gathered}\\tag{Q.0.1aa.13}\n$$\n\nThis gives exponential equivalence via maximal path coupling, hence transfers the good path LDP to $Q_n$. It also follows directly from probability inequalities with a superexponential additive error. Arbitrarily small entries may become zero after dyadic approximation; (Q.0.1aa.13), rather than unsupported pointwise relative-error claims, is the reason the LDP survives.\n\n#### IV. Effective compilation and finite resources\n\nFor an effective uniform compiler assume positive rational T, computable coefficient evaluations on rational points with supplied finite magnitude and Lipschitz bounds, and fixed finite d,m. Then $K_n$, all $\\Delta_k$ and grid coordinates are exact rational data. At a grid state, the mean and covariance (Q.0.1aa.2) are computable; covariance has the supplied strictly positive lower bound\n\n$$\n\\lambda_{\\min}(C_{k,n})\\ge2\\eta_n\\Delta_k\\delta_n>0.\n$$\n\nUpper covariance bounds are supplied as well. Gaussian mass outside a sufficiently large rational box can therefore be bounded effectively by coordinate tail bounds. Intersect the wrapped rounding cells with that box, leaving finitely many rational rectangles. On the box the positive-definite Gaussian density is a computable smooth function with effective bounds/moduli obtained from covariance lower/upper bounds. Certified quadrature, with a density approximation error and a separate tail budget, computes all required probabilities to any absolute rational accuracy. Cell boundaries have zero Gaussian measure. No equality comparison between a true probability and a dyadic threshold is required: approximate rational values feed Part III.\n\nThe covariance may be very ill-conditioned as n grows, and an exceptionally short last cell can increase integration difficulty; this does not invalidate termination at a fixed positive rational $\\Delta$. It does invalidate inferring a practical or polynomial compilation-time bound from table size alone. A general computable T without a partition/comparison promise could also make ceiling/equality decisions problematic, which is why the effective theorem states rational T. The abstract mathematical LDP does not need rational T.\n\nOnly two distinct step lengths need tables. Let $L_n\\le2$ be their number. Store each row as $N_n$ nonnegative integer counts $c_j$, each in $[0,2^{B_n}]$, so $B_n+1$ bits per count suffice. Then:\n\n- Grid states: $N_n=n^{2d}$; a packed state label uses $\\lceil\\log_2N_n\\rceil$ bits (coordinatewise encoding uses $d\\lceil\\log_2(n^2)\\rceil$ bits).\n- Transition-count tables: at most $L_nN_n^2(B_n+1)$ bits, bounded by $2n^{4d}(B_n+1)$.\n- Per-step fair randomness: exactly $B_n$ bits, forming U uniform on $\\{0,\\ldots,2^{B_n}-1\\}$.\n- Sampling rule: select the first j with $U<\\sum_{i\\le j}c_i$. Each destination j has exactly $c_j$ preimages and hence probability $q_j$, with no rejection and no Gaussian primitive.\n- Total randomness: exactly $K_nB_n$ bits; $B_n=n^2+O(\\log n)$, hence $K_nB_n=O(n^3)$ at fixed T,d.\n- Online workspace apart from stored tables: current/next state labels, a step counter, a $B_n$-bit draw, and a ($B_n+1$)-bit cumulative count suffice, with $O(B_n+\\log N_n+\\log K_n)$ bits. A linear scan is a valid finite sampler; compiled cumulative tables permit binary search. These algorithmic choices are not a physical binary structural-operation identity.\n- A full sampled node record requires $(K_n+1)\\lceil\\log_2N_n\\rceil$ bits, $O(n\\log n)$; its piecewise-geodesic path is specified exactly by these rational nodes, the rational time partition, and the fixed interpolation rule. An actual continuous-time display or physical trajectory generator is not included.\n- The number of distinct output paths is at most $N_n^{K_n}$, and the fair-bit input space has exactly $2^{K_nB_n}$ elements. Unused random bits are allowed; there is no entropy-optimality assertion.\n\nThese are finite-description and online-randomness bounds after table compilation. The table compiler uses only finite work for each n under the stated effective hypotheses, but its required time and temporary memory are separate resources and have not been bounded above by the listed storage counts. Arbitrary computable coefficient evaluation procedures can themselves be slow. No claim of a uniform fixed-size machine independent of n is made.\n\n∎\n\n#### Explicit computable changing-rank source\n\nOn the unit two-torus take $m=1$,\n\n$$\nb(x,y)=(1,0),\\qquad\\sigma(x,y)=(0,\\sin(2\\pi x))^T,\\qquad x_0=(0,0),\\quad T=1.\n$$\n\nAll coefficient evaluations are computable; $|b|=1,\\ \\|\\sigma\\|\\le1,\\ L_b=0,\\text{ and }L_\\sigma\\le2\\pi<7$ are supplied bounds. The limiting controlled path\n\n$$\n\\gamma(t)=\\left(t,\\frac{1-\\cos(2\\pi t)}{2\\pi}\\right)\\bmod\\mathbb Z^2.\n$$\n\nhas residual $(0,\\sin(2\\pi t))$, lies in the instantaneous covariance range, and has $I_0(\\gamma)=1/4$. The noise rank is zero at $t=0,1/2,1$ and one between them. The least control $q=1/\\sqrt2$ gives that action; values at the zero-rank times do not affect the integral. In contrast $\\gamma_{\\rm bad}(t)=(t,t)$ has infinite rate because its residual second component is 1 and $\\int_0^\\epsilon\\csc^2(2\\pi t)\\,dt$ diverges, despite almost-everywhere range membership.\n\nFor this example $K_n=n,\\ N_n=n^4,\\ B_n=n^2+\\lceil5\\log_2n\\rceil$, and the two-step-length bound improves to one table of $n^8(B_n+1)$ bits. The exact fair-bit count is $nB_n$. These large explicit bounds demonstrate finiteness, not an efficient implementation or thermodynamic cost. The positive floor remains in every finite-n reference kernel even where $\\sigma$ vanishes.\n\nThese are complete finite mathematical path sources with their stated algorithmic records. A physical clock, preparation, memory, transport, lookup, erasure or bath realization requires its own source contract and cost accounting. The rate action and bit counts supply no reset-heat or mechanical-action identity.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q01ab-slowly-diverging-fair-bit-depth-for-multiplicative-diffusion-paths\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.1ab (Slowly Diverging Fair-Bit Depth for Multiplicative Diffusion Paths).** Fix the dimensions, zero start, torus, bounded periodic globally Lipschitz $b,\\sigma$, and time partition of Theorem Q.0.1aa. Thus $h_n=\\eta_n=\\delta_n=1/n$, grid spacing $r_n=n^{-2}$, $K_n=\\lceil Tn\\rceil$, and the actual positive cell lengths are $\\Delta_k=t_{k+1}-t_k$. Put $p=m+d$. Let $\\ell_n\\ge1$ be any integer sequence tending to infinity. No lower divergence rate or upper growth rate is assumed.\n\n#### I. The exact finite source\n\nWrite $\\Phi$ for the standard normal CDF, and define\n$$\nq_{\\ell,j}=\\Phi^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{j+1/2}{2^\\ell}\\right),\\quad 0\\le j<2^\\ell,\n\\qquad a_n=\\lceil\\log_2(8n^3)\\rceil.\n\\tag{Q.0.1ab.1}\n$$\nChoose dyadic rationals $\\widehat q_{n,j}$ of denominator $2^{a_n}$ with error at most $n^{-3}$. For each of the at most two distinct positive step lengths and every grid state, likewise approximate every entry of\n$$\nD_{n,\\Delta}(x)=\\Delta b(x),\\qquad\nH_{n,\\Delta}(x)=\\sqrt{2\\Delta/n}\\,[\\sigma(x),n^{-1/2}I_d]\n\\tag{Q.0.1ab.2}\n$$\nby $\\widehat D,\\widehat H$ of the same dyadic denominator with entry error at most $n^{-3}$. Reuse these fixed arrays whenever the same pair $(\\Delta,x)$ occurs and extend them periodically. A reproducible effective selection is given in Part V. Different allowed arrays need not define the same finite-$n$ source.\n\nAt each step draw $p$ independent uniform $\\ell_n$-bit words $V_{k,i}$ and set $\\widehat Q_k=(\\widehat q_{n,V_{k,1}},\\ldots,\\widehat q_{n,V_{k,p}})$. The actual source is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX_0&=0,\\\\\nX_{k+1}&=R_n\\bigl(X_k+\\widehat D_{n,\\Delta_k}(X_k)\n+\\widehat H_{n,\\Delta_k}(X_k)\\widehat Q_k\\bigr)\\pmod{\\mathbb Z^d},\\\\\nq_n(\\Delta,x,y)&=2^{-p\\ell_n}\\#\\{v\\in\\{0,\\ldots,2^{\\ell_n}-1\\}^p:\n\\text{the displayed update sends }(x,v)\\text{ to }y\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{Q.0.1ab.3}\n$$\nAll online arithmetic, rounding ties and reduction modulo one are rational operations. Word collisions are allowed. Literal counting proves that $q_n$ is stochastic and that independent words give exactly its product Markov law. Output the shortest-coordinate polygon $J_n(X_0,\\ldots,X_{K_n})$ defined in Theorem Q.0.1aa.\n\nThese actual finite output laws have a good full-path LDP at speed $n$ in the uniform topology, with exactly the constrained action (Q.0.1aa.4), including changing rank and zeros of $\\sigma$. Fresh randomness is exactly $pK_n\\ell_n$ fair bits. In particular $\\ell_n=\\lceil\\log_2(n+1)\\rceil$ gives $O(n\\log n)$ bits per path, and $\\ell_n=\\lceil\\log_2\\log_2(n+4)\\rceil$ gives $O(n\\log\\log n)$. Arbitrarily slower divergence still gives this qualitative LDP; no effective convergence rate or entropy optimality is asserted.\n\n#### II. Quantile coupling with all exponential moments\n\n*Proof.* Let $U$ be uniform on $(0,1)$, $Z=\\Phi^{-1}(U)$ and\n$$\nQ_\\ell=\\Phi^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{\\lfloor2^\\ell U\\rfloor+1/2}{2^\\ell}\\right).\n\\tag{Q.0.1ab.4}\n$$\nThen $Q_\\ell\\to Z$ almost surely. For every $a\\ge0$,\n$$\n\\mathbb E e^{a|Q_\\ell|}\\le2\\mathbb E e^{a|Z|},\\qquad \\ell\\ge1.\n\\tag{Q.0.1ab.5}\n$$\nIndeed $e^{a|\\Phi^{-1}(u)|}$ is monotone on each side of $1/2$, a dyadic-cell boundary. On the upper side its midpoint value times cell length is at most twice the integral on the upper half; the lower side uses the lower half. Sum, using monotone integration at the unbounded end cells.\n\nFor any $\\theta>0$ and $s>1$, Cauchy--Schwarz and (Q.0.1ab.5) imply\n$$\n\\mathbb E e^{s\\theta|Z-Q_\\ell|}\n\\le[\\mathbb E e^{2s\\theta|Z|}\\mathbb E e^{2s\\theta|Q_\\ell|}]^{1/2}\n\\le\\sqrt2\\,\\mathbb E e^{2s\\theta|Z|}<\\infty.\n\\tag{Q.0.1ab.6}\n$$\nThus $e^{\\theta|Z-Q_\\ell|}$ is uniformly integrable and its expectation tends to one. The rational reconstruction changes the scalar coupling error by at most $n^{-3}$. For independent coordinates $G\\sim N(0,I_p)$ coupled to $\\widehat Q_n$, Euclidean norm is bounded by the sum of coordinate absolute errors. Hence independence gives\n$$\nM_n(\\theta):=\\mathbb E e^{\\theta|G-\\widehat Q_n|}\\longrightarrow1\n\\quad\\text{for every fixed }\\theta>0.\n\\tag{Q.0.1ab.7}\n$$\nNo pointwise domination by the coupled Gaussian or quantitative inverse-CDF error rate is used.\n\n#### III. Multiplicative-noise propagation with actual rounding\n\nTake independent copies $(G_k,\\widehat Q_k)$ and put\n$$\nA_n=\\frac1n\\sum_{k<K_n}|G_k|,\\qquad\n\\overline E_n=\\frac1n\\sum_{k<K_n}|G_k-\\widehat Q_k|.\n\\tag{Q.0.1ab.8}\n$$\nFor every $\\epsilon,\\theta>0$, Chernoff gives\n$$\n\\Pr(\\overline E_n>\\epsilon)\\le e^{-\\theta n\\epsilon}M_n(\\theta)^{K_n},\n\\qquad\n\\limsup_n\\frac1n\\log\\Pr(\\overline E_n>\\epsilon)=-\\infty.\n\\tag{Q.0.1ab.9}\n$$\nFor the limit take $n\\to\\infty$ first, using $K_n/n\\to T$ and (Q.0.1ab.7), and then $\\theta\\to\\infty$. Writing $\\Lambda(\\lambda)=\\log\\mathbb E e^{\\lambda|G|}$, for each $\\lambda>0$,\n$$\n\\limsup_n\\frac1n\\log\\Pr(A_n>R)\\le-\\lambda R+T\\Lambda(\\lambda).\n\\tag{Q.0.1ab.10}\n$$\nThis tends to $-\\infty$ as $R\\to\\infty$ even at $\\lambda=1$. It is an exponential-tightness localization; $A_n$ is not claimed to have superexponential tails at fixed $R$.\n\nCouple the lifted Gaussian reference chain of Theorem Q.0.1aa to a lifted rational chain retaining the full rounding increments before projection. This lift is a proof variable, not an online unbounded register. Let $d_k$ be their Euclidean node distance. Boundedness and Lipschitz continuity give, uniformly in the cell lengths $\\Delta\\le1/n$,\n$$\n\\|H(x)\\|_{\\rm op}\\le C/n,\\qquad\n\\|H(x)-H(y)\\|_{\\rm op}\\le\\frac{\\sqrt2L_\\sigma}{n}|x-y|,\n\\qquad |D(x)-D(y)|\\le\\frac{L_b}{n}|x-y|.\n\\tag{Q.0.1ab.11}\n$$\nEach nearest-grid rounding residual is at most $c_dn^{-2}$, so their difference is at most $2c_dn^{-2}$. Rounding is not assumed Lipschitz. Split\n$$\nH(Z_k)G_k-H(\\widetilde X_k)\\widehat Q_k\n=[H(Z_k)-H(\\widetilde X_k)]G_k\n+H(\\widetilde X_k)(G_k-\\widehat Q_k).\n\\tag{Q.0.1ab.12}\n$$\nAdding the entrywise rational coefficient errors, with the fixed-dimensional operator-norm factors absorbed in $C$, gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd_{k+1}\\le{}&[1+C/n+C|G_k|/n]d_k\n+(C/n)|G_k-\\widehat Q_k|\\\\\n&+Cn^{-3}(1+|\\widehat Q_k|)+2c_dn^{-2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{Q.0.1ab.13}\n$$\nNo Lipschitz property of the rational approximants is required. Using $|\\widehat Q_k|\\le|G_k|+|G_k-\\widehat Q_k|$ and discrete Gronwall yields\n$$\n\\max_{k\\le K_n}d_k\n\\le e^{CK_n/n+CA_n}\n\\left[C\\overline E_n+Cn^{-2}(K_n/n+A_n+\\overline E_n)\n+2c_dK_n/n^2\\right].\n\\tag{Q.0.1ab.14}\n$$\nFor fixed $R$ and $\\epsilon>0$, choose $\\epsilon_R>0$ so the right side is at most $\\epsilon$ for large $n$ on $\\{A_n\\le R,\\overline E_n\\le\\epsilon_R\\}$. Equations (Q.0.1ab.9)--(Q.0.1ab.10) then imply\n$$\n\\limsup_n\\frac1n\\log\\Pr(\\max_kd_k>\\epsilon)\n\\le-R+T\\Lambda(1).\n\\tag{Q.0.1ab.15}\n$$\nLet $R\\to\\infty$. The lifted polygons are exponentially equivalent at speed $n$: interpolation on their identical actual time partition, including its short last cell, does not increase maximum node discrepancy. Torus projection contracts the distance.\n\n#### IV. Finite-register path decoding and the inherited action\n\nThe finite source stores no winding increments, so one further comparison is required. Let $L_n^G$ be the event that some coordinate of some rounded Gaussian lifted increment has magnitude at least $1/4$. Conditional Gaussian variance is at most $2(S^2+1)/n^2$, where $S=\\sup\\|\\sigma\\|_{\\rm op}$. Drift plus rounding is at most $1/8$ for large $n$. Gaussian coordinate tails and a union bound, without independence of state-dependent increments, give\n$$\n\\Pr(L_n^G)\\le2dK_n\\exp[-c n^2],\\qquad\nc=\\frac1{256(S^2+1)}.\n\\tag{Q.0.1ab.16}\n$$\nOn $\\{\\max_kd_k<1/16\\}\\cap(L_n^G)^c$, each rational lifted coordinate increment has magnitude at most $1/4+2(1/16)=3/8<1/2$. It therefore equals the shortest-coordinate displacement, and the projected lifted polygon equals the actual output $J_n$. Decoder failure is superexponential by (Q.0.1ab.15)--(Q.0.1ab.16). This proof allows every diverging $\\ell_n$, with no upper growth restriction. For the logarithmic and double-logarithmic choices one may instead use the deterministic bound $O(\\sqrt{\\ell_n}/n)+O(n^{-2})$ on rational increments.\n\nThe complete rounded reference LDP is proved in Theorem Q.0.1aa. Exponential equivalence transfers its good constrained rate (Q.0.1aa.4) to these actual finite fair-bit laws. This is path-distance coupling, not total-variation approximation of every Gaussian transition or of the entire Gaussian path law. The continuously varying non-Lipschitz coefficient class is outside the propagation proof (Q.0.1ab.13). Fixed bit depth also cannot replace divergence: with constant scalar nonzero dispersion and bounded fixed reconstructed increments, lifted velocities remain uniformly bounded up to vanishing rounding error, excluding some finite-rate Brownian skeletons. This counterexample concerns this construction, not a universal lower bound on finite-source bit budgets.\n\n#### V. Effective coefficient construction and complete finite resources\n\nA uniformly effective compiler requires positive rational $T$, computable coefficient evaluation with supplied effective magnitude and Lipschitz bounds, and a computable sequence $\\ell_n$. The grid, $K_n$ and actual positive $\\Delta$ values are then decidable rational data. Equation (Q.0.1ab.2) is computable at every grid state. Each normal quantile is computable: its CDF argument is rational in $(0,1)$, the CDF is computable and strictly increasing with positive density, and tail bounds give a finite root bracket. Certified bisection refines any straddling interval until a sufficiently short bracket is certified; it need not decide equality to an unknown real threshold.\n\nTo select dyadic approximants without a real equality test, first find a rational $v$ with certified error at most $n^{-3}/4$, then round $v$ exactly to the nearest multiple of $2^{-a_n}$. Since $2^{-a_n}\\le n^{-3}/8$, total error is less than $n^{-3}$. Fix this algorithm and its rational tie rules before random sampling. It defines an actual reproducible table, not a future-dependent existential choice.\n\nFor fixed coefficient bounds, each entry of $\\widehat D,\\widehat H$ has $O(\\log(n+1))$ bits. Gaussian tails give\n$$\n\\max_j|q_{\\ell,j}|\\le\\sqrt{2(\\ell+1)\\log2},\n\\tag{Q.0.1ab.17}\n$$\nsince the endpoint tail probability is $2^{-\\ell-1}$ and the Gaussian upper tail is at most $e^{-x^2/2}$. Thus each $\\widehat q$ needs $O(\\log(n+1)+\\log(\\ell_n+1))$ bits; the common denominator is stored once. With $N_n=n^{2d}$ and $L_n\\le2$ distinct lengths, an arithmetic-table implementation has:\n\n- $2^{\\ell_n}$ scalar quantile entries, requiring $O(2^{\\ell_n}[\\log(n+1)+\\log(\\ell_n+1)])$ bits.\n- $L_nN_n(d+dp)$ increment-coefficient entries, requiring $O(N_n\\log(n+1))$ bits at fixed dimensions and bounds.\n- $O(\\log(n+1))$ variable clock/index bits, plus the fixed rational encoding of $T$.\n- Online workspace $O(\\ell_n+\\log(n+1)+\\log(\\ell_n+1))$ bits apart from tables and output. This includes all $p$ random words, state/step labels, addresses and rational intermediates. Products have denominator $2^{2a_n}$; adding the state uses $n^22^{2a_n}$. Numerators add only $O(\\log(\\ell_n+1))$ bits, so exact floor and reduction modulo $n^2$ fit these widths.\n- A complete node record of $(K_n+1)\\lceil\\log_2N_n\\rceil$ packed bits, with a one-bit convention for $N_n=1$, or $O(K_nd\\lceil\\log_2(n^2+1)\\rceil)$ coordinatewise. It is $O(n\\log n)$ and, with the rational clock and fixed geodesic convention, specifies the exact output path.\n- Exactly $p\\ell_n$ fresh fair bits per step, or $pK_n\\ell_n$ per path, without rejection or random stopping.\n\nFor logarithmic depth, quantile storage is $O(n\\log n)$, total persistent storage is $O(n^{2d}\\log n+n\\log n)$ and online workspace is $O(\\log n)$. For double-logarithmic depth, $2^{\\ell_n}=O(\\log n)$, so the corresponding quantities are $O((\\log n)^2)$, $O(n^{2d}\\log n+(\\log n)^2)$ and $O(\\log n)$.\n\nAlternatively store the deterministic destination for every $(\\Delta,x,v)$. Its literal table occupies at most\n$$\nL_nN_n2^{p\\ell_n}\\lceil\\log_2N_n\\rceil\n\\tag{Q.0.1ab.18}\n$$\nbits, with the same $N_n=1$ convention. It needs no stored irrational quantity or Gaussian probability integration. The arithmetic tables are usually smaller; a dense stochastic count table is unnecessary because (Q.0.1ab.3) already defines the exact kernel.\n\nPer step, arithmetic uses a fixed-dimensional number of additions, multiplications, exact integer divisions/floors and table accesses at the stated finite widths. Physical access circuitry, bit-operation complexity of the chosen lookup, and compiler time are separate resources. Arbitrarily slow computable coefficient evaluators exclude any uniform polynomial preprocessing bound from these assumptions. This is a family of finite machines, not one fixed register size. The compiler terminates at each fixed $n$, but no effective LDP convergence rate or decision of a rate threshold follows, especially for arbitrarily slow $\\ell_n$ divergence. $\\square$\n\nThe midpoint inverse-normal random-bit quantizer and random-bit Euler simulation are established methods (Giles et al. 2019, §3.2). The result here is the supplied speed-$n$ superexponential transfer for the actual rational, rounded, state-dependent path source. It assigns no fixed-endpoint exponent or arbitrary closed-event lower bound, and it identifies no register count, randomness count or rate action with physical reset heat, a free bath, mechanical action or $\\hbar$. Physical preparation, memory, clock, transport, lookup, erasure and bath services require their own declared source and resource laws.\n\n## Q.0.7 Selected Update-Count Character and Its $U(1)$ Closure\n\nThis section studies the cyclic character generated by the chosen phase increment $\\ln2$. Since $\\ln2/(2\\pi)$ is irrational, its image is dense in $U(1)$. Density is an arithmetic and topological statement; the physical phase assignment enters through the calibration of Theorem Q.0.2e, and the local gauge and Noether conclusions through Theorem Q.0.7d2 and Corollary Q.0.7d3 on their registered branches.\n\n---\n\n### Q.0.7.1 The Landauer Angle\n\nDefine the *Landauer angle* and its reciprocal by:\n$$\\alpha_L := \\frac{\\ln 2}{2\\pi} \\approx 0.110317800$$\n$$\\beta_L := \\frac{2\\pi}{\\ln 2} = \\frac{1}{\\alpha_L} \\approx 9.064720284$$\n\nThe number $\\alpha_L$ is the registered ratio $\\ln2/(2\\pi)$. Interpreting it as a physical phase per event requires the action--entropy and quantum-phase calibrations; Theorem 31 does not supply that identification.\n\n---\n\n### Q.0.7.2 Transcendence of the Landauer Angle\n\n<a id=\"lemma-q07b-transcendence-of-the-landauer-phase-ratio\"></a>**Lemma Q.0.7b (Transcendence of the Landauer Phase Ratio).** *The ratio*\n$$\n\\alpha_L = \\frac{\\ln 2}{2\\pi}\n$$\n*is transcendental over $\\mathbb{Q}$. In particular, it is irrational.*\n\n*Proof.* Suppose for contradiction that $\\alpha_L$ is algebraic. Define\n$$\nb := -2i\\alpha_L.\n$$\nThen $b$ is algebraic. Since $\\alpha_L>0$, $b$ is non-real and therefore $b\\notin\\mathbb{Q}$. By the Gelfond–Schneider theorem (Gelfond 1934; Schneider 1935), if $a\\in\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\setminus\\{0,1\\}$ and $b\\in\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\setminus\\mathbb Q$, then every value of $a^b$ is transcendental. Taking $a=-1$ and using the branch $\\log(-1)=i\\pi$ gives one value\n$$\n(-1)^b\n=\n\\exp(b\\,i\\pi)\n=\n\\exp(2\\pi\\alpha_L)\n=\n\\exp(\\ln 2)\n=\n2.\n$$\nThis value is algebraic, contradicting Gelfond–Schneider. Hence $\\alpha_L$ is not algebraic, so it is transcendental and therefore irrational. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"remark-q07b1-scope-of-the-transcendence-statement\"></a>**Remark Q.0.7b.1: Scope of the Transcendence Statement.** The proof of Lemma Q.0.7b does not require algebraic independence of $\\pi$ and $\\ln 2$. It uses only the Gelfond–Schneider theorem and the identity $\\exp(2\\pi\\alpha_L)=2$. The subsequent equidistribution and three-gap results require only irrationality, but the stronger transcendence statement rules out every rational or algebraic phase-locking replacement of $\\alpha_L$.\n\n---\n\n### Q.0.7.3 Exact Matching for a Registered Irrational Phase Step\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q07c-exact-matching-for-the-registered-irrational-phase-step\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.7c (Exact Matching for the Registered Irrational Phase Step).** *Assume that each event in the declared history family is independently assigned the phase increment $\\ln2$, and assume Lemma Q.0.7b. Then the exact selected-character phase-matching condition*\n$$(N[\\phi_1] - N[\\phi_2]) \\cdot \\ln 2 = 2\\pi k \\qquad (k \\in \\mathbb{Z})$$\n\n*implies:*\n$$N[\\phi_1] = N[\\phi_2] \\quad \\text{and} \\quad k = 0$$\n\n*Proof.* Dividing by $2\\pi$:\n$$(N[\\phi_1] - N[\\phi_2]) \\alpha_L = k$$\n\nIf $N[\\phi_1] - N[\\phi_2] \\neq 0$, then $\\alpha_L = k/(N[\\phi_1] - N[\\phi_2]) \\in \\mathbb{Q}$, contradicting Lemma Q.0.7b. Hence $N[\\phi_1] = N[\\phi_2]$, and then $k = 0$. $\\square$\n\n**Interpretation.** On this registered phase-assignment branch, two paths with different integer event counts cannot have exactly equal phase modulo $2\\pi$. Theorem Q.0.2e supplies that phase assignment once the PU and reference coherent channels are identified.\n\n---\n\n### Q.0.7.4 Dense Cyclic Subgroup of $U(1)$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q07d-density-of-the-registered-phase-generator\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.7d (Density of the Registered Phase Generator).** *On the branch that assigns phase $N\\ln2$ to an integer registered event count $N$, define the generator*\n$$g_L := e^{i\\ln 2} \\in U(1)$$\n\n*The subgroup generated by integer powers of $g_L$:*\n$$G_L := \\{e^{iN\\ln 2} : N \\in \\mathbb{Z}\\} = \\{g_L^N : N \\in \\mathbb{Z}\\}$$\n\n*is dense in $U(1)$. Equivalently, the sequence $\\{N\\alpha_L \\bmod 1\\}_{N \\in \\mathbb{Z}}$ is dense (and equidistributed) in $[0,1)$.*\n\n*Proof.* By Lemma Q.0.7b, $\\alpha_L$ is irrational. The one-dimensional case of Kronecker's approximation theorem (Kronecker 1884) states that for irrational $\\alpha$, the set $\\{N\\alpha \\bmod 1 : N \\in \\mathbb{Z}\\}$ is dense in $\\mathbb{R}/\\mathbb{Z}$.\n\nApplying this to $\\alpha = \\alpha_L$ implies $\\{e^{2\\pi i N\\alpha_L}\\}_{N \\in \\mathbb{Z}} = \\{e^{iN\\ln 2}\\}_{N \\in \\mathbb{Z}}$ is dense in $U(1)$.\n\nMoreover, Weyl's equidistribution theorem (Weyl 1916) strengthens this: for irrational $\\alpha$, the sequence $\\{N\\alpha \\bmod 1\\}_{N=1}^{M}$ becomes uniformly distributed as $M \\to \\infty$:\n$$\\lim_{M \\to \\infty} \\frac{1}{M} \\#\\{1 \\leq N \\leq M : N\\alpha \\bmod 1 \\in [a,b)\\} = b - a$$\n\nfor any $0 \\leq a < b \\leq 1$. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q07d-closure-of-the-registered-dense-cyclic-subgroup\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.7d′ (Closure of the Registered Dense Cyclic Subgroup).** *The topological closure of the registered phase subgroup is the full circle group:*\n$$\\overline{G_L} = U(1)$$\n\n*Equivalently, for every target phase $e^{i\\theta} \\in U(1)$ and every tolerance $\\nu > 0$, there exists $N \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ such that:*\n$$|e^{iN\\ln 2} - e^{i\\theta}| < \\nu$$\n\n*Proof.* The closure of a dense subset equals the ambient space. Apply Theorem Q.0.7d. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q07d2-registered-phase-action-and-holonomy-completion\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.7d2 (Registered Phase-Action and Holonomy Completion).** Let\n$$\nG_L=\\{e^{iN\\ln2}:N\\in\\mathbb Z\\}\\subset U(1),\n$$\nwhich is dense by Theorem Q.0.7d, and let $(Y,d)$ be a complete metric space of finite-resolution response configurations. Suppose there is an isometric action\n$$\n\\rho_L:G_L\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{Isom}(Y)\n$$\nsuch that every orbit map is uniformly continuous when $G_L$ carries the inherited circle metric. Then there is a unique continuous isometric action\n$$\n\\overline\\rho:U(1)\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{Isom}(Y)\n$$\nextending $\\rho_L$. Every continuous $G_L$-invariant response functional is $\\overline\\rho(U(1))$-invariant. The same statement holds cellwise for a finite product cover. If a continuous $U(1)$ action is registered from the outset, the invariance conclusion follows directly from density and continuity, with no metric or isometry hypothesis.\n\nIf, in addition, $Y$ is a smooth manifold, $Z_1(Y)$ denotes its group of smooth singular integral $1$-cycles, $h:Z_1(Y)\\to U(1)$ is a homomorphism, and a closed real two-form $\\Omega$ satisfies, for every smooth singular integral $2$-chain $C$,\n$$\n\\left[\\frac{\\Omega}{2\\pi}\\right]\n\\in\n\\operatorname{im}\\!\\left(H^2(Y;\\mathbb Z)\\to H^2_{\\mathrm{dR}}(Y;\\mathbb R)\\right),\n\\qquad\nh(\\partial C)=\\exp\\!\\left(i\\int_C\\Omega\\right),\n\\tag{Q.0.7d2.1}\n$$\nthen $(h,\\Omega)$ is a degree-two differential character. It determines, up to connection-preserving isomorphism, a principal $U(1)$ bundle with connection of curvature $\\Omega$ and holonomy $h$. The physical charge convention is $\\Omega=qF$.\n\n*Proof.* For $z\\in U(1)$ choose $g_n\\in G_L$ with $g_n\\to z$. Uniform continuity makes $\\rho_L(g_n)y$ Cauchy; completeness supplies a limit independent of the approximating sequence. Define $\\overline\\rho(z)y$ by that limit. Passing to limits proves the isometry and group laws, while\n$$\nd(\\overline\\rho(z_n)y_n,\\overline\\rho(z)y)\n\\le d(y_n,y)+d(\\overline\\rho(z_n)y,\\overline\\rho(z)y)\n$$\ngives joint continuity. Density gives uniqueness and extends invariance. If the $U(1)$ action is registered and continuous, then for continuous $G_L$-invariant $Z$ and $g_m\\to z$ in $G_L$, continuity of the action and of $Z$ give $Z(z\\cdot y)=\\lim_mZ(g_m\\cdot y)=Z(y)$. The final assertion is the differential-character classification of principal $U(1)$ bundles with connection under the integral-period and boundary identities (Q.0.7d2.1). ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q07d3-local-gauge-and-noether-completion-on-the-registered-branch\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.7d3 (Local Gauge and Noether Completion on the Registered Branch).** Assume the cellwise completed actions and differential characters are compatible under refinement and descent, and the continuum branch has the differentiable minimally coupled action and boundary hypotheses of Theorem G.6b. Then\n$$\n\\phi\\mapsto e^{iq\\alpha(x)}\\phi,\n\\qquad\nA_\\mu\\mapsto A_\\mu-\\partial_\\mu\\alpha,\n$$\nand on shell\n$$\n\\nabla_\\mu J^\\mu=0,\n\\qquad\nJ^\\mu=-iq\\bigl(\\phi^*D^\\mu\\phi-(D^\\mu\\phi)^*\\phi\\bigr),\n$$\nin the sign convention of Theorem G.6b.\n\n*Proof.* Refinement compatibility and differential-character descent produce one local $U(1)$ bundle with connection. Varying the differentiable invariant action by compactly supported $\\alpha$ and integrating by parts gives the result. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q07d4-explicit-refining-event-character-gauge-and-ward-model\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.7d4 (Explicit Refining Event-Character Gauge and Ward Model).** Let $K$ be a finite oriented cell complex. Assign $U_e\\in U(1)$ to every oriented edge with $U_{\\bar e}=U_e^{-1}$ and $\\phi_v\\in\\mathbb C$ to every vertex. For $z_v=e^{i\\alpha_v}$ define\n$$\n\\phi_v\\longmapsto z_v\\phi_v,\n\\qquad\nU_e\\longmapsto z_{t(e)}U_ez_{s(e)}^{-1}.\n\\tag{Q.0.7d4.1}\n$$\nFor positive weights $w_e,\\beta_f$ and any real $C^1$ function $V$ on $[0,\\infty)$, the finite action\n$$\nS_K\n=\\sum_e w_e|\\phi_{t(e)}-U_e\\phi_{s(e)}|^2\n+\\sum_f\\beta_f\\bigl(1-\\operatorname{Re}\\operatorname{Hol}_{\\partial f}(U)\\bigr)\n+\\sum_vV(|\\phi_v|^2)\n\\tag{Q.0.7d4.2}\n$$\nis invariant under (Q.0.7d4.1). If a refinement replaces $e$ by the oriented path $e_1\\cdots e_r$, impose\n$$\nU_e=U_{e_r}\\cdots U_{e_1}.\n\\tag{Q.0.7d4.3}\n$$\nThen every coarse-loop holonomy equals its refined-loop holonomy, and the rule is compatible with successive refinements.\n\nSuppose the admitted oriented event loops carry an additive homological count\n$$\nN:H_1(K;\\mathbb Z)\\longrightarrow\\mathbb Z.\n$$\nBy the universal-coefficient isomorphism, choose an integral cellular cocycle $n\\in Z^1(K;\\mathbb Z)$ representing $N$. The background assignment\n$$\nU_e^{(N)}:=e^{i n(e)\\ln2}\n$$\nthen gives, simultaneously for every admitted loop $\\gamma$,\n$$\n\\operatorname{Hol}_\\gamma(U^{(N)})=e^{iN([\\gamma])\\ln2}.\n\\tag{Q.0.7d4.4}\n$$\nGive every edge a unit parameter interval. On any subdivision, assign to a child interval $I$ the phase $\\exp(i n(e)|I|\\ln2)$, with the inherited orientation. Products over partitions recover $U_e^{(N)}$, and partition refinement is associative, so the whole event character has an explicit refinement-compatible connection representative.\n\nVariation of the vertex phase gives the oriented edge current\n$$\nJ_e:=2w_e\\operatorname{Im}\n\\bigl(\\overline{\\phi_{t(e)}}U_e\\phi_{s(e)}\\bigr)\n$$\nand define $(\\operatorname{div}J)_v:=\\sum_{t(e)=v}J_e-\\sum_{s(e)=v}J_e$. The matter Euler--Lagrange equation then gives the on-shell interior identity\n$$\n(\\operatorname{div}J)_v=0.\n\\tag{Q.0.7d4.5}\n$$\nSumming over a vertex region $R$ gives the exact boundary Ward balance\n$$\n\\sum_{v\\in R}(\\operatorname{div}J)_v\n=\\sum_{e\\in\\partial R}\\operatorname{sgn}_R(e)J_e.\n\\tag{Q.0.7d4.6}\n$$\nThe finite-dimensional reference measure $\\prod_vd^2\\phi_v\\prod_edU_e$, with normalized Haar measure $dU_e$, is invariant under (Q.0.7d4.1); hence this bosonic finite realization has unit Jacobian and zero gauge anomaly. Fermionic determinant phases require a separate anomaly calculation.\n\n*Proof.* Each covariant edge difference in (Q.0.7d4.2) is multiplied by $z_{t(e)}$, face holonomies are conjugated trivially in $U(1)$, and the potential depends only on $|\\phi_v|$, proving invariance. Associativity of group multiplication proves refinement functoriality and holonomy preservation. Evaluation of the cocycle on a cycle is $N([\\gamma])$, proving (Q.0.7d4.4) for all admitted loops at once; additivity of interval lengths proves the subdivision statement. Differentiating the $C^1$ action with respect to $\\alpha_v$ gives (Q.0.7d4.5); cancellation of oppositely oriented interior edges gives (Q.0.7d4.6). Lebesgue rotations and Haar translations preserve the finite measure, so its change-of-variables Jacobian is one. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q07d5-lattice-holonomy-classification-of-event-ledgers\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.7d5 (Lattice-Holonomy Classification of Event Ledgers).** Keep the finite oriented cell complex $K$ and the edge variables of Theorem Q.0.7d4. Fix one orientation of each edge, let $C_1(K)$ be the free abelian group of integral $1$-chains and $Z_1(K)=\\ker\\partial_1$ its cycle group, and for an edge assignment $U$ and a cycle $z=\\sum_ez_ee$ put $\\operatorname{Hol}_z(U):=\\prod_eU_e^{z_e}$. This is a homomorphism $Z_1(K)\\to U(1)$ that agrees with the loop holonomy of Theorem Q.0.7d4 on closed edge paths. Let $\\Gamma$ be a set of admitted oriented closed edge paths with cycles $[\\gamma]\\in Z_1(K)$, and let $N:\\Gamma\\to\\mathbb Z$ be an event ledger. Then:\n\n1. Some edge assignment satisfies\n   $$\n   \\operatorname{Hol}_\\gamma(U)=e^{iN(\\gamma)\\ln2}\n   \\qquad(\\gamma\\in\\Gamma)\n   \\tag{Q.0.7d5.1}\n   $$\n   if and only if $N$ is additive on cycle relations: whenever $\\sum_jc_j[\\gamma_j]=0$ in $Z_1(K)$ with $c_j\\in\\mathbb Z$ and $\\gamma_j\\in\\Gamma$, also $\\sum_jc_jN(\\gamma_j)=0$.\n2. Two edge assignments with equal holonomy on all of $Z_1(K)$ differ by a gauge transformation (Q.0.7d4.1). Hence, when the admitted cycles generate $Z_1(K)$, an additive ledger fixes its realizing connection up to gauge.\n3. Let $S$ be the subgroup generated by the admitted cycles and $\\tilde N$ the additive extension of $N$ to $S$. For every face $f$ with $\\partial f\\in S$, every realizing connection has $\\operatorname{Hol}_{\\partial f}(U)=e^{i\\tilde N(\\partial f)\\ln2}$, which equals $1$ exactly when $\\tilde N(\\partial f)=0$. When all face boundaries lie in $S$, the realizing connections are flat exactly for ledgers with $\\tilde N(\\partial f)=0$ on every face, the homological situation of Theorem Q.0.7d4. A nonhomological additive ledger, one with $\\tilde N(\\partial f)\\ne0$ for some face with $\\partial f\\in S$, is realized only by curved connections, and their plaquette term in (Q.0.7d4.2) is $\\beta_f\\bigl(1-\\cos(\\tilde N(\\partial f)\\ln2)\\bigr)>0$ on every such face.\n4. No lattice $U(1)$ connection represents a ledger with $N(\\gamma)+N(\\bar\\gamma)\\ne0$ for an admitted loop and its reversal, in particular a forward event count with $N(\\gamma)\\ge1$ and $N(\\bar\\gamma)\\ge1$, or with $N(\\gamma^k)\\ne kN(\\gamma)$ for an admitted power.\n\nGauge invariance, the refinement rule (Q.0.7d4.3), the Ward identities (Q.0.7d4.5)--(Q.0.7d4.6) and the unit Jacobian of Theorem Q.0.7d4 hold for every realizing connection.\n\n*Proof.* $\\operatorname{Hol}$ is a homomorphism on $Z_1(K)$ because it is a product of integer powers of commuting unit numbers, and orientation reversal and concatenation of closed edge paths correspond to negation and addition of cycles. If $U$ satisfies (Q.0.7d5.1) and $\\sum_jc_j[\\gamma_j]=0$, then $\\exp(i\\ln2\\sum_jc_jN(\\gamma_j))=\\operatorname{Hol}_0(U)=1$, so $\\ln2\\sum_jc_jN(\\gamma_j)\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z$, and irrationality of $\\ln2/(2\\pi)$ (Lemma Q.0.7b) forces $\\sum_jc_jN(\\gamma_j)=0$. Conversely, additivity makes $\\chi(\\sum_jc_j[\\gamma_j]):=\\exp(i\\ln2\\sum_jc_jN(\\gamma_j))$ a well-defined homomorphism $S\\to U(1)$. By the elementary-divisor theorem there are a basis $e_1,\\ldots,e_r$ of the free group $C_1(K)$ and integers $d_1,\\ldots,d_s\\ge1$ such that $d_1e_1,\\ldots,d_se_s$ is a basis of $S$; choosing $\\chi(e_i)$ as a $d_i$-th root of $\\chi(d_ie_i)$ for $i\\le s$ and $\\chi(e_i)=1$ for $i>s$ extends $\\chi$ to $C_1(K)$, and $U_e:=\\chi(e)$, $U_{\\bar e}:=U_e^{-1}$ satisfies (Q.0.7d5.1). This proves item 1.\n\nIf $U$ and $U'$ have equal holonomy on $Z_1(K)$, the edge ratios $V_e:=U'_eU_e^{-1}$ define a homomorphism $C_1(K)\\to U(1)$ that is trivial on $Z_1(K)=\\ker\\partial_1$, so $V=\\psi\\circ\\partial_1$ for a homomorphism $\\psi$ on the subgroup $\\partial_1C_1(K)$ of the free group $C_0(K)$. The same elementary-divisor extension extends $\\psi$ to $C_0(K)$, and with $z_v:=\\psi(v)$ one gets $V_e=\\psi(t(e)-s(e))=z_{t(e)}z_{s(e)}^{-1}$, which is (Q.0.7d4.1). This proves item 2.\n\nFor item 3, $\\operatorname{Hol}_{\\partial f}(U)=\\chi(\\partial f)=e^{i\\tilde N(\\partial f)\\ln2}$ for every realizing $U$, which equals $1$ exactly when $\\tilde N(\\partial f)=0$ by Lemma Q.0.7b, and $\\operatorname{Re}e^{i\\theta}=\\cos\\theta$ gives the plaquette value. Item 4 is item 1 applied to the relations $[\\gamma]+[\\bar\\gamma]=0$ and $[\\gamma^k]-k[\\gamma]=0$. The proofs of gauge invariance, holonomy refinement, current variation, boundary cancellation and measure invariance in Theorem Q.0.7d4 use only the edge variables and group multiplication, and never flatness. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q07d6-finite-vector-like-fermionic-matter-on-the-event-character-lattice\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.7d6 (Finite Vector-Like Fermionic Matter on the Event-Character Lattice).** On $K$ attach to each vertex $v$ Grassmann generators $\\psi_{v,\\alpha},\\bar\\psi_{v,\\alpha}$, $\\alpha=1,\\ldots,r$, and fix complex $r\\times r$ matrices $M_v$, $A_e$ and $B_e$. With one fixed orientation per edge, define for every edge assignment $U$ the finite Dirac-type matrix on $\\mathbb C^{K_0}\\otimes\\mathbb C^r$ and the fermionic action\n$$\nD(U):=\\sum_vE_{vv}\\otimes M_v\n+\\sum_e\\bigl(U_eE_{t(e)s(e)}\\otimes A_e+U_e^{-1}E_{s(e)t(e)}\\otimes B_e\\bigr),\n\\qquad\nS_F(U):=\\bar\\psi D(U)\\psi,\n\\tag{Q.0.7d6.1}\n$$\nand extend (Q.0.7d4.1) by $\\psi_v\\mapsto z_v\\psi_v$ and $\\bar\\psi_v\\mapsto z_v^{-1}\\bar\\psi_v$. Then:\n\n1. $D(U^z)=(Z\\otimes I_r)D(U)(Z\\otimes I_r)^{-1}$ with $Z=\\operatorname{diag}(z_v)$, so $S_F$ is gauge invariant and the fermionic partition function\n   $$\n   Z_F(U):=\\int\\prod_{v,\\alpha}d\\bar\\psi_{v,\\alpha}d\\psi_{v,\\alpha}\\,e^{-S_F(U)}=\\det D(U)\n   $$\n   satisfies $Z_F(U^z)=Z_F(U)$.\n2. The Berezin integral is invariant under the vertex phase rotations, which multiply the top Grassmann monomial by $\\prod_v(z_v^{-1}z_v)^r=1$. This vector-like realization therefore has unit Jacobian and zero gauge anomaly, jointly with the bosonic sector of Theorem Q.0.7d4.\n3. With the edge current\n   $$\n   J_e^F:=i\\bigl(\\bar\\psi_{s(e)}U_e^{-1}B_e\\psi_{t(e)}-\\bar\\psi_{t(e)}U_eA_e\\psi_{s(e)}\\bigr)\n   \\tag{Q.0.7d6.2}\n   $$\n   and $(\\operatorname{div}J^F)_v$ formed as in Theorem Q.0.7d4, every element $\\mathcal O$ of the Grassmann algebra satisfies the Schwinger--Dyson Ward identity\n   $$\n   \\int\\prod_{u,\\alpha}d\\bar\\psi_{u,\\alpha}d\\psi_{u,\\alpha}\\;e^{-S_F(U)}\\Bigl[(\\operatorname{div}J^F)_v\\,\\mathcal O-\\delta_v\\mathcal O\\Bigr]=0,\n   \\tag{Q.0.7d6.3}\n   $$\n   where $\\delta_v\\mathcal O$ is the derivative at $\\alpha=0$ of $\\mathcal O$ under $\\psi_v\\mapsto e^{i\\alpha}\\psi_v$, $\\bar\\psi_v\\mapsto e^{-i\\alpha}\\bar\\psi_v$. In particular $\\langle(\\operatorname{div}J^F)_v\\rangle_U=0$ whenever $\\det D(U)\\ne0$, and the cancellation identity (Q.0.7d4.6) turns this into vanishing expected fermionic flux through the boundary of every vertex region.\n\n*Proof.* The edge $e$ contributes the block $U_eA_e$ at $(t(e),s(e))$ and $U_e^{-1}B_e$ at $(s(e),t(e))$; conjugation by $Z\\otimes I_r$ multiplies them by $z_{t(e)}z_{s(e)}^{-1}$ and $z_{s(e)}z_{t(e)}^{-1}$, exactly as $U\\mapsto U^z$ does, and fixes the diagonal blocks. Similarity preserves determinants, and the Gaussian Berezin integral with the displayed measure equals $\\det D(U)$. The Berezin integral is a fixed multiple of the linear functional that extracts the coefficient of the top monomial $\\prod_{v,\\alpha}\\bar\\psi_{v,\\alpha}\\psi_{v,\\alpha}$. A vertex rotation $T_\\alpha$ is an algebra automorphism that multiplies each generator by a phase and the top monomial by $\\prod_v(e^{-i\\alpha_v}e^{i\\alpha_v})^r=1$, so $\\int T_\\alpha X=\\int X$ for every $X$. For $X=e^{-S_F}\\mathcal O$ one has $T_\\alpha X=e^{-T_\\alpha S_F}T_\\alpha\\mathcal O$; since $T_\\alpha S_F$ is even it commutes with its $\\alpha$-derivative, and $\\partial_{\\alpha_v}e^{-T_\\alpha S_F}=-(\\partial_{\\alpha_v}T_\\alpha S_F)e^{-T_\\alpha S_F}$. Differentiating $\\sum_{u,w}e^{-i\\alpha_u}\\bar\\psi_uD_{uw}e^{i\\alpha_w}\\psi_w$ at $\\alpha=0$ cancels the diagonal blocks and gives $\\partial_{\\alpha_v}T_\\alpha S_F|_{\\alpha=0}=\\sum_{t(e)=v}J_e^F-\\sum_{s(e)=v}J_e^F=(\\operatorname{div}J^F)_v$. Differentiating $\\int T_\\alpha X=\\int X$ at $\\alpha=0$ gives (Q.0.7d6.3). For $\\mathcal O=1$, $\\delta_v\\mathcal O=0$, and division by $Z_F(U)=\\det D(U)\\ne0$ gives the normalized statement; summation over a vertex region cancels interior edges as in (Q.0.7d4.6). ∎\n\n<a id=\"definition-q07d7-static-chiral-common-input-model-and-typed-outputs\"></a>**Definition Q.0.7d7 (Static Chiral Common-Input Model and Typed Outputs).** Fix the nonempty static curved torus family, trivial line bundle, periodic spin structure, ten stated left-handed charges, event/connection calibration and complete finite open/driven source constructed below. At background $A$ the mathematical state has the fixed derived section $L_{5,A}=Z_5(A)W_A$, $Z_5(0)=1$; independent scalar rescalings are not extra states. Retain separately the event/geometry record $E$, fixed mathematical section $M$, normalized field law $N$, positive physical source $P$, historical regulator/comparison quantities $H$ and complete physical ledger $S$. The source realizes $N$ through the proved normalization/extraction maps. Its parity denominator is not $Z_5$ or a vacuum determinant.\n\nThe Q03 finite logarithmic apparatus is the supporting source submodule reproduced in Theorem M.6.6c: the same native constructor, $m=2$ setting, raw source law, logarithmic gap and full inventory. Only that exact apparatus submodule is consumed; no M06-wide observer or gravity claim is a premise. New signed-loop controls and all added services are specified and priced below.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q07d8-chiral-event-character-measure-and-continuum-physical-realization\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.7d8 (Chiral Event-Character Measure and Continuum Physical Realization).** Definition Q.0.7d7 constructs genuinely chiral matter, an explicit phase-fixed regulator and nonzero relative continuum measure section, and a response-faithful positive-source realization of the normalized continuum field law, with the same minimally coupled physical Hamiltonian, event/charge/clock units, gauge/refinement/composition maps and connected internal-region Ward exporters. Every quantifier below retains its declared source/test/gauge/region domain.\n\n*Proof and complete source construction.*\n\n**Controlling conventions.** The exact tree, base-toron, minor-sign, inverse and comparison-scope conventions stated below apply to every component of this proof. The projector identity is $P_M=P_-+\\gamma_5 D_M/2$. In every connected insertion, apply one global fermionic Euclidean time ordering to all outer and insertion fields before smearing; $R[O\\,I(a)]$ is never a product of separately time-ordered integrated matrices. The abort bound is $P(\\text{return AND inaccurate})\\le\\alpha$, not a conditional-on-return bound. All original source/history and physical costs remain.\n\n## 1. Exact original interface and declared base\n\nQ.0.7d4 uses a finite oriented cell complex $K$ with $U_{\\mathrm{reverse}}=U_{\\mathrm{edge}}^{-1}$ and, in its flat branch, an integer character on $H_1(K;\\mathbb{Z})$. Q.0.7d5 permits a character on the cycle group $Z_1(K)$, including curved face-count holonomies. Neither requires the entire apparatus, its fuel, or its shadows to recur. Here $K$ is explicitly the controller's registered three-vertex base, not the Q03 motor graph or the complete source-state graph.\n\nTake vertices $a,b,c$ and oriented edges $e_0:a\\to b$, $e_1:b\\to c$, $e_2:c\\to a$, plus reversals. Set $n(e_0)=1$, $n(\\text{reverse } e_0)=-1$, and $n=0$ on the other edges. A base path $w=e_1\\dots e_l$ is admitted only if endpoints match and $l\\le K_{\\max}$, a positive integer fixed at assembly. Its signed count is $N(w)=\\sum n(e_i)$. A closed base path returns its base-vertex label. It does not return its consumed preparation/record registers. The full source projection to this base forgets no records in the invoice or response law; it is a declared observable quotient.\n\nThe same star alone cannot do this: it is a tree, with zero cycle group. Every local antisymmetric edge form has zero integral on every closed star walk, and every $U(1)$ edge assignment there is pure gauge. Q03's $\\ln 2$ is a raw initial-prior/experiment ratio, not a cycle affinity of symmetric star rates. This construction does not identify the triangle with that star.\n\n## 2. Signed setup instruments and raw joint likelihood\n\nUse only the adopted $m=2$ star primitive, with its existing $\\kappa=1/\\tau_A$, horizon $H=\\tau_A$, four installed motor levels, second-jump stop, finite shadow cells, and full preparation/cost records. All potential stock, including the unused third leaf, remains charged. Let $F$ start at center and $R$ start uniformly at leaves 1,2. On a matched one-jump leaf/time record $y=(j,t)$, write $Jy$ for its reversed trajectory. The actual raw densities obey\n\n$$\np_F(y)=2 p_R(J y).\n$$\n\nNo exact continuous timestamps are retained. The same ratio holds for the retained leaf/count-one records after integrating times, and for the count-one family $A$. Specifically $p_F(A)=2e^{-1}(1-e^{-1})$ and $p_R(A)=e^{-1}(1-e^{-1})$.\n\nFor a positive marked-edge occurrence use the actual $F$ setup and comparator $R$; for a negative occurrence use actual $R$ and comparator $F$. These are different explicitly supplied preparations. Define $p_+(y)=p_F(y)$, $q_+(y)=p_R(Jy)$, and $p_-(y)=p_R(y)$, $q_-(y)=p_F(Jy)$. Hence $p_{\\sigma}/q_{\\sigma}=2^{\\sigma}$. The sign is a registered reversal of the complete comparison experiment, not an unsupported reversal of a positive event count.\n\nAllocate independent fresh packages to all marked occurrences of the predetermined word. Let $A_{w}$ be the event that every such package has count one. On its full retained motor record tuple y, product independence gives\n\n$$\nP_w(y)/Q_w(J_w y)=2^{N(w)},\\qquad L_w=\\log(P_w/Q_w)=N(w)\\ln 2.\n\\tag{1}\n$$\n\n$J_{w}$ reverses each trajectory and, if the comparator word is reversed, also reorders the independent records. Its Jacobian is one. Integration over the retained leaf records preserves (1). Actual readiness probability is $p_F(A)^{n_+}p_R(A)^{n_-}>0$ for finite word; the empty product is one. Every failed setup, exhausted second jump, timeout, preparation label and shadow remains in the actual law. No unbounded retry is allowed.\n\nSetup and all readiness/routing occur BEFORE accepting unknown $Q$/reference. Conditional-on-ready setup laws must not replace the raw weights in (1): their separately normalized likelihood ratio would be one. Equation (1) is the registered motor-event ledger only. Full supplier likelihoods and thermodynamic reset entropies are not asserted to equal it. Retained side information is neither erased nor ignored to claim such a stronger identity.\n\n## 3. Paid positive-Hamiltonian inverse controls\n\nKeep exactly the adopted $m=2$ idle gap $h=\\Delta|0\\rangle\\langle 0|$, $\\Delta=(\\hbar/\\tau_A)\\ln 2$, and $F(t)=\\exp(-iht/\\hbar)$. Put $\\tau=\\delta=\\tau_A$. The hold $W=F(\\tau)=\\operatorname{diag}(e^{-iL},1)$, $L=\\ln 2$.\n\nThe following finite-duration control lemma is a new explicitly installed port using the adopted M10/Q03 driven-source type. For each target $T$ in $\\{I,X\\}$, choose the fixed two-by-two Hermitian spectral logarithm $K_{T}$ of $F(\\delta)^*T$ with $\\exp(-iK_T)=F(\\delta)^*T$ and $0\\le K_T<2\\pi I$. Supply\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_T(t)&=h+(\\hbar/\\delta)g(t)F(t)K_TF(t)^{*}, \\\\\ng(t)&=1-\\cos(2\\pi t/\\delta),\\quad 0\\le t\\le \\delta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts exact endpoint is T, by direct interaction-picture integration. $H_{T}$ is positive, its peak norm is at most Delta+4pi hbar/delta, and g vanishes at endpoints. Thus these are actual finite-duration identity-buffer and X operations, not zero-time gates, negative Hamiltonians, or unpriced gap cancellations. The complete waveform and positive service price are part of this new port.\n\nImplement every forward marked edge by $T_{I}$, then W, then $T_{I}$; its full unitary is W. Implement every reversed marked edge by $T_{X}$, then W, then $T_{X}$; its full unitary is\n\n$$\nW_-=X W X=\\operatorname{diag}(1,e^{-iL}).\n$$\n\nEach edge block lasts $3\\tau$. Forward and reverse use the SAME positive static gap. Their relative channels are $U_+=\\operatorname{diag}(1,e^{iL})$ and $U_-=U_+^*$. The full forward unitary is $e^{-iL}U_+$; the full reverse unitary is $U_-$. The differing common factors are not discarded from the source path record.\n\nAn unmarked edge uses three $T_{I}$ pulses, also lasting $3\\tau$, and has full endpoint $I$. The same identity blocks pad short words to $K_{\\max}$ blocks. All pulse work obeys the exact operator identity $W_{\\mathrm{drive}}=U^*hU-h$. An $X$ pulse has work norm $\\le\\Delta$, an identity pulse has zero net idle-energy work. The complete forward, reverse and unmarked blocks commute with $h$ at their endpoints and therefore have zero net idle-energy work over the entire block, while retaining finite intermediate work exchange and positive control fees/peak reserves. This is an ideal supplied driver, not a derivation of an autonomous recoil-free apparatus.\n\n## 4. Exact full-reference word channel and independently specified readout\n\nAt ready, accept arbitrary $\\rho_{\\mathrm{QR}}$ independently of every complete prepared supplier state conditional on the fixed word and retained setup. Supplier instruments are those of the declared native driven-source boundary. The deterministic word pulse unitaries act on Q; all setup shadows and subsequent independent service histories act as identity on Q and R. The complete finite instrument therefore factors through the exact prescribed Q unitary, just as in the adopted Q03 hold source. Extra physical driver outputs that distinguish arms are not silently included; admitting them would require a new factorization proof.\n\nThe ordered product is\n\n$$\nW_w=e^{-i n_+(w)L} \\operatorname{diag}(1,e^{i N(w)L}).\n\\tag{2}\n$$\n\nThus $\\operatorname{Ad}(W_w)\\otimes\\mathrm{Id}_R$ equals $\\operatorname{Ad}[\\operatorname{diag}(1,e^{iL_w})]\\otimes\\mathrm{Id}_R$ for all references, using the independent raw ledger (1). Its full common factor is explicitly retained. Backtracking $e_0,\\text{reverse }e_0$ gives $W_-W_+=e^{-iL}I$, so it is identity as a channel while having the recorded nonzero common source phase. A claim of identity on the entire apparatus or an unrecorded absolute phase would be false.\n\nFor diagnostics, use the adopted finite-duration preparation ending exactly at word entrance, giving $|+x\\rangle$. At the end use the independently specified Q03 $x$ or $y$ pointer instrument: its actual Kraus operators are $F(\\delta)\\Pi_{b\\pm}$, with a later pointer-copy slot. There is no idle gap between the word endpoint and detector entrance. Pointer effects commute with all subsequent $Q$ evolution. Hence\n\n $$\n p_{x+}=(1+\\cos[N(w)\\ln 2])/2,\\quad p_{y+}=(1+\\sin[N(w)\\ln 2])/2.\n \\tag{3}\n $$\n\nNo phase is inferred from a time-zero measurement fiction. These normalized records recover the character, and distinct integer counts give distinct pairs by the already proved irrationality of ln2/(2pi). Words with the same N have the same declared probe response; their full retained histories remain distinguishable. No response-faithfulness claim on unquotiented words is made.\n\n## 5. Connection, frames, and flat versus curved bases\n\nDefine $U_e=\\exp(i\\,n(e)L)$. For every path, the relative probe channel is precisely the ordered connection transport. For a cycle $z$ its character is $\\operatorname{Hol}_z(U)=\\exp(i\\,N(z)L)$; additivity on all integer cycle relations is immediate from the cochain $n$. Orientation reversal negates $N$ and inverts this holonomy. Concatenation adds $N$. The nonzero triangle $a\\to b\\to c\\to a$ has $N=1$ and holonomy $e^{iL}$.\n\nUnder a vertex frame convention $z_{v}$ in $U(1)$, use $E_v=\\operatorname{diag}(1,z_v)$ on the probe reference. Then $E_t\\operatorname{diag}(1,U_e)E_s^*=\\operatorname{diag}(1,z_tU_ez_s^{-1})$. Along a path the intermediate frame factors cancel, and on a closed loop the scalar holonomy is unchanged. This is covariance of the registered source-to-connection dictionary under changes of reference coordinates, not a claim that a finite device executes every possible real-valued gauge command. Physical frame controls, if requested, would be a new finite menu with separately priced pulses/readout references.\n\nTwo DIFFERENT complexes are available:\n- On the unfilled triangle graph, $H_1=\\mathbb{Z}$. The count is a homological winding character, and there are no two-faces on which to assert nonzero curvature.\n- If a two-cell filling the triangle is part of the registered base, $H_{1}=0$ but $Z_{1}$ still contains its boundary. Then $dn$ evaluates to 1 on that face, the same connection has nontrivial plaquette holonomy $e^{iL}$, and $N$ does not descend to $H_{1}$. It is the curved Q.0.7d5 branch. The scalar action's plaquette term is exactly $\\beta[1-\\cos L]>0$.\n\nThe last expression is the value of the original finite scalar gauge action for this connection, not a new assertion that source tariffs or measured heat equal that action. The finite bosonic/vector-like Ward identities in Q.0.7d4/d6 can consume this very connection; they do not thereby become a chiral regulator or continuum physical realization.\n\n## 6. Fixed subdivision and its paid clock change\n\nFor any nominated finite partition $\\tau=\\sum\\tau_j$ with every $\\tau_{j}>0$, split a marked hold into $F(\\tau_j)$. Relative phases are $\\exp(\\pm i\\Delta\\tau_j/\\hbar)$, whose product is $\\exp(\\pm iL)$. To remain in the same laboratory endpoint frame, each child can use its own two $T_{I}$ or $T_{X}$ pulses of duration $\\delta$; their products have exactly the same full edge unitary as above, including the forward common phase. Adjacent inverse-frame $X$ factors cancel algebraically; their paid finite-duration pulses are still present physically.\n\nThe refined edge takes tau+2r $\\delta$ instead of tau+2delta and has2(r−1) additional pulse services, clock duration and records. A descriptor reserves these before assembly. This is exact phase/refinement consistency, not equality of physical traversal durations or unbounded refinement at fixed inventory. The fractional child phases are subdivision data of ONE registered event; no fractional raw motor event or fractional readiness probability is invented.\n\n## 7. Complete finite source/cost reservation\n\nFor fixed $K_{\\max}$ and nominated subdivisions, own $K_{\\max}$ complete adopted Q03 trial-package inventories, each including unused $m=2$ leaf 3 stock, its 32-cell record, four motor levels, eight reservoir/receipt levels, eight quantum levels and any nominated oscillator module. Only the signed marked occurrences are activated; all unmarked/padding packages remain charged. Reuse of the existing native star law is literal; the bounded loop composition, reversed readiness use, and $T_I/T_X$ ports are additional operations that enter as explicit source premises. The six-trial Q03 descriptor is not claimed unchanged.\n\nA conservative invoice can purchase $K_{\\max}$ FULL adopted Q03 descriptor inventories, not merely those trial packages, and then retain every unused component. Let $C_{\\mathrm{Q03,ready}}(N)$, $S_{\\mathrm{Q03}}(N)$ and $E_{\\mathrm{Q03}}(N)$ denote the exact amended inventory/stock/energy bounds from the adopted source. Pay $K_{\\max}$ times these full ready/stock/energy bounds. Running only the stated raw primitive in a copy is a new restricted command composition; no old output qualification is borrowed.\n\nAdditionally reserve one probe qubit, three degenerate detector/record qubits, two fixed $2\\times 2$ pulse logarithm ports, a $K_{\\max}$-entry 3-bit word register, $K_{\\max}$ fresh 2-bit base-vertex shadows, a 2-bit initial vertex and a finite step/ready/failure controller. Let $b=\\lceil\\log_2(K_{\\max}+1)\\rceil+8$. A conservative total delivered lookup has $2^{b+4}$ rows of $b$ bits, with $K_{\\max}+2$ fresh $b$-bit output banks. It validates incidence, signed edge code, cap, readiness and protected pulse phase; invalid input produces retained INVALID and only dummies, no unknown input. Extra bit stock\n\n$$\nB_{\\mathrm{extra}}=5K_{\\max}+2+b\\cdot 2^{b+4}+(K_{\\max}+2)b\n$$\n\nis sufficient for those registers, shadows and the table; modes/signs are in the fixed word codes and two-bit source labels already paid. Lookup gates use the same supplied finite reversible/involution service with fresh destinations; delivery of the actual native lookup apparatus is included, not inferred from passive ROM syntax.\n\nWithout subdivision the fixed active schedule is $2K_{\\max}$ setup/routing slots, one diagnostic-preparation-or-input-routing slot, $3K_{\\max}$ word slots, one detector slot and one copy slot: $T=(5K_{\\max}+3)\\tau$. All unused/failure slots are paid dummies. On failure no unknown input is accepted; the apparatus retains its complete finite record. The controller updates the registered base label in each fixed block, independently of $Q$. Choose retention deadline $T_{\\mathrm{ret}}=2T+512\\tau$, and pay every imported and added stock through it; extend the adopted source's holding tariff rather than stopping its charge at its old deadline.\n\nThere are at most $5K_{\\max}+3$ time/control bundles, at most $2K_{\\max}$ actual collisions, $K_{\\max}$ collision-readiness services, and one fresh detector package for the one final diagnostic (or an unused reserved package in arbitrary-input mode). The full copied Q03 inventories already over-reserve detector/qubit stock; paying the additional probe/detector separately is conservative. Let all extra physical levels, cells, supplied matrix entries and services have the same positive tariff types $r_{\\mathrm{level}},r_{\\mathrm{bit}},r_{\\mathrm{mat}},r_{\\mathrm{svc}},r_{\\mathrm{hold}}$ as Q03. An explicit upper resource reservation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC_{\\mathrm{extra}}&=8 r_{\\mathrm{level}}+B_{\\mathrm{extra}} r_{\\mathrm{bit}}+8 r_{\\mathrm{mat}}+(8K_{\\max}+4)r_{\\mathrm{svc}} \\\\\n&\\quad +r_{\\mathrm{hold}}[8+B_{\\mathrm{extra}}+8]T_{\\mathrm{ret}}, \\\\\nC_{\\mathrm{total}}&=K_{\\max} C_{\\mathrm{Q03,ready}}(N)+C_{\\mathrm{extra}} \\\\\n&\\quad +r_{\\mathrm{hold}} K_{\\max} S_{\\mathrm{Q03}}(N)T_{\\mathrm{ret}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe 8 local levels are four added qubits; the 8 matrix entries are $K_I,K_X$. Counting an already supplied copy twice is an intentional over-reservation, not a missing charge. All apparatus bits/pointers are degenerate at idle by the adopted convention, with positive physical readiness/retention prices. Controller pulse peaks are separately at most $2\\pi E_{q}$. A sufficient extra energy reservation is $\\Delta+4\\pi E_q+2\\pi E_q+(2K_{\\max}+2)\\Delta$ plus the resistor-clock heat $I_{\\mathrm{el}}^{2}R_{\\mathrm{el}}T$. Pulse work exchanges need no arm-measuring energy record; their signed Heisenberg work observables and positive reserve are retained. Imported source energy/receipts remain in the $K_{\\max}E_{\\mathrm{Q03}}$ bound. Every nominated subdivision adds its exact $2(r-1)$ pulse bundles, duration, fresh destination shadows and corresponding retention/clock charges before assembly.\n\nThis is a finite supplied/open source construction, with no empirical manufacture or autonomous clock claim. It neither resets the complete record nor identifies $L_{w}$ with total action, heat, entropy or PCE expenditure.\n\nFor the nonzero closed triangle witness require $K_{\\max}\\ge 3$. The raw likelihood, finite word channel, open-path transport, cost and schedule formulas remain valid for every positive $K_{\\max}$. At $K_{\\max}=1$ or 2 the triangle cannot be executed, so those descriptors do not witness nontrivial closed-loop holonomy. The nominated nontrivial descriptor uses $K_{\\max}=3$, the word $(a\\to b,b\\to c,c\\to a)$, and $N=1$.\n\nThe conservative imported bound $K_{\\max}C_{\\mathrm{Q03,ready}}(N)$ means copies of the full Q03 descriptor inventory reproduced in Theorem M.6.6c and retained by Definition Q.0.7d7. In particular each imported copy includes all 384 extra local levels in the 64 spare detector/dummy packages, as well as every reservoir/receipt cell, all 82 service reservations, inactive quantum stocks, matrices and physical record cells. Holding every imported component for the new deadline is separately priced as in Section 7; the old deadline does not silently extinguish a retained stock.\n\nThe exact $T_{I}$ and $T_{X}$ pulse ports, signed readiness routing and bounded loop controller are explicitly specified finite native operations/compositions added to the Q03 apparatus. Their Hamiltonians, prices, full stocks and response laws are explicit additional premises of this specified source.\n\n## Lemma Q.0.7d8.1: genuinely chiral integer representation\n\nLet $q=(-9,-5,-1,7,8)$, and take two identical copies, $Q=q\\oplus q$. All ten candidate Weyl flavours are left-handed. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum q &= -9-5-1+7+8 = 0, \\\\\n\\sum q^3 &= -729-125-1+343+512 = 0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNo $q_{i}$ is zero and no $q_i+q_j$ is zero, including $i=j$. The representation $R(z)=\\operatorname{diag}(z^{Q_a})$ has no conjugate-charge pairs, is not isomorphic to its conjugate, and admits no gauge-invariant bare Weyl mass bilinear: an entry $m_{ab}$ must obey $z^{Q_a+Q_b}m_{ab}=m_{ab}$ for every $z$ and thus vanishes. Doubling identical chirality and charges does not make the representation vector-like. It ensures that each odd absolute charge occurs twice. The representation is faithful because charge $-1$ occurs.\n\nFor the conventional four-dimensional spin Weyl anomaly polynomial, the degree-six part of $(\\sum\\exp(Q_a c_1))(1-p_1/24+\\dots)$ is\n\n$$\nI_6=(\\sum Q_a^3)c_1^3/6-(\\sum Q_a)c_1 p_1/24=0.\n$$\n\nThis explicit formal expansion checks the cubic and mixed linear coefficients. It is not a global-anomaly, determinant-line trivialization, or physical regulator proof by itself. No Standard Model selection is asserted.\n\nFor every accepted edge and path define $R(U_e)$ and $R(\\operatorname{Hol}_w)$. Matrix multiplication, orientation reversal and subdivision commute exactly with $R$. Hence the flavour-$a$ holonomy is $\\exp(iQ_aN(w)\\ln 2)$. Charge multiplication does not change the actual motor ledger $N(w)\\ln 2$ or create $Q_{a}$ physical events. It specifies a candidate matter representation of the connection $U_{e}$ defined above. Realizing the charged coupling by paid physical ports remains a new source requirement.\n\n## Lemma Q.0.7d8.2: exact flat embedding, including direction convention\n\nPut $L=\\ln 2$. Choose any periodic hypercubic lattice $\\Lambda_M=(\\mathbb{Z}/M\\mathbb{Z})^4$ with $M=3r$, $r\\ge 1$. Let the Lüscher-convention link $u(x,\\mu)$, which transports from $x+\\mu$ back to $x$, be\n\n$u(x,0)=\\exp(-iL)$ if $x_0=0$, and 1 otherwise;\n$u(x,\\mu)=1$ for $\\mu=1,2,3$.\n\nEvery elementary plaquette is 1: transverse shifts leave $u(x,0)$ unchanged and all transverse links are 1. Thus this background is admissible for every positive plaquette-angle bound, for every integer charge, and lies in the zero-flux sector.\n\nEmbed $a,b,c$ at $(0,0,0,0),(r,0,0,0),(2r,0,0,0)$. Map $e_0,e_1,e_2$ to the consecutive positive-direction paths between these vertices, with the last wrapping to $a$. Forward transport is the inverse of the product of $u$ links. It is respectively $\\exp(iL),1,1$, exactly the source connection values. Consequently every word has the same count/holonomy as the source, including reverse edges and all cycle relations. This algebraic map works for every finite $M=3r$, although the physical source admits only its nominated bounded words.\n\nFor source vertex frames $z_a,z_b,z_c$ choose any extension $z_{x}$ to the other lattice vertices. The transformation $u(x,\\mu)\\to z_x u(x,\\mu)z_{x+\\mu}^{-1}$ induces forward path transport $z_tU_{\\mathrm{path}}z_s^{-1}$, exactly the source convention. Choice of extension changes only internal frames. No additional physical gauge commands are inferred.\n\nEquivalently, a periodic gauge transformation distributes the holonomy uniformly: $u(x,0)=\\exp(-iL/M)$, other links 1. This makes the next spectral calculation elementary. The triangle is mapped into the one-skeleton as a noncontractible circle; its putative filling is NOT mapped to an elementary face or to a bounding disk.\n\n## Lemma Q.0.7d8.3: explicit gapped overlap operator at the matched flat background\n\nFix Hermitian Euclidean Clifford matrices $\\gamma_\\mu$ and $\\gamma_5$. In the uniform gauge, charge $Q$ has momentum\n\n$$\np_0=(2\\pi k_0-Q L)/M,\\quad p_j=2\\pi k_j/M\\ (j=1,2,3),\\quad k_\\mu\\in \\{0,\\dots,M-1\\}.\n$$\n\nDefine the Wilson symbol with Wilson parameter 1 and negative mass -1,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_Q(p)&=i \\sum_\\mu \\gamma_\\mu \\sin p_\\mu + [\\sum_\\mu(1-\\cos p_\\mu)-1] I, \\\\\nD_Q(p)&=I+A_Q(p)/\\sqrt{A_Q(p)^{\\dagger}A_Q(p)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $t_\\mu=1-\\cos p_\\mu\\ge 0$ gives the exact scalar identity\n\n$$\nA_Q^{\\dagger}A_Q = [1+2 \\sum_{\\mu<\\nu} t_\\mu t_\\nu] I \\ge I.\n$$\n\nThus no denominator singularity occurs, uniformly in $M$, $Q$ or toron phase. This is an analytic gap proof, not a numerical sample. Let $V=A(A^\\dagger A)^{-1/2}$. Then $V^\\dagger V=I$ and $\\gamma_5V\\gamma_5=V^\\dagger$. Direct expansion gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\gamma_5D+D\\gamma_5=D\\gamma_5D, \\\\\nD^\\dagger=\\gamma_5D\\gamma_5, \\\\\n\\hat{\\gamma}_5=\\gamma_5(1-D),\\quad (\\hat{\\gamma}_5)^\\dagger=\\hat{\\gamma}_5,\\quad (\\hat{\\gamma}_5)^2=I.\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\hat{P}_-=(1-\\hat{\\gamma}_5)/2$ and $P_+=(1+\\gamma_5)/2$ are exact projectors and $D\\hat{P}_-=P_+D$. Impose $\\hat{P}_-\\psi=\\psi$ and $\\bar{\\psi}P_+=\\bar{\\psi}$ separately on every flavour. This is a genuine Weyl projection rather than the unconstrained vector-like Grassmann model of Q.0.7d6. As a finite matrix it is fully specified by the Fourier symbols above; transforms to the slab gauge by conjugation.\n\nFor this specific toron no $D_{Q}$ zero mode occurs. $D=0$ requires $A$ negative scalar, hence every $\\sin p_\\mu=0$ and $\\sum(1-\\cos p_\\mu)<1$; this forces all $p_\\mu=0$ modulo $2\\pi$. But $p_0=0$ would imply $Q\\ln 2\\in 2\\pi\\mathbb{Z}$, excluded for nonzero integer $Q$ by the original irrationality lemma. Each Weyl matrix between its projected spaces is therefore invertible. Choosing orthonormal bases at this one background defines a finite Berezin integral, up to its basis phase. This does NOT fix a smooth gauge-covariant measure across the full background space, nor prove a native physical realization of that integral.\n\n## 1. One fully fixed matrix and phase convention\n\nFix lattice side $m=3$, periodic boundary conditions, $\\theta=L=\\ln 2$, and ordered charges $(-9,-5,-1,7,8,-9,-5,-1,7,8)$. The accepted triangle's positive winding has $N=1$ and forward holonomy $\\exp(iL)$. Use the interface's uniform backward links $u_0=\\exp(-iL/3)$, $u_j=1$, and its momenta $p_0=(2\\pi k_0-qL)/3$, $p_j=2\\pi k_j/3$. The slab/uniform map is a coordinate change, not a physical command silently added to the source.\n\nUse Pauli matrices $\\sigma_j$, $\\gamma_0=\\begin{bmatrix} 0 & I \\\\ I & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$, $\\gamma_j=\\begin{bmatrix} 0 & -i\\sigma_j \\\\ i\\sigma_j & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$, and $\\gamma_5=\\operatorname{diag}(I,-I)$. These explicitly fix Clifford and chirality conventions; do not infer an additional convention for $\\gamma_{5}$ from an unstated ordered product. Define $A,D,\\hat{P}_-$ and $P_+$ exactly as in the input interface. In each charge/momentum block, $\\operatorname{tr}(\\gamma_5)=\\operatorname{tr}(\\gamma_5A)=0$, so $\\hat{P}_-$ has trace/rank two. The gap identity $A^\\dagger A=[1+2\\sum_{\\mu<\\nu}(1-\\cos p_\\mu)(1-\\cos p_\\nu)]I$ proves the matrix functions exist. The two projector ranks are equal independently of any invertibility claim.\n\nOrder blocks by flavour index, then lexicographic $k$ in $\\{0,1,2\\}^4$. In each 4-dimensional block take the ordered output frame $e_1,e_2$. Construct the ordered input frame by exact Gram-Schmidt on $\\hat{P}_-e_1,\\dots,\\hat{P}_-e_4$: discard exactly zero residuals, divide nonzero residuals by their positive real norm, and stop after two vectors. No numerical tolerance defines this mathematical frame. This determines $E$ (input) and $F$ (output), each with $n=2\\cdot 10\\cdot 3^4=1620$ orthonormal columns. Set $C=F^\\dagger DE$. We do not need $\\det C$ nonzero.\n\nThe nominated complex Weyl determinant is $\\det C$ in THESE frames. Equivalently introduce independent Grassmann coordinates $\\eta_i$, $\\bar{\\eta}_i$ and the linear integration functional taking $\\eta_n\\bar{\\eta}_n\\cdots\\eta_1\\bar{\\eta}_1$ to 1 and all other monomials to zero. Then $\\int\\exp(-\\sum\\bar{\\eta}_iC_{ij}\\eta_j)=\\det C$. Indeed reordering each selected term to this top monomial gives the permutation sign; reversing the order of even pairs has no sign. This specifies the Berezin orientation and all overall signs.\n\nFor frame changes $E\\to ES$ and $F\\to FT$, $S,T$ unitary, $C\\to T^\\dagger CS$ and $\\det C\\to\\det(T)^{-1}\\det(S)\\det C$. The readout changes by this same phase when recompiled in those frames. It is not a frame-independent absolute determinant. A simultaneous ambient gauge-coordinate change $D\\to GDG^\\dagger$, $E\\to GE$, $F\\to GF$ leaves $C$ unchanged. Re-running the canonical coordinate Gram-Schmidt instead need not give those transported frames; the induced $S,T$ phases must then be retained. No continuous extension of these frame choices is asserted.\n\nSince $D=I+V$ with $V$ unitary, $\\lVert C\\rVert\\le 2$. Fix $\\kappa=2$ and $B=C/\\kappa$. Other explicit $\\kappa\\ge\\lVert C\\rVert$ also work, with the factor $\\kappa^{n}$ retained throughout.\n\n## 1. Inputs and retained representation\n\nUse the exact signed source, flat chiral regulator and finite Berezin frame/orientation constructed above.\n\nFix one $M=3r$ ($M=3$ suffices), $V=M^4$ sites, and charges $Q=(-9,-5,-1,7,8)$ twice, all left-handed. They have $\\sum Q=\\sum Q^3=0$, and no conjugate pairs or gauge-invariant mass bilinears. This is not the Standard Model representation.\n\nUse the flat slab background $u^*$ from the interface, with forward embedded triangle-path holonomies $(\\exp(i\\ln 2),1,1)$. Select a spanning tree $T$ of the periodic lattice graph. Let $C$ be the embedded triangle circle, comprising $M$ edges. Let $S$ be the real vector space of oriented-edge assignments $\\alpha$ which vanish on $T\\cup C$; reversal changes sign. There are at most $(V-1)+M$ constrained independent undirected edges among $4V$, so $\\dim S\\ge 3V+1-M>0$. A perturbation of a single edge outside $T\\cup C$ has a nontrivial plaquette curl, so $S$ contains curved backgrounds. Define $u_{\\alpha}(e)=u^*(e)\\exp(i\\alpha_e)$ in the backward-link convention, with $\\lVert\\alpha\\rVert_{\\infty}<\\delta$. This is a convex bounded chart; it deliberately fixes all source-circle link values, not all lattice holonomies.\n\nThe admitted full family is $B=\\{g\\cdot u_{\\alpha}:\\alpha\\in S,\\ \\lVert\\alpha\\rVert_{\\infty}<\\delta,\\ g\\text{ a periodic }U(1)\\text{ vertex map}\\}$, where $(g\\cdot u)(x,\\mu)=g_xu(x,\\mu)g_{x+\\mu}^{-1}$. Let root $o$ lie on $C$ and select the unique representative $g_o=1$. Uniqueness: tree links of $u_{\\alpha}$ and $u_{\\beta}$ coincide with $u^*$, so equality $g\\cdot u_{\\alpha}=h\\cdot u_{\\beta}$ forces $h^{-1}g$ constant on $T$, hence all vertices; basedness makes it identity, then $\\alpha=\\beta$ since $2\\delta<2\\pi$. Existence is part of the definition; extraction of $g$ uses unique tree-path products. An arbitrary gauge $h$ acts by $g\\to hg/h_o$ and leaves $\\alpha$ invariant. Hence $B/G$ is exactly the specified convex $\\alpha$ chart. There is no Gribov ambiguity here.\n\nAll embedded source paths retain their exact original transports in the slice and have endpoint-frame covariance after g. Every admitted original word still has character exp(i N ln2), with no change to its raw ledger. Curvature off the source circle is an independent paid external background. This domain is open relative to its fixed-event-holonomy constraints; it is not an open set of every possible four-dimensional connection.\n\n## 2. Explicit uniform regulator and chart radius\n\nOn each charge block let $T_{\\mu}(u)\\psi(x)=u(x,\\mu)^Q\\psi(x+\\mu)$. With the Hermitian $\\gamma$ matrices fixed in the matrix and phase convention above, set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(u) &= 3 I - (1/2) \\sum_\\mu [(I-\\gamma_\\mu)T_\\mu +(I+\\gamma_\\mu)T_\\mu^\\dagger], \\\\\nH(u) &= \\gamma_5 A(u),\\quad D(u)=I+\\gamma_5 \\operatorname{sign} H(u),\\quad P(u)=(I+\\operatorname{sign} H(u))/2,\\quad P_+=(I+\\gamma_5)/2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen $H$ is Hermitian. At $u^*$, $(H^*)^2=A^{*\\dagger}A^*\\ge I$ by the already proved Fourier identity. Since $\\lVert(I\\pm\\gamma_{\\mu})/2\\rVert=1$ and $|\\exp(iQ\\alpha)-1|\\le|Q\\alpha|$,\n\n$$\n\\lVert H(u_{\\alpha})-H^*\\rVert\\le 8\\lvert Q\\rvert\\delta\\le 72\\delta=:\\eta.\n$$\n\nLet $s_{M}$ be the minimum of the positive singular values of all $D_Q(u^*)$; it is positive by the interface's finite momentum proof. It is an explicitly defined finite minimum, not an unknown continuum gap. Choose\n\n$$\n\\delta=\\min(10^{-4}, s_M/288),\\quad g_0=1-\\eta.\n$$\n\nThen $\\eta\\le 0.0072$, $H(u_{\\alpha})$ has spectral gap at least $g_0$, and all matrix functions above are smooth throughout the chart. No admissibility theorem is needed for this bound. The elementary plaquette angles have magnitude $\\le 4\\delta$, so $9(4\\delta)\\le 0.0036<1/30$ as an additional check, but that alone is not used as a chiral-measure theorem.\n\nFor completeness, the sign bound is elementary. Subtract the imaginary-axis resolvent representations for two $g_0$-gapped Hermitian matrices. The difference is absolutely integrable; the resolvent identity bounds its norm by $\\eta/(g_0^2+t^2)$. Integration with factor $1/\\pi$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\lVert\\operatorname{sign}H-\\operatorname{sign}H^*\\rVert\\le\\eta/g_0, \\\\\n\\lVert P-P^*\\rVert\\le\\eta/(2g_0)<1, \\\\\n\\lVert D-D^*\\rVert\\le\\eta/g_0<s_M/2.\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nThus $D$ is invertible on this entire family. Its chiral restriction has equal input/output rank $n_Q=2V$ (constant along the chart), and is invertible because $DP=P_+D$. These statements remain true on every gauge orbit by conjugation. The bound $s_{M}$ is dimensionless; it is not a physical energy gap.\n\n## 3. Derived measure, including all phase transports\n\nAt u*, take the ordered orthonormal input E*_Q and output F*_Q fixed in the matrix and phase convention above (Fourier frames transported to the slab coordinate gauge). Define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nG_Q(\\alpha)&=E^{*\\dagger}_Q P_Q(\\alpha) E^{*}_Q, \\\\\nE_Q(\\alpha)&=P_Q(\\alpha) E^{*}_Q G_Q(\\alpha)^{-1/2},\\quad F_Q(\\alpha)=F^{*}_Q.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bound $\\lVert P-P^*\\rVert<1$ makes $G$ positive definite. Direct multiplication gives $E^\\dagger E=I$; $E$ spans $\\operatorname{ran}P$; $E(0)=E^*$. Positive matrix inverse square roots are smooth on this bounded positive cone. This explicit polar chart derives the family frame and its phase, without parameter-dependent Gram-Schmidt pivot choices or an assumed measure.\n\nFor $u=g\\cdot u_{\\alpha}$ with based $g$ define $E_Q(u)=R_Q(g)E_Q(\\alpha)$, $F_Q(u)=R_Q(g)F^*_Q$ and $C_Q(u)=F_Q(u)^\\dagger D_Q(u)E_Q(u)$. Then $C_Q(u)=C_Q(\\alpha)$. Under an arbitrary $h$, based normalization gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_Q(h.u)&=h_o^{-Q} R_Q(h)E_Q(u), \\\\\nF_Q(h.u)&=h_o^{-Q} R_Q(h)F_Q(u).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe residual constant phases cancel in C. They also cancel between input and output determinant lines, since both ranks are 2V. Therefore C, det C and the resulting joint measure orientation descend exactly to B/G, including the constant stabilizer. This argument does not need anomaly cancellation: its nonlocal phase is precisely why it must not be mistaken for an anomaly-free local-measure theorem.\n\nLet $C$ be the direct sum in the fixed flavour order, $n=20V$. The Berezin functional is specified by the same ordered top monomial as in the matrix and phase convention above, in coordinates $\\psi=E\\eta$ and $\\bar{\\psi}=\\bar{\\eta}F^\\dagger$. Expanding the exponential proves $Z(\\alpha)=\\int\\exp(-\\bar{\\eta}C\\eta)=\\det C$. Because $C$ is invertible, every normalized finite Grassmann expectation exists; its numerator is a finite signed sum of minors and its denominator $Z$. These are smooth functions on the chart. A chosen finite polynomial test is part of the descriptor; the claim is not that an arbitrary Grassmann polynomial is a positive random variable.\n\nFor other frames $E'=ES,F'=FT$, $C'=T^\\dagger CS$ and $Z'=\\det(T)^{-1}\\det(S)Z$. Coordinates and integration volume transform inversely, yielding the same ambient density/functionals when the phase convention is transported. These exact cocycles multiply on triple overlaps. The chart and based gauge section provide a global trivialization on the admitted family, not a trivialization on all admissible fields or topologies.\n\nThe differential response is not stipulated: $dZ=Z\\operatorname{tr}(C^{-1}dC)$, with $dC=(dF^\\dagger)DE+F^\\dagger(dD)E+F^\\dagger D(dE)$. All terms, including the measure-frame derivative, are retained. On vertical gauge directions $C$ is constant and $dZ=0$. Dropping $dE$ would generally give a different current. Gauge differentiation with polynomial insertions gives the finite integrated Ward identity, including their representation variation. No assertion identifies this nonlocal chart current with a local physical region-boundary flux.\n\nIn section 1, the spanning tree $T$ is chosen to contain $C$ with one edge removed. Such a tree exists because the remaining $M-1$ circle edges form a forest, and a forest in a connected finite graph extends to a spanning tree. It cannot contain the last circle edge, which would create a cycle. Consequently $|T\\cap C|=M-1$, $|T\\cup C|=(V-1)+M-(M-1)=V$, and the chart dimension is exactly $4V-V=3V$. At $M=3$ this is 243.\n\nThe theorem's prior general lower bound $\\dim S\\ge 3V+1-M$ was valid for arbitrary spanning trees; an exact 243-dimensional claim required this stronger choice. The published check's BFS tree already satisfies it: 80 tree edges, 3 circle edges, 81 union edges, hence 2 circle edges in the tree. No numerical rerun or change to those results is required. All analytic bounds and transport proofs remain unchanged.\n\nThe estimate $\\lVert D-D^*\\rVert<s_M/2$ holds on the entire declared open chart, so $\\sigma_{\\min}(C)>s_M/2$ and $|\\det C|>(s_M/2)^n$ there, not merely on smaller compact subcharts. This strengthens the earlier conditioning statement; the bound is still extremely small and does not make estimation efficient.\n\nFor an off-diagonal minor instrument, an occupation-state permutation may introduce a known fermionic wedge sign. Retain that sign explicitly in the reported coefficient, or compose the one-particle permutation with a phase on one occupied output mode so the nominated occupation transition amplitude is +1. This is an explicit number-preserving quadratic phase port with its own paid delivery, never a dropped common phase. For zero-particle minors the vacuum amplitude is +1 by definition and no such correction is required.\n\n## 1. Explicit common backgrounds for a finite refinement pair\n\nFix physical torus side $\\ell$ and coarse $M=3r$, fine $2M$. Fix a finite collection of real smooth periodic one-forms $b_{j}$ supported away from the embedded source circle $C$; take at least one with $db_j$ nonzero and a sampled nonzero plaquette integral. Put $\\mathcal{A}(t)=\\mathcal{A}_0+\\sum_j t_jb_j$, where $\\mathcal{A}_{0}$ has circle period $\\ln 2$, and $|t_j|<\\rho$. Each link is $\\exp(-i\\int_e\\mathcal{A}(t))$. Exact integrals imply coarse link equals the ordered product of its two fine sublinks. Gauge endpoint transport commutes with this restriction. Support away from $C$ preserves every original event-path holonomy, not only their total cycle.\n\nPass separately to the background slab coordinates and the based tree slice at each level. Use the spanning trees containing $C$ minus one edge. The required gauge transformation is obtained by tree products; along $C$ it is identity since the relative perturbation links there are 1. Thus it does not spoil the fixed event links. If $B=\\max_j\\max_e|\\int_e b_j|$ over BOTH finite lattices and $d$ is the parameter count, the raw edge angle is at most $d\\rho B$. Relative tree gauge fixing changes a chord angle by two tree-path sums, so its real lift has magnitude at most $(2V_f-1)d\\rho B$, $V_f=(2M)^4$. Choose $\\rho$ so this is smaller than both chart radii and $\\pi$. This gives an explicit nonempty curved shared family. The fixed finite collection can be chosen with an edge-sampling nonzero curl by a smooth bump around an off-circle edge segment away from its transverse competitors.\n\nOnly a finite pair (or a specified finite chain) is claimed. The factor $V_{f}$ in this sufficient bound is not uniform as the cutoff grows. It cannot establish a fixed nonflat continuum source class at arbitrarily fine scales.\n\n## 2. A fixed, gauge-compatible low/high split\n\nUse the previous explicit overlap/projector-chart construction at each level with the same charges, source character and $\\gamma$ conventions. At the flat fine background choose Fourier base frames ordered by flavour and momentum. Let the low set consist of integer momentum labels in a nominated coarse Brillouin representative set $K_{M}$ in each direction; use those same integers as fine labels. For example $M=3$ uses $\\{-1,0,1\\}$, embedded in the fine 6-point set. Each flavour has $2M^4$ low input/output coordinates, and the remaining fine coordinates are high. This split is on the fixed chart coordinate labels. At nonflat $t$ the chart frame transports these labels, so the split is fully specified and smooth, though not geometrically local.\n\nLet the fine Weyl matrix in this order be\n\n$$\nC_f(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}A(t)&B(t)\\\\ C(t)&H(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHere $C(t)$ in the lower-left block is a block name, not the complete Weyl matrix. At $t=0$ the fine operator is momentum diagonal: $B=C=0$ and $H$ is the direct sum of the nonzero high-momentum Weyl blocks. Let $s_{H}>0$ be the minimum singular value of those finitely many blocks, calculated from the explicit flat Fourier symbols. This is a finite computable positive constant.\n\nThe chart bounds give an explicit smallness condition. Write $\\epsilon_P=\\max_Q\\lVert P_Q(t)-P_Q(0)\\rVert$. For $\\epsilon_{P}<0.1$ the polar frame formula gives $\\lVert E(t)-E(0)\\rVert\\le 3\\epsilon_P$: indeed its two contributions are bounded by $\\epsilon_P/\\sqrt{1-\\epsilon_P}$ and $(1/\\sqrt{1-\\epsilon_P}-1)$. Also $\\lVert D(t)-D(0)\\rVert\\le 2\\epsilon_P$. Therefore $\\lVert C_f(t)-C_f(0)\\rVert\\le 8\\epsilon_P$. Shrink $\\rho$ to ensure $\\epsilon_P<s_H/16$, as well as the original radius requirements. Then $H(t)$ is invertible with $\\lVert H(t)^{-1}\\rVert\\le 2/s_H$. Every quantity in the following transition is smooth and explicitly calculated on the entire common family.\n\nGauge transformations leave the chart-coordinate matrix $C_{f}$ invariant, as proved previously. They therefore leave each block and this transition invariant. In ambient field variables the low/high embeddings transform with the charged frames, including their common stabilizer phases. This is gauge compatibility on the bounded quotient; it is not a proof of a local gauge-covariant blocking kernel.\n\n## 3. Derived measure transition, including sources and phases\n\nDefine the effective coarse matrix\n\n$$\nS(t)=A(t)-B(t) H(t)^{-1} C(t).\n$$\n\nComplete the Grassmann square with triangular shifts $h'=h+H^{-1}Cl$ and $\\bar{h}'=\\bar{h}+\\bar{l}BH^{-1}$. Their Berezin Jacobians are exactly 1. With the fixed block ordering, all integration pairs are even, so there is no hidden exchange sign. Direct expansion yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\int_{\\mathrm{high}}\\exp(-\\bar{\\psi}C_f\\psi)=\\det H\\cdot\\exp(-\\bar{l}Sl), \\\\\n\\det C_f=\\det H\\det S.\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nNo absolute value replaces the complex high-mode factor; its phase is retained. Since $C_{f}$ and H are invertible, S is invertible. This proves an exact finite measure pushforward onto the declared low coordinates.\n\nFor arbitrary formal Grassmann sources $\\bar{J}_L,J_L,\\bar{J}_H,J_H$, the same calculation gives the retained factor\n\n$\\det H\\cdot\\exp(\\bar{J}_HH^{-1}J_H)$,\n\nand low sources\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ_L^{\\mathrm{eff}}&=J_L-B H^{-1}J_H, \\\\\n\\bar{J}_L^{\\mathrm{eff}}&=\\bar{J}_L-\\bar{J}_H H^{-1}C,\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncoupled to the effective action -barl S l. Thus differentiation recovers all original polynomial response coefficients, including high insertions; they are not lost by suppressing high variables. At zero high sources, normalized low expectations use S alone because det H cancels between numerator and denominator. That cancellation does not authorize deleting det H from the unnormalized event-source response or invoice.\n\nChanging low/high frames by separate unitaries produces the usual determinant-line factors; the fixed shifts and products transform covariantly. Arbitrary frame changes mixing low and high change the nominated blocking map, so they require transforming the projector/split too. The low/high split is retained descriptor data.\n\nFor a finite nested sequence, wherever every pivot block is invertible, repeated Grassmann integration is associative: expanding the finite polynomial and taking its designated top coefficients commutes for the even variable pairs. Hence the effective kernel and total determinant factor do not depend on whether adjacent blocking steps are composed or eliminated together. This is a genuine finite recomposition identity, with its hypotheses explicit. It does not supply uniform continuum estimates.\n\n## 4. Source and instrument compatibility, without a determinant oracle\n\n$H$ and $S$ are obtained by finite matrix entries, products and inverses, without first evaluating their determinants. $\\lVert H\\rVert\\le 2$, while $\\lVert S\\rVert\\le 2+8/s_H$; nominate $\\kappa_{H}=3$ and $\\kappa_S=3+8/s_H$. The same positive-defect unitary dilation and CAR instruments implement their determinants and every required minor with these known scales. The direct fine instrument uses $\\kappa_{f}=3$. Exact normalized response coordinates obey\n\n$$\na_f = a_H a_S \\cdot (\\kappa_H^{n_H} \\kappa_S^{n_L} / 3^{n_f}),\n$$\n\nwhere $a_H=\\det H/\\kappa_H^{n_H}$ and $a_S=\\det S/\\kappa_S^{n_L}$. The scale factor is retained; these are not claims that an arbitrary scaled complex number is a probability. Equality follows from the exact determinant identity. Independent finite trials estimate the factors with propagated errors; a joint tensor-product controlled experiment can also measure the product of the normalized amplitudes. Every extra preparation, output-comparison permutation, driver, pointer and trial is charged.\n\nThis connects formal instrument responses on the same accepted motor-event source and common clock. It does not assert equality of the entire physical apparatus channels, whose residual quantum states and supplier records differ. The new high-mode stock, phases, matrix-delivery and inverse-calibration services, full failed/unused records and original raw readiness law remain retained. Both schedules require parameterized source ports, which remain an explicit source hypothesis. Matrix inversion near a small $s_{H}$ has explicit conditioning cost; it is not a free background-renormalization primitive.\n\nThe effective action is still Euclidean finite Grassmann data. It has not become the physical Hamiltonian of the CAR determinant-computing apparatus.\n\n## 5. Exact obstruction to identifying S with the old coarse overlap matrix\n\nAt $t=0$, low/high momentum blocks decouple, so $S$ is exactly the fine Weyl operator restricted to the selected low momenta. Compare its physical-unit version $S/a_f$ with the earlier coarse $D_c/a_c$ restricted to their Weyl spaces, where $a_c=\\ell/M$, $a_f=\\ell/(2M)$. Singular values are invariant under all admissible orthonormal input/output frame choices, so a mismatch cannot be cured by a phase convention.\n\nTake charge $Q=-1$ and a momentum with only its 0-component nonzero. Write $p=(2\\pi k+\\ln 2)/M$ in $(-\\pi,\\pi)$. The Wilson symbol is $A=-\\cos p+i\\gamma_{0}\\sin p$, so $A^\\dagger A=I$ and $D=1-\\cos p+i\\gamma_{0}\\sin p$. Therefore both Weyl singular values are $2|\\sin(p/2)|$. The corresponding selected fine momentum is $p/2$ and has singular values $2|\\sin(p/4)|$. In physical units the fine/coarse singular-value ratio is exactly\n\n$$\n[(2M/\\ell)2|\\sin(p/4)|]/[(M/\\ell)2|\\sin(p/2)|]=1/\\cos(p/4).\n$$\n\nAt $M=3$, $k=0$ and $k=1$ both satisfy $0<p<\\pi$ and have distinct $p$. Their ratios are unequal because cosine is strictly decreasing on $(0,\\pi/4)$. Thus:\n\n1. Under the nominated momentum-preserving identification, $S/a_f$ is not unitarily frame-equivalent to the nominated coarse $C_c/a_c$.\n2. No one scalar field-strength factor, even charge dependent, repairs both momenta of this one charge.\n3. A background phase/counterphase multiplying the determinant cannot fix this action/singular-value defect.\n\nOne can algebraically introduce a momentum-dependent field redefinition or simply adopt $S$ as the coarse action. Either changes the nominated coarse source/operator and must carry its Jacobian, determinant normalization, locality analysis and supplier map. It is not already supplied by the original chart construction. The defect is $O(p^2)$ for these individual fixed smooth modes as $M$ grows, but that elementary statement is not an all-mode determinant, local-current or continuum convergence estimate; ultraviolet modes and growing multiplicities remain uncontrolled.\n\n## Base connection\n\nThe base connection in the section “Explicit common backgrounds for a finite refinement pair” is $\\mathcal{A}_0=(\\ln 2/\\ell)\\,dx_0$ in the uniform periodic gauge on the side-$\\ell$ torus, with the remaining three periods zero. This is the inherited flat toron, subsequently transformed to the existing slab coordinates. Its period on $C$ alone would not imply flatness or fix the other periods, both of which the Fourier calculations that follow require.\n\n## Precise two-mode obstruction\n\nThe two singular-value ratios in section 5 compare CORRESPONDING selected momentum blocks under the nominated integer-momentum identification. They prove failure of that momentum-preserving identification, even after arbitrary unitary input/output frame changes within those blocks and one scalar field-strength factor for the charge. They also exclude determinant phase changes as a repair of that blockwise action mismatch.\n\nThey do not, by themselves, rule out an arbitrary unitary transformation mixing different momentum blocks, or such a transformation combined with an arbitrary scalar. Item 1 of section 5, and every statement that frame changes are excluded, carries this momentum-preserving qualification. An unrestricted unitary-equivalence claim requires a comparison of the complete singular-value multisets, not two corresponding labels. No unrestricted obstruction is claimed.\n\nThe exact Schur-complement transition, high-factor retention, source responses and finite associativity are unaffected. The claim that the resulting effective matrix is not equal to the nominated coarse matrix in the specified momentum-compatible refinement map remains proved.\n\n## 1. Inputs, source and exact common nonflat domain\n\nUse the finite chiral chart and exact Schur-refinement construction proved in the preceding parts of this theorem, including the base connection and momentum-preserving identification qualifications. Preserve its source character, charges $Q=(-9,-5,-1,7,8)$ twice and all original supplier/phase records. The following construction explicitly supplies the common nonflat domain and its fixed-test limit.\n\nWrite $x=\\ell y$ on the physical side-$\\ell$ torus, $y$ in $[0,1)^4$. Let $f$ be the explicit smooth periodic bump: $f(y)=\\exp[-1/(1-((y-1/2)/(3/10))^2)]$ for $|y-1/2|<3/10$, zero otherwise. Thus $f(0)=0$, $F=\\lVert f\\rVert_{\\infty}=\\exp(-1)$, and $f'$ is not identically zero. Set\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A(t)=L\\, dy_0+t f(y_2)\\,dy_1,\\quad L=\\ln 2.\n$$\n\nIts curvature is $t f'(y_2)\\,dy_2\\wedge dy_1$, nonzero for $t\\ne 0$, while its restriction to the source circle is exactly $L\\,dy_0$. All accepted event-path holonomies and raw ledgers remain the original ones. Sample backward links by exact line integrals: $u_0=\\exp(-iL/M)$, $u_1(y)=\\exp(-it f(y_2)/M)$, $u_2=u_3=1$. Coarse links equal products of fine links before any coordinate gauge changes.\n\nUse the following rooted coordinate-comb tree on $j_{\\mu}$ in $\\{0,\\dots,M-1\\}$. The parent of a nonroot vertex decrements the first nonzero coordinate in the priority order (0,3,2,1). Consequently direction-1 tree edges occur only where $j_0=j_2=j_3=0$. Their perturbation is exactly zero since $f(0)=0$; all other tree-edge perturbations are zero because they are in directions 0,2,3. Every relative perturbation tree link is therefore 1, exactly, for all $M$. The tree contains the source circle minus its wrapping edge.\n\nTransform the fixed flat base to the inherited slab gauge. This does not change the $U(1)$ relative perturbation. The entire sampled family is already in the required relative tree slice, with\n\n$$\n\\lVert\\alpha_M(t)\\rVert_{\\infty}\\le\\lvert t\\rvert F/M.\n$$\n\nThe transformation from uniform to slab base is retained; it is not omitted when comparing refinements. This construction improves the previous conservative finite-pair estimate by using the actual structured source, not by assuming a cutoff-uniform gauge-fixing bound.\n\n## 2. Uniform stability derived from the explicit toron spectrum\n\nLet $d_*=\\min_Q\\operatorname{dist}(QL,2\\pi\\mathbb{Z})>0$. This is a finite positive constant fixed by the accepted character and charges. For every flat momentum $p$ in principal coordinates, its temporal component has $|p_0|\\ge d_*/M$. Write $W=\\sum_{\\mu}(1-\\cos p_{\\mu})-1$, $R=\\sqrt{W^2+\\sum\\sin^2 p_{\\mu}}$. The overlap singular value is $s(p)=\\sqrt{2+2W/R}$.\n\nIf $W\\ge 0$ then $s\\ge\\sqrt{2}$. If $W<0$ then $\\sum(1-\\cos p)<1$, hence every $|p_{\\mu}|<\\pi/2$. The identity\n\n$$\ns^2=2 \\sum \\sin^2 p/[R(R-W)] \\ge \\sum \\sin^2 p/R^2\n$$\n\nand $R<8$, $|\\sin p_{\\mu}|\\ge 2|p_{\\mu}|/\\pi$ imply $s\\ge|p|/(4\\pi)\\ge d_*/(4\\pi M)$. The same lower bound is harmless in the $W\\ge 0$ case. Thus the previously defined flat minimum obeys $s_M\\ge d_*/(4\\pi M)$, with no numerical extrapolation.\n\nChoose a fixed positive t0 satisfying\n\n$$\nt_0 F < \\min(3\\cdot 10^{-4}, d_{*}/(1152\\pi)).\n$$\n\nThen for every $M\\ge 3$, $\\lVert\\alpha_M\\rVert<\\min(10^{-4},s_M/288)$, exactly the earlier admitted chart radius. The family is genuinely nonflat for every $0<|t|<t_0$. The Wilson perturbation bound gives $\\eta_M\\le 72t_0F/M$ and kernel gap $g_M\\ge 1-\\eta_M>0.99$. The sign bound then gives $\\lVert D_M(t)-D_M(0)\\rVert\\le\\eta_M/g_M<s_M/2$. With $a=\\ell/M$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\sigma_{\\min}(D_M(t)/a)>d_*/(8\\pi\\ell), \\\\\n\\lVert(D_M(t)/a)^{-1}\\rVert<8\\pi\\ell/d_*.\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nThese are uniform over $M$ and $|t|<t_0$ and transfer exactly to each Weyl restriction. They are dimensionful Euclidean inverse estimates, not a physical Hamiltonian energy-gap claim.\n\n## 3. Continuum operator and smooth inverse, constructed without a continuum measure premise\n\nFor each charge define the continuum first-order operator on periodic smooth spinors\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nK_Q(t) &= (1/\\ell) \\sum_\\mu \\gamma_\\mu[\\partial_{y_\\mu}-iQ A_\\mu(t)], \\\\\nA_0 &= L,\\quad A_1=t f(y_2),\\quad A_2=A_3=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$K_Q(0)$ is explicitly Fourier diagonal, with inverse norm at most $\\ell/d_*$. The perturbation is bounded multiplication $V_Q(t)=-(iQt/\\ell)\\gamma_1f(y_2)$, with norm $\\le 9t_0F/\\ell$. The chosen $t_0$ implies $\\lVert K_Q(0)^{-1}V_Q\\rVert<1/2$. Therefore the convergent operator-norm Neumann series\n\n$$\nK_Q(t)^{-1}=[I+K_Q(0)^{-1}V_Q(t)]^{-1} K_Q(0)^{-1}\n$$\n\nconstructs a bounded inverse with norm $\\le 2\\ell/d_*$ throughout the same family. It is not assumed as a primitive continuum propagator.\n\nSmoothness of the inverse on smooth sources also follows directly. The Fourier multiplier $K_Q(0)^{-1}$ maps $H^s$ to $H^{s+1}$, because its nonzero shifted momenta have a fixed gap and its symbol decays as $|k|^{-1}$. Multiplication by smooth $f$ preserves each integer Sobolev space by the Leibniz rule. From $\\psi=K_Q(0)^{-1}h-K_Q(0)^{-1}V_Q\\psi$, start with $\\psi$ in $L^2$ and bootstrap one derivative at a time. For fixed smooth $h$ this yields all Sobolev norms, uniformly in $|t|\\le t_0$ after slightly reducing the strict interval if desired. The initial inverse bound and recursive multiplication constants are explicit finite constants depending on the finitely many derivatives of $f$ and $h$ used. Sobolev embedding supplies uniform pointwise derivative bounds for the Taylor estimates below. No external elliptic-regularity theorem is needed.\n\nK anticommutes with gamma5; its inverse maps Pplus spinors to Pminus spinors. This is a genuinely charged chiral continuum TEST kernel on a prescribed external nonflat background. It is not yet a source measure or Lorentzian realization.\n\n## 4. A uniform O(a) consistency and inverse-error proof\n\nUse the sampling map $(J_M\\psi)(j)=a^2\\psi(aj)$, so Euclidean lattice $\\ell^2$ approximates continuum $L^2$ on the side-$\\ell$ torus. All lattice $\\gamma$ matrices and links are expressed in the uniform gauge in this proof; the previously retained slab/tree maps give unitary-equivalent statements.\n\nLet $A_{M}$ be the Wilson matrix and $B_M=A_M^\\dagger A_M$. For each fixed smooth $\\psi$, covariant Taylor expansion of the exact link integral and nearest-neighbor shift gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\lVert(I+A_M)J_M\\psi-aJ_MK_Q(t)\\psi\\rVert\\le c_1(\\psi)a^2, \\\\\n\\lVert(B_M-I)J_M\\psi\\rVert\\le c_2(\\psi)a^2.\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nThe constants are uniform in $M$ and $|t|\\le t_0$. Here the first-order expansion is $A_M=-I+aK+O(a^2)$; its adjoint has first-order term $-aK$ because $K$ is anti-Hermitian. Thus the first-order terms in $B_M-I$ cancel. The second estimate can equivalently be checked by expanding the finite width-two stencil directly. Finite derivatives of $f$ and $\\psi$ bound all pointwise remainders; the $a^{2}$ sampling weight turns the sum over $M^4$ sites into a bounded volume factor. These are estimates on smooth vectors, not a false operator-norm claim that $B_{M}$ tends to $I$ on ultraviolet modes.\n\nSince $B_M\\ge g_M^2I$ with $g_{M}>0.99$, the scalar identity\n\n$$\n\\lambda^{-1/2}-1=(1-\\lambda)/[\\sqrt{\\lambda}(1+\\sqrt{\\lambda})]\n$$\n\nand spectral calculus give\n\n$$\n\\lVert(B_M^{-1/2}-I)J_M\\psi\\rVert\\le[g_M(1+g_M)]^{-1}\\lVert(B_M-I)J_M\\psi\\rVert.\n$$\n\nAlso $\\lVert A_M\\rVert\\le 11$ from its finite hopping formula. With $D_M=I+A_MB_M^{-1/2}$, combine these bounds to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\lVert(D_M/a)J_M\\psi-J_MK_Q(t)\\psi\\rVert\\le C(\\psi)a, \\\\\nC=c_1+11c_2/[0.99(1+0.99)].\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nFor a fixed smooth source $h$ put $\\psi=K_Q(t)^{-1}h$. The smooth-inverse construction supplies a uniform bound on $C(\\psi)$. The resolvent identity on this vector, together with section 2 stability, proves\n\n$$\n\\lVert(D_M/a)^{-1}J_Mh-J_MK_Q(t)^{-1}h\\rVert\\le(8\\pi\\ell/d_*)C(\\psi)a.\n$$\n\nThis is actual controlled convergence on the common nonflat family. It neither uses nor implies convergence uniformly over cutoff-dependent rough tests.\n\n## 5. Chiral normalized responses and low-test Schur actions\n\nThe exact identity $D_MP_M=P_+D_M$ gives $P_M(D_M/a)^{-1}=(D_M/a)^{-1}P_+$. Thus the ambient Weyl covariance is precisely\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\nG_M(t)=(D_M/a)^{-1}P_+, \\\\\nG(t)=K_Q(t)^{-1}P_+.\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nThe matrix-element pairing with any two fixed smooth source tests converges at $O(a)$, adding the ordinary smooth periodic quadrature error. A finite product of these pairings and any fixed finite Wick determinant converges at $O(a)$: expand a determinant into its finitely many permutations and telescope each product, using the uniform inverse bounds. The doubled charge list gives the corresponding finite flavour products. These are normalized Berezin expectations. The determinant-line phase cancels between numerator and denominator, which is why this result does not resolve that phase.\n\nTo connect directly to the Schur transition, fix a finite set of Fourier source labels $K$ and constant input $P_-$/output $P_+$ spinor frames, independent of $M$. At sufficiently large $M$ these sampled Fourier vectors are exactly orthonormal. Project the input test vectors by $P_{M}$ and apply the same positive Gram inverse-square-root construction. Since $P_M=P_-+\\gamma_5D_M/2$, consistency implies these projected low frames differ from the sampled continuum input frames by $O(a)$ on this fixed set. Complete them to the full Weyl frames, retaining all frame-change phases. The particular complement is descriptor data and need not be local.\n\nThe low compression of the full inverse therefore converges at $O(a)$ to the finite matrix\n\n$$\nB_K(t)=[\\langle e_i,K_Q(t)^{-1} f_j\\rangle]_{i,j \\in K}.\n$$\n\nAt $t=0$ and matching Fourier labels this matrix is block diagonal and invertible. The resolvent bound controls its change: $\\lVert B_K(t)-B_K(0)\\rVert\\le\\lVert K(t)^{-1}-K(0)^{-1}\\rVert$, bounded by the Neumann-series estimate. Reduce $t_0$ further, independently of $M$, so this is less than $\\sigma_{\\min}(B_K(0))/4$. For all sufficiently large $M$, the discrete compression is then invertible as well. In a complete low/high coordinate decomposition, invertibility of the full operator and this inverse block implies invertibility of the complementary high block (equivalently apply block Gaussian elimination to the inverse). The physical-unit Schur effective matrix is\n\n$$\nS_{M,K}^{\\mathrm{phys}}=[\\text{low compression of }(D_M/a)^{-1}]^{-1},\n$$\n\nand converges to $S_K^{\\mathrm{cont}}=B_K(t)^{-1}$, with error bounded by the inverse-product identity and the preceding $O(a)$ estimate. This is convergence to the exact continuum low-source effective action, which includes coupling through the continuum high sector. It is not identification with the naive projection of $K$ or with the old coarse overlap matrix.\n\nThe changed fixed-test split is explicit; it is not silently equated with the earlier complete coarse-Brillouin-zone split. Its background, measure and source are the same. Complementary frame determinants and the high-mode factor are retained in the exact finite pushforward. No locality of this Fourier-based blocking has been proved.\n\n## 6. Absolute determinant and instrument normalization: a separate unresolved join\n\nAt every finite $M$ the exact Schur formula still reads $Z_M=\\det H_{M,K}\\det S_{M,K}$, in the original dimensionless action, with its complex high factor and all coordinate/physical-unit powers retained. Physical-unit rescaling of an $n$-dimensional matrix multiplies its determinant by $a^{-n}$; such factors cannot be silently absorbed into a continuum measure.\n\nFor the existing dimensionless determinant instrument, $n_M=20M^4$, $\\kappa=3$ and $\\lVert C_M\\rVert\\le 2$. Therefore, on the ENTIRE common nonflat family,\n\n$$\n\\lvert a_M\\rvert=\\lvert\\det C_M\\rvert/3^{n_M}\\le(2/3)^{n_M}\\to 0.\n$$\n\nIts $X$/$Y$ outcome laws converge uniformly to $1/2$, and their maximum deviation is at most $(1/2)(2/3)^{20M^4}$. This is an exact obstruction to treating the unrescaled determinant-readout law as a nontrivial separating continuum response. Multiplying the observed expectation by $3^{n_M}$ recovers the finite determinant but exponentially amplifies errors; it is not a proved renormalized continuum vacuum normalization.\n\nNormalized low correlations converge because det H cancels from their ratios. It must nevertheless remain in the unnormalized response map, source records and resource census. The present proof supplies neither a convergent subtraction of log det H, nor a local counterterm/phase prescription, nor uniform derivatives of the absolute determinant. Its complex phase is still fixed only by the finite chart convention. A new continuum phase or vacuum factor cannot be declared primitive and counted as proved by the covariance limit.\n\nChanging the finite determinant-line convention by a background-dependent unit phase leaves all normalized Wick expectations unchanged. Hence the convergence of those expectations alone cannot possibly determine the missing absolute phase. This logical distinction holds even though our current finite convention is fully specified.\n\nAll per-cutoff CAR modes, matrices, parameterized compiler/calibration services, high-factor instruments, finite trials, original motor records, common clock and failed/unused stocks remain charged as in the preceding census. The growing inventory and precision requirements are retained; there is no claimed uniform finite apparatus or native continuum field dynamics.\n\n## 1. Background, operators and a uniform complex neighborhood\n\nUse the same toron and nonflat family $\\mathcal{A}(t)=L\\,dy_0+t f(y_2)\\,dy_1$, ten left-handed charges, fixed $\\ell$, $M=3\\cdot 2^j$, $a=\\ell/M$, comb tree and all retained source/frame maps proved above in the common nonflat fixed-test construction, with its stated projector-sign convention. Let $F=\\lVert f\\rVert_{\\infty}$, $d_*=\\min_Q\\operatorname{dist}(QL,2\\pi\\mathbb{Z})>0$.\n\nComplex $z$ below is only an auxiliary analytic variable. It is not a physical $U(1)$ link command or an admitted new source. Define $T_{\\mu}(z)$ using $\\exp(-iQ\\int\\mathcal{A}(z))$; in the backward hopping term use the algebraic inverse link, not its complex conjugate. Then $A_M(z)$ is entire, $H_M(z)=\\gamma_5A_M(z)$, and $B_M(z)=H_M(z)^2=\\gamma_5A_M(z)\\gamma_5A_M(z)$. On real $t$ these are exactly the earlier Wilson kernel and $A_M(t)^\\dagger A_M(t)$.\n\nChoose $\\rho>0$ satisfying\n\n$$\n\\rho F < \\min(3\\cdot 10^{-5}, d_{*}/(4608\\pi)).\n$$\n\nFor $|z|\\le\\rho$, $|\\exp(w)-1|\\le|w|\\exp(|w|)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\epsilon_M:=\\lVert H_M(z)-H_M(0)\\rVert\\le 144 F \\rho/M.\n$$\n\nThe factor 144 bounds eight hoppings, $|Q|\\le9$ and $\\exp(9F\\rho/M)<2$. In particular $\\epsilon_M\\le0.00144$ and $\\epsilon_M\\le d_*/(32\\pi M)\\le s_M/8$, using the previously proved $s_M\\ge d_*/(4\\pi M)$.\n\nThe Hermitian $H_M(0)$ has gap at least 1. Its imaginary-axis resolvent is bounded by $(1+y^2)^{-1/2}$. The Neumann resolvent identity therefore gives\n\n$$\n\\lVert(H_M(z)-iy)^{-1}\\rVert\\le[(1-\\epsilon_M)\\sqrt{1+y^2}]^{-1}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix sign defined by the imaginary-axis contour is holomorphic throughout this disk. Subtracting its resolvent integral from the base integral yields\n\n$$\n\\lVert\\operatorname{sign}H_M(z)-\\operatorname{sign}H_M(0)\\rVert\\le\\epsilon_M/(1-\\epsilon_M).\n$$\n\nEquivalently $\\operatorname{sign}H=H(H^2)^{-1/2}$ on the same spectral branch. Thus $D_M(z)=I+\\gamma_5\\operatorname{sign}H_M(z)$ is holomorphic and $\\lVert D_M(z)-D_M(0)\\rVert<s_M/4$. The physical inverse exists holomorphically and obeys the conservative bound\n\n$$\n\\lVert(D_M(z)/a)^{-1}\\rVert\\le 8\\pi\\ell/d_*.\n$$\n\nAlso $\\lVert H_M(0)\\rVert\\le 11$ and\n\n$$\n\\lVert B_M(z)-B_M(0)\\rVert\\le(22+\\epsilon_M)\\epsilon_M<0.04.\n$$\n\nConsequently $\\operatorname{Re}B_M(z)\\ge 0.96I$ and, conservatively, $\\operatorname{Re}B_M(z)\\ge 0.9I$. For $s\\ge 0$,\n\n$$\n\\lVert(B_M(z)+s)^{-1}\\rVert\\le(0.9+s)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nThis coercive estimate justifies the principal inverse square root and all of the following integral manipulations without treating a non-Hermitian matrix as Hermitian.\n\n## 2. Complex smooth-vector consistency\n\nFor each fixed smooth spinor $\\psi$, the same exact-link Taylor expansion gives, uniformly for $|z|\\le\\rho$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\lVert(I+A_M(z))J_M\\psi-aJ_MK_Q(z)\\psi\\rVert\\le c_1(\\psi)a^2, \\\\\n\\lVert(B_M(z)-I)J_M\\psi\\rVert\\le c_2(\\psi)a^2.\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nHere $K_Q(z)=(1/\\ell)\\sum\\gamma_{\\mu}(\\partial_{y_{\\mu}}-iQA_{\\mu}(z))$. The second cancellation uses $\\gamma_5K_Q(z)\\gamma_5=-K_Q(z)$, not anti-Hermiticity at complex $z$: the first-order terms in $\\gamma_5A(z)\\gamma_5A(z)$ cancel algebraically. Smooth coefficients and their derivatives are uniformly bounded on this fixed compact disk.\n\nThe resolvent integral for $B^{-1/2}$ gives\n\n$$\nB^{-1/2}-I=(1/\\pi) \\int_0^\\infty s^{-1/2}[(B+s)^{-1}-(1+s)^{-1}I]\\, ds.\n$$\n\nUsing the resolvent identity and the previous coercive bound,\n\n$$\n\\lVert(B^{-1/2}-I)J_M\\psi\\rVert\\le[\\sqrt{0.9}(1+\\sqrt{0.9})]^{-1}\\lVert(B-I)J_M\\psi\\rVert.\n$$\n\nSince $\\lVert A_M(z)\\rVert<12$, the earlier smooth-vector consistency estimate therefore holds uniformly on the complex disk:\n\n$$\n\\lVert(D_M(z)/a)J_M\\psi-J_MK_Q(z)\\psi\\rVert\\le C(\\psi)a.\n$$\n\nThe continuum Neumann-series inverse $K_Q(z)^{-1}$ exists uniformly on this disk, with norm $\\le 2\\ell/d_*$. The Fourier Sobolev bootstrap is unchanged for complex $z$ because smooth multiplication remains bounded; it gives uniform smooth bounds for $\\psi_z=K_Q(z)^{-1}h$ with any fixed smooth source $h$. Therefore\n\n$$\nE_{M,h}(z):=(D_M(z)/a)^{-1}J_M h-J_M K_Q(z)^{-1}h\n$$\n\nis a holomorphic vector-valued function with $\\lVert E_{M,h}(z)\\rVert\\le C_ha$ throughout $|z|\\le\\rho$. The constant is cutoff independent and depends on finitely many source/bump derivatives, $\\ell$ and $d_*$.\n\n## 3. Actual derivative convergence\n\nFor real $|t|\\le\\rho/4$ the circle $|z-t|=\\rho/2$ lies inside the established disk. Cauchy's formula gives, for every fixed integer $r\\ge0$,\n\n$$\n\\lVert\\partial_t^rE_{M,h}(t)\\rVert\\le r!\\,(2/\\rho)^rC_ha.\n$$\n\nThis independently establishes derivative convergence. The tiny permitted $\\rho$ can make the constants large; it is a rigorous bounded interval, not a numerical-efficiency assertion.\n\nFor fixed smooth test $g$, the scalar pairing with $J_{M}g$ inherits the same $O(a)$ derivative bound. The continuum/discrete quadrature discrepancy also has an $O(a)$ holomorphic bound on the disk, hence the same Cauchy argument controls its derivatives. Finite Wick determinants and finite products are holomorphic combinations of a fixed number of these uniformly bounded pairings; their derivatives converge by Cauchy's formula as well. The chiral projector on the source is the fixed $P_+$, so no varying Berezin-frame phase enters these normalized ambient responses.\n\nThe first derivative is explicitly\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\partial_tG_Q(t)=-K_Q(t)^{-1}V'_QK_Q(t)^{-1}P_+, \\\\\nV'_Q=-(iQ/\\ell)\\gamma_1f(y_2).\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nAt finite cutoff, $\\partial_tG_M=-(D_M/a)^{-1}(\\partial_tD_M/a)(D_M/a)^{-1}P_+$, with the derivative of the sign/regulator included. The proof establishes convergence of this entire insertion, not just of its formal nearest-neighbor part.\n\nFor smooth $h$ and $g$ put $\\psi=K_Q(t)^{-1}P_+h$ and $\\chi=K_Q(t)^{-*}g$. Then\n\n$$\n\\partial_t \\langle g,G_Q(t)h\\rangle = (iQ/\\ell) \\int \\chi^\\dagger \\gamma_1 f(y_2) \\psi\\, d^4x.\n$$\n\nThis is a finite smooth-source current-profile response to the actual nonflat perturbation. It is not the coincident-point trace of a vacuum current. The factors and physical length unit are explicit; Q is the representation charge and the accepted event phase L is unchanged.\n\n## 4. Ward and region-flux content, with limits\n\nThe family includes its gauge orbits. For a smooth real periodic $\\lambda$, let $g_s=\\exp(iQs\\lambda)$. Both finite and continuum operators transform by conjugation, with all source endpoint frames retained. Differentiation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\delta_\\lambda K &= iQ[\\lambda,K]=-(iQ/\\ell)\\sum \\gamma_\\mu \\partial_{y_\\mu} \\lambda, \\\\\n\\delta_\\lambda G &= iQ[\\lambda,G].\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse-insertion identity agrees exactly with this commutator. Matrix elements give the standard source-contact Ward identity: variation of the operator response equals the difference of the gauge variations at the two external source insertions. Sampling a fixed smooth $\\lambda$ and using the normalized fixed-test convergence yields its continuum version. This is a separate exact gauge identity, not an inference from the transverse t derivative. A gauge change that changes source vertex frames must transform those frames rather than pretending their numerical edge values remain fixed.\n\nFor the smooth solutions $\\psi=K^{-1}h_+$ and $\\chi=K^{-*}g$, define $j_{\\mu}=\\chi^\\dagger\\gamma_{\\mu}\\psi$ in physical $x$ coordinates. At real background, $K$ is anti-Hermitian and $K^\\dagger\\chi=g$, so direct product differentiation gives\n\n$$\n\\partial_{x_\\mu} j_\\mu = \\chi^{\\dagger} h_{+} - g^{\\dagger} \\psi.\n$$\n\nThe $U(1)$ connection terms cancel in this neutral bilinear. Thus for any smooth region $R$ with smooth boundary the ordinary divergence theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\int_{\\partial R} n_\\mu j_\\mu\\, d\\Sigma = \\int_R (\\chi^\\dagger h_{+}-g^\\dagger \\psi)\\, d^4x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying by the same charge/i conventions recovers the corresponding insertion-current balance. This proves a mathematical continuum region balance for these smooth source solutions. It does not prove that the old native detector/source apparatus implements that flux, nor a Lorentzian probability/charge current for physical states.\n\nAt finite cutoff the exact gauge Ward identity can be summed over a vertex region. With the overlap operator, its commutator has entries linking every pair across the region split; it is not automatically the original nearest-edge d4/d6 flux. Calling that nonlocal cross-region sum a local boundary instrument would require an additional map. Sharp region indicators are also not the fixed smooth tests used in the convergence theorem; no uniform sharp-boundary limit has been shown. Vacuum loop insertions require an ultraviolet trace and are not covered by the two smooth external solutions above. Exterior boundary conditions, determinant inflow, and the original physical source/region Ward compatibility remain separate.\n\n## 1. Inputs and notation\n\nUse the actual curved family $\\mathcal{A}(t)=L\\,dy_0+t f(y_2)\\,dy_1$, inherited toron, comb tree, all ten charges and exact real-axis frames from the preceding constructions. Use the uniform complex-neighborhood, normalized-response derivative and Ward estimates just proved in this theorem. In particular choose $\\rho$ with $\\rho F<\\min(3\\cdot 10^{-5},d_*/(4608\\pi))$. For $|z|\\le\\rho$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD(z) &= I+\\gamma_5 \\operatorname{sign} H(z),\\quad P(z)=(I+\\operatorname{sign} H(z))/2, \\\\\nD(z)P(z) &= P_+ D(z), \\\\\n\\lVert D(z)-D(0)\\rVert &< s_M/4, \\\\\n\\lVert P(z)-P(0)\\rVert &\\le \\epsilon_M/[2(1-\\epsilon_M)],\\quad \\epsilon_M=144 F \\rho/M.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe analytic sign is defined by the same fixed spectral split; $P(z)^2=P(z)$ although $P(z)$ need not be Hermitian at nonreal $z$. Its rank is constant on the disk. At $z=0$ take the exact ordered orthonormal frames $E_0$ of $\\operatorname{ran}P(0)$ and $F_0$ of $\\operatorname{ran}P_+$ inherited from the fixed determinant convention. Their charge/flavour/momentum order is unchanged. Let $C_0=F_0^\\dagger D(0)E_0$. Work blockwise or in the fixed direct sum, with total Weyl dimension $n_M=20M^4$.\n\nThe adjoints on E0 and F0 below refer to fixed matrices; they do not conjugate z.\n\n## 2. Holomorphic frame and correct dual\n\nSet\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nG(z)&=E_0^{\\dagger} P(z)E_0, \\\\\nE(z)&=P(z)E_0 G(z)^{-1/2}, \\\\\nE^{\\sharp}(z)&=G(z)^{-1/2}E_0^{\\dagger} P(z).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $\\lVert G-I\\rVert\\le\\lVert P-P_0\\rVert<1$, the principal inverse square root is given by its convergent binomial series about $I$ and is holomorphic on the common disk. Direct multiplication, using $P^2=P$ and $G$ commuting with its own square root, gives\n\n$$\nE^\\sharp E=I,\\quad EE^\\sharp=P.\n$$\n\nFor the second identity the product is an idempotent with range ran P and acts as identity there: P E0 is a basis of that range, since G is invertible. Thus Esharp is exactly the coordinate inverse on the moving Weyl space.\n\nFor real $t$, $P(t)$ and $G(t)$ are Hermitian, $G(t)>0$, and $E^\\sharp(t)=E(t)^\\dagger$. Hence $E(z)$ is the holomorphic continuation of the ORIGINAL real orthonormal polar frame, not a new frame prescription. At complex $z$, $E^\\sharp(z)$ is generally NOT $E(z)^\\dagger$. Replacing it by that adjoint would destroy holomorphy and is forbidden.\n\nThese formulas also give bounds $\\lVert E\\rVert,\\lVert E^\\sharp\\rVert\\le(1+d)/\\sqrt{1-d}$, $d=\\lVert P-P_0\\rVert$, independent of cutoff on the disk. The dual formula fixes the continued coordinate contractions; the analytically continued Grassmann sources use it, not a complex-conjugated source frame.\n\n## 3. Invertibility and exact relative phase formula\n\nDefine the actual continued Weyl matrix\n\n$$\nC(z)=F_0^{\\dagger} D(z)E(z).\n$$\n\nIt maps between fixed coordinate spaces, is holomorphic, and agrees with the original phase-fixed real matrix. Since $D(z)$ is invertible and intertwines the two chiral spaces, $C$ is invertible with\n\n$$\nC(z)^{-1}=E^{\\sharp}(z)D(z)^{-1}F_0.\n$$\n\nThis gives a uniform physical-unit inverse bound after multiplying by a, using the already proved full D inverse estimate. No frame-invertibility inference is based on a non-Hermitian orthogonality assertion.\n\nNow put $W(z)=F_0^\\dagger D(z)E_0$. Because $F_0^\\dagger D(z)P(z)=F_0^\\dagger D(z)$,\n\n$$\nC(z)=W(z)G(z)^{-1/2}.\n$$\n\nAt $z=0$, $G=I$ and $W=C_0$. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(z)&=[W(z)-C_0]C_0^{-1}, \\\\\n\\lVert R(z)\\rVert&\\le \\lVert D(z)-D(0)\\rVert/s_M<1/4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth $\\operatorname{Log}(I+R)$ and $\\operatorname{Log}G$ have their principal power-series branches on the same cutoff-independent disk. Define\n\n$$\nL_M(z)=\\operatorname{Tr} \\operatorname{Log}(I+R(z))-(1/2)\\operatorname{Tr} \\operatorname{Log} G(z).\n$$\n\nThen $L_M(0)=0$ and the EXACT identity is\n\n$$\n\\det C(z)/\\det C_0=\\exp L_M(z).\n$$\n\nThis is the original relative determinant including its phase. On the real axis $G(t)>0$, so the Gram factor is positive real; it does not rotate the inherited phase. The complex logarithm formula nevertheless retains its full analytic contribution. The baseline $\\det C_0$ remains the inherited finite determinant, with its original frame convention and source normalization; no continuum baseline is selected by this formula.\n\nA useful equivalent fixed-frame expression follows from $D(0)^{-1}F_0=E_0C_0^{-1}$:\n\n$$\nR(z)=F_0^{\\dagger} [D(z)-D(0)]D(0)^{-1}F_0.\n$$\n\nThus the remaining trace problem involves an actual fixed-output-frame overlap-relative operator. It is not a newly assumed linear continuum perturbation.\n\n## 4. Stronger Gram estimate and fifth-derivative bound\n\nLet $\\Delta=P(z)-P_0$. Projector algebra gives\n\n$$\nE_0^{\\dagger} \\Delta^2 E_0=I-G(z),\n$$\n\nhence $\\lVert G-I\\rVert\\le\\lVert\\Delta\\rVert^2$. This estimate is valid for complex idempotents; it does not use $\\Delta$ being Hermitian.\n\nThe Gram construction does not require the matter operator $D(z)$ to be invertible. It only requires the Wilson sign split and the small projector chart. Consequently it extends to a much larger auxiliary disk whose radius grows with $M$. Set $r=1/(10000F)$. For $|z|\\le rM$, the same link estimate gives $\\epsilon\\le 144Fr=0.0144$ and\n\n$$\n\\delta P:=\\lVert P(z)-P_0\\rVert\\le 0.0144/[2(1-0.0144)]=:d_{\\mathrm{big}}<0.008.\n$$\n\nThus $\\lVert G-I\\rVert\\le d_{\\mathrm{big}}^2=:b<1$ throughout this disk. The principal $\\operatorname{Log}G$ is holomorphic there and\n\n$$\n\\lvert\\operatorname{Tr}\\operatorname{Log}G(z)\\rvert\\le n_M[-\\log(1-b)].\n$$\n\nFor real $|t|\\le\\rho/4$, the circle $|z-t|=rM/2$ is contained in $|z|\\le rM$ for every $M\\ge3$, by the displayed choices of $\\rho$ and $r$. Cauchy's estimate yields\n\n$$\n\\lvert\\partial_t^5\\operatorname{Tr}\\operatorname{Log}G(t)\\rvert\\le 5!\\,(2/(rM))^5n_M[-\\log(1-b)] =[5!\\cdot 2^5\\cdot 20\\cdot(-\\log(1-b))/r^5]/M.\n$$\n\nThis is a cutoff-uniform $O(1/M)$ estimate for the ENTIRE ten-flavour Gram contribution, not just its individual matrix entries. The constant is deliberately crude and potentially enormous. The same method gives $O(M^{4-k})$ for fixed derivative order $k>4$. Lower derivatives are not shown to vanish and cannot be discarded from lower-order normalization terms.\n\nThe large complex disk is auxiliary only. No physical nonunitary background source, determinant log domain on that larger disk, or matter inverse there is asserted. Only the Wilson sign/projector/Gram functions are used for this bound.\n\n## 1. An explicit chiral, relative continuum construction\n\nFor one charge, restrict $K_Q(0)^{-1}V'_Q$ to the left-handed Hilbert space $H_-$; $V'_Q$ takes left to right and the inverse takes right to left. Put $T_Q=K_Q(0)^{-1}V'_Q|_{H_-}$. This is not the paired vector-like determinant. Let $T$ be the direct sum over the ten left-handed flavours, preserving the previously specified order.\n\nThe flat inverse has two chiral singular values $\\ell/|2\\pi k-QLe_0|$ for each Fourier label $k$ in $\\mathbb{Z}^4$. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\lVert K_Q(0)^{-1}\\vert_{H_+}\\rVert_5^5=2\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb{Z}^4}\\ell^5/\\lvert 2\\pi k-QLe_0\\rvert^5<\\infty.\n$$\n\nThe finite shifted low modes are gapped by $d_*$. For large $|k|$ the summand is $O(|k|^{-5})$; four-dimensional shells have $O(r^3)$ points, and $\\sum r^{-2}$ converges. Bounded multiplication by $V'_Q$ preserves Schatten class $S_{5}$. Thus $T$ belongs to $S_{5}$, with a bound obtained from this explicit Fourier sum and $\\lVert V'_Q\\rVert\\le|Q|F/\\ell$. No pseudodifferential asymptotic or unexplained determinant measure is required.\n\nFor $|t|\\,\\lVert T\\rVert<1$ define\n\n$$\n\\log Z_5(t)=\\sum_{n=5}^{\\infty} [(-1)^{n+1}t^n/n] \\operatorname{Tr}(T^n),\\quad Z_5(t)=\\exp(\\log Z_5(t)).\n$$\n\nSchatten Holder gives $\\lVert T^{5}\\rVert_1\\le\\lVert T\\rVert_5^{5}$ and $|\\operatorname{Tr}T^{n}|\\le\\lVert T\\rVert_5^{5}\\lVert T\\rVert^{n-5}$ for $n\\ge5$. Hence the series converges absolutely and uniformly on smaller disks. Its tail after $N\\ge 5$ obeys\n\n$$\n\\lvert\\mathrm{tail}_N\\rvert\\le\\lvert t\\rvert^5\\lVert T\\rVert_5^5(\\lvert t\\rvert\\,\\lVert T\\rVert)^{N-4}/[(N+1)(1-\\lvert t\\rvert\\,\\lVert T\\rVert)].\n$$\n\nThis specifies a nonzero holomorphic relative determinant, including its phase, with $Z_5(0)=1$ and a fixed logarithm. It is the fifth regularized Fredholm determinant $\\det_5(I+tT)$. The index five is a sufficient first integer above the generic four-dimensional order-one summability threshold; no claim is made that every special bump trace requires all four subtractions.\n\nSee Britz et al. (2020, section 1, equation (1.3)). It defines the higher regularized determinant for Schatten-class operators and records its continuity. The source also warns that ordinary determinant multiplicativity acquires trace-polynomial correction terms. We therefore do not identify its composition law with the uncorrected finite Schur source law.\n\n## 2. Controlled finite approximation of this target, distinct from overlap cutoffs\n\nLet $P_{N}$ be a finite Fourier projection tending strongly to identity and $T_N=P_NTP_{N}$. The $S_5$-norm difference tends to zero. To see the nontrivial two-sided point explicitly: approximate $T$ in $S_{5}$ by a finite-rank operator; strong convergence of $P_{N}$ is uniform on that finite-dimensional range and co-range, while projection contraction bounds the $S_{5}$ remainder. This proves convergence without claiming an unsupported rate for the right-projected multiplication tail.\n\nFor the logarithmic series, expand $T_N^n-T^n$ into $n$ terms. Schatten Holder bounds the first five factors in $S_{5}$ and the remaining $n-5$ factors in operator norm. Uniformly bounded $S_{5}$ norms and $\\lVert tT_N\\rVert\\le\\lVert tT\\rVert<1$ give a summable telescoping majorant, proportional to $\\lVert T_N-T\\rVert_5$. Thus $\\log\\det_5(I+tT_N)\\to\\log Z_5(t)$, uniformly on smaller disks and with derivative convergence by Cauchy's formula.\n\nEach finite approximation has the explicit identity\n\n$$\n\\det_5(I+tT_N)=\\det(I+tT_N) \\exp(\\sum_{j=1}^4 (-1)^j t^j \\operatorname{Tr}(T_N^j)/j).\n$$\n\nAll finite trace terms, including their complex phases, are retained. This is a constructed, convergent relative normalization on a continuum Fourier regulator. It is NOT yet the existing overlap/projector-chart regulator, nor an admitted native source.\n\n## 1. Exact operator identity and orientation\n\nUse the previous accepted analytic-frame/log notation, including the algebraic dual and common disk $|z|\\le\\rho$. Put $a=\\ell/M$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB_M &= (D_M(0)/a)^{-1}P_+, \\\\\nU_M(z) &= [D_M(z)-D_M(0)]/a.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the fixed output frame, the actual relative operator is exactly\n\n$$\nR_M(z)=F_0^{\\dagger} U_M(z) B_M F_0.\n$$\n\nOne may express F0 in the standard Pplus Fourier coordinates by a fixed unitary change of basis, which preserves all trace powers and the relative determinant. No moving input frame or new determinant phase is introduced by this representation.\n\nLet $H_+$ be the continuum right-handed Hilbert space and $B=K(0)^{-1}P_+$. Let $V=\\partial_zK(z)=-(iQ/\\ell)\\gamma_1f(y_2)$ on each charge, with the fixed ten-flavour direct sum. The target operator on $H_+$ is\n\n$$\nR(z)=z P_+ V B|_{H_+}=z V K(0)^{-1}|_{H_+}.\n$$\n\nThis is the RIGHT-space orientation. The preceding target note used $T_-=K(0)^{-1}V$ on $H_-$. The two regularized determinants agree: for every $n\\ge 5$ the trace-class products have $\\operatorname{Tr}_{H_+}(VK_0^{-1})^n=\\operatorname{Tr}_{H_-}(K_0^{-1}V)^n$ by cyclicity, or equivalently the regularized $AB/BA$ identity. Thus the limit below is exactly the previously nominated chiral $\\det_5$, not its inverse or conjugate.\n\n## 2. Common Fourier embedding and the required free tail\n\nIdentify each finite lattice Hilbert space isometrically with the trigonometric polynomials on the side-$\\ell$ torus whose labels lie in the usual centered Brillouin cube $K_{M}$. Let $I_{M}$ denote this interpolation isometry and extend embedded finite operators by zero on the orthogonal complement. A fixed smooth sampled vector $J_M\\psi$ becomes $I_MJ_M\\psi$, which converges to $\\psi$ in $L^2$. This is the ordinary Fourier alias sum: sufficiently many decaying Fourier coefficients make its tail and aliases summable by Cauchy-Schwarz. It is uniform for families with uniformly bounded sufficiently high Sobolev norms, as supplied by the earlier consistency proof. It is NOT an isometry claim for sampling arbitrary $L^2$ functions.\n\nAt the flat background, $B_{M}$ is diagonal in momentum labels. Its two nonzero singular values per charge/label are $a/s_Q(p)$, where $p=(2\\pi k-QLe_0)/M$ modulo $2\\pi$ and $s_{Q}$ is the overlap singular value. Let $k$ be the centered integer representative. The torus metric inequality gives\n\n$$\nM |p_{\\mathrm{principal}}| \\ge 2\\pi |k|-|QL|.\n$$\n\nSince $\\max|QL|=9\\ln 2<2\\pi$, for $|k|\\ge 2$ the right-hand side is at least $\\pi|k|$. If the Wilson scalar $W<0$, the established bound $s\\ge|p_{\\mathrm{principal}}|/(4\\pi)$ gives $a/s\\le 4\\ell/|k|$. If $W\\ge 0$, $s\\ge\\sqrt{2}$, hence $a/s\\le\\ell/(\\sqrt{2}M)\\le\\ell/(\\sqrt{2}|k|)$, because $|k|\\le M$ in the four-dimensional centered cube. For the finitely many $|k|<2$, the established $s_M\\ge d_*/(4\\pi M)$ gives $a/s\\le 4\\pi\\ell/d_*$.\n\nTherefore one cutoff-independent constant, for example\n\n$$\nC_B=\\max(6\\ell,12\\pi\\ell/d_*),\n$$\n\nsatisfies $a/s_Q(p)\\le C_B/(1+|k|)$ for every $M\\ge3$ and every label. The continuum inverse has the same bound. This is the explicit ultraviolet estimate missing from strong fixed-test convergence.\n\nFor the Fourier projection $P_{N}$ onto $|k|\\le N$, $N\\ge4$, shell counting gives\n\n$$\n\\lVert B_M(I-P_N)\\rVert_5^5,\\lVert B(I-P_N)\\rVert_5^5\\le 6400C_B^5/N.\n$$\n\nHere the ten flavours and two chiral singular values are included. One conservative count uses max-norm shells, with $(2m+1)^4-(2m-1)^4=64m^3+16m\\le 80m^3$; $|k|>N$ implies $m>N/2$ and the remaining sum is bounded by a multiple of $\\sum m^{-2}$. The displayed constant deliberately over-reserves that bound.\n\nOn each fixed finite set of momenta, the explicit free symbol converges to $K(0)^{-1}P_+$, including its full matrix orientation. Hence\n\n$$\n\\lVert B_M-B\\rVert_5\\le\\epsilon_B(M,N)+2(6400C_B^5/N)^{1/5},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\epsilon_B(M,N)=\\lVert(B_M-B)P_N\\rVert_5$ is a finite, explicitly evaluable Fourier-symbol sum and tends to zero for each fixed $N$. This is genuine $S_5$ convergence with a two-cutoff error bound.\n\nFor later use, the same symbol estimate gives, for $j=1,2,3,4$,\n\n$$\n\\lVert B_M\\rVert_j^j\\le 20C_B^j[1+80\\sum_{m=1}^{\\lceil M/2\\rceil}m^3/(1+m)^j].\n$$\n\nThus these quantities are $O(M^3)$, $O(M^2)$, $O(M)$, $O(1+\\log M)$, respectively. They are not uniformly trace-class or uniformly $S_4$. Those divergences must be carried in the nonlinear analysis.\n\n## 3. S5 convergence of the actual relative operator\n\nThe common-disk sign estimate gives $\\lVert U_M(z)\\rVert\\le C_U$ uniformly in $M$ and $|z|\\le\\rho$; one can take $C_U=144F\\rho/[\\ell(1-0.00144)]$. The continuum $\\lVert zV\\rVert$ is bounded by the same conservative constant.\n\nFor each fixed smooth spinor $\\psi$, the accepted complex consistency theorem applied to $D_M(z)$ and $D_M(0)$ gives\n\n$$\nI_M U_M(z)J_M \\psi \\to zV \\psi\n$$\n\nin $L^2$, uniformly on $|z|\\le\\rho$. Sampling/interpolation errors are controlled by the uniformly smooth coefficient family. For a fixed trigonometric polynomial, $J_{M}$ and the Fourier coordinates agree once $M$ is large. Density and the uniform operator bound then give strong convergence of embedded $U_M(z)$ to $zV$, uniformly on each fixed vector over this disk. No ultraviolet norm convergence is claimed.\n\nNow split the product using a fixed finite Fourier projection $P_{N}$:\n\n$$\n\\lVert U_MB_M-zVB\\rVert_5 \\le C_U\\lVert B_M-B\\rVert_5 +\\lVert(U_M-zV)BP_N\\rVert_5 +2C_U\\lVert B(I-P_N)\\rVert_5.\n$$\n\nThe middle term tends uniformly to zero because $BP_{N}$ has finite rank and smooth Fourier columns. For a quantitative bound it is at most its Hilbert-Schmidt norm, obtained by summing the finitely many squared consistency/interpolation errors on those columns; call this explicitly bounded finite quantity $\\epsilon_U(M,N)$. It is $O(a)$ for fixed $N$ with constants from the already established stencil estimates and Fourier alias bounds. The other two terms are controlled by section 2.\n\nThus, after compression to H_+,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sup_{|z|\\le \\rho} \\lVert R_M(z)-zT_+\\rVert_5 \\\\\n&\\le C_U \\epsilon_B(M,N)+\\epsilon_U(M,N)+4 C_U(6400 C_B^5/N)^{1/5} \\\\\n&=:\\delta_5(M,N).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst choose N to control the displayed tail, then M to control the finite-symbol/stencil quantities. This proves uniform S5 convergence constructively. All norms here are on the common Fourier Hilbert spaces; a changing-dimensional matrix norm is not silently equated to an infinite-dimensional one.\n\n## 4. The regularized trace-log part converges\n\nThe actual relative-log lemma gives $\\lVert R_M(z)\\rVert<1/4$ on the disk. The same smallness holds for $zT_+$ by its continuum inverse/multiplication bound. The $S_5$ norms are uniformly bounded by section 3. Define\n\n$$\nH_M(z)=\\sum_{n=5}^{\\infty} (-1)^{n+1} \\operatorname{Tr}(R_M(z)^n)/n.\n$$\n\nIts series converges normally and starts at degree at least five because $R_M(0)=0$. Let $B_5$ bound the $S_5$ norms of $R_{M}$ and $zT_+$, and let $\\kappa<1$ bound their operator norms. Expanding $R_M^n-(zT_+)^n$ into $n$ telescoping terms and applying Schatten Holder with five $S_5$ factors gives\n\n$$\n\\lvert H_M(z)-\\log Z_5(z)\\rvert\\le[B_5^4/(1-\\kappa)]\\lVert R_M(z)-zT_+\\rVert_5.\n$$\n\nIndeed each term contains the S5 difference, four other S5 factors and n-5 operator-norm factors; the n terms cancel the denominator n. The preceding $\\delta_{5}$ bound therefore controls this regularized part uniformly.\n\nThis is only part of the ordinary actual trace-log. The four lower powers cannot be dropped until their nonlinear Taylor remainders have been estimated.\n\n## 5. Nonlinear vertex suppression with the divergent lower ideals retained\n\nUse the auxiliary Wilson-sign disk $|z|\\le rM$, $r=1/(10000F)$, established in the analytic-frame lemma. No matter inverse on that large disk is needed. On it $\\lVert D_M(z)-D_M(0)\\rVert$ is bounded by a cutoff-independent constant $A_*=0.0144/(1-0.0144)$. Cauchy's coefficient bound yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_M(z)-D_M(0)&=\\sum_{k\\ge 1} z^k D_{k,M}, \\\\\n\\lVert D_{k,M}\\rVert&\\le A_{*}/(rM)^k.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the coefficients of $U_{M}$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\lVert U_{k,M}\\rVert\\le A_*r^{-k}M^{1-k}/\\ell.\n$$\n\nThis is the actual nonlinear overlap-vertex estimate. It is not an assumption that the lattice action is linear in z.\n\nFor $j$ in $\\{1,2,3,4\\}$, write $Q_{j,M}(z)=\\operatorname{Tr}R_M(z)^j$. The coefficient of $z^n$, $n\\ge j$, is a sum over $\\binom{n-1}{j-1}$ ordered compositions $k_1+\\dots+k_j=n$. Each factor is a bounded $U_{k_i,M}$ times $B_{M}$, compressed to $H_+$. Schatten Holder in $S_{j}$, including the $j=1$ trace-class case, gives\n\n$$\n\\lvert[z^n]Q_{j,M}\\rvert \\le\\binom{n-1}{j-1}(A_*/\\ell)^jr^{-n}M^{j-n}\\lVert B_M\\rVert_j^j.\n$$\n\nThe growth estimates in section 2 now give a uniform coefficient bound of the form\n\n$$\n\\lvert[z^n]Q_{j,M}\\rvert\\le C_j\\binom{n-1}{j-1}r^{-n}M^{4-n}(1+\\log M),\n$$\n\nwhere for $j\\le 3$ the logarithm can be omitted. This retains exactly the ultraviolet growth which a bare operator-norm argument would miss.\n\nLet $T_4$ denote the Taylor polynomial through degree four at zero. For $|z|\\le\\rho$, sum the $n\\ge5$ coefficients. Since $\\rho/(rM)<1/2$ for every $M\\ge3$, the binomial-series tail is bounded by a constant depending only on $j$ and $\\rho/r$. Consequently\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sup_{|z|\\le \\rho} |Q_{j,M}(z)-T_4 Q_{j,M}(z)| \\\\\n&\\le C'_j (1+\\log M)/M.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bound is explicit via the displayed shell sum and geometric/binomial series; no cancellation between charges is required. This proves suppression of the nonlinear contribution of the lower trace powers ABOVE fourth order. Their degree $\\le 4$ coefficients need not converge and remain retained actual finite subtractions.\n\n## 6. The actual chart determinant now matches the relative target\n\nThe accepted exact phase formula is\n\n$$\nL_M(z)=\\operatorname{Tr} \\operatorname{Log}(I+R_M(z))-(1/2)\\operatorname{Tr} \\operatorname{Log} G_M(z),\\quad L_M(0)=0.\n$$\n\nWrite $F_M=L_M-T_4L_M$. Since $H_{M}$ begins in degree five,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_M&=H_M+\\sum_{j=1}^4 (-1)^{j+1}[Q_{j,M}-T_4 Q_{j,M}]/j \\\\\n&\\quad -(1/2)[\\operatorname{Tr} \\operatorname{Log} G_M-T_4 \\operatorname{Tr} \\operatorname{Log} G_M].\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Gram lemma, or its large-disk Taylor coefficient bound, gives an $O(1/M)$ bound for the last bracket on the common disk. Combining sections 3–5 proves\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sup_{|z|\\le \\rho} |F_M(z)-\\log Z_5(z)| \\\\\n&\\le [B_5^4/(1-\\kappa)] \\delta_5(M,N) \\\\\n&\\quad + C_{\\mathrm{nl}}(1+\\log M)/M + C_{\\mathrm{Gram}}/M.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe constants and finite error terms are supplied by the preceding formulas. Taking N then M proves uniform convergence. On any smaller disk, Cauchy's formula gives convergence of every fixed derivative, in particular the previously missing fifth logarithmic derivative. This is a theorem about the actual overlap measure convention, including its analytic frame phase, not just a continuum Fourier regulator substituted in its place.\n\nExponentiation yields the exact finite subtracted relative response\n\n$$\n[Z_M(z)/Z_M(0)] \\exp(-\\sum_{j=1}^4 L_M^{(j)}(0) z^j/j!) \\to Z_5(z).\n$$\n\nThe baseline $Z_M(0)$, the four actual Taylor coefficients, high-mode factors and all phase choices remain recorded. The limiting relative phase is fixed by the common log branch and $Z_5(0)=1$. No statement is made that $Z_{M}$ itself has a finite nonzero absolute continuum limit or that the original normalized determinant instrument stops collapsing without these explicit factors.\n\n## 1. An exact t -> -t transformation of the actual source family\n\nUse $\\mathcal{A}(t)=L\\,dy_0+t f(y_2)\\,dy_1$, with $f(0)=0$, temporal toron $L=\\ln 2$, and the inherited lattice/operator conventions. Let $r$ act on coordinates by\n\n$$\nr(y_0,y_1,y_2,y_3)=(y_0,-y_1,y_2,-y_3),\n$$\n\nwith periodic coordinates understood. Let $S=\\gamma_1\\gamma_3$, a unitary spin rotation with $S\\gamma_1S^{-1}=-\\gamma_1$, $S\\gamma_3S^{-1}=-\\gamma_3$, and $S\\gamma_0S^{-1}=\\gamma_0$, $S\\gamma_2S^{-1}=\\gamma_2$. It commutes with $\\gamma_5$. The unitary $U$ combining $r$ with $S$ is a proper two-axis spatial rotation, not a chirality-changing single reflection.\n\nDirect substitution into the covariant shifts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_M(-t)&=U D_M(t) U^{\\dagger}, \\\\\nP_M(-t)&=U P_M(t) U^{\\dagger}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse link appearing under a reversed direction-1 shift sends $t$ to $-t$; $f(y_2)$ is unchanged. The temporal background is unchanged. The source circle lies at $y_1=y_2=y_3=0$ and is fixed pointwise, with its original orientation and event transports retained. This argument is in uniform gauge; the already retained fixed slab-coordinate transformation conjugates $U$ and preserves the same identities there. No new physical spatial-rotation actuator is claimed.\n\nAt $t=0$, $U$ commutes with $D_0$ and $P_0$ and preserves $P_+$. Write $UE_0=E_0A$ and $UF_0=F_0B$, with fixed unitaries $A,B$ in the ordered coordinate spaces. The polar-frame definition then gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nG(-t)&=A G(t) A^{\\dagger}, \\\\\nE(-t)&=U E(t) A^{\\dagger}, \\\\\nC(-t)&=B C(t) A^{\\dagger}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $t=0$, $C_0=BC_0A^\\dagger$ and $C_0$ is invertible, hence $\\det B\\det A^\\dagger=1$. Consequently\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nZ_M(-t)&=Z_M(t), \\\\\nL_M(-t)&=L_M(t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\non the common analytic log disk, where the logarithm is anchored by $L_M(0)=0$. This includes the inherited determinant phase; it is not merely a modulus statement. Thus $c_{1,M}=c_{3,M}=0$ exactly, as do all odd Taylor coefficients.\n\nThe same $U$ preserves the continuum reference $K_0$ and reverses $V$, so it sends $T_+$ to $-T_+$. Trace powers of odd order vanish whenever trace class, including orders $\\ge 5$. The constructed $\\det_5$ logarithm is therefore even and its first possible nonzero term is degree six. This symmetry does not imply $c_2$ or $c_4$ vanishes.\n\n## 2. Exact surviving finite coefficients\n\nWrite the actual fixed-frame and Gram series as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_M(t)&=\\sum_{k\\ge 1} t^k R_{k,M}, \\\\\nG_M(t)&=I+\\sum_{k\\ge 1} t^k G_{k,M}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $R_{k,M}=F_0^\\dagger D_{k,M}D_0^{-1}F_0$. Projector algebra gives $G_{1,M}=0$ and $G_{2,M}=-E_0^\\dagger P_{1,M}^2E_0$. All these are explicitly derived from the actual link exponentials and Wilson sign, not adjustable constants.\n\nExpanding the exact logarithm yields\n\n$$\nc_2=\\operatorname{Tr} R_2 -(1/2)\\operatorname{Tr} R_1^2 -(1/2)\\operatorname{Tr} G_2,\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc_4&=\\operatorname{Tr} R_4-\\operatorname{Tr}(R_1 R_3)-(1/2)\\operatorname{Tr} R_2^2+\\operatorname{Tr}(R_1^2 R_2) \\\\\n&\\quad -(1/4)\\operatorname{Tr} R_1^4 -(1/2)\\operatorname{Tr} G_4+(1/4)\\operatorname{Tr} G_2^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubscripts M are suppressed only in these two lines. Cyclicity, rather than commutativity, combines the ordered products. These formulas retain the nonlinear regulator vertices and the Gram-measure terms. The R1 powers contain the inverse D0 between insertions; they are not pointwise polynomial densities. They include finite-volume propagation as well as ultraviolet contributions.\n\nThe accepted estimates provide conservative growth bounds, not a local decomposition: $c_2$ can be bounded by $O(M^2)$, and $c_4$ by $O(1+\\log M)$, with fixed physical units and source profile. These bounds follow from the same vertex factors and $S_{j}$ sums used in the ultraviolet match; the Gram growing-disk estimate contributes $O(M^2)$ and $O(1)$, respectively. They do not prove these leading terms actually diverge, that gauge cancellations cannot improve them, or that their finite parts are disposable.\n\nThe exact finite replacement leading to the proved relative limit is\n\n$$\nZ_M^{\\mathrm{sub}}(t)=[Z_M(t)/Z_M(0)] \\exp(-c_{2,M} t^2-c_{4,M} t^4).\n$$\n\nIt is even and gauge invariant on the already declared orbit because every ingredient is extracted from the gauge-quotient matrix. This is a well-defined finite functional transformation. It removes ALL quadratic and quartic response along this profile, including any finite parts, not just established divergent local terms.\n\n## 1. Exact split and units; no physical clock inferred\n\nRetain $A=L\\,dy_0+t f(y_2)\\,dy_1$, $L=\\ln 2$, ten left-handed charges $Q=(-9,-5,-1,7,8)$ repeated twice, all four periodic spin directions, physical side $\\ell$, and the prior small real interval. Here $t$ is a background control, never time.\n\nUse $y_3$ as the declared Euclidean-time direction. Spatial coordinates are $(x_0,x_1,x_2)=\\ell(y_0,y_1,y_2)$; $u=x_3=\\ell y_3$ is an auxiliary Euclidean length of period $\\ell$. The Lorentzian completion lives on $\\mathbb{R}_s$ times the spatial three-torus with metric $-c^2ds^2+dx_0^2+dx_1^2+dx_2^2$. The independently calibrated physical clock $s$ is not the periodic coordinate $y_3$. The declared state temperature is fixed by $\\beta_E=\\ell/(\\hbar c)$, with $\\beta_{E}$ in inverse energy units; equivalently $k_BT=\\hbar c/\\ell$. No claim that the old clock already has this calibration is made.\n\nChoose a declared charge unit $q_*$ and $q_Q=Qq_*$. With the existing convention $D=\\partial-iQA$, the physical spatial one-form is\n\n$$\nA_{\\mathrm{phys}}=(\\hbar/q_*)[L\\,dx_0/\\ell+t f(x_2/\\ell)\\,dx_1/\\ell],\\quad \\Phi_{\\mathrm{phys}}=0.\n$$\n\nThen $q_QA_{\\mathrm{phys}}/\\hbar=QA$ exactly. The covariant momentum is $-i\\hbar\\nabla-q_QA_{\\mathrm{phys}}$. For every retained source path of winding $N$ along $x_0$ at the source locus,\n\n$$\n\\exp[(i q_Q/\\hbar) \\int A_{\\mathrm{phys}}]=\\exp(i Q N \\ln 2).\n$$\n\nForward/backward path orientation and the backward links $\\exp(-iQ\\int A)$ retain their original signs. Curvature is $(\\hbar/q_*)\\,t f'(x_2/\\ell)\\,dx_2\\wedge dx_1/\\ell^2$. Thus the event phase is a dimensionless holonomy, not an energy, a clock period or a numerical electric charge. The opposite derivative convention in G.6b is matched by $q_GA_G=-q_QA_{\\mathrm{phys}}/\\hbar$ in its unit-action convention; it cannot be ignored. The physical connection is common to the event channel and the matter Hamiltonian, conditional on the explicit controller/calibration join.\n\nThe signed setup and positive-Hamiltonian word construction at the start of this theorem retain $\\Delta=(\\hbar/\\tau_A)\\ln 2$ and the marked hold $\\tau=\\tau_A$, so $L=\\Delta\\tau_A/\\hbar$. Its source-circle map therefore requires $(q_*/\\hbar)\\int_{\\mathrm{source}}A_{\\mathrm{phys}}=\\Delta\\tau_A/\\hbar$. Neither the Euclidean circumference $\\ell/c$ nor the thermal scale $\\beta_E\\hbar$ is automatically $\\tau_{A}$. Equality $\\ell=c\\tau_A$ is not assumed. Even that nomination does not identify a loop's laboratory duration with its geometric length: the signed-word refinement and complete source reservation proved above retain extra finite-duration $I$/$X$ pulses, refinement overhead and failed/unused stocks. All those clock and energy records survive this join. A fixed spatial Wilson loop is the connection image of the event ledger, not the event apparatus's worldline with its paid timing erased.\n\nAn oriented Lorentzian tetrad and the map from the original $\\gamma$ representation must be retained. We keep the original $P_-$ subspace throughout; renaming axes is not permission to replace it by the other chirality.\n\n## 2. Static minimally coupled Hamiltonian derived from the actual K\n\nUse the given Hermitian Euclidean $\\gamma$ matrices and physical coordinates. On $H_-=L^2(T^3_{\\ell},\\mathbb{C}^2)$ identified with the original $P_-$ space define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB_Q(t) &= \\sum_{j=0,1,2} \\gamma_3 \\gamma_j (\\partial_{x_j}-i Q A_{x_j}), \\\\\n\\alpha_j &= i \\gamma_3 \\gamma_j \\text{ restricted to } P_-, \\\\\nh_Q(t) &= \\hbar c B_Q(t)=c \\sum_j \\alpha_j(-i \\hbar \\partial_{x_j}-q_Q A_{\\mathrm{phys},j}).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $\\alpha_{j}$ are Hermitian Clifford matrices; $\\gamma_3\\gamma_{j}$ and the covariant derivatives are anti-Hermitian, so their commuting product is symmetric. At $t=0$, Fourier decomposition gives eigenvalues of $B$ equal to plus/minus $|2\\pi n-QLe_0|/\\ell$. $B(0)$ is self-adjoint on $H^1$; the added multiplication operator is bounded self-adjoint with norm $\\le|Qt|F/\\ell$. The resolvent Neumann argument for a bounded symmetric perturbation gives the same self-adjoint domain. Its resolvent is compact: the flat inverse has Fourier multipliers tending to zero and the perturbation resolvent identity preserves compactness.\n\nIf $\\delta=(d_*-9|t|F)/\\ell>0$, the spectrum avoids $(-\\delta,\\delta)$. This is now a genuine spatial Hamiltonian gap, proved separately rather than imported from the four-dimensional overlap singular-value bound. The prior interval is far smaller than necessary for $\\delta>0$.\n\nThe actual continuum Weyl operator factorizes as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nK|_- &= \\gamma_3 (\\partial_u+B_Q(t)), \\\\\nG&=K^{-1}P_{+}=(\\partial_u+B_Q(t))^{-1} \\gamma_3 P_{+}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the candidate Lorentzian equation $i\\hbar\\partial_s\\psi=h_Q(t)\\psi$ is minimally coupled to exactly the retained connection. Its local action is $\\int ds\\,d^3x\\,\\psi^\\dagger(i\\hbar\\partial_s-h_Q(t))\\psi$, with its source/current variations, not an unrelated dense-matrix action. This is a background-field matter construction; it does not assert that this arbitrary external $A$ satisfies an unsourced Maxwell equation.\n\n## 1. Common observable algebra is enough for comparison\n\nLet $H$ be the direct sum of the ten original left-handed $L^2(T^3_{\\ell})$ spinor spaces, with the original flavour order and the $y_3$ split of the preceding assessment. At each real control $t$ in the retained small interval,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\nB(t)=B_0+tV,\\quad V_Q=-(Q/\\ell)\\alpha_1f(y_2), \\\\\n\\lVert tV\\rVert\\le v=9t_0F/\\ell,\\quad \\delta=d_*/\\ell-v>0.\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nAll $B(t)$ have common domain $H^1$, are self-adjoint, and have gap $\\delta$. The observable algebra is ONE $\\mathrm{CAR}(H)$: generators $a(f)$, with $\\{a(f),a(g)^*\\}=\\langle f,g\\rangle$. Fields at different controls are compared by the identity on these test labels. Dynamics at control $t$ is the Bogoliubov automorphism induced by $\\exp(-icsB(t))$; a fixed convention for the antilinear generator $a(f)$ fixes the corresponding sign on test evolution. It is not necessary to identify all GNS Hilbert spaces by a vacuum-preserving unitary.\n\nThe positive gauge-invariant quasi-free state is specified by the bounded covariance\n\n$C(t)=(1+\\exp(\\ell B(t)))^{-1}$, $0<C(t)<1$,\n$\\omega_t(a(f)^*a(g))=\\langle g,C(t)f\\rangle$ (inner-product convention fixed accordingly).\n\nOne direct construction takes any finite orthonormal test family, compresses $C$ to it, diagonalizes that finite positive contraction, and assigns independent Bernoulli occupations with its eigenvalues. Its Wick functional is positive on the finite matrix CAR algebra. These finite restrictions are consistent because their two-point functions agree and Wick determinants determine all monomials. Their union is norm dense, so the state extends to $\\mathrm{CAR}(H)$. This derivation does not require a trace density on a common infinite Fock representation.\n\nAt each fixed $t$ it agrees with the positive excitation thermal Fock state already constructed. The same state on $\\mathrm{CAR}(H)$ is comparable across $t$ even if the sea representations are inequivalent. Smooth bounded-perturbation resolvent calculus, or the finite-test inverse constructions below for the exported law, controls parameter dependence. No assertion that the physical controller continuously turns one infinite vacuum into another is included.\n\n## 2. Replace the sharp spectral cutoff by fixed Fourier spaces\n\nTake $P_{N}$ onto spatial Fourier labels $|n_j|\\le N$, with both chiral spin components and all ten flavours. Put $H_N=P_NH$, $D_N=\\dim H_N=20(2N+1)^3$, and\n\n$$\nB_N(t)=P_N B(t) P_N \\text{ on } H_N.\n$$\n\nThese are affine Hermitian matrices on one fixed space, with $\\lVert B_N(t)-B_N(0)\\rVert\\le v$. $B_N(0)$ is a subset of the explicit flat Fourier blocks, so its distance from zero is $\\ge d_*/\\ell$. Consequently every $B_N(t)$ has gap $\\ge\\delta$, without any assumed smooth spectral cutoff or eigenbasis. The negative eigenvalue count is constant in $t$. Each flavour has one negative mode per Fourier label, so total negative count is $10(2N+1)^3$, which is even.\n\nThe CAR inclusions $\\mathrm{CAR}(H_N)\\to\\mathrm{CAR}(H_{N+1})$ preserve the generators exactly. Matrix compression also obeys $P_NB_{N+1}(t)P_N=B_N(t)$. Finite Gibbs states need not restrict exactly to one another: eliminating coupled high modes changes the low covariance. Thus this is a consistent kinematical diagram with a convergent state/response family, not a falsely exact cylinder-measure or Schur pushforward theorem. Regulator inclusion is not event-edge subdivision.\n\nA useful uniform bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb_{\\max,N} &= (2 \\pi \\sqrt{3} N+9 L+9 t_0 F)/\\ell, \\\\\n\\lVert B_N(t)\\rVert &\\le b_{\\max,N}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNo exponentially growing physical register dimension is concealed: its Hilbert space dimension is $2^{D_N}$, even when described by $D_{N}$ modes.\n\n## 3. Explicit bounded preparation, with no chosen eigenvectors\n\nOn the fixed empty-vacuum Fock space $F(H_N)$, define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_N^{\\mathrm{exc}} &= \\hbar c[d\\Gamma(B_N)-\\operatorname{Tr}(B_N P_N^-) I], \\\\\nP_N^- &= 1_{(-\\infty,0)}(B_N).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts excitation spectrum is nonnegative and its norm is $\\le\\hbar cD_Nb_{\\max,N}$. The scalar subtraction is an explicit finite controller energy offset; its background dependence is retained. It does not prove a continuum vacuum-energy normalization. The prepared Gibbs state has covariance $C_N=(1+\\exp(\\ell B_N))^{-1}$. Both $C_{N}$ and $P_N^-$ are smooth matrix functions of real $t$ because the gap persists; degenerate eigenvalues cause no problem.\n\nHere is a finite unitary preparation that does not assume the Gibbs state as an unexplained primitive. Add $D_{N}$ ancillary CAR modes. Start the system modes empty and all ancilla modes filled. Define $\\Theta_N=\\arcsin(\\sqrt{C_N})$, with spectrum strictly between 0 and $\\pi/2$. On system plus ancilla one-particle space use\n\n$$\nU_N=\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos \\Theta_N & -\\sin \\Theta_N\\\\ \\sin \\Theta_N & \\cos \\Theta_N\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe initial covariance $\\operatorname{diag}(0,I)$ is sent to a covariance whose system block is $\\sin^2\\Theta_N=C_N$. Second quantization of $U_{N}$ therefore prepares a purification of the required quasi-free density matrix; trace out or retain the ancilla records. The ancillas are explicitly supplied preparation registers, not an extra propagating right-handed matter representation.\n\n$U_N=\\exp(J_N)$ with $J_N=\\begin{bmatrix} 0 & -\\Theta_N \\\\ \\Theta_N & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$ anti-Hermitian. A finite quadratic control Hamiltonian $i\\hbar\\,d\\Gamma(J_N)/\\tau_p$ implements it in positive nominated time $\\tau_{p}$ (using $\\exp(-iH\\tau_p/\\hbar)$); a scalar shift makes the control Hamiltonian nonnegative. Its unshifted norm is bounded conservatively by $\\hbar D_N\\pi/\\tau_p$, so twice this bound suffices after a positive shift. The common scalar phase, pulse duration, waveform/compiler, control supplier, energy reserve, ancilla retention and failed runs must be charged. Matrix-function evaluation is a specified calibration task, not a new law asserting the desired response. Arbitrarily accurate finite compilation may replace exact controls if its errors are included.\n\n## 4. Actual parity readout and its exact target\n\nLet $\\Gamma_N=(-1)^{\\text{number of all system modes}}$. Because the negative count is even, this equals excitation parity globally; for another finite charge/mode choice one must retain the known factor $(-1)^{\\text{negative count}}$. It cancels from normalized ratios but not raw records.\n\nThe finite source exports\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_{N,t}(O) &= \\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho_N \\Gamma_N O)/d_N(t), \\\\\nd_N(t) &= \\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho_N \\Gamma_N)=\\prod_{r=1}^{D_N} \\tanh(\\ell |b_{r,N}|/2)>0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor an eigenmode, the directly evaluated graded trace yields $n_P(b)=-1/(\\exp(\\ell b)-1)$, including $n_{P}>1$ for negative $b$. Euclidean-time-ordered fields therefore have periodic kernel $\\exp(-ub)/(1-\\exp(-\\ell b))$, $0<u<\\ell$. Transforming back without selecting eigenvectors proves the exact finite identity\n\n$$\nG_N(t)=(\\partial_u+B_N(t))^{-1}_{\\mathrm{periodic}} \\gamma_3 P_{+}\n$$\n\nfor normalized field responses. The original periodic law is a signed ratio from a positive source, never the source's positive state itself.\n\nTo read a requested finite Euclidean Wick test, form its finite matrix $O_{N}$ from the prescribed test vectors and imaginary-time factors $\\exp(-uB_N)$. This is a numerical/operator specification derived from the actual minimally coupled $B_{N}$, not a unitary imaginary-time evolution claim. Let $X_N=(\\Gamma_NO_N+O_N^*\\Gamma_N)/2$ and $Y_N=(\\Gamma_NO_N-O_N^*\\Gamma_N)/(2i)$. For any explicit bound $\\Lambda_N\\ge\\max(\\lVert X_N\\rVert,\\lVert Y_N\\rVert)$, the two-outcome effects $(I\\pm X_N/\\Lambda_N)/2$ and $(I\\pm Y_N/\\Lambda_N)/2$ measure the corresponding numerator parts on fresh prepared copies. A separately measured $\\Gamma_{N}$ gives the denominator. All outcome records, scale multipliers and normalization are kept. Finite spectral measurement/control compilation is an additional named ideal supplier, not supplied automatically by the mathematical CAR algebra.\n\nFor $r$ field factors at Euclidean times in $[0,\\ell]$, the crude bound $\\exp(r\\ell b_{\\max,N})$ times the product of their test norms bounds the operator norm; integrate absolute time-test weights for smeared tests. Thus a finite $\\Lambda_{N}$ is constructible without assuming efficient readout. Odd fermionic outputs vanish in the even state; the retained Wick tests are even. No physically superselection-forbidden odd expectation needs measurement.\n\nIf $d_N\\ge d_0$ and numerator magnitude $\\le a_N$, with errors $\\epsilon_A,\\epsilon_d$ and $\\epsilon_d<d_0/2$, the ratio error is bounded by $2\\epsilon_A/d_0+2a_N\\epsilon_d/d_0^2$. If a contraction readout uses $\\Lambda_{N}$, its raw expectation error is multiplied by $\\Lambda_{N}$. Preparation trace-distance error $\\eta$ contributes at most $\\Lambda_N\\eta$ to the numerator and $\\eta$ to parity, with the norm convention for trace distance stated. Resource demands can grow very rapidly with $N$ and the tests.\n\n## 5. A denominator bound uniform in N and t\n\nLet $s_{k}^{0}$ be the increasing list of absolute flat eigenvalues of $B_0$, including spin/flavour multiplicity. Min-max applied to $\\lVert B\\psi\\rVert$ on the common $H^1$ form domain, and its finite analog, gives $s_k(B_N(t))\\ge s_k(B_N(0))-v\\ge s_k^0-v$, as well as $s_k\\ge\\delta$. Set $a_k=\\max(\\delta,s_k^0-v)$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\nd_N(t)\\ge\\prod_{k=1}^{D_N}\\tanh(\\ell a_k/2)\\ge d_0, \\\\\nd_0:=\\prod_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\tanh(\\ell a_k/2)>0.\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nThe flat lattice counting estimate is $O(R^3)$, so $\\sum_k\\exp(-\\ell a_k)<\\infty$. The logarithm of the product converges: away from finitely many low modes, $-\\log\\tanh(x/2)\\le C\\exp(-x)$. This gives an explicit computable lower bound by finite shifted Fourier shells plus an exponential shell-tail bound. A positive $d_0$ can be tiny; the statement is mathematical noncollapse, not affordable sampling. It contrasts with, but does not rescue, the earlier unrescaled overlap determinant instrument.\n\n## 6. Controlled convergence on the common smooth test class\n\nWrite $L(t)=\\partial_u+B(t)$, $L_N(t)=\\partial_u+B_N(t)$ on periodic $u$. Since $B$ is self-adjoint and static, temporal Fourier decomposition gives $\\lVert L^{-1}\\rVert,\\lVert L_N^{-1}\\rVert\\le\\delta^{-1}$. For a fixed smooth spacetime source $h$ let $\\psi=L^{-1}h$. Smoothness and uniform Sobolev bounds follow from the same flat inverse and bounded smooth-multiplication bootstrap used in the prior Euclidean proof. $P_{N}$ commutes with $\\partial_u$ and $B_0$. Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_N P_N \\psi&=P_N h-P_N tV(1-P_N)\\psi, \\\\\n\\lVert L_N^{-1}P_N h-P_N \\psi\\rVert&\\le \\delta^{-1}v \\lVert(1-P_N)\\psi\\rVert.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExtending finite outputs by inclusion gives\n\n$$\n\\lVert L_N^{-1}P_Nh-L^{-1}h\\rVert\\le(1+v/\\delta)\\lVert(1-P_N)\\psi\\rVert.\n$$\n\nFor each fixed integer $k$, the last norm is $\\le\\text{constant}_k\\,(1+N)^{-k}$ times the spatial $H^k$ norm of $\\psi$ (with the chosen Fourier norm convention). This is a quantitative fixed-smooth-test convergence result for this replacement source. It implies convergence of each fixed Wick determinant by finite-product telescoping. It is not a bound on arbitrary cutoff-dependent tests, nor identification of its determinant with the old overlap determinant.\n\nFor derivatives, use complex $z$ with $|z|\\,\\lVert V\\rVert<d_*/(2\\ell)$. The flat periodic inverse is bounded by $\\ell/d_*$ at every $N$, so a uniform Neumann series provides holomorphic inverses and the same smooth-tail proof on a smaller common disk. Cauchy's formula differentiates the error with factor $r!\\,\\text{radius}^{-r}$. The normalized fixed-test response family therefore has controlled derivatives without assuming smooth eigenvectors or sharp cutoffs.\n\nPositive states are comparable too: extend $B_{N}$ as $P_NB(t)P_N$ plus the unchanged flat $B_0$ on the complement. These self-adjoint operators converge to $B(t)$ in strong resolvent sense by convergence on the common smooth Fourier core and the uniform bounded perturbation. For the Fermi function one can split it into the gapped negative spectral projection plus a function vanishing at both infinities; the gap gives strong convergence of the projections, and ordinary resolvent functional calculus handles the remainder. Thus $C_{N}$ converges strongly on fixed tests to $C(t)$, and finite CAR moments converge. This does not assert convergence in trace norm on one infinite Fock space.\n\n## 7. Exact gauge transport, with its scope stated\n\nA fixed Fourier space is not invariant under a general spatial gauge phase $g_Q(x)=\\exp(iQ\\lambda(x))$. Instead use a transported regulated fiber $H_N^g=gH_N$ with inclusion into $H$, and define $B_N^g=gB_Ng^{-1}$. The exact map of CAR algebras sends $a(f)$ to $a(gf)$; its finite Fock lift is the ordinary exterior-algebra lift, with functorial composition. Then $C_N^g=gC_Ng^{-1}$, the purification/control matrices transport likewise, $\\Gamma_{N}$ is unchanged, and every numerator and denominator is intertwined. No determinant anomaly is solved by this finite construction: the regulator, high-mode normalization and continuum sea-phase problem are distinct.\n\nFor the actual one-parameter family, $t$ is gauge invariant: at nonzero curvature it is fixed by $F=t\\,f'(y_2)\\,dy_2\\wedge dy_1$; at $t=0$ the same conclusion follows. Stabilizers of a fixed connection on the connected torus are constant phases, which preserve $H_{N}$. Thus different gauge descriptors of the same admitted connection give compatible fibers and no new ambiguity in the normalized law.\n\nFor a periodic gauge phase depending also on Euclidean $u$, set $H_N^{g}(u)=g(u)H_N$ and transport the whole differential operator $gL_Ng^{-1}$, including the derivative-of-$g$ term. This gives exact Euclidean source-contact Ward covariance with periodicity preserved. It is a response-frame transport, not a claim that an arbitrary Euclidean-time gauge function has been Wick-rotated into a real Lorentzian electrostatic controller. The physical static-source supplier and its real-time unitary gauge interpretation are only asserted for time-independent spatial gauges here. If the owner requires a physical independently varied time-dependent background family, that remains a further source extension.\n\n## 8. Concrete Shale test; what it obstructs and what it does not\n\nAt the flat background let P_-(n) be the negative spectral projection of alpha·(2 $\\pi$ n-Q L e0). A nonconstant smooth gauge multiplier g has Fourier coefficient $g_{k}\\ne 0$ for some $k\\ne 0$. The commutator matrix has entries\n\n$$\n[P_-,g]_{n+k,n}=g_k [P_-(n+k)-P_-(n)].\n$$\n\nOn an angular cone not parallel to $k$, the squared two-spin matrix norm of this difference is bounded below by $c/|n|^2$ for sufficiently large $n$: differentiating $(I-\\alpha\\cdot p/|p|)/2$ yields its nonzero transverse derivative, with uniform $O(|n|^{-2})$ remainder in the projection difference. The cone contains order $R^3$ lattice points in a dyadic shell $R\\le|n|\\le 2R$. The shell contribution to the Hilbert--Schmidt sum is therefore at least $c'R$, and diverges. Choose, for example, $g_Q=\\exp(iQ\\epsilon\\sin(2\\pi y_1))$ with a nonzero first Fourier coefficient; this is a genuinely periodic small gauge function within the stated class.\n\nHence the common flat-sea Fock representation cannot implement all these gauge multipliers by unitaries satisfying the CAR field intertwiner. The Shale--Stinespring criterion is verified in Deckert and Merkl (2015, Theorem 1.2). The explicit Fourier divergence here is for this torus and does not depend on transplanting their massive noncompact theorem. Ten direct-summed flavours do not cancel a sum of squared Hilbert--Schmidt norms.\n\nThis is not an obstruction to the single abstract CAR algebra, its positive state family, finite source resets, or intertwiners between appropriately transported sea fibers. It forbids a specific unsupported common-vacuum implementation. A stronger assertion that any two distinct bump controls have disjoint thermal representations is not required and not proved here. A physical protocol continuously changing an infinite sea would need its own implementation; the present protocol instead prepares each finite trial at its nominated control.\n\n## 9. Source census and event calibration that must accompany admission\n\nPer nominated N, t, test list, errors and finite number of trials, retain: $D_{N}$ matter modes; $D_{N}$ preparation ancillas; reference empty/filled occupations; matrix-function calibration and control description; positive preparation pulse reserve and duration $\\tau_{p}$; the quadratic matter Hamiltonian with its scalar energy offset; independent physical clock; parity detector; numerator X/Y contraction measurements with $\\Lambda_{N}$; all sampling records; retained ancillas or discard/retention service; source-field controller, charge/length references, and all failed or unused stocks. Precision and trial count are specified by the preceding error law, not discarded after dividing by parity.\n\nThe connection shared with the event ledger is still $q_*A_{\\mathrm{phys}}/\\hbar=L\\,dx_0/\\ell+t\\,f(x_2/\\ell)\\,dx_1/\\ell$. The already supplied marked event hold has $\\Delta\\tau_A/\\hbar=\\ln 2$. The controller must enforce $(q_*/\\hbar)\\int_{\\mathrm{source}}A_{\\mathrm{phys}}=\\Delta\\tau_A/\\hbar$ and preserve orientation and charge-$Q$ powers. It must also supply the $t$-profile acting in $B_{N}$. No equality $\\ell/c=\\tau_A$ is inferred; if nominated it is a new calibration. All old pulse/refinement time overhead and common phase records survive. Fourier inclusions do not implement geometric edge subdivision; the old exact line-integral connection refinement and the new cutoff inclusions coexist as separate maps.\n\n## 1. Declared source class and physical units\n\nA descriptor fixes: the inherited physical side $\\ell>0$; inherited marked-event duration $\\tau_A>0$; $\\hbar$, $c$ and installed charge reference $q_*>0$; ten ordered left-handed charges $(-9,-5,-1,7,8)$ twice; a positive integer $N$; one real control $t$ in the previously retained common interval; a finite list of tests; finite trial/accuracy/confidence data; and any registered original event words/subdivisions. No spin structure changes: all four Euclidean directions remain periodic.\n\nUse $T_*=\\tau_A$ as clock unit, $E_*=\\hbar/\\tau_A$ as energy/service-cost unit, $L_*=\\ell$ as spatial length unit, and $q_*$ as charge unit. The original supplier retains $\\Delta=E_*\\ln 2$, so $\\Delta\\tau_A/\\hbar=\\ln 2$ exactly. The calibrated dimensionless number $r=\\ell/(c\\tau_A)>0$ is retained in the descriptor; it is NOT set to one. The auxiliary Euclidean period remains $\\ell$ and inverse energy temperature $\\beta_E=\\ell/(\\hbar c)$. The physical Lorentzian clock is $s$, read by the installed clock in units $\\tau_{A}$. Euclidean $u$ is not that clock.\n\nThe earlier G.6b derivative convention is matched explicitly by $q_GA_G=-Qq_*A_{\\mathrm{phys}}/\\hbar$ for each represented charge in its unit-action convention; the present derivative is $\\partial-iQA$. No scalar G.6b action is silently substituted for the Weyl Hamiltonian.\n\nThe controller supplies the single real, static physical spatial connection\n\n$$\nA_{\\mathrm{phys}}=(\\hbar/q_*)[(\\ln 2)\\,dx_0/\\ell+t\\,f(x_2/\\ell)\\,dx_1/\\ell],\\quad \\Phi=0.\n$$\n\nBoth the event-holonomy extractor and the chiral Hamiltonian consume this same registered object, calibration and controller identity. The contract requires\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(q_*/\\hbar) \\int_{\\mathrm{source}} A_{\\mathrm{phys}} &= \\Delta \\tau_A/\\hbar=\\ln 2, \\\\\nU_Q(\\mathrm{path}) &= \\exp\\Bigl[(iQq_*/\\hbar) \\int_{\\mathrm{path}} A_{\\mathrm{phys}}\\Bigr].\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus all retained signed event winding laws exp(i Q $N_{\\mathrm{event}}$ ln 2), endpoint frames, backward-link signs and exact line-integral subdivisions survive. This is an explicit common-input/source-identity constraint, not two independently tuned copies with equal labels. No geometric loop length is identified with the old event apparatus's physical traversal duration. Original I/X pulse overhead, failures, unused stock and invoices remain attached in full.\n\nThe matter carrier is $H_N=P_N\\bigoplus_Q L^2(T^3_\\ell,\\mathbb{C}^2)$, $D=20(2N+1)^3$, $B_N(t)=P_NB(t)P_N$. Its real-time Hamiltonian and response maps are the already derived ones. The Fourier cutoff inclusions and the geometric event subdivisions are separately recorded maps, never identified.\n\n## 2. Test/output scope\n\nAdmit finite products and linear combinations of the already proved, per-flavour charge-neutral even Wick tests: equal numbers of $\\psi_{Q}$ and $\\psi_Q^\\dagger$ for each flavour, with fixed smooth spacetime test vectors. For a fully effective finite descriptor, use spatial/time trigonometric-polynomial test functions with rational complex coefficients, and explicitly recorded finite combinations and Euclidean ordering/integration rules. This class is dense and separates each flavour's covariance; its finite products separate the nominated normalized quasi-free law. Smooth tests outside this effective class remain mathematical limits unless their evaluation/norm-error certificates are supplied. No cutoff-dependent rough-test uniformity is claimed. The charge-neutral bilinears $\\psi_Q(f)\\,\\psi_Q(g)^\\dagger$ for arbitrary admitted $f,g$ determine every matrix element of the $Q$ covariance; polarization of test vectors gives its complex off-diagonal entries. Since the theory is flavour-block-diagonal and quasi-free, these covariances determine every nonzero Wick function; monomials with unmatched flavour occupation vanish. Thus the restricted native test family still determines the claimed normalized field law. This narrows the native readout class explicitly without narrowing the already proved mathematical covariance theorem.\n\nA descriptor records $O_{j,N}$ computed from $B_{N}$ and the test rule, and a certified $\\Lambda_{j}\\ge 1$ bounding the norms of the Hermitian and anti-Hermitian parts of $\\Gamma O_{j,N}$. Exact matrix functions/integrals are accepted in exact-control mode as specified computable mathematical values; finite precision mode must provide certified errors. One common source run produces the positive state's parity-weighted numerator and denominator laws, with normalized output $R_j=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho\\Gamma O_j)/\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho\\Gamma)$. These ratios need not be positive states. Lorentzian real-time CAR responses of $\\rho$ and $H_{N}$ are available under the same carrier but are not substituted for the periodic Euclidean target.\n\nThe neutrality restriction avoids an undeclared charged phase reference. More general charge-changing observables would require an additional, explicit reference/source extension. Different-flavour nonneutral monomials have zero expectation under the nominated gauge-invariant state and are not measured by silently breaking charge superselection.\n\n## 3. Nominated new ideal primitives and their boundary\n\nP1. Fresh-register supply: finite CAR modes with declared charges, an empty system reference state, and a filled ancillary reference state. The supplier delivers those states at the preparation entrance. This is paid fresh preparation, not a free reset of a used register.\n\nP2. Static field and calibration service: the specified common external connection, spatial Fourier/Galerkin coupling matrix, physical charge/length/clock calibration, and certified parameter t. It is an open, driven ideal source. The waveform and mathematical transfer law are explicit; microscopic Maxwell-source manufacture or quantized backreaction is not asserted. If a sourced Maxwell equation is later claimed, its external current and equation require an additional proof.\n\nP3. Finite positive control service: the exact total preparation waveform below, or its certified approximate realization, on the supplied finite modes. It includes the actual matter drift, clock reference, common phase, energy reserve and finite time interval. Merely knowing the target unitary is not counted as supplying this primitive.\n\nP4. Finite neutral even binary instrument service: for a prescribed finite contraction X/Lambda commuting with the flavour charges and fermion parity, the source supplies the two effects (I±X/Lambda)/2 and a retained neutral pointer/copy record. The full finite-slot CP instrument, including drift and output-state convention, is specified in section 5. This is an explicit ideal quantum-instrument primitive, not a claimed Hamiltonian derivation of a detector from the CAR algebra.\n\nP5. Clock, computation/compilation, classical record and holding services: these retain the descriptor, matrices/precision certificates, outcomes, controller/phases, energy reservations, common-source identifiers and all failed/unselected stocks. No desired determinant or continuum response is a primitive. The nontrivial preparation, normalized response and limit laws are derived from P1-P5.\n\nThe older signed-loop event apparatus remains its separately adopted complete source. These new primitives do not replace or discount any of its suppliers.\n\n## 4. Charged state, reference and ancilla inventory per trial\n\nThere are $D$ system matter modes and $D$ preparation ancilla modes. Every ancilla has the SAME charge $Qq_*$ as the system mode with which it mixes. Ancillas begin filled, system modes empty. The one-particle rotation $U=\\begin{bmatrix} \\cos\\Theta & -\\sin\\Theta \\\\ \\sin\\Theta & \\cos\\Theta \\end{bmatrix}$, $\\Theta=\\arcsin\\sqrt{C_N}$, preserves charge and total particle number. Both matrices are flavour block diagonal.\n\nThe filled ancillary reference carries, for each flavour, charge $2(2N+1)^3Qq_*$. Summing all ten flavours gives zero because $\\sum Q=0$. This net neutrality does not erase the individual charged occupations: all $2D$ modes and their charge labels are inventoried. The system's reduced thermal charge fluctuations are compensated by retained ancillary correlations. The pure combined prepared state has fixed total charge zero. No charged coherent phase reference is borrowed.\n\nThe preparation reference is a definite occupation state, not a new independent chiral matter multiplet. Ancillas are paid finite control registers and have no claimed continuum propagation. The system's empty-vacuum finite charge operator and any sea-charge/sea-energy offsets are explicitly distinct; this contract claims normalized responses only.\n\nEach trial additionally has: one neutral binary outcome pointer, one neutral binary durable copy register, one neutral success/failure flag, one neutral detector-dilation binary ancilla; one local controller/clock endpoint record; and its own reserved control/readout energy buffer. A finite additional classical descriptor-memory budget $M$ is fixed before the run and must cover the stored finite descriptions and certified error tables; exact real controls are named by finite computable descriptions, not stored as infinitely many literal bits. Take, concretely, $M=B_{\\mathrm{desc}}+J[64+\\lceil\\log_2(J+1)\\rceil+\\lceil\\log_2(K_{\\mathrm{out}}+1)\\rceil]+256$, where $B_{\\mathrm{desc}}$ is the certified bit length of all finite symbolic control/test/calibration/error descriptions and lookup-table labels. Times and exactly specified real constants are retained by those finite descriptions, not rounded literal expansions. If a requested description does not fit $M$, enlarge and invoice $M$ before starting. There is no unrecorded reuse of failed trial modes.\n\n## 5. Exact finite timing and total-effect detector convention\n\nEach trial has four slots, each of duration $\\tau_{A}$:\n\n1. Fresh-reference entrance and checks; install the declared empty/filled states and test/control label.\n2. Preparation, $\\tau_p=\\tau_A$.\n3. Detector/instrument slot.\n4. Copy, verify and retain outcome/success flag.\n\nThe clock schedules trials serially; parallel hardware is not presumed. If $J$ trials are reserved, execution ends at $4J\\tau_{A}$. Retention ends at $T_{\\mathrm{ret}}=(4J+8)\\tau_A$. All reserved stocks, including those used late or never used, are invoiced from the common start through $T_{\\mathrm{ret}}$.\n\nPreparation uses $H_0=H_N^{\\mathrm{exc}}$ on system, zero ancillary idle drift, $F(s)=\\exp(-iH_0s/\\hbar)$, $G=i\\hbar\\,d\\Gamma(J_N)/\\tau_A$, $\\kappa=E_*D\\pi$, $K=G+\\kappa I\\ge 0$. The actual total preparation Hamiltonian is $H_0+F(s)KF(s)^*$. Its propagator is $F(s)\\exp(-iKs/\\hbar)$; its reduced endpoint state is exactly $\\rho_{N}$ since $[C_N,B_N]=0$. The positive peak reserve is at most $\\hbar cDb_{\\max,N}+2\\kappa$. The full waveform and scalar phase $\\exp(-i\\kappa\\tau_A/\\hbar)$ are retained. There is no unpriced drift cancellation.\n\nThe detector has a TOTAL-EFFECT convention: its input is the state at the detector entrance, immediately after the preparation slot. For effect $E_z=(I+zX/\\Lambda)/2$, $z=\\pm 1$, its nominated instrument over the entire detector slot is\n\n$$\nI_z(\\rho)=F_{\\mathrm{sys}}(\\tau_A) \\sqrt{E_z} \\rho \\sqrt{E_z} F_{\\mathrm{sys}}(\\tau_A)^{*} \\otimes |z\\rangle\\langle z|_{\\mathrm{pointer}},\n$$\n\nwhere $F_{\\mathrm{sys}}(\\tau_A)=\\exp(-iH_N^{\\mathrm{exc}}\\tau_A/\\hbar)$, with retained preparation ancillas carried alongside and the declared CP map applied only to system/pointer. For parity use $E_z=(I+z\\Gamma)/2$. The effects and full slot maps refer to the entrance state; any drift inside the slot is included in this ideal instrument supplier's contract. We do NOT add an unaccounted free evolution before these effects or claim the Lüders operation follows merely from $H_0$. The output is a retained postmeasurement register, not a reset. The copy slot copies the classical pointer outcome to its durable neutral record. No second measurement reuses the disturbed input.\n\nFor numerator $j$, $X$ is either $\\operatorname{Re}(\\Gamma O_j)$ or $\\operatorname{Im}(\\Gamma O_j)$, measured on separate fresh trials. Both are charge neutral and parity even. There is no odd detector coupling. Positivity and normalization of all effects are guaranteed by $\\Lambda$. Detector/energy stocks are charged even for the parity measurement, which needs no large $\\Lambda$.\n\nThe finite binary coupling can itself be specified with a positive finite waveform. On system plus a neutral detector ancilla initially $|0\\rangle$, take the block rotation $U_E=\\begin{bmatrix} \\sqrt{E_+} & -\\sqrt{E_-} \\\\ \\sqrt{E_-} & \\sqrt{E_+} \\end{bmatrix}$. It is unitary because $E_++E_-=I$ and their functional square roots commute. Its angle is $\\Theta_E=\\arcsin\\sqrt{E_-}$, of norm at most $\\pi/2$. With $J_E=\\begin{bmatrix} 0 & -\\Theta_E \\\\ \\Theta_E & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$, $G_E=i\\hbar J_E/\\tau_A$ and $K_E=G_E+\\pi E_*I\\ge 0$, supply total $H_0+F(s)K_EF(s)^*$ over the detector slot, where $H_0$ is the system drift and the detector ancilla is degenerate. The peak is at most $\\lVert H_0\\rVert+2\\pi E_*$. At its endpoint a supplied neutral computational-basis pointer instrument produces exactly the CP map above; that elementary classical-pointer primitive has its own declared $E_*$ reserve. This factors the large finite effect through an explicit paid positive controller rather than leaving its energy reservation unspecified. The pointer/copy operation remains an ideal paid measurement primitive, not a microscopic derivation.\n\nThis total-effect instrument and positive-control preparation are explicit new ideal primitives of this source. They are not smuggled into a claim that a generic physical detector automatically realizes these matrices.\n\n## 6. Finite trials and error convention\n\nChoose a certified rational $0<\\bar{d}\\le d_0$, where the proved positive infinite-product lower bound is evaluated by finite shifted Fourier shells plus a certified exponential tail. Thus $d_N(t)\\ge\\bar{d}$ uniformly over the nominated controls/cutoffs. The rational certificate and its calculation are retained.\n\nLet $m$ be the number of complex test outputs and $K_{\\mathrm{out}}=2m+1$ the two numerator components plus one shared parity estimate. Fix rational target component error $\\zeta>0$ and failure probability $\\alpha$ in $(0,1)$. Put $\\Lambda_{\\max}=\\max_j\\Lambda_j$ and choose a positive rational\n\n$$\n\\epsilon\\le\\min(\\bar{d}/4,\\ \\zeta\\bar{d}^2/(8\\Lambda_{\\max})).\n$$\n\nExact-source mode is the default: state preparation, specified control, calibrated effects and scale factors are exact ideal values. Take\n\n$$\nn=\\lceil 2 \\epsilon^{-2} \\log(2 K_{\\mathrm{out}}/\\alpha)\\rceil,\\quad J=K_{\\mathrm{out}} n.\n$$\n\nFor independent fresh trials, the Rademacher Hoeffding bound and a union bound put all raw expectation errors below $\\epsilon$ with probability at least $1-\\alpha$. Numerator component errors are at most $\\Lambda_j\\epsilon$; denominator error is at most $\\epsilon$. The previously derived ratio bound then bounds each normalized real/imaginary component by $\\zeta$ (the chosen constants are conservative). This is a finite statistical certificate, not a deterministic guarantee of the observed sample.\n\nApproximate-source mode is separately declared: let $\\eta=\\lVert\\rho_{\\mathrm{actual}}-\\rho_{\\mathrm{target}}\\rVert_1$ be FULL trace-norm preparation error; effect errors are operator norm; all calibration/scale errors have explicit bounds. Require total systematic raw-expectation error $\\le\\epsilon/2$ and use statistical tolerance $\\epsilon/2$, increasing $n$ to $\\lceil 8\\epsilon^{-2}\\log(2K_{\\mathrm{out}}/\\alpha)\\rceil$. For example $\\eta\\le\\epsilon/4$ and $\\lVert E_{\\mathrm{actual}}-E_{\\mathrm{target}}\\rVert\\le\\epsilon/8$ provide raw expectation error $\\le\\epsilon/2$; remaining error types need extra allocated budget, not those same allowances again. Conventional half trace distance $T$ contributes $2T$. Failed calibration/trials are never counted as certified samples: either a pre-reserved replacement is used and included in $J$/invoice, or the report is a flagged unsuccessful run without a claimed estimate.\n\nThe source expectation law itself is exact in exact mode. Finite sampling is the specified readout protocol for estimating that law. Continuum normalized convergence is taken over these source laws, or with separately tightened sampling/systematic budgets; no fixed finite sample is claimed to have zero continuum error.\n\n## 7. Concrete positive rational tariff schedule\n\nAll following prices are integer multiples of $E_*$; holding rates are integer multiples of $E_*/\\tau_A$. They are nominated positive ideal supplier tariffs, not measured thermodynamic lower bounds or a claim of optimal cost. Physical peak reserves are separately recorded, never replaced by tariff prices.\n\n- Each fresh system or ancilla CAR mode: establishment price 1 $E_*$; holding rate 1 $E_*/\\tau_A$.\n- Each of the four neutral binary detector/record/flag/dilation registers per trial: establishment 1 $E_*$; holding 1 $E_*/\\tau_A$.\n- Each classical memory bit in the pre-reserved budget $M$: establishment 1 $E_*$; holding 1 $E_*/\\tau_A$.\n- Each trial's reference-state service: 2 $E_*$.\n- Each preparation waveform service: 3 $E_*$ plus 1 $E_*$ per reserved independent real waveform/matrix-description entry $W_{N}$; use the conservative fixed $W_N=8D^2+16$ real-entry slots, with symbolic/precision metadata covered by $M$; all unused slots are paid.\n- Each detector total-instrument service: 3 $E_*$ plus 1 $E_*$ per reserved independent real instrument-description entry $V_{j,N}$; reserve the conservative $V_{j,N}=8\\times 4^D+16$ entry slots for every trial, including parity trials, whether or not a sparse description is used. Its specified duration remains $\\tau_{A}$. A parity trial still pays at least 3 $E_*$.\n- Each copy/verification service: 1 $E_*$.\n- Each unit of reserved energy buffer $E_*$: establishment 1 $E_*$ and holding 1 $E_*/\\tau_A$. For EACH trial reserve $R_E=\\lceil 2\\hbar cDb_{\\max,N}/E_*+2D\\pi+2\\pi+1\\rceil+1$ buffer units. This explicitly exceeds the positive preparation peak, detector coupling peak and elementary pointer reserve combined. It is a nominated finite source reserve; microscopic detector thermodynamics is not derived.\n- One common calibrated field service, one clock service and one charge/length/energy-reference service: each establishment 2 $E_*$ and holding 1 $E_*/\\tau_A$ for the whole reservation window.\n- Each source-control or error-certificate evaluation service: 1 $E_*$ per declared call, with all calls counted in the finite descriptor.\n\nNo price is zero. Tariff coefficients are fixed positive rationals (integers); N-dependent inventory, description length, clock duration, trial count and reserve may grow. Each reserved service is prepaid even when a failure makes it unused. Ancillary idleness has a positive holding price even when its idle Hamiltonian is degenerate.\n\nFor an integer reservation table $(n_{\\mathrm{modes}},n_{\\mathrm{bits}},n_{\\mathrm{buffers}},W,V,n_{\\mathrm{ref}},n_{\\mathrm{prep}},n_{\\mathrm{det}},n_{\\mathrm{copy}},n_{\\mathrm{eval}})$ and $H=4J+8$, the additional invoice in $E_*$ units is\n\n$$\n(n_{\\mathrm{modes}}+n_{\\mathrm{bits}}+n_{\\mathrm{buffers}})(1+H)+2n_{\\mathrm{ref}}+3n_{\\mathrm{prep}}+W+3n_{\\mathrm{det}}+V+n_{\\mathrm{copy}}+n_{\\mathrm{eval}}+6+3H.\n$$\n\nHere $n_{\\mathrm{modes}}=2DJ$, $n_{\\mathrm{bits}}$ includes $4J$ binary registers plus $M$, $n_{\\mathrm{buffers}}$ is the total number of $E_*$ buffer units across all trials, $W$ and $V$ sum reserved description-entry counts, $n_{\\mathrm{ref}}=n_{\\mathrm{prep}}=n_{\\mathrm{det}}=n_{\\mathrm{copy}}=J$, and $n_{\\mathrm{eval}}$ is declared. All clocks/units/calibrations and integer roundups are retained. The older event apparatus invoice is ADDED in full with its original retention-extension terms; it is not absorbed by the $+6+3H$ common-service term. Any extra original event experiment duration extends $H$ and retention for every affected new stock before assembly.\n\n## 8. Source factorization and records\n\nEach fresh trial has joint initial reference state $|\\text{empty}\\rangle_{\\mathrm{sys}}\\otimes|\\text{filled}\\rangle_{\\mathrm{anc}}\\otimes|\\text{ready}\\rangle_{\\mathrm{neutral}}$, conditional on its common classical descriptor. Different trials use fresh independent supplied registers. The field/controller, clock and charge/length references are common retained suppliers, not falsely independent stochastic copies. The contract conditions the ideal quantum law on their fixed calibrated values; finite uncertainty is explicitly part of approximate mode.\n\nAfter preparation the system and ancilla are generally entangled. Only the reduced system state factorizes into quasi-free mode occupations in an eigenbasis; the physical ancilla is NOT discarded from the source census. The detector CP map is tensored with the identity on those retained ancillas. The output classical record contains trial ID, control t, source/controller IDs, test/component, $\\Lambda$, parity convention, all clock entrances/exits, calibration and common-phase IDs, outcome, success flag, reserve/invoice ID and retention deadline. Approximate mode adds all error certificates. All old signed-loop raw motor records, supplier IDs and phases remain cross-referenced.\n\nThe response extractor uses only the declared certified records and the prescribed ratio. It reports failure rather than silently deleting unsuccessful trials or conditioning on an unrecorded success event. No supplier, phase, ancilla, normalization factor or failed stock is declared spectator merely because it cancels in the ratio.\n\n## 1. Preparation generator and the retained matter drift\n\nThe quadratic rotation generator in section 3 must not be read as a control simply added to an uncontrolled matter drift. Either it is supplied as the total preparation Hamiltonian, with switching/control explicitly charged, or its interaction-picture implementation must be given. We specify the latter here so no drift cancellation is assumed.\n\nOn the combined system/ancilla register, let $H_0=H_N^{\\mathrm{exc}}\\otimes I_{\\mathrm{anc}}$, with degenerate ancillary idle Hamiltonian, and $F(s)=\\exp(-iH_0s/\\hbar)$. Let $J_{N}$ be the anti-Hermitian one-particle block matrix already defined. On the combined finite Fock space put\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nG_N&=(i \\hbar/\\tau_p)\\, d\\Gamma(J_N), \\\\\n\\kappa_N&=\\hbar D_N \\pi/\\tau_p, \\\\\nK_N&=G_N+\\kappa_N I.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe conservative earlier norm bound gives $\\lVert G_N\\rVert\\le\\kappa_N$, so $K_{N}$ is positive and $\\lVert K_N\\rVert\\le 2\\kappa_N$. The actual nominated laboratory waveform for $0\\le s\\le\\tau_p$ is the TOTAL Hamiltonian\n\n$$\nH_{\\mathrm{total}}(s)=H_0+F(s) K_N F(s)^{*}.\n$$\n\nIt is positive. Its norm is at most $\\hbar cD_Nb_{\\max,N}+2\\kappa_N$. Direct differentiation verifies the exact propagator\n\n$$\nU_{\\mathrm{total}}(s)=F(s) \\exp(-i K_N s/\\hbar).\n$$\n\nAt $\\tau_{p}$, $\\exp(-iG_N\\tau_p/\\hbar)=\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{Fock}}(U_N)$, the second quantization of the stated one-particle preparation rotation. Thus the final combined state is the intended purification conjugated by $F(\\tau_p)$, with the scalar phase $\\exp(-i\\kappa_N\\tau_p/\\hbar)$ retained in the control record. After tracing the ancilla, its system covariance is\n\n$$\n\\exp(-i c \\tau_p B_N) C_N \\exp(i c \\tau_p B_N)=C_N,\n$$\n\nsince $C_{N}$ is a function of $B_{N}$. Therefore the reduced state is exactly the required $\\rho_{N}$ despite the surviving physical drift. No negative drift compensation, instantaneous cancellation, or unpriced replacement of the matter Hamiltonian is used.\n\nThe finite controller must supply the actual time-dependent conjugated waveform $F(s)K_NF(s)^*$, duration $\\tau_{p}$, reference clock, common phase record and peak positive reserve above. This is an explicit ideal control supplier still requiring admission. It does not follow from the abstract CAR algebra. After preparation, $H_0$ is the retained matter drift; any switching transients or approximate pulse synthesis belong to the preparation error budget. An ideal nominated exact piecewise waveform includes its switching convention as supplier data; no physical implementation beyond that formal ideal source is claimed.\n\n## 2. Unambiguous preparation-error convention\n\nIn section 4 take $\\eta$ to mean the FULL trace-norm error\n\n$$\n\\eta=\\lVert \\rho_{\\mathrm{actual}}-\\rho_{\\mathrm{target}}\\rVert_1.\n$$\n\nThen $|\\operatorname{Tr}[(\\rho_{\\mathrm{actual}}-\\rho_{\\mathrm{target}})X_N]|\\le\\Lambda_N\\eta$ and the parity expectation error is $\\le\\eta$. If instead reporting conventional trace distance $T=(1/2)\\lVert\\rho_{\\mathrm{actual}}-\\rho_{\\mathrm{target}}\\rVert_1$, the corresponding bounds are $2\\Lambda_{N}T$ and $2T$. This factor of two must be included before using the ratio-error formula. Statistical, calibration, pulse-approximation and readout errors are then added to their appropriate numerator/denominator budgets; the denominator condition remains total $\\epsilon_d<d_0/2$.\n\nThe default $J=(2m+1)n$ reserves no replacement trials. Every scheduled outcome is recorded and used. If a calibration certificate fails, a required outcome is missing, or verification fails, the default report is an unsuccessful run, with the full reservation invoice and records retained. The stated Hoeffding guarantee applies to the completed, certified independent-trial protocol; it is not inferred for a subsequence selected by successful measurement outcomes.\n\nAn optional larger reservation $J=(2m+1)n+J_{\\mathrm{spare}}$ must nominate the nonnegative integer $J_{\\mathrm{spare}}$ and its scheduling before execution, update $T_{\\mathrm{ret}}$ and every stock/invoice, and use replacements only for failures detected before the affected quantum preparation/measurement and independent of its unseen outcome. Any outcome-dependent or postmeasurement missing-record failure makes the run unsuccessful rather than permitting biased deletion/replacement. This preserves the declared independent source sampling law.\n\nThe positive lower bound $d_N(t)\\ge\\bar{d}$ concerns the exact source mean. It does not guarantee that a particular finite empirical parity mean is nonzero or large enough for safe division. The finite-record exporter is therefore defined on EVERY record as follows.\n\n1. Perform all existing completeness, calibration and failure checks. If any fails, retain the full records/invoice and report an unsuccessful estimate.\n2. Compute the empirical parity mean $\\hat{d}$ from the complete reserved parity trials.\n3. If $\\hat{d}<\\bar{d}/2$, do not divide. Retain every numerator/parity outcome and all resource records; report an unsuccessful estimate with the denominator-guard flag.\n4. If $\\hat{d}\\ge\\bar{d}/2$, compute the declared normalized numerator-component estimates using this $\\hat{d}$ and return them with their specified confidence/error certificate.\n\nNo outcome is deleted; there is no outcome-selected replacement, automatic retry or conditioning of the reported confidence guarantee on passing this gate. A later separately authorized fresh run would be a different fully invoiced experiment, not a repair of these records.\n\nOn the simultaneous good-error event used in the existing proof, $|\\hat{d}-d_N|\\le\\epsilon\\le\\bar{d}/4$, so $\\hat{d}\\ge3\\bar{d}/4>\\bar{d}/2$. Thus the good event always passes the guard, and with probability at least $1-\\alpha$ the complete experiment returns estimates satisfying the stated component error bound. Outside that event it may return an unsuccessful estimate or estimates without guaranteed realized accuracy, exactly as allowed by the unconditional confidence statement. The guard makes the exporter total without strengthening or changing the statistical theorem.\n\nThe probability statement takes the following form:\n\n- For the certified, failure-free independent sampling law, the probability of returning estimates with all specified component errors within $\\zeta$ is at least $1-\\alpha$. The simultaneous good-error event implies the denominator guard passes.\n- If an additional abort/failure process is allowed with no probability bound, no positive lower bound on successful return is asserted. The universal error-control statement is $P(\\text{a returned estimate is inaccurate})\\le\\alpha$, provided the retained trial outcomes obey the certified underlying independent sampling law and the protocol does not substitute a postselected trial population. Returning nothing cannot count as accuracy or success.\n- If the probability of the additional abort process is certified at most $\\beta$, then $P(\\text{successful return with all specified errors within }\\zeta)\\ge 1-\\alpha-\\beta$, by the union bound. The lower bound may be replaced by zero when negative. No independence between the abort event and the sampling-error event is required for this union bound.\n\nThe universal statement bounds the joint event 'return AND inaccurate'; it is NOT the conditional probability of inaccuracy given return. The latter can be larger after outcome-dependent abortion. We never condition the Hoeffding proof on completion or on passing the denominator guard.\n\nThese bounds presuppose certified source/readout error limits on the data-generating trials. An arbitrary undetected physical corruption of the sampling law is outside that certificate. Detected failure leads to the already specified whole-run abort, retained records and full invoice; it does not authorize selective data removal or outcome-selected replacement.\n\nFollowing the computational-basis detector measurement, the already inventoried detector-dilation ancilla is retained in the outcome basis state $|z\\rangle$. The separate pointer and durable copy likewise retain that classical label. The displayed section 5 CP map is the marginal after suppressing this explicitly retained dilation register from the notation, not an instruction to discard it from the source boundary or invoice.\n\nFor a joint system/preparation-ancilla input $\\rho_{\\mathrm{SA}}$, the unnormalized conditional output is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&[(F_{\\mathrm{sys}} \\sqrt{E_z}) \\otimes I_A]\\, \\rho_{SA} [(\\sqrt{E_z} F_{\\mathrm{sys}}^{*}) \\otimes I_A] \\\\\n&\\quad \\otimes |z\\rangle\\langle z|_{\\mathrm{dilation}} \\otimes |z\\rangle\\langle z|_{\\mathrm{pointer}},\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the durable copy added in its scheduled copy slot. All preparation-ancilla correlations, flags and clocks remain retained as specified. This is a completely positive extension to the actual supplied joint state; no arbitrary-reference performance claim is added beyond the nominated trial source.\n\n## 2. Scalar-kernel theorem at finite regulator\n\nLet A denote a declared background, $C_{A}$ an invertible phase-fixed finite Weyl matrix and $L_{A}$ the Gaussian Berezin functional on its Grassmann algebra:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\nL_A(O)=\\int d\\bar{\\psi}\\,d\\psi\\,\\exp(-\\bar{\\psi}C_A\\psi)\\,O, \\\\\nZ_A=L_A(1)=\\det C_A\\ne 0, \\\\\nW_A(O)=L_A(O)/Z_A.\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nFor any nonvanishing smooth complex scalar $c(A)$, define $L'_A=c(A)L_A$. Then\n\n$$\nW'_A(O)=W_A(O)\n$$\n\nidentically for every Grassmann O, including neutral tests. Consequently every fixed-order parameter derivative, every finite linear combination, product of normalized output numbers and covariance-derived Wick minor is identical wherever defined. The statement is exact before taking the accepted continuum limit; no derivative-convergence inference is needed.\n\nConversely, if two linear functionals with nonzero values at 1 have identical normalized values on the whole Grassmann algebra, they differ by this scalar and nothing else. For the adopted neutral class, the converse is asserted ONLY within the declared flavour-block-diagonal gauge-invariant Gaussian class: the neutral bilinears separate the covariance, Wick's formula determines every allowed neutral moment, and unmatched-flavour moments vanish. This does not classify arbitrary nongeneric or nongauge-invariant functionals invisible to a restricted test class.\n\nThus the exact normalized Gaussian quotient is $[L_A]=\\{c(A)L_A:c(A)\\ne 0\\}$. Its covariance is a complete coordinate within this class. A background-independent baseline is a special case. A background-dependent lower polynomial $\\exp(P(A))$ is equally null for $W$ and its derivatives, even when $P$ is nonlocal. Nullity here means zero change of THESE response functions, not zero functional, zero vacuum response or zero source cost.\n\n## 3. Ward and gauge distinctions\n\nA matter Ward operator $Q_{A}$ acts on the Grassmann variables/test algebra at fixed $A$. The homogeneous equation $L_A(Q_AO)=0$ is equivalent to $W_A(Q_AO)=0$ and survives every $c(A)$. More generally every homogeneous linear relation among values of $L$ at the same $A$ descends. A background derivative of a normalized expectation also survives: the derivative of $c$ cancels between numerator and denominator.\n\nBy contrast, an inhomogeneous identity specifying $L_A(1)=\\det C_A$ does not descend as a numerical identity. Nor does the claim $L_{A^g}(g\\cdot O)=L_A(O)$ for a fixed measure unless $c(A^g)=c(A)$. A gauge-variant scalar can create a measure transition character while leaving all normalized covariances gauge covariant. The normalized Ward theorem alone therefore cannot prove unit Jacobian or absence of determinant-line holonomy.\n\nOn the bounded family used here, a gauge-invariant scalar section CAN be explicitly selected. The representative $A(t)=L\\,dy_0+t\\,f(y_2)\\,dy_1$ is unique on each gauge orbit: equal curvature forces $t=s$ ($f'$ is not identically zero), and a gauge map stabilizing the same connection is constant on the connected torus. Transport $E$ and $F$ by the same charged multiplication $U_{g}$, as already proved. A constant stabilizer acts by the same charge phase in their equal-dimensional domain and target spaces, so $\\det C$ has no residual stabilizer factor. The exact multiplication identity $U_{gh}=U_gU_h$ provides the cocycle; this is not an assumption of a local chiral measure. The same reasoning permits all smooth gauge maps included through the transported-fibre construction, including any admitted nontrivial components, without identifying them with a sharp fixed Fourier cutoff.\n\nHence any nonzero scalar $c(t)$ extended constantly along these orbits has compatible transitions on THIS family. This proves bounded gauge consistency, not locality or an all-background anomaly theorem. Old event common phases are not altered by this mathematical choice.\n\n## 4. Continuum normalized cylinder functional and det5 section\n\nUse the accepted fixed-test covariance $G_{A}$ and its Wick functional $W_{A}$ on the algebra generated by finitely many smooth test fields. Enlarging the finite test list restricts to the previous Wick functional because every old contraction is unchanged. This yields a compatible algebraic Gaussian cylinder functional, not a claimed positive Grassmann probability measure. It includes the adopted neutral class and the already established controlled derivative laws.\n\nThe accepted relative determinant is nonzero on its common analytic disk:\n\n$$\nZ_5(t)=\\exp L_5(t),\\quad L_5(0)=0.\n$$\n\nThus $L_{5,A}(O)=Z_5(t)W_A(O)$ is a well-defined nonzero algebraic unnormalized section, with gauge transport as in section 3. More generally\n\n$$\nL_{(a,P),A}(O)=a \\exp[P(t)] Z_5(t) W_A(O),\\quad a\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nis another compatible section whenever P is a nominated smooth/analytic gauge-invariant scalar. All these sections give the same adopted normalized laws. For the reflection-preserving one-parameter convention one can restrict P to even functions and retain a fixed baseline phase. This is an explicit construction rather than an assumption that a continuum Berezin volume already exists.\n\nThe actual-overlap theorem supplies a particular regulator comparison:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nZ_M(t)&=Z_M(0) \\exp[c_{2,M} t^2+c_{4,M} t^4] Z_{5,M}(t), \\\\\nZ_{5,M} &\\to Z_5,\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith phases and derivatives controlled on the certified domain. Dividing by the retained scalar factors is legal for the normalized quotient and recovers precisely $W_{M}$. It does not prove that c2,M or c4,M are local counterterms, nor that a or P is a source-produced sea normalization. Calling P a counterterm does not provide the missing locality theorem.\n\n## 5. Exactly which composition maps descend\n\n(A) Test restriction and Wick contractions: descend by the cylinder construction.\n\n(B) Independent matter blocks: $W_{1\\oplus 2}(O_1O_2)=W_1(O_1)W_2(O_2)$, with fixed Grassmann ordering. Scalar normalizations multiply, so their projective quotient respects this product. $\\det_5$ also multiplies under direct sums because the defining regularized log traces add; no nontrivial product identity for noncommuting operators is being assumed.\n\n(C) Finite Gaussian elimination: for a block matrix $C=\\begin{bmatrix} A & B \\\\ C & H \\end{bmatrix}$ with invertible $H$ and Schur complement $S$, the exact identity is\n\n$$\n\\int d(\\text{high})\\,\\exp(-\\bar{\\psi}C\\psi)=\\det(H)\\,\\exp(-\\bar{\\psi}_{\\mathrm{low}}S\\psi_{\\mathrm{low}}),\n$$\n\nwith the fixed Grassmann ordering and all shifted source terms. After division by the full partition function, the low marginal is the normalized Gaussian for S. Therefore the marginal map descends to the quotient. The high determinant does not vanish: it is retained in the unnormalized recomposition identity and in any scalar-valued determinant comparison. Successive elimination is compatible as a normalized map while the separate high factors multiply according to the exact finite Schur identity.\n\n(D) Comparing raw determinants across cutoffs or backgrounds does not descend. The source and phase factors must remain in that comparison. No claim is made that coarse overlap equals the fine Schur matrix, or that finite thermal states form exact cylinders under Fourier inclusion; those lie outside the stated scope.\n\n## 7. Physical source records are not the Gaussian scalar quotient\n\nThe adopted source has fixed positive $\\rho_{N}$ and fixed preparation/detector instruments. Its raw denominator $d_N=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho_N\\Gamma)$, raw numerator components, empirical guard and all raw outcome laws are retained observable data. The theorem $L\\to cL$ does not authorize scaling $\\rho_{N}$, changing $d_{N}$, or changing any probability. In particular $\\rho_{N}$ must still have trace one. The parity denominator is not identified with $Z_{M}$, $Z_5$ or a sea partition function.\n\nA scalar Hamiltonian offset E0(t)I illustrates the distinction. At fixed t it cancels from normalized Gibbs $\\rho$ and from adjoint CAR dynamics, but the actual propagator gains exp[-i E0(t)s/hbar]. Under parameter changes it may alter source work, force or energy accounting. The source contract explicitly retains common phases, positive reserve bounds, waveforms and tariffs; no such offset is free or null in that ledger. Likewise the known preparation $\\kappa$ phase is retained even when a reduced state does not detect it.\n\nConsequently a safe pre-subtraction record consists of (normalized response class; regulator scalar record; physical source record). The normalized extractor factors through the first component, with an exact zero proof for changes only of the Gaussian scalar in that extractor. Determinant, vacuum-current and composition extractors still consume the second; invoice/common-phase/reserve extractors consume the third. No owner is deleted from either record.\n\n## 1. Differential character with the actual source calibration\n\nOn $Y=T^4$ with $x=\\ell y$ the real, globally defined dimensionless connection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= (\\ln 2)\\,dy_0+t f(y_2)\\,dy_1, \\\\\n\\Omega &= dA=t f'(y_2)\\,dy_2 \\wedge dy_1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor every smooth singular integral one-cycle $z$ define $h(z)=\\exp(i\\int_zA)$. Integration is additive on chains, so $h$ is a homomorphism. Every smooth integral two-chain $C$ obeys\n\n$$\nh(\\partial C)=\\exp\\Bigl(i \\int_C dA\\Bigr).\n$$\n\nAll closed two-cycle periods of $\\Omega$ are zero because $\\Omega$ is exact. In particular Omega/(2pi) has integral periods and specifies the trivial-bundle differential character with this possibly nontrivial flat holonomy. Exact curvature does NOT mean trivial one-cycle holonomy: the source-circle value is exp(i ln 2).\n\nFor flavour $Q$ the character is $h_Q=h^Q$, curvature $\\Omega_Q=Q\\,dA$. The adopted physical dictionary gives $A=(q_*/\\hbar)A_{\\mathrm{phys}}$, so $\\Omega_Q=(Qq_*/\\hbar)F_{\\mathrm{phys}}$. This is Q.0.7d2's $\\Omega=qF$ with its unit-action charge restored. Its G.6b derivative sign is recovered by $q_GA_G=-QA$, since the present derivative is $\\partial-iQA$. The single adopted controller identifies this holonomy with the actual event hold $\\Delta\\tau_A/\\hbar=\\ln 2$. It does not identify $\\ell/c$ and $\\tau_{A}$ or erase paid event pulse durations.\n\nEvery path subdivision commutes with extraction: $\\exp(iQ\\int_eA)=\\prod_{\\mathrm{children}}\\exp(iQ\\int_{\\mathrm{child}}A)$. On a smooth patch cover, local potentials differ by exact gauge forms; local endpoint phase factors cancel on composable edges and satisfy the ordinary triple-overlap cocycle. Here a single global potential is already available, so patch descent introduces no hidden bundle sector.\n\nThe gauge-response group can include all smooth maps $g:T^4\\to U(1)$, not merely maps admitting a global real logarithm. Set $A^g=A+(1/i)g^{-1}dg$. The added real one-form has $2\\pi$-integral periods, giving $h_Q^g=h_Q$ for integer $Q$. Local logarithms give the original endpoint transformation law. The spin structure is unchanged. Source-path endpoint frames and open-path transport are transformed, not suppressed.\n\n## 2. Exterior boundary applicability, including what is still not disposed of\n\nEuclidean $T^4$ has no exterior boundary. Physical spacetime is $\\mathbb{R}_s\\times T^3$ with the adopted static spatial connection and independently calibrated Lorentzian clock. There is no spatial exterior; smooth variations with compact support in $s$ give no temporal endpoint contribution. For the actual Weyl action\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_Q &= \\int ds\\, d^3x\\, \\psi_Q^\\dagger(i \\hbar \\partial_s-h_Q)\\psi_Q, \\\\\nh_Q &= c\\, \\alpha\\cdot(-i \\hbar \\nabla-Qq_* A_{\\mathrm{phys}}),\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nintegration by parts is therefore valid on the declared domain. Its classical on-shell continuity equation is $\\partial_s(q_Q\\psi^\\dagger\\psi)+\\operatorname{div}(q_Qc\\,\\psi^\\dagger\\alpha\\psi)=0$. This supplies the needed exterior integration-by-parts applicability for this chiral action; it does not pretend that the scalar action in G.6b itself is the Weyl action. Smooth compactly supported test variations require no spatial boundary apparatus.\n\nFor the ADOPTED NORMALIZED source, finite gauge-response transport is exact and composes: $H_N^g=gH_N$; CAR maps send $a(f)$ to $a(gf)$; preparation, detector and external test frames transform together. Exterior-algebra lifts between these finite regulated fibers are functorial, so a closed gauge descriptor loop with identity endpoint induces the identity normalized response map. Constant stabilizers act trivially on the retained neutral outputs. Passing to the controlled fixed-test limit preserves these equalities. Thus no nonzero anomalous character of THIS normalized response functor calls for an exterior bulk supplier. This is a proved closed-branch applicability statement, not an assertion that every chiral determinant-line connection is trivial.\n\nAn unnormalized gauge-measure phase is a different structure. The fixed relative $\\det_5$ section and frame/cocycle construction supply the distinct unnormalized structure on this declared family; they assert no local-counterterm theorem on an enlarged family. Zero exterior boundary and exact normalized transport do not decide its global gauge holonomy. Any later unnormalized measure extension must independently show whether a residual relative/inflow datum exists. The cancellation sums $\\sum Q=\\sum Q^3=0$ are retained but not promoted into an unstated all-bordisms theorem. No exterior inflow primitive is invented here.\n\nInternal region boundaries remain present and are addressed next.\n\n## 3. Smooth currents from the actual adopted source\n\nLet $P_{N}$ be the common spatial Fourier projection, extended identically on the spinor components, and let $K_N=P_NKP_{N}$ with continuous periodic Euclidean $u=x_3$. For one flavour take fixed smooth source tests $h$ in the output/right-handed space and $g$ in the input/left-handed space, with the effective trigonometric subclass used for finite descriptors. Put\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\psi&=K^{-1}h,\\quad \\chi=K^{-*}g, \\\\\n\\psi_N&=K_N^{-1}P_N h,\\quad \\chi_N=K_N^{-*}P_N g, \\\\\nj_\\mu&=\\chi^{\\dagger} \\gamma_\\mu \\psi,\\quad j_{\\mu,N}=\\chi_N^{\\dagger} \\gamma_\\mu \\psi_N.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll are the same inverse kernels exported by the adopted positive source through its parity ratio. h and g are source labels, not additional charged dynamical matter. The bilinear is charge neutral.\n\nFor any fixed smooth compactly supported-in-chart or periodic profile $a_{\\mu}$ define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ(a)&=\\sum_\\mu \\int a_\\mu j_\\mu, \\\\\nJ_N(a)&=\\sum_\\mu \\int a_\\mu j_{\\mu,N}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese are native-derived connected responses, not merely values of an unrelated matrix calculator. At finite $N$ introduce the Euclidean, time-ordered quadratic insertion $I(a)=\\int\\bar{\\psi}\\gamma_\\mu a_\\mu\\psi$, with $\\bar{\\psi}=\\psi^\\dagger\\gamma_3$, and the outer two-field test $O(g,h)$. Choose the outer ordering so its normalized Gaussian expectation is $\\langle g,K_N^{-1}h\\rangle$. Wick expansion with this ordering gives\n\n$$\nR_N[O I(a)]-R_N[O] R_N[I(a)]=J_N(a).\n$$\n\nFor clarity, in Gaussian indices $\\langle\\psi_i\\bar{\\psi}_j\\rangle=G_{ij}$ and $I=\\bar{\\psi}V\\psi$, the connected part of $\\langle\\psi_i\\bar{\\psi}_jI\\rangle$ is $+(GVG)_{ij}$. Therefore varying the action by $V$ gives $\\delta\\langle O\\rangle=-J_N(V)$, with no missing minus sign. The Euclidean derivative $\\delta K=-iQ\\gamma\\cdot\\delta A$ produces $\\delta\\langle O\\rangle=iQJ_N(\\delta A)$. In $y$ coordinates it carries the factor $1/\\ell$.\n\nEvery term on the left is even and per-flavour neutral. Its real and imaginary parts are measured by the already adopted finite P4 contraction readout on fresh P1-P3 trials; products of ratios are formed using independently recorded ensembles and their finite error bounds. All extra test components, $\\Lambda$ values, trial counts and stocks are added before the experiment, with the same tariffs/retention rules. No new physical gauge drive is required to measure a mathematically specified insertion profile.\n\nThe local insertion is rigorously a limit within the adopted test class: at finite $N$ the spatial field has finitely many modes, and its time evolution is a finite matrix exponential. Use a fixed one-sided time-order prescription and approximate the insertion by finite sums of smooth separated time/test smearings. Their connected Wick contractions converge to $J_N(a)$; the possible equal-time CAR constant is a disconnected term and cancels in the connected subtraction. Matrix-exponential norm/derivative bounds give a finite approximation error for each $N$. Smooth time/profile quadratures can be replaced by the dense effective trigonometric tests to any certified tolerance. Thus no new $\\delta$-function probe or microscopic point detector has been presumed. Pointwise bilinear formulae identify the limit of existing readout descriptors.\n\nThe individual $R_N[I]$ term can contain a large sea/vacuum contribution. It remains in finite records and error amplification until the explicit connected subtraction. Convergence is claimed only for the connected combination. This is not a construction of the separately renormalized vacuum current.\n\nThe $\\gamma$/units map is explicit: the Hamiltonian density insertion for the Euclidean direction 3 is $\\psi^\\dagger\\psi$; for spatial $j$ it is $\\psi^\\dagger\\gamma_3\\gamma_j\\psi=-i\\psi^\\dagger\\alpha_j\\psi$. Thus physical Lorentzian charge/current densities have the factors $q_{Q}$ and $iq_{Q}c$ respectively relative to these formal Euclidean bilinears. The present time-ordered connected law is not itself the expectation of the Lorentzian probability current at real time. Physical real-time local currents may be studied using the same Hamiltonian, but their vacuum coincident limits are not supplied by this theorem.\n\n## 4. Exact finite leakage/contact balance\n\nThe continuum product rule, using $K\\psi=h$ and $K^\\dagger\\chi=g$, gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{div} j=s:=\\chi^\\dagger h-g^\\dagger \\psi.\n$$\n\nA naive local equation at finite Fourier cutoff is false. Let $V=K-K_0$ be the bounded smooth multiplication perturbation, and define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{\\psi,N}&=(1-P_N) V \\psi_N, \\\\\nr_{\\chi,N}&=(1-P_N) V^{\\dagger} \\chi_N.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the flat derivative operator commutes with $P_{N}$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nK \\psi_N&=P_N h+r_{\\psi,N}, \\\\\nK^{\\dagger} \\chi_N&=P_N g+r_{\\chi,N}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the EXACT pointwise finite identity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{div} j_N &= s_N+e_N, \\\\\ns_N &= \\chi_N^\\dagger P_N h-(P_N g)^\\dagger \\psi_N, \\\\\ne_N &= \\chi_N^\\dagger r_{\\psi,N}-r_{\\chi,N}^\\dagger \\psi_N.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor every smooth scalar test $\\phi$,\n\n$$\n\\int (\\nabla \\phi)\\cdot j_N=-\\int \\phi s_N-\\int \\phi e_N.\n$$\n\nThe last term is the regulator's actual high-mode leakage. It is not dropped or called nearest-edge flux. Each of these expressions is a finite sum/limit of the same neutral source readouts and their contact terms. In particular $s_{N}$ consists of external-source contact responses, not a new vacuum insertion.\n\nEquivalent exact finite gauge transport uses the entire moving regulated fiber and its endpoint tests. A fixed-space compressed multiplication generator $M=P_N\\phi P_N$ produces a commutator whose difference from $P_N[K,\\phi]P_N$ is\n\n$$\n-P_NK(1-P_N)\\phi P_N+P_N\\phi(1-P_N)KP_N.\n$$\n\nThose terms, together with the projected external-test tails, are exactly the nonlocal corrections needed to match the source-contact identity. Thus the smooth insertion identity is consistent with the adopted gauge diagram, rather than an assumption that a fixed Fourier cutoff commutes with local gauge multiplication.\n\nAt the continuum limit the signs agree with the contact law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\niQ \\int (\\nabla \\phi)\\cdot j \\\\\n&=iQ(\\langle g,\\phi K^{-1}h\\rangle-\\langle g,K^{-1}(\\phi h)\\rangle) \\\\\n&=\\langle g,\\delta_\\phi(K^{-1})h\\rangle,\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\delta_\\phi K=-iQ \\gamma\\cdot\\nabla \\phi.\n$$\n\nAll flavour factors are applied separately; summing flavours is optional and does not hide an anomalous individual vacuum loop.\n\n## 5. Quantitative smooth-vector control of the leakage\n\nThe already proved Galerkin estimate gives $\\lVert\\psi_N-P_N\\psi\\rVert_{L^2}\\le C_k(1+N)^{-k}$ for every fixed $k$ and smooth $h$, uniformly over the retained real $t$ interval. It applies also to $\\chi$ and to every fixed Euclidean-time derivative, because $B$ is static and $\\partial_u$ commutes with the inverse. Spatial derivatives of a $P_N$-vector of order $m$ cost at most $C_m(1+N)^m$. Applying the estimate with $k$ increased by $m$, then adding the smooth Fourier tail of $\\psi$, proves convergence in every fixed mixed Sobolev norm. On the compact four-torus, a Fourier Cauchy-Schwarz estimate with more than two spare derivatives converts those bounds into uniform $C^r$ convergence for any fixed $r$.\n\nMultiplication by smooth $V$ preserves those norms. The tail of $V\\psi_{N}$ splits into the tail of $V\\psi$ plus $V(\\psi_N-\\psi)$, and likewise for $\\chi$. Consequently for every fixed $k$ there is a computable smooth-test constant $C_{k}$ with\n\n$$\n\\lVert j_N-j\\rVert_{C^1}+\\lVert s_N-s\\rVert_{L^1}+\\lVert e_N\\rVert_{L^1}\\le C_k(1+N)^{-k}.\n$$\n\nThe constants depend on finitely many derivatives of the fixed tests and bump, the uniform inverse bound and physical units; they are not cutoff-independent for arbitrary rough test families. This establishes disappearance of the ACTUAL leakage on the declared external-source responses, rather than asserting locality in operator norm on all ultraviolet states.\n\nFor smooth $\\phi$ the Ward error is bounded by $\\lVert\\phi\\rVert_\\infty C_k(1+N)^{-k}$ on the contact/leakage side. This formulation avoids estimating $\\nabla\\phi$ when approaching an indicator.\n\n## 6. Controlled internal-region limit with all contacts retained\n\nLet R be a nominated region of the physical Euclidean four-torus with compact C2 boundary and specified orientation. No source support is assumed disjoint from R. For the continuum smooth fields,\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Flux}_R:=\\int_{\\partial R} n_\\mu j_\\mu\\, d\\Sigma=\\int_R s.\n$$\n\nThe right side includes both external-source contacts, $\\chi^\\dagger h$ and $-g^\\dagger\\psi$. Removing these contacts would produce a false zero-flux statement. $R=T^4$ gives zero total flux and cancellation of the integrated contacts.\n\nChoose smooth $0\\le\\phi_\\epsilon\\le 1$ converging to $1_R$, differing only in a tubular layer of thickness $C\\epsilon$. The $C^2$ boundary gives layer volume $\\le C_R\\epsilon$ for sufficiently small $\\epsilon$ (from the normal-coordinate Jacobian). Define the outward smeared flux as\n\n$$\nF_\\epsilon=-\\int \\nabla \\phi_\\epsilon\\cdot j=\\int \\phi_\\epsilon s.\n$$\n\nThen $|F_\\epsilon-\\mathrm{Flux}_R|\\le C_R\\epsilon\\lVert s\\rVert_\\infty$. At finite cutoff define the total corrected native exporter\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_{N,\\epsilon} &= -\\int \\nabla \\phi_\\epsilon\\cdot j_N \\\\\n&=\\int \\phi_\\epsilon(s_N+e_N).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the exact finite current/contact identity, with leakage retained. Uniformly in $\\epsilon$,\n\n$$\n\\lvert F_{N,\\epsilon}-\\mathrm{Flux}_R\\rvert\\le C_k(1+N)^{-k}+C_R\\epsilon\\lVert s\\rVert_\\infty.\n$$\n\nThus the double limit can be taken with an explicit choice of $N$ and $\\epsilon$; no illicit fixed-smooth-test theorem is applied to a cutoff-dependent discontinuous multiplier. In particular choose $\\epsilon$ first for the desired boundary tolerance, then $N$ for the smooth-source bound, then the finite insertion-smearing/quadrature and statistical precision. The bound also allows diagonal sequences once the finite descriptor/numerical errors are controlled. Because $j_N\\to j$ uniformly, its ordinary surface integral converges separately with error $\\le\\operatorname{Area}(\\partial R)\\lVert j_N-j\\rVert_\\infty$. The surface formula is the same limiting response, not a new bare surface detector.\n\nFor a simpler sharp INTERNAL CONTACT exporter one may set $\\phi=1_R$ on the right, obtaining $\\int_R(s_N+e_N)$. The same $L^1$ bound proves convergence without differentiating the discontinuous indicator. Native implementation still uses certified smooth-profile approximations and recorded integration tolerances; the indicator is not silently admitted as an exact primitive test. Effective regions require computable geometry/tubular constants to produce explicit finite error certificates; abstract $C^2$ regions retain the mathematical statement only.\n\nFinite readout error is propagated through the connected subtraction, finite sums and products using the already adopted Lambda/dbar bounds. Allocate an error budget to each term before choosing its finite trials. The empirical parity guard and abort-probability qualification are unchanged. This establishes a total family of finite native approximations to a region flux/contact response. It is not a promise of a fixed-cost sharp measurement at arbitrarily small $\\epsilon$.\n\n## 1. Exact integrated model statement\n\nOn the declared nonempty static curved $U(1)$ torus family and its specified gauge-response orbit, there is a genuinely chiral event-character matter construction with:\n\n1. one shared calibrated event/connection input and charged differential character;\n2. a compatible algebraic chiral measure section, explicitly normalized by the phase-preserving relative det5 scheme and obtained as a controlled renormalized limit of the actual overlap regulator;\n3. a normalized continuum Gaussian field law exported by an independently constructed, already adopted positive finite common-CAR source with minimally coupled Lorentzian dynamics;\n4. exact gauge/character/refinement/descent and typed composition maps, controlled fixed-test values and derivatives, and the applicable closed-branch/compact-support and internal-region Ward maps.\n\nThe mathematical unnormalized section and the physical normalized extractor are connected by division by the SAME section's nonzero value at 1. This is the declared realization map. It is not a claim that a physical parity denominator equals a vacuum determinant. Historical unsubtracted overlap determinants, their lower coefficients, and finite nonlocal vacuum responses remain distinct retained quantities, not zero sources.\n\nThe problem addressed here is to extend to chiral matter and construct a continuum physical realization. As stated, it does not quantify over all topologies, all physically time-dependent gauge controls, all renormalization schemes or all vacuum instruments. This construction supplies one sufficient declared branch, with genuine curvature variations and a separating fixed-test family. It does not narrow any quantifier INTERNAL to that branch: every declared gauge descriptor, source test, registered loop/subdivision, and admitted region operation must satisfy its stated map.\n\n## 2. Exact declared domain and common input\n\nFix $\\ell>0$, $\\tau_A>0$, $\\hbar,c,q_*>0$ and $r=\\ell/(c\\tau_A)>0$. Do not set $r=1$. Let $L=\\ln 2$ and choose the previously certified small real interval $|t|\\le t_0$, with the narrower common complex disk used whenever analytic derivative or determinant estimates require it. The profile is the retained smooth bump $f(y_2)$, supported away $y_2=0$, with $f'$ not identically zero.\n\nUse the trivial line bundle on Euclidean $Y=T^4_\\ell$, periodic spin structure, and\n\n$$\nA(t)=L\\, dy_0+t f(y_2)\\,dy_1,\\quad y=x/\\ell.\n$$\n\nThe physical real-time space is $\\mathbb{R}_s\\times T^3_\\ell$ with spatial coordinates $x_0,x_1,x_2$ and Euclidean auxiliary direction $u=x_3$. Its static controller supplies\n\n$$\nA_{\\mathrm{phys}}=(\\hbar/q_*)[L\\,dx_0/\\ell+t\\,f(x_2/\\ell)\\,dx_1/\\ell],\\quad \\Phi=0.\n$$\n\nThe ten ordered LEFT-handed flavours have charges (-9,-5,-1,7,8), each twice, in units $q_*$. There are no opposite-charge pairs and no gauge-invariant quadratic mass pairing. $\\sum Q=\\sum Q^3=0$ is retained as a representation check, not substituted for a general anomaly theorem. Preparation ancillas are finite charged registers, not additional propagating right-handed fields.\n\nThe already adopted common controller enforces\n\n$$\n\\Delta \\tau_A/\\hbar=L=(q_{*}/\\hbar) \\int_{\\mathrm{source}} A_{\\mathrm{phys}}.\n$$\n\nThus the actual signed event record $N_{\\mathrm{event}}$ produces $\\exp(iQN_{\\mathrm{event}}L)$, and the connection consumed by the matter Hamiltonian is the same calibrated object, not an independently tuned duplicate. The source loop lies at $y_1=y_2=y_3=0$ and remains unchanged by $t$. All old event pulse overhead, common phases, endpoint frames, failures and invoices stay attached. No loop length is identified with the event hold duration.\n\nPhysical native gauge transformations are time-independent smooth spatial $U(1)$ maps, with all test/control frames transformed. Mathematical Euclidean response transport also admits the specified smooth periodic maps $T^4\\to U(1)$, using transported regulated fibres and the derivative-of-$g$ term. This is not an independently driven real-time electric-source claim. Bundle, spin structure and retained descriptor identities remain fixed.\n\nNative outputs are finite products/linear combinations of per-flavour neutral even Wick tests with effective smooth trigonometric descriptors, plus their certified limits and connected current/contact/region exporters established by the boundary certificate. The neutral bilinears separate the covariance; Wick determinants and vanishing unmatched-flavour moments determine the nominated Gaussian law. Arbitrary rough cutoff-dependent tests and an unpriced charged phase reference are not asserted.\n\n## 3. Consumer-typed dictionary\n\n**E: event/geometry.** Input: raw signed event records and the shared $A$. Output: $h_Q(z)=\\exp(iQ\\int_zA)$, endpoint transport, curvature $Q\\,dA$, chain/refinement identities. Consumer: original event-character and connection clauses.\n\n**M: mathematical unnormalized measure.** Input: the same $A,Q$,spin/domain and nominated relative scheme. Output: $L_{5,A}(O)=Z_5(A)\\,W_A(O)$, with $Z_5(0)=1$, including its complex phase. Consumer: the analogous chiral determinant/measure, gauge transition and homogeneous Grassmann Ward package. It is an algebraic Gaussian cylinder functional, not a positive classical probability measure.\n\n**N: normalized field law.** Output: $W_A(O)=L_{5,A}(O)/L_{5,A}(1)$, with contractions of $K_A^{-1}$. Consumer: the declared continuum field tests, derivatives, connected source currents and Ward/contact identities.\n\n**P: positive physical source.** Output: fresh-trial positive $\\rho_{N}$, retained numerator/denominator binary laws and their empirical guarded ratio. Its ratio converges to N. Consumer: physical realization of N and the associated error-certified connected/region exporters. P is not identified with the possibly nonpositive periodic graded functional N before extraction.\n\n**H: historical regulator/vacuum record.** Output: unsubtracted phase-fixed $Z_M$, $Z_M(0)$, $c_{2,M},c_{4,M}$, exact high-mode Schur determinants, and separately proved finite nonlocal quadratic response. These remain retained nonzero mathematical quantities. No equality of H with $Z_5$ is asserted without its explicit subtraction/recomposition formula.\n\n**S: physical source ledger.** Output: all actual waveforms, common phases, charged ancillas, raw outcomes, denominator guards, energy offsets/reserves, failures, unused stock, tariffs and clocks. It is retained in full and is not quotiented by a mathematical Gaussian scalar.\n\nThe only scalar-null assertion is proved for the N extractor: multiplying $L$ by nonzero $c(A)$ leaves $L(O)/L(1)$, its fixed-order derivatives and homogeneous Ward laws unchanged. It does not erase H or S. No physical absolute-$Z$/vacuum instrument is introduced as an unexplained primitive or silently claimed.\n\n## 4. Actual measure/regulator construction and its gauge law\n\nThe overlap branch has explicit Wilson gap, phase-fixed analytic polar frames $E,F$ and algebraic holomorphic dual $E^\\sharp$. The exact finite Weyl $C_M=F^\\dagger D_ME$ is invertible on the common bounded family. Its measure is an actual finite Berezin functional, not a determinant symbol assigned at one point. Gauge transport acts on both frames; equal ranks cancel constant-stabilizer determinant phases. The group multiplication law supplies the transition cocycle on the declared orbit.\n\nThe accepted ultraviolet theorem proves, with phases and derivatives,\n\n$$\n[Z_M(t)/Z_M(0)] \\exp[-c_{2,M} t^2-c_{4,M} t^4] \\to Z_5(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the odd coefficients vanish by the proved proper-rotation symmetry. The relative logarithm is anchored at zero and\n\n$$\nZ_5(t)=\\det_5(1+t V K_0^{-1})|_{H_+},\n$$\n\nwith the equivalent left-space cyclic orientation established. Actual S5 convergence includes high-momentum tails and nonlinear-vertex/Gram estimates; it is not inferred from fixed-test convergence alone. On the common disk it is nonzero. Baseline and lower factors are retained in the exact recomposition identity rather than misdescribed as proved local counterterms.\n\nDefine $L_{5,A}(O)=Z_5(t)W_A(O)$. Fixed-test restrictions are compatible because Wick contractions restrict exactly; the value at 1 is always $Z_5$. Extend the scalar constantly along each gauge orbit. Representatives $t$ are unique because curvature determines $t$; stabilizers of a fixed connection are constant and act compatibly on equal-rank frames. This constructs a gauge-consistent algebraic section over the entire declared family and its gauge-response orbit, including the specified component transport. No all-bordism theorem or local determinant-line connection is asserted.\n\nThe unnormalized Ward equation follows without dropping $Z_5$: if $W_A(Q_AO)=0$, then $L_{5,A}(Q_AO)=Z_5W_A(Q_AO)=0$. Gauge invariance of $Z_5$ and transport of $W$ give invariance of the full section. Its smooth background derivative is exactly\n\n$$\n\\partial L_{5,A}(O)=(\\partial Z_5)W_A(O)+Z_5\\,\\partial W_A(O).\n$$\n\nThe scalar first term is retained. It is not falsely equated with the bare coincident matter current. The printed original measure/gauge/Ward analogue is thus distinguished from an additional vacuum-force normalization prescription.\n\nThis scheme is deliberately relative and need not preserve unsubtracted quadratic vacuum susceptibilities. The separated-profile theorem demonstrates that some such susceptibilities are finite and nonlocal. They remain in H; this construction never calls their deletion a local renormalization or a zero theorem. Whether this typed relative section suffices for any wider formulation is a question of scope, not a new analytic claim.\n\n## 5. Exact join to the positive physical CAR source\n\nFor the SAME $A$ and charges, use $K_A=\\sum\\gamma_\\mu(\\partial_\\mu-iQA_\\mu)$ in physical coordinates. With $\\alpha_j=i\\gamma_3\\gamma_{j}$ on the left-handed space,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nK_A &= \\gamma_3(\\partial_u+B_A), \\\\\nB_A &= \\alpha\\cdot(-i \\nabla-Q A), \\\\\nK_A^{-1} &= (\\partial_u+B_A)^{-1} \\gamma_3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the explicit operator dictionary between the overlap continuum target and the physical source target. All factors of $\\ell$ are supplied by $x=\\ell y$; physical $h_A=\\hbar cB_{A}$. Physical clock $s$ is independent of Euclidean $u$, and $\\beta_E=\\ell/(\\hbar c)$.\n\nUse fixed spatial Fourier $H_{N}$ with $D_N=20(2N+1)^3$ and $B_N=P_NB_AP_N$. The uniform gap $\\delta>0$ and common $H^1$ domain are proved. On $\\mathrm{CAR}(H)$, the positive Gaussian state covariance is $C_A=(1+\\exp(\\ell B_A))^{-1}$; its existence follows from consistent finite positive Gaussian restrictions. A common infinite sea-vacuum unitary is neither assumed nor needed.\n\nAt each finite descriptor, the adopted source constructs $\\rho_{N}$ with covariance $C_{N}$ by a paid charged ancilla purification and explicit positive preparation waveform, retaining its scalar phase and matter drift. Neutral finite instruments measure numerator $\\operatorname{Re}/\\operatorname{Im}\\,\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho_N\\Gamma O_N)$, and parity gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd_N&=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho_N \\Gamma)>0, \\\\\nR_N(O)&=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho_N \\Gamma O_N)/d_N.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe exact finite graded calculation gives the periodic inverse $(\\partial_u+B_N)^{-1}\\gamma_3$ and its Wick laws. This is the same $K_A^{-1}$ target as M/N. Consequently the realization map is\n\n$$\nL_{5,A} \\to W_A \\to \\lim_N R_N,\n$$\n\nwith the first arrow exact normalization and the second the proved field/source comparison. It is not a spectator tensor-product argument or a second unrelated background.\n\nAll positive-state probabilities, ratios, common phases and raw denominator values remain typed as P/S. In particular $d_{N}$ is neither $Z_{M}$ nor Z5. The source scalar excitation-energy offset and controller $\\kappa$ are retained physical records, not the relative determinant subtraction.\n\n## 6. Convergence, derivatives and finite error maps\n\nThe overlap construction has uniform inverse/domain/stencil estimates and controlled fixed-smooth-test errors, together with complex-disk Cauchy estimates for every fixed derivative order. The independent CAR Galerkin construction has\n\n$$\n\\lVert L_N^{-1}P_Nh-L^{-1}h\\rVert\\le(1+v/\\delta)\\lVert(1-P_N)L^{-1}h\\rVert,\n$$\n\nwhich is bounded by $C_k(1+N)^{-k}$ for every fixed smooth test and fixed $k$. Uniform complex inverse bounds justify derivative estimates directly. Wick determinants converge by finite-product bounds. These are compatible routes to the SAME fixed-test continuum object; no equality of finite regulators is required.\n\nThe parity denominator has a proved positive infinite-product lower bound uniform in $N,t$, certified by finite shells and exponential tails. Every finite descriptor retains a rational $\\bar{d}>0$ certificate. The adopted empirical denominator guard precedes every division. Source laws are exact in the nominated ideal mode; finite sampling has its Hoeffding/union-bound confidence with all scale factors $\\Lambda$ and systematic-error allocations retained. Failure-free guarantees, arbitrary-abort qualification and bounded-abort $\\beta$ are those of the probability statement accompanying the preparation-error convention above; no outcome-selected replacement is permitted.\n\nError order is explicit: choose continuum/test or region tolerance; choose N and any smoothing/geometry tolerance from the proved estimates; choose finite descriptor approximation and compilation errors; allocate ratio/connected-product error budgets; reserve finite trials and stocks; then perform the guarded extractor. No fixed-cost perfect continuum measurement, fixed finite sample with zero error, or uncontrolled rough-test passage is asserted.\n\n## 7. Gauge, refinement, descent and composition\n\nGeometric path subdivision is exact because line integrals add and endpoint phases cancel. Registered event relations keep their original winding character. Subdivision does not alter A, K, Z5 or W; extra physical event durations and invoices remain in S.\n\nFourier inclusion preserves CAR generators exactly and matrix compression is compatible. The finite positive states are not asserted to be exact cylinders; they converge on the declared tests. The overlap finite Schur pushforward separately retains its exact complex high determinant and shifted sources. Neither map is identified with edge subdivision or with coarse overlap equality.\n\nGeneral spatial gauge multiplication does not preserve a sharp fixed Fourier subspace. Instead $H_N^g=gH_N$, $B_N^g=gB_Ng^{-1}$ and all tests/preparations/instruments transport together. Exterior-algebra lifts compose exactly. Euclidean-time-dependent response frames transport the entire differential operator, including derivative-of-$g$. This avoids the false fixed-cutoff local gauge identity.\n\nNormalized test restriction, Gaussian marginalization and independent-block composition descend to N. Independent blocks multiply Z5 by direct-sum trace additivity. Unnormalized Schur recomposition retains detH and is used only with its proved finite identity. A claim of generic noncommuting det5 multiplicativity is not used. The integrated diagram preserves each operation in its actual domain rather than promoting unrelated discretizations to exact equality.\n\n## 8. Character, exterior and internal boundary conditions\n\nThe boundary/current certificate supplies $h_Q(\\partial C)=\\exp(iQ\\int_C dA)$ for EVERY admitted smooth integral two-chain. Since $A$ is global, curvature periods on closed two-cycles vanish integrally, while the source-circle holonomy remains $\\exp(iQL)$. For gauges without global logarithms, the added form has $2\\pi$-integral periods, so integer charges preserve closed holonomies. Patch cocycles are exact; no hidden bundle sector is required.\n\nEuclidean T4 has no exterior. Physical $R_{s}$ times T3 has no spatial exterior and compact-time-support variations remove temporal endpoint terms. The actual minimally coupled Weyl action, not the scalar G.6b action renamed, supplies the needed integration-by-parts and classical continuity applicability. The gauge-consistent measure section of section 4 has trivial declared transition character; together with the normalized source transport, no additional exterior inflow supplier is required on THIS branch. This is not an all-gauge-anomaly classification.\n\nInternal regions remain nontrivial. The boundary certificate constructs connected native bilinears $j_\\mu=\\chi^\\dagger\\gamma_\\mu\\psi$ for $\\psi=K^{-1}h$, $\\chi=K^{-*}g$ and retains external contacts $\\operatorname{div}j=\\chi^\\dagger h-g^\\dagger\\psi$. Finite Fourier leakage is explicit, not discarded: $\\operatorname{div}j_N=s_N+e_N$. Smooth-source Sobolev bounds make $e_{N}$ vanish with a controlled rate. Connected insertion differences are finite neutral tests realized by the adopted source, including their large individual disconnected terms and full error propagation.\n\nFor the nominated effective $C^2$ regions, smooth-cutoff or internal-contact exporters converge to the oriented flux with error of the form $C_k(1+N)^{-k}+C_R\\epsilon\\lVert s\\rVert_\\infty$, plus finite descriptor and sampling errors. Thus no illegal rough-test inference or new sharp detector is hidden. Euclidean insertion/current factors and Lorentzian charge/current factors remain distinct as stated in the final certificate. This recovers the applicable internal Ward/region content with contacts; it does not claim a renormalized vacuum current or zero flux in the presence of external insertions.\n\nThe connected-current equations use the authoritative single global fermionic time ordering before smearing stated above.\n\n## 9. Source inventory and retained non-null records\n\nConsume the adopted P1–P5 boundary exactly: fresh system/charged ancilla registers; common static field/calibration; positive timed control; neutral even instrument/pointer/copy; clock, compiler, memory, error and holding services. $D_{N}$ modes and their exponentially large finite Hilbert space are not confused. All positive tariffs, integer reserves, waveform entries, memories, clocks and retention windows remain those of the source contract above.\n\nEach new connected or region test expands the descriptor, $\\Lambda$ bounds, trials and paid stock BEFORE the run. Failed or unused stocks and original event invoices remain, with actual duration extensions. Exact computably described controls are the adopted ideal mode; approximate controls require their separate certificates. No physical vacuum offset, baseline phase, local counterterm, or supplier becomes free because N is a ratio.\n\nThe retained audit object is (E,M,N,P,H,S), with declared consumer maps. H's lower coefficients and the historical separated nonlocal response remain observable in their original mathematical definitions, while M has its explicitly different relative scheme. S retains raw physical phases and costs. This is a checked change of normalized functional section, not erasure of old outputs.\n\n## 11. Fixed-section response faithfulness and exact scope adjudication\n\nThe model fixes $Z_5$ once as the derived function of $A$ in section 4. Arbitrary functions $a\\exp(P)$ are NOT additional admissible physical states of this proposed model. Their existence in the quotient theorem explained a potential ambiguity; it is not an uncontrolled degree of freedom retained here. The exact mathematical output is ($A$ modulo declared gauge, $L_{5,A}$), with $L_{5,A}$ determined by $A$ and the fixed scheme. The physical descriptor consumes that same $A$, and its normalized response extractor returns $L_{5,A}(O)/L_{5,A}(1)$.\n\nThe neutral test set is separating in the needed sense. Equality of all its bilinear values gives equality of every matrix element of $G_A=K_A^{-1}$ on a dense smooth test space, hence equality of the bounded inverse. The range is the common operator domain, so inversion recovers $K_{A}$. At the fixed gauge representative $K_t-K_s=(t-s)V$ with $V$ nonzero; therefore $t=s$. Under transported gauge frames the statement is equality modulo the declared gauge relation. Thus an independent scalar state has not been collapsed by the physical extractor: there is no such variable in the fixed-section model, and the retained background is recoverable from its normalized field law. This is not a claim that a finite chosen sample identifies $t$ perfectly; faithfulness is a statement about the declared family of exact response laws, with finite inference errors separately controlled.\n\nThe physical source quotient used for this comparison is the test/gauge response quotient together with its retained records. Preparations or tariffs are not identified merely because one normalized number agrees; S remains available to every original source-accounting consumer. Likewise Z5 is an explicitly retained mathematical output, not a newly promised physical amplitude detector.\n\nThe preceding construction supplies the common A/K/charges, fixed-section faithfulness, gauge/unnormalized Ward and used-composition maps. The physical source, fixed mathematical section and historical comparison records remain distinct. The closed-branch current/region result combines with the proved measure transition law; closed spacetime alone is not a measure-consistency proof.\n\nThe defined source includes no physical unsubtracted-vacuum-susceptibility consumer; all historical mathematical comparison outputs remain retained. Historical nonlocal susceptibilities remain valid mathematical comparison outputs. If a future extension asks for a physical vacuum force, local subtraction preserving the enlarged-profile unsubtracted determinant, a dynamical gauge source, a different spin/bundle sector or arbitrary exterior boundary, its additional maps require new proof. Such future extensions are outside this nominated existential witness, not disproved or retroactively supplied.\n\n## 1. Family, units and source\n\nUse dimensionless $y=x/\\ell$ in the unit four-torus with periodic spin structure, $Q=(-9,-5,-1,7,8)$, each twice, and $L=\\ln 2$. Set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(t,s) &= L\\, dy_0+[t f(y_2)+s g(y_2)]\\,dy_1, \\\\\nK_q(t,s) &= \\ell^{-1} \\sum_\\mu \\gamma_\\mu(\\partial_\\mu-iq A_\\mu).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe real smooth periodic profiles vanish on a neighborhood of $y_2=0$. The source circle $y_1=y_2=y_3=0$, its orientation, toron, event character $\\exp(iL)$, slab identification and comb-tree conditions are unchanged. Restrict $|t|\\,\\lVert f\\rVert_\\infty+|s|\\,\\lVert g\\rVert_\\infty$ to the previously certified small bound. Both directions are genuine nonflat deformations. The inverses, normalized source responses and complex parameter derivatives already constructed apply on this common polydomain. All source, high-mode and phase ledgers remain retained.\n\nWrite $S_q=(\\ell K_q(0,0))^{-1}$, $v_f=-iq\\gamma_1f$ and $v_g=-iq\\gamma_1g$. Products $vS$ are dimensionless, so the quadratic log response below has no remaining power of $\\ell$. We compute the anchored log-determinant mixed derivative, rather than the coefficient of $t^{2}$: its formal value is\n\n$$\nB_q(f,g)=-\\operatorname{Tr}_{H_+}(v_f S_q v_g S_q).\n$$\n\nFor separated supports this trace exists without a coincident-point prescription, as shown below. It is the connected two-insertion loop, with the minus sign fixed by differentiating $\\log(1+tT_f+sT_g)$. The chirality is the same $H_+$/right-space orientation used in the accepted $\\det_5$ join.\n\n## 2. Exact one-dimensional Green kernel\n\nFourier transform y0,y1,y3. Put\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np&=(2\\pi k_0-qL,\\ 2\\pi k_1,\\ 2\\pi k_3),\\quad \\omega=|p|>0, \\\\\nd&=\\operatorname{dist}(u,\\mathbb{Z}) \\in [0,1/2].\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar inverse of $-\\partial_2^2+\\omega^2$ has kernel\n\n$$\nG_\\omega(u)=\\cosh[\\omega(1/2-d)]/[2 \\omega \\sinh(\\omega/2)].\n$$\n\nThis follows directly by solving the homogeneous ODE off $u=0$, imposing periodicity and the derivative jump $-G'(0+)+G'(0-)=1$. Hence, away from $u=0$,\n\n$$\nS_p(u)=-\\gamma_2 G'_\\omega(u)-i(\\gamma_0 p_0+\\gamma_1 p_1+\\gamma_3 p_3)G_\\omega(u).\n$$\n\nThe trace over Hplus is one half the ordinary four-spin trace for this product. The gamma5 part vanishes: the only possible antisymmetric expression has two identical transverse p vectors. Direct Clifford multiplication gives the real even kernel\n\n$$\nJ_q(u)=q^2 \\sum_{k_0,k_1,k_3 \\in \\mathbb{Z}} 2\\{G'_\\omega(u)^2+(\\omega^2-2p_1^2)G_\\omega(u)^2\\}.\n$$\n\nThe complete answer is\n\n$$\nB(f,g)=\\sum_{q\\in Q,\\ \\text{twice}} \\int_0^1 dy \\int_0^1 dz\\, f(y)g(z) J_q(y-z).\n$$\n\nNo absolute value of a determinant was substituted. The specified separated quadratic response is real; this says nothing about a general-background phase or higher-order odd/chiral terms.\n\n## 3. Absolute convergence and a computable tail\n\nRecenter $k_0$ at its nearest toron integer, so $p_0=2\\pi n_0+\\alpha_q$ with $|\\alpha_q|\\le\\pi$. For the shell $m=\\max(|n_0|,|k_1|,|k_3|)\\ge 1$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\omega\\ge w_m=2\\pi(m-1/2), \\\\\n\\text{number of shell points}=24m^2+2.\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nIf $d\\ge\\delta>0$, the image/exponential formulas imply\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\lvert G\\rvert\\le\\exp(-\\omega\\delta)/[\\omega(1-\\exp(-\\omega))], \\\\\n\\lvert G'\\rvert\\le\\exp(-\\omega\\delta)/[1-\\exp(-\\omega)].\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nConsequently each summand of $J_{q}$ has absolute value at most\n\n$4q^2\\exp(-2\\omega\\delta)/(1-\\exp(-\\omega))^2$.\n\nAfter retaining all recentered shells $m\\le N$, the full ten-flavour kernel error is bounded by\n\n$$\nE_N(\\delta)=1760 \\sum_{m>N} (24m^2+2) \\exp[-4\\pi \\delta(m-1/2)]/[1-\\exp(-2\\pi(m-1/2))]^2.\n$$\n\nHere sum $q^{2}=440$, including multiplicity. Multiplying $E_{N}$ by ||f||L1 ||g||L1 bounds the response error. The sum is bounded by an elementary geometric-polynomial tail, or evaluated with a further such tail certificate. It is uniform in y,z on separated supports. The finitely many $m=0$ modes are nonsingular because every $\\alpha_{q}$ is nonzero.\n\nThis also justifies the trace: multiplication f S g has a smooth kernel because the supports have positive separation. Its transverse Fourier components and every derivative decay exponentially; its one-dimensional kernels are smooth on the support product. Smooth periodic kernels are trace class, as can be proved by integrating their Fourier coefficients by parts more than four times in each variable. Composing with bounded S preserves trace class. The displayed diagonal integral and sum therefore equal the operator trace, not just a conditionally ordered diagram.\n\n## 4. An explicit strictly nonzero separated-support theorem\n\nChoose nonzero nonnegative smooth $f$ supported in $(0.24,0.26)$ and $g$ in $(0.74,0.76)$. Their circular separation satisfies $0.48\\le d\\le 0.5$. Both obey the source-circle condition.\n\nFor each charge the recentered $m=0$ mode has $p_1=p_3=0$ and contributes nonnegatively. For $q=-9$, $\\alpha$ has magnitude $2\\pi-9\\ln 2<0.05$. For every $d$ in this interval its contribution is at least\n\n$$\n81/[2 \\sinh(\\alpha/2)^2]>64000.\n$$\n\nThere are two $q=-9$ flavours. For every remaining $m\\ge 1$ mode, the preceding absolute bound gives, after division by $q^{2}$, a total less than 6:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{m\\ge 1} (24m^2+2)\\, 4 \\exp[-0.96\\cdot 2\\pi(m-1/2)]/[1-\\exp(-2\\pi(m-1/2))]^2 < 6.\n$$\n\nThis strict bound needs no floating-point premise: use $\\pi>3.14$, $\\exp(-3.14)<0.044$, $\\exp(-0.96\\times 3.14)<0.05$ and $\\exp(-1.92\\times 3.14)<0.0025$. The sum is at most\n\n$$\n[4\\cdot 0.05/(1-0.044)^2] \\sum_{m\\ge 1}(24m^2+2)(0.0025)^{m-1}<6,\n$$\n\nusing $\\sum m^2r^{m-1}=(1+r)/(1-r)^3$. The elementary exponential inequalities can equally be certified by their positive Taylor series. Also $\\sinh(.025)<.02501$ proves the 64000 bound.\n\nThus the full kernel is greater than $128000-440\\times 6=125360$ on this support product, and\n\n$$\nB(f,g)>125360\\,(\\int f)(\\int g)>0.\n$$\n\nThis is a rigorous continuum finite nonlocal susceptibility, amplified here by the inherited near-resonant $q=-9$ toron. It is not a large-cutoff extrapolation. The numerical constants are deliberately loose.\n\n## 5. Exact point-splitting contact decomposition\n\nThe shell estimate, now with $\\delta=d$ tending to zero, proves $|J(u)|\\le C(1+d^{-3})$ for the total kernel $J=\\sum J_q$: $\\sum m^{2}\\exp(-cmd)=O(d^{-3})$. Constants are finite for the fixed inherited toron. No asymptotic heat-kernel coefficient is assumed.\n\nFor arbitrary smooth $f,g$ define $h(u)=\\int f(y)g(y-u)\\,dy$ and $h_e(u)=[h(u)+h(-u)]/2$. Let $\\chi$ be an even smooth periodic function equal to 1 near zero and supported in $|u|<1/4$, using $u$ in $[-1/2,1/2]$. Then\n\n$$\nh_e(u)-\\chi(u)[h(0)+(u^2/2)h''(0)]=O(u^4).\n$$\n\nDefine the explicitly finite extension\n\n$$\nB_\\chi(f,g)=\\int_{-1/2}^{1/2} J(u)\\{h_e(u)-\\chi(u)[h(0)+(u^2/2)h''(0)]\\}\\,du.\n$$\n\nThe omitted $|u|<\\epsilon$ integral is bounded by $C'\\epsilon^{2}$ times an explicit fourth-derivative norm of $h$, for sufficiently small $\\epsilon$. Combining this with the transverse tail gives a convergent two-stage certified evaluation: first fix $\\epsilon$, truncate transverse modes with $E_N(\\epsilon)$, then bound the small-$u$ remainder.\n\nThe EXACT point-split expression decomposes as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{|u|>\\epsilon} J(u)h_e(u)\\,du \\\\\n&= B_\\chi(f,g)+A_\\epsilon h(0)+D_\\epsilon h''(0)+O(\\epsilon^2),\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_\\epsilon&=\\int_{|u|>\\epsilon} J(u)\\chi(u)\\,du, \\\\\nD_\\epsilon&=(1/2)\\int_{|u|>\\epsilon} J(u)\\chi(u)u^2\\,du.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese have rigorous upper growth bounds $A_\\epsilon=O(\\epsilon^{-2})$, $D_\\epsilon=O(\\log(1/\\epsilon))$; these are bounds, not assertions of nonzero divergent coefficients. Since $h(0)=\\int fg$ and $h''(0)=-\\int f'g'$, the contact terms are local in these profiles. Changing $\\chi$ changes only these two local bilinear terms. Additional finite constants $a\\,h(0)+b\\,h''(0)$ specify an explicit extension scheme.\n\nThis constructs a local-contact/finite-remainder decomposition for THIS continuum point-splitting prescription, including its finite nonlocal piece. It does not prove the masslike contact term is gauge admissible. Full local gauge Ward restoration must exclude/fix any inadmissible contact term and match the curvature term with physical normalization conditions. A profile-only point-splitting prescription is not automatically a full gauge-covariant determinant regulator. No numerical or analytic claim here identifies $A_\\epsilon,D_\\epsilon$ with the actual overlap divergences.\n\nFor separated $f,g$, $h$ vanishes near zero, $h(0)=h''(0)=0$, and $B_\\chi=B$ for every $\\chi,a,b$. Thus the positive response of section 4 is completely independent of all these contact choices. Any integral of a local finite-jet density also has zero mixed variation on disjoint profile supports. It cannot cancel $B$.\n\n∎\n\n---\n\n### Q.0.7.5 Registered Event-Phase Decomposition\n\n<a id=\"definition-q07e-registered-forward-event-phase-ledger-and-overhead-decomposition\"></a>**Definition Q.0.7e (Registered Forward Event-Phase Ledger and Overhead Decomposition).** Let $\\phi$ be a concatenated forward history of registered events carrying dimensionless increments $\\{\\varepsilon_i\\}_{i\\in\\phi}$, and define\n$$\n\\Theta_{\\mathrm{evt}}(\\phi):=\\sum_{i\\in\\phi}\\varepsilon_i,\n\\qquad\nU_{\\mathrm{evt}}(\\phi):=e^{i\\Theta_{\\mathrm{evt}}(\\phi)}.\n$$\nIf $\\phi$ belongs to the calibrated action ensemble, then $\\Theta_{\\mathrm{evt}}(\\phi)=\\mathcal S(\\phi)/\\hbar$. If every registered event is assigned the structural phase reference $\\varepsilon_0=\\ln2$ and a certified phase overhead $\\delta\\varepsilon_i:=\\varepsilon_i-\\ln2\\ge0$, then\n$$\n\\Theta_{\\mathrm{evt}}(\\phi)\n=N(\\phi)\\ln2+\\Delta(\\phi),\n\\qquad\n\\Delta(\\phi):=\\sum_{i\\in\\phi}\\delta\\varepsilon_i\\ge0.\n\\tag{Q.0.7e.1}\n$$\nThese are structural phase weights. Identifying $\\varepsilon_i$ with a registered physical reset ledger requires the separate eventwise calibration\n$$\n\\varepsilon_i=\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{reset},i}=H_{q_i}(P_i\\mid R_i)+\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{diss},i}.\n\\tag{Q.0.7e.0}\n$$\nOn the conditionally uniform branch $H_{q_i}(P_i\\mid R_i)=\\ln2$, this gives $\\delta\\varepsilon_i=\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{diss},i}$. Without (Q.0.7e.0), structural phase overhead, reset entropy, and bath heat remain distinct.\n\nOn the joint branch where the action calibration is eventwise additive, (Q.0.7e.0) holds, $H_{q_i}(P_i\\mid R_i)=\\ln2$, and $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{diss},i}=0$, write $\\Delta\\mathcal S_i$ for one event's action increment and $T_i$ for its registered reset temperature. That event satisfies\n$$\n\\frac{\\Delta\\mathcal S_i}{\\hbar}\n=\n\\frac{\\langle Q_{\\mathrm{bath},i}\\rangle}{k_BT_i}\n=\n\\varepsilon_i\n=\\ln2.\n$$\nThese are three calibrated projections of one event. A source-exhaustive cost ledger counts that event once rather than summing the action, reset, and phase readings as independent costs. The equality supplies no calibration or common microscopic mechanism beyond the stated joint branch.\n\nThis additive positive ledger is not, by itself, connection holonomy: holonomy inverts under oriented path reversal, whereas entropy production of a physically reversed protocol need not. It becomes a holonomy representation only on a branch carrying the differential character of Theorem Q.0.7d2 and the explicit compatibility certificate\n$$\nh(\\gamma)=e^{i\\Theta_{\\mathrm{evt}}(\\gamma)}\n\\tag{Q.0.7e.2}\n$$\nfor the admitted oriented loops. The formal inverse phase is distinct from the entropy ledger of a physically reversed process.\n\n<a id=\"definition-q07e-arithmetic-phase-defect-functionals\"></a>**Definition Q.0.7e′ (Arithmetic Phase-Defect Functionals).** For $x\\in\\mathbb R$, let $\\|x\\|_{\\mathbb Z}:=\\inf_{m\\in\\mathbb Z}|x-m|$. For $N\\ge1$, define\n$$\n\\delta_N\n:=\\min_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}|N\\ln2-2\\pi k|\n=2\\pi\\left\\|\\frac{N\\ln2}{2\\pi}\\right\\|_{\\mathbb Z}.\n$$\nFor a fixed positive exact-closure integer $k$, define\n$$\n\\Delta_k^*\n:=\\min_{N\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}}|2\\pi k-N\\ln2|.\n$$\nThese are arithmetic mismatch functionals. Neither is a topological invariant without an independent differential-character or homotopy certificate.\n\n### Q.0.7.6 Exact-Closure Overhead Principle\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q07f-registered-exact-phase-closure-identity\"></a>**Proposition Q.0.7f (Registered Exact-Phase-Closure Identity).** Let $\\gamma$ be a nonempty finite registered forward loop on the nonnegative-overhead branch of Definition Q.0.7e, with $N[\\gamma]\\ge1$ and $\\varepsilon_i=\\ln2+\\delta\\varepsilon_i$, $\\delta\\varepsilon_i\\ge0$, for every event. Assume the exact closure condition\n$$\nU_{\\mathrm{evt}}(\\gamma)=1.\n$$\nThen there is a unique integer lift $k\\ge1$ such that\n$$\n\\Theta_{\\mathrm{evt}}(\\gamma)=2\\pi k,\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\Delta[\\gamma]\n=2\\pi k-N[\\gamma]\\ln2.\n\\tag{Q.0.7f.1}\n$$\nConsequently $\\Delta[\\gamma]\\ge0$ implies $N[\\gamma]\\ln2\\le2\\pi k$. The integer $k$ is an exact-phase lift; it is topological only when the separate certificate (Q.0.7e.2) and a homotopy/cohomology identification are supplied.\n\n*Proof.* Equation (Q.0.7e.1) and $U_{\\mathrm{evt}}(\\gamma)=1$ give $\\Theta_{\\mathrm{evt}}(\\gamma)\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z$. Nonemptiness and $\\varepsilon_i\\ge\\ln2>0$ make this phase lift positive. Substitution gives (Q.0.7f.1). ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q07g-strict-overhead-on-a-nonzero-exact-closure-branch\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.7g (Strict Overhead on a Nonzero Exact-Closure Branch).** Under Proposition Q.0.7f,\n$$\n\\Delta[\\gamma]>0.\n$$\n\n*Proof.* If $\\Delta[\\gamma]=0$, then $N[\\gamma]\\ln2=2\\pi k$ with positive integers $N[\\gamma]$ and $k$, contradicting the irrationality of $\\ln2/(2\\pi)$. ∎\n\n**Registered interpretation.** A nonempty forward event ledger that closes its selected phase exactly has positive structural phase overhead. It is a topological cost only when the differential-character and topological-identification premises are registered, and it is physical entropy production only when the eventwise reset equality (Q.0.7e.0) is certified.\n\n<a id=\"definition-q07g1-closure-lift-ledger-mathfrak-c_mathrmref\"></a>**Definition Q.0.7g.1 (Closure-Lift Ledger $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{ref}}$).** For a closed retained predictive loop $\\gamma$ satisfying Proposition Q.0.7f, define\n$$\nq_{\\mathrm{ref}}(\\gamma)\n:=\\frac{\\Theta_{\\mathrm{evt}}(\\gamma)}{2\\pi}\n\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}.\n$$\nA closure-lift ledger is the finite record\n$$\n\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{ref}}\n=\n(\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{loop}},q_{\\mathrm{ref}},N,\\Delta,\n\\text{exact-closure domain},\\text{deformation class},\n\\text{resolution window},\\text{outcome independence}).\n$$\nThe integer is a topological or gauge charge only when the ledger also contains the differential-character compatibility and homotopy/cohomology identification of Definition Q.0.7e.\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q07g2-closure-lift-constancy-on-continuous-exact-closure-families\"></a>**Proposition Q.0.7g.2 (Closure-Lift Constancy on Continuous Exact-Closure Families).** Let a connected continuously parameterized family of loops remain in the exact-closure domain, and let $\\Theta_{\\mathrm{evt}}$ vary continuously on that family. Then $q_{\\mathrm{ref}}$ is constant. A change of $q_{\\mathrm{ref}}$ requires loss of exact closure or a discontinuity of the registered phase ledger. This is an integer-lift statement, not a universal local Noether-current law.\n\n*Proof.* On the exact-closure family,\n$$\nq_{\\mathrm{ref}}\n=\\Theta_{\\mathrm{evt}}/(2\\pi)\n$$\nis a continuous integer-valued function on a connected set and is therefore constant. Proposition Q.0.7f supplies the positive structural-overhead identity. A topological or gauge interpretation requires the additional identification certificate in Definition Q.0.7g.1, and a reset-entropy interpretation requires (Q.0.7e.0). ∎\n\n---\n\n### Q.0.7.7 Sector-Minimal Overhead\n\n<a id=\"definition-q07h-one-sided-and-symmetric-arithmetic-mismatches\"></a>**Definition Q.0.7h (One-Sided and Symmetric Arithmetic Mismatches).** For $k\\ge1$, define\n$$\n\\Delta_k^+\n:=\n\\min_{\\substack{N\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\\\N\\ln2\\le2\\pi k}}\n(2\\pi k-N\\ln2)\n=\n2\\pi k-\\left\\lfloor\\frac{2\\pi k}{\\ln2}\\right\\rfloor\\ln2\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\Delta_k^{\\mathrm{sym}}\n:=\n\\min_{N\\in\\mathbb Z}|2\\pi k-N\\ln2|.\n$$\nThese are arithmetic mismatches. They equal the structural phase overhead of a realized loop only when an admitted exact-closure loop realizes the same pair $(k,N)$ under Definition Q.0.7e and Proposition Q.0.7f. Identifying that value with physical entropy production additionally requires the eventwise reset calibration (Q.0.7e.0).\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q07i-bounds-and-equidistribution-of-arithmetic-mismatches\"></a>**Proposition Q.0.7i (Bounds and Equidistribution of Arithmetic Mismatches).** For every $k\\ge1$,\n$$\n0<\\Delta_k^+<\\ln2,\n\\qquad\n0<\\Delta_k^{\\mathrm{sym}}<\\frac{\\ln2}{2}.\n$$\nWith $\\beta_L=2\\pi/\\ln2$,\n$$\n\\frac{\\Delta_k^+}{\\ln2}=\\{\\beta_Lk\\}\\in(0,1),\n$$\nand $\\{\\Delta_k^+/\\ln2\\}_{k\\ge1}$ is equidistributed in $(0,1)$.\n\n*Proof.* The floor and nearest-integer definitions give $\\Delta_k^+<\\ln2$ and $\\Delta_k^{\\mathrm{sym}}\\le\\ln2/2$. Irrationality of $\\beta_L$ makes both mismatches nonzero. Equality $\\Delta_k^{\\mathrm{sym}}=\\ln2/2$ would make $\\beta_Lk$ a half-integer and hence $\\beta_L$ rational, which is impossible; therefore the symmetric upper bound is strict. Weyl's theorem applied to the irrational rotation $k\\mapsto\\{\\beta_Lk\\}$ gives equidistribution. ∎\n\n---\n\n### Q.0.7.8 Beatty Staircase Structure\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q07j-beatty-staircase-and-the-two-step-lower-approximant-spectrum\"></a>**Proposition Q.0.7j (Beatty Staircase and the Two-Step Lower-Approximant Spectrum).** Let\n$$\n\\beta_L=\\frac{2\\pi}{\\ln2}\\in(9,10),\n\\qquad\nN_k^*:=\\lfloor\\beta_Lk\\rfloor.\n$$\nThen:\n\n1. $N_k^*$ is the largest integer $N$ satisfying $N\\ln2\\le2\\pi k$.\n2. $N_{k+1}^*-N_k^*\\in\\{9,10\\}$.\n3. The limiting frequencies of the $10$- and $9$-steps are $\\beta_L-9$ and $10-\\beta_L$, respectively.\n\nThese are statements about lower integer approximants. A cycle count or exact-closure loop is obtained only if the corresponding registered physical ledger realizes that integer pair.\n\n*Proof.* Item 1 is the floor definition. Since $9<\\beta_L<10$, consecutive floors differ by $9$ or $10$. The $10$-step occurs exactly when $\\{\\beta_Lk\\}+\\{\\beta_L\\}\\ge1$; equidistribution from Proposition Q.0.7i gives limiting frequency $\\{\\beta_L\\}=\\beta_L-9$. ∎\n\n---\n\n### Q.0.7.9 Continued Fraction Spectrum\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q07k-continued-fraction-spectrum-of-exceptional-phase-mismatch\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.7k (Continued-Fraction Spectrum of Exceptional Phase Mismatch).** Let\n$$\n\\alpha_L=\\frac{\\ln2}{2\\pi}=[0;a_1,a_2,\\ldots]\n$$\nand let $p_n/q_n$ be its convergents. Then:\n\n1. for every $p\\in\\mathbb Z$ and $1\\le q\\le q_n$,\n   $$\n   \\left|\\alpha_L-\\frac{p_n}{q_n}\\right|\n   \\le\n   \\left|\\alpha_L-\\frac pq\\right|,\n   $$\n   while the second-kind bound is\n   $$\n   |q_n\\alpha_L-p_n|\n   \\le|q\\alpha_L-p|\n   \\qquad(1\\le q<q_{n+1});\n   $$\n2. \n   $$\n   \\left|\\alpha_L-\\frac{p_n}{q_n}\\right|\n   <\\frac1{q_nq_{n+1}}\n   \\le\\frac1{q_n^2},\n   $$\n   and therefore\n   $$\n   |2\\pi p_n-q_n\\ln2|\n   <\\frac{2\\pi}{q_{n+1}}\n   \\le\\frac{2\\pi}{q_n};\n   $$\n3. if $\\gcd(p,q)=1$ and\n   $$\n   \\left|\\alpha_L-\\frac pq\\right|<\\frac1{2q^2},\n   $$\n   then $p/q$ is a convergent.\n\n*Proof.* Write $\\alpha=\\alpha_L$. Lemma Q.0.7b gives irrationality and an infinite simple continued fraction. The classical approximation criteria are Khinchin (1964, Theorems 16, 17, and 19); the following argument establishes the required inequalities with the present denominator conventions. Since $0<\\alpha<1/2$, its simple continued fraction has $a_0=0$ and $a_1\\ge2$. Use\n$$\np_{-1}=1,\\quad p_0=0,\\qquad q_{-1}=0,\\quad q_0=1,\n\\qquad\np_{n+1}=a_{n+1}p_n+p_{n-1},\\quad\nq_{n+1}=a_{n+1}q_n+q_{n-1}.\n$$\nThe positive partial quotients imply $q_{n+1}>q_n\\ge1$ and $q_n\\to\\infty$. The determinant changes sign at each recurrence step, starting from $p_0q_{-1}-p_{-1}q_0=-1$, so\n$$\np_nq_{n-1}-p_{n-1}q_n=(-1)^{n-1}.\n$$\nFor the complete quotient $\\alpha_{n+1}=[a_{n+1};a_{n+2},\\ldots]>a_{n+1}$, start from $\\alpha=1/\\alpha_1$. Substituting $\\alpha_{n+1}=a_{n+1}+1/\\alpha_{n+2}$ and collecting numerator and denominator coefficients proves by induction that\n$$\n\\alpha=\\frac{p_n\\alpha_{n+1}+p_{n-1}}{q_n\\alpha_{n+1}+q_{n-1}},\n\\qquad\ne_n:=q_n\\alpha-p_n=\\frac{(-1)^n}{q_n\\alpha_{n+1}+q_{n-1}}.\n$$\nThus consecutive errors have opposite signs. Writing $\\delta_n=|e_n|$, the denominator identity gives\n$$\n0<\\delta_n<\\frac1{q_{n+1}},\n\\qquad\n\\left|\\alpha-\\frac{p_n}{q_n}\\right|\n=\\frac{\\delta_n}{q_n}\n<\\frac1{q_nq_{n+1}}\\le\\frac1{q_n^2}.\n$$\nMultiplication by $2\\pi q_n$ proves item 2.\n\nFor item 1, fix $p\\in\\mathbb Z$ and $1\\le q<q_{n+1}$. The determinant identity makes the two integer vectors $(p_n,q_n)$ and $(p_{n+1},q_{n+1})$ a basis of $\\mathbb Z^2$, so write\n$$\n(p,q)=u(p_n,q_n)+v(p_{n+1},q_{n+1}),\\qquad u,v\\in\\mathbb Z.\n$$\nIf $v=0$, then $u\\ge1$ and $|q\\alpha-p|=u\\delta_n\\ge\\delta_n$. If $v>0$, the upper bound on $q$ forces $u<0$; if $v<0$, positivity of $q$ forces $u>0$. In either nonzero-$v$ case, $ue_n$ and $ve_{n+1}$ have the same sign, and hence\n$$\n|q\\alpha-p|=|u|\\delta_n+|v|\\delta_{n+1}>\\delta_n.\n$$\nThis proves the second-kind inequality, with equality only at $(p,q)=(p_n,q_n)$. If $q\\le q_n$, division additionally gives\n$$\n\\left|\\alpha-\\frac pq\\right|\n=\\frac{|q\\alpha-p|}{q}\n\\ge\\frac{\\delta_n}{q}\n\\ge\\frac{\\delta_n}{q_n}\n=\\left|\\alpha-\\frac{p_n}{q_n}\\right|,\n$$\nwhich proves the first-kind inequality.\n\nFor item 3, take the positive denominator $q$ of the reduced fraction $p/q$ and choose $n$ with $q_n\\le q<q_{n+1}$. Put $\\delta=|q\\alpha-p|<1/(2q)$. The second-kind inequality gives $\\delta_n\\le\\delta$. If $p/q\\ne p_n/q_n$, the nonzero integer $pq_n-qp_n$ would satisfy\n$$\n1\\le|pq_n-qp_n|\n\\le q_n|p-q\\alpha|+q|q_n\\alpha-p_n|\n=q_n\\delta+q\\delta_n\n\\le(q_n+q)\\delta\n\\le2q\\delta<1,\n$$\na contradiction. Therefore $p/q=p_n/q_n$; the determinant identity makes $p_n/q_n$ reduced, and the assumed reduction identifies the numerator and positive denominator. This proves the Legendre criterion. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q07k-legendre-criterion-for-a-small-phase-mismatch\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.7k′ (Legendre Criterion for a Small Phase Mismatch).** Let $k,N\\ge1$, put $d=\\gcd(k,N)$, and set $(p,q)=(k/d,N/d)$. If\n$$\n\\left|\\alpha_L-\\frac{k}{N}\\right|<\\frac1{2N^2},\n$$\nequivalently\n$$\n|N\\ln2-2\\pi k|<\\frac\\pi N,\n$$\nthen $p/q$ is a convergent of $\\alpha_L$.\n\n*Proof.* Reduction gives\n$$\n\\left|\\alpha_L-\\frac pq\\right|\n<\\frac1{2N^2}\n\\le\\frac1{2q^2},\n$$\nso Legendre's theorem applies. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q07k1-landauer-arithmetic-index-for-integer-phase-pairs\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.7k.1 (Landauer Arithmetic Index for Integer Phase Pairs).** For $(N,k)\\in\\mathbb Z^2$, define\n$$\n\\mathfrak I_L(N,k):=N\\alpha_L-k,\n\\qquad\n\\delta_L(N,k):=2\\pi|\\mathfrak I_L(N,k)|.\n$$\nThen:\n\n1. $\\mathfrak I_L$ is additive under addition of integer pairs:\n   $$\n   \\mathfrak I_L(N_1+N_2,k_1+k_2)\n   =\n   \\mathfrak I_L(N_1,k_1)+\\mathfrak I_L(N_2,k_2).\n   $$\n   It represents loop composition only on a realized additive loop ledger.\n2. If $(k_n,N_n)=(p_n,q_n)$ is a convergent pair, then\n   $$\n   |q_n\\alpha_L-p_n|\n   \\le|N\\alpha_L-k|\n   \\qquad(1\\le N<q_{n+1}).\n   $$\n3. If $N\\ge1$, $\\gcd(k,N)=1$, and $\\delta_L(N,k)<\\pi/N$, then $k/N$ is a convergent.\n\n*Proof.* Item 1 is direct algebra. Item 2 is the second-kind approximation theorem. Item 3 is the Legendre criterion in Theorem Q.0.7k. These statements classify integer pairs; physical loop realization is an independent premise. ∎\n\n---\n\n### Q.0.7.10 Explicit Low-Mismatch Pairs\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q07l-explicit-low-mismatch-kn-pairs\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.7l (Explicit Low-Mismatch $(k,N)$ Pairs).** For $(k_n,N_n)=(p_n,q_n)$,\n$$\n|2\\pi k_n-N_n\\ln2|<\\frac{2\\pi}{N_n},\n$$\nand the sign of $2\\pi k_n-N_n\\ln2$ alternates. A positive sign is a candidate one-sided mismatch. It becomes structural phase overhead only if Definition Q.0.7e and Proposition Q.0.7f are satisfied by an independently realized loop with that pair; physical entropy production additionally requires (Q.0.7e.0).\n\nThe continued fraction begins\n$$\n\\alpha_L=[0;9,15,2,4,1,1,1,1,2,2,3,1,1,1,1,3,4,\\ldots].\n$$\nIts first convergent pairs are\n$$\n(k,N)\\in\n\\{(1,9),(15,136),(31,281),(139,1260),(170,1541),\n(309,2801),(479,4342),(788,7143),\\ldots\\}.\n$$\nThey follow from\n$$\np_n=a_np_{n-1}+p_{n-2},\n\\qquad\nq_n=a_nq_{n-1}+q_{n-2},\n$$\nwith $(p_{-1},p_0)=(1,0)$ and $(q_{-1},q_0)=(0,1)$. The first absolute mismatches, rounded to the displayed digits, are\n$$\n0.0448606821,\\quad\n0.0202369485,\\quad\n0.00438678522,\\quad\n0.00268980757.\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Q.0.7.11 Universal Crossover Bounds\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q07m-arbitrarily-small-positive-arithmetic-mismatches\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.7m (Arbitrarily Small Positive Arithmetic Mismatches).** For every $\\zeta>0$, there exist integers $N,k\\ge1$ such that\n$$\n0<2\\pi k-N\\ln2<\\zeta.\n$$\nNo loop or physical sector is asserted by this arithmetic existence statement.\n\n*Proof.* Set\n$$\n\\eta:=\\min\\left\\{\\frac{\\zeta}{2\\pi},\\frac12\\right\\}.\n$$\nDensity of $\\{N\\alpha_L\\bmod1:N\\ge1\\}$ gives an $N$ with\n$$\n\\{N\\alpha_L\\}\\in(1-\\eta,1).\n$$\nWith $k=\\lceil N\\alpha_L\\rceil$,\n$$\n0<2\\pi(k-N\\alpha_L)<2\\pi\\eta\\le\\zeta.\n$$\nThe interval excludes $0$, so $k\\ge1$. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q07m-universal-arithmetic-crossover-bound\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.7m′ (Universal Arithmetic Crossover Bound).** Hurwitz's theorem gives infinitely many integer pairs satisfying\n$$\n|2\\pi k-N\\ln2|<\\frac{2\\pi}{\\sqrt5\\,N}.\n$$\nConsequently, along this subsequence, $N>2\\pi/(\\sqrt5\\,\\zeta)$ implies mismatch below $\\zeta$. The constant $\\sqrt5$ is the optimal universal Hurwitz constant; it is not a primitive physical proportion.\n\n---\n\n### Q.0.7.12 Three-Gap Structure\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q07n-three-gap-structure-for-finite-arithmetic-phase-sets\"></a>**Proposition Q.0.7n (Three-Gap Structure for Finite Arithmetic Phase Sets).** For $N_{\\max}\\ge1$, the set\n$$\n\\{e^{in\\ln2}:0\\le n<N_{\\max}\\}\n$$\npartitions the circle into arcs of at most three distinct lengths.\n\n*Proof.* Apply the three-gap theorem to the irrational rotation by $\\alpha_L$. ∎\n\n**Interpretation.** This is a finite arithmetic phase set. It describes accessible physical phases only on a branch that realizes the corresponding update histories and calibrates their ledger values as phases.\n\n---\n\n### Q.0.7.13 Finite-Budget Phase-Mismatch Selection\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q07o-finite-budget-phase-mismatch-selection-rule\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.7o (Finite-Budget Phase-Mismatch Selection Rule).** Define\n$$\n\\Delta\\theta(n)\n:=\n\\min_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}|n\\ln2-2\\pi k|\n=2\\pi\\|n\\alpha_L\\|_{\\mathbb Z}.\n$$\nIf $q_j\\le N_{\\max}<q_{j+1}$, then\n$$\n\\min_{1\\le n\\le N_{\\max}}\\Delta\\theta(n)\n=\\Delta\\theta(q_j),\n\\qquad\n\\Delta\\theta(q_j)<\\frac{2\\pi}{q_{j+1}}.\n$$\nIf $k$ is a nearest integer to $n\\alpha_L$, $\\gcd(k,n)=1$, and $\\Delta\\theta(n)<\\pi/n$, then $k/n$ is a convergent. These statements determine arithmetic phase positions. They constrain interference only for independently realized, phase-calibrated histories and do not determine amplitudes.\n\n*Proof.* The second-kind best-approximation property gives\n$$\n\\|q_j\\alpha_L\\|_{\\mathbb Z}\n\\le\\|n\\alpha_L\\|_{\\mathbb Z}\n\\qquad(1\\le n<q_{j+1}),\n$$\nand Theorem Q.0.7k gives the upper bound. The last statement is Legendre's criterion. ∎\n\n---\n\n### Q.0.7.14 Decoherence from Overhead Fluctuations\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q07p-conditional-ensemble-dephasing-from-phase-overhead-fluctuations\"></a>**Proposition Q.0.7p (Conditional Ensemble Dephasing from Phase-Overhead Fluctuations).** *Let a family of processes $\\phi$ share the same baseline cycle count $N[\\phi]=N$, let $\\Delta[\\phi]$ have probability law $P(\\Delta)$, and assume the accepted phase calibration gives $\\mathcal S[\\phi]/\\hbar=N\\ln2+\\Delta[\\phi]$ for every member of the ensemble. Then the ensemble-averaged phase factor factorizes as:*\n$$\\langle e^{i\\mathcal{S}[\\phi]/\\hbar} \\rangle = e^{iN\\ln 2} \\langle e^{i\\Delta[\\phi]} \\rangle = e^{iN\\ln 2} \\Phi_\\Delta(1)$$\n\n*where $\\Phi_\\Delta(t):=\\langle e^{it\\Delta}\\rangle$ is the characteristic function of $\\Delta$. If a registered coherence observable is multiplied by this ensemble average, its visibility is multiplied by $|\\Phi_\\Delta(1)|\\le1$.*\n\nBecause Definition Q.0.7e requires $\\Delta\\ge0$, a nondegenerate Gaussian law is inadmissible. For example, if $\\Delta$ has a $\\Gamma$ law with shape $r>0$ and scale $\\theta>0$ on $[0,\\infty)$, then\n$$\n\\Phi_\\Delta(1)=(1-i\\theta)^{-r},\n\\qquad\n|\\Phi_\\Delta(1)|=(1+\\theta^2)^{-r/2}.\n$$\nThis is an exact supported-law example. It becomes a dephasing prediction only after the coherence observable, ensemble preparation, and pushforward law of Remark Q.0.7p.1 are registered.\n\n<a id=\"remark-q07p1-conditional-pce-induced-overhead-distribution\"></a>**Remark Q.0.7p.1: Conditional PCE-Induced Overhead Distribution.** Let a specified PCE state process $X_t$ satisfy the hypotheses of the applicable Appendix D stationary-law theorem, and let the overhead be a measurable observable $\\Delta=\\Delta(X_t)$. The stationary law of $X_t$ then induces a pushforward law $P_\\Delta$. Concentration of $P_\\Delta$ near zero requires, in addition, that $\\Delta$ vanish on the relevant minimizing set and that the Appendix D concentration estimate control excursions into states with $|\\Delta|>\\epsilon$.\n\n---\n\n### Q.0.7.15 Modular Structure from Continued Fractions\n\n<a id=\"remark-q07q-the-modular-itinerary-canonically-induced-by-alpha_l\"></a>**Remark Q.0.7q: The Modular Itinerary Canonically Induced by $\\alpha_L$.** The continued fraction of $\\alpha_L$ canonically produces a sequence of matrices in $GL_2(\\mathbb{Z})$ via convergents:\n$$M_n := \\begin{pmatrix} p_n & p_{n-1} \\\\ q_n & q_{n-1} \\end{pmatrix}, \\qquad \\det(M_n) = (-1)^{n-1}, \\qquad M_n(\\infty) = \\frac{p_n}{q_n}$$\n\nThe digit string $[0; 9, 15, 2, 4, 1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3, \\ldots]$ supplies one-sided continued-fraction data for Farey cutting sequences on the modular surface $\\mathcal{H}/PSL_2(\\mathbb{Z})$ (Series 1985). To specify a complete oriented geodesic, one must also supply a backward endpoint or equivalent initial geometric data; to encode its cuts as a word in the generators $S: \\tau \\mapsto -1/\\tau$ and $T: \\tau \\mapsto \\tau + 1$, one must declare the initial cut and orientation convention. In particular, the matrices $M_n$ with determinant $-1$ are not themselves elements of $PSL_2(\\mathbb Z)$.\n\nWith these additional choices, the continued-fraction data yield a $PSL_2(\\mathbb Z)$-structured itinerary for the chosen irrational number $\\alpha_L=\\ln2/(2\\pi)$. The arithmetic construction alone supplies no bridge to $M=24$, the Leech lattice, $V^\\natural$, the Monster, or a physical modular symmetry.\n\n*Note:* The Klein $j$-invariant is defined on the upper half-plane $\\mathcal{H} = \\{\\tau \\in \\mathbb{C} : \\mathrm{Im}(\\tau) > 0\\}$; the real axis $\\mathbb{R}$ lies on the boundary of $\\mathcal{H}$, so expressions like $j(\\alpha_L)$ are not defined as holomorphic values. The modular itinerary construction avoids this issue entirely.\n\n---\n\n### Q.0.7.16 Gauge-Holonomy Interface\n\n<a id=\"remark-q07r-gauge-holonomy-interface\"></a>**Remark Q.0.7r: Gauge-Holonomy Interface.** Appendix G derives a local $U(1)$ redundancy with covariant derivative:\n$$D_\\mu\\Psi = (\\partial_\\mu + \\Omega_\\mu + iq A_\\mu)\\Psi$$\n\nand associated $U(1)$ holonomies $\\exp(iq\\oint_\\gamma A_\\mu dx^\\mu)$ (Section G.4).\n\nThe chosen structural phase increment $\\ln2$ gives $g_L=e^{i\\ln2}$, and the irrationality of $\\ln2/(2\\pi)$ makes the cyclic subgroup $G_L=\\langle g_L\\rangle$ dense in $U(1)$. This is a group-closure statement conditional on assigning that phase increment to the registered event family.\n\nTheorem Q.0.7d2 supplies the separate operational completion: a uniformly continuous isometric $G_L$ action extends uniquely to $U(1)$, while genuine connection holonomy requires its differential-character certificate. On the additional compatibility branch $h(\\gamma)=e^{i\\Theta_{\\mathrm{evt}}(\\gamma)}$, the registered event phase represents holonomy; exact trivial phase then gives Proposition Q.0.7f's closure identity. Corollary Q.0.7g gives positive structural phase overhead for a nonzero exact-closure lift. The formal inverse phase is not identified with entropy production of a physically reversed protocol.\n\nAccordingly, the exact finite-cycle object is the integer-indexed subgroup $G_L$; its continuous closure is used only on the registered action/continuity branch, and its local gauge interpretation only on the differential-character/descent branch.\n\n<a id=\"remark-q07r-conditional-event-phaseconnection-compatibility\"></a>**Remark Q.0.7r′ (Conditional Event-Phase/Connection Compatibility).** On the differential-character branch of Theorem Q.0.7d2, let $h$ be the registered holonomy character. If (Q.0.7e.2) is independently certified, then for every admitted oriented loop $\\gamma$,\n$$\nh(\\gamma)\n=e^{i\\Theta_{\\mathrm{evt}}(\\gamma)}\n=e^{i(N(\\gamma)\\ln2+\\Delta(\\gamma))}.\n$$\nIf the bundle is trivial over a neighborhood of $\\gamma$ and $A$ is a chosen local connection form there, this may be written as\n$$\nh(\\gamma)=e^{iq\\oint_\\gamma A};\n$$\non a nontrivial bundle the transition functions are part of the holonomy. For exact closure, $h(\\gamma)=1$ and $N\\ln2+\\Delta=2\\pi k$. The quantity $\\Delta$ is structural phase overhead and is physical entropy production only under the separate registered-reset calibration. Local changes of section change the local potential but not $h$.\n\n---\n\n### Q.0.7.17 Non-Abelian Extension\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q07s-non-abelian-density-mechanism-from-near-identity-generation\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.7s (Non-Abelian Density Mechanism from Near-Identity Generation).** *Let $G$ be a connected semisimple real Lie group with Lie algebra $\\mathfrak{g}$. Then there exists an identity neighborhood $\\Omega \\subset G$ on which $\\log = \\exp^{-1}$ is defined such that for any $g_1, \\ldots, g_m \\in \\Omega$, if the Lie algebra generated by $\\log(g_1), \\ldots, \\log(g_m)$ equals $\\mathfrak{g}$, then the subgroup $\\Gamma := \\langle g_1, \\ldots, g_m \\rangle$ is dense in $G$, i.e., $\\overline{\\Gamma} = G$.*\n\n*Proof.* This is the near-identity density criterion of Breuillard–Gelander (Breuillard and Gelander 2003, Theorem 2.1). $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q07t-su2-case-torus-density-plus-non-normalizer-implies-density\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.7t (SU(2) Case: Torus Density Plus Non-Normalizer Implies Density).** *Let $G = SU(2)$ and let $T \\subset SU(2)$ be a maximal torus. Suppose $\\Gamma$ contains an element $t \\in T$ whose rotation angle is an irrational multiple of $\\pi$ (so $\\overline{\\langle t\\rangle} = T$). If $\\Gamma$ also contains an element $g \\notin N_{SU(2)}(T)$ (equivalently, $gTg^{-1} \\ne T$), then $\\overline{\\Gamma} = SU(2)$.*\n\n*For a finite inverse-closed gate set generating this dense $SU(2)$ subgroup, the Solovay–Kitaev theorem (Kitaev, Shen and Vyalyi 2002) gives polylogarithmic word length in the inverse approximation tolerance for single-carrier $SU(2)$ gates. The universal protected computation of Appendix A additionally requires its full gate-realization, error-correction, working-memory, and execution-resource hypotheses.*\n\n*Justification.* Since $\\overline{\\langle t\\rangle} = T$, the closure $\\overline{\\Gamma}$ contains $T$. The only proper closed subgroups of $SU(2)$ containing a maximal torus are $T$ itself and its normalizer $N_{SU(2)}(T)=T\\sqcup jT\\cong\\operatorname{Pin}(2)$, where $j^2=-I$. Moreover $N_{SU(2)}(T)/T\\cong\\mathbb Z_2$ and $N_{SU(2)}(T)/\\{\\pm I\\}\\cong O(2)$. If $g\\notin N_{SU(2)}(T)$, the closed subgroup generated by $T$ and $g$ is therefore all of $SU(2)$.\n\n<a id=\"remark-q07t1-registered-non-normalizer-requirement\"></a>**Remark Q.0.7t.1: Registered Non-Normalizer Requirement.** For the standard diagonal torus,\n$$\nU_\\iota=i\\sigma_x\n$$\nsatisfies $U_\\iota T U_\\iota^{-1}=T$, so $U_\\iota\\in N_{SU(2)}(T)$ and cannot be the element $g\\notin N_{SU(2)}(T)$ required by Corollary Q.0.7t. Indeed a dense torus together with $i\\sigma_x$ closes only to $N_{SU(2)}(T)\\cong\\operatorname{Pin}(2)$. Dense $SU(2)$ requires an additional registered element outside the normalizer, for example\n$$\ng_\\beta=\\exp(i\\beta\\sigma_x),\n\\qquad\n\\beta\\notin(\\pi/2)\\mathbb Z,\n$$\nor near-identity generators whose logarithms are proved to span $\\mathfrak{su}(2)$ as in Theorem Q.0.7s. QFI mode availability alone does not construct those retained group elements.\n\n*Proof.* Conjugation by $i\\sigma_x$ sends $\\sigma_z$ to $-\\sigma_z$ and $(i\\sigma_x)^2=-I$, proving membership in the nonidentity normalizer component. Conjugation by $g_\\beta$ sends $\\sigma_z$ to $\\cos(2\\beta)\\sigma_z+\\sin(2\\beta)\\sigma_y$, which lies on the torus axis exactly when $\\beta\\in(\\pi/2)\\mathbb Z$. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q07t2-minimal-two-control-dense-su2-holonomy-witness\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.7t.2 (Minimal Two-Control Dense $SU(2)$ Holonomy Witness).** Choose nonzero $\\alpha,\\beta$ small enough that\n$$\nU_x=e^{i\\alpha\\sigma_x},\n\\qquad\nU_z=e^{i\\beta\\sigma_z}\n$$\nbelong to the logarithm neighborhood of Theorem Q.0.7s. Then $\\log U_x=i\\alpha\\sigma_x$ and $\\log U_z=i\\beta\\sigma_z$ generate $\\mathfrak{su}(2)$ because their bracket is a nonzero multiple of $i\\sigma_y$. Hence $\\langle U_x,U_z\\rangle$ is dense in $SU(2)$. No one-element set can be dense in $SU(2)$, because the closure of a cyclic subgroup is abelian. Thus two is the minimum number of near-identity generators for this carrier.\n\nLet a two-valued control register have basis $|x\\rangle,|z\\rangle$ and define the controlled update\n$$\nU_{\\mathrm{ctrl}}\n=|x\\rangle\\!\\langle x|\\otimes U_x\n+|z\\rangle\\!\\langle z|\\otimes U_z.\n\\tag{Q.0.7t.2.1}\n$$\nThis is unitary. For forward parallel transport with $D=d+\\mathcal A$, use the constant anti-Hermitian connection forms $\\mathcal A_x=-i\\alpha\\sigma_x\\,ds$ and $\\mathcal A_z=-i\\beta\\sigma_z\\,ds$ on a unit parameter loop. The equation $\\dot U=-\\mathcal A_sU$, $U(0)=I$, gives holonomies $U_x$ and $U_z$, and finite forward control words realize the positive-word monoid generated by $U_x,U_z$. Exact realization of the full subgroup additionally requires realization of the inverse gates. Parallel logarithms instead generate one torus, while adjoining only a torus-normalizer element gives the proper closed normalizer described in Remark Q.0.7t.1; either failure is a finite falsifier of density.\n\n*Proof.* The Lie-bracket calculation and Theorem Q.0.7s prove density. Every cyclic subgroup is abelian, and closure preserves commutation, whereas $SU(2)$ is nonabelian, proving minimality. Orthogonal control projections make (Q.0.7t.2.1) a block-diagonal unitary. For the stated forward transport convention, the solution is $U(1)=\\mathcal P\\exp(-\\int_0^1\\mathcal A_s\\,ds)$. Constancy of $\\mathcal A_s$ removes path ordering and gives $U_x$ or $U_z$ from the displayed negative connection forms, proving the holonomy statement. The proper-closure alternatives follow from the one-dimensional Lie algebra and Corollary Q.0.7t. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q07t3-minimal-dense-generation-for-compact-unitary-type-gauge-groups\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.7t.3 (Minimal Dense Generation for Compact Unitary-Type Gauge Groups).** Let\n$$\nG=\\Bigl(\\prod_{j=1}^mSU(n_j)\\times U(1)^k\\Bigr)\\Big/D,\n\\qquad m\\ge1,\\quad n_j\\ge2,\\quad k\\ge0,\n\\tag{Q.0.7t.3.1}\n$$\nwith $D$ a finite central subgroup; the class contains $SU(n)$, $U(n)$ and $(SU(3)\\times SU(2)\\times U(1))/F$ for every finite central $F$. For $n\\ge2$ put\n$$\nX^{(n)}:=i\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_n),\n\\quad\n\\lambda_a:=2^a-\\frac1n\\sum_{b=1}^n2^b,\n\\qquad\nY^{(n)}:=i(J_n-I_n),\n\\tag{Q.0.7t.3.2}\n$$\nwith $J_n$ the all-ones matrix, and for $t>0$ let $g_1(t),g_2(t)\\in G$ be the images of\n$$\n\\Bigl(\\bigl(e^{tjX^{(n_j)}}\\bigr)_{j=1}^m;\\ e^{2\\pi i\\alpha_L},e^{2\\pi i\\alpha_L^2},\\ldots,e^{2\\pi i\\alpha_L^k}\\Bigr),\n\\qquad\n\\Bigl(\\bigl(e^{tY^{(n_j)}}\\bigr)_{j=1}^m;\\ 1,\\ldots,1\\Bigr).\n$$\nThen:\n\n1. No single element generates a dense subgroup of $G$.\n2. There is $t_0>0$ such that $\\langle g_1(t),g_2(t)\\rangle$ is dense in $G$ for $0<t\\le t_0$. Every group in the class therefore has exactly two as its minimal number of topological generators. The semisimple components of $g_1(t),g_2(t)$ tend to the identity as $t\\to0$, and for $k\\ge1$ the first abelian component of $g_1$ is the registered Landauer generator $g_L=e^{i\\ln2}$. Density persists when the abelian component of $g_1$ is replaced by $(e^{2\\pi i\\alpha_L^l/q})_{l=1}^k$ for an integer $q\\ge1$, so for small $t$ and large $q$ both generators lie in any prescribed identity neighborhood of $G$.\n3. For every finite-dimensional unitary representation $\\rho$ of $G$, the controlled update $|x\\rangle\\!\\langle x|\\otimes\\rho(g_1)+|z\\rangle\\!\\langle z|\\otimes\\rho(g_2)$ is unitary, and the constant connection forms $\\mathcal A_i=-iH_i\\,ds$, with Hermitian $H_i$ satisfying $e^{iH_i}=\\rho(g_i)$, have holonomies $\\rho(g_1),\\rho(g_2)$ in the transport convention of Theorem Q.0.7t.2.\n\n*Proof.* Item 1: the closure of a cyclic subgroup is abelian, while $G$ contains the nonabelian image of $SU(n_1)$.\n\nLie algebra. Let $\\mathfrak s_j:=\\mathfrak{su}(n_j)$, $\\mathfrak s:=\\bigoplus_j\\mathfrak s_j$, $X:=(jX^{(n_j)})_j$, $Y:=(Y^{(n_j)})_j$, and let $\\mathfrak h\\subseteq\\mathfrak s$ be the real Lie algebra generated by $X$ and $Y$. For one factor, the complex span of the algebra generated by $X^{(n)}$ and $Y^{(n)}$ contains $p(\\operatorname{ad}_{X^{(n)}})Y^{(n)}$ for every polynomial $p$. Since $\\operatorname{ad}_{X^{(n)}}E_{ab}=i(\\lambda_a-\\lambda_b)E_{ab}$ and the numbers $2^a-2^b$, $a\\ne b$, are nonzero and pairwise distinct by uniqueness of binary expansions, Lagrange interpolation isolates $iE_{ab}$ for every $a\\ne b$. These matrix units generate $\\mathfrak{sl}_n(\\mathbb C)$, and $\\mathfrak{su}(n)\\cap i\\,\\mathfrak{su}(n)=0$, so the real algebra has dimension $n^2-1$ and equals $\\mathfrak{su}(n)$; a positive scalar multiple of $X^{(n)}$ generates the same algebra with $Y^{(n)}$. For the sum, induct on $m$, allowing arbitrary pairwise distinct positive scalars in place of $j$. Each projection $\\pi_j(\\mathfrak h)$ equals $\\mathfrak s_j$, and $\\mathfrak h\\cap\\mathfrak s_1$ is an ideal of the simple algebra $\\mathfrak s_1$, because $[h,a]=[\\pi_1h,a]$ for $a\\in\\mathfrak s_1$. The projection $\\pi'$ onto $\\mathfrak s':=\\bigoplus_{j\\ge2}\\mathfrak s_j$ maps $\\mathfrak h$ onto $\\mathfrak s'$ by induction. If $\\mathfrak h\\cap\\mathfrak s_1=\\mathfrak s_1$, then $\\pi'h=h-\\pi_1h\\in\\mathfrak h$ for all $h\\in\\mathfrak h$, so $\\mathfrak h=\\mathfrak s$. If $\\mathfrak h\\cap\\mathfrak s_1=0$, then $\\pi'|_{\\mathfrak h}$ is injective and $\\pi_1\\circ(\\pi'|_{\\mathfrak h})^{-1}$ is a surjective homomorphism $\\mathfrak s'\\to\\mathfrak s_1$; its kernel is a sum of simple ideals, so it restricts to an isomorphism $\\phi:\\mathfrak s_{j_0}\\to\\mathfrak s_1$ with $\\phi(c_{j_0}X^{(n)})=c_1X^{(n)}$, $n=n_1=n_{j_0}$, for the two distinct scalars $c_1,c_{j_0}$. Isomorphisms preserve the Killing form, which is negative definite on $\\mathfrak{su}(n)$, so $c_{j_0}^2=c_1^2$, a contradiction. Hence $\\mathfrak h=\\mathfrak s$.\n\nDensity. Choose $t_0$ so that for $t\\le t_0$ the components of $g_1(t),g_2(t)$ in $G_{ss}:=\\prod_jSU(n_j)$ lie in the neighborhood $\\Omega$ of Theorem Q.0.7s and have logarithms $tX,tY$; these generate $\\mathfrak s$, so the projection to $G_{ss}$ of $\\Gamma:=\\langle g_1(t),g_2(t)\\rangle$, formed first in $\\tilde G:=G_{ss}\\times U(1)^k$, is dense. Let $H$ be the closure of $\\Gamma$ in $\\tilde G$. Its projection to $G_{ss}$ is compact and dense, hence equal to $G_{ss}$. The closed subgroup $S_0:=\\{g\\in G_{ss}:(g,1)\\in H\\}$ is normal in $G_{ss}$, because conjugating $(s,1)$ by any $(g,\\tau)\\in H$ gives $(gsg^{-1},1)$. For $(g_1',\\tau_1),(g_2',\\tau_2)\\in H$ the commutator is $([g_1',g_2'],1)$, so $G_{ss}/S_0$ is an abelian compact connected Lie group; its Lie algebra is an abelian quotient of $\\mathfrak s=[\\mathfrak s,\\mathfrak s]$ and therefore vanishes, whence $S_0=G_{ss}$. The projection of $H$ to $U(1)^k$ is closed and contains the powers of the abelian component $(e^{2\\pi i\\alpha_L^l/q})_{l=1}^k$ of $g_1$, with $q=1$ for the displayed pair. Since $\\alpha_L$ is transcendental (Lemma Q.0.7b), $1,\\alpha_L/q,\\ldots,\\alpha_L^k/q$ are linearly independent over $\\mathbb Q$, and Kronecker's theorem makes these powers dense in $U(1)^k$. For $(g,\\tau)\\in\\tilde G$ choose $(g',\\tau)\\in H$; then $(g,\\tau)=(gg'^{-1},1)(g',\\tau)\\in H$. Thus $H=\\tilde G$, and the continuous quotient map onto $G$ preserves density. Item 3: orthogonal control projections make the controlled update block-diagonal unitary, and a constant connection form integrates to its exponential as in Theorem Q.0.7t.2. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q07t4-complete-compact-connected-lie-group-control-census\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.7t.4 (Complete Compact Connected Lie-Group Control Census).** Let $G$ be a finite-dimensional compact connected Lie group, and let $d_{\\mathrm{top}}(G)$ be the least number of elements generating a dense subgroup, with the empty set generating $\\{1\\}$. Then\n$$\nd_{\\mathrm{top}}(G)=\n\\begin{cases}\n0,&G=\\{1\\},\\\\\n1,&G\\ne\\{1\\}\\text{ is abelian},\\\\\n2,&G\\text{ is nonabelian}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{Q.0.7t.4.1}\n$$\nThis census includes every compact simple Lie-algebra type, repeated simple factors, torus factors and finite central quotients. The following exact controls approach the identity as their integer parameter $q\\to\\infty$.\n\nPresent $G$ as\n$$\n\\pi:S\\times U(1)^k\\longrightarrow G\\cong(S\\times U(1)^k)/D,\n\\tag{Q.0.7t.4.2}\n$$\nwhere $S$ is compact, connected, simply connected and semisimple, $k\\ge0$, and $D$ is finite and central. The case $S=\\{1\\}$ is allowed. This presentation follows from the compact-group structure and compactness of the semisimple universal cover (Knapp 2002, Theorems 4.29 and 4.69). For $S\\ne\\{1\\}$, put $\\mathfrak s=\\operatorname{Lie}(S)$ and choose a Chevalley basis of $\\mathfrak s_{\\mathbb C}$ compatible with its compact real form: compact conjugation sends $E_\\alpha$ to $-E_{-\\alpha}$ and $H_a$ to $-H_a$. Enumerate the simple roots of the entire semisimple root system by $a=1,\\ldots,r$, assigning different indices to different factors even when they are isomorphic. Define\n$$\nE_a:=E_{\\alpha_a},\\quad F_a:=E_{-\\alpha_a},\\quad H_a:=[E_a,F_a],\n\\quad C_{ab}:=\\alpha_a(H_b),\\quad b_a:=2^a,\\quad c:=C^{-1}b,\n$$\n$$\nX:=i\\sum_{a=1}^r c_aH_a,\n\\qquad Y:=\\sum_{a=1}^r(E_a-F_a).\n\\tag{Q.0.7t.4.3}\n$$\nThe displayed row convention fixes the Cartan matrix $C$; it is integral and invertible, so $c\\in\\mathbb Q^r$ and $X,Y\\in\\mathfrak s$. The compact Chevalley construction and simple-root generation are given in (Chevalley 1955; Serre 1987, Chapter VI, Sections 4 and 6). For every integer $q\\ge1$, put\n$$\n\\tau_q:=\\bigl(e^{2\\pi i\\alpha_L^\\ell/q}\\bigr)_{\\ell=1}^k,\n\\qquad g_{1,q}:=\\pi(e^{X/q},\\tau_q),\n\\qquad g_{2,q}:=\\pi(e^{Y/q},1).\n\\tag{Q.0.7t.4.4}\n$$\nThese two elements generate densely in $G$. For $S=\\{1\\}$ and $k>0$, the single element $\\pi(1,\\tau_q)$ generates densely; for $S=\\{1\\}$ and $k=0$, the empty set suffices. If $k>0$, the first torus coordinate before quotienting at $q=1$ is $g_L=e^{i\\ln2}$.\n\nFor any continuous finite-dimensional unitary representation $\\rho:G\\to U(d)$, define\n$$\nZ_{1,q}:=d\\pi\\!\\left(X/q;\\,(2\\pi i\\alpha_L^\\ell/q)_{\\ell=1}^k\\right),\n\\qquad Z_{2,q}:=d\\pi(Y/q;0),\n\\tag{Q.0.7t.4.5}\n$$\nomitting absent semisimple terms in the torus case. Thus $g_{j,q}=\\exp_G Z_{j,q}$. On a two-dimensional control register with orthonormal basis $|x\\rangle,|z\\rangle$, the controlled update\n$$\n|x\\rangle\\!\\langle x|\\otimes\\rho(g_{1,q})\n+|z\\rangle\\!\\langle z|\\otimes\\rho(g_{2,q})\n$$\nis unitary, and the constant connection $\\mathcal A_{j,q}=-d\\rho(Z_{j,q})\\,ds$ on a unit parameter loop has holonomy $\\rho(g_{j,q})$ for $D=d+\\mathcal A$. The one-control case uses its one unitary block. The minimality assertion concerns $G$ itself; a faithful $\\rho$ preserves that assertion in its image.\n\nThe group also has an explicit finite separating family of normalized matrix responses. Choose a faithful $\\rho$, which exists for compact Lie groups (Knapp 2002, Corollary 4.22), and let $R(g):=1\\oplus\\rho(g)$ on $\\mathbb C|0\\rangle\\oplus\\mathbb C^d$. For $1\\le a,j\\le d$, set\n$$\n|\\psi_j\\rangle:=\\frac{|0\\rangle+|j\\rangle}{\\sqrt2},\n\\quad X_{0a}:=|0\\rangle\\!\\langle a|+|a\\rangle\\!\\langle0|,\n\\quad Y_{0a}:=-i|0\\rangle\\!\\langle a|+i|a\\rangle\\!\\langle0|,\n\\quad M_{a,\\pm}^{x}:=\\frac{I\\pm X_{0a}}2,\n\\quad M_{a,\\pm}^{y}:=\\frac{I\\pm Y_{0a}}2.\n$$\nEach effect pair is positive and sums to $I$, with probabilities\n$$\np_{aj,\\pm}^{x}(g)\n:=\\langle\\psi_j|R(g)^*M_{a,\\pm}^{x}R(g)|\\psi_j\\rangle\n=\\frac{1\\pm\\operatorname{Re}\\rho(g)_{aj}}2,\n\\qquad\np_{aj,\\pm}^{y}(g)\n:=\\langle\\psi_j|R(g)^*M_{a,\\pm}^{y}R(g)|\\psi_j\\rangle\n=\\frac{1\\pm\\operatorname{Im}\\rho(g)_{aj}}2.\n\\tag{Q.0.7t.4.6}\n$$\nEquality of these finite arrays is equivalent to equality in $G$. For control words, the response equivalence is exactly equality of their products in $G$.\n\n*Proof.* Put $U_a=E_a-F_a$ and $V_a=i(E_a+F_a)$. The Cartan relations and $Cc=b$ give\n$$\n[X,U_a]=2^aV_a,\n\\qquad [X,V_a]=-2^aU_a,\n\\qquad (\\operatorname{ad}_X)^2U_a=-4^aU_a.\n$$\nFor each $a$, the real polynomial\n$$\nP_a(z):=\\prod_{\\substack{1\\le b\\le r\\\\b\\ne a}}\n\\frac{z+4^b}{4^b-4^a}\n$$\n(with empty product $1$) satisfies $P_a(-4^b)=\\delta_{ab}$. Hence $\\mathfrak l:=\\operatorname{Lie}_{\\mathbb R}(X,Y)$ contains\n$$\nP_a\\bigl((\\operatorname{ad}_X)^2\\bigr)Y=U_a,\n\\qquad 2^{-a}[X,U_a]=V_a.\n$$\nIts complexification contains $E_a=(U_a-iV_a)/2$, $F_a=-(U_a+iV_a)/2$ and $H_a=[E_a,F_a]$. Every positive root can be written as a sum of simple roots with every partial sum a root, and the associated iterated root-vector bracket is nonzero. Thus the simple positive and negative root vectors generate every root space and the Cartan subalgebra. Therefore $\\mathfrak l_{\\mathbb C}=\\mathfrak s_{\\mathbb C}$ and $\\mathfrak l=\\mathfrak s$. The global indices separate repeated factors without a diagonal identification.\n\nTo pass to the specified group elements, note that $\\operatorname{ad}_X$ and $\\operatorname{ad}_Y$ have algebraic entries in the Chevalley basis: its structure constants are integers and $c$ is rational. Compactness makes these operators skew-adjoint for a positive-definite inner product. For $Z=X$ or $Y$, their eigenvalues are therefore $i\\lambda_1,\\ldots,i\\lambda_N$ with real algebraic $\\lambda_\\nu$. Diagonalize the operator over $\\mathbb C$. An integer character $m\\in\\mathbb Z^N$ annihilates the cyclic subgroup generated by $(e^{i\\lambda_\\nu/q})_\\nu$ precisely when\n$$\nq^{-1}\\sum_{\\nu=1}^N m_\\nu\\lambda_\\nu\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z.\n$$\nIt annihilates the continuous subgroup $(e^{it\\lambda_\\nu})_\\nu$, $t\\in\\mathbb R$, precisely when $\\sum_\\nu m_\\nu\\lambda_\\nu=0$. The conditions agree: the sum is algebraic, while a nonzero integer multiple of $2\\pi q$ is transcendental (Lindemann 1882). Closed subgroups of a finite torus are determined by their annihilating integer characters, the subgroup form of Kronecker's theorem (Kronecker 1884). Thus the cyclic and continuous adjoint subgroups have the same closure.\n\nLet $K_q:=\\overline{\\langle e^{X/q},e^{Y/q}\\rangle}\\subseteq S$. Its adjoint image is compact and closed, and contains $\\operatorname{Ad}(e^{tX})$ and $\\operatorname{Ad}(e^{tY})$ for every real $t$. Consequently\n$$\n\\operatorname{ad}X,\\operatorname{ad}Y\n\\in\\operatorname{Lie}(\\operatorname{Ad}K_q)\n=\\operatorname{ad}(\\operatorname{Lie}K_q).\n$$\nThe adjoint differential is injective on the semisimple algebra $\\mathfrak s$, so $X,Y\\in\\operatorname{Lie}K_q$. The Lie-generation result gives $\\operatorname{Lie}K_q=\\mathfrak s$. A closed subgroup with the full Lie algebra is open, and connectedness of $S$ gives $K_q=S$. This proves equality in $S$, including its finite center.\n\nLemma Q.0.7b gives rational independence of $1,\\alpha_L/q,\\ldots,\\alpha_L^k/q$: a rational relation would give a nonzero rational polynomial vanishing at the transcendental $\\alpha_L$. Hence $\\overline{\\langle\\tau_q\\rangle}=U(1)^k$ by Kronecker's theorem. Let $H_q$ be the closure of the two-generator subgroup in $S\\times U(1)^k$ before quotienting. Both projections are surjective, by compactness and the density just proved. The subgroup $N_q:=\\{s\\in S:(s,1)\\in H_q\\}$ is closed and normal, since every $s\\in S$ has a lift $(s,\\tau)\\in H_q$. Given arbitrary $s_1,s_2\\in S$, their lifts have commutator $([s_1,s_2],1)$, so $S/N_q$ is abelian and connected. Its Lie algebra is an abelian quotient of $\\mathfrak s=[\\mathfrak s,\\mathfrak s]$, hence zero. Thus $S/N_q$ is trivial and $N_q=S$. Surjectivity onto the torus now yields $H_q=S\\times U(1)^k$. The continuous surjection $\\pi$ preserves density. For $S=\\{1\\}$ the torus argument alone applies. All displayed controls tend to the identity as $q\\to\\infty$.\n\nAn empty set generates $\\{1\\}$, and the closure of a cyclic subgroup is abelian because commutation is preserved under limits. These give the required lower bounds, attained by the constructions. A finite central quotient preserves the Lie algebra, so $G$ is nonabelian exactly when $S\\ne\\{1\\}$; otherwise it is a $k$-torus. The low-rank identifications $B_1=C_1=A_1$, $B_2=C_2$, $D_2=A_1\\oplus A_1$ and $D_3=A_3$ all therefore have minimum two. In particular, $SO(1)$ has minimum zero, $SO(2)$ minimum one, and $SO(n)$ for $n\\ge3$ minimum two. The construction applies without alteration to the remaining classical and exceptional compact forms.\n\nOrthogonal control projections prove unitarity. Forward transport with $\\mathcal A=-d\\rho(Z)\\,ds$ integrates to $e^{d\\rho(Z)}=\\rho(\\exp_G Z)$, giving the holonomies. The eigenvalues of $X_{0a}$ and $Y_{0a}$ lie in $\\{-1,0,1\\}$, so the effects are positive and normalized. The output state is $(|0\\rangle+\\sum_a\\rho(g)_{aj}|a\\rangle)/\\sqrt2$; its displayed expectations are the real and imaginary parts of $\\rho(g)_{aj}$. Equation (Q.0.7t.4.6) therefore recovers every entry of $\\rho(g)$, and faithfulness recovers $g$. The invariant reference turns scalar-center phases into relative phases, retaining distinctions erased by unreferenced conjugation on an irreducible carrier. ∎\n\n<a id=\"definition-q07t5-finite-spin-source-for-decorated-control-words\"></a>**Definition Q.0.7t.5 (Finite-Spin Source for Decorated Control Words).** Fix the finite Hilbert/instrument branch of Definition 27, reuse the local primitive-service contracts and tariff of Definition C.6c.4a, and fix positive units $\\tau_*,u_*,\\hbar$. Use one fixed two-port graph with one lane of descriptor $1$. Each port has the three labeled register factors of Section 7.1.3. All additional data, detector, controller and archive factors below are individually owned local auxiliaries at port $0$. The other port and the unused lane remain in the allocation and holding ledger. No communication service or initial shared input-dependent state is used.\n\nThe source's input is a finite dimension, a finite list of explicitly described Hermitian matrices $L_j$, certified bounds $\\|L_j\\|\\le B_j$, and a finite typed endpoint-protocol tree. The elementary quantum commands are $\\exp(-iL_j)$, their inverses, coherent control by the interface register, the preparations and binary instruments specified below, and identity padding. Each matrix has a finite exact description and an explicit Hermiticity and bound certificate. A formal exact matrix-function description is used in the same ideal-service sense as Definition C.6c.4a; a finite-precision description instead carries its error certificate. This source syntax is fixed for all finite dimensions and matrices before a group or a preferred control word is inserted. Its primitive services name no group, group element or measurement probability.\n\nEncode an $r$-dimensional data carrier in the first $r$ computational basis states of $b=\\lceil\\log_2r\\rceil$ local data qubits. Extend every data operation by the identity on its orthogonal complement. For a controlled operation, the two blocks act on the actual interface qubit, not on a measured copy of that qubit. Let $n$ be the total number of allocated quantum bits, including the six port-carrier bits, data, fresh detector and quantum-record bits, all padded or unused bits and any explicitly retained preparation factors. The bare Hamiltonian is zero, exactly as in Definition C.6c.4a:\n$$\nH_{\\rm idle}=0.\n\\tag{Q.0.7t.5.1}\n$$\nEach gate occupies one complete slot of duration $\\tau_*$. For its full supported Hermitian matrix $L$, choose an integer $m\\ge0$ with $2\\pi m\\ge\\|L\\|$, and put\n$$\nK_L=\\frac\\hbar{\\tau_*}(L+2\\pi m I),\\qquad\nH_L(s)=K_L,\\quad 0<s<\\tau_*.\n\\tag{Q.0.7t.5.2}\n$$\nThe identity in $K_L$ is the identity on the full allocated quantum carrier. One common $m$ is used across both blocks of a coherently controlled operation. Its $2\\pi m$ phase is recorded, not independently removed in the two blocks. The actual local coupling is the following finite spin Hamiltonian, including its scalar part:\n$$\nH_L(s)=\\sum_{P\\in\\{I,X,Y,Z\\}^{\\otimes n}}a_P(s)P,\n\\qquad a_P(s)=2^{-n}\\operatorname{Tr}[PH_L(s)]\\in\\mathbb R.\n\\tag{Q.0.7t.5.3}\n$$\nOnly the port-$0$ factors occur in its nontrivial control support. All Pauli coefficients are constant during the slot, and the pulse is switched off at its endpoints. The finitely many coefficients, their supports, their entrance and exit switches and their bounds are supplied by the local pulse/controller service. This is a finite explicitly driven spin source. Equation (Q.0.7t.5.3) gives the delivered coupling, rather than only a stored target matrix. It is a prescribed certified local pulse of the already nominated finite local-unitary service. The graph, primitive contracts, tariff and inter-port communication rule are unchanged. The endpoint grammar is the control-word grammar defined here; the passive-payload heat grammar of Definition C.6c.4a and its decoder are separate.\n\nAt a slot boundary the source admits arbitrary states on the data and coherent interface carrier, with arbitrary external spectators, independently of its fresh supplier stock conditional on the classical descriptor. Invalid data-code states are not discarded: all gates and effects have the complete extensions specified here. Endpoint arming is the next valid typed command. Each unitary command has one instrument outcome; the perspective kernel is the identity on one nominated perspective. Readout commands have their complete two-outcome instruments. This specifies both clauses of Definition 27.\n\nThe fixed preparation of $(|0\\rangle+|j\\rangle)/\\sqrt2$ uses the unitary $V_j$ that is the Hadamard matrix on $\\operatorname{span}\\{|0\\rangle,|j\\rangle\\}$ and the identity on its complement. Its generator is $L_j^{\\rm prep}=\\frac\\pi2(I-V_j)$, with spectrum in $\\{0,\\pi\\}$. For a Hermitian contraction $A$ on the data, the binary effects are $E_\\pm=(I\\pm A)/2$. Allocate a fresh detector qubit in $|0\\rangle$ and use\n$$\n\\Theta=\\arcsin\\sqrt{E_-},\\qquad\nL_E=Y_{\\rm det}\\otimes\\Theta,\n\\qquad\nU_E=e^{-iL_E}=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\\sqrt{E_+}&-\\sqrt{E_-}\\\\ \\sqrt{E_-}&\\sqrt{E_+}\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\tag{Q.0.7t.5.4}\n$$\nThe source applies the explicit pulse (Q.0.7t.5.2), then the admitted computational-basis record service on that detector, with the detector and every durable outcome copy retained. Thus its selective data map at the complete slot boundary is $\\rho\\mapsto\\sqrt{E_z}\\rho\\sqrt{E_z}$, accompanied by its detector, outcome and source records. A record service occupies its own complete clock slot. No detector, archive or coherent control is reset or reused as fresh.\n\nThe protocol tree has a fixed maximum slot count $S$; short branches use identity slots. Each fresh source preparation, prescribed pulse, record service, classical control evaluation and clock update is listed separately in the emitted finite word. A direct pulse occupies one slot; a binary readout therefore occupies its coupling slot and its separate record slot. Initial installation, coefficient production and checks precede acceptance of the unknown input and have their own finite scheduled slots and costs. The retention deadline is $(S+S_{\\rm setup}+1)\\tau_*$. It includes every reserved but unused branch stock. The source exposes completed quantum outputs, all selective outcomes, the complete classical word and branch history, the full preparation/driver/clock records and the complete resource ledger. It exposes no extra intrapulse command port and no archive-feedback port.\n\nUse exactly the fixed positive tariff coefficients and counters of Definition C.6c.4a: allocated lanes, their idle time, all nonlane bits and bit-time, local pulse and record calls, link calls, clock slots and actual peak strength; the pulse action remains its separate retained record. The finite descriptions of all $4^n$ Pauli coefficients, including zero coefficients, occupy a certified finite number $B_{\\rm desc}$ of controller bits. Coefficient production, checks, loading and all phase/clock preparation are emitted finite local calls; their supports and durations enter those same counters. A supplied external waveform is the declared open control service, not an unlisted autonomous battery. Its required positive peak reserve is bounded by\n$$\nR_L=1+\\left\\lceil B+2\\pi m\\right\\rceil\n\\tag{Q.0.7t.5.5}\n$$\nunits of $\\hbar/\\tau_*$ for every pulse with certified bound $B$. The actual peak is charged by the inherited peak-strength term; the bound is not charged again as a second copy of that service. The source retains its full signed-work and gross-throughput formulae below, without representing the control reserve as reset heat. Each classical description bit and allocated quantum factor is established and maintained once under its named owner. Unused branch stock and the common phase/clock service retain their allocated support, calls and intervals. Every counter is obtained from the emitted finite word and its owned intervals. The full source reference is the **decorated control-word experiment**: its quantum states and elementary instruments, together with those same source, clock, outcome and cost records. Words with equal products remain distinct in it.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q07t6-response-faithful-pu-realization-of-the-compact-group-controls\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.7t.6 (Response-Faithful PU Realization of the Compact-Group Controls).** For every group and every $q$ in Theorem Q.0.7t.4, its minimal generating controls, their inverses and their coherently controlled updates have a realization in the source of Definition Q.0.7t.5. The same source realizes the reference-augmented separating preparations and effects of (Q.0.7t.4.6). For every finite admitted protocol tree and every external quantum reference, an exact intertwiner preserves its complete selective instruments, output states, source records, physical clock and resource vector. The group action on the retained payload is faithful. Product-equal control words have equal payload transformations; their possibly different durations, programmes, invoices and old records remain distinct in the full source response.\n\nFor the exact finite-description mode the construction applies to the canonical compact-Lie presentations and representations with their explicitly described structure and representation matrices. Every compact connected Lie-group isomorphism class admits such a presentation: root and weight lattices, finite central subgroups and torus characters are finite integral data, and the compact highest-weight matrix constructions use finite algebraic operations and square roots. The specified $\\alpha_L$, exponentials and scalar constants have finite descriptions. Arbitrary changes of basis do not need to be physically programmed to realize an isomorphic representative. The abstract census for all continuous representations in Theorem Q.0.7t.4 remains mathematical: a representation given in a basis without finite description has no finite controller programme.\n\n*Proof.* **One admitted compiler and positive physical pulse.** Every input word has finitely many finite-dimensional supports and a finite tree. Its matrix operations, Pauli expansion, norm-bound checks, slot emission and occurrence accounting are finite. A Hermitian matrix has real coefficients in the orthogonal Pauli basis, which proves (Q.0.7t.5.3) and gives an explicit finite list of supplied spin couplings. For $\\|L\\|\\le B$ and $2\\pi m\\ge B$, $K_L\\ge0$, so the total pulse Hamiltonian is nonnegative. Moreover\n$$\n\\|H_L(s)\\|\\le\\frac\\hbar{\\tau_*}(B+2\\pi m),\\qquad\n\\int_0^{\\tau_*}\\|H_L(s)\\|\\,ds\n\\le\\hbar(B+2\\pi m).\n\\tag{Q.0.7t.6.1}\n$$\nThe constant pulse has propagator\n$$\nU_L(s)=e^{-iK_Ls/\\hbar}.\n$$\nAt the endpoint $e^{-2\\pi im}=1$, so\n$$\nU_L(\\tau_*)=e^{-iL}\n\\tag{Q.0.7t.6.2}\n$$\non the entire carrier, with no data-dependent phase. Identity padding has zero Hamiltonian and endpoint $I$, while retaining its time, idle stock and clock charge. The same calculation with block-diagonal $L$ proves the coherent-control assertion: no measurement of its control and no control-conditioned supplier record occurs.\n\nThe waveform's finite work bookkeeping is explicit. There is no continuous variation inside a pulse. Switching on contributes $\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho K_L)$ and switching off contributes $-\\operatorname{Tr}(U_L\\rho U_L^*K_L)$. Since $[U_L,K_L]=0$, they cancel for every input, including inputs entangled with a spectator. The signed work of this ideal complete pulse is zero, and its total absolute mean switching-work throughput is bounded by\n$$\n2\\|K_L\\|\\le\\frac{2\\hbar}{\\tau_*}(B+2\\pi m).\n\\tag{Q.0.7t.6.3}\n$$\nThis is an isolated zero-idle driven pulse with no bath. Prepared stock, controller, holding, peak reserve and later record services retain their positive source charges, and the tariff is separate from net work. The actual peak is $\\|K_L\\|$ and its pulse action is $\\tau_*\\|K_L\\|$, with (Q.0.7t.6.1) as their certified bounds. Thus no drift cancellation, negative total Hamiltonian or unrecorded phase is needed.\n\nThe local service alphabet already admits a finitely described unitary with its complete supported generator and normalization certificate, fresh finite registers, finite classical controls and projective records. Equations (Q.0.7t.5.1)--(Q.0.7t.6.3) supply those data explicitly. All active factors are at the named port, so the source's inter-port rule is satisfied without an uncharged link. The two three-register port carriers and every auxiliary, including unused support, have allocated owners. The new control-word decoder specified here accepts exactly the emitted finite typed expansions and rejects an additional access port, unowned initial resource, missing spectator extension or unlisted command. It uses the inherited primitive contracts without changing the separate heat-source decoder. Conversely every descriptor satisfying the listed finite tests has the displayed expansion. This is admission and exhaustion of one prescribed source class on Definition 27's finite Hilbert/instrument branch.\n\n**Group generators on the same carrier.** Choose a faithful finite unitary representation $\\rho:G\\to U(d)$ and use the reference-augmented $R=1\\oplus\\rho$ on $r=d+1$ dimensions. For the two Lie-algebra elements $Z_{j,q}$ in (Q.0.7t.4.5), set\n$$\nL_{j,q}=i\\,dR(Z_{j,q}),\\qquad\ne^{-iL_{j,q}}=R(g_{j,q}).\n\\tag{Q.0.7t.6.4}\n$$\nThese matrices are Hermitian, their zero reference block is explicit, and their norms have finite certified upper bounds, for example their Frobenius norms enlarged to positive rationals. Inverse controls use $-L_{j,q}$. The coherent command uses\n$$\nL_{{\\rm ctrl},q}=|x\\rangle\\!\\langle x|\\otimes L_{1,q}\n+|z\\rangle\\!\\langle z|\\otimes L_{2,q}\n\\tag{Q.0.7t.6.5}\n$$\non the actual two-level interface. Use the same $m$ for its full matrix. By (Q.0.7t.6.2) the supplied pulse is exactly the controlled $R(g_{j,q})$, with the reference component retained in each block. In the one-generator case retain just its block; the trivial group needs no generating pulse. Multiplying the exact endpoint unitaries realizes every finite word and every inverse word. The constant connection and its ordered transports in Theorem Q.0.7t.4 are realized by these endpoint matrices on the same reference and representation; the connection's unit path parameter is not substituted for the pulse duration $\\tau_*$.\n\nEvery group isomorphism class has a finite exact description. The semisimple simply connected cover is specified by its finite Cartan data. Its integral dominant weights give finite-dimensional representations whose matrix entries are obtained by the finite root-string construction; an admissible lattice gives integer matrices for the Chevalley generators (Humphreys 1972, Section 27). Tensoring and taking finite direct sums supplies representations with the prescribed central characters; finite central descent and a faithful finite sum recover the chosen compact quotient. Torus characters are integral weights. These are the usual finite root/weight constructions behind the faithful-representation and compact-group structure results used in Theorem Q.0.7t.4. Their generators are algebraic after a compact orthonormal basis choice; the displayed $q$ and $\\alpha_L$ add only explicitly described constants. A chosen presentation and its verified representation matrices enter the source descriptor, while all subsequent pulse and record compilation is the single rule above.\n\n**Preparations and complete measurements.** The Hadamard reflection satisfies $V_j^2=I$, so its spectral generator gives $e^{-iL_j^{\\rm prep}}=V_j$. It sends the fresh data vector $|0\\rangle$ to the required $|\\psi_j\\rangle$ without borrowing an undeclared coherent state. The supplied phase/drive reference is already in the inventory. For $A=X_{0a}$ or $Y_{0a}$, extend $A$ by zero on padding. Its spectrum lies in $\\{-1,0,1\\}$, so both effects are positive and normalized on the entire data carrier. Their square roots commute, $\\|\\Theta\\|\\le\\pi/2$, and multiplication of (Q.0.7t.5.4) by its adjoint gives $I$. Applied to $|0\\rangle_{\\rm det}$ it has the two components $\\sqrt{E_+}$ and $\\sqrt{E_-}$. The retained computational record consequently gives exactly the selective maps stated in the definition, including their zero-probability leaves and their detector output states. Summing the maps preserves trace. The separate record slot has the same zero-idle convention; its ideal elementary computational record service is the same registered primitive as in Definition C.6c.4a, not an inferred measurement postulate for the spin Hamiltonian.\n\nTheorem Q.0.7t.4 now gives (Q.0.7t.4.6) as the actual source probabilities. Recovering every entry of the faithful $\\rho(g)$ proves payload response-faithfulness, including scalar central phases that unreferenced conjugation would erase. For example, $R(g)=\\operatorname{diag}(1,zI)$ with $z\\ne1$ changes at least one of these real or imaginary interference responses. No claimed central distinction rests on an unobservable common phase of the entire carrier.\n\n**Full retained intertwiner.** Build the reference experiment with exactly the encoded data and interface carrier, the fresh detector factors, and every prepared supplier and record factor specified by the compiler. Its record label includes command, protocol-tree node, physical entrance and exit times, source/phase identities, outcome, success or failure flag, coefficient-certificate identity and the complete resource counters. The physical encoding is the computational-basis isometry on the data code, tensored with the identity identification on all other retained factors; invalid data-code states use their explicitly defined complementary reference sector. It therefore defines a unitary identification of the full physical carrier with the complete encoded reference carrier, rather than a partial trace over unobserved supplier stock.\n\nFor each full selective label $a$ at history $h$, the equality of the unitary or detector Kraus matrices gives\n$$\n\\widetilde{\\mathcal I}_{a|h}\\bigl(E_h\\rho E_h^*\\bigr)\n=E_{ha}\\mathcal I_{a|h}(\\rho)E_{ha}^*.\n\\tag{Q.0.7t.6.6}\n$$\nEvery fresh source factor is inserted with the same specified state on the two sides; if a supplied state is mixed, that same density operator and its retained supplier factor are part of both reference carriers, rather than represented as a pure-state isometry. Equation (Q.0.7t.6.6) holds for all matrix units and after every spectator identity extension. Composition proves equality of every unnormalized finite history state. Taking traces gives all probabilities; conditioning is used only at positive trace, and null leaves remain zero operators. Adaptive commands are functions of the same previous retained records, so induction over the finite protocol tree proves the same equality for every admitted policy. Summing outcomes, retaining subsets of records and adjoining spectators commute with this equality. The identical emitted schedule and owner assignment preserve every clock and resource entry, including unused stock and the idle second port and lane.\n\nThis full statement is stronger than equality of the matrix-response array but has the correct quotient. A longer word representing the identity still has its executed pulses, clock duration and invoice. The full reference carries that decoration. Projecting both sides to the final data operation forgets only the explicitly selected source records and gives the homomorphism $w\\mapsto R(g_w)$; equality of this payload action is exactly equality of $g_w$, by the reference-augmented separating experiment.\n\n**Finite accuracy and resource completion.** In exact mode all source identities above are exact mathematical laws. For an implemented pulse $\\widetilde H$ with the same duration,\n$$\n\\|\\widetilde U-U\\|\\le\\hbar^{-1}\\int\\|\\widetilde H(s)-H(s)\\|\\,ds\n\\tag{Q.0.7t.6.7}\n$$\nfollows by differentiating the interaction-picture relative propagator and integrating. Its unitary channels differ in full diamond norm by at most twice this quantity. For comparison with an approximate implementation, retain the same actual descriptor, certificate labels, schedules and resource records in the exact-reference experiment and replace only its quantum primitive maps by their specified ideal maps. Different physical descriptor strings are not called trace-distance-close. Assign each full primitive instrument with that common record convention, including preparation and complete outcome flags, a half-diamond error $\\varepsilon_j$. A hybrid argument on the history-extended state bounds the half trace distance of the entire retained output, and hence its total-variation record error, by the largest root-to-leaf sum $\\sum_j\\varepsilon_j$. Thus no conditional rare outcome is assigned an unproved error bound after division by its probability. Classical calibration and missing-record failures are explicit output flags; they are not deleted to improve the estimate.\n\nFor a requested finite matrix-response test, fresh independent trials under the same fixed calibrated descriptor estimate each binary probability. If $K$ probabilities are requested, $N\\ge\\lceil\\log(2K/\\alpha)/(2\\delta^2)\\rceil$ trials per setting give simultaneous sampling error at most $\\delta$ with probability at least $1-\\alpha$. Systematic complete-instrument errors add to this bound, and all $KN$ trial stocks and their retention intervals are reserved beforehand. Exact group separation is independent of this sampling estimate.\n\nAll counts and durations are finite for every finite descriptor and protocol. The Pauli list, setup computation, prepared support, detector and source records, phase/clock services, reserves and holding intervals have explicit disjoint owners. The fixed positive tariff prices their union; each resource occurs once, and shared services retain a single owner across trials. Restriction to a shorter protocol retains its already reserved stocks and invoices rather than making them free. No cyclic erasure, global minimality, autonomous control or uniform resource bound as dimension or word length grows is a premise of this finite source realization. This completes the source, response, clock, composition, resource and error predicates. $\\square$\n\n---\n\n### Q.0.7.18 Summary\n\nThis section shows that repeatedly adding the registered binary phase step fills the circle densely. Physical gauge symmetry and interference still require the separately stated dynamics and calibration records.\n\n#### Technical binary-phase results ledger\n\nThis section has established:\n\n1. **Landauer Irrationality:** $\\alpha_L = \\ln 2/(2\\pi) \\notin \\mathbb{Q}$ (Lemma Q.0.7b), proven via Gelfond–Schneider transcendence\n\n2. **Dense U(1) Emergence:** $\\overline{G_L} = U(1)$ where $G_L = \\{e^{iN\\ln 2}\\}$ (Theorem Q.0.7d)\n\n3. **Exact Phase Separation:** On the registered action-calibrated branch, distinct update counts have distinct baseline phases; an interference claim additionally requires realized coherent histories and their amplitudes (Corollary Q.0.7c)\n\n4. **Exact-Closure Overhead:** On the registered calibrated branch, every nonzero exact-closure lift has $\\Delta>0$; a topological interpretation additionally requires the differential-character identification (Corollary Q.0.7g)\n\n5. **Continued-Fraction Spectrum and Arithmetic Index:** Convergent pairs $(k,N)$ of $\\alpha_L$ satisfy the stated first- and second-kind best-approximation bounds; reduced pairs meeting the strict Legendre threshold are convergents. Physical loop realization remains a separate certificate (Theorem Q.0.7k, Theorem Q.0.7k.1, Corollary Q.0.7l)\n\n6. **Three-Gap Arithmetic:** Finite arithmetic phase sets partition the circle into at most three arc lengths; accessibility requires realized phase-calibrated histories (Proposition Q.0.7n)\n\n7. **Conditional Dephasing:** On the calibrated coherent-ensemble branch, overhead fluctuations suppress the averaged phase by $|\\Phi_\\Delta(1)|$ (Proposition Q.0.7p)\n\n8. **Conditional Gauge-Holonomy Interface:** The discrete $G_L$ action extends to $U(1)$ under the continuity hypotheses; connection holonomy additionally requires the differential-character compatibility certificate (Remark Q.0.7r)\n\n9. **Landauer-Noether Closure:** Continuous finite-resolution response functionals invariant under $G_L$ are invariant under $U(1)$, and, on the separate local-coupling closure branch, the Noether current of Appendix G follows (Theorem Q.0.7d2; Corollary Q.0.7d3)\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q07d8a-necessary-face-budget-for-a-filled-event-holonomy-loop\"></a>**Proposition Q.0.7d8a (Necessary Face Budget for a Filled Event-Holonomy Loop).** Retain the event-character holonomy $e^{iL}$, $L=\\log2$, of Theorem Q.0.7d8. Suppose a proposed filling is an actual oriented disk made of $R$ elementary faces, with their $U(1)$ link variables agreeing on shared edges and with the prescribed loop as its boundary. Let the principal plaquette angles satisfy $|\\theta_f|<\\epsilon$ for a specified $0<\\epsilon<1/270$. Then\n$$\nR\\epsilon>L,\\qquad R>270\\log2>187,\\qquad R\\ge188.\n\\tag{Q.0.7d8a.1}\n$$\nIn particular one elementary face with angle $L$ violates this specified small-field bound. Subdividing only the boundary cannot change its parent holonomy or create the required interior faces.\n\n*Proof.* In the product of the oriented face holonomies, every internal edge cancels with its oppositely oriented occurrence. Thus\n$$\n\\prod_{f=1}^R e^{i\\theta_f}=e^{iL},\\qquad\n\\sum_{f=1}^R\\theta_f=L+2\\pi k\\quad\\text{for some }k\\in\\mathbb Z.\n\\tag{Q.0.7d8a.2}\n$$\nSince $0<L<1<\\pi$, every integer $k$ gives $|L+2\\pi k|\\ge L$. The triangle inequality and strict face bounds therefore give $R\\epsilon>\\sum_f|\\theta_f|\\ge L$. To certify the integer endpoint without a decimal logarithm, integrate the positive geometric series for $1/(1-x^2)$ between zero and $1/3$:\n$$\n\\log2=2\\operatorname{artanh}(1/3)\n=2\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{(1/3)^{2j+1}}{2j+1} >2\\left(\\frac13+\\frac1{81}+\\frac1{1215}\\right)\n=\\frac{842}{1215}\n=\\frac{187}{270}+\\frac1{2430}.\n\\tag{Q.0.7d8a.3}\n$$\nThis proves (Q.0.7d8a.1). The single-face and boundary-only statements follow because the boundary product remains $e^{iL}$. ∎\n\nThe bound is necessary for this filled-disk and prescribed face-angle contract. It supplies neither a sufficient filling nor an admitted physical subdivision, and it does not assert a singular overlap operator, an all-regulator obstruction or a four-dimensional extension. The unfilled flat toron in Lemma Q.0.7d8.2 has all plaquettes equal to one: its circle is noncontractible and is not a filling of the disk in this proposition. The two background domains remain distinct.\n\n### Q.0.8 Conditional Update-Count Phase Structure\n\nThe count $N$ is integer by definition. The phase $e^{iN\\ln2}$ is the selected character of Convention Q.0.6a.0 and becomes a quantum phase only under the declared action calibration and ledger-identification branch.\n\n---\n\n#### Q.0.8.1 The Cycle Number\n\n<a id=\"definition-q02-cycle-number\"></a>**Definition Q.0.2 (Cycle Number).** For a registered finite history $\\phi$, define\n$$\nN[\\phi]:=\\#\\{\\text{registered update events along }\\phi\\}\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}.\n$$\nIf the branch assigns the structural ledger value $\\ln2$ to every counted event, then $\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{disc}}=N[\\phi]\\ln2$. If it also supplies $\\mathcal S=\\kappa_A\\mathcal L$ and $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$, then $\\mathcal S=N[\\phi]\\hbar\\ln2$. Each implication is conditional on its own ledger.\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q03-discrete-integrality\"></a>**Proposition Q.0.3 (Discrete Integrality).** The count $N[\\phi]$ is a nonnegative integer. This is immediate from the cardinality definition and supplies no quantization of an independently defined mechanical action. ∎\n\n#### Q.0.8.2 Topological Quantization for Closed Paths\n\nFor a closed-path branch on which the total transported amplitude is required to have trivial holonomy, the following conditional quantization identity holds.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q04-conditional-phase-holonomy-quantization\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.4 (Conditional Phase-Holonomy Quantization).** Let $\\vartheta$ be a locally defined action one-form on a closed path $\\gamma$, and suppose parallel transport multiplies the amplitude by\n$$\n\\exp\\!\\left(\\frac{i}{\\hbar}\\oint_\\gamma\\vartheta\\right).\n$$\nRequiring the transported amplitude to return to the same value gives\n$$\n\\frac1\\hbar\\oint_\\gamma\\vartheta=2\\pi k,\n\\qquad k\\in\\mathbb Z.\n$$\nIf a separately calibrated phase ledger identifies $\\hbar^{-1}\\oint_\\gamma\\vartheta$ with $\\sum_iq_i$, then $\\sum_iq_i=2\\pi k$. A globally single-valued scalar action instead satisfies $\\oint_\\gamma d\\mathcal S=0$. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q04a-selected-character-closed-sector-obstruction\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.4a (Selected-Character Closed-Sector Obstruction).** On the selected character branch $N\\mapsto e^{iN\\ln2}$, exact trivial holonomy requires\n$$\nN\\ln2=2\\pi k,\n\\qquad N,k\\in\\mathbb Z.\n$$\nSince $\\ln2/(2\\pi)$ is irrational, the only solution is $N=k=0$. This is a statement about the selected character, not a universal obstruction on physical cycles. ∎\n\n<a id=\"remark-q04b-non-integer-period-and-resolution\"></a>**Remark Q.0.4b: Non-Integer Period and Resolution.** The formal real-valued period $\\Delta N_0=2\\pi/\\ln2\\approx9.0647$ is not an admissible integer count period. On the selected-character branch,\n$$\ne^{iN\\ln2}=1\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nN\\ln2=2\\pi k.\n$$\nIrrationality of $\\ln2/(2\\pi)$ implies $N=k=0$, so the character has no nonzero integer period. This is an arithmetic obstruction, not a physical tension requiring a dynamical resolution.\n\nFor consecutive integer labels, the orbit $\\{e^{iN\\ln2}\\}$ is equidistributed in $U(1)$. A physical ensemble is uniform only if realized histories sample those labels with the required weights. At finite budget, convergent denominators give record-small arithmetic mismatches. Approximate interference additionally requires separately realized coherent histories, amplitudes, and an operational phase tolerance.\n\n---\n\n#### Q.0.8.3 Vacuum Excitation Structure from Leech Geometry\n\nThe Leech shell ledger is available only on a branch that independently registers an even unimodular rootless rank-$24$ lattice, such as the lattice that the explicit coordinate construction following Lemma R.4.5 builds from a marked Golay copy, or supplies the full discriminant-form and coset-minimum datum of Lemma R.4.5.\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q05-conditional-leech-shell-structure\"></a>**Proposition Q.0.5 (Conditional Leech Shell Structure).** Assume a registered discriminant-form, marking, and coset-minimum certificate of Lemma R.4.5 constructs an even unimodular rootless rank-$24$ lattice $\\Lambda$. Then $\\Lambda\\cong\\Lambda_{24}$ by a Euclidean isometry, its nonzero minimum squared norm is $4$, and its first shell norms are $4,6,8,\\ldots$. The certificate fixes the marked representative; classification alone fixes only its isometry class.\n\n*Proof.* The registered certificate supplies evenness, unimodularity, rank $24$, and rootlessness. Niemeier's classification identifies the unique Euclidean-isometry class with those properties as the class represented by $\\Lambda_{24}$. In the convention used here, its $\\theta$ series is (Conway and Sloane 1999)\n$$\n\\Theta_{\\Lambda_{24}}(q)\n=\n1+196560q^4+16773120q^6+398034000q^8+\\cdots.\n$$\nThe first nonconstant term therefore occurs at squared norm $4$, and the displayed subsequent terms give shell norms $6$ and $8$. Golay parameters alone do not supply the registered gluing or coset-minimum certificate. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q05a-mass-action-correspondence-on-the-canonical-norm-information-and-mathfrak-b_mass-branches\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.5a (Mass--Action Correspondence on the Canonical Norm--Information and $\\mathfrak B_{mass}$ Branches).** On the canonical Leech norm--information calibration branch, where $\\mathcal I_{\\mathrm{rel}}(v)=|v|$, and on the canonical mass--action branch $\\mathfrak B_{mass}=\\mathfrak B_{\\mathrm{mass}}(1)$ of Appendix N, whose independent records include the certified relational-cut decomposition, additive concurrent saturated activity, $C_*^{\\mathrm{rel}}=2\\varepsilon_0$, stationarity on the integration interval (or the pointwise reading), the same-cycle action/update map, and the clock/spacing calibration, a vacuum excitation labeled by $v\\in\\Lambda_{24}$ satisfies\n$$\nm^2(v)=(\\mu_0^{\\mathrm{alg}})^2|v|^2,\n\\qquad\n\\mu_0^{\\mathrm{alg}}:=\\frac{m_P}{2\\sqrt{8\\varepsilon_0}}.\n$$\nThe coefficient $\\mu_0^{\\mathrm{alg}}$ is an algebraic normalization and becomes a physical mass coefficient only on $\\mathfrak B_{mass}$.\n\n*Proof.* On $\\mathfrak B_{mass}$, Theorem N.5 gives $m=\\mu_0^{\\mathrm{alg}}\\mathcal I_{\\mathrm{rel}}$. The independent canonical norm--information certificate gives $\\mathcal I_{\\mathrm{rel}}(v)=|v|$. Combining and squaring proves the formula. Neither branch follows from Leech shell structure alone. ∎\n\n<a id=\"remark-q05a1-conditional-derivation-chain\"></a>**Remark Q.0.5a.1: Conditional Derivation Chain.** A registered Lemma R.4.5 gluing/rootlessness certificate first identifies the Leech shell geometry. The physical mass map then additionally requires $\\mathfrak B_{mass}$ and the norm--information calibration.\n\n<a id=\"remark-q05a2-phenomenological-status\"></a>**Remark Q.0.5a.2: Phenomenological Status.** Leech geometry provides a discrete norm spectrum. On $\\mathfrak B_{mass}$ with canonical calibration it induces the stated conditional mass spectrum. Connecting that branch to observed particles requires an independently specified identification between lattice excitations and particle states.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q05a3-finite-marked-excitation-carrier-and-unfixed-mass-scale\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.5a.3 (Finite Marked Excitation Carrier and Unfixed Mass Scale).** Let $V=\\{v_1,\\ldots,v_r\\}$ be any finite marked set of Leech vectors and let $\\mathcal H_V$ have orthonormal basis $|v_j\\rangle$. The map $v_j\\mapsto|v_j\\rangle$ is injective, the commuting projectors\n$$\nP_j:=|v_j\\rangle\\!\\langle v_j|\n$$\nretain all concurrent marked labels, and for every $\\mu>0$\n$$\nM_\\mu^2\n:=\\mu^2\\sum_{j=1}^r|v_j|^2P_j\n\\tag{Q.0.5a.3.1}\n$$\nis a positive self-adjoint finite mass-squared operator satisfying $M_\\mu^2|v_j\\rangle=\\mu^2|v_j|^2|v_j\\rangle$. Its positive square root is\n$$\nM_\\mu:=\\mu\\sum_{j=1}^r|v_j|P_j.\n\\tag{Q.0.5a.3.2}\n$$\nThe singleton effects $(P_j)_{j=1}^r$ form a normalized projection-valued measure because $\\sum_jP_j=I_{\\mathcal H_V}$, and every subset-label event is represented by the corresponding sum of projectors.\n\nThe dimensionless marked-lattice data do not select $\\mu$. For any $c>0$, the models $(\\mathcal H_V,P_j,M_\\mu)$ and $(\\mathcal H_V,P_j,M_{c\\mu})$ have identical lattice norms, markings, label probabilities and automorphism relations, but every nonzero mass is multiplied by $c$. Therefore a target-independent physical mass unit is non-identifiable from the marked Leech carrier alone; it requires an additional dimensionful clock/action record such as $\\mathfrak B_{mass}$.\n\n*Proof.* Orthogonality proves injectivity, commutativity, normalization and positivity. Equations (Q.0.5a.3.1)--(Q.0.5a.3.2) are diagonal in the marked basis, give the stated eigenvalues, and satisfy $(M_\\mu)^2=M_\\mu^2$. Scaling $\\mu$ changes no dimensionless lattice or projector datum and multiplies every nonzero eigenvalue of $M_\\mu$ by $c$ (and of $M_\\mu^2$ by $c^2$), producing two models indistinguishable by the registered lattice data. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q05a4-calibrated-marked-leech-particle-realization\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.5a.4 (Calibrated Marked Leech Particle Realization).** On the canonical four-dimensional complex-scalar source with marked internal configuration torus, isotropic metric, common gradient law and independently specified positive calibrator inputs below, every finite marked Leech-vector set has a positive physical particle mass operator, a singleton positive calibration fiber and finite normalized label/preparation/response instruments. The internal dimension is a configuration dimension. The registered class consists of exactly reducing finite marked sectors; it uses no unregulated infinite internal tower. Empty sectors have zero carrier and no experiment; a singleton permits the trivial label readout.\n\n*Proof.*\n\n### 2. Marked lattice, configuration torus and exact mode map\n\nLet $\\Lambda$ be a fixed, coordinate-marked, positive-definite even unimodular rootless lattice of rank 24 with its accepted Euclidean inner product. Its nonzero minimum squared norm is 4. Let $\\Lambda^*$ be its Euclidean dual. Self-duality follows from unimodularity and integrality; retaining the dual notation prevents a marking from being silently replaced by an abstract isometry.\n\nDefine the compact internal configuration manifold\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nY&=\\mathbb{R}^{24}/(2\\pi \\Lambda^{*}), \\\\\nds_Y^{2}&=R^{2}\\, dy\\cdot dy,\\quad R>0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse normalized Haar measure dnu on Y. Equivalently, with the unnormalized metric volume measure one uses characters divided by the square root of that known volume. This is the actual source/mode normalization whose detector coupling must accompany any field redefinition. The physical source specifies this entire isotropic metric shape; only its scale R is determined by the separate calibration in Section 5. One clock measurement alone would not identify an arbitrary anisotropic internal metric.\n\nFor every $v$ in $\\Lambda$ define $e_v(y)=\\exp(iv\\cdot y)$. It is well defined because $v\\cdot\\lambda^*$ is an integer for every $\\lambda^*$ in $\\Lambda^*$. The characters are orthonormal in $L^2(Y,d\\nu)$: integrating a nontrivial character over its compact group gives zero by translation invariance, while each character has norm one. They are complete, for example by transferring the standard Fourier basis on the marked lattice-coordinate torus.\n\nThe nonnegative internal Laplacian $A=-\\Delta_Y$, defined by the closed quadratic form $R^{-2}\\int|\\nabla_yf|^2\\,d\\nu$ on $H^1(Y)$, is self-adjoint with domain $H^2(Y)$, and\n\n$$\nA e_v=|v|^{2}/R^{2}\\, e_v.\n$$\n\nEquivalently its exact spectral domain is $\\{\\sum a_ve_v:\\sum(1+|v|^4/R^4)|a_v|^2<\\infty\\}$. This character expansion proves self-adjointness directly and fixes its boundary conditions. The scalar gradient action, rather than a postulated particle mass matrix, supplies $A$.\n\nFor every finite set V of distinct marked vectors, including zero if desired, the map\n\n$$\nF_V: H_V \\to L^{2}(Y),\\quad F_V|v\\rangle=e_v\n$$\n\nis an isometry onto the reducing subspace $E_{V}$. Its adjoint transports the internal Laplacian, translation operators and all subset label effects exactly. The state-amplitude translation convention on this Fourier space is $T(a)f(y)=f(y+a)$, so $T(a)e_v=\\exp(iv\\cdot a)e_v$. It is the inverse of the field-operator pullback in Section 4. A marked lattice isometry acts on amplitudes as $T(g)f(y)=f(g^{-1}y)$, giving $T(g)e_v=e_{gv}$. No norm shell is collapsed. In particular $e_{v}$ and $e_{-v}$ are orthogonal whenever $v$ is nonzero.\n\n### 3. The four-dimensional action and particle pole\n\nPhysical spacetime is $M=\\mathbb{R}^{1,3}$, $x^0=ct$, with signature $(+---)$. The source field $\\Phi(x,y)$ is complex and takes values in $L^2(Y)$. Its dimension is inverse length in the canonical action\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS[\\Phi]&=\\hbar \\int_M d^{4}x \\int_Y d\\nu \\\\\n&\\quad[\\partial_\\mu \\Phi^{*}\\, \\partial^\\mu \\Phi \\\\\n&\\qquad-R^{-2} \\nabla_y \\Phi^{*} \\cdot \\nabla_y \\Phi].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3.1}\n$$\n\nThe internal derivative is a potential operator in this four-dimensional Hilbert-valued field theory. It does not enlarge the spacetime causal metric or its Lorentz group. The source inputs are the marked configuration geometry, its isotropic metric, c, the external action unit $\\hbar$ and the displayed common gradient law. The common gradient coefficient is an admitted physical coupling law of this source, not a deduction from the lattice or a coordinate choice. They contain no target particle masses or separate shell gains. A source with an extra free internal-gradient coefficient would be a different branch requiring its own independent coupling record; the present theorem does not silently set an unmeasured coefficient to one.\n\nThe admitted theorem class consists of every finite marked $V$, with initial data and retained drives in $E_{V}$. Each such sector is an ordinary finite collection of free fields with its canonical Fock representation. Formula (3.1) is an economical common notation; no trace over an unregulated infinite tower, absolute vacuum sum or interacting continuum limit is used. $E_{V}$ is an exact invariant sector of the free action, not an approximate truncation. Substitution $\\Phi=\\sum_{v\\in V}\\phi_v(x)e_v(y)$ and character orthogonality give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_V&=\\hbar \\sum_v \\int d^{4}x \\\\\n&\\quad[\\partial_\\mu \\phi_v^{*}\\, \\partial^\\mu \\phi_v \\\\\n&\\qquad-\\kappa_v^{2} |\\phi_v|^{2}], \\\\\n\\kappa_v^{2}&=|v|^{2}/R^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3.2}\n$$\n\nVarying the action gives $(\\Box+\\kappa_v^2)\\phi_v=0$. There are no cross terms even when masses coincide. Complex fields are used deliberately: no reality constraint identifies the marked coefficient fields $v$ and $-v$.\n\nWith a source term $\\hbar\\int(J_v^*\\phi_v+\\phi_v^*J_v)$, the driven equation is $(\\Box+\\kappa_v^2)\\phi_v=J_v$. In four-dimensional wavenumber variables $k$, the canonically normalized Feynman two-point kernel is\n\n$$\nG_{vw}(k)=\\delta_{vw}\\, i/[k^{2}-\\kappa_v^{2}+i0].\n\\tag{3.3}\n$$\n\nThis follows by inverting the quadratic action, or from the canonical expansion below. The unit residue is tied to this actual action and source normalization. Under a nonzero constant field redefinition $\\Phi'=z\\Phi$, the quadratic coefficient becomes $1/|z|^2$, the field two-point residue becomes $|z|^2$ and the source becomes $J'=J/z^*$. Transforming the detector $\\Phi=\\Phi'/z$ at the same time leaves its physical source-to-readout response invariant. Thus the pole is invariant under a field convention, while a bare residue without the source/detector normalization is not an invariant prediction. Its residue at $k^2=\\kappa_v^2$ is the identity on that exact marked mode, and the corresponding Kallen–Lehmann spectral measure is $\\delta(\\sigma-\\kappa_v^2)$ with positive unit coefficient. Degenerate masses produce a diagonal residue matrix with full marked multiplicity, rather than one unidentified pole state. The inverse susceptibility in Section 7 independently derives the same pole and normalization from the driven action.\n\nFor completeness, put $\\omega_v(k)=\\sqrt{|k|^2+\\kappa_v^2}$. The free canonical expansion in $x^0$ has particle and antiparticle operators with\n\n$$\n[a_v(k),a_w^{*}(k')]=\\delta_{vw} (2\\pi)^{3}\\, 2\\omega_v(k)\\, \\delta^{3}(k-k'),\n$$\n\nand the same relation for $b$, all other elementary commutators zero. The field expansion has measure $d^3k/[(2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega_v]$ and positive/negative frequency factors $\\exp(-i\\omega_{v}x^0+ik\\cdot x)$ and their conjugates. It gives $[\\phi_v(x^0,x),\\partial_0\\phi_w^*(x^0,x')]=i\\delta_{vw}\\delta^3(x-x')$, as fixed by (3.1), and the two-point function (3.3).\n\nThe positive one-particle Hilbert space is the direct sum over $v$ of $L^2(\\mathbb{R}^3,d^3k/[(2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega_v])$, for each particle/antiparticle charge sector. Physical translations act with\n\n$$\nP_0=\\hbar \\omega_v(k),\\quad P=\\hbar k,\\quad E=cP_0.\n$$\n\nThese are the Noether translation generators of (3.1). Each mode carries the scalar positive-energy Poincare representation with Casimir\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_\\mu P^\\mu&=\\hbar^{2} \\kappa_v^{2}, \\\\\nM^{2}&=(P_\\mu P^\\mu)/c^{2}, \\\\\nm_v^{2}&=\\hbar^{2} |v|^{2}/(c^{2}R^{2}).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3.4}\n$$\n\nHence (3.4) is a particle pole/Casimir consequence of the actual action. It was not inserted as a diagonal norm operator. The positive square root is $M=(\\hbar/(cR))\\sum|v|P_v$. For $v=0$ this is a massless scalar sector; no rest frame or normalizable zero-momentum rest state is claimed. Its one-particle state is distinct from the Fock vacuum: particle number separates them. The marked zero vector is not silently identified with the source vacuum.\n\nVacuum subtraction is fixed in the free Fock representation: translation generators annihilate the vacuum. It changes no pole, residue, excitation energy or relative label. One can define this reference by finite-volume/momentum-cutoff normal ordering before taking the free limit. No absolute gravitational vacuum-energy conclusion is consumed here.\n\n### 4. Particle-sector identification and all marked labels\n\nThe abstract carrier $H_{V}$ embeds into the particle sector through the internal Fourier map and a separately registered normalized momentum wavepacket. More intrinsically, the particle Hilbert space is a direct sum of mass-shell wavepacket spaces indexed by $V$, with exact effects $P_{v}$ that are identity on that wavepacket sector and zero elsewhere. Its mass operator is $M\\otimes\\text{identity}$ on each momentum fiber. Explicitly, a Lebesgue-normalized wavepacket $\\psi_v(k)$ is sent to $f_v(k)=\\sqrt{(2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega_v(k)}\\,\\psi_v(k)$ in the displayed invariant-measure space. This is unitary by direct integration, giving a realization on $H_{V}\\otimes L^2(\\mathbb{R}^3,d^3k)$; it does not change $P_{v}$ or $M$. The Poincare action on this tensor realization is the direct sum of its mass-dependent scalar representations, including the known measure Jacobian in Lebesgue normalization. A fixed-wavepacket injection of $H_{V}$ alone is not claimed to be a finite-dimensional Poincare-invariant subspace.\n\nAll concurrent subset effects $P_A=\\sum_{v\\in A}P_v$ are normalized, positive, commuting and exhaustive. The particle/antiparticle charge is retained as an additional label. We use the positive particle sector for the original $H_{V}$ identification; antiparticles are not silently counted as replacements for opposite marked vectors.\n\nInternal translations act by characters on particle labels. Fix the convention that $U(a)\\Phi(x,y)U(a)^*=\\Phi(x,y-a)$; then $a_{v}^*$ creates a particle transforming by $\\exp(iv\\cdot a)$, while its antiparticle has the opposite internal character. Indeed the one-particle wavefunction $\\psi_\\chi(y)=\\langle 0|\\Phi(y)|\\chi\\rangle$ satisfies $\\psi_{U(a)\\chi}(y)=\\psi_\\chi(y+a)$. Thus the Fourier-to-particle square uses the state-amplitude $T(a)$, not the field-operator adjoint pullback with the inverse argument. For a marking isometry choose $U(g)\\Phi(y)U(g)^*=\\Phi(gy)$; its state amplitude transforms as $\\psi(g^{-1}y)$. A marked lattice isometry $g$ therefore transports $e_{v}$ to $e_{gv}$, preserves the gradient action and maps $P_{v}$ to $P_{gv}$. If $g$ does not stabilize $V$ it transports the whole registered sector to $E_{gV}$; no illegal invariance of a noninvariant finite set is assumed. Physical Poincare transformations act within the scalar mass-shell representation and commute with the internal label effects.\n\nOn the full positive-frequency solution/wavepacket spaces the source-to-Fourier-to-particle map intertwines these translations, marked isometries and Poincare actions, the internal Laplacian and physical mass Casimir, and every subset-label readout. The isometry invariance follows from preservation of both $\\Lambda$ and the Euclidean metric. Thus the maps are on the same marked source, not a relabeling of unrelated equal-norm levels.\n\nFor a fixed fully marked finite $V$, all internal isometries implementing the same singleton label maps differ by independent phases on each one-dimensional internal mode. This is the complete $U(1)^{|V|}$ freedom. A change of marking additionally permutes the labels by its registered lattice isometry. If one deletes singleton labels and retains only mass shells, extra unitary rotations within equal-norm eigenspaces become possible; this larger equivalence is explicitly excluded from the present marked source. Momentum-fiber intertwiners are fixed separately by the scalar Poincare and normalization conventions.\n\nFor each nonzero fixed label, the same exact dispersion gives physical inertial response: $E_v(p)=\\sqrt{c^2|p|^2+m_v^2c^4}$, velocity $\\nabla_pE_v=c^2p/E_v$, and its inverse $p=m_vu/\\sqrt{1-|u|^2/c^2}$. The low-momentum kinetic Hessian is $I/m_v$. These statements follow from the same translation Hamiltonian, rather than assigning an independent mechanical mass. For wavepackets the velocity is the corresponding operator function; no narrow-packet approximation is silently used as an exact mixed-state identity.\n\nMixed or coherent internal states retain the whole operator $M$. The fully quantum energy is $\\sqrt{c^2P^2+c^4M^2}$ by the direct-sum dispersion law. Replacing this by the expectation of $M$ is not part of the source model or any response claim.\n\n### 5. Independent geometry/action/clock calibration\n\nSelect a marked primitive $\\lambda^*$ in $\\Lambda^*$. A minimal Leech vector is primitive: if $\\lambda^*=nw$ with $|n|\\ge 2$, then $|w|\\le 1$ contradicts the lattice minimum 2. The coordinate-marked minimal $\\lambda^*$ in Section 8 has squared norm 4.\n\nThe same configuration metric is independently interrogated by a classical geodesic calibrator. Its configuration $q(t)$ is a point of $Y$, with a separately calibrated inertial standard $M_{\\mathrm{cal}}>0$ and action\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{\\mathrm{cal}}&=(M_{\\mathrm{cal}}/2) \\int ds_Y^{2}/dt^{2}\\, dt \\\\\n&=(M_{\\mathrm{cal}} R^{2}/2) \\int |\\dot{q}|^{2}\\, dt.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5.1}\n$$\n\nThis is a mechanical calibration of the internal configuration metric, not additional physical spacetime. Its clock and impulse are external calibration records; they do not use any particle spectrum from (3.1). The overlap with the field source is the identical metric $ds_Y^2$ in (3.1) and (5.1), not two independently adjustable radii.\n\nPrepare the geodesic $q(t)=s(t)\\lambda^*$ with no orthogonal momentum. The angle $s$ has first period $2\\pi$ because $\\lambda^*$ is primitive. An independently calibrated generalized angular impulse fixes its canonical momentum $p_s=J>0$. Its exact reduced Lagrangian, momentum and cycle period are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{\\mathrm{cal}}&=(I_{\\mathrm{cal}}/2)\\, \\dot{s}^{2}, \\\\\nI_{\\mathrm{cal}}&=M_{\\mathrm{cal}} R^{2} |\\lambda^{*}|^{2}, \\\\\np_s&=I_{\\mathrm{cal}}\\, \\dot{s}=J, \\\\\n\\tau_{\\mathrm{cal}}&=2\\pi I_{\\mathrm{cal}}/J.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5.2}\n$$\n\nThe mean preparation work is the owned calibrator kinetic energy $J^2/(2I_{\\mathrm{cal}})=\\pi J/\\tau_{\\mathrm{cal}}$; it is not a free initialization. There is no force or dissipation on the registered free geodesic interval. The separately recorded first-return clock therefore fixes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR^{2}&=J \\tau_{\\mathrm{cal}}/[2\\pi M_{\\mathrm{cal}} |\\lambda^{*}|^{2}], \\\\\n\\mu^{2}&=\\hbar^{2}/(c^{2}R^{2}) \\\\\n&=2\\pi M_{\\mathrm{cal}} |\\lambda^{*}|^{2} \\hbar^{2}/(c^{2} J \\tau_{\\mathrm{cal}}).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5.3}\n$$\n\nAll entries on the last line are external geometry/action/clock calibration data. Their mass standard $M_{\\mathrm{cal}}$ is an independently specified calibrator, not a mass of a target lattice particle. No selected $m_{v}$ is used in determining $\\mu$. The physical scale-modulus freedom of the accepted theorem is therefore broken by exactly a new dimensionful source record, as its negative result requires. This is not merely naming the free mass modulus a radius: with $M_{\\mathrm{cal}}$ and $J$ fixed, two different radii give different directly observed return times $\\tau_{\\mathrm{cal}}$ proportional to $R^2$. The positive-radius calibration fiber for a fixed record is a singleton. In ordinary mechanical terms, choosing a dual lattice basis writes $q=B_{\\mathrm{dual}}\\theta$ with 24 periodic coordinates $\\theta_{j}$; (5.1) is a constant positive 24-rotor kinetic matrix $M_{\\mathrm{cal}}R^2B_{\\mathrm{dual}}^TB_{\\mathrm{dual}}$. It is an internal mechanical configuration with an explicit action, not a claim of 24 additional spacetime directions.\n\nA populated choice in consistent fixed units is $M_{\\mathrm{cal}}=5$, $c=3$, $\\hbar=2$, $J=6$ and $\\tau_{\\mathrm{cal}}=7$, with $|\\lambda^*|^2=4$. It gives $R^2=21/(20\\pi)$, $\\mu^2=80\\pi/189$ and calibrator energy $6\\pi/7$. These inputs are not chosen from or adjusted to particle mass data.\n\nFor bounded calibration uncertainty, suppose each positive input has a registered interval and the exact same geometric overlap. Monotonicity of (5.3) supplies the rigorous interval obtained by putting $M_{\\mathrm{cal}}$ and $\\hbar$ at their lower bounds and $c,J,\\tau$ at their upper bounds for $\\mu^2_{\\mathrm{lower}}$, and the reverse endpoints for $\\mu^2_{\\mathrm{upper}}$. If $\\hbar,c,M_{\\mathrm{cal}},J$ are exact and $\\tau$ belongs to $[\\tau_-,\\tau_+]$, this reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&2\\pi M_{\\mathrm{cal}} |\\lambda^{*}|^{2} \\hbar^{2}/(c^{2}J \\tau_{+}) \\\\\n&\\quad\\le\\mu^{2}\\le \\\\\n&2\\pi M_{\\mathrm{cal}} |\\lambda^{*}|^{2} \\hbar^{2}/(c^{2}J \\tau_{-}).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe same interval multiplied by $|v|^2$ predicts every squared particle mass, without per-label compensation. Calibration precision controls the prediction width; it does not change the marked response law.\n\n### 6. Finite exact label readout, including opposite vectors and degeneracies\n\nChoose a marked lattice basis $B$ of $\\Lambda$ and dual basis columns $q_{j}$ of $B^{-T}$. Write every $v$ in $V$ uniquely as $v=Bn(v)$, $n(v)$ in $\\mathbb{Z}^{24}$. Choose an integer $N$ greater than $\\max_{v,w,j}|n_j(v)-n_j(w)|$. The retained 24 commuting translation unitaries\n\n$$\nT_j=U(2\\pi q_j/N)\n$$\n\nhave eigenvalues $\\exp(2\\pi i\\,n_j(v)/N)$ on particle label $v$. Their joint residue vectors $n(v)$ modulo $N$ distinguish all elements of $V$, including $v$ and $-v$ whenever both occur. The exact joint effect for a residue $r$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_r&=\\prod_{j=1}^{24} [(1/N)\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{N-1} \\\\\n&\\quad\\exp(-2\\pi i \\ell r_j/N)\\, T_j^\\ell].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6.1}\n$$\n\nOn the finite sector this equals the sum of $P_{v}$ with $n(v)=r\\bmod N$. The chosen $N$ makes each populated effect a singleton projector. The root-of-unity geometric sum proves (6.1), positivity, normalization and exhaustiveness exactly.\n\nAn actual finite ancilla protocol prepares a uniform $N$-state register, applies controlled powers $T_j^\\ell$, applies the inverse discrete Fourier transform and reads its computational basis. Repeating for all 24 commuting controls implements (6.1). It owns 24 finite $N$-level outcome registers; a unary implementation uses at most $24(N-1)$ controlled translations. The internal translation charges $Q_{j}$ are the self-adjoint generators of the configuration-translation symmetry of (3.1), with one-particle eigenvalues $n_j(v)$. On the admitted one-particle finite-$V$ sector they are bounded. The controlled Hamiltonian with blocks $-(2\\pi\\hbar\\ell/(Nt_c))Q_j$ implements each controlled power in time $t_{c}$ in the interaction picture, with action norm at most $2\\pi\\hbar\\max_v|n_j(v)|$. Any needed positive offset is an owned control-apparatus term and is not inserted into $M$. The free one-particle Hamiltonian commutes with all $Q_{j}$, so its common evolution factors out and leaves every label effect unchanged. Ancilla Fourier transforms and retained readout registers are separate finite controls. Records are retained and no free reset is asserted. The conditional internal translation acts on the same field configuration fiber and does not change its particle mass or momentum sector. It is not an assumed mass measurement.\n\nFor an approximately bandlimited preparation with outside-V probability $\\delta$, the normalized projected state differs in trace norm by at most 2sqrt(delta), so every bounded retained readout changes by at most 2||O||sqrt(delta). Unbounded energy/mass comparisons additionally require their own weighted-tail bound. The exact admitted finite class has $\\delta=0$, because both the free dynamics and registered drives preserve $E_{V}$.\n\n### 7. Observable particle pole and certified finite-time source response\n\nThe particle pole can be checked by an independent driven response of the same action. Nonvacuous single-particle preparation is also explicit in a finite physical calibration box: select one marked mode and nonzero physical momentum, couple its annihilator $a$ to a two-level preparation ancilla by $\\hbar\\gamma(\\sigma_-a^*+\\sigma_+a)$, and give that ancilla the mode energy dictated by (3.2). Its excited state owns particle charge one and internal character $v$, while its ground state is neutral. Thus $\\sigma_-a^*$ and its adjoint are invariant under the jointly transported charge/translation action; a marking update transports the ancilla label along with $v$. Choose $0<\\gamma\\le c\\,\\omega_v$, which the populated $\\gamma=1$ obeys. In the one-excitation sector its resonant Hamiltonian is a scalar plus $\\hbar\\gamma\\sigma_x$. Starting from the excited ancilla and the selected oscillator vacuum, a $\\pi/(2\\gamma)$ pulse produces the exact one-particle mode. Total excitation is conserved, and the transferred energy $\\hbar c\\,\\omega_{v}$ is the owned preparation cost. The ancilla is used after the independent metric calibration; its mode-dependent setting is a derived preparation control, not data used to fit $R$. The finite-box protocol retains only its selected nonzero physical Fourier momentum and its conjugate partner, an exact reducing restriction of the free field and these drives. Every retained oscillator then has positive frequency, including the zero-mass internal label. No normalized oscillator vacuum is assigned to the massless spatial $k=0$ box coordinate; that coordinate is outside this retained preparation/observation sector. The later infinite-volume free-field limit preserves the exact internal label and mass operator. For a normalized physical spatial Fourier mode, or a finite periodic calibration box mode, the marked coefficient obeys\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ddot{q}+\\omega^{2} q&=c^{2} j(t), \\\\\n\\omega^{2}&=c^{2}(|k|^{2}+|v|^{2}/R^{2}).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinite box normalization is owned in the spatial source/readout mode; it changes no mass term. Infinite-volume particle poles are the exact free distributional limit of the same dispersion law. Choose a drive $j(t)=j_0\\exp[(\\epsilon-i\\Omega)t]$ for $-L\\le t\\le 0$, $\\epsilon>0$, with zero response at $t=-L$. Variation of (3.1) and the retarded oscillator kernel give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nq(0)/j_0&=c^{2} \\int_0^L \\exp[-(\\epsilon-i \\Omega)s] \\\\\n&\\quad\\sin(\\omega s)/\\omega\\, ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $\\omega=0$ use the continuous value $\\sin(\\omega s)/\\omega=s$. The infinite-window response is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\chi_v(\\Omega+i \\epsilon,k) \\\\\n&=c^{2}/[\\omega^{2}-(\\Omega+i \\epsilon)^{2}].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7.1}\n$$\n\nThe tail is uniformly bounded for every $\\omega\\ge 0$ by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|q(0)/j_0-\\chi_v| \\\\\n&\\le c^{2} \\exp(-\\epsilon L)[L/\\epsilon+1/\\epsilon^{2}].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7.2}\n$$\n\nIndeed $|\\sin(\\omega s)/\\omega|\\le s$ and the remaining exponential integral is exact. Thus any desired absolute susceptibility accuracy is attained by an explicitly finite window chosen from (7.2). No unbounded-time pole observation is assumed to be an executed experiment.\n\nA classical source term linear in a free quantum field creates a coherent displacement whose mean satisfies this same retarded equation exactly. Measuring its two field quadratures therefore supplies the declared observable response; its normalization is fixed by the source coupling in (3.1), not fitted to a desired residue. Equation (7.1) has the same canonical inverse-operator coefficient and mass pole as (3.3), with the retarded sign convention accounted for. The independently predicted intercept $|v|^2/R^2$ can be compared at any admitted off-pole frequencies. A classical drive profile and mean-field amplitude transform together under $T(a)J(y)=J(y+a)$ and $T(g)J(y)=J(g^{-1}y)$. These are obtained by conjugating the linear source Hamiltonian and changing its Haar integration variable, so the driven-response square uses the same amplitude convention as the particle map. A labeled source uses $e_{v}$ and the independently implemented translation label readout of Section 6, so equal masses or opposite labels are not merged by this comparison.\n\nThe observation also has a finite statistical record. Use a normalized periodic-box spatial mode with a nonzero physical wavenumber $k$, set $\\kappa_0=\\sqrt{|k|^2+|v|^2/R^2}>0$, and choose the drive phase so $j_0>0$ is real. Canonical quantization gives its complex coordinate $q=(a+b^*)/\\sqrt{2\\kappa_0}$, with independent particle and antiparticle oscillators. In the coherently driven vacuum, $\\operatorname{Var}(\\operatorname{Re}q)=\\operatorname{Var}(\\operatorname{Im}q)=1/(4\\kappa_0)$. These two position quadratures commute; the statement does not assert joint sharp access to a position and its conjugate momentum. For $n$ independent repetitions, the sample susceptibility has\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb{E} |\\hat{\\chi}-\\mathbb{E} \\hat{\\chi}|^{2}&=1/(2\\kappa_0 n j_0^{2}) \\\\\n&\\le 1/(2|k| n j_0^{2}).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently the probability of a statistical error greater than $\\delta$ is at most $1/(2|k|\\,nj_0^2\\delta^2)$. Choosing $n$ at least the ceiling of that expression without $n$, divided by the desired failure probability, gives a finite protocol with total complex-response error at most $\\delta$ plus (7.2). A separate canonical source preparation supplies each repetition. Its finite coherent amplitudes $\\alpha,\\beta$ have excitation energy $\\hbar c\\,\\kappa_0(|\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2)$, charged to that repetition. The time-Hamiltonian source is $-\\hbar c(j^*q+q^*j)$. Its exact oscillator displacement integral gives $|\\alpha|,|\\beta|\\le cj_0(1-\\exp(-\\epsilon L))/(\\sqrt{2\\kappa_0}\\,\\epsilon)$, and hence an excitation-energy bound $\\hbar c^3j_0^2/\\epsilon^2$ per repetition. Label readout on a one-particle preparation and susceptibility readout on a coherent preparation are separate registered protocols of the same action; they are not falsely identified as the same state or as identical measurement statistics.\n\nThe observed pole frequency is a test of (5.3), not an input to it. Source normalization, physical clock, momentum convention, finite-window tail, shot count and owned preparation energy stay in the same comparison record.\n\n### 8. A populated finite marked class\n\nUse the explicit coordinate Leech construction in Appendix R. Let C be a marked doubly even self-dual binary [24,12,8] code, let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nK_C&=\\{x \\in \\mathbb{Z}^{24}: x \\bmod 2 \\in C,\\ \\sum_i x_i=0 \\bmod 4\\}, \\\\\nL_B&=K_C/\\sqrt{2}, \\\\\n\\nu&=(-3,1,\\ldots,1)/\\sqrt{8}, \\\\\n\\Lambda&=L_B \\cup (\\nu+L_B).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts accepted coordinate proof gives an even unimodular rootless marked lattice. The fresh model may choose C by the twelve shifted binary polynomial rows with support {0,1,5,6,7,9,11}, extended by their parity coordinate. The complete finite code record has rank 12, self-orthogonality, minimum distance8 and weights(1,759,2576,759,1); these are the stated marked extended-Golay data.\n\nChoose\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na&=2(e_1+e_2)/\\sqrt{2}, \\\\\nb&=2(e_1-e_2)/\\sqrt{2}, \\\\\nd&=2(e_2-e_3)/\\sqrt{2}, \\\\\nV&=\\{0,a,-a,b,\\nu,\\nu+a,\\nu+d\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll three a,b,d belong to $L_{B}$ because their integer numerators are even and their sums are divisible by four. The glue vectors therefore belong to $\\Lambda$. Their squared norms in the displayed order are\n\n$$\n0,4,4,4,4,6,8.\n$$\n\nThe seven marked vectors are distinct. This populated class contains opposite vectors, four distinct norm-four states, an irrational norm $\\sqrt{6}$ and a distinct norm $\\sqrt{8}$. It exercises exactly the distinctions that a shell-only or opposite-pair quotient would lose. The calibrator primitive vector is $\\lambda^*=a$, using $\\Lambda^*=\\Lambda$ with the retained Euclidean marking.\n\nThe fully specified observation uses a periodic physical box of side $2\\pi$ in the fixed length unit, $k=(1,0,0)$, $\\Omega=1$, $j_0=1$, $\\epsilon=1$, $L=10$, $\\delta=1/20$ and $n=20000$ independent repetitions per label. Its per-label statistical failure bound is 1/100. The deterministic tail is $99\\exp(-10)<1/200$, so total complex susceptibility error is at most 11/200 with probability at least 99/100, for that tested frequency. The theorem permits any finite registered frequency list, charging the corresponding independent preparations and union-bound budget. The inequality follows without floating-point assumptions from a finite rational Taylor lower bound $\\exp(10)>19800$. To require this confidence simultaneously for all seven labels, take 140000 repetitions per label and use the union bound. The excitation-energy bound is 54 per repetition; all preparation and retained data registers are owned. The one-particle preparation has $\\gamma=1$ and duration $\\pi/2$; translation controls have $t_{c}=1$. These are admitted fixed controls, with no finite register claimed to encode an exact continuum of adjustable programs.\n\nA lattice basis can be constructed effectively without guessing: generate $\\sqrt{8}\\Lambda$ over $\\mathbb{Z}$ by $4(e_i-e_{24})$, $8e_{24}$ for the $2D_{24}$ parity sublattice after rescaling, twice the marked Golay rows, and the glue numerator $(-3,1,\\dots,1)$, then take an exact integer Hermite basis. The finite record retains the precise generators, determinant, Gram integrality/unimodularity, membership coordinates for all seven vectors and a sufficient label-readout $N$. The generator normalization is verified directly against $2K_C$ and the glue vector, rather than inferred from a determinant alone.\n\n∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q05b-numerical-cross-check-of-separate-record-and-leech-branches\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.5b (Numerical Cross-Check of Separate Record and Leech Branches).** On the structural record/capacity/no-surplus branch, $a=2$ and hence $a^2=4$. On a separately registered Leech gluing/rootlessness branch, $|v|_{\\min}^2=4$. Therefore\n$$\na^2=|v|_{\\min}^2=4\n$$\non the intersection of those branches.\n\n*Proof.* Each equality follows from its own branch theorem. Their common numerical value supplies no implication from the record dimension to the lattice, no gluing construction, and no energy identification. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q05c-conditional-discrete-mass-spectrum\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.5c (Conditional Discrete Mass Spectrum).** On the full branch of Theorem Q.0.5a,\n$$\nm^2\n\\in\n\\{0,4,6,8,10,12,\\ldots\\}\n(\\mu_0^{\\mathrm{alg}})^2.\n$$\nThe first nonzero mass is $2\\mu_0^{\\mathrm{alg}}$.\n\n*Proof.* The registered Leech branch gives\n$$\n|v|^2\\in\\{0,4,6,8,10,12,\\ldots\\}.\n$$\nTheorem Q.0.5a gives $m^2(v)=(\\mu_0^{\\mathrm{alg}})^2|v|^2$. Substitution proves the set, and the minimum nonzero norm $4$ gives $m_{\\min}=2\\mu_0^{\\mathrm{alg}}$. This is a physical gap only on $\\mathfrak B_{mass}$ with the canonical norm-information calibration. ∎\n\n---\n\n#### Q.0.8.4 The Conditional Count-Character Correspondence\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q06-conditional-count-character-representation\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.6 (Conditional Count-Character Representation).** On the branch $\\mathcal S=N\\hbar\\ln2$,\n$$\ne^{i\\mathcal S/\\hbar}=e^{iN\\ln2}=2^{iN}.\n$$\nThis is a substitution identity under the stated calibration, not a derivation of quantum mechanics from thermodynamics. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q06a-selected-character-phase-matching\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.6a (Selected-Character Phase Matching).** Two selected-character values agree exactly when\n$$\n(N_1-N_2)\\ln2=2\\pi k,\n\\qquad k\\in\\mathbb Z.\n$$\nIrrationality implies $N_1=N_2$. Approximate matches are governed by the continued-fraction statements above. A physical interference claim additionally requires the phase-identification branch. ∎\n\n#### Q.0.8.5 Observable Consequences\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q07-integer-counts-and-separate-discrete-labels\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.7 (Integer Counts and Separate Discrete Labels).**\n\n1. If $N$ is the number of elementary cycles in a finite discrete MPU process, then $N\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}$ by definition.\n2. Winding and instanton numbers are integer topological labels. Equality or proportionality between such a label and $N$ requires a separately stated holonomy/count calibration.\n3. On a registered Leech branch, squared norms are discrete even integers beginning $4,6,8,\\ldots$. They are not restricted to $4\\mathbb Z$, and no Leech shell index is identified with $N$ without an additional bridge.\n\n*Proof.* Item 1 is the definition of a finite count. Item 2 follows from the integrality of the relevant homotopy or characteristic class, while the logical independence of two integer-valued quantities precludes identifying them without a map. Item 3 follows from Proposition Q.0.5's $\\theta$ series, whose nonzero exponents include $6$. ∎\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q08-conditional-structural-consequences\"></a>**Proposition Q.0.8 (Conditional Structural Consequences).**\n\n1. Leech-lattice shell norms are discrete on the registered Leech branch.\n2. The character $e^{iN\\ln2}$ assigns a phase increment $\\ln2$ per count by definition; a physical phase interpretation requires Convention Q.0.6a.0.\n3. One stipulated binary structural step contributes $\\kappa_A\\ln2$ to the calibrated action ledger. This is not a universal minimum action, and $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$ is an independent calibration.\n4. Mass statements require the complete branch of Theorem Q.0.5a.\n\nNo process is excluded merely because a mechanical action is smaller than $\\hbar\\ln2$. ∎\n\n<a id=\"remark-q08a-experimental-accessibility\"></a>**Remark Q.0.8a: Experimental Accessibility.**\n\n(i) Precision tests of quantum coherence at mesoscopic scales where $N$ is moderately small\n\n(ii) Cosmological signatures where Planck-scale physics imprints on large-scale structure\n\n(iii) Derived consequences at accessible scales—particle mass ratios, coupling constants, and symmetry structures (Sections 13, G.8.4, Appendix T)\n\n<a id=\"remark-q08b-action-scale-status\"></a>**Remark Q.0.8b (Action-Scale Status).** The candidate value $\\kappa_A\\ln2$ exists only on a calibrated one-step structural-action branch. Choosing $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$ gives $\\hbar\\ln2$ by definition.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q08c-conditional-exact-discrimination-relative-energy-bound\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.8c (Conditional Exact-Discrimination Relative-Energy Bound).** Let a registered two-hypothesis protocol $\\mathcal P$ admit an interaction picture in which the output under the mass hypothesis is\n$$\n|\\psi_m(T_{\\mathcal P})\\rangle\n=\ne^{-iH_{\\mathrm{rel}}(m)T_{\\mathcal P}/\\hbar}|\\psi\\rangle,\n$$\nwhile the zero-mass output is $|\\psi\\rangle$. Assume $H_{\\mathrm{rel}}(m)$ is time independent and bounded below on the retained probe support, and that its ground-subtracted mean is\n$$\nE_{\\mathrm{rel}}(m)\n:=\n\\langle\\psi|H_{\\mathrm{rel}}(m)|\\psi\\rangle\n-\n\\inf\\operatorname{spec}H_{\\mathrm{rel}}(m)\n=\n\\kappa_{\\mathcal P}mc^2,\n\\qquad\n\\kappa_{\\mathcal P}>0.\n\\tag{Q.0.8c.1}\n$$\nIf $\\mathcal P$ achieves exact one-shot discrimination by making the two output rays orthogonal within duration $T_{\\mathcal P}$, then\n$$\nm\n\\ge\n\\frac{\\pi\\hbar}{2\\kappa_{\\mathcal P}c^2T_{\\mathcal P}}.\n\\tag{Q.0.8c.2}\n$$\n\n*Proof.* The two outputs are orthogonal exactly when the registered relative evolution orthogonalizes $|\\psi\\rangle$. The Margolus--Levitin inequality applied to $H_{\\mathrm{rel}}(m)$ gives $T_{\\mathcal P}\\ge\\pi\\hbar/(2E_{\\mathrm{rel}}(m))$. Substitution of (Q.0.8c.1) yields (Q.0.8c.2). ∎\n\n<a id=\"remark-q08d-conditional-protocol-mass-scale\"></a>**Remark Q.0.8d (Conditional Protocol Mass Scale).** Equation Q.18 determines the spatial length $\\delta=\\sqrt{8\\ln2}\\,L_P$ on its residual-budget, throughput-saturated, ideal-packing branch; it does not determine a protocol duration. If the serialized-frontier calibration of Proposition Q.6.1 is also imposed so that $T_{\\mathcal P}=\\delta/c$, Theorem Q.0.8c gives\n$$\nm\\ge \\frac{\\pi}{2\\kappa_{\\mathcal P}\\sqrt{8\\ln2}}\\,m_P.\n$$\nThe numerical value $m\\ge0.667\\,m_P$ follows on the additional response calibration $\\kappa_{\\mathcal P}=1$. A universal mass gap would further require a theorem that this calibrated discrimination protocol is physically realized and that every massive excitation is subject to its one-shot orthogonalization bound.\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q09-zero-additional-phase-bohr-sommerfeld-branch\"></a>**Proposition Q.0.9 (Zero-Additional-Phase Bohr--Sommerfeld Branch).** Assume a closed canonical orbit $\\gamma$ has action $\\oint_\\gamma p\\,dq$, and assume the transport law has no additional Maslov, Berry, boundary, or spin phase. Imposing trivial total transport holonomy then gives\n$$\n\\oint_\\gamma p\\,dq=2\\pi k\\hbar=kh,\n\\qquad k\\in\\mathbb Z.\n$$\nIf a dimensionless additional phase $\\Phi_{\\mathrm{add}}$ is present with the displayed sign convention, the applicable condition is\n$$\n\\frac1\\hbar\\oint_\\gamma p\\,dq+\\Phi_{\\mathrm{add}}=2\\pi k.\n$$\nInteger event counting does not supply either canonical-action identification. ∎\n<a id=\"remark-q09a-quantization-scope\"></a>**Remark Q.0.9a (Quantization Scope).** Proposition Q.0.9 is the ordinary trivial-holonomy condition under explicit canonical-action and phase hypotheses.\n\n---\n\n#### Q.0.8.7 Summary\n\nThis section keeps event counting, action calibration, geometric shell values, mass interpretation, and orbital quantization as separate results. They combine only on the stated calibration branches.\n\n##### Technical event-and-action results ledger\n\nThis section has established:\n\n1. **Discrete Integrality:** $N \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ at the fundamental (MPU network) level (Proposition Q.0.3)\n\n2. **Conditional Count-Character Identity:** On the separately calibrated branch $\\mathcal S=N\\hbar\\ln2$, $e^{i\\mathcal S/\\hbar}=2^{iN}$ (Corollary Q.0.6)\n\n3. **Topological Constraints:** The action one-form obeys Theorem Q.0.4 only under its trivial-total-holonomy hypothesis\n\n4. **Vacuum Shell and Conditional Mass Structure:** Leech geometry fixes $|v|^2\\in\\{4,6,8,\\ldots\\}$; $m^2=(\\mu_0^{\\mathrm{alg}})^2|v|^2$ additionally requires Theorem Q.0.5a's $\\mathfrak B_{mass}$ and canonical-calibration branch\n\n5. **Conditional Bohr--Sommerfeld Identity:** Under the canonical-action, trivial-total-holonomy, and zero-additional-phase hypotheses, $\\oint p\\,dq=kh$ (Proposition Q.0.9)\n\n6. **Conditional Consequences:** Discrete shell norms and calibrated ledger phases/actions have the scope stated in Proposition Q.0.8\n\nAny correspondence between update-count characters and physical interference is conditional on the independent calibration and phase-identification ledger.\n\n---\n\n### <a id=\"q09-the-conditional-rindler-landauer-timescale\"></a>Q.0.9 The Conditional Rindler--Landauer Timescale\n\n#### Q.0.9.1 Independent Ingredients\n\nThe Unruh temperature is\n$$\nT_U(a)=\\frac{\\hbar a}{2\\pi k_Bc}.\n$$\nOn a registered uniformly accelerated reset branch with constant acceleration magnitude $a>0$ and a reservoir at the displayed Unruh temperature, impose the complete Definition 28 contract at $T_U(a)$. A conditionally uniform binary record then satisfies the ensemble-mean Landauer bound $\\langle Q_{\\mathrm{bath}}\\rangle\\ge k_BT_U\\ln2$. This is not a heat floor for every individual realization and supplies no cycle duration. Introduce, as a separate calibration hypothesis with $\\kappa_A>0$, a protocol action-duration ledger\n$$\nE\\tau=\\kappa_A\\ln2.\n$$\n\n#### Q.0.9.2 The Conditional Timescale\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q010-conditional-rindler-landauer-timescale\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.10 (Conditional Rindler--Landauer Timescale).** Assume the complete registered reset branch just stated, exact ensemble-mean Landauer heat saturation, a separately certified cyclic mean-energy balance $E=\\langle Q_{\\mathrm{bath}}\\rangle$, and the independent action-duration equality $E\\tau=\\kappa_A\\ln2$. Then\n$$\n\\tau_U(a)=\\frac{\\kappa_A}{\\hbar}\\frac{2\\pi c}{a}.\n$$\nIndeed, substitution of $E=k_BT_U(a)\\ln2$ and $T_U(a)=\\hbar a/(2\\pi k_Bc)$ into the action-duration equality gives the formula. On the separately calibrated branch $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$, this becomes $\\tau_U=2\\pi c/a$. The equality is conditional and is not a thermodynamic speed limit; exact quasistatic saturation is ordinarily an asymptotic ideal. ∎\n\n<a id=\"remark-q010a-no-energy-time-monotonicity-without-a-protocol-law\"></a>**Remark Q.0.10a (No Energy--Time Monotonicity without a Protocol Law).** Increasing dissipated energy does not by itself imply a shorter duration. The relation $\\tau=\\kappa_A\\ln2/E$ holds only while the independent action-duration equality remains valid for the changed protocol.\n\n#### Q.0.9.3 Physical Interpretation\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q010b-conditional-geometric-comparison\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.10b (Conditional Geometric Comparison).** On the full hypotheses of Theorem Q.0.10 with $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$, the numerical timescale $2\\pi c/a$ equals $2\\pi$ times the light-crossing time to the Rindler horizon. The KMS period $2\\pi c/a$ is an imaginary-time periodicity; it does not by itself establish a real reset-cycle duration. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q010c-conditional-rate\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.10c (Conditional Rate).** On the calibrated branch $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$, if a real protocol independently realizes the duration in Theorem Q.0.10, its event rate is $a/(2\\pi c)$. No rate follows from the Unruh temperature alone. ∎\n\n<a id=\"remark-q010d-no-universal-speed-or-efficiency-law\"></a>**Remark Q.0.10d (No Universal Speed or Efficiency Law).** Extra energy need not shorten a protocol. Any energy--duration relation requires a separate speed-limit or control theorem.\n\n<a id=\"definition-q010d1-margolus-levitin-saturation-certificate\"></a>**Definition Q.0.10d.1 (Margolus-Levitin Saturation Certificate).** A Margolus-Levitin saturation certificate for a finite-response branch is a finite record\n$$\n\\mathfrak Q_{\\mathrm{ML}}\n=\n(H,\\rho,\\mathcal C_\\perp,E,\\dot C_\\perp,\\mathcal O_\\perp,\\chi_{\\mathrm{ML}})\n\\tag{Q.0.10d.1}\n$$\nwhere:\n\n1. $H$ is the branch Hamiltonian with the ground energy subtracted and with the retained spectral domain fixed.\n2. $\\rho$ is the finite-response state or state family on which the rate is evaluated.\n3. $E=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho H)$ is fixed before comparison.\n4. $\\mathcal C_\\perp$ is a counted orthogonalization-event coordinate in the retained protocol units; it is not the static predictive complexity $C_P$.\n5. $\\dot C_\\perp$ is the corresponding event rate.\n6. $\\mathcal O_\\perp$ proves that the counted events are realized by orthogonalizing two-level geodesic components using the energy recorded in $E$, with no response-relevant idle sector included in the numerator.\n7. $\\chi_{\\mathrm{ML}}=1$ records that the state, Hamiltonian, event coordinate, energy normalization, and comparison window were fixed before any rate comparison.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q010d2-conditional-margolus-levitin-rate-equality\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.10d.2 (Conditional Margolus-Levitin Rate Equality).** Let a registered comparison window have duration $T>0$, a finite count $N_\\perp$ of distinct realized orthogonalization events, and registered mean-energy budget $E\\ge0$. Define the window-averaged rate $\\dot C_\\perp:=N_\\perp/T$. For every counted event $j$, require an autonomous time-independent retained Hamiltonian and state satisfying the complete Margolus--Levitin hypotheses, an event duration $\\tau_j>0$, and finite mean energy $E_j$ above that Hamiltonian's ground energy. In addition, require a common-window energy-ownership certificate proving\n$$\n\\sum_{j=1}^{N_\\perp}E_j\\tau_j\\le ET,\n$$\nwith each counted event and energy contribution assigned once. This certificate must justify any sequential or parallel composition used in the count. Then\n$$\n\\dot C_\\perp\\le \\frac{2E}{\\pi\\hbar}.\n\\tag{Q.0.10d.2}\n$$\nFor a positive event count, equality requires an accepted certificate $\\mathfrak Q_{\\mathrm{ML}}$ whose entry $\\mathcal O_\\perp$ proves saturation for every counted event and equality in the common-window energy budget. PCE no-surplus selection alone does not imply these equalities, autonomous positive complexity drift from $E>0$, or an identification of the Action-Entropy Identity with any Complexity=Action dictionary without an additional finite-response reconstruction map.\n\n*Proof.* For each counted event, Margolus--Levitin gives $E_j\\tau_j\\ge\\pi\\hbar/2$. Finite summation and the energy-ownership premise give\n$$\nN_\\perp\\frac{\\pi\\hbar}{2}\n\\le\\sum_jE_j\\tau_j\\le ET.\n$$\nDivision by $T>0$ proves the window-averaged rate bound. For $N_\\perp>0$, equality holds only if each nonnegative eventwise deficit vanishes and the energy budget is exhausted; these are the required certificate entries. If $E=0$, the same inequality forces $N_\\perp=0$. A single-event duration bound alone supplies neither a count of distinct events nor their common energy/time allocation, and no instantaneous event-rate bound is asserted. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q010d3-no-common-cycle-rindler-action-and-margolus-levitin-saturation\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.10d.3 (No Common-Cycle Rindler-Action and Margolus--Levitin Saturation).** Let the same finite cycle use the same mean energy $E>0$ and duration $\\tau>0$ in both the action-duration ledger\n$$\nE\\tau=\\kappa_A\\ln2\n\\tag{Q.0.10d.3.1}\n$$\nand an orthogonalizing event subject to the Margolus--Levitin bound. Then necessarily\n$$\n\\kappa_A\\ln2\\ge\\frac{\\pi\\hbar}{2}.\n\\tag{Q.0.10d.3.2}\n$$\nConsequently the calibrated branch $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$ of Theorem Q.0.10 cannot carry a Margolus--Levitin-saturating event on that same energy and duration, because $\\ln2<1<\\pi/2$. Simultaneous equality would instead require\n$$\n\\kappa_A=\\frac{\\pi\\hbar}{2\\ln2}.\n\\tag{Q.0.10d.3.3}\n$$\n\nBoth component classes are nonempty. The two-level Hamiltonian $H=2E|1\\rangle\\langle1|$ and state $(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)/\\sqrt2$ have mean energy $E$ and reach the orthogonal state at $\\pi\\hbar/(2E)$, saturating Margolus--Levitin. Separately, positive numbers $(E,\\tau)$ satisfying (Q.0.10d.3.1) exist for every $E,\\kappa_A>0$; they do not orthogonalize on the calibrated $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$ branch.\n\n*Proof.* Margolus--Levitin gives $E\\tau\\ge\\pi\\hbar/2$. Substitution of (Q.0.10d.3.1) proves (Q.0.10d.3.2), and equality gives (Q.0.10d.3.3). The strict calibrated contradiction follows from $\\ln2<1$ and $\\pi>2$. For the displayed qubit,\n$$\ne^{-iH\\tau/\\hbar}\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}\n=\\frac{|0\\rangle-|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}\n$$\nat $\\tau=\\pi\\hbar/(2E)$, proving nonvacuity. ∎\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q010d4-action-scale-threshold-and-calibrated-overlap-floor-for-common-cycle-events\"></a>**Proposition Q.0.10d.4 (Action-Scale Threshold and Calibrated Overlap Floor for Common-Cycle Events).** Let a finite cycle carry mean energy $E>0$ and duration $\\tau>0$ obeying (Q.0.10d.3.1), and let an event on the same cycle be a pure state $\\psi$ evolving for the duration $\\tau$ under a time-independent Hamiltonian $H\\ge0$ with $\\inf\\operatorname{spec}H=0$ and $\\langle\\psi|H|\\psi\\rangle=E$. Then:\n\n1. An exactly orthogonalizing event, $\\langle\\psi|e^{-iH\\tau/\\hbar}\\psi\\rangle=0$, exists on the cycle if and only if $\\kappa_A\\ln2\\ge\\pi\\hbar/2$. It can be realized on a three-level carrier, and it saturates the Margolus--Levitin bound exactly when $\\kappa_A=\\pi\\hbar/(2\\ln2)$.\n2. Every such event satisfies\n   $$\n   \\bigl|\\langle\\psi|e^{-iH\\tau/\\hbar}\\psi\\rangle\\bigr|\n   \\ge\\frac{1-2\\kappa_A\\ln2/(\\pi\\hbar)}{\\sqrt{1+4/\\pi^2}}.\n   \\tag{Q.0.10d.4.1}\n   $$\n   On the calibrated branch $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$ the right side is $(1-2\\ln2/\\pi)/\\sqrt{1+4/\\pi^2}=0.47132\\ldots$, so every event on the calibrated common cycle keeps fidelity at least $0.2221$ with its initial state.\n\n*Proof.* Necessity in item 1 is Theorem Q.0.10d.3. For sufficiency let $\\kappa_A\\ln2\\ge\\pi\\hbar/2$, so that $E\\ge\\pi\\hbar/(2\\tau)$. On $\\operatorname{span}\\{|g\\rangle,|a\\rangle,|b\\rangle\\}$ take $H=E_a|a\\rangle\\!\\langle a|+E_b|b\\rangle\\!\\langle b|$ with $E_a:=E-\\pi\\hbar/(2\\tau)\\ge0$, $E_b:=E_a+\\pi\\hbar/\\tau$, and $\\psi=(|a\\rangle+|b\\rangle)/\\sqrt2$. The ground energy is $0$, the mean energy is $(E_a+E_b)/2=E$, and $\\langle\\psi|e^{-iH\\tau/\\hbar}\\psi\\rangle=\\frac12e^{-iE_a\\tau/\\hbar}(1+e^{-i\\pi})=0$. Equality $E\\tau=\\pi\\hbar/2$ is Margolus--Levitin saturation and corresponds to $\\kappa_A=\\pi\\hbar/(2\\ln2)$. For item 2, let $\\mu$ be the spectral measure of $H$ in $\\psi$ and $S:=\\int e^{-iu\\tau/\\hbar}d\\mu(u)$. The inequality $\\cos x\\ge1-\\frac2\\pi(x+\\sin x)$ for $x\\ge0$ holds because $q(x):=\\frac2\\pi(x+\\sin x)-1+\\cos x$ vanishes at $0$ and $\\pi$, has derivative $2\\cos(x/2)\\bigl[\\frac2\\pi\\cos(x/2)-\\sin(x/2)\\bigr]$, which is positive and then negative on $[0,\\pi]$, and satisfies $q\\ge0$ on $[\\pi,\\infty)$ because $x+\\sin x\\ge\\pi$ there. Integrating it with $x=u\\tau/\\hbar$ gives $\\operatorname{Re}S-\\frac2\\pi\\operatorname{Im}S\\ge1-\\frac{2E\\tau}{\\pi\\hbar}$, and Cauchy--Schwarz bounds the left side by $|S|\\sqrt{1+4/\\pi^2}$. Substituting $E\\tau=\\kappa_A\\ln2$ proves (Q.0.10d.4.1), and $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$ gives the numerical values. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q010d5-a-complete-supplied-temperature-detector-and-retained-record-reset-cycle\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.10d.5 (A Complete Supplied-Temperature Detector and Retained-Record Reset Cycle).**\n\nThe following finite Gibbs-collision source and growing-resource ideal supply the conditional Rindler–Landauer detector/reset existence branch. The reservoir temperature, proper-time geometry and action calibration remain independently supplied. The supporting reset-only lemma is used through the complete-cycle construction; its earlier composition residual is answered by that construction. Net work and total autonomous Hamiltonian energy retain their separate ledgers.\n\n## A finite autonomous thermal reset, asymptotically pure work, and a real cyclic clock\n\n### Result\n\nFor any independently fixed temperature $T>0$ and phase constant $\\hbar$, there is a sequence of finite, nonnegative, time-independent Hamiltonians which, in the fixed real duration $\\tau=\\hbar/(k_BT)$:\n\n1. resets an initially uniform, degenerate memory bit with error at most $\\exp(-n)$;\n2. deposits mean heat $Q_n\\to k_BT\\ln 2$ into explicitly supplied, initially independent Gibbs cells;\n3. debits exactly $Q_{n}$ from a finite battery;\n4. returns an independently supplied two-level clock exactly to its initial pure state, without clock correlations or a readout/reset operation;\n5. makes the battery's entropy and its correlations with the rest tend to zero, with explicit bounds;\n6. obeys $Q_n\\tau\\to\\hbar\\ln 2$ on the independently calibrated $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$ branch.\n\nThe clock period is proved from its real Hamiltonian evolution. It is not inferred from a KMS imaginary period. The temperature-to-clock-gap ratio is an explicitly designed and calibrated parameter, not a universal consequence of thermality. The bare battery mean energy diverges as n⁶; it belongs in the total ML energy. There is no claim of ML saturation or of equality between total Hamiltonian energy and battery debit.\n\n### 1. Boundary Hamiltonians and initial state\n\nWrite $\\Theta=k_BT$, $\\beta=1/\\Theta$. For integer $n\\ge 2$ set\n\n$$\nN=n^3,\\quad \\delta=\\Theta/n^2,\\quad L=n^8,\\quad d_W=L+4N.\n$$\n\nThe logical memory M has $H_{M}=0$. Supply N bath qubits, with\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_B &= \\delta \\sum_{j=1}^N j\\, |1\\rangle\\langle 1|_{B_j}, \\\\\n\\gamma_B &= \\bigotimes_{j=1}^N \\operatorname{diag}(1-g_j,g_j), \\\\\ng_j &= 1/(1+\\exp(j/n^2)).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{1}\n$$\n\nThus $\\gamma_{B}$ is exactly Gibbs for the displayed, fixed, bare $H_{B}$. Every cell has a named initial and spent owner; none is reused or regenerated for free.\n\nThe finite work battery has\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_W &= \\delta \\sum_{k=0}^{d_W-1} k\\, |k\\rangle\\langle k|, \\\\\n|\\eta\\rangle &= L^{-1/2} \\sum_{k=2N}^{2N+L-1}|k\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n$$\n\nInitially M is I/2, independently of $\\gamma_{B}$ and $\\eta$. The clock will be added below and is also independent. All entropies and energies are finite for every n. The battery's initial mean energy is\n\n$$\nE_{W,\\mathrm{in}} = \\Theta[2n + n^6/2 - 1/(2n^2)].\n\\tag{3}\n$$\n\nIt is an explicitly supplied coherent, nonthermal resource, not a free ready state.\n\nThe claim of saturation at $\\ln 2$ uses a uniform record with no informative retained classical side record: $R$ is absent or independent of $M$. The arbitrary-reference channel statements below do not imply conditional Landauer saturation when another retained record contains a copy. In that case the displayed reset still has its actual heat, but $H(M|R)$ can be zero.\n\n### 2. Exact energy-conserving finite lift\n\nFor a computational basis word $x=(m,b_1,\\dots,b_N)$, let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVx&=(b_N,m,b_1,\\dots,b_{N-1}), \\\\\ns(x)&=m+\\sum_{j=1}^{N-1}b_j-N b_N.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4}\n$$\n\nThen $|s(x)|\\le N$ and the bath energy change under this cyclic permutation is $\\delta s(x)$. On the initial battery support define the basis map\n\n$$\n\\lvert x,k\\rangle\\mapsto\\lvert Vx,k-s(x)\\rangle,\\qquad 2N\\le k\\le 2N+L-1.\n\\tag{5}\n$$\n\nEvery output battery index lies between $N$ and $L+3N-1$, strictly within the finite ladder. The map is injective and preserves the eigenvalue of $H_0:=H_B+H_W$. For each finite $H_{0}$ eigenspace, its specified domain subset and image subset have the same cardinality. Match the unused domain and image basis vectors in lexicographic order within that eigenspace. This extends (5) to an explicit permutation unitary $U_{n}$ with\n\n$$\n[U_n,H_0]=0.\n\\tag{6}\n$$\n\nThe zero-energy eigenspace does not meet the specified domain or image because all their battery indices are positive; choose identity there. This makes a literal zero-energy vector available for the Hamiltonian below. The finite extension algorithm is part of the construction. No cyclic wraparound, negative battery level, infinite ladder or unspecified reservoir is used.\n\n#### Exact output and heat\n\nLet $p_{x}$ be the initial product classical probabilities on MB. The complete output is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\sum_x p_x |Vx\\rangle\\langle Vx| \\otimes |\\eta_{s(x)}\\rangle\\langle \\eta_{s(x)}|, \\\\\n&|\\eta_s\\rangle=L^{-1/2}\\sum_{k=2N}^{2N+L-1}|k-s\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7}\n$$\n\nHence $M$ has output $\\operatorname{diag}(1-g_N,g_N)$, independent of the other classical output coordinates, and\n\n$$\nQ_n = \\Delta\\langle H_B\\rangle = \\delta \\mathbb{E}_p[s(x)] = -\\Delta\\langle H_W\\rangle.\n\\tag{8}\n$$\n\nThe same memory replacement works for arbitrary reference-correlated inputs, with the initial memory/reference correlations transferred to the spent bath/battery; the heat value (8) is for the registered uniform marginal. In particular the reduced memory channel is replacement by $\\operatorname{diag}(1-g_N,g_N)$, so its unhalved diamond distance from pure reset is $2g_N\\le 2e^{-n}$.\n\nFor the nominal input, put $g_{0}=1/2$ and $x_j=j/n^2$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\beta Q_n &= \\sum_{j=1}^N x_j(g_{j-1}-g_j) \\\\\n&= \\ln 2-h(g_N)+\\sum_{j=1}^N d(g_{j-1}\\Vert g_j),\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{9}\n$$\n\nwhere h and d are binary Shannon entropy and relative entropy in nats. The same elementary log-partition Taylor argument as the accepted M10 reset construction gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0&<\\sum_j d(g_{j-1}\\Vert g_j)\\le 1/(8n), \\\\\nh(g_N)&\\le (n+2)e^{-n}, \\\\\n|\\beta Q_n-\\ln 2|&\\le (n+2)e^{-n}+1/(8n).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{10}\n$$\n\nFor completeness, with $f(x)=\\ln(1+e^{-x})$, $f''(x)=g(x)(1-g(x))\\le 1/4$. Every Bregman remainder is at most $(x_j-x_{j-1})^2/8$. Summing $N$ increments of size $1/n^2$ gives $1/(8n)$. Identity (9) follows by expanding the two Bernoulli relative entropies and telescoping their entropy difference. The final entropy bound follows from $g_N\\le e^{-n}$.\n\nThe stochastic bare work has finite variance\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathrm{Var}(\\delta s)&=\\delta^2[1/4+\\sum_{j=1}^{N-1}g_j(1-g_j)+N^2g_N(1-g_N)] \\\\\n&\\le \\Theta^2[1/(4n)+n^2e^{-n}] \\to 0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{11}\n$$\n\nThus this is also an explicit concentrating-work sequence, not just a mean energy identity.\n\n### 3. Battery entropy and correlations, with dimension-aware convergence\n\nThe translated battery packets satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\langle \\eta|\\eta_s\\rangle&=1-|s|/L, \\\\\n(1/2)\\lVert\\, |\\eta_s\\rangle\\langle \\eta_s|-|\\eta\\rangle\\langle \\eta|\\, \\rVert_1 \\\\\n&\\le \\sqrt{2|s|/L} \\le t_n:=\\sqrt{2N/L}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{12}\n$$\n\nSince the MB blocks in (7) are orthogonal,\n\n$$\n(1/2)\\lVert \\rho_{MB,W'}-(V\\rho_{MB} V^{\\dagger})\\otimes|\\eta\\rangle\\langle \\eta|\\rVert_1\\le t_n.\n\\tag{13}\n$$\n\nThis controls the *complete output*, not just the battery marginal. For $n\\ge 2$, $t_n<1-1/d_W$, and the finite-dimensional entropy continuity bound yields\n\n$$\nS(\\rho_{W'})\\le h(t_n)+t_n \\ln(d_W-1)=:b_n\\to 0.\n\\tag{14}\n$$\n\nThe output (7) is classical on MB with pure conditional battery states, so exactly\n\n$$\nI(W:MB)_{\\rho'}=S(\\rho_{W'})\\le b_n.\n\\tag{15}\n$$\n\nThe memory/reference extension need not remain classical, but $I(W:MBR)\\le 2S(W)$ still bounds its correlations whenever the same marginal estimate applies. The actual Gibbs/mean-heat calculation uses the nominated uniform classical branch.\n\nThe limit therefore has vanishing battery entropy increase and vanishing battery correlations, while the mean energy debit tends to $\\Theta\\ln 2$. This is a justified ideal-work-source *limit*, not an exact ideal work source at finite $n$. Trace convergence to the initial packet is not used to infer energy convergence: (8) computes the mean energy debit exactly, and the growing battery-energy norm is retained. Finite nonzero $b_{n}$ must not be relabeled as exact Definition 28 auxiliary cyclicity.\n\n#### Stronger operational work statement: asymptotically no passive-energy loss\n\nOnly $2N+1$ different battery shifts occur, so $\\operatorname{rank}(\\rho_W')\\le 2N+1$. Moreover,\n\n$$\n\\lambda_{\\max}(\\rho_{W'})\\ge \\langle \\eta|\\rho_{W'}|\\eta\\rangle\\ge (1-N/L)^2\\ge 1-2N/L.\n$$\n\nLet $P_{W,\\mathrm{out}}$ be the minimum battery energy over its unitary orbit, i.e. the energy of its passive rearrangement. Put its largest eigenvalue on the ground level and its remaining eigenvalues on levels at most $2N$. This gives\n\n$$\n0\\le P_{W,\\mathrm{out}}\\le (2N\\delta)(2N/L)=4\\Theta n^{-4}.\n\\tag{15a}\n$$\n\nThe initial pure packet has ergotropy $E_{W,\\mathrm{in}}$. The final ergotropy is $E_{W,\\mathrm{in}}-Q_n-P_{W,\\mathrm{out}}$, so the ergotropy loss is $Q_n+P_{W,\\mathrm{out}}$ and converges to $\\Theta\\ln 2$. This is a precise unitary-orbit extractable-work statement, stronger than mean debit alone. It neither supplies an autonomous extractor nor claims battery renewal for free. The proof uses the output rank and tail eigenweight; multiplying small entropy by the full growing battery energy would not give this result. Energy-weighted finite-state approximation errors as in Section 6 also control passive energy, since the minimum unitary-orbit energy is Lipschitz with constant $\\lVert H_W\\rVert$ in trace norm.\n\n### 4. Positive autonomous Hamiltonian for any prescribed duration\n\nFix a duration $\\tau>0$ before computing $Q_{n}$. Because $U_{n}$ and $H_{0}$ commute, diagonalize them jointly. For a joint eigenvector with $H_{0}$ energy $e\\ge 0$ and $U_{n}$ eigenvalue $\\exp(-i\\varphi)$, $\\varphi\\in[0,2\\pi)$, choose the unique $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$ congruent to $\\varphi-e\\tau/\\hbar$ modulo $2\\pi$, and give $K_{n}$ the eigenvalue $\\hbar\\theta/\\tau$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0\\le K_n&<(2\\pi \\hbar/\\tau)I,\\qquad [K_n,H_0]=0, \\\\\n\\exp[-i(H_0+K_n)\\tau/\\hbar]&=U_n.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{16}\n$$\n\nOn the zero-energy eigenspace choose $\\theta=0$. Therefore $H_{\\mathrm{reset}}=H_0+K_n\\ge 0$ has actual ground energy zero. This uses a positive interaction term, not a compensating $-H_0$ term, and does not remove a baseline to force a desired mean energy. Both bare total energy $H_{0}$ and interaction energy $K_{n}$ are exactly conserved throughout the autonomous evolution:\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\langle H_0\\rangle=\\Delta\\langle K_n\\rangle=0.\n\\tag{17}\n$$\n\nThe interaction need not vanish at the boundaries. It is the same finite Hamiltonian before and after the nominated interval, its mean energy is retained, and its expectation does not supply any of the heat in (8). All entropy-bearing factors are M, the stated bath, battery and the clock below. In this mathematical service class there is no additional hidden switching register.\n\nThis is highly nonlocal many-body control with a rapidly growing finite description, not an efficient circuit, local interaction theorem, fixed-apparatus limit or experimental implementation. The compiler admits the displayed finite matrices as new augmented-service data; original PU hierarchy admission is not inferred.\n\nTaranto et al. (2023), Section III.2 and Corollary 1, establish finite-time Landauer-limit cooling with diverging control complexity. The construction here supplies an explicitly positive commuting realization, finite battery guards and the complete energy, entropy and precision ledger.\n\n### 5. Real clock and independently set period\n\nFor the calibrated action branch $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$, specify in advance a two-level clock\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_C &= 2\\pi\\Theta |1\\rangle\\langle 1|, \\\\\n|c_0\\rangle&=(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)/\\sqrt{2}, \\\\\n\\tau&=\\hbar/\\Theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{18}\n$$\n\nIts real propagator gives $\\exp(-iH_C\\tau/\\hbar)=I$. The smallest positive return period of $c_{0}$ is $\\tau$. Add this decoupled clock to the total Hamiltonian:\n\n$$\nH_{\\mathrm{tot}}=H_{\\mathrm{reset}}\\otimes I_C+I\\otimes H_C.\n$$\n\nThe clock returns exactly, stays uncorrelated throughout, has fixed positive mean energy $\\pi\\Theta$, and supplies no energy debit. No clock measurement is performed during the operation, so no clock-readout memory is silently reset. The nominated endpoint is a stroboscopic return, not an autonomously emitted classical tick or permanent halting signal.\n\nEquations (8), (10) and (18) now prove\n\n$$\nW_n=Q_n,\\qquad \\lim W_n\\tau=\\lim Q_n\\tau=\\hbar\\ln 2.\n\\tag{19}\n$$\n\nThe input calibration sets $\\Theta$ and $H_{C}$ before heat is evaluated. It never chooses $\\tau=\\hbar\\ln 2/Q_n$ after observing the heat. The factor $2\\pi$ in the engineered clock gap is an explicit design choice: for a separately chosen positive rational $r$, the gap $2\\pi\\Theta/r$ has period $r\\hbar/\\Theta$ and yields $Q_n\\tau\\to r\\hbar\\ln 2$. Thermality alone does not select $r=1$. If $\\kappa_{A}$ is independently calibrated to $r\\hbar$, that designed family realizes its matching work-duration limit; this is engineering existence, not a derivation of $\\kappa_{A}$ from a reservoir.\n\n#### Same proper-time coordinate, without using an imaginary period\n\nOn an accepted uniformly accelerated worldline, write its independently geometry-calibrated proper lapse as $f(u)=d\\tau_{\\mathrm{prop}}/du$. Applying the same lapse to $H_{\\mathrm{reset}}$ and $H_{C}$ gives\n\n$$\nU(u_1,u_0)=\\exp[-i(H_{\\mathrm{reset}}+H_C)(\\tau_{\\mathrm{prop}}(u_1)-\\tau_{\\mathrm{prop}}(u_0))/\\hbar].\n$$\n\nTherefore an interval of proper duration $\\hbar/\\Theta$ implements (19) and returns the clock in exactly the same proper coordinate used for a detector gap. This is a direct real-time propagator statement. Any source-time lapse drive and its endpoint energy changes remain external control resources; this coordinate substitution alone does not establish a cyclic full detector/lapse apparatus. A symmetric isolated interval has equal endpoint lapse and zero net lapse work because $\\langle H_{\\mathrm{tot}}\\rangle$ is constant. Appending an arbitrary detector interval requires its own combined boundary/work accounting.\n\nSharing a calibrated proper-time coordinate is not automatically identity with every physical clock/readout register in the existing N03 theorem. The two-level clock here cannot count an unbounded number of return periods or replace N03's no-wrap growing-window readout. A complete common-clock detector construction must own that extra counter/readout information and its cyclic return; this theorem supplies the real-time reset interface only.\n\n### 6. Finite precision and exact scope of the limit\n\nFor the selected $\\tau=\\hbar/\\Theta$, every $H_0\\tau/\\hbar$ is rational because its eigenvalues are integer multiples of $\\Theta/n^2$; permutation eigenphases are rational multiples of $2\\pi$. The energy-shell spectral construction has computable entries. No unspecified arbitrary real coefficient is required. Matrix descriptions and supplied states grow with $n$.\n\nExact mathematical matrices have finite-tolerance implementations: approximate $K_{n}$ within each exact $H_{0}$ eigenspace by a Hermitian matrix to norm error $a_{n}$, add a scalar correction of at most $a_{n}$ to preserve positivity, and retain the zero block separately. Then the approximation still commutes with $H_{0}$, while its propagator operator-norm error is at most $2\\tau a_n/\\hbar$ and its full-state trace-norm error is at most $4\\tau a_n/\\hbar$. Heat and battery-energy errors are bounded by that latter error times their *actual* finite observable norms, at most order $\\Theta n^6$. Choosing, for example, $a_n\\le\\Theta n^{-10}$ makes those energy errors vanish. Preparation errors require the same energy-weighted accounting. The accuracy demand is explicit and increases with $n$; there is no claim of a uniformly bounded-precision apparatus realizing the exact limit.\n\nThe reset lemma has large total ground-subtracted energy, including its finite battery baseline and clock; its net work has the different stated ledger. The following source-responsive construction supplies the full detector composition.\n\n## A complete source-responsive collision detector/reset cycle\n\n### The result\n\nFix independently $\\Theta=k_BT>0$ and $\\hbar$. An explicit sequence of finite positive-Hamiltonian devices has a genuinely source-responsive detector, a degenerate reusable memory, owned source and reset baths, a finite work/control store and a cyclic program. In each complete real cycle of pre-registered duration $\\tau=\\hbar/\\Theta$:\n\n- The memory starts in ready state $|1\\rangle$, contains an exactly uniform classical detector record at an actual intermediate time, and returns to $|1\\rangle$ with error $\\le e^{-n}$.\n- Every unchanged classical side record is retained explicitly. The actual conditional entropy is $s_nh(g_n)$, tending to $\\ln 2$. No retained copy is omitted.\n- The source transition ratio is $\\exp(-\\beta\\Delta_n)$, and changing the source population with the detector held fixed changes its output.\n- Complete detector-plus-reset heat equals actual work-store debit and tends to $\\Theta\\ln 2$. The detector's positive heat $\\Delta_n/2$ is included.\n- Program and oscillator clocks return exactly over the whole cycle. The work/control store's endpoint entropy, correlations and passive-energy corrections vanish, with explicit bounds.\n- Thus $E_{\\mathrm{work},n}\\tau\\to\\hbar\\ln 2$ on the independently calibrated $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$ branch. Total ground-subtracted Hamiltonian energy is much larger and is not substituted for $E_{\\mathrm{work}}$.\n\nThis is a declared growing-resource limiting ideal. No finite member is asserted to have pure reset, exactly conditionally uniform records given all side information, or exactly ideal entropy-free work storage.\n\n### 1. Initial resources and fixed energy\n\nFor integer $n\\ge 2$ put\n\n$$\nN=n^3,\\quad \\delta=\\Theta/n^2,\\quad \\Delta_n=\\delta,\\quad A=N+1,\\quad L=n^8,\\quad d_W=L+4A.\n$$\n\nThe factors are initially independent:\n\n- $M$: a memory with $H_{M}=0$ throughout, initially $|1\\rangle$.\n- $D$: the source qubit, $H_D=\\delta|1\\rangle\\langle 1|$, initially $\\operatorname{diag}(1-g,g)$, $g=1/(1+e^{1/n^2})$.\n- $S$: a classical control coin with $H_{S}=0$ and initial law $\\Pr(S=1)=s=1/[2(1-g)]=(1+e^{-1/n^2})/2$. Its nonthermal input law is an explicit resource. It is never resampled, erased or discarded during the cycle.\n- $B_1,\\dots,B_N$: reset cells with $H_B=\\delta\\sum_j j|1\\rangle\\langle 1|_j$ and independent Gibbs populations $g_j=1/(1+e^{j/n^2})$.\n- $W$: a battery, $H_W=\\delta\\sum_{k=0}^{d_W-1}k|k\\rangle\\langle k|$, initially $\\eta=L^{-1/2}\\sum_{k=2A}^{2A+L-1}|k\\rangle$.\n- $C$: a three-state program register, bare Hamiltonian zero, initially $|0\\rangle$.\n- $O$: an oscillator with $H_O=2\\pi\\Theta|1\\rangle\\langle 1|$, initially $|+\\rangle$.\n\nThe source D and reset cells together are exactly Gibbs at the common T for their fixed sum Hamiltonian. They are fresh owned inputs and become owned spent outputs; none is regenerated for free. The battery and clocks are supplied nonthermal resources. The coin's initial state and its return/correlations are separately accounted below.\n\nDefine fixed bare energy $H_0=H_D+H_B+H_W$. The explicitly nominated work/control store is\n\n$$\n\\Sigma = W\\otimes C\\otimes O,\\qquad H_\\Sigma=H_W+H_O.\n\\tag{1}\n$$\n\nThis choice is made at the outset, including at the reset cut. C and O are timing/control degrees of this work store, not undeclared separately cyclic reset auxiliaries. Their entropy, correlations, state changes, mean energy and ergotropy are proved below. The whole-cycle clocks do return exactly.\n\n### 2. An actual source-responsive detector\n\nOn $M,D,W$ define $F_{E}$ by exchanging, for each $k=1,\\dots,d_W-1$,\n\n$$\n\\lvert 1_M,0_D,k_W\\rangle\\leftrightarrow\\lvert 0_M,1_D,{k-1}_W\\rangle.\n\\tag{2}\n$$\n\nIt fixes all other basis vectors, including $|00,k\\rangle$, $|11,k\\rangle$ and the unmatched edge vectors. It is a full finite permutation unitary, $[F_E,H_0]=0$. The detector gate is the classically controlled full exchange\n\n$$\nU_D=|0\\rangle\\langle 0|_S\\otimes I + |1\\rangle\\langle 1|_S\\otimes F_E.\n\\tag{3}\n$$\n\nAll source/coin/memory variables remain classical in their computational bases at this endpoint; only the battery has coherent packets. There is no dephasing primitive, pure pointer, external outcome copy or omitted selector factor.\n\nLet b denote the initial D bit. On the actual guarded inputs:\n\n- If $S=0$: $M_{\\mathrm{out}}=1$, $D_{\\mathrm{out}}=b$, and the battery shift is 0.\n- If $S=1$: $M_{\\mathrm{out}}=b$, $D_{\\mathrm{out}}=1$, and the battery is debited $1-b$ levels.\n\nHence the actual memory $P:=M_{\\mathrm{out}}$ and source satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Pr(P=0)&=s(1-g)=1/2,\\qquad \\Pr(P=1)=1/2, \\\\\n\\Pr(D_{\\mathrm{out}}=1)&=g+1/2, \\\\\nQ_D&=\\delta/2=-\\Delta\\langle H_W\\rangle_D.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4}\n$$\n\nThe battery–memory marginal is exactly\n\n$$\n(1/2)|1\\rangle\\langle 1|\\otimes|\\eta\\rangle\\langle \\eta| +(1/2)|0\\rangle\\langle 0|\\otimes|\\eta_-\\rangle\\langle \\eta_-|,\n\\tag{5}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\eta_-$ is the one-level downward translate of $\\eta$.\n\n#### Detector response and ready-state direction\n\nWith the installed control law s fixed, replacing the source population g by g' gives\n\n$$\n\\Pr(P=1)=1-s(1-g'),\\qquad d\\Pr(P=1)/dg'=s>0.\n\\tag{6}\n$$\n\nThus the fair output is a calibrated response to this source, not an independent fair coin. At fixed positive source gap, its Gibbs $g'$ changes strictly with the source temperature. For separately prepared memory inputs $|0\\rangle$ and $|1\\rangle$, respectively, the source-induced up/down probabilities are\n\n$$\np_{\\mathrm{up}}=s g,\\qquad p_{\\mathrm{down}}=s(1-g),\\qquad p_{\\mathrm{up}}/p_{\\mathrm{down}}=e^{-\\beta\\delta}.\n\\tag{7}\n$$\n\nThe complete permutation gives a normalized instrument for either input and every outcome, without postselection. These are finite collision-source response statements; no scalar-field KMS spectrum is inferred.\n\nMore fully, on arbitrary memory inputs with the prescribed independent guarded battery/source/coin, the reduced detector channel is $\\Phi=(1-s)\\mathrm{id}+sR_{\\gamma_D}$, where $R_{\\gamma_D}$ replaces the memory by $\\gamma_{D}$. The full-swap branch transfers the input and its reference correlations to the owned source/battery factors. On the registered diagonal input algebra its stochastic columns are $(1-sg,sg)$ for initial 0 and $(s(1-g),1-s(1-g))$ for initial 1. These sum to one and are realized by (2)–(3), including their retained $S,D$ records. The nominated ready input is diagonal, so its memory record is already classical without a further readout or dephasing operation. This supplies the response-faithful normalized map, rather than merely assigning a thermal number to a source-independent qubit.\n\nThe ready state is $|1\\rangle$ in a degenerate memory. Starting ground-ready in $|0\\rangle$ would give excitation probability at most $g<1/2$ and would not establish (4). Its pure initial preparation is an explicit supplied resource, returned by the cycle in the declared limit. There is no hidden $\\delta$ preparation energy: $H_{M}=0$ throughout. The real detector cost is the battery transfer in (2), not a temporary memory gap. An alternative raised-gap realization would pay $\\delta$ to raise an excited-ready level, deposit $\\delta/2$ through collision, and recover $\\delta/2$ when lowering it. That alternative has the same positive net detector cost but is not assumed here.\n\nThis is an energy-conserving, work-assisted population detector with source gap $\\delta$. It is not falsely described as a stationary detector whose bare memory gap is $\\delta$.\n\n### 3. Complete classical records and reset-boundary conditions\n\nAt the reset cut retain the complete unchanged classical side record\n\n$$\nR=(S,D_{\\mathrm{out}}).\n\\tag{8}\n$$\n\nBoth variables are explicitly present in the state; neither is forgotten. When $S=0$, $P$ is fixed at 1. When $S=1$, $D_{\\mathrm{out}}$ is fixed at 1 and $P$ has the original source Bernoulli law $g$. Therefore exactly\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nH(P|R)&=s\\, h(g), \\\\\nI(P:R)&=\\ln 2-s\\, h(g) \\to 0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{9}\n$$\n\nHere h is binary entropy in nats. Every conditional law is accounted; no branch or failed event is removed. The record is uniformly distributed marginally at every finite n, and conditionally uniform given the complete R in the limit only.\n\nAfter (3), $P,R$ are classical and the conditional battery is the pure packet $\\eta$ or $\\eta_-$ according only to $P$. Since $\\langle\\eta|\\eta_-\\rangle=1-1/L$, its two nonzero marginal eigenvalues are $1-1/(2L)$ and $1/(2L)$. Consequently\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS(W_{\\mathrm{in,reset}})&=h(1/(2L)), \\\\\nI(W:PR)_{\\mathrm{in,reset}}&=S(W_{\\mathrm{in,reset}})\\to 0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{10}\n$$\n\nAt this actual boundary $C$ and $O$ are known pure product factors. Thus the same entropy and correlation statements hold for $\\Sigma$. The reset bath $B$ is exactly Gibbs and independent of $P,R,\\Sigma$. There are no other nonbath auxiliary degrees of freedom. In particular $D_{\\mathrm{out}}$ is not called a fresh Gibbs bath at this cut: it belongs to the retained classical $R$.\n\nThis is also explicit product convergence, not only a small information number: quantum Pinsker gives half trace distance at most $\\sqrt{h(1/(2L))/2}$ between the actual reset-input state on $\\Sigma PR$ and the product of its actual $\\Sigma$ and $PR$ marginals. The fresh $B$ factor is exact. This bound is not multiplied by the growing bath Hamiltonian to infer heat convergence; the actual heat is proved directly by the finite permutation and shift identities below. Conditional entropy, auxiliary independence and energy convergence thus have separate justified estimates.\n\nIf an additional classical side record were obtained from the source, coin and battery without making a new copy from $P$, the more inclusive bound is $I(P:SDW)\\le\\ln 2-s\\,h(g)+h(1/(2L))$. Thus its conditional uncertainty is at least $s\\,h(g)-h(1/(2L))$, still tending to $\\ln 2$. This does not silently authorize such a measurement or its apparatus: the actual protocol's complete $R$ is (8). It shows that inaccessible battery-readable information is not being used to hide a limiting perfect copy.\n\nThe source/coin variables remain unchanged during the reset and final X. Thus R's full joint law is preserved, not just its separate marginals. The coin is an owned classical control state, not an unlimited source of fresh randomness.\n\n#### Coin return over the complete cycle\n\n$S$ is unchanged exactly in distribution. It remains classical, so its mutual information with the rest is at most $h(s)\\to 0$. More explicitly, writing the final state as $s|1\\rangle\\langle 1|\\otimes\\rho_1+(1-s)|0\\rangle\\langle 0|\\otimes\\rho_0$ gives half trace distance at most $2s(1-s)$ from its initial coin state tensored with the rest's final marginal. This vanishes because $1-s\\le 1/(2n^2)$. Its Hamiltonian and mean energy stay zero, and its entropy $h(s)$ is unchanged. Thus even if viewed as a controller resource across whole cycles, its cyclic/uncorrelated return is a proved limiting statement, not an assertion of exact finite independent reuse.\n\n### 4. A finite guarded reset and return to the original ready state\n\nUse the reset permutation on $M,B_1,\\dots,B_N$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_R(m,b_1,\\dots,b_N)&=(b_N,m,b_1,\\dots,b_{N-1}), \\\\\ns_R&=m+\\sum_{j=1}^{N-1}b_j-N b_N,\\qquad |s_R|\\le N.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{11}\n$$\n\nOn the central battery band $A\\le k\\le d_W-A-1$ prescribe\n\n$$\n\\lvert x,k\\rangle\\mapsto\\lvert V_Rx,k-s_R(x)\\rangle.\n\\tag{12}\n$$\n\nIts output indices lie between $A-N=1$ and $d_W-A-1+N=d_W-2$. It is an energy-preserving injection. Within every finite $H_B+H_W$ energy shell, match unused domain and image basis vectors in lexicographic order to obtain a full permutation unitary $U_{R}$. The ground shell is completed by identity. Hence $[U_R,H_0]=0$; $D,S$ are untouched.\n\nThe initial battery support and the support after the detector lie inside the central band. Every cumulative shift has magnitude at most $N+1=A$. Thus the whole realized cycle stays in the proved part of the lift; it never invokes an arbitrary complement, a negative battery level or a wraparound.\n\nThis gate puts the actual last fresh reset bit into $M$. Hence its memory output is $\\operatorname{diag}(1-g_N,g_N)$, independent of $R$. The exact reset heat is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_R/\\Theta&=\\sum_{j=1}^N (j/n^2)(g_{j-1}-g_j),\\qquad g_0=1/2 \\\\\n&=\\ln 2-h(g_N)+D_n, \\\\\n0<D_n&=\\sum_j d(g_{j-1}\\Vert g_j)\\le 1/(8n).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{13}\n$$\n\nThe preceding reset lemma supplies the log-partition proof: $f''(x)\\le 1/4$ and the $N$ increments are $1/n^2$. In particular $h(g_N)\\le(n+2)e^{-n}$.\n\nThe final gate $X_{M}$ costs no bare energy because $H_{M}=0$ and restores the original ready direction. Its output is $(1-g_N)|1\\rangle\\langle 1|+g_N|0\\rangle\\langle 0|$, with error $g_N\\le e^{-n}$. Its actual final conditional entropy is $h(g_N)$.\n\nThe exact finite conditional-entropy/heat ledger is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_R/\\Theta - [H(P|R)-H(P_{\\mathrm{out}}|R)] \\\\\n&= \\ln 2-s\\, h(g)+D_n \\ge 0,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{14}\n$$\n\nand the excess tends to zero. This establishes conditional Landauer saturation in the declared limit, not merely a numerical heat limit. Finite work-store correlations are also present and quantified by (10) and the following sections; no finite member is mislabeled as an exact Definition-28 process.\n\nThere is also an exact finite global entropy identity. Let $A_{\\mathrm{reset}}=PR\\Sigma$ at both reset boundaries and let $\\gamma_{B}$ be the fresh reset Gibbs state. Unitarity, initial $B$ independence, invariance of $R$, and $\\beta Q_R=\\Delta S(B)+D(\\rho_{B,\\mathrm{out}}\\|\\gamma_B)$ give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\beta Q_R-[H(P|R)-H(P_{\\mathrm{out}}|R)] \\\\\n&=c_\\Sigma+I(A_{\\mathrm{reset}}:B)_{\\mathrm{out}}+D(\\rho_{B,\\mathrm{out}}\\Vert\\gamma_B), \\\\\nc_\\Sigma &= S(\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{in}})-S(\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{out}})-I(PR:\\Sigma)_{\\mathrm{in}}+I(PR:\\Sigma)_{\\mathrm{out}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{14a}\n$$\n\nTo check the signs, expand $S(A_{\\mathrm{reset}})=S(PR)+S(\\Sigma)-I(PR:\\Sigma)$, then use $I(A_{\\mathrm{reset}}:B)_{\\mathrm{out}}=\\Delta S(A_{\\mathrm{reset}})+\\Delta S(B)$. Here $S(\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{in}})=I(PR:\\Sigma)_{\\mathrm{in}}$ exactly by (10), while the output entropy and correlation terms vanish by (20). Thus $c_\\Sigma\\to 0$. Since the left side tends to zero by (14), nonnegativity forces both the reset-bath relative entropy and its final correlations with all nonbath factors to vanish. In fact the reset-bath marginal is the product with populations $1/2,g_1,\\dots,g_{N-1}$, so its relative entropy is exactly $D_{n}$. This global identity directly controls the actual correlated finite process; no energy estimate is deduced from a small trace distance against a diverging Hamiltonian.\n\n### 5. Actual intermediate times and one cyclic program\n\nLet the program shift be\n\n$$\nJ=|1\\rangle\\langle 0|_C\\otimes U_D + |2\\rangle\\langle 1|_C\\otimes U_R + |0\\rangle\\langle 2|_C\\otimes X_M.\n\\tag{15}\n$$\n\nEach gate is a full finite unitary and commutes with the same $H_{0}$, so $J$ is unitary and $[J,H_0]=0$. Starting $C$ in $|0\\rangle$, $J$ executes the detector and puts $C$ in $|1\\rangle$; $J^2$ executes the reset and puts it in $|2\\rangle$; $J^3$ executes $X$ and returns it to $|0\\rangle$. The program is a pure product factor at every such boundary. This is the actual state sequence to be generated, not merely a factorization of a claimed endpoint.\n\nPre-register $\\tau=\\hbar/\\Theta$ and $t=\\tau/3$. On a joint $H_0,J$ eigenvector of energy $e\\ge 0$ and $J$ eigenvalue $\\exp(-i\\varphi)$, choose $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$ congruent to $\\varphi-et/\\hbar$ and assign $K$ the eigenvalue $\\hbar\\theta/t$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0\\le K&<(6\\pi\\Theta)I,\\qquad [K,H_0]=0, \\\\\n\\exp[-i(H_0+K)t/\\hbar]&=J.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{16}\n$$\n\nSet $H_{\\mathrm{tot}}=H_0+K+H_O$. It is a single finite positive time-independent Hamiltonian, with actual ground zero. For a ground witness take $D,B,W$ in their bare ground, $M$ in $|+\\rangle$, any fixed coin basis vector, and $C$ uniform over its three positions. $U_{D}$ and $U_{R}$ fix the relevant zero-energy physical vectors, and $X$ fixes $|+\\rangle$; hence $J$ has eigenvalue 1, $K=0$, and taking $O$ ground gives $H_{\\mathrm{tot}}=0$.\n\nAt real time $t$ the actual classical detector record exists. At $2t$ it is reset. At $3t=\\tau$ it has the original ready direction, and $C$ returns exactly and uncorrelated. The oscillator also returns since $\\exp(-iH_O\\tau/\\hbar)=I$. Its mean energy $\\pi\\Theta$ is constant and retained. The time law is established from the real propagator before heat is evaluated.\n\nBetween these boundaries the program can be correlated with the target; its physical factor and Hamiltonian remain included. No permanent halting, emitted classical tick, uncharged readout, bath regeneration or unlimited unattended reuse is asserted.\n\n### 6. Complete cycle energy and battery quality\n\nBecause $H_{0}$ and K commute, both expectations are conserved at all times. At the complete boundary the degenerate memory and clocks have their original Hamiltonians, and all bath Hamiltonians are unchanged. Thus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{\\mathrm{work},n} &:= -\\Delta\\langle H_\\Sigma\\rangle = -\\Delta\\langle H_W\\rangle = Q_D+Q_R, \\\\\nQ_D&=\\Theta/(2n^2), \\\\\n|E_{\\mathrm{work},n}/\\Theta-\\ln 2|&\\le (n+2)e^{-n}+1/(8n)+1/(2n^2).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{17}\n$$\n\nThe source and reset baths are spent outputs, with all correlations retained in the global state. Their free renewal is not claimed. The positive interaction K supplies no net hidden work: its expectation is exactly constant, and no bare-energy baseline was canceled.\n\nThe entropy boundaries are different for the reset and the complete cycle. The reset removes $H(P|R)\\to\\ln 2$; the complete cycle starts and ends with ready memory, so its full-boundary memory entropy decrease tends to zero. The source's entropy loss $h(g)-h(g+1/2)\\to\\ln 2$ is retained in the spent-source inventory. Accordingly, (17) is the complete-cycle heat/work identity, while (14) is the Landauer saturation statement on the actual reset subinterval. The vanishing additional detector heat makes their limiting mean heats coincide; it does not turn the full cycle into an erasure of an initially random memory or assert that all spent bath resources are returned.\n\nFor a detected memory value $m$, the detector debits $1-m$ battery levels and the reset debits $m+\\sum_{j<N}b_j-Nb_N$. Their complete sum is\n\n$$\ns_{\\mathrm{cycle}}=1+\\sum_{j=1}^{N-1}b_j-N b_N,\n\\tag{18}\n$$\n\nindependent of $m$ and the source/coin branch. This is a cancellation of two explicitly owned stochastic work transfers, not removal of a Hamiltonian energy. The source heat $\\delta/2$ remains in (17). It gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathrm{Var}(E_{\\mathrm{work},n})&=\\delta^2[\\sum_{j<N}g_j(1-g_j)+N^2g_N(1-g_N)] \\\\\n&\\le \\Theta^2[1/(4n)+n^2e^{-n}]\\to 0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{19}\n$$\n\nThe nominal source/coin/reset inputs are classical; all gates are permutations. The complete output is therefore a classical mixture of distinct computational outputs and pure translated battery packets, with shifts of magnitude $\\le A$. Comparing every translated packet to $\\eta$ and using their overlap $1-|s|/L$ gives the full-output half trace bound\n\n$$\nt_n=\\sqrt{2A/L}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, with $b_n=h(t_n)+t_n\\ln(d_W-1)$,\n\n$$\nS(W_{\\mathrm{out}})\\le b_n\\to 0,\\qquad I(W_{\\mathrm{out}}:\\mathrm{rest})\\le 2b_n\\to 0.\n\\tag{20}\n$$\n\nThe same estimates hold at the completed reset step, whose cumulative detector-plus-reset shift already equals (18). At either endpoint the oscillator/program are pure product factors, so $S(\\Sigma)=S(W)$ and its correlations with the rest are those of $W$.\n\nAt most 2A+1 battery shifts occur and the largest eigenvalue is at least 1−2A/L. The final passive energy for W is at most\n\n$$\nP_{W,\\mathrm{out}}\\le 4\\delta A^2/L\\to 0.\n\\tag{21}\n$$\n\nFor $\\Sigma$ the same bound holds: assign its eigenvalues to low-lying $W$ states with $O$ grounded and one $C$ basis vector. Its initial full-cycle state is pure and has passive energy zero. Thus whole-cycle ergotropy loss differs from $E_{\\mathrm{work},n}$ by at most $4\\delta A^2/L$ and tends to $\\Theta\\ln 2$. This is a unitary-orbit extractability statement, not an unproved autonomous extraction or renewal protocol.\n\n#### The reset cut and the literal ideal-work exception\n\nThe reset subinterval is $[t,3t]$. Its program begins in $|1\\rangle$ and ends in $|0\\rangle$, and the oscillator begins at a noninitial pure phase and ends at its initial phase. They are NOT separately asserted cyclic on this subinterval. They belong to the declared work/control store $\\Sigma$ in (1).\n\nAt its input $\\Sigma$ has entropy $h(1/(2L))$ and correlation $I(\\Sigma:PR)=h(1/(2L))$; at its output these are bounded by (20), and both tend to zero. Its Hamiltonian $H_\\Sigma$ is unchanged, and $O$'s mean energy is constant while $C$'s bare Hamiltonian is zero. Its actual mean energy debit over $[t,3t]$ is exactly $Q_{R}$, with $K$'s expectation unchanged. At the input its rank is two with tail eigenweight $1/(2L)$, so its passive energy is at most $\\delta/(2L)$ (in fact the degenerate $C$ factor permits zero). At the output it is bounded by (21). Its reset-leg ergotropy loss therefore differs from $Q_{R}$ by at most $\\delta/(2L)+4\\delta A^2/L\\to 0$.\n\nThis proves the stated ideal-work-source limit: no source of entropy or correlation is hidden in a renamed clock, and the mean debit is asymptotically pure extractable work. The literal Definition-28 work-source exception permits state changes with the proved entropy/correlation/work character; it does not demand that this work source return its state. Over the whole detector/reset cycle C and O nevertheless return exactly, and S's correlation return is proved above. At finite n, the displayed small entropy and correlation terms are retained; exact auxiliary/work-source hypotheses are claimed only in the justified limit.\n\nThe initial battery mean is $\\Theta[2A/n^2+(L-1)/(2n^2)]$, asymptotic to $\\Theta n^6/2$. The oscillator energy $\\pi\\Theta$ and the nonnegative $K$ expectation also belong in total Hamiltonian mean energy. Hence $E_{\\mathrm{total}}$ is not $E_{\\mathrm{work},n}$ and cannot be replaced by it in a Margolus–Levitin inequality.\n\n### 7. Acceleration, proper clock and action-duration identity\n\nRegister $a>0$ independently, the usual uniformly accelerated worldline and its geometry-calibrated proper time. Supply the actual Gibbs bodies at\n\n$$\n\\Theta=\\hbar a/(2\\pi c).\n$$\n\nThe single Hamiltonian above generates the complete detector/reset cycle in that proper coordinate. Thus its directly proved real duration and work-duration limit are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tau&=\\hbar/\\Theta=2\\pi c/a, \\\\\n\\lim E_{\\mathrm{work},n}\\, \\tau&=\\hbar\\ln 2=\\kappa_A \\ln 2\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{22}\n$$\n\non the separately calibrated $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$ branch. The oscillator/control energy ratio is explicitly designed before evaluating the heat. Choosing a different ratio would give a different real period; thermality alone does not select this engineering choice. Neither an imaginary KMS period nor a posterior choice $\\tau=\\hbar\\ln 2/Q$ is used.\n\nFor an explicit common inertial chart $u$, use the independently geometric proper lapse $f(u)=d\\tau_{\\mathrm{prop}}/du$ and the generator $f(u)H_{\\mathrm{tot}}$. Its actual propagator is $\\exp[-iH_{\\mathrm{tot}}\\Delta\\tau_{\\mathrm{prop}}/\\hbar]$. All source, reset, control and oscillator phases use the same lapse. Choose the full cycle symmetrically about the hyperbola's closest-approach event, with proper endpoints $\\pm\\tau/2$. Then $f$ has equal endpoint values, and the lapse drive's net work is $\\int f'(u)\\langle H_{\\mathrm{tot}}\\rangle\\,du=0$ because $\\langle H_{\\mathrm{tot}}\\rangle$ is constant. Its peak and integrated controls, which scale with the actual large $\\lVert H_{\\mathrm{tot}}\\rVert$, are retained source data; the proper-time Hamiltonian implementation itself is autonomous. This is the formal externally calibrated worldline/service convention already allowed for the conditional accelerated branch, not a derivation of a moving chassis or a free clock-preparation device.\n\nExplicitly, $\\tau_{\\mathrm{prop}}(u)=(c/a)\\operatorname{asinh}(au/c)$, $f(u)=[1+(au/c)^2]^{-1/2}$, and the inertial endpoints are $\\pm b$ with $b=(c/a)\\sinh\\pi$. Put $p=\\operatorname{sech}\\pi$ and use baseline $pH_{\\mathrm{tot}}$ plus positive zero-endpoint control $(f(u)-p)H_{\\mathrm{tot}}$. Its norm peak is $(1-p)\\lVert H_{\\mathrm{tot}}\\rVert$, its integrated norm is $(2c/a)(\\pi-\\tanh\\pi)\\lVert H_{\\mathrm{tot}}\\rVert$, and its derivative norm is at most $(a/c)\\lVert H_{\\mathrm{tot}}\\rVert$. A finite spectral bound is $\\lVert H_{\\mathrm{tot}}\\rVert\\le\\Theta[N(N+1)/(2n^2)+d_W/n^2+8\\pi]$. These are charged, generally large resources. Calorimetry in (17) uses local proper energy units; corresponding inertial boundary energies and temperature carry the common factor $p$, rather than being silently identified with the proper values. The conditional branch prescribes this common clock/worldline map and does not claim a separately cost-free mechanically accelerated chassis.\n\nThis construction is an engineered conditional Rindler–Landauer detector at a supplied reservoir temperature. It does not show that acceleration thermalizes the source, reproduce the Dirac continuum detector spectrum of Theorem N.4a, or prove the microscopic Minkowski-vacuum Unruh effect. Those stronger source questions are separate from this conditional witness.\n\n### 8. Precision, resources and verification scope\n\nEvery $n$ uses finite matrices, finite Gibbs bodies, a finite classical coin and a finite coherent battery. Its exact parameters are computable. The permutation/energy-shell compiler is finite. The battery energy norms and total bare spectral span are $O(\\Theta n^6)$; the interaction $K$ is bounded by $6\\pi\\Theta$ but its matrix description and addressed dimension grow. The source sensitivity at $\\Delta_{n}>0$ is nonzero at every finite $n$ and tends to zero in the ideal limit; no uniform sensitivity or experimentally efficient thermometry claim is made.\n\nImplementation errors must be selected against actual energy norms. For example a complete state error $O(n^{-10})$, including preparation, timing, Gibbs and gate coefficient errors, gives vanishing $O(\\Theta n^{-4})$ mean-energy errors. Energy-shell-preserving Hamiltonian approximations maintain exact bare conservation; their unitary and trace errors have explicit Duhamel bounds as in the frozen reset construction. Finite classical entropy errors use finite-alphabet continuity for $(P,R)$; work-store entropy errors include $\\ln d_{W}$. Increasing precision and matrix size are declared resources, not an unbounded-precision finite apparatus.\n\nThe initial ready preparation, source/coin law, battery state, clocks, interaction program and calibration are supplied inputs. Ready, clocks and coin return have the exact or limiting properties stated; bath and battery resources are spent and owned. The claimed interval is the complete detector/reset operation with no hidden N03 exposure or archived readout added to its duration.\n\n## Binary reset imposes a stronger common-total-energy obstruction\n\n### 1. Setup\n\nLet $M$ be a logical qubit, $A$ include every reservoir, battery, controller and other physical auxiliary, and let $H$ be a self-adjoint, time-independent total Hamiltonian with $\\inf\\operatorname{spec}H=0$. The initial state is\n\n$$\n\\rho = I_M/2 \\otimes \\sigma_A,\n$$\n\nwith finite $E=\\operatorname{Tr}(H\\rho)$. For $t>0$ write $U=\\exp(-iHt/\\hbar)$, $\\Pi_1=|1\\rangle\\langle 1|_M\\otimes I_A$, and\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\Pi_1U\\rho U^\\dagger).\n$$\n\nThis is the failure probability for resetting the uniform record to the nominated ready state $|0\\rangle$. It is not the error of distinguishing the entire initial and final joint states. The auxiliary state may be mixed, arbitrarily large, and correlated internally. Its initial independence from $M$ is the actual reset-branch assumption.\n\nSet $c=\\sqrt{1+4/\\pi^2}$.\n\n### 2. Theorem: sector-resolved finite-error lower bound\n\nEvery such process obeys\n\n$$\nE t \\ge (\\pi\\hbar/4) [1 - c \\sqrt{2\\varepsilon}]_+.\n\\tag{1}\n$$\n\nIn particular, exact reset requires $Et\\ge\\pi\\hbar/4$. A sequence of resets with $\\varepsilon_n\\to 0$ obeys $\\liminf E_nt_n\\ge\\pi\\hbar/4$, even with changing auxiliary dimension, changing spectra and varying durations. Thus neither exact reset nor asymptotically exact reset is compatible with $E_nt_n\\to\\hbar\\ln 2$.\n\nUnder the exact calibrated product $Et=\\hbar\\ln 2$, every finite implementation has\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\varepsilon &\\ge (1 - 4 \\ln 2/\\pi)^2 / [2(1 + 4/\\pi^2)] \\\\\n&= 0.004908716118142115\\ldots .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient in the finite-error estimate is valid but is not asserted sharp.\n\nMore directly, the conditional input-1 failure $\\varepsilon_{1}$ obeys $Et\\ge(\\pi\\hbar/4)[1-c\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_1}]_+$. Thus on the calibrated product $\\varepsilon_1\\ge 0.00981743223628423\\ldots$. If $\\Phi$ is the induced memory channel and $\\delta=(1/2)\\lVert\\Phi-R_0\\rVert_\\diamond$ is its half-diamond distance from pure reset, $\\delta\\ge\\varepsilon_1$, so $\\delta$ obeys the same 0.00981743223628423… floor. The unhalved diamond norm floor is 0.01963486447256846… . These constants use different error conventions and must not be interchanged.\n\n#### Proof\n\nPurify $\\sigma_{A}$ to $|s\\rangle$ on $A\\otimes R$, where $R$ is only a mathematical purification reference and $H$ acts trivially on it. Put $|\\psi_j\\rangle=|j\\rangle\\otimes|s\\rangle$, $E_j=\\langle\\psi_j|H\\otimes I_R|\\psi_j\\rangle$, and $\\varepsilon_j=\\lVert\\Pi_1U|\\psi_j\\rangle\\rVert^2$. Then $E=(E_0+E_1)/2$, $\\varepsilon=(\\varepsilon_0+\\varepsilon_1)/2$, and positivity of $H$ gives $E\\ge E_1/2$.\n\nThe branch-1 survival amplitude $A_1=\\langle\\psi_1|U|\\psi_1\\rangle$ obeys $|A_1|\\le\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_1}$, since $\\Pi_1|\\psi_1\\rangle=|\\psi_1\\rangle$ and Cauchy–Schwarz applies to its projection. For $x\\ge 0$ the valid Margolus–Levitin cosine inequality is\n\n$$\n\\cos x \\ge 1 - (2/\\pi)(x + \\sin x).\n$$\n\nIntegration against the H-spectral measure of $\\psi_{1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Re} A_1 - (2/\\pi) \\operatorname{Im} A_1 \\ge 1 - 2E_1t/(\\pi\\hbar).\n$$\n\nThe left side is at most $c|A_1|\\le c\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_1}$. Therefore $E_1t\\ge(\\pi\\hbar/2)[1-c\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_1}]$. Use $E\\ge E_1/2$, $\\varepsilon_1\\le 2\\varepsilon$, and $Et\\ge 0$ to obtain (1). For exact erasure, $U\\psi_1$ lies in the memory-0 sector and is orthogonal to $\\psi_{1}$, so the standard ML orthogonalization bound gives the same endpoint directly. Taking $\\liminf$ proves the limiting statement. Solving (1) under $Et=\\hbar\\ln 2$ gives (2). ∎\n\nThe tempting inequality $\\cos x\\ge 1-2x/\\pi$ is false and is not used.\n\n### 3. Sharp exact endpoint, without pretending it is a thermal eraser\n\nLet $A$ be a single pure ready qubit $|0\\rangle$, let $v=(|10\\rangle-|01\\rangle)/\\sqrt{2}$, and set\n\n$$\nH = (\\pi\\hbar/t)|v\\rangle\\langle v|.\n$$\n\nThen $\\exp(-iHt/\\hbar)$ swaps $|10\\rangle$ and $|01\\rangle$, leaves $|00\\rangle$ fixed, resets $I_M/2\\otimes|0\\rangle\\langle 0|_A$ exactly, and has $E=\\pi\\hbar/(4t)$. This proves sharpness of the exact $\\pi/4$ constant for the stated autonomous reset class. The ancillary output is maximally mixed: this example consumes a pure resource and is not a Gibbs-bath Landauer cycle. Its purpose is to prove sharpness, not thermal admissibility.\n\n### 4. Exact relevance to the original Q07 predicates\n\nThe owning source already proves that an orthogonalizing *whole event* with a common energy/time obeys $Et\\ge\\pi\\hbar/2$, contradicting $Et=\\hbar\\ln 2$. The present theorem needs only that a uniform binary record is erased, and reduces the threshold to $\\pi\\hbar/4$. Since $\\ln 2<\\pi/4$, the calibrated contradiction survives without assuming that the full mixed state orthogonalizes.\n\nIf the cyclic balance uses $E_{\\mathrm{work}}=\\langle Q_{\\mathrm{bath}}\\rangle$, rather than $E_{\\mathrm{total}}=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho H)$, (1) does not identify those quantities. A large finite reservoir, positive interaction energy, a battery baseline or clock energy may make $E_{\\mathrm{total}}$ much larger than $E_{\\mathrm{work}}$. This theorem neither forbids an externally driven same-cycle work/heat construction nor closes the residual Q07 existential claim. Autonomy is explicitly not an original missing gate.\n\nFor a nonuniform record with $P(M=1)=p>0$, the same proof yields $Et\\ge(\\pi\\hbar p/2)[1-c\\sqrt{\\varepsilon/p}]_+$, where $\\varepsilon$ is total output failure probability and $E$ is its actual initial total mean energy. The $p=0$ case gives the trivial nonnegative bound. The uniform $\\pi/4$ value uses $p=1/2$ and may not be imposed on a detector whose actual record law is nonuniform.\n\nInitial product structure is sufficient and is part of the theorem. It cannot be replaced merely by a uniform reduced memory state. For an explicit correlated-input counterexample, let the memory be the first qubit, put\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|b\\rangle&=(|00\\rangle+|11\\rangle)/\\sqrt{2}, \\\\\n|a\\rangle&=|00\\rangle, \\\\\n|h\\rangle&=(|b\\rangle-|a\\rangle)/\\sqrt{2-\\sqrt{2}}, \\\\\nH&=(\\pi \\hbar/t)|h\\rangle\\langle h|,\\quad t>0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe initial memory marginal is $I_2/2$. This total Hamiltonian is positive with global ground energy zero. Since $\\exp(-iHt/\\hbar)=I-2|h\\rangle\\langle h|$ and $\\langle h|b\\rangle=(1-1/\\sqrt{2})/\\sqrt{2-\\sqrt{2}}$, its actual unitary maps $|b\\rangle$ exactly to $|a\\rangle$. The final memory failure probability is zero. Its actual initial total energy satisfies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE t&=\\pi \\hbar\\, |\\langle h|b\\rangle|^{2} \\\\\n&=(\\pi \\hbar/2)(1-1/\\sqrt{2}) \\\\\n&< \\pi \\hbar/4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus uniform reduced memory alone would violate the claimed product-input pi/4 bound. The joint preparation is entangled and its correlations are consumed, so it does not satisfy the theorem's initial product premise or supply an independent fresh-resource reset cycle. Initially classical block-diagonal memory/resource correlations can instead be treated sectorwise if their actual energies and probabilities are retained; that extension is distinct from an independent fresh-resource reset.\n\n---\n\n#### Q.0.9.4 Conditional Algebraic Cancellation\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q010e-conditional-cancellation\"></a>**Proposition Q.0.10e (Conditional Cancellation).** Under the full hypotheses of Theorem Q.0.10,\n$$\n\\frac{\\kappa_A\\ln2}{k_BT_U\\ln2}\n=\\frac{\\kappa_A}{\\hbar}\\frac{2\\pi c}{a}.\n$$\nThe cancellation is algebraic. It does not show that Unruh thermality, Landauer reset heat, and a mechanical action have a common microscopic origin. ∎\n\n#### Q.0.9.5 Limiting Cases\n\n| Acceleration on the $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$ branch | Conditional $\\tau_U$ | Conditional rate (if realized) | Comparison regime |\n|:-------------|:--------------------|:---------------------|:----------------|\n| $a \\to 0$ | $\\tau_U \\to \\infty$ | $\\dot{N}_U \\to 0$ | Inertial limit: Unruh bath vanishes |\n| $a = c^2/L_P$ | $\\tau_U = 2\\pi t_P$ | $\\dot{N}_U = 1/(2\\pi t_P)$ | Planck acceleration |\n| $a = g \\approx 9.8$ m/s² | $\\tau_U \\approx 1.92 \\times 10^{8}$ s | $\\dot{N}_U \\approx 5.2 \\times 10^{-9}$ Hz | Earth surface gravity |\n\n<a id=\"remark-q010f-inertial-limit\"></a>**Remark Q.0.10f (Inertial Limit).** At $a=0$ the Unruh bath is absent, so Theorem Q.0.10 does not apply. A separate thermal bath fixes a Landauer heat scale but no cycle duration without an independent action-duration or control law.\n\n<a id=\"remark-q010g-earth-gravity\"></a>**Remark Q.0.10g: Earth Gravity.** At Earth's surface gravity ($g \\approx 9.8$ m/s²), the Unruh temperature is:\n\n$$T_U = \\frac{\\hbar g}{2\\pi k_B c} \\approx 4.0 \\times 10^{-20} \\text{ K}$$\n\nOn the full branch of Theorem Q.0.10 with $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$, the same acceleration gives $\\tau_U=2\\pi c/g\\approx1.92\\times10^{8}$ s, the value tabulated in Section Q.0.9.5.\n\n---\n\n#### Q.0.9.6 Connection to Horizon Information Bounds\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q010h-consistency-with-the-bekenstein-inequality\"></a>**Proposition Q.0.10h (Consistency with the Bekenstein Inequality).** Assume the Bekenstein bound in nat units,\n$$\nI\\le\\frac{2\\pi ER}{\\hbar c},\n$$\nand insert the comparison scales\n$$\nE=k_BT_U\\ln2,\n\\qquad\nR=\\frac{c^2}{a}.\n$$\nThen\n$$\nI\n\\le\n\\frac{2\\pi(k_BT_U\\ln2)(c^2/a)}{\\hbar c}\n=\n\\ln2.\n$$\n\n*Proof.* Substitute $T_U=\\hbar a/(2\\pi k_Bc)$; every dimensional factor cancels and leaves the stated upper bound. Equality requires independent saturation of the Bekenstein inequality and is not implied by the scale substitution. ∎\n\n---\n\n#### Q.0.9.7 Summary\n\nUnder four independent calibration conditions, the model assigns one acceleration-based duration to a uniform one-bit reset cycle. The result is a consistency relation between the registered scales.\n\n##### Technical status ledger\n\nUnder the full independent hypotheses of Theorem Q.0.10—uniform binary reset, exact Landauer saturation, cyclic energy balance, and the action-duration calibration with $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$—the conditional timescale is $\\tau_U=2\\pi c/a$.\n\nThe cancellation of $\\hbar$, $k_B$, and $\\ln2$ is algebraic (Proposition Q.0.10e), and Proposition Q.0.10h is a consistency comparison on the same conditional branch.\n\n---\n\n### Q.0.9.8 Conditional Comparison with Emergent Gravity\n\nThe branch-conditional Rindler–Landauer timescale $\\tau_U=2\\pi c/a$ shares formal ingredients with the emergent-gravity ledger. This section compares those ingredients.\n\n---\n\n#### Q.0.9.8.1 The Shared Structure\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q011-shared-ingredient-comparison-for-horizon-physics\"></a>**Proposition Q.0.11 (Shared-Ingredient Comparison for Horizon Physics).** *On their separately declared branch packages, the Jacobson and conditional Rindler–Landauer constructions use formally corresponding thermal and information-theoretic ingredients. This comparison does not identify their microscopic mechanisms or make either construction a derivation of the other:*\n\n| Jacobson construction | Conditional Rindler–Landauer construction | Shared formal ingredient |\n|:--------------------|:----------------------------|:--------------|\n| Unruh temperature $T = \\hbar\\kappa/(2\\pi k_B c)$ for acceleration $\\kappa$ | Unruh temperature $T_U = \\hbar a/(2\\pi k_B c)$ | Horizon thermal character |\n| Area-law entropy $\\delta S = \\eta \\, \\delta\\mathcal{A}$ | Registered reset: $\\langle Q_{\\mathrm{bath}}\\rangle/(k_BT)\\ge H_q(P\\mid R)$; $\\ln2$ equality only under the uniform-binary, no-copy, and zero-overhead hypotheses | Conditional reset ledger |\n| Heat flux $\\delta Q = T \\, \\delta S$ | Calibrated event step: $\\mathcal S=\\kappa_A\\ln2$ only under the independently assumed action calibration | Conditional action ledger |\n\n*The formula for $\\tau_U$ follows only after the saturation, cyclic-balance, action-duration, and $\\kappa_A$ calibration hypotheses of Theorem Q.0.10 are imposed; it is not a cancellation derived from SPAP.*\n\n*Proof.* The Jacobson derivation (Theorem 12.1) combines:\n1. Unruh temperature: $T = \\hbar\\kappa/(2\\pi k_B c)$ (Definition 40)\n2. Area-law entropy: on Theorem 49's saturated and derivative-controlled continuum branch, $\\delta S = [k_Bc^3/(4G\\hbar)]\\delta\\mathcal{A}$, with $G=G_{\\mathrm{op}}$ and a separate calibration to measured Newton $G$\n3. Clausius relation: $\\delta Q = T \\, \\delta S$\n\nThe Rindler-Landauer derivation (Theorem Q.0.10) combines:\n1. Unruh temperature: $T_U = \\hbar a/(2\\pi k_B c)$\n2. Landauer bound: $Q_{\\min} = k_B T \\ln 2$\n3. SPAP action: $\\kappa_A\\ln2$ for one stipulated structural step on a calibrated branch\n\nBoth constructions import the Unruh response and use separately declared entropy/energy ledgers. The Jacobson result additionally requires its local Clausius, area-density, and metric-action hypotheses; the Rindler–Landauer equality additionally requires the saturation and action-duration hypotheses of Theorem Q.0.10. Shared formal ingredients do not establish a common microscopic origin or logical dependence. $\\square$\n\n---\n\n#### Q.0.9.8.2 Conditional Schwarzschild-Horizon Protocol Rate\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q012-conditional-schwarzschild-horizon-protocol-rate\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.12 (Conditional Schwarzschild-Horizon Protocol Rate).** *Assume the complete hypotheses of Theorem Q.0.10: a conditionally uniform binary reset into the Unruh bath, exact Landauer heat saturation, cyclic energy balance, the independent action-duration equality, and the calibration $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$. Assume also that a real protocol realizes this duration and that an accepted horizon thermal/redshift bridge identifies the acceleration scale entering the conditional temperature formula with the asymptotically normalized Schwarzschild surface gravity $\\kappa=c^4/(4GM)$. Then:*\n\n$$\\boxed{\\tau_{BH} = \\frac{8\\pi GM}{c^3}, \\qquad \\dot{N}_{BH} = \\frac{c^3}{8\\pi GM}}$$\n\n*Proof.* Theorem Q.0.10 gives $\\tau=2\\pi c/a$ under the stated protocol hypotheses. Substitution of the registered scale $a=\\kappa=c^4/(4GM)$ yields\n\n$$\\tau_{BH} = \\frac{2\\pi c}{\\kappa} = \\frac{2\\pi c \\cdot 4GM}{c^4} = \\frac{8\\pi GM}{c^3}.$$\n\nThe realized protocol rate is its inverse, $\\dot{N}_{BH}=c^3/(8\\pi GM)$. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q012a-conditional-protocol-mass-rate-scaling\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.12a (Conditional Protocol Mass--Rate Scaling).** On the complete realized-protocol and thermal/redshift branch of Theorem Q.0.12,\n$$\n\\dot N_{BH}\\propto M^{-1}.\n$$\nThis is the scaling of the stipulated surface-gravity protocol branch; Hawking-temperature scaling alone supplies no computation rate. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q012b-conditional-numerical-protocol-values\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.12b (Conditional Numerical Protocol Values).** On the complete branch of Theorem Q.0.12:\n\n| Black Hole Mass | Conditional $\\tau_{BH}$ | Conditional $\\dot{N}_{BH}$ if realized |\n|:----------------|:------------|:---------------|\n| $M_\\odot$ (solar) | $1.24 \\times 10^{-4}$ s | $8.1 \\times 10^{3}$ Hz |\n| $10^6 M_\\odot$ (galactic center) | $1.24 \\times 10^{2}$ s | $8.1 \\times 10^{-3}$ Hz |\n| $10^9 M_\\odot$ (supermassive) | $1.24 \\times 10^{5}$ s | $8.1 \\times 10^{-6}$ Hz |\n| $m_P$ (Planck mass) | $8\\pi t_P$ | $(8\\pi t_P)^{-1}$ |\n\n---\n\n#### <a id=\"q0983-fixed-mass-conditional-rate-lifetime-scaling\"></a>Q.0.9.8.3 Fixed-Mass Conditional Rate--Lifetime Scaling\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q013-fixed-mass-conditional-rate-lifetime-scaling\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.13 (Fixed-Mass Conditional Rate--Lifetime Scaling).** On the complete realized-protocol branch of Theorem Q.0.12, fix an initial mass $M_0$ and assume a registered evaporation model\n$$\nt_{\\mathrm{evap}}(M_0)\n=\nC_{\\mathrm{evap}}\\frac{G^2M_0^3}{\\hbar c^4},\n\\qquad\nC_{\\mathrm{evap}}>0,\n$$\nwhere $C_{\\mathrm{evap}}$ records the particle-content and greybody conventions. Define the fixed-initial-mass surrogate\n$$\nN_{\\mathrm{sur}}\n:=\n\\dot N_{BH}(M_0)t_{\\mathrm{evap}}(M_0).\n$$\nThen\n$$\nN_{\\mathrm{sur}}\n=\n\\frac{C_{\\mathrm{evap}}}{32\\pi^2}S_{BH}(M_0).\n\\tag{Q.0.13.1}\n$$\nFor the explicitly adopted ideal blackbody coefficient $C_{\\mathrm{evap}}=5120\\pi$, this becomes\n$$\nN_{\\mathrm{sur}}=\\frac{160}{\\pi}S_{BH}(M_0).\n$$\nThe ideal coefficient is not a universal Page greybody/species coefficient, and $N_{\\mathrm{sur}}$ is not an integrated evaporation count or a proof of physical bit erasure.\n\n*Proof.* The conditional rate in Theorem Q.0.12 is\n$$\n\\dot N_{BH}(M_0)=\\frac{c^3}{8\\pi GM_0},\n$$\nwhile\n$$\nS_{BH}(M_0)=\\frac{4\\pi GM_0^2}{\\hbar c}.\n$$\nTherefore\n$$\nN_{\\mathrm{sur}}\n=\n\\frac{c^3}{8\\pi GM_0}\nC_{\\mathrm{evap}}\\frac{G^2M_0^3}{\\hbar c^4}\n=\n\\frac{C_{\\mathrm{evap}}GM_0^2}{8\\pi\\hbar c}\n=\n\\frac{C_{\\mathrm{evap}}}{32\\pi^2}S_{BH}(M_0).\n$$\nSubstitution of $C_{\\mathrm{evap}}=5120\\pi$ gives $160/\\pi$. An actual operation count would require a microscopic protocol and integration of its realized rate along $M(t)$. ∎\n\n<a id=\"remark-q013a-scope-of-the-rate-lifetime-surrogate\"></a>**Remark Q.0.13a (Scope of the Rate--Lifetime Surrogate).** The proportionality is conditional algebraic consistency. Establishing an actual horizon operation count requires a microscopic protocol, a particle-content/greybody evaporation law, and integration of its rate along $M(t)$; none follows from the area law or a lifetime formula.\n\n---\n\n#### Q.0.9.8.4 Algebraic Re-expression of the Gravitational Constant\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q014-algebraic-re-expression-of-g-on-the-joint-horizon-branch\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.14 (Algebraic Re-expression of $G$ on the Joint Horizon Branch).** On the joint hypotheses of Theorem Q.0.12 and the dimensionless Bekenstein--Hawking area law, solving the already calibrated identities for $G$ gives\n$$\nG=\\frac{c^3\\tau_{BH}}{8\\pi M}\n=\n\\frac{c^3}{4\\hbar}\\left(\\frac{S_{BH}}{A}\\right)^{-1}.\n$$\nThese are algebraic re-expressions, not an independent derivation or measurement of $G$.\n\n*Proof.* Theorem Q.0.12 assumes the Schwarzschild surface-gravity relation and yields $\\tau_{BH}=8\\pi GM/c^3$; solving gives the first expression. The area law already contains $G$ through $S_{BH}/A=c^3/(4G\\hbar)$; solving gives the second. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q014a-consistency-with-equation-e9\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.14a (Consistency with Equation E.9).** On Appendix E's capacity-achieving, entropy-saturating, additive-ledger branch, assume the same-family equality $C(\\mathcal E_N)=C_{\\max}$ and the independent calibration $G_{\\mathrm{op}}=G$. Identifying the limiting dimensionless horizon entropy density with $S_{BH}/A$ gives\n$$\n\\frac{S_{BH}}A=\\frac{\\chi C_{\\max}}{\\eta\\delta^2}\n$$\ninto the second re-expression gives\n$$\nG=\\frac{\\eta\\delta^2c^3}{4\\hbar\\chi C_{\\max}},\n$$\nwhich is Equation (E.9). This is a consistency substitution, not an independent derivation. ∎\n\n---\n\n#### <a id=\"q0985-joint-gravity-computation-consistency-ledger\"></a>Q.0.9.8.5 Joint Gravity--Computation Consistency Ledger\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q015-joint-gravity-computation-consistency-ledger\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.15 (Joint Gravity--Computation Consistency Ledger).** Assume simultaneously: (a) the local Rindler/KMS and Clausius hypotheses of Theorem 12.1; (b) the density-certificate hypotheses of Theorem E.3 and the capacity-achieving, entropy-saturating, additive-ledger hypotheses of Theorem E.6, together with the same-family equality $C(\\mathcal E_N)=C_{\\max}$ and the independent horizon-entropy and $G_{\\mathrm{op}}=G$ calibrations; (c) the MPU stress-energy source construction of Appendix B; and (d) the complete protocol hypotheses of Theorem Q.0.10 with $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$, a real protocol realizing the conditional duration, and the local identification $a=\\kappa$. Then the following statements hold on that joint branch:\n\n1. the Jacobson construction gives the Einstein equations (Theorem 12.1);\n2. the calibrated horizon entropy obeys the Bekenstein--Hawking area law in the continuum limit (Theorem E.6 on Theorem E.3's density branch);\n3. the calibration gives $G=\\eta\\delta^2c^3/(4\\hbar\\chi C_{\\max})$ (Equation (E.9)); and\n4. the independently realized conditional protocol has rate $\\dot N=\\kappa/(2\\pi c)$ (Theorem Q.0.10 and Corollary Q.0.10c).\n\nThese are compatible projections of the stated joint hypothesis package. No implication among items 1--4 is asserted after removing the hypotheses used to establish the target item.\n\n*Proof.* Items 1 and 2 follow from the local-Rindler, Clausius, horizon-saturation, density-certificate, and stress-energy hypotheses by Theorem 12.1 and Theorems E.3 and E.6. Equation (E.6c), the same-family capacity equality, and $G_{\\mathrm{op}}=G$ give item 3 algebraically. Under the independent reset, saturation, energy-balance, action-duration, real-protocol, and $a=\\kappa$ hypotheses, Theorem Q.0.10 and Corollary Q.0.10c give item 4. Each conclusion therefore follows from the union of the hypotheses declared in the theorem, without using any conclusion to prove one of its own antecedents. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q015a-no-closed-implication-loop\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.15a (No Closed Implication Loop).** On the hypotheses of Theorem Q.0.15, the gravity, area-law, coupling-calibration, and computational-rate ledgers are mutually consistent. The computational-rate identity alone implies neither the horizon entropy density nor the Einstein equations. $\\square$\n\n---\n\n#### Q.0.9.8.6 Conditional Conversion-Factor Cancellation\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q016-conditional-conversion-factor-cancellation\"></a>**Proposition Q.0.16 (Conditional Conversion-Factor Cancellation).** Assume the complete reset, saturation, energy-balance, action-duration, real-protocol, and calibration hypotheses of Theorem Q.0.10, including $\\kappa_A=\\hbar$ and $a>0$. Then the realized duration obeys\n$$\n\\tau_U\n=\\frac{\\hbar\\ln2}{k_BT_U\\ln2}\n=\\frac{\\hbar\\ln2}{k_B(\\hbar a/(2\\pi k_Bc))\\ln2}\n=\\frac{2\\pi c}{a}.\n$$\nThe cancellation is an algebraic consistency identity on this protocol branch. It does not imply that gravitational, thermodynamic, and information-theoretic theories are equivalent or that any output of Theorem Q.0.15 implies another.\n\n*Proof.* Substitute $T_U=\\hbar a/(2\\pi k_Bc)$ into the conditional action-duration equality and cancel the nonzero factors $\\hbar$, $k_B$, and $\\ln2$. This gives the displayed duration. No field equation, entropy-density law, or converse implication occurs in the calculation. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q017-conditional-four-dimensional-metric-uniqueness-on-the-joint-horizon-branch\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.17 (Conditional Four-Dimensional Metric Uniqueness on the Joint Horizon Branch).** Assume the complete joint package of Theorem Q.0.15 and the four-dimensional Lovelock hypothesis class. Then the Einstein equations already supplied by Theorem 12.1 are unique within that class. The conditional protocol-rate identity is compatible with the package but supplies no metric-equation selection.\n\n*Proof.*\n\n**Step 1 (Conditional information-bound comparison).** Proposition Q.0.10h gives only\n$$\nI\\le\\ln2\n$$\nafter inserting the declared comparison scales. Equality requires separate saturation of the Bekenstein inequality, and no rate or universal horizon protocol follows.\n\n**Step 2 (Independent entropy-density bridge).** The universal Bekenstein--Hawking entropy density is part of the horizon-saturation and area-law calibration package; it is not derived from the conditional protocol rate.\n\n**Step 3 (Jacobson construction).** The independently supplied entropy density, local Clausius relation, Unruh/KMS input, stress-energy flux identification, and remaining hypotheses of Theorem 12.1 give the Einstein equations on the joint branch.\n\n**Step 4 (Lovelock uniqueness).** Within the stated four-dimensional Lovelock class, the divergence-free second-order local metric tensor is the Einstein tensor plus a cosmological term. Thus Theorem 12.1's equation is unique in that class; the conditional protocol rate is not used in this uniqueness step. ∎\n\n---\n\n#### Q.0.9.8.7 Summary\n\nThe horizon and computation constructions use matching thermal, information, and rate ingredients on a joint branch. Their agreement is a compatibility result; none alone generates the others.\n\n##### Technical compatibility ledger\n\n| Result | Statement | Significance |\n|:-------|:----------|:-------------|\n| Proposition Q.0.11 | Jacobson and conditional Rindler–Landauer constructions share formal ingredients on separate branch packages | Comparison only; no common microscopic origin derived |\n| Theorem Q.0.12 | $\\tau_{BH} = 8\\pi GM/c^3$ on its black-hole protocol branch | Conditional black-hole computational timescale |\n| Theorem Q.0.13 | $N_{\\mathrm{sur}} \\sim S_{BH}$ on its evaporation-model branch | Conditional rate--lifetime surrogate proportionality |\n| Theorem Q.0.14 | $G$ is algebraically expressible through the calibrated horizon rate on its stated branch | Conditional gravity-computation relation |\n| Theorem Q.0.15 | EFE, the area law, the $G$ calibration, and the computational rate coexist on the joint gravity-computation package | Compatibility ledger; no reverse implication |\n| Proposition Q.0.16 | The declared conversion factors cancel to a geometric identity on the common calibration branch | Conditional consistency identity |\n| Theorem Q.0.17 | The Einstein equation supplied by Theorem 12.1 is unique within the declared four-dimensional Lovelock class on the full joint branch | The conditional protocol rate is compatible but supplies no selection |\n\nOn the joint branch of Theorem Q.0.15, the Rindler-Landauer rate is compatible with the local-horizon thermodynamic derivation and the area-density calibration. The cancellation of $\\hbar$, $k_B$, and $\\ln 2$ is an identity under the declared conversion and protocol hypotheses. The gravitational constant, horizon entropy, and computational rate retain their respective bridge and calibration premises; none of the three quantities alone determines the other two or the Einstein equations.\n\n---\n\n#### Q.0.9.8.8 Effective Horizon MPU Count\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q018-effective-horizon-mpu-count-on-the-combined-spacing-branch\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.18 (Effective Horizon MPU Count on the Combined Spacing Branch).** *Assume a Schwarzschild black hole of mass $M$ and the residual-budget, throughput-saturated, ideal-packing branch on which Equation Q.18 gives $\\delta^2=8\\ln2\\,L_P^2$. Then the horizon area measured in units of $\\delta^2$ is*\n$$\nN_{\\mathrm{MPU}}^{\\mathrm{eff}}(M)\n:=\n\\frac{\\mathcal{A}_H}{\\delta^2}\n=\n\\frac{2\\pi}{\\ln 2}\\left(\\frac{M}{m_P}\\right)^2.\n$$\nThe superscript “eff” is essential: this area ratio need not be an integer and, without a separate horizon-cell realization or tiling certificate, is not a count of microscopic horizon units.\n\n*Proof.* The Schwarzschild horizon area is\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}_H = \\frac{16\\pi G^2 M^2}{c^4} = 16\\pi \\left(\\frac{M}{m_P}\\right)^2 L_P^2,\n$$\nusing $m_P = \\sqrt{\\hbar c/G}$ and $L_P^2 = G\\hbar/c^3$. Equation (E.16) gives $\\delta^2 = 8\\ln 2\\,L_P^2$. Therefore\n$$\nN_{\\mathrm{MPU}}^{\\mathrm{eff}}(M)\n=\n\\frac{\\mathcal{A}_H}{\\delta^2}\n=\n\\frac{16\\pi (M/m_P)^2 L_P^2}{8\\ln 2\\,L_P^2}\n=\n\\frac{2\\pi}{\\ln 2}\\left(\\frac{M}{m_P}\\right)^2.\n$$\n$\\square$\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q018a-conditional-horizon-entropy-decomposition\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.18a (Conditional Horizon Entropy Decomposition).** *Assume the hypotheses of Theorem Q.0.18, the residual-capacity branch $C_{\\max}^{*}=2\\ln2$, and the dimensionless Bekenstein-Hawking normalization $S_{\\mathrm{BH}}=\\mathcal A_H/(4L_P^2)$. Then*\n$$\nN_{\\mathrm{MPU}}^{\\mathrm{eff}}(M)\\,C_{\\max}^{*}\n=\n\\frac{\\mathcal{A}_H}{4L_P^2}\n=\nS_{\\mathrm{BH}}(M).\n$$\n\n*Proof.* Equation (E.15) gives $C_{\\max}^{*} = 2\\ln 2$. Using Theorem Q.0.18 and Equation (E.16),\n$$\nN_{\\mathrm{MPU}}^{\\mathrm{eff}}(M)\\,C_{\\max}^{*}\n=\n\\frac{\\mathcal{A}_H}{8\\ln 2\\,L_P^2}(2\\ln 2)\n=\n\\frac{\\mathcal{A}_H}{4L_P^2}\n=\nS_{\\mathrm{BH}}(M).\n$$\nAt $M = m_P$, this gives $N_{\\mathrm{MPU}}^{\\mathrm{eff}}(m_P) = 2\\pi/\\ln 2$ and $S_{\\mathrm{BH}}(m_P) = 4\\pi$. $\\square$\n\n#### Q.0.9.8.9 Conditional Landauer Horizon Spectroscopy for Horizon Closed Loops\n\n<a id=\"definition-q07u-horizon-closed-loop-transfer-record\"></a>**Definition Q.0.7u (Horizon Closed-Loop Transfer Record).** A horizon scattering mode lies on the horizon closed-loop transfer branch exactly when the branch supplies an accepted finite record\n$$\n\\mathfrak T_{\\mathrm{hor}}\n=\n\\left(\n\\mathfrak H_n^{\\mathrm{ret}},\n\\mathcal S_\\omega,\n\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{scat}},\nT_H,\n\\tau_H,\n\\Theta_{\\mathrm{dyn}},\n\\Theta_{\\mathrm{MPU}},\n\\mathcal D_{\\mathrm{loop}},\n\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{peak}},\n\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}},\n\\chi_{\\mathrm{T}}\n\\right)\n\\tag{Q.0.7u.1}\n$$\nwith the following entries:\n\n1. $\\mathfrak H_n^{\\mathrm{ret}}$ is the retained finite-response horizon channel of Definition E.9.5d.\n2. $\\mathcal S_\\omega$ is the finite horizon scattering data and mode domain on which the transfer map is claimed.\n3. $\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{scat}}$ is the retained scattering map from near-horizon loop data to the reduced thermal phase coordinate.\n4. $T_H$ is the Hawking temperature in the accepted metric branch, and the normalized thermal coordinate is derived as\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\hbar\\omega}{k_BT_H}.\n\\tag{Q.0.7u.2}\n$$\n5. $\\tau_H$ is the near-horizon loop time certified by the scattering record and satisfies\n$$\nx=\\omega\\tau_H,\n\\qquad\n\\tau_H=\\frac{2\\pi c}{\\kappa}\n\\tag{Q.0.7u.3}\n$$\nwhen the branch uses the standard Rindler-Hawking normalization.\n6. $\\Theta_{\\mathrm{dyn}}=x\\ \\mathrm{mod}\\ 2\\pi$ is the dynamical loop phase.\n7. $\\Theta_{\\mathrm{MPU}}=N\\ln2\\ \\mathrm{mod}\\ 2\\pi$ is an independently registered structural phase contribution for an integer $N\\ge1$; its identification with the same closed-loop phase is part of the accepted transfer record, not a consequence of SPAP or reset heat.\n8. $\\mathcal D_{\\mathrm{loop}}$ is the transfer-map domain, including boundary conditions, single-valuedness convention, and finite resolution.\n9. $\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{peak}}$ is the peak-identification protocol and $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}}$ is the certified phase tolerance. These entries concern phase positions only; amplitudes, widths, greybody factors, and nonresonant envelopes are separate scattering observables.\n10. $\\chi_T$ records that the loop map, tolerance, and mode domain are fixed before spectral comparison.\n\nThe horizon closed-loop transfer branch is accepted only after the additional finite entries of $\\mathfrak T_{\\mathrm{hor}}$ are supplied and verified.\n\n<a id=\"lemma-q07v-thermal-phase-identity\"></a>**Lemma Q.0.7v (Thermal Phase Identity).** For any horizon with Hawking temperature $k_B T_H=\\hbar\\kappa/(2\\pi c)$ and Rindler-Landauer cycle time $\\tau_H=2\\pi c/\\kappa$,\n$$\n\\frac{\\hbar\\omega}{k_B T_H}=\\omega\\tau_H.\n$$\n\n*Proof.* Multiplying $k_B T_H=\\hbar\\kappa/(2\\pi c)$ by $\\tau_H=2\\pi c/\\kappa$ gives $k_B T_H\\tau_H=\\hbar$. Dividing $\\hbar\\omega$ by $k_B T_H$ gives $\\omega\\tau_H$. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q07v1-explicit-finite-lossless-same-loop-transfer-map\"></a>**Proposition Q.0.7v.1 (Explicit Finite Lossless Same-Loop Transfer Map).** Fix an accepted retained horizon channel $\\mathfrak H_n^{\\mathrm{ret}}$, a finite mode set $\\Omega$, and the Rindler--Hawking entries $(T_H,\\tau_H)$ of Definition Q.0.7u. On a one-dimensional retained scattering-mode fiber, define\n$$\n\\mathcal S_\\omega=e^{i\\omega\\tau_H},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{scat}}(\\mathcal S_\\omega)\n:=[\\omega\\tau_H]_{2\\pi}\n=\\left[\\frac{\\hbar\\omega}{k_BT_H}\\right]_{2\\pi}.\n\\tag{Q.0.7v.1.1}\n$$\nFor an independently registered structural label $N\\ge1$, let the same loop fiber subsequently acquire the controlled phase $e^{-iN\\ln2}$. The complete loop multiplier is therefore\n$$\nU_{\\omega,N}\n=e^{i(\\omega\\tau_H-N\\ln2)},\n\\tag{Q.0.7v.1.2}\n$$\nso the complete loop multiplier equals one exactly when\n$$\n\\omega\\tau_H-N\\ln2\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z.\n\\tag{Q.0.7v.1.3}\n$$\nChoose $\\mathcal D_{\\mathrm{loop}}=\\Omega\\times\\{1,\\ldots,N_{\\max}\\}$, and register the phase-acceptance predicate $\\operatorname{dist}_{2\\pi}(\\omega\\tau_H,N\\ln2)\\le\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}}$ on that domain before spectral comparison. This supplies a finite lossless abstract phase map and a tolerance predicate. On the one-dimensional fiber, $U_{\\omega,N}\\rho U_{\\omega,N}^{\\dagger}=\\rho$ for every phase, so trivial loop holonomy alone is not an observable constructive-interference or resonance-peak condition. An operational $\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{peak}}$ additionally requires a registered coherent reference and interference/readout certificate relating its measured response to that predicate, with the stated resolution and uncertainty. The retained horizon channel and its KMS/metric descent are also imported premises.\n\n*Proof.* The scattering multiplier is unitary and its argument is the first expression in (Q.0.7v.1.1); Lemma Q.0.7v gives the thermal-coordinate equality. Serial phases on the same one-dimensional fiber multiply, proving (Q.0.7v.1.2). A unit complex number equals one exactly when its phase is in $2\\pi\\mathbb Z$, proving (Q.0.7v.1.3). Scalar phases cancel in the density-matrix channel, which proves the stated readout limitation. The displayed finite domain and predicate are specified independently of spectral data; they do not construct the additional interference instrument. ∎\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q07v2-greybody-interferometric-readout-of-the-same-loop-predicate\"></a>**Proposition Q.0.7v.2 (Greybody Interferometric Readout of the Same-Loop Predicate).** Keep the data of Proposition Q.0.7v.1 and replace the lossless multiplier $\\mathcal S_\\omega=e^{i\\omega\\tau_H}$ by a registered unitary two-port on the loop mode $|l\\rangle$ and a loss mode $|\\ell\\rangle$ whose loop-to-loop amplitude is $\\mathcal T_\\omega e^{i\\omega\\tau_H}$, with greybody transmission $\\mathcal T_\\omega=|\\mathcal T_\\omega|e^{i\\varphi_\\omega}$, and whose loop-to-loss amplitude is $\\mathcal R_\\omega$, where $|\\mathcal T_\\omega|^2+|\\mathcal R_\\omega|^2=1$; the case $\\mathcal T_\\omega=1$ is (Q.0.7v.1.1). Add a reference mode $|r\\rangle$, prepare the single excitation $(|r\\rangle+|l\\rangle)/\\sqrt2$, send $|l\\rangle$ through the two-port and then through the controlled phase $e^{-iN\\ln2}$, recombine $|r\\rangle$ and $|l\\rangle$ on the balanced splitter $|r\\rangle\\mapsto(|+\\rangle+|-\\rangle)/\\sqrt2$, $|l\\rangle\\mapsto(|+\\rangle-|-\\rangle)/\\sqrt2$, and read out the projective measurement $\\{|+\\rangle,|-\\rangle,|\\ell\\rangle\\}$. With $\\Phi_{\\omega,N}:=\\omega\\tau_H-N\\ln2$ the outcome probabilities are\n$$\nP_\\pm=\\frac14\\Bigl(1+|\\mathcal T_\\omega|^2\\pm2|\\mathcal T_\\omega|\\cos(\\Phi_{\\omega,N}+\\varphi_\\omega)\\Bigr),\n\\qquad\nP_\\ell=\\frac12|\\mathcal R_\\omega|^2,\n\\tag{Q.0.7v.2.1}\n$$\nand the contrast is $\\mathcal K:=P_+-P_-=|\\mathcal T_\\omega|\\cos(\\Phi_{\\omega,N}+\\varphi_\\omega)$. Then:\n\n1. If $|\\mathcal T_\\omega|>0$ and $\\varphi_\\omega$ is registered, then for every $\\varepsilon\\in[0,\\pi]$\n   $$\n   \\operatorname{dist}_{2\\pi}(\\omega\\tau_H+\\varphi_\\omega,N\\ln2)\\le\\varepsilon\n   \\iff\n   \\mathcal K\\ge|\\mathcal T_\\omega|\\cos\\varepsilon.\n   \\tag{Q.0.7v.2.2}\n   $$\n   At $\\mathcal T_\\omega=1$ this is the tolerance predicate of Proposition Q.0.7v.1 as an observable threshold.\n2. Two greybody records with equal $|\\mathcal T_\\omega|$ and phases differing by an element of $\\mathbb R\\setminus2\\pi\\mathbb Z$ have the same loss probability and the same fringe visibility $2|\\mathcal T_\\omega|/(1+|\\mathcal T_\\omega|^2)$, while at fixed $\\omega$, hence fixed $\\mathcal T_\\omega$, $P_+$ as a function of the loop phase $\\Phi_{\\omega,N}$ is maximal exactly on $\\Phi_{\\omega,N}+\\varphi_\\omega\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z$. In a frequency scan, where $|\\mathcal T_\\omega|$ and $\\varphi_\\omega$ vary with $\\omega$, the normalized contrast $\\mathcal K/|\\mathcal T_\\omega|=\\cos(\\Phi_{\\omega,N}+\\varphi_\\omega)$, with $|\\mathcal T_\\omega|^2=1-2P_\\ell$ read from the loss outcome, attains its maximum $1$ exactly on that set; the raw probability $P_+$ has $\\omega$-derivative $\\frac12(1+|\\mathcal T_\\omega|)\\,\\partial_\\omega|\\mathcal T_\\omega|$ there, so its frequency peaks are displaced from that set wherever $\\partial_\\omega|\\mathcal T_\\omega|\\ne0$. Maxima of the normalized contrast therefore locate the Landauer grid of Proposition Q.0.19 only after the greybody phase is registered, and the grid appears displaced by $-\\varphi_\\omega$ in the thermal coordinate $x=\\omega\\tau_H$. If $\\mathcal T_\\omega=0$, then $P_\\pm=1/4$ for every $\\Phi_{\\omega,N}$.\n3. For $n$ independent repetitions with outcome counts $n_\\pm$, the estimator $\\hat{\\mathcal K}:=(n_+-n_-)/n$ satisfies $\\Pr(\\hat{\\mathcal K}-\\mathcal K\\ge s)\\le e^{-ns^2/2}$ and $\\Pr(\\hat{\\mathcal K}-\\mathcal K\\le-s)\\le e^{-ns^2/2}$ for $s>0$. For $0<\\vartheta\\le\\min\\{\\varepsilon,\\pi-\\varepsilon\\}$ put $s_\\vartheta:=|\\mathcal T_\\omega|\\min\\{\\cos(\\varepsilon-\\vartheta)-\\cos\\varepsilon,\\ \\cos\\varepsilon-\\cos(\\varepsilon+\\vartheta)\\}$. The rule that accepts exactly when $\\hat{\\mathcal K}\\ge|\\mathcal T_\\omega|\\cos\\varepsilon$ rejects a pair at phase distance at most $\\varepsilon-\\vartheta$, and accepts a pair at phase distance at least $\\varepsilon+\\vartheta$, each with probability at most $e^{-ns_\\vartheta^2/2}$.\n\n*Proof.* After the two-port and the controlled phase the state is $\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\bigl(|r\\rangle+\\mathcal T_\\omega e^{i\\Phi_{\\omega,N}}|l\\rangle+\\mathcal R_\\omega|\\ell\\rangle\\bigr)$, and balanced recombination gives the amplitudes $\\frac12(1\\pm\\mathcal T_\\omega e^{i\\Phi_{\\omega,N}})$ at $|\\pm\\rangle$ and $\\mathcal R_\\omega/\\sqrt2$ at $|\\ell\\rangle$. Squaring gives (Q.0.7v.2.1), and the three probabilities sum to one because $|\\mathcal T_\\omega|^2+|\\mathcal R_\\omega|^2=1$. For item 1, $\\cos(\\Phi_{\\omega,N}+\\varphi_\\omega)=\\cos\\operatorname{dist}_{2\\pi}(\\omega\\tau_H+\\varphi_\\omega,N\\ln2)$, the distance lies in $[0,\\pi]$, and cosine is strictly decreasing there; division by $|\\mathcal T_\\omega|>0$ gives (Q.0.7v.2.2). Item 2 follows from (Q.0.7v.2.1), since $P_\\ell$ and the visibility depend only on $|\\mathcal T_\\omega|$; differentiating $P_+$ along a frequency scan at $\\Phi_{\\omega,N}+\\varphi_\\omega\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z$, where the cosine is stationary, leaves the term $\\frac12(1+|\\mathcal T_\\omega|)\\,\\partial_\\omega|\\mathcal T_\\omega|$. For item 3, each repetition contributes an independent variable in $\\{1,-1,0\\}\\subset[-1,1]$ with mean $\\mathcal K$, so Hoeffding's inequality (Hoeffding 1963) gives both tails; on the two stated distance ranges, monotonicity of cosine places $\\mathcal K$ at least $s_\\vartheta$ above or below the threshold. ∎\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q07v3-sharp-characteristic-return-bound\"></a>**Proposition Q.0.7v.3 (Sharp Characteristic Return Bound).** The characteristic translation of Construction E.9.5f.29 has the following sharp one-time return bound, including its finite physical-source comparison. The retained longitudinal support and real elapsed time determine the bound independently of the KMS period. For any Hilbert space $\\mathcal K$, $a>0$, and norm-one $f\\in L^2(\\mathbb R;\\mathcal K)$ supported in an interval of length $W>0$, put $n=\\lceil W/a\\rceil$ and $T_af(y)=f(y-a)$. Then\n\n$$\n|\\langle f,T_af\\rangle|\\le\\cos\\frac{\\pi}{n+1}.\n$$\n\nThe bound is sharp, and the return-error, leakage and finite-source estimates below hold on their specified classes.\n\n*Proof.*\n\nLet $a>0$, let $T_af(y)=f(y-a)$ on $L^2(\\mathbb R;\\mathcal K)$, and suppose $f$ has norm one and is supported in an interval of length $W>0$. Put $n=\\lceil W/a\\rceil$. Translation of the interval does not change the overlap. Decompose the interval into residues modulo $a$. Almost every residue has a chain of at most $n$ occupied sites. On a chain of length $m$, the relevant compressed translation is the truncated shift $J_m$.\n\nFor every real $\\theta$, the Hermitian matrix\n\n$$\n\\frac{e^{i\\theta}J_m+e^{-i\\theta}J_m^*}{2}\n$$\n\nis diagonally unitarily equivalent to the path adjacency matrix with off-diagonal entries $1/2$. Its eigenvectors have components $\\sin(jk\\pi/(m+1))$, $1\\le j\\le m$, and its eigenvalues are $\\cos(k\\pi/(m+1))$. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Re}(e^{i\\theta}\\langle v,J_mv\\rangle)\n\\le \\cos\\frac{\\pi}{m+1}\\,\\|v\\|^2\n\\le \\cos\\frac{\\pi}{n+1}\\,\\|v\\|^2.\n$$\n\nIntegrate over residues, then maximize over $\\theta$. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\langle f,T_af\\rangle|\\le\\cos\\frac{\\pi}{n+1}.\n$$\n\nThe bound is attained by a common smooth bump supported inside a residue interval of width less than $W-(n-1)a$, copied to the $n$ consecutive translated cells with coefficients\n\n$$\nc_j=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{n+1}}\\sin\\frac{j\\pi}{n+1},\\qquad 1\\le j\\le n.\n$$\n\nThis construction is smooth and compactly supported. The endpoint case $W/a\\in\\mathbb N$ causes no correction: interval endpoints have measure zero. For $W\\le a$, the overlap is zero.\n\nConsequently the minimum vector return error after a freely optimized scalar phase and the leakage from the initial one-dimensional fibre obey\n\n$$\n\\min_\\theta\\|T_af-e^{i\\theta}f\\|^2\n\\ge 2\\left(1-\\cos\\frac{\\pi}{n+1}\\right),\n\\qquad\n1-|\\langle f,T_af\\rangle|^2\\ge\\sin^2\\frac{\\pi}{n+1}.\n$$\n\nEven without compact support, a nonzero normalizable vector cannot have unit return overlap at $a\\ne0$. Equality in Cauchy–Schwarz would imply $T_af=e^{i\\theta}f$; its squared modulus would be periodic with positive integral over some period, contradicting square integrability. This is a single-time statement, stronger on its particular operand than an all-time invariant-subspace argument, but its source class remains unchanged translation.\n\n**Finite physical-source comparison.**\n\nUse the actual massless Dirac call of Construction E.9.5f.50 with fixed boost duration $a>0$, normalized initial mode\n\n$$\nJ_{S,0}(y,z)=f(y+S)g(z)v_+,\n\\qquad \\alpha^1v_+=v_+,\n$$\n\nwhere $v_+$ is a unit vector, $\\|f\\|=\\|g\\|=1$, $g\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^2)$, $f$ is smooth and supported in a longitudinal interval of width $W$, and $b$ bounds $e^y$ on that unshifted interval. (E.9.5f.50.N2) proves, for the actual geometric Dirac generator $K$ and chiral generator $K_0$,\n\n$$\n\\|e^{-iaK}J_{S,0}-e^{-iaK_0}J_{S,0}\\|\n\\le d_S,\n\\qquad d_S=be^{-S}(e^a-1)\\|\\nabla g\\|_2.\n$$\n\nProposition Q.0.7v.3 therefore gives, with $n=\\lceil W/a\\rceil$,\n\n$$\n\\left|\\left\\langle J_{S,0},e^{-iaK}J_{S,0}\\right\\rangle\\right|\n\\le \\min\\left\\{1,\\cos\\frac{\\pi}{n+1}+d_S\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFor a finite unitary $U_{\\mathrm{fin}}$, normalized source input $j$ and isometric common realization $I$, suppose\n\n$$\n\\|Ij-J_{S,0}\\|\\le\\epsilon,\\qquad\n\\|IU_{\\mathrm{fin}}j-e^{-iaK}J_{S,0}\\|\\le\\delta.\n$$\n\nThen its actual same-fibre return amplitude $A_{\\mathrm{fin}}=\\langle j,U_{\\mathrm{fin}}j\\rangle$ obeys\n\n$$\n|A_{\\mathrm{fin}}|\n\\le \\min\\left\\{1,\\cos\\frac{\\pi}{n+1}+d_S+\\epsilon+\\delta\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe difference between the physical Dirac overlap and the chiral overlap is at most $d_S$ by Cauchy–Schwarz. The chiral overlap is exactly $\\langle f,T_af\\rangle$ because the normalized transverse/spin factors are unchanged. For the finite source, subtract the two overlaps after using the isometry $I$. The error from the first vector is at most $\\epsilon$ and the error from the second is at most $\\delta$. Both finite vectors have unit norm. Apply the triangle inequality and the universal unitary-overlap bound one. This proves the finite-source estimate. The leakage from the retained input fibre is exactly $1-|A_{\\mathrm{fin}}|^2$, so it has the corresponding squared lower bound.\n\nAt the actual thermal-period time $a=2\\pi$, a longitudinal packet of width $W\\le2\\pi$ has zero ideal return overlap and finite-source overlap at most $d_S+\\epsilon+\\delta$. This bound concerns the unchanged one-pass source; the return of Theorem Q.0.7v.4 comes from its added loop boundary.\n\nFor $f_\\lambda(y)=e^{i\\lambda y}\\chi(y)$ with real $\\chi$, the unmodified chiral return amplitude is\n\n$$\n\\langle f_\\lambda,T_af_\\lambda\\rangle\n=e^{-i\\lambda a}\\int\\chi(y)\\chi(y-a)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nIt is derived from the source packet and is generally attenuated. Its temporal Schrödinger sign is distinct from the positive spatial propagation phase $e^{i\\lambda L}$ used in the stationary graph scattering calculation. Those two conventions must be related explicitly by a port dictionary rather than identified by symbol alone.\n\n$\\square$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q07v4-explicit-horizon-lead-loop-scattering-model\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.7v.4 (Explicit Horizon-Lead Loop Scattering Model).** Take one invariant characteristic horizon channel of Constructions E.9.5f.29 and E.9.5f.50, with incoming one-particle space $L^2(\\mathbb R)$, generator $h_0=-i\\partial$ and its actual covariance $C_0=(1+e^{2\\pi h_0})^{-1}$. Attach the oriented lead–loop quantum graph specified below, with loop length $L>0$, $0<r<1$, $t=\\sqrt{1-r^2}$ and independently registered $\\sigma\\in\\mathbb C$, $|\\sigma|=1$. Then the explicit incoming wave operator is unitary onto the complete graph space; the graph dynamics and inherited quasifree reference have the same $2\\pi$ KMS normalization; and its per-pass complex scattering amplitudes, eventual reflection, internal resonances and finite echo errors are the expressions below. A smooth bounded finite-width chiral coupling profile derives $r,t$ and its additional phase and delay. The real Rindler travel time is determined by the independently specified return geometry.\n\nThis is a continuum quantum-graph source with its complete incoming field and loop sector. Its realization by the finite Dirac/material source and the joint Definition-E.9.5d/F.10.12a comparison remain the finite-source interface described after the proof.\n\n*Proof.*\n\n**Stationary source and amplitudes.**\n\nLet $0<r<1$, $t=\\sqrt{1-r^2}$ and $|\\sigma|=1$. Use an external oriented line, cut at zero, and an internal oriented loop $s\\in(0,L)$. The unitary vertex rule is\n\n$$\n\\begin{pmatrix}g(0+)\\\\a(0+)\\end{pmatrix}\n=\n\\begin{pmatrix}r&t\\\\-t&r\\end{pmatrix}\n\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&\\sigma\\end{pmatrix}\n\\begin{pmatrix}f(0-)\\\\a(L-)\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$\n\nOn a stationary solution of $-i\\partial_s$ with frequency $\\lambda$, normalize the incoming amplitude to one and write $a(s)=Ae^{i\\lambda s}$. With $z=e^{i\\lambda L}$,\n\n$$\nA=-t+r\\sigma zA,\n\\qquad\nS=r+t\\sigma zA.\n$$\n\nSince $|r\\sigma z|<1$, this gives\n\n$$\nA=-\\frac{t}{1-r\\sigma z},\n\\qquad\nS=\\frac{r-\\sigma z}{1-r\\sigma z},\n\\qquad |S|=1.\n$$\n\nThe internal same-pass retention amplitude is $r\\sigma z$ and its escape amplitude is $t\\sigma z$. The complete stationary one-lead reflection is all-pass; its modulus is not a greybody attenuation. Its internal intensity is\n\n$$\n|A|^2=\\frac{1-r^2}{1+r^2-2r\\operatorname{Re}(\\sigma z)}.\n$$\n\nFor $r>0$ it is maximal exactly at $\\sigma z=1$. If $\\sigma=e^{-iN\\ln2}$, this is the registered structural-phase condition $\\lambda L-N\\ln2\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z$. This calculation distinguishes the per-pass two-port amplitude from the eventual one-lead all-pass reflection.\n\n**Onto incoming wave operator and physical graph generator.**\n\nFor almost every $s\\in(0,L)$ let $f_j(s)=F(s+jL)$, $j\\ge0$, for the positive half of an incoming $F\\in L^2(\\mathbb R)$. Define\n\n$$\na_j=-t\\sum_{k\\ge0}(r\\sigma)^k f_{j+k},\n\\qquad\ng_j=rf_j+t\\sigma a_{j+1}.\n$$\n\nThe sums converge by Cauchy–Schwarz. They obey\n\n$$\n\\begin{pmatrix}g_j\\\\a_j\\end{pmatrix}\n=\n\\begin{pmatrix}r&t\\sigma\\\\-t&r\\sigma\\end{pmatrix}\n\\begin{pmatrix}f_j\\\\a_{j+1}\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix is unitary, so\n\n$$\n\\sum_{j=0}^M|g_j|^2+|a_0|^2\n=\\sum_{j=0}^M|f_j|^2+|a_{M+1}|^2.\n$$\n\nThe bound $|a_{M+1}|\\le(\\sum_{j\\ge M+1}|f_j|^2)^{1/2}$ makes the last term tend to zero. Thus the map from $(f_j)_{j\\ge0}$ to $(a_0,(g_j)_{j\\ge0})$ is isometric.\n\nFor arbitrary square-summable $(g_j)$ and arbitrary $a_0$, invert recursively:\n\n$$\nf_j=rg_j-ta_j,\n\\qquad\na_{j+1}=\\bar\\sigma(tg_j+ra_j).\n$$\n\nThe resulting sequence $a_j$ is the sum of the square-summable sequence $(r\\bar\\sigma)^ja_0$ and a convolution of $g_j$ with an $\\ell^1$ geometric sequence. Thus $a\\in\\ell^2$, $a_j\\to0$, and $f\\in\\ell^2$. Backward iteration recovers the displayed infinite series. This proves onto-ness. Integrating over $s$ and retaining the unchanged negative external half-line proves that $\\Omega$ is unitary from the incoming line onto the full graph Hilbert space.\n\nThe inverse relation also checks every boundary sign. If $F\\in H^1(\\mathbb R)$, then $a_0(L-)=a_1(0+)$ and the vertex equations hold. The positive-line interval endpoints glue continuously. Differentiation commutes with the bounded geometric series. Therefore $h=\\Omega(-i\\partial)\\Omega^*$ acts as $-i\\partial$ on each edge with precisely the stated boundary condition and is self-adjoint. Conversely the inverse gluing formulas give the corresponding domain.\n\nThis $\\Omega$ is the incoming wave operator. A compact incoming profile translated sufficiently far into the negative lead has no positive-half-line components. Its loop component and positive outgoing component under $\\Omega$ vanish exactly. Hence $\\Omega$ agrees with the natural embedding before that profile reaches the vertex. Density and unitary boundedness give the usual incoming asymptotic identity. Its onto-ness excludes omitted dark or bound modes for $0<r<1$.\n\n**Inherited KMS reference and physical-time convention.**\n\nIf the incoming horizon field has the actual covariance\n\n$$\nC_0=(1+e^{2\\pi h_0})^{-1},\\qquad h_0=-i\\partial,\n$$\n\nthen the graph state obtained by the incoming field realization has\n\n$$\nC=\\Omega C_0\\Omega^*=(1+e^{2\\pi h})^{-1}.\n$$\n\nUse the CAR convention\n\n$$\n\\alpha_t(a^\\dagger(f))=a^\\dagger(e^{\\mathrm{ith}}f),\\qquad\n\\alpha_t(a(f))=\\alpha_t(a^\\dagger(f))^*,\\qquad\n\\omega(a^\\dagger(f)a(g))=\\langle g,Cf\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThen $C$ defines the gauge-invariant quasifree $2\\pi$-KMS state for this physical boost flow. Indeed $Ce^{2\\pi h}=I-C$ on bounded spectral supports supplies the upper-strip two-point KMS boundary identity; the CAR and Wick expansion extend that identity to every finite analytic monomial. Bounded spectral test vectors are dense, so the identity extends to the CAR algebra. The state is inherited through the incoming scattering transform. No Fock-normal density matrix or trace-class Gibbs partition function on the infinite graph is required.\n\nThe input covariance must remain the full correlated horizon covariance. Temporal wavepacket bins generally have nonzero correlations. Replacing them by independent local thermal ancillas changes the source and does not follow from this construction. Likewise the Gibbs formula at this stage is a continuum graph statement. A finite matrix compression is not automatically invariant under the graph flow and its compressed covariance need not equal the Gibbs function of the compressed physical generator.\n\n**Finite echo errors and retained boundary defect.**\n\nTruncating the internal sum after $k=M-1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\|a_0-a_0^{(M)}\\|_{L^2(0,L)}\\le r^M\\|F\\|_{L^2(0,\\infty)}.\n$$\n\nFor the positive outgoing field,\n\n$$\ng(x)=rF(x)-t^2\\sum_{k\\ge0}(r\\sigma)^k\\sigma F(x+(k+1)L),\\qquad x>0.\n$$\n\nEach positive translation is a contraction on the half-line, hence\n\n$$\n\\|g-g^{(M)}\\|\\le t^2\\sum_{k\\ge M}r^k\\|F\\|\n=(1+r)r^M\\|F\\|.\n$$\n\nThe combined graph error is at most $\\sqrt{1+(1+r)^2}\\,r^M\\|F\\|$. For $F\\in H^1$, the same estimates apply to $F'$ on the separate edges. They give an edgewise $H^1$ estimate. The truncated fields generally fail the vertex boundary condition: direct cancellation in the finite sum gives\n\n$$\na_0^{(M)}(0+)+tF(0-)-r\\sigma a_0^{(M)}(L-)\n=t(r\\sigma)^M F(ML).\n$$\n\nThus the truncated field need not belong to $\\operatorname{Dom}(h)$, and the edgewise derivative estimate is not an operator graph-norm estimate for $h$. For an $H^1$ representative, the retained trace defect is bounded by\n\n$$\nt r^M |F(ML)|\\le t r^M\\|F\\|_{H^1(\\mathbb R)}.\n$$\n\nThe last bound follows from $|F(x)|^2\\le2\\|F\\|_2\\|F'\\|_2\\le\\|F\\|_{H^1}^2$, obtained by integrating the derivative of $|F|^2$ from a sequence tending to $-\\infty$ on which $F$ tends to zero. An exact finite experiment instead retains the final loop memory and every emitted lead sector; deleting the tail is an approximation, not an exact unitary source.\n\n**Source-derived finite-width coupling and complete phase.**\n\nA two-component co-propagating chiral segment with source Hamiltonian\n\n$$\nh_c=-i\\partial_s I-v(s)\\sigma_y,\n\\qquad v\\in C_c^\\infty((0,d);\\mathbb R),\n$$\n\nhas stationary propagation\n\n$$\n\\psi(d)=e^{i\\lambda d}\\exp\\left(i\\sigma_y\\int_0^d v(s)\\,ds\\right)\\psi(0).\n$$\n\nExtend $v$ by zero outside the segment and set $M(s)=\\exp(i\\sigma_y\\int_{-\\infty}^sv(q)\\,dq)$. Multiplication by $M$ is unitary, preserves $H^1(\\mathbb R;\\mathbb C^2)$, and direct differentiation gives $h_c=M(-i\\partial_s I)M^*$. This proves self-adjointness on that domain. The stationary formula follows by integrating the first-order equation; the matrices commute along this segment. For $\\theta=\\int v\\in(0,\\pi/2)$ the stripped scattering matrix is $B$ with $r=\\cos\\theta$, $t=\\sin\\theta$. The amplitudes are thus derived from a retained source coupling profile.\n\nLet $L_{\\mathrm{free}}$ be the return-path optical length excluding this common coupling segment. Its real delay $d$ enters the total loop length $L_{\\mathrm{tot}}=L_{\\mathrm{free}}+d$. Solving the same two boundary equations with the full segment matrix $e^{i\\lambda d}B$ gives\n\n$$\nA_d(\\lambda)=-\\frac{t e^{i\\lambda d}}{1-r\\sigma e^{i\\lambda L_{\\mathrm{tot}}}},\\qquad\nS_d(\\lambda)=e^{i\\lambda d}\\frac{r-\\sigma e^{i\\lambda L_{\\mathrm{tot}}}}{1-r\\sigma e^{i\\lambda L_{\\mathrm{tot}}}}.\n$$\n\nThe loop resonance uses $L_{\\mathrm{tot}}$, and the external common phase $e^{i\\lambda d}$ remains in its comparison with a coherent reference unless that reference traverses the same owned delay. Stripping this factor is a coordinate convention, not permission to omit its measured phase. Taking a point-contact limit with fixed $\\theta$ forces $\\|v\\|_\\infty\\ge\\theta/d$, since $|\\int_0^d v|\\le d\\|v\\|_\\infty$. The strength, real delay and source work/control ledger remain charged. This chiral matrix Hamiltonian requires its own realization in the original Dirac/material source for the finite transfer record of Definition Q.0.7u.\n\n**Real return geometry.**\n\nIn a Rindler radial section, null propagation satisfies $|d\\eta|=|d\\rho|/\\rho$. A reflection between boost-static radii $\\rho_a<\\rho_b$ therefore has round-trip boost time\n\n$$\nL=2\\log(\\rho_b/\\rho_a).\n$$\n\nThe equality $L=2\\pi$ is an independently fixed geometric condition $\\rho_b/\\rho_a=e^\\pi$ (modified by any separately present coupler delay). KMS periodicity supplies no equation selecting that radius ratio. Once the geometry is specified, the real travel time is derived rather than inserted as a nominal gate period.\n\nFor reference-clock radius $\\rho_*=c^2/\\kappa$, physical time is $s=(c/\\kappa)\\eta$, where $\\eta$ is boost time. Hence a loop with total optical length $L_{\\mathrm{tot}}$ has\n\n$$\n\\tau_{\\mathrm{loop}}=\\frac{c}{\\kappa}L_{\\mathrm{tot}},\\qquad\nk_BT_H=\\frac{\\hbar\\kappa}{2\\pi c},\\qquad\n\\frac{\\hbar\\omega}{k_BT_H}=\\frac{2\\pi c\\omega}{\\kappa}.\n$$\n\nDefinition Q.0.7u's required duration $\\tau_H=2\\pi c/\\kappa$ therefore holds exactly when $L_{\\mathrm{tot}}=2\\pi$; on that branch the real-time thermal-coordinate identity follows for every $\\omega$. If the radial free path has $L_{\\mathrm{free}}=2\\log(\\rho_b/\\rho_a)$ and a separate coupler adds $d>0$, that condition is $2\\log(\\rho_b/\\rho_a)+d=2\\pi$, with no double counting of a coupling segment already included in the radial length. The frequency relation is $\\lambda=c\\omega/\\kappa$. On the independently registered structural-phase branch $\\sigma=e^{-iN\\ln2}$, the internal resonance condition becomes $\\omega\\tau_{\\mathrm{loop}}-N\\ln2\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z$. The first-pass instrument of Proposition Q.0.7v.2 must use its actual complex per-pass amplitude and owned reference delay, and retain or separately resolve later echoes. The eventual reflection coefficient has unit modulus and is a different observable.\n\n$\\square$\n\n**Finite-source comparison.** Constructions E.9.5f.50, E.9.5f.51 and E.9.5f.53, Proposition E.9.5f.52 and Theorem E.9.5f.53a supply the selected finite one-pass horizon/KMS/common-code source. Theorem Q.0.7v.5 below extends that source by finite smooth material barriers and a static phase plate, with its actual clock, supports, retained controllers and output sectors, interaction/energy/work bounds and common field comparison. It supplies the actual changed generator/reference, overlaps and local flux/capacity record consumed by Definition E.9.5d and Definition F.10.12a on the asserted cover. The exact graph KMS statement of Theorem Q.0.7v.4 does not supply these finite comparisons, as the following compression constraint shows.\n\nA two-mode calculation identifies a specific finite-compression constraint.\n\nFix $\\beta=2\\pi$, $E>0$ and\n\n$$\nh=\\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\\\0&E\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\nF(x)=\\frac1{1+e^{\\beta x}},\\qquad\nv=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe physical generator compressed to the $v$ mode is the scalar $h_v=E/2$, whereas the restriction of its faithful quasifree reference has covariance\n\n$$\nc_v=\\langle v,F(h)v\\rangle\n=\\frac14+\\frac1{2(1+e^{\\beta E})}.\n$$\n\nSet $q=e^{\\beta E/2}>1$. Direct subtraction gives\n\n$$\nc_v-F(E/2)\n=\\frac{(q-1)^3}{4(1+q^2)(1+q)}>0.\n$$\n\n*Proof.* Substitute $e^{\\beta E}=q^2$. The common numerator of\n$(q^2+3)/(4(1+q^2))-1/(1+q)$ is\n\n$$\nq^3-3q^2+3q-1=(q-1)^3.\n$$\n\nPositivity follows from $q>1$. The full covariance is the physical Gibbs covariance by its defining functional calculus; restriction to the one-mode CAR algebra gives the displayed scalar covariance. Its compressed physical Hamiltonian is $\\langle v,hv\\rangle=E/2$. Thus the two quantities are explicitly unequal. $\\square$\n\nThe one-mode modular generator can instead be defined by\n\n$$\nh_{\\mathrm{mod}}=\\beta^{-1}\\log\\frac{1-c_v}{c_v},\n$$\n\nbut the strict inequality proves $h_{\\mathrm{mod}}\\ne E/2$. That choice changes the finite physical-time evolution. A legitimate finite source must provide the actual state/dynamics error comparison or an invariant finite realization; neither is supplied by writing a Gibbs formula after compression. The actual finite state/dynamics comparison therefore belongs to the new return-boundary source; the established selected source of Constructions E.9.5f.50, E.9.5f.51 and E.9.5f.53, Proposition E.9.5f.52 and Theorem E.9.5f.53a remains available.\n\nTheorem Q.0.7v.4 establishes the stated graph-level return, derived scattering and inherited KMS construction. Theorem Q.0.7v.5 supplies the finite-source realization and its separate state/dynamics comparison on the stated operational domain. The structural $N\\ln2$ phase retains its independent registration.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q-0-7v-5-finite-horizon-loop-source\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q07v5-finite-material-horizon-loop-kms-reference-and-readout\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.7v.5 (Finite Material Horizon Loop, KMS Reference and Readout).** In the prescribed-background, driven-material source class of Constructions E.9.5f.37, E.9.5f.48–51 and E.9.5f.53, Proposition E.9.5f.52 and Theorem E.9.5f.53a, the selected characteristic operational cover admits a finite-source horizon closed-loop transfer record of Definition Q.0.7u. Fix a finite spectral band, finitely many nominated mode centers, a finite structural-label range $1\\le N\\le N_{\\max}$, strictly positive comparison and phase tolerances, and a confidence level before spectral comparison. The construction has the following properties.\n\n1. Two smooth finite-width real mass barriers and a smooth static vector phase plate act on four identical Dirac information species. Their continuum scattering amplitudes follow from their specified differential operator. The actual finite pulsed source supplies its own matrix elements, with a certified comparison error to those amplitudes. Both leads, cavity occupation, spectator spin sectors, source controls, archives and all other carrier factors remain retained.\n2. The inner-face distance is $\\ell=\\pi$ and the phase-plate integral is $(N\\ln2+\\pi)/2$. At the registered Rindler clock radius $\\rho=c^2/\\kappa_H$, the geometrical round-trip time is $\\tau_H=2\\pi c/\\kappa_H$ and $\\hbar\\omega/(k_BT_H)=\\omega\\tau_H$. The exact finite-width continuum multiplier is $q(E)=G(E)e^{i(2\\pi E-N\\ln2)}$, with the source-derived complex greybody factor $G(E)=-r'_1(E)r_2(E)$. Its additional phase and dwell delay are retained; the geometrical round-trip time is not asserted to equal the full Wigner delay.\n3. The true static KMS reference, actual finite geometric Gibbs covariance, installed active/ambient reference and driven scattering preparation have distinct specified roles. Exact incoming active compressions preserve the characteristic regional maps. Explicit covariance-column, local regularity, finite-code, graph and cell estimates supply their required comparisons. The local Hilbert heat/capacity identity is tested before the incoming packet reaches a potential; a later no-return receiver retains the entire scattered ququart, reflected and transmitted branches together.\n4. Two reference quadratures measure the full complex reflection and recover the loop multiplier by a controlled rational inversion. Finite preparation, capture and observation tails, finite physical-source errors and registered confidence bounds enter the phase tolerance. The selected diagnostic clears the entire finite regulated field and reference-path field in their native basis, with full displacement archives. It requires no exact-rank decision on a backward-image span and does not assume time-isolated narrow-band echoes.\n5. The common source-first schedule is terminating at every fixed strictly positive tolerance. It includes the complete covariant action, source and current payers, finite reference installation, full-carrier response gap, and actual preparation, control, timing, storage and readout costs. Old retained outputs and their geometric tests are preserved by the full-carrier hold/swap construction and actual support exclusion.\n\nThe theorem establishes existence in the prescribed-background finite operational source class on the selected characteristic cover. The structural phase is an independent physical registration. Global gravitational and Wightman constructions and autonomous Maxwell–Dirac dynamics lie outside this theorem's domain.\n\n*Proof.* The nine parts below use the same physical profiles, clock, finite carrier, reference maps and error schedule. In this proof, E37, E44, E46, E48, E49, E50, E51 and E53 abbreviate the corresponding numbered Constructions E.9.5f.37, E.9.5f.44, E.9.5f.46, E.9.5f.48, E.9.5f.49, E.9.5f.50, E.9.5f.51 and E.9.5f.53 and their displayed local labels. Theorem 48a.10 is the geometry-first regulator theorem already used by that source chain. Every additional scattering, reference, regulator and readout argument is given here.\n\n##### Q.0.7v.5.1. Source and joint error schedule\n\nThe construction uses the exact finite two-barrier source. The perfect-reflector and graph limits are comparison results, not premises of this finite-source theorem.\n\n###### Required record and operational cover\n\nDefinition Q.0.7u and the readout of Proposition Q.0.7v.2 require an accepted retained finite horizon/KMS channel, finite source-derived scattering and complex greybody data, a real loop/thermal-clock dictionary, an independently registered structural phase, a finite domain and an actual confidence-aware interference/readout protocol. The source class and operational cover are those stated in the theorem.\n\nUse the same selected characteristic finite operational cover as E50: transverse cell unions with a fixed compact longitudinal incoming/crossing family, the existing normalized selector variations, and the actual local tube flux. Every barrier, source profile, clock, finite field mode, outer lead, cavity, preparation/readout factor and archive remains charged. The cover excludes arbitrary barrier-region cuts and general bulk diamonds. The full source dynamics is retained even though the local thermodynamic test cover is narrower.\n\n###### Fixed physical data\n\n1. Fix the accepted Rindler clock radius with the explicit calibration $\\rho=c^2/\\kappa_H$. Then the E50 energy unit is exactly $E_*=\\hbar c/\\rho=\\hbar\\kappa_H/c$, and $k_BT_H=E_*/(2\\pi)$. A branch using another reference radius must retain its actual Tolman/redshift conversion instead of this identity.\n2. Fix a finite spectral band containing the nominated mode centers and every allowed packet support, and a finite $1\\le N\\le N_{\\max}$. Fix all error/confidence budgets before any data.\n3. Use the appended four identical massless Dirac information species of the calibration service and a specified spectator-spin block; all other spin blocks remain physical. The old Page field/key/support sector is not changed. Extend only this appended calibration/scattering sector, retaining its input-independent relation to the old Page input and the complete all-sector hold during the old Page interval. Use two smooth real scalar mass barriers with zero-width target reflection moduli $1/2$ and $3/4$. Choose strictly positive finite widths from the explicit transfer bound so all scattering amplitude errors are at most $1/20$ over the band. No limiting wall is substituted.\n4. Fix the distance between the adjacent inner barrier faces to $\\ell=\\pi$ in logarithmic radial coordinate. Inside that length install a smooth static vector phase plate of integral $(N\\ln2+\\pi)/2$. Its width is included in $\\ell$. The full finite barrier phases remain in $G(E)=-r'_1(E)r_2(E)$.\n5. The exact finite-width continuum cavity multiplier is $q(E)=G(E)e^{i(2\\pi E-N\\ln2)}$. Its free geometrical duration is $\\tau_H=2\\pi c/\\kappa_H$. Source-derived greybody delay is retained in $\\arg G(E)$, not identified with zero or hidden in a fitted reference phase.\n6. Place the first barrier sufficiently far outside the horizon crossing tube. The access path and its physical phase are separately registered and transported into the fixed barrier-face port dictionary. They are not part of the internal loop length and are not dropped from a coherent measurement.\n\n###### Distinct programs on the same specified source\n\nThe following four objects remain distinct throughout:\n\n- The continuum prescribed static field has generator $K^V$ and covariance $C^V=F(K^V)$.\n- Its actual geometry-first finite matrix is $K_{V,n}$ with geometric Gibbs covariance $C_{V,n}=F(K_{V,n})$.\n- When exact incoming active restriction maps are used, the installed full finite reference is the separately specified active/ambient covariance with its actual logit Hamiltonian, purification and cancellation. It is not silently identified with either preceding covariance.\n- The driven physical scattering program is the actual finite ordinary matching circuit $U_h$ on its full carrier, with its actual finite matrix elements and readout instrument. It need not equal the stationary reference evolution exactly.\n\nThe finite transfer record contains the actual $U_h$ input/output matrix elements together with the proved packet, source, geometry, reference, matching, timing and readout error certificate to the derived continuum scattering formula. The formula is exact for finite-width material profiles, not claimed identical to a finite-duration finite-dimensional pulsed response. The registered tolerance consumes that actual difference.\n\nThe local capacity/heat calibration uses the complete E50-style displaced fixture, a compact incoming species ququart, and a final no-return exterior cut. Choose its finite call duration and tube-to-barrier distance using the causal-port inequalities. Keep the original local tube crossing/test window fixed after the entire incoming packet has passed. The full retained program may continue to a later loop/readout cut; the no-return bound ensures zero additional ideal logical tube flux before that cut. Choose the fixed local test endpoint $\\Delta<D-b$, before any logical support reaches a potential, so its energy cap is genuinely the free Hilbert cap. Any later vector-potential electric work and cap terms remain in the full source ledger; static coefficients are not an argument that they vanish. Its whole receiver retains both scattered leads and any residual cavity occupation. The selector variation gives $C_R(p)=pa_R\\ln4$ and tube heat slope $E_*\\nu a_R$ with $\\nu=\\ln4/(2\\pi)$. Reference correlations are displaced in both branches, not ignored.\n\nThe spectral phase measurement is a separately prepared and paid narrow-band diagnostic on the same static coefficients. It uses the two matched output modes and clears the entire actual finite regulated diagnostic field, including its reference path, in its native basis. This selected fixture contains all backward-propagated detector modes without a rank decision, as proved in §Q.0.7v.5.8. All other outputs are retained. It measures complete complex reflection by two reference quadratures, then uses the derived finite rational inversion to extract the complex loop multiplier and its greybody phase. It does not assume narrow-band echoes are temporally isolated. Fresh calls or the complete E53 swap sandwich preserve every old retained output and arbitrary external reference.\n\n###### Finite KMS/reference record\n\nThe exact incoming radial wave operator is identity on compact right-moving frames entirely before the potential. Hence their actual compressed KMS covariance is the same free characteristic covariance used by E50.N27. This preserves the declared active reference restriction maps on nested incoming catalogues. Full covariance columns differ; the one-sided thermal-distance bound is used wherever E46/E50 requires them.\n\nThe four-dimensional transverse error has both the explicit norm and boost-energy bounds in the causal-port lemma. The actual geometry-first cell matrix adds the source's onsite scalar/vector terms, and the bounded-potential regulator lemma gives finite-time, graph and Fermi-covariance column errors. Use the actual finite spectral buffer and the finite CAR/logit lifts only for the physical approximation at a fixed longitudinal stage, not a Gibbs formula after compression. The intrinsic longitudinal projection/modular limit remains the inherited E44 clipped-logit/fixed-test argument on the unchanged E50.N27 characteristic covariance: choose its spectral clipping and polynomial order before the longitudinal projection stage. It is not inferred by dividing the intrinsic projection tail by an uncontrolled shrinking full-active eigenvalue gap. After that stage is fixed, the independently adjustable physical separation, transverse, cell and control errors may be chosen below its actual finite gap. For an exact active-marginal implementation, retain E48.L8's actual dense logit controller, cancellation, purification and metric derivatives. The ambient changed-reference comparisons remain separate and charged.\n\nEach finite operational chart, overlap and triple overlap uses the actual incoming cell/species inclusion on this nominated algebra. Its exact active reference maps compose as in E50.N27. Physical raw-regulator representatives incur the stated source, Gram, covariance, gauge, clock and readout errors. No exact raw-region locality of the global encoding is inferred. The branch uses the operational split/finite-response record.\n\n###### Source-first error order\n\nAt a stage fix all input and detector mode frames, input/output access, transverse cells, support gaps, a reservation for the entire finite regulated diagnostic field plus its reference-path field and isomorphic archives, comparison scales, normalized selector increments and assembly weights first. The full diagnostic fixture is specified in the native basis after the actual finite regulator is fixed. Its entire dimension and isomorphic archive are retained; no backward-image span or exact rank decision is needed. Fix the finite barrier and plate coefficients and the reference-plane dictionary next.\n\nChoose finite smooth preparation/capture truncations and times from the actual Fourier/energy tail estimates. Choose the access distance and causal calibration duration with a strict no-return margin, and enough separation for the required local reference column bound. Then choose the near-horizon shift $S$ after the resulting time, packet derivatives, profile norms and transverse gaps, using the displayed norm and energy estimates. These choices may be very large and are charged.\n\nChoose the actual field-regulator box/mesh after the entire smooth source and test family. Use the bounded-potential stencil and Fermi-sine bounds on actual orbit columns, including all extra columns used by the reference/graph/capture tests. Normalize the finite frames with their real Gram errors. Select reference precision and active gauge/syndrome penalties after the actual finite diagnostic norms.\n\nFinally compute the delivery/scattering ordinary source's $A,D,B$ and all metric/current/clock first-variation constants, and choose the E48 matching step and timing precision from those actual bounds. This fixes its actual finite one-particle matrix $u_h$ on the good field sector. The **selected closure witness clears the entire finite regulated diagnostic field**, including the reference-path field, in its exact native finite basis. Its full dimension is known after the mesh is fixed. Reserve and charge all those modes and their isomorphic displacement archives; no rank decision on $\\operatorname{span}(u_h^*D)$ is needed. The boundary apparatus is predeclared and held outside delivery and old test windows, so its preparation does not change $u_h$ or the delivery constants. Clear only a fresh diagnostic service carrier, or use the complete E53 swap sandwich; never reset an old retained logical output. The input/detector frames use the original near-identity Gram bounds from 48a.10, not an exact rank oracle. The full-field preparation may grow in dimension and cost with the regulator. No ultraviolet-uniform normal-state limit of that driven empty-field diagnostic is claimed; the stationary KMS/Hadamard reference remains its separately proved state. Choose repetition count from the registered Hoeffding confidence. Every step is a terminating strict-tolerance selection because the explicit tails vanish with the corresponding parameter; no bound is assumed uniform in a previously changed source constant.\n\nFor local differential heat/capacity claims, use the exact E50.N32 order with the current positive regional scales and selector increment. In this source the actual output error includes the full-frame transverse rotation error, cell/Gram and gauge errors, matching error, coefficient/clock precision, capture and whole-receiver readout errors. The heat-slope error includes the displayed norm/graph cap bound, actual cell/cap residual, twice the complete scheduled-versus-unsplit current/work/boundary operator bound, and any nonzero coefficient/clock or support contribution. Source apparatus disjoint from the tested tube has zero tested contribution by its actual support, not by naming it a controller.\n\nChoose the common positive comparison scale $Q_R^{\\mathrm{ref}}=Q_*:=k_BT_H\\ln4$ for every region, including zero-mass regions. Thus $Q_{\\min,n}=Q_*$; no growing untracked energy scale is used. Let $Q_{\\min,n}>0$ be this smallest registered regional comparison scale, $r_n>0$ the actual selector increment, $W_n$ the finite consumed assembly-weight bound, and $\\eta_n\\downarrow0$. Choose the errors so that\n\n$$\nc_4(\\delta_{\\mathrm{out,n}})\\le\n\\frac{\\eta_n r_nQ_{\\min,n}}{2k_BT_H(1+W_n)},\\qquad\n\\max_R z_{R,n}\\le\\frac{\\eta_nQ_{\\min,n}}{1+W_n}.\n$$\n\nThe resulting consumed weighted physical heat/capacity tail is at most $2\\eta_nQ_*$ under this fixed-scale choice. The reference-column comparison separately includes its intrinsic characteristic projection tail, one-way barrier-distance tail, four-dimensional/radial thermal tail, actual cell covariance-column residual, Gram normalization, and the chosen gauge/reference installation errors. Each is fed to its actual finite tested norm or CAR lift before the resulting dimensionless Definition F.10.12a defect is allocated. Other consumed dimensionless defects receive a summable bound after their actual finite weights are fixed. Uniform channel-value convergence is not differentiated without its modulus divided by the selector increment.\n\n###### Complete retained channel and diagnostic gap\n\nAt each finite stage register the entire new tested ordinary carrier before the program, including every added source/control/readout factor assigned to that tested update. Let its actual dimension be $D$ and its chronological complete unitary be $U$. All new quantum factors are held on all sectors through the unchanged old Page interval; the full cancellation and later release are owned source operations, not ready-sector statements. Classical apparatus stress is excluded by support: park every new mass/vector source, support, preparation region, rod, clock and its full varied-action neighborhood outside the old compact geometric-test support. Activate/deploy the new profiles only after all old registered output/test windows, using owned finite timelike routing and actual onsite/profile ramps. Their preparation, rest stress, field energy, support forces and ramps are included in the enlarged source word and budget. The old geometric-test variations therefore vanish by actual support exclusion.\n\nOn the full tested algebra $M_D$, the response-null ideal for the complete separating grammar is zero. The retained automorphism is $\\operatorname{Ad}_U$. The actual predeclared raw partition is $\\mathcal H_T=\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{ext}}\\otimes\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{hid}}$, retaining the same two-sided buffered matrix-unit and hidden-store access dictionary and both leads/cavity when exterior. Set $d_{\\mathrm{hid}}=\\dim\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{hid}}$ and\n\n$$\n\\pi_{\\mathrm{hor}}(X)=d_{\\mathrm{hid}}^{-1}\\operatorname{Tr}_{\\mathrm{hid}}(X)\\otimes I_{\\mathrm{hid}},\\qquad\n\\ker_{\\mathrm{hid}}\\pi_{\\mathrm{hor}}=\\ker\\pi_{\\mathrm{hor}}.\n$$\n\nThis is the unital completely positive idempotent conditional expectation onto $B(\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{ext}})\\otimes I_{\\mathrm{hid}}$ on the actual full ordinary carrier. The Schrödinger exterior output is its actual partial-trace restriction, and the buffer/code argument proves the stated logical channel inside that raw output.\n\nRecompute E53's complete separating family $m=2D^2-D$ and its noninjective excluded-update menu on this actual carrier. Its analytic margin is $g_D=1/(72m^2D^6)>0$ for the registered dephasing comparison; use the conservative checked margin $g_D/2$ for the finite diagnostic record. Recompute every fresh-pack, table, processor, routing, clock, support, action and work reservation from this new $D$ and actual $U$ duration. The external diagnostic service remains a separate explicitly declared boundary role and is not silently included in its own self-certification.\n\nThe E53 full-carrier swap identity preserves the old source and arbitrary references at completed calls. Its actual support exclusion preserves old local tests. This supplies Definition E.9.5d's retained automorphism, expectation, hidden kernel and positive violation-cost entries with the updated carrier, rather than reusing the old dimension's numerical gap.\n\n###### Resource and source-action rule\n\nThe covariant scalar/vector source action, finite onsite cell matrices and full off-reference derivatives are specified in §Q.0.7v.5.7. The source belongs to the prescribed-background driven-material class specified in the theorem. All maintaining force/current Euler payers, including state-dependent terms outside the tube, remain in the complete work/current/boundary identity.\n\nThe finite storage, energy, time and work values are the actual dimensions and norms/integrals of the specified finite source and schedule, with the full E48/E53 reservations. The growing inverse radii, thin-barrier strengths, field gradients, field/regulator dimension, fresh detector fixtures, reference preparation, full archives, clocks and routing are not zero-cost assumptions. The positive response-violation coordinate supplies the stated diagnostic gap.\n\nThe proofs below use these same source profiles, reference maps, full carrier, regulator, source derivatives and diagnostic preparation. They establish the stated finite-source result.\n\n##### Q.0.7v.5.2. Finite radial barriers and loop phase\n\nAll phases below are physical complex amplitudes at explicitly stated barrier faces. The claims concern specified finite-band incoming scattering packets, not convergence of entire ultraviolet unit balls.\n\n###### 1. General mass phase and the registered structural factor\n\nOn the two-component radial block let\n\n$$\nh=-i\\sigma_3\\partial_x+M(x)(\\cos\\phi\\,\\sigma_1+\\sin\\phi\\,\\sigma_2).\n$$\n\nFor a thin smooth barrier of integrated mass $\\theta>0$ and width $d$, the zero-energy transfer matrix is\n\n$$\nP_0=\\exp[\\theta(\\cos\\phi\\,\\sigma_2-\\sin\\phi\\,\\sigma_1)]\n=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cosh\\theta&-ie^{-i\\phi}\\sinh\\theta\\\\ie^{i\\phi}\\sinh\\theta&\\cosh\\theta\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThus, with reference planes at its two faces,\n\n$$\nt=t'=\\operatorname{sech}\\theta,\n\\quad r_L=-ie^{i\\phi}\\tanh\\theta,\n\\quad r_R=-ie^{-i\\phi}\\tanh\\theta.\n$$\n\nFor every $|E|\\le B$, variation of constants gives\n\n$$\n\\|P_d(E)-P_0\\|\\le Bd\\,e^{\\theta+Bd}=:\\delta(\\theta,d,B).\n$$\n\nLet the first barrier have phase zero and strength $\\theta=\\operatorname{artanh}r$, $0<r<1$. The second has phase $\\phi_N=\\pi-N\\ln2$ and strength $\\Lambda$. Let the distance between their adjacent faces be $\\ell=\\pi$. In the thin-barrier, perfectly reflecting outer-wall limit,\n\n$$\nr_{1,R}r_{2,L}=(-ir)(-ie^{i\\phi_N})=r e^{-iN\\ln2}.\n$$\n\nThe exact multiple-reflection denominator tends to\n\n$$\n1-r e^{i(2\\pi E-N\\ln2)}.\n$$\n\nThis derives the same-loop structural multiplier from an explicitly registered material phase. It does not infer that phase from heat or KMS. The scalar/pseudoscalar source coefficients needed to implement this phase remain physical source entries. All other complex amplitudes, including the common $-i$ reflection phase and free reference-path phase, remain in the interference instrument.\n\nThe finite barriers do not have zero dwell time. Their complete energy-dependent matrices are used at every finite stage. The displayed denominator is the comparison limit, with a uniform finite-band error; it is not an exact formula for every finite source.\n\n###### 2. Contractive scattering bound, retaining the outer lead\n\nWrite $\\epsilon_j$ for a common bound on the four scattering-amplitude errors of barrier $j$ relative to its zero-width counterpart. The transfer estimate implies the conservative bound\n\n$$\n\\epsilon_1\\le(1+\\sinh\\theta)\\delta(\\theta,d_1,B).\n$$\n\nFor the outer barrier compared directly with a perfect reflector,\n\n$$\n\\epsilon_{2,r}\\le(1+\\sinh\\Lambda)\\delta(\\Lambda,d_2,B)+1-\\tanh\\Lambda,\n$$\n\n$$\n\\epsilon_{2,t}\\le\\delta(\\Lambda,d_2,B)+\\operatorname{sech}\\Lambda.\n$$\n\nAll exact scattering amplitudes are bounded in modulus by one, by the conserved Dirac current. Choose $\\epsilon_1\\le(1-r)/2$, and set $g=(1-r)/2$. Then on the whole band,\n\n$$\n|1-r'_{1,d}(E)r_{2,d}(E)e^{2iE\\ell}|\\ge g.\n$$\n\nThis is a uniform nonzero denominator. No resonance is dropped; the fixed nonperfect entrance coupler prevents a real-axis pole.\n\nFor incidence from the left, the total reflection and transmission are\n\n$$\nR_d=r_{1,d}+\\frac{t'_{1,d}r_{2,d}e^{2iE\\ell}t_{1,d}}\n{1-r'_{1,d}r_{2,d}e^{2iE\\ell}},\n$$\n\n$$\nT_d=\\frac{t_{2,d}e^{iE\\ell}t_{1,d}}\n{1-r'_{1,d}r_{2,d}e^{2iE\\ell}}.\n$$\n\nTheir comparison with the perfect-reflector loop obeys\n\n$$\n|R_d-R_0|\\le \\epsilon_1+\n\\frac{2\\epsilon_1+\\epsilon_{2,r}}g+\n\\frac{\\epsilon_1+\\epsilon_{2,r}}{g^2},\n$$\n\n$$\n|T_d|\\le\\epsilon_{2,t}/g.\n$$\n\nFor the right-moving internal cavity coefficient $A_d=t_{1,d}/(1-r'_{1,d}r_{2,d}e^{2iE\\ell})$,\n\n$$\n|A_d-A_0|\\le\\epsilon_1/g+(\\epsilon_1+\\epsilon_{2,r})/g^2.\n$$\n\nThe left-moving coefficient has the additional reflected multiplier, so its difference is bounded by $|A_d-A_0|+\\epsilon_{2,r}/g$. These follow by the elementary reciprocal difference identity and the modulus-one bounds. They retain both finite leads. In particular the nonzero outer transmission is not silently traced away when comparing complete states.\n\nThe constants are deliberately loose. An explicit convergent choice is $\\Lambda=n$, $d_1=e^{-n}$, $d_2=e^{-5n}$, with fixed $B,r$, starting after the entrance-error condition holds. Then every displayed scattering error tends to zero. Every finite width, strength, gradient and source norm is finite, but grows with $n$ and must remain in the cost and physical regulator schedule.\n\n###### 3. Wavepacket and barrier-occupation comparison\n\nFor a normalized incoming spectral packet $A(E)$ supported in $[-B,B]$, use the generalized stationary scattering solutions with Fourier convention $(2\\pi)^{-1/2}$. On each free edge the above coefficients are Fourier multipliers. Plancherel therefore bounds the full free-edge packet discrepancy by the sum of the corresponding amplitude suprema. Restricting a free-line packet to a half-line or to the cavity does not increase its norm. The bound is uniform in time, since multiplication by $e^{-iEt}$ is unitary on the spectral input.\n\nInside a barrier of width $d$ and strength $\\vartheta$, the ODE fundamental matrix has norm at most $e^{\\vartheta+Bd}$. If the incident face spinor is bounded by $C/g$ per unit incoming spectral amplitude, then Cauchy–Schwarz in the spectral integral gives\n\n$$\n\\|\\psi_{\\mathrm{barrier}}(t)\\|\n\\le \\sqrt{Bd/\\pi}\\,(C/g)e^{\\vartheta+Bd}\\|A\\|.\n$$\n\nOne can take a fixed conservative $C=4$ for the above one-channel incidence using $|A_d|\\le1/g$ and the corresponding reflected bounds. The barrier's occupation is thus a retained explicit error against an ideal graph embedding that has no barrier interior. With $d_2=e^{-5n}$ and $\\Lambda=n$, this contribution tends to zero. The same estimate applies to the entrance barrier with fixed $\\theta$.\n\nTo compare the graph and finite physical coordinates, keep the same cavity length between barrier faces, align each free lead by its physical face, and account for their finite coordinate shifts in the reference dictionary. A free phase shift over width $d$ differs from unity by at most $Bd$ on the band. No phase is removed by a hidden redefinition of the coherent reference.\n\nThe finite outer lead is an additional retained output. For two incoming lead families the same calculation is performed for right incidence; the comparison limit is the loop plus a separately reflected outer lead. Ultraviolet degrees concentrated in the shrinking barriers are not declared absent. They are outside the finite tested band and remain in the full finite physical carrier and reference.\n\n###### 4. Finite-band scope\n\nThe estimates give a quantitative stationary finite-band loop realization and an explicit finite material approach to its phase record. They supply no right to discard the barrier dwell phase at finite resolution. The phase/contrast error must be propagated to the actual finite packet/interference protocol using a lower bound on the measured contrast and the chosen finite frequency width.\n\nA compact smooth spatial preparation is not exactly bandlimited. If that preparation is used, retain both its norm spectral tail and its energy-weighted tail. Alternatively start with smooth compactly supported spectral packets and physically truncate their Schwartz spatial tails, charging the resulting norm and graph errors. The causal horizon-capacity calibration may use a separate compact spatial packet on the same source; its causal proof does not use the finite-band approximation.\n\nThe four-component embedding follows below. Sections Q.0.7v.5.3–7 establish the changed KMS reference, compatible finite records, whole-port heat/capacity comparison and ordinary source/clock implementation. Sections Q.0.7v.5.8–9 supply the finite readout and tail bounds used in the common schedule.\n\n###### 5. Selected four-component implementation: real masses and a static phase plate\n\nThe complex-mass option in §1 is not needed. Use two real scalar Dirac mass barriers. Between them, in a subinterval of the massless cavity, choose a smooth real static vector-potential coefficient $v_N(x)$, supported strictly inside that interval, with\n\n$$\n\\alpha_N=\\int v_N(x)\\,dx=(N\\ln2+\\pi)/2.\n$$\n\nThe radial operator in the cavity is $-i\\sigma_3\\partial_x+v_N(x)I$. Its exact transfer across the entire free-plus-plate cavity is\n\n$$\nD_N(E)=\\operatorname{diag}(e^{iE\\ell-i\\alpha_N},e^{-iE\\ell+i\\alpha_N}).\n$$\n\nThis follows by integrating the diagonal first-order equation. The total cavity width, including the phase plate, is $\\ell=\\pi$. The plate does not cause radial reflection in the ideal massless one-dimensional block. Its physical energy-dependent propagation phase has slope $\\ell$; its extra phase is energy independent. Thus in the ideal thin-barrier limit\n\n$$\nr_{1,R}r_{2,L}e^{2iE\\ell-2i\\alpha_N}\n=(-ir)(-i)e^{2iE\\ell-i(N\\ln2+\\pi)}\n=r e^{i(2\\pi E-N\\ln2)}.\n$$\n\nThe estimates of §2 remain unchanged after inserting the modulus-one plate factor. Every finite barrier's true dispersive phase remains in the same error bound.\n\nFor an explicit four-component representation take the usual $\\beta=\\operatorname{diag}(I_2,-I_2)$ and $\\alpha^1=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}0&\\sigma_1\\\\\\sigma_1&0\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)$. If $e_1,e_2$ is the standard two-dimensional basis, set\n\n$$\nv_j=2^{-1/2}(e_j,\\sigma_1e_j),\\qquad w_j=\\beta v_j.\n$$\n\nThe unitary with ordered columns $(v_1,v_2,w_1,w_2)$ sends $\\alpha^1$ to $\\sigma_3\\otimes I_2$ and $\\beta$ to $\\sigma_1\\otimes I_2$. A real scalar mass and the identity phase potential therefore produce two identical radial blocks. Choose and retain a fixed spectator-spin input; the other block remains in the actual carrier and reference. Transverse Dirac terms may mix these blocks, with the explicit norm/energy errors of the causal-port lemma. Four independent information species repeat this entire construction identically.\n\nThe phase potential is an actual source coefficient. A covariant realization uses a specified static external one-form $A=A_\\eta(x,z)d\\eta$ with the minimally coupled term $qA_\\eta=v_N$ in the boost generator. The nonzero charge $q$, field normalization and its material/power source are fixed before spectral comparison. The gradients of $A_\\eta$, its Maxwell/source energy, supports, boundary conditions and metric variations are not omitted or inferred from the transfer phase. It is not a gauge relabeling of the clock. A plateau in the transverse coordinates contains the full finite packet catalogue and its causal collar; smooth cutoff gradients lie outside the occupied protocol region, and their finite field/source costs remain charged.\n\nLikewise the real mass coefficient is the actual scalar profile needed to give the boost-frame potential $M(y+S)\\beta$. Its physical radial scaling, all profile gradients and finite energy costs belong to the source. The norm and graph estimates apply to $V_4=M\\beta+v_NI$, with $V_{\\max}$ the full sum bound. Staticness means zero explicit boost-time profile power during the stationary scattering window, not zero preparation/support cost.\n\nThe proper-time dictionary uses $E=c\\omega/\\kappa_H$, with $\\kappa_H$ the independently normalized horizon surface gravity. At $\\ell=\\pi$, the real ideal loop duration is $2\\pi c/\\kappa_H$ and its phase is $\\omega\\tau_H-N\\ln2$. The phase plate's geometric width is already included in $\\ell$, so it is not counted twice or removed. The finite material-barrier discrepancy is consumed by the finite-resolution transfer tolerance, not declared identically zero.\n\n##### Q.0.7v.5.3. Spectral completeness and normalization\n\nLet $h=-i\\sigma_3\\partial_x+M(x)\\sigma_1+v(x)I$ on $H^1(\\mathbb R;\\mathbb C^2)$, with real smooth compactly supported $M,v$. Bounded symmetric perturbation gives self-adjointness. This calculation supplies the spectral normalization for the finite-band packet estimates.\n\n###### Jost Green kernel\n\nChoose the left edge of the support to be positive for notational convenience. For $\\operatorname{Im}z>0$, let $u_+(x,z)$ be the solution equal to $e^{izx}e_+$ on the right free lead, and $u_-(x,z)$ the solution equal to $e^{-izx}e_-$ on the left free lead. Both are obtained uniquely inside the compact interaction by the first-order Volterra equation. Let $Y_z(x)=(u_+(x,z),u_-(x,z))$.\n\nThe resolvent kernel is\n\n$$\nG_z(x,y)=\\begin{cases}\ni u_+(x,z)e_1^T Y_z(y)^{-1}\\sigma_3,&x>y,\\\\\n-i u_-(x,z)e_2^T Y_z(y)^{-1}\\sigma_3,&x<y.\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nIndeed it solves the homogeneous equation away from $x=y$, decays at the required infinity, and its jump is $i\\sigma_3$, so $-i\\sigma_3[G_z(y+,y)-G_z(y-,y)]=I$. No nonzero square-integrable homogeneous solution exists at nonreal $z$ by self-adjointness, so the Green kernel is the resolvent kernel.\n\nOn the real axis the current $\\psi^*\\sigma_3\\psi$ is conserved. The finite transfer matrix is in $SU(1,1)$: its form is $\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}a&b\\\\\\bar b&\\bar a\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)$ and $|a|^2-|b|^2=1$. In particular the transmission denominator never vanishes on the real axis. The Volterra solution is analytic in $z$; on every compact real energy interval the upper/lower Green boundary kernels therefore exist and are continuous, uniformly on compact $x,y$ sets. There is no special zero-energy threshold for these massless first-order leads.\n\n###### Exact spectral jump\n\nLet $\\psi_L(x,E)$ and $\\psi_R(x,E)$ have unit incoming amplitude from the left and right respectively. On the left lead they have the forms\n\n$$\n\\psi_L(x,E)=\\binom{e^{\\mathrm{iEx}}}{r_L(E)e^{-iEx}},\\qquad\n\\psi_R(x,E)=\\binom{0}{t_R(E)e^{-iEx}}.\n$$\n\nCurrent conservation and the same transfer matrix give $|r_L|^2+|t_R|^2=1$.\n\nFor $x,y$ on this left lead the upper Green kernel has diagonal entries\n\n$$\ni e^{iE(x-y)}1_{x>y},\\qquad i e^{-iE(x-y)}1_{x<y},\n$$\n\nits $(2,1)$ entry is $i r_L(E)e^{-iE(x+y)}$, and its $(1,2)$ entry is zero. The lower boundary kernel is $G_{E-i0}(x,y)=G_{E+i0}(y,x)^*$. Subtracting proves on this open region\n\n$$\nG_{E+i0}(x,y)-G_{E-i0}(x,y)\n=i\\sum_{a=L,R}\\psi_a(x,E)\\psi_a(y,E)^*.\n$$\n\nThe diagonal jumps of the two Green functions cancel in their difference. Consequently both sides solve the same homogeneous first-order equation in $x$, and the adjoint equation in $y$, everywhere. Uniqueness propagates the equality from the left-lead open set to all $x,y$.\n\nStone's formula now gives, first for compactly supported smooth test functions,\n\n$$\ndP_h(E)(x,y)=\\frac1{2\\pi}\\sum_{a=L,R}\\psi_a(x,E)\\psi_a(y,E)^*\\,dE.\n$$\n\nThere is no omitted singular spectral part: the resolvent kernels are locally bounded up to every compact real energy interval on compact spatial test supports, as just proved. Their scalar spectral measures are therefore locally absolutely continuous by the resolvent/Stieltjes boundary formula. Compact smooth test vectors are dense. There is also no bound state: every real-energy exterior solution is a sum of constant-modulus plane waves; square integrability forces both coefficients to vanish on each exterior lead, and ODE uniqueness then forces the whole solution to vanish.\n\nThus the transform\n\n$$\n(\\mathcal{F}_h f)_a(E)=(2\\pi)^{-1/2}\\int\\psi_a(x,E)^*f(x)\\,dx\n$$\n\nis an isometry from the physical Hilbert space into $L^2(\\mathbb R;\\mathbb C^2)$ and intertwines $h$ with multiplication by $E$. It is onto: its range projection commutes with every bounded function of $E$, so is fiberwise. At every real energy the two scattering solutions are independent, since the finite transmission amplitude $1/\\bar a(E)$ is nonzero. Compactly supported test vectors hence span both fiber directions; the fiberwise range projection is the identity almost everywhere. This proves the unitary scattering transform and the inverse packet formula used in the barrier bounds.\n\nThe gauge-invariant quasifree static reference is therefore exactly\n\n$$\nC_V=\\mathcal{F}_h^*\\frac1{1+e^{2\\pi E}}\\mathcal{F}_h.\n$$\n\nIt is not the free covariance on the entire physical line. Its full thermal incoming channels have the same energy-dependent occupation, and its reflected/transmitted correlations remain present. Any finite compression or local replacement must retain the separate error/provenance record.\n\n###### Exact incoming active marginal, despite the changed full reference\n\nLet $h_{\\mathrm{free}}=-i\\sigma_3\\partial_x$, and let $J$ be any finite frame of compact smooth right-moving vectors whose supports lie strictly to the left of the potential. Define the incoming wave operator with the convention\n\n$$\nW_-=\\operatorname*{s-lim}_{t\\to-\\infty}e^{\\mathrm{ith}}e^{-ith_{\\mathrm{free}}}.\n$$\n\nFor every frame column $f$, backward free propagation moves its support still farther left. It never reaches the potential. The same characteristic uniqueness argument as in the causal-port lemma proves $e^{-ith}f=e^{-ith_{\\mathrm{free}}}f$ for all $t\\le0$. Hence every product in the limit equals $f$, and $W_-J=J$. Completeness gives $W_-^*J=J$ as well. Intertwining then yields\n\n$$\nJ^*F(h)J=J^*W_-F(h_{\\mathrm{free}})W_-^*J\n=J^*F(h_{\\mathrm{free}})J.\n$$\n\nThus the active incoming finite covariance is exactly the old free characteristic covariance. Its tensor extensions over identical species and transverse catalogue labels have the same property in the ideal radial model. Nested all-right-moving incoming catalogues therefore retain the exact covariance restriction maps of E50.N27, provided each current source's potential lies strictly to the right of the full catalogue and its stated comparison window.\n\nThe identity is one of compressions; the full covariance columns differ. In general\n\n$$\nF(h)J=W_-F(h_{\\mathrm{free}})J\\ne F(h_{\\mathrm{free}})J,\n$$\n\nbecause the Fermi function creates tails outside the compact incoming subspace. The separated-potential column bound, physical transverse approximation and actual regulator/ambient comparison must still be retained. No identity is asserted on arbitrary left-supported spinors, on the minus component, or on field observables that already straddle the barriers.\n\nFor precision in the onto argument above, choose a countable dense family of compact smooth test vectors. Their fiber spanning property holds on one common full-measure energy set. Independence of the two generalized eigenfunctions then forces the range projection to have rank two almost everywhere.\n\n##### Q.0.7v.5.4. Causal whole-port and heat comparison\n\nThe whole-port calculation uses neither even greybody probabilities, a shifted energy convention, nor a transmitted-branch capacity proxy.\n\n###### A. Exact one-dimensional statement\n\nLet\n\n$$\nh_V=-i\\sigma_3\\partial_x+V(x)\n$$\n\non $L^2(\\mathbb R;\\mathbb C^2)$ with domain $H^1$, where $V$ is a smooth bounded Hermitian matrix, zero for $x<D$, and compactly supported. The profile is static in boost time. Its strengths and derivatives are finite but are not required to be bounded uniformly over a source refinement.\n\nTake real $\\chi\\in C_c^\\infty((a,b))$, $a<b<0$, with norm one, and a real carrier $\\nu>0$. Set $f(x)=e^{i\\nu x}\\chi(x)e_+$, where $\\sigma_3e_+=e_+$. Choose\n\n$$\n-a<T<2D-b.\n$$\n\nThe exact evolved state $f_T=e^{-iTh_V}f$ is supported in $x>0$. This holds irrespective of barrier reflection probability or phase.\n\nProof. The free right-moving part initially follows $x=x_0+t$. Before a path encounters the potential at $x=D$, its minus component is zero. The first such interaction can occur no earlier than $D-b$. From there finite propagation speed one implies that the leftmost affected point at time $t$ is no smaller than $D-[t-(D-b)]=2D-b-t$. The unaffected free right-moving part has support in $[a+t,b+t]$. Thus for $t=T$ both lower bounds are positive. This can be proved directly from the two Volterra characteristic equations, by iteration: every term containing a potential insertion has its first insertion at $x\\ge D$ and time at least $D-b$, and subsequent characteristic segments have speed at most one. Bounded $V$ gives convergence of the Volterra series on every finite interval. It also proves finite propagation without a spectral or group-velocity approximation.\n\nThe same statement holds for every member of a finite frame supported in the same interval, and for the full frame with additional independent internal species or transverse labels.\n\nThe incident energy is\n\n$$\n\\langle f,h_Vf\\rangle=\\nu,\n$$\n\nbecause $V=0$ on the initial support and the real envelope derivative integrates to zero. Since $V$ is time independent, the total energy remains $\\nu$. No scalar potential, chemical-potential shift or even-scattering-probability argument enters this identity.\n\n###### B. Exact tube flux before any return\n\nPut a smooth cut $w(x)$ between zero and one, whose transition lies in $[0,c]$ with $0<c<D$. Choose a separate fixed tube-test endpoint $\\Delta$ after the entire incoming packet passes the transition, with $\\Delta<D-b$, so no part has yet reached the potential. The later final receiver cut may have $T>\\Delta$, with the strengthened no-return inequality $T<2D-b-c$. The potential vanishes on the cut. The local Dirac energy flux there is therefore the free flux. There is no ideal reflected contribution at the cut before $T$. Integration over the fixed early window $[0,\\Delta]$ gives $\\nu$ in boost-energy units, exactly as the free E50 computation. On this early window the entire logical causal support, and not only the cut, is in the free region.\n\nA sharp cut follows by a smooth-cut limit on the finite smooth solution. Alternatively free local energy conservation gives the same result from the initially empty and fully crossed exterior cap at the early time $\\Delta$, before any potential interaction. A post-scattering $\\langle h_V\\rangle$ cap is not automatically a Hilbert-energy cap for a vector potential. The symmetric Dirac energy density may be signed pointwise, but neither proof assumes pointwise positivity.\n\nStatic prescribed scalar mass profiles can exchange radial momentum with their supports. A static vector potential can also do electric work $F_{\\eta i}j^i$ on the matter even though $\\partial_\\eta A=0$. The conserved generator $h_V$ is the static canonical/Noether energy including its potential term, not automatically the matter Hilbert energy. All actual electric, medium, source-support, switching and Euler work/cap terms remain in the complete E48.L19 identity. The present heat proof avoids that substitution by using the early free tube window. It does not erase medium stress or claim bare commutator-current convergence.\n\n###### C. Whole spatial receiver and capacity\n\nUse four identical independent information species. At every stage include the entire finite incoming frame in a fixture, displace its old state and correlations to inaccessible archives, and prepare that fixture empty in both success/failure branches. Success with probability $p$ places one ququart into the four species of $f$; failure leaves the input in an inaccessible held store. Apply the identical field propagator in all species.\n\nBy part A the entire evolved fixture is in the exterior at the final cut. The exterior spatial projection therefore reduces that fixture. The actual full exterior output, including its input-independent side state, contains the species ququart on success and its orthogonal vacuum flag on failure. It is receiver-locally equivalent to the erasure channel $\\mathcal E_p^{(4)}$.\n\nFor transverse catalogue columns in separated cells, use the ideal tensor product source $h_V\\otimes I$ and one real transverse packet $g_v$. For a union $R$ of complete cells put $a_R=\\|P_Rg_v\\|^2$. Its entire spatial output is $\\mathcal E_{pa_R}^{(4)}$. The capacity per fresh independent call is\n\n$$\nC_R(p)=pa_R\\ln4.\n$$\n\nThe source calculation in B gives\n\n$$\n\\partial_pQ_R=E_*\\nu a_R.\n$$\n\nChoosing the phase gradient $\\nu=\\ln4/(2\\pi)$ before varying $p$ gives $\\partial_pQ_R=T_*\\partial_p(k_BC_R)$ at $T_*=E_*/(2\\pi k_B)$. The old constant-capacity Page bypass, if retained in the receiver, is added unchanged and cancels in the difference.\n\nThis argument includes both scattered leads, all residual cavity occupation and every exterior fixture mode. A receiver that keeps only transmitted modes has a different capacity and heat record. No statement here substitutes its transmission probability for $a_R$.\n\nThe thermal occupation of the displaced old fixture does not change the success-minus-failure flux. Its complete prior state and correlations were archived identically in both branches. The finite prepared difference is exactly the one-particle code covariance. Unoccupied modes and archives remain part of the complete carrier and budget, not an informal vacuum replacement.\n\n###### D. Finite observation of many cavity echoes\n\nLet the first barrier begin at $D$, with fixed cavity round-trip scale $L$. Choose a finite echo count $M$ and let $T_{\\mathrm{scat}}$ be a proved finite duration sufficient for that packet's scattering/echo observation, including pulse width and finite barrier corrections. The first arrival occurs near $D$. Choose $D$ large enough that a final time of the form $T=D+T_{\\mathrm{scat}}$ satisfies the inequalities in A and B. For example, with fixed $a,b,c$, any $D>T_{\\mathrm{scat}}+b+c$ satisfies the upper bound. The inward return cannot reach the horizon tube before the final cut.\n\nThe extra flight distance $D$ is an owned preparation/readout latency, not the loop time. The round-trip phase record must still derive its own $L=2\\pi$ relation, with the finite-barrier tolerance. Moving the horizon cut farther away does not change the internal cavity free distance or discard its greybody phase.\n\nThe observation time is finite, and the return remains in the retained carrier.\n\n###### E. Physical four-dimensional norm comparison\n\nThe intended actual boost-frame source is\n\n$$\nK_S=-i\\alpha^1\\partial_y+V_4(y+S)-ie^y\\alpha^\\perp\\cdot\\nabla_z,\n$$\n\nwhere $V_4=M\\beta+v_NI$ is the specified smooth bounded Hermitian radial scalar-mass and vector-potential matrix supported in a finite interval to the right of the incoming tube. Its material mass coefficient carries the compensating $e^{-y}$ factor. Fix the entire finite longitudinal catalogue and all transverse derivatives before $S$.\n\nFor the ideal source $K_{S,0}=-i\\alpha^1\\partial_y+V_4(y+S)$, finite propagation confines the occupied radial support to $y+S\\le b+T$. Duhamel then gives the explicit norm estimate\n\n$$\n\\sup_{0\\le t\\le T}\\|(e^{-itK_S}-e^{-itK_{S,0}})J\\|\n\\le e^{-S+b}(e^T-1)\\|\\nabla_z g\\|.\n$$\n\nHere the ideal longitudinal evolution is unitary and commutes with transverse derivatives. The estimate applies columnwise and hence by column-square-sum to the full fixture. Mass terms do not alter the maximum propagation speed. A separate graph/energy estimate must include the actual finite $V_4,V_4'$ constants; norm convergence alone is not the Hilbert-flux estimate.\n\nPart F supplies the graph-energy bound; §§Q.0.7v.5.5–7 give the reference, regulator and complete source current/work comparison. The norm estimate alone is not a heat or reference certificate.\n\n###### F. Explicit boost-energy graph bound\n\nThe separate graph bound in E can be obtained without differentiating an asymptotic scattering matrix. Put $V_{\\max}=\\|V_4\\|_\\infty$, $F_1=\\|\\partial_y f\\|$, $G_1=\\|\\nabla g\\|$, and $G_2=\\|\\Delta_zg\\|$. Use a real transverse envelope $g$. The initial longitudinal support lies to the left of the potential, so $\\|K_{S,0}J\\|=F_1$. If $\\phi_s=e^{-isK_{S,0}}J$, conservation of the ideal generator norm gives\n\n$$\n\\|\\partial_y\\phi_s\\|\\le F_1+V_{\\max}.\n$$\n\nLet $W=-ie^y\\alpha^\\perp\\cdot\\nabla_z$. On the radial support $y\\le b-S+s$,\n\n$$\n\\|K_{S,0}W\\phi_s\\|\n\\le e^{b-S+s}(F_1+1+2V_{\\max})G_1,\n$$\n\n$$\n\\|W^2\\phi_s\\|\\le e^{2(b-S+s)}G_2.\n$$\n\nThe first inequality follows by differentiating the factor $e^y$ and the longitudinal spinor in the kinetic term, then adding the bounded mass multiplication. The real transverse envelope makes the Clifford cross term vanish in the squared transverse derivative norm. The second uses $W^2=-e^{2y}\\Delta_z$.\n\nThe actual generator $K_S=K_{S,0}+W$ commutes with its own propagator. Applying it to the Duhamel integral, justified first on the smooth compact core, therefore gives\n\n$$\n\\sup_{0\\le t\\le T}\\|K_S(e^{-itK_S}-e^{-itK_{S,0}})J\\|\n\\le e_S,\n$$\n\n$$\ne_S=e^{b-S}(e^T-1)(F_1+1+2V_{\\max})G_1\n+\\frac{e^{2(b-S)}}2(e^{2T}-1)G_2.\n$$\n\nTogether with $d_S=e^{b-S}(e^T-1)G_1$, this is an actual norm-and-energy comparison. For a finite frame use the respective column-square-sum bounds and retain its actual occupation trace bound.\n\nThe ideal initial generator norm remains $F_1$, since its initial support is outside the mass. The actual ideal-orbit bound needed in the quadratic cap estimate is\n\n$$\n\\sup_{t\\le T}\\|K_S\\phi_t\\|\\le F_1+e^{b-S+T}G_1.\n$$\n\nThus the endpoint **generator-energy** quadratic-form discrepancy is bounded by the same algebraic expansion as E50.N22,\n\n$$\nE_*\\operatorname{Tr}|A|[d_S(M_S+e_S)+e_S],\n$$\n\nwith $M_S=F_1+e^{b-S+T}G_1$. For the physical Hilbert heat use this cap bound at the early $\\Delta<D-b$ window, where the whole logical support has not reached the vector/mass source and its generator-energy cap is the free Hilbert cap. Do not use a later post-scattering $K_V$ cap as Hilbert heat without the actual potential/current-work corrections. The final receiver support statement at $T$ remains a separate channel fact. For transverse cell-union tests, choose $S$ after their smallest positive gap collar, so physical finite propagation cannot reach the transverse test gradients during the call. The lateral logical flux and spin-boundary term then vanish there, as in E50.N22. For smooth non-cell-union tests the explicit E50 spin-boundary term must instead remain in the bound.\n\nThis is the finite transverse energy estimate for the specified static radial potential. The ordinary regulator, reference preparation and matching/clock first variations are established separately in §§Q.0.7v.5.5–7.\n\n###### G. Actual four-dimensional covariance comparison with finite transverse source support\n\nThe physically installed radial mass and phase plate have a smooth compact transverse multiplier $\\zeta(z)$ equal to one on a neighborhood of every catalogue support. The ideal radial comparison $K_{S,0}$ may be taken independent of $z$ as a mathematical intermediate, not an infinite-energy physical source. The actual source is\n\n$$\nK_S=-i\\alpha^1\\partial_y+\\zeta(z)V_4(y+S)-ie^y\\alpha^\\perp\\cdot\\nabla_z.\n$$\n\nOn every ideal orbit $\\phi_t(y)g(z)$, $(\\zeta-1)V_4\\phi_tg=0$ because the ideal orbit leaves the transverse support fixed. Thus the norm Duhamel bound remains exactly the one in E. For the one-generator estimate, $\\zeta=1$ on the supports of both $g$ and its derivatives, so the estimates in F also remain unchanged. The finite cutoff gradients and their field/source energy are charged even though they do not act on these comparison columns.\n\nFor both positive and negative times use the conservative radial support bound $y\\le b-S+|t|$. The sine-integral identity then gives, on a fixed normalized catalogue column,\n\n$$\n\\|[F(K_S)-F(K_{S,0})]J\\|\n\\le \\frac{2e^{b-S}G_1}{\\pi}(e^{T_0/2}-1)\n+\\frac2\\pi\\log\\coth(T_0/4).\n$$\n\nIndeed the finite-time sine difference is bounded by $e^{b-S}(e^t-1)G_1$ and $(e^t-1)/\\sinh(t/2)=2e^{t/2}$; the tail uses the norm-two difference bound. Choose the thermal cutoff $T_0$ and then $S$ after the actual finite catalogue derivatives. Combine the bound with the radial exact incoming compression and column-tail estimate, and finally with the actual geometry-first cell covariance column bound. This supplies the source-specific four-dimensional/radial covariance comparison without pretending the ambient perturbed reference is free.\n\n##### Q.0.7v.5.5. Local reference, spectral buffer and modular comparison\n\nThe following estimates concern the protected operational cover and the finite tested mode frames.\n\nLet $h_0$ and $h_V=h_0+V$ be self-adjoint first-order Dirac generators on the same line or complete collar, with propagation speed at most one and bounded self-adjoint multiplication potential $V$. Let $P$ be multiplication by an observation region separated from $\\operatorname{supp}V$ by distance $D>0$ in the propagation metric. Assume the free and perturbed dynamics both have that speed and share the same domain. Then\n\n$$\n\\|P[(1+e^{2\\pi h_V})^{-1}-(1+e^{2\\pi h_0})^{-1}]P\\|\n\\le \\frac{2}{\\pi}\\log\\coth(D/2).\n$$\n\nFor $|t|<2D$, the local propagators agree. For $t>0$, the Duhamel integrand of $P(e^{ith_V}-e^{ith_0})P$ is\n\n$$\niP e^{i(t-s)h_V}V e^{ish_0}P.\n$$\n\nIts right factor vanishes if $s<D$ and its left factor vanishes if $t-s<D$. Thus it vanishes for every $0\\le s\\le t<2D$. Negative times follow by adjunction.\n\nFor a real scalar $\\lambda$,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{1+e^{2\\pi\\lambda}}\n=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int_0^\\infty \\frac{\\sin(t\\lambda)}{\\sinh(t/2)}\\,dt.\n$$\n\nUse truncated functional calculus and the local cancellation before $2D$; the difference integral on $[2D,\\infty)$ converges in operator norm. Since the norm of each compressed sine is at most one,\n\n$$\n\\|P(f(h_V)-f(h_0))P\\|\n\\le \\frac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{2D}^\\infty \\frac{dt}{\\sinh(t/2)}\n=\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\log\\coth(D/2).\n$$\n\nOne can justify the scalar identity by expanding $1/\\sinh(t/2)$ into its positive exponential series and integrating, or by the elementary Fourier transform of $\\tanh$. For the unbounded functional calculus, the truncated scalar integrals are uniformly bounded (Dirichlet estimate); convergence is strong, sufficient to identify the norm-convergent difference after cancellation.\n\nFor a finite isometry $W$ with range inside $P$, the same estimate bounds $W^*f(h_V)W-W^*f(h_0)W$. If both finite covariances have spectra in $[\\eta,1-\\eta]$, $\\eta>0$, the logarithm integral representation yields\n\n$$\n\\|\\log(C_V(I-C_V)^{-1})-\\log(C_0(I-C_0)^{-1})\\|\n\\le \\frac{2}{\\eta}\\|C_V-C_0\\|.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalue buffer must be established for the actual chosen finite modes; it is not dimension-independent by default. The full-column and finite CAR estimates below supply the additional reference comparisons; §Q.0.7v.5.4 supplies the local heat/capacity calculation, and §Q.0.7v.5.7 supplies the source and preparation costs. The actual perturbed covariance remains $f(h_V)$, not $f(h_0)$ or $f(W^*h_VW)$.\n\n###### Full columns and exact incoming compression\n\nUnder the separated-support hypotheses, the one-sided Duhamel expression gives\n\n$$\n(e^{ith_V}-e^{ith_0})P=0\\quad (|t|<D),\n$$\n\nbecause the freely evolved range of $P$ has not reached $V$. Thus the stronger, full-column estimate is\n\n$$\n\\|(f(h_V)-f(h_0))P\\|\n\\le \\varepsilon_D:=\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\log\\coth(D/4).\n$$\n\nThis is the estimate required for the installed-versus-true covariance columns in E46.R24–R27; the earlier sandwich estimate alone is insufficient.\n\nThere is an exact compression statement for the radial incoming characteristic catalogue. Let $h_0=-i\\sigma_3\\partial_x$, $V$ a smooth compactly supported static matrix potential lying to the right of every catalogue support, and $J$ a finite isometry of right-moving smooth compact packets, with no left-moving component. For all $t<0$, $e^{-ith_0}J$ moves farther left. Both the free backward path and its perturbed inverse avoid $\\operatorname{supp}V$. Consequently\n\n$$\ne^{ith_V}e^{-ith_0}J=J\\quad(t<0),\\qquad \\Omega_-J=J.\n$$\n\nUse the separately proved completeness of the incoming wave operator $\\Omega_-$ for the actual two-lead source; then\n\n$$\nf(h_V)=\\Omega_-f(h_0)\\Omega_-^*,\\qquad\nJ^*f(h_V)J=J^*f(h_0)J.\n$$\n\nThe equality is one of compressions on this right-moving incoming catalogue, which $\\Omega_-$ fixes; the full covariance columns differ.\n\nAt every finite longitudinal stage choose $P_N$ from such compact incoming packets, before choosing the barrier distance. The active compression $C_N=P_Nf(h_V)P_N$ then equals $P_Nf(h_0)P_N$ exactly. Nested catalogue inclusions preserve these compressions exactly, even when successive barrier profiles differ, because the common characteristic reference is the same $f(h_0)$. Tensor with the unchanged transverse catalogue and four-species factor as in E50.N27. The actual scalar source is identical for all those species. The actual four-dimensional comparison and cell regulator add their separately proved errors; they are not erased by this radial identity.\n\nInstall the active covariance $C_N$ and the actual compressed ambient covariance on its complement, retaining the same source counterterm, preparation, derivative and purification accounts as E49.D25/E46.R20–R23. For $J$ in the finite active range, the installed covariance obeys\n\n$$\n\\widehat C_N J=P_N C_V J,\\qquad\n\\|(\\widehat C_N-C_V)J\\|\n=\\|(I-P_N)C_VJ\\|\n\\le\\|(I-P_N)C_0J\\|+\\varepsilon_D\\|J\\|.\n$$\n\nHere $C_V=f(h_V)$ and $C_0=f(h_0)$; the same formula is used in the common one-particle realization. The projection tail must be evaluated on the fixed tested frame and driven down before claiming a limit. Choose $N$ and its actual compact supports first, then choose $D$ after them; do not hold the barrier distance fixed while expanding the frame through it.\n\nThe exact source-relative modular matrices and cross-stage regional maps therefore remain those of E50.N27 on the declared characteristic transverse cover. The true static radial reference is still $C_V$, and its full column comparison uses the displayed tail. The full four-dimensional perturbed reference and physical cell source require the actual transverse propagation, covariance, frame and cell-regulator estimates, plus the finite variation norms of the enlarged source. The regional maps act on the declared characteristic transverse cover.\n\n###### Explicit finite-code spectral buffer\n\nLet $W$ be a finite isometry into $\\operatorname{Dom}h$ with $\\|hW\\|\\le H$. For any $E>H$, spectral Markov gives\n\n$$\nW^*1_{[-E,E]}(h)W\\succeq(1-H^2/E^2)I.\n$$\n\nMonotonicity of the scalar Fermi function then implies\n\n$$\naI\\preceq W^*f(h)W\\preceq(1-a)I,\n\\qquad a=\\frac{1-H^2/E^2}{1+e^{2\\pi E}}>0.\n$$\n\nThe upper bound applies the same lower-bound argument to $1-f(h)=f(-h)$. This is a finite-code bound with its actual $H$, not a dimension-uniform spectral gap. For an approximation error $\\varepsilon<a/2$, both actual and approximating covariances lie in $[a/2,1-a/2]$, so the logarithm bound above applies with $\\eta=a/2$.\n\n###### Finite CAR state and tested-energy comparisons\n\nFor two $m$-mode covariances $C,D$ with $0\\le C,D\\le I$ and $\\|C-D\\|\\le\\varepsilon$, take canonical doubled one-particle isometries\n\n$$\nV_C=\\binom{\\sqrt C}{\\sqrt{I-C}},\\qquad\nV_D=\\binom{\\sqrt D}{\\sqrt{I-D}}.\n$$\n\nEach has $m$ orthonormal columns. Their filled Slater vectors purify the respective gauge-invariant quasifree states on the first $m$ modes. The square-root bound $\\|\\sqrt A-\\sqrt B\\|\\le\\sqrt{\\|A-B\\|}$ follows from $A\\le B+\\varepsilon I$, operator monotonicity of the square root, and $\\sqrt{B+\\varepsilon I}\\le\\sqrt B+\\sqrt\\varepsilon I$, with the reverse inequality obtained by interchanging $A,B$. Thus each corresponding column differs by at most $\\sqrt{2\\varepsilon}$. Telescoping the $m$-fold exterior product and then tracing out the second $m$ modes yields\n\n$$\n\\tfrac12\\|\\omega_C-\\omega_D\\|_1\\le m\\sqrt{2\\varepsilon}.\n$$\n\nUse the minimum with one when desired. This is a finite-mode bound; its actual $m$ enters the error schedule.\n\nFor fixed smooth catalogue vectors $f,g$ whose supports lie in the protected region, locality of the differential $h_0$ preserves the supports of $h_0^jf,h_0^kg$. The sandwich covariance estimate therefore also gives the finite tested matrix-element bound\n\n$$\n|\\langle h_0^j f,(C_V-C_0)h_0^k g\\rangle|\n\\le \\frac{2}{\\pi}\\log\\coth(D/2)\\,\\|h_0^j f\\|\\,\\|h_0^k g\\|.\n$$\n\nNo global energy-weighted operator norm follows. Physical cap/flux comparisons instead use the explicitly proved graph-energy error on the actual finite code, complete success-minus-failure fixture clearing, and the source's full current/work/boundary identity. In the finite physical-cell bridge the column errors add to E46.R27 and are multiplied by its actual tested norms and dimensions before any limiting claim.\n\n###### Local regularity on the protected operational cover\n\nThe Hadamard property on the protected cover, where $V$ vanishes, is proved by a separate smoothing argument, since norm-small covariance columns carry no regularity information.\n\nLet $\\mu$ be a smooth compact spatial cutoff with support separated from $\\operatorname{supp}V$ by $D>0$. Local finite propagation gives $\\mu(e^{ith_V}-e^{ith_0})\\mu=0$ for $|t|<2D$. Choose a smooth even temporal cutoff $\\zeta$ equal to zero near zero and equal to one for $|t|\\ge2D$, with its transition contained in $|t|<2D$. Define\n\n$$\ng(\\lambda)=-\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int_0^\\infty\n \\frac{\\zeta(t)\\sin(t\\lambda)}{\\sinh(t/2)}\\,dt.\n$$\n\nThe odd extension of $\\zeta(t)/\\sinh(t/2)$ vanishes near zero and all its derivatives decay exponentially at infinity. It is a Schwartz function; hence $g$ is Schwartz. Causal cancellation gives the exact local identity\n\n$$\n\\mu(C_V-C_0)\\mu\n=\\mu[g(h_V)-g(h_0)]\\mu.\n$$\n\nFor each integer $m$, spectral calculus bounds $h^mg(h)$, and also $h^mg(h)h^m$. A spatial Dirac operator is elliptic on every compact collar at positive distance from the horizon. Applying local elliptic estimates on each side therefore shows that localized Schwartz functions of $h$ map arbitrary negative Sobolev order to arbitrary positive Sobolev order. Their kernels are smooth on the compact collar. The same is true of the displayed covariance difference.\n\nOn spacetime neighborhoods whose causal domains remain in the protected region, the two Dirac equations coincide. Propagating the smooth Cauchy covariance difference gives a smooth difference of two-point distributions there. The actual changed reference consequently inherits the accepted free reference's local Hadamard singularity on precisely this operational cover. This is a local statement about the actual $C_V$ reference, not an identification with $C_0$. Finite installed smooth-mode replacements preserve this local regularity; their finite mode and preparation costs remain in the source.\n\n###### Order of limits for the logarithm\n\nThe finite-code spectral buffer and logarithm inequality above compare physical approximations only **after the active frame has been fixed**. The expanding active logarithm is a separate limit, in which the product of an intrinsic projection tail with the inverse of its shrinking gap is not controlled.\n\nIn particular, the limit\n\n$$\n\\eta_N^{-1}\\|(I-P_N)C_0J\\|\\longrightarrow0\n$$\n\nis not used. The exact active reference remains $C_N$ and its expanding-frame modular action/form comparison uses the original E44 clipped-logarithm, log-resolvent and fixed-test spectral-tail estimates, transported as E46.R25. The intrinsic covariance-column projection tail enters E46.R27 and tested local flux with its actual fixed-test constants. Errors controlled by choosing the remote support, radial placement and physical mesh after $N$ is fixed may be made smaller than the particular finite gap; this does not turn the intrinsic $N$-tail into such an independently adjustable error. Keep these two limit arguments separate in the joint certificate.\n\n##### Q.0.7v.5.6. Geometry-first regulator for the actual source\n\nThe construction uses the cell averaging, zero-boundary stencil, norm and proper-time conventions of Theorem 48a.10.\n\n###### Actual finite source and consistency\n\nLet $K$ be the geometric Dirac generator in (48a.10-L2), and let $V(y,z)$ be a smooth compactly supported Hermitian spin/species matrix multiplication operator, fixed before choosing the regulator. Put $v=\\|V\\|_\\infty$ and $K_V=K+V$. Bounded perturbation preserves self-adjointness on $\\operatorname{Dom}K$ and the compact-smooth core. The principal hyperbolic part is unchanged, so finite propagation is unchanged; every compact-smooth orbit over a fixed finite time interval remains compact smooth. All derivative bounds below are those of this actual perturbed orbit.\n\nUse the original $I_n,E_n=I_n^*,P_n=I_nE_n$ and geometric $K_n$. Define the **actual onsite cell matrix**\n\n$$\nV_n=E_n V I_n,\\qquad K_{V,n}=K_n+V_n.\n$$\n\nDisjoint cell supports make $V_n$ block diagonal in the cell index; each block is the normalized cell average of the Hermitian matrix $V$. It is Hermitian and $\\|V_n\\|\\le v$. No spectral projection or state-dependent fitting is involved. Every original lattice mode, including high-frequency branches, remains in the source. The finite Fock Hamiltonian is $E_*d\\Gamma(K_{V,n})$, and its actual Gibbs covariance is\n\n$$\nC_{V,n}=(I+e^{2\\pi K_{V,n}})^{-1}.\n$$\n\nIt is faithful and exactly KMS at the original physical temperature. $V_n$ is an additional onsite coefficient in the already admitted ordinary-source construction; its physical action and first variations are the separate covariant scalar/vector source dictionary, not frozen matrix derivatives.\n\nFor compact smooth $g$ with the required box/stencil collar, (F.10.4b.8b-L5) and cell Poincaré give the explicit new residual\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|K_{V,n}E_ng-E_nK_Vg\\|\n&\\le r_n(g)+\\|E_nV(P_ng-g)\\|\\\\\n&\\le r_n(g)+\\frac{v a_n}{\\pi}\\|\\nabla g\\|\n=:r_n^V(g).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe identity in the first line uses exact cell averages $V_n$, rather than sampling $V$ at cell centers. It does not omit a potential-gradient error: the projection discrepancy is bounded before averaging. Potential gradients still enter the actual orbit's derivative seminorms and the physical source/variation ledger.\n\n###### Finite-time and generator-graph errors\n\nFor a fixed $f$ and $T$, define\n\n$$\nR_n^V(f,T)=\\sup_{|s|\\le T}r_n^V(e^{isK_V}f),\\qquad\nG_V(f,T)=\\sup_{|s|\\le T}\\|\\nabla e^{isK_V}f\\|.\n$$\n\nDuhamel in the averaged finite space, followed by cell Poincaré, gives\n\n$$\n\\sup_{|s|\\le T}\n\\|I_ne^{isK_{V,n}}E_nf-e^{isK_V}f\\|\n\\le T R_n^V(f,T)+\\frac{a_n}{\\pi}G_V(f,T)\n=:\\epsilon_n^V(f,T).\n$$\n\nThe finite source is unitary, so its large cutoff norm does not multiply the Duhamel residual. Every term tends to zero after $V,f,T$ and their support collar are fixed.\n\nFor the actual generator graph, use $K_Vf$ as an additional compact-smooth test vector. Commutation of each generator with its own unitary group then gives\n\n$$\n\\sup_{|s|\\le T}\n\\|I_nK_{V,n}e^{isK_{V,n}}E_nf\n -K_Ve^{isK_V}f\\|\n\\le r_n^V(f)+\\epsilon_n^V(K_Vf,T).\n$$\n\nThis gives the finite-code graph-energy comparison used with the separately specified finite variation tests.\n\nThe required orbit seminorms are finite with computable conservative bounds. In the finite causal support collar, write $K_V=-i\\sum_j A_j(x)\\partial_j+B(x)$ with its actual smooth coefficients. For each multi-index $\\alpha$ of order $\\le3$, differentiate the evolution equation. The principal $K_V$ term is norm preserving; commutators contain\n\n$$\n\\sum_{0<\\beta\\le\\alpha}\\binom{\\alpha}{\\beta}\n[(\\partial^\\beta A_j)\\partial_j\\partial^{\\alpha-\\beta}g\n +(\\partial^\\beta B)\\partial^{\\alpha-\\beta}g],\n$$\n\nwith irrelevant unit factors in the norm. Every derivative of $g$ on the right has order $\\le3$. Sum the corresponding coefficient suprema, binomial factors and finite multiplicities to obtain $C_3$; for the sum of the derivative $L^2$ norms this gives the conservative Gronwall bound\n\n$$\n\\sum_{|\\alpha|\\le3}\\|\\partial^\\alpha e^{isK_V}f\\|\n\\le e^{C_3|s|}\\sum_{|\\alpha|\\le3}\\|\\partial^\\alpha f\\|.\n$$\n\nUse coefficients on a known finite propagation collar, or a bounded smooth continuation agreeing there. For $K_Vf$ repeat the bound with its actual derivative norms (one extra initial derivative may be required). All thin-wall, radial and phase-plate derivatives are included in $C_3$. This is deliberately a finite, potentially very expensive bound, not a uniform thin-wall estimate.\n\n###### True covariance columns\n\nLet $C_V=F(K_V)$. The same scalar sine identity as (F.10.4b.8b-L9), the averaged Duhamel estimate before $T_0$, and the unitary tail after $T_0$ yield\n\n$$\n\\|C_{V,n}E_nf-E_nC_Vf\\|\n\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}R_n^V(f,T_0)\n +\\frac{2\\|f\\|}{\\pi}\\log\\coth(T_0/4)\n=:b_n^V(f,T_0).\n$$\n\nThis is a full covariance-column estimate for the actual changed field and actual changed finite matrix, not $F(E_nK_VI_n)$ substituted for $E_nF(K_V)I_n$. Choose $T_0$ first to control the exponential tail, then the box/mesh to control the actual perturbed orbit on that window. The potential remains fixed during these choices.\n\nFor a finite smooth frame $J$, Gram normalize $E_nJ$ exactly as in Theorem 48a.10. Its Gram defect, column normalization error, finite CAR state lift and all dimension factors are included before evaluating the target error. Noncompact columns such as $C_0J$ are first approximated by their actual norm tails on the fixed finite list, as in E46.R27; they are not treated as compact by assertion.\n\nCombine this bound with the separate radial incoming-reference theorem: the exact installed characteristic covariance has the unchanged E50.N27 active compression, while true radial columns differ by the actual longitudinal projection tail plus the one-way thermal distance bound. The actual four-dimensional/radial comparison adds its proved transverse and finite support terms. For each fixed tested frame, the physical-versus-installed covariance column therefore has the explicit triangle bound of E46.R27 with these new values substituted. The reference's purification, full logit matrix, gap-dependent derivative and counterterm work remain charged.\n\nThe finite-code positive covariance buffer proved in §Q.0.7v.5.5 can be applied before taking logarithms. Alternatively use the actual finite matrix gap with a certified enclosure. No uniform gap independent of frame or regulator is assumed.\n\n###### Terminating order and scope\n\nFreeze the mode/time/cover/variation list, finite source profiles and all comparison scales. Fix the required probability and covariance tolerances, then $T_0$, the physical support collar, the actual derivative bounds and a sufficiently fine geometry-first mesh. Evaluate $r_n^V$, $b_n^V$ and Gram errors on the finite expanded frame; all displayed bounds tend to zero at this stage. Choose the ordinary pair-local schedule only afterwards from the enlarged source's actual coefficients and first-variation constants. A change of source width, profile, code dimension or test list requires recomputing this schedule.\n\nThe estimates prove smooth finite-response and reference transfer for the prescribed bounded smooth scalar/vector profiles. The finite geometric Gibbs covariance and installed active marginal retain their separately specified dynamics and physical current/work/boundary accounts.\n\n##### Q.0.7v.5.7. Ordinary source, covariant action and costs\n\nThe following action extends E37/E48's prescribed, externally maintained material subclass.\n\n###### 1. Added covariant source terms\n\nUse the four identical Dirac species, the same material cells, proper clock and regulated mode/Gram/Berry construction. Add a real prescribed scalar mass profile $m$ and a prescribed real one-form $A$, with fixed nonzero charge $q$ identical across the information species. The matter action adds the standard scalar bilinear and vector-current coupling,\n\n$$\nS_{\\mathrm{add}}=-\\sum_{a=1}^4\\int\\sqrt{|g|}\\,[m\\bar\\psi_a\\psi_a+q A_\\mu\\bar\\psi_a\\gamma^\\mu\\psi_a]\\,d^4x.\n$$\n\nThe signs of $A$ are fixed so that its static boost-frame Hamiltonian term is $+qA_\\eta I$. They are tested against the stationary Dirac equation rather than inferred from a desired phase. Choose $qA_\\eta=v_N(x)$ on the protocol plateau. The scalar mass supplies the two real barriers. Both vanish on the horizon crossing tube and its support collar. Their transverse plateau contains every logical fixture column and its finite causal expansion; smooth outer cutoffs are retained in the source.\n\nThe fields and sources are static under the registered boost clock during the entire comparison interval. Their installation, removal and switching occur at owned boundaries and have actual work/precision charges. Maintaining prescribed profiles can require momentum-force and current-source payers depending on the field state. Such terms are retained, as E37.B12/B14a requires.\n\nIf a Maxwell background is included, its field tensor, prescribed background-source current and positive finite field energy are calculated from the same compact smooth potential. The background-source current is an external maintaining payer in the specified source class.\n\nThe heat statement is local to the original tube where $m=A=0$: the logical pulse crosses it before the support can send a return. The complete fixture success-minus-failure subtraction removes identical background source stress there. It does not erase the actual profile, apparatus and switching cost elsewhere.\n\n###### 2. Finite field matrices and full-carrier support\n\nAt a fixed physical regulator the added multiplication operators have matrix elements in the already specified normalized cell basis. Their supports are within each cell, so they add onsite Hermitian blocks to the one-particle matrix $K[g,q]$. The off-reference expression is obtained from the covariant action above, with the existing material Gram/Berry map, proper lapse, tetrad, field/charge normalization, transported scalar profile and one-form pullback. It is not defined by freezing its reference matrix and declaring its derivative zero.\n\nThe derivative includes the actual mass and potential gradients times support displacements, tetrad and lapse changes, changes of $A_\\mu\\gamma^\\mu$, the cell measure, both normalized mode columns and their Gram derivative, and the stated controller/clock pullbacks. Every coefficient is a smooth finite function on the fixed finite material chart. Thus its operator norm and every registered finite first-variation norm can be computed or conservatively bounded before choosing a matching time step. Large radial and thin-wall gradients enter those constants without omission.\n\nE48.L4 applies to a general Hermitian one-particle coefficient matrix. These additional onsite spin/species terms are complete single-pack operators. The original edge hopping terms remain neighboring-pair operators. The exact algebraic intertwining and its derivative in E48.L5 therefore hold for the enlarged matrix with the same fixed representation. No new nonlocal or three-pack term is introduced by the prescribed multiplication potentials.\n\nUse the selected prescribed-profile, fixed-enable, decoupled-spectator subclass in part 2 of E48. The unsplit source is the actual Dirac-plus-profile finite regulator, not the old free regulator. Its channel and flux comparison must use the new physical estimates. Hold the entire new generator, including its potentials and every off-code sector, during the old Page interval by the actual full negative cancellation; retain that cancellation's boundary action/work. Release the source only in its appended scattering/calibration interval. This preserves the old Page law without retrospectively changing its input or timing.\n\n###### 3. No free implementation or stress transfer\n\nAfter the regulator and all finite tests are fixed, compute the actual $A,D,B$ and first-variation constants of E48.L12–L19 for the enlarged source. The same matching construction then supplies a full-carrier pair/free-layer history. Its explicit E48.L23 switching-work and E48.L24 timing bounds include the new onsite norms, mass gradients, vector-potential derivatives and full source clock. The finite comparison error is the actual E48.L15/L19 bound for this source; the current/work/boundary comparison uses it, not endpoint channel agreement alone.\n\nThe complete inventory includes four fields and spectator spin sectors, radial/transverse regulators, scalar/vector profile supports, source charge/field or externally maintained potential payers, both barriers and their finite strength/width precision, clocks/enable modules, doubled endpoint and gauge factors, all fixture displacement archives and failed-input stores, reference purification, penalty preparation/cancellation, both lead outputs, cavity memory, physical capture/readout and any renewal service.\n\nFor fixed barrier parameters all these matrices and bounds are finite. Choose the ordinary regulator before the matching step, and the matching precision after its actual norms. As the barrier parameters refine, redo that finite computation; do not exchange the thin-wall limit with a fixed ultraviolet or clock bound.\n\n###### 4. Reference remains a separate proved comparison\n\nThe true static field reference is the quasifree state with covariance $F(h_V)$ at the registered boost inverse temperature. The finite matrix source may use either the actual-regulator Gibbs covariance or a correctly installed actual-marginal covariance under E48.L7/L8, but its choice must be explicit. Compression and the Fermi function do not commute. The Definition F.10.12a certificate uses the finite-code field/unitary/covariance comparison, actual spectral buffer and logit/CAR lift in §§Q.0.7v.5.4–6, together with the regional maps, reference preparation and joint error schedule in §Q.0.7v.5.1.\n\nThe separated-support Fermi and causal-port estimates supply their stated comparison terms; they are used together with the exact incoming reference maps, regulator bounds and full source action, not as substitutes for those additional entries.\n\n###### Identical charge and absence of an unrecorded species-message side channel\n\nThe four information species have identical masses, charge, spatial input profile and source couplings. Every physical source current or stress in the prescribed quadratic matter model is the sum of the same diagonal species bilinear. Restricted to the one-particle internal ququart with its common spatial wavefunction, such a bilinear compresses to its scalar spatial value times $I_4$. Off-diagonal species matrix elements vanish because the action does not convert species; diagonal values agree. This remains true for arbitrary ququart states entangled with an external reference.\n\nThus the external profile/support response within this specified model cannot reveal which internal-species message was sent. Its common dependence on success probability, occupation, spatial motion and source parameters is retained in the work and source-state record. The selected source contains no species-sensitive coupling.\n\n##### Q.0.7v.5.8. Coherent reflection readout and finite diagnostic preparation\n\nThis component uses the actual finite two-barrier source and a registered coherent reference. It avoids assigning a narrow-frequency, long wavepacket to an exactly time-isolated single lap of a finite cavity.\n\nFix $r=1/2$, $\\ell=\\pi$, the real-barrier/phase-plate source, and the reference planes of §Q.0.7v.5.2. The ideal one-lead reflection coefficient is\n\n$$\nR_0(E)=i\\frac{w(E)-r}{1-rw(E)},\\qquad w(E)=e^{i(2\\pi E-N\\ln2)}.\n$$\n\nThe factor $i$ is part of the actual port dictionary. It is not dropped from the interferometer. Solving this equation gives the source-derived extraction map\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{r-iR_0}{1-irR_0}=:\\mathcal{M}(R_0).\n$$\n\nFor $|z|,|z'|\\le1$,\n\n$$\n|\\mathcal{M}(z)-\\mathcal{M}(z')|\n\\le\\frac{1+r}{1-r}|z-z'|=3|z-z'|.\n$$\n\nThis follows by subtracting the fractions; the numerator is $(1-r^2)(-i)(z-z')$ and each denominator has modulus at least $1-r$. The ideal reflection derivative obeys\n\n$$\n|R_0'(E)|\\le2\\pi\\frac{1+r}{1-r}=6\\pi.\n$$\n\n###### Actual finite-band experiment\n\nChoose a normalized smooth spectral packet $A$ supported within $[E_0-h,E_0+h]$ and the globally certified band. Prepare one excitation coherently split between the incoming physical lead and an owned reference path with the registered matching output packet. All propagation phases and delays of that reference are measured/derived source entries. They are not fixed after observing a resonance.\n\nLet $R_d(E)$ and $T_d(E)$ be the complete finite-source amplitudes. The reflected component is $R_d A$, and the outer-lead component and all unmatched reflected modes are retained in the zero-score outcome. Select the normalized matched reflected output mode specified by the same packet dictionary. Recombine that mode with the reference by a balanced splitter and a reference phase $\\vartheta$. With\n\n$$\n\\bar R_d=\\int |A(E)|^2R_d(E)\\,dE,\n$$\n\nthe exact ideal-apparatus probabilities are\n\n$$\nP_\\pm(\\vartheta)=\\frac{1+|\\bar R_d|^2\\pm2\\operatorname{Re}(e^{-i\\vartheta}\\bar R_d)}4,\n\\qquad P_0=\\frac{1-|\\bar R_d|^2}2.\n$$\n\nThus the outcome variable $Z\\in\\{-1,0,+1\\}$ has mean $\\operatorname{Re}(e^{-i\\vartheta}\\bar R_d)$. Separate $\\vartheta=0$ and $\\pi/2$ trials estimate the real and imaginary parts. No division by a small raw reflection probability occurs.\n\nWith $n$ independent fresh trials on each axis, Hoeffding gives simultaneous error at most $\\eta$ on both components, except with probability $4e^{-n\\eta^2/2}$. The complex statistical error is at most $\\sqrt2\\eta$. Fresh preparation or an explicitly verified renewal channel, all copies of the reference, clocks, splitter, detector, archives and output sectors are charged.\n\nIf $\\Delta_R$ is the uniform finite-barrier bound on $|R_d-R_0|$ from §Q.0.7v.5.2, then\n\n$$\n|\\bar R_d-R_0(E_0)|\\le\\Delta_R+6\\pi h.\n$$\n\nAdd the actual physical preparation/capture/readout error in this complex expectation, denoted $\\epsilon_{\\mathrm{phys}}$. A half-trace error $\\delta$ per setting contributes at most $2\\delta$ to its bounded $Z$ mean and hence at most $2\\sqrt2\\delta$ to the complex estimate. The already retained norm/energy and field/ordinary-source comparisons must supply this $\\delta$.\n\nProject the noisy complex estimate onto the closed unit disk. Euclidean projection cannot increase its distance to the true $\\bar R_d$, which lies in that disk. The resulting $\\widetilde R$ obeys, with the stated confidence,\n\n$$\n|\\widetilde R-R_0(E_0)|\\le\n\\Delta_R+6\\pi h+\\epsilon_{\\mathrm{phys}}+\\sqrt2\\eta=:\\epsilon_R.\n$$\n\nCompute $\\widehat w=\\mathcal{M}(\\widetilde R)$. Then $|\\widehat w-w(E_0)|\\le3\\epsilon_R$. If $3\\epsilon_R<1/2$, $\\widehat w$ is nonzero. Its normalized phase satisfies\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{dist}_{2\\pi}(\\arg\\widehat w,2\\pi E_0-N\\ln2)\n\\le2\\arcsin(3\\epsilon_R).\n$$\n\nIndeed normalization changes a complex number within $\\epsilon<1$ of a unit number by at most another $\\epsilon$, giving chord distance at most $2\\epsilon$. The corresponding angular distance is at most $2\\arcsin\\epsilon$.\n\nThis is an explicit confidence-aware phase acceptance protocol: register the mode centers, widths, $N$ range, all source/refinement parameters, statistical confidence and phase margin before data. Accept a phase-grid position only when its confidence interval lies within the registered tolerance. Reject only when the interval is disjoint; otherwise report unresolved. The protocol reconstructs the loop phase from derived complete complex scattering; raw intensity maxima are not used as grid positions.\n\nIn physical units $E_0=c\\omega/\\kappa_H$, so $2\\pi E_0=\\omega\\tau_H=\\hbar\\omega/(k_BT_H)$ for $\\tau_H=2\\pi c/\\kappa_H$. The material plate supplies the independently registered $N\\ln2$ phase. The finite barrier dwell phases are retained in $\\Delta_R$ (or can be used through the exact finite transfer matrix); they are not treated as absent.\n\n###### Finite spatial and temporal realizations\n\nA smooth compact spectral packet has Schwartz free spatial tails. Choose finite preparation and capture windows using its actual norm and energy-weighted tail integrals and the finite-source scattering coefficients. Physically truncate, smooth and renormalize the initial/readout modes, and retain the resulting norm/graph errors in $\\epsilon_{\\mathrm{phys}}$ and the energy ledger. The two-channel scattering transform is unitary by the separate Jost/Stone proof. The free-edge multiplier and barrier-occupation bounds control the complete finite packet, including the outer lead and finite cavity occupation.\n\nThe exact field experiment is never claimed to start from an infinitely extended monochromatic mode or to have completed infinitely many laps at finite time. Any finite time selected to approximate the packet-scattering experiment must include its actual incoming/outgoing and residual-cavity tails, rather than just a nominal $r^M$ echo count. All such tails can be bounded from the registered smooth compact spectral packet and the nonzero-denominator bound before the trial.\n\n###### Exact finite greybody variant: no perfect-mirror requirement\n\nThe original transfer record permits greybody attenuation. Thus a fully finite source can avoid the thin-wall/perfect-reflector limit entirely. Fix two finite smooth real mass barriers. Choose their strengths and widths from the transfer bounds so that on the entire admitted band\n\n$$\n|r'_1(E)|\\ge\\rho_1>0,\\quad |r_2(E)|\\ge\\rho_2>0,\n\\quad |t_1(E)|,|t'_1(E)|\\ge\\tau>0,\\quad |r'_1(E)|\\le r_*<1.\n$$\n\nFor example choose zero-width target moduli $1/2$ and $3/4$, and widths sufficiently small that each amplitude error is at most $1/20$. All four positive constants then follow before measuring a spectrum. Both finite leads and barrier interiors are retained. There is no claim of zero outer transmission.\n\nKeep the inner reference faces separated by exactly $\\ell=\\pi$, including the phase plate. Set\n\n$$\nz(E)=r_2(E)e^{2iE\\ell-2i\\alpha_N},\\quad\nq(E)=r'_1(E)z(E),\\quad G(E)=-r'_1(E)r_2(E).\n$$\n\nThen the **exact finite source** has\n\n$$\nq(E)=G(E)e^{i(2\\pi E-N\\ln2)}.\n$$\n\nIts complex greybody factor $G(E)$, including both barrier phases and their dispersive delay, is derived by the actual finite Volterra transfer matrices. It is not fitted after spectral comparison. The free-cavity loop time and the greybody phase are distinct entries, exactly as in the original greybody-inclusive record.\n\nLet $Y(E)=R(E)-r_1(E)$ and $a(E)=t'_1(E)t_1(E)$. The exact reflection formula gives\n\n$$\nY=\\frac{az}{1-r'_1z},\\qquad\nz=\\frac{Y}{a+r'_1Y},\\qquad\nq=\\frac{r'_1Y}{a+r'_1Y}.\n$$\n\nAt the true value, $a+r'_1Y=a/(1-q)$, so its modulus is at least $\\tau^2/2$. If the complex reflection estimate has error $\\epsilon<\\tau^2/4$, the estimated denominator has modulus at least $\\tau^2/4$, since $|r'_1|\\le1$. Subtracting the two rational expressions gives the conservative bound\n\n$$\n|\\widehat q-q|\\le 8\\epsilon/\\tau^4.\n$$\n\nIndeed the numerator of that difference is $r'_1a\\,\\Delta Y$, of modulus at most $\\epsilon$, and the product of denominator lower bounds is at least $\\tau^4/8$.\n\nDividing by the **derived** nonzero $G$ gives an estimate of the intrinsic loop unit phase with error at most $8\\epsilon/(\\tau^4\\rho_1\\rho_2)$. Alternatively retain $\\arg G$ and report the greybody-inclusive phase predicate directly. Neither operation removes an unmeasured phase by convention; it consumes source-derived complex amplitudes at the fixed physical reference planes.\n\nFor a finite-width packet centered at $E_0$, replace the ideal $6\\pi h$ bound by $L_Rh$, where $L_R=\\sup_{|E|\\le B}|R'(E)|$ is bounded from the exact smooth transfer ODE and the positive scattering denominator. All profile coefficients and their finite energy derivatives are fixed first; the packet width is chosen afterward. The finite $L_R$ is computable without any experimental fit. The same two-quadrature/Hoeffding and physical-tail argument supplies $\\epsilon$. The source amplitude tables can be evaluated with certified interval errors, which are added to numerator and denominator bounds before inversion.\n\nThe exact finite greybody source has the required geometrical loop phase, source-derived nontrivial greybody amplitudes, full retained losses and a certified interference/readout extraction. The actual finite horizon/KMS/material and Definition F.10.12a conjunction is supplied by §§Q.0.7v.5.1–7, with the finite diagnostic preparation and tails below; it does not require another ideal-wall limit.\n\n###### Detector fixture and the actual thermal background\n\nAt the finite physical stage let $U$ be the actual one-particle propagation, including its owned reference-path map, and let $D$ be the finite orthonormal frame of the matched reflected and reference detection modes. Choose $F$ to be the entire actual finite regulated field together with its reference-path field. Its native basis and dimension are known from the regulator, so $F$ contains every prepared packet, every column of $U^*D$, and all physical preparation, splitting and capture modes. This inclusion requires no rank decision or new span orthogonalization.\n\nBefore admitting the diagnostic excitation, swap out all of $F$, including its old correlations, into a full isomorphic inaccessible retained archive. Prepare every fixture mode empty, then create the one-excitation coherent input. Since $D\\subset UF$, the detected mode algebra reduces the propagated fixture. Its output probabilities are exactly the matched-mode formulas above, with the actual finite propagation matrix element replacing the asymptotic spectral expression. All unmatched excitations, both leads, cavity occupation and other full-field modes remain in the source and yield the zero-score event for this instrument. Charge the complete archive, state swap, fresh preparation, constraints/parity, routing and storage.\n\nAt continuum comparison level choose smooth compact physical input and detector modes. Finite-time propagation under the specified smooth source carries their preimages to smooth compact Cauchy data. The physical approximation and its near-identity Gram lower bound normalize the fixed input/detector frames. The diagnostic fixture itself is the full native finite field, with its actual dimension, covariance, placement, source and preparation costs. That dimension and its cost may grow with the regulator. The stationary reference retains its separately proved KMS, local Hadamard and logit record; no ultraviolet-uniform normal-state or Hadamard limit is claimed for the driven empty-field diagnostic family.\n\nThe scattering diagnostic is separately prepared and paid on the same installed static coefficients. Its long narrow-band capture window and driven preparation serve a different role from the short causal horizon-capacity calibration and the stationary reference, as specified in E50/E53.\n\nIf an old retained source output must persist while the diagnostic runs, use the full-carrier E53 swap sandwich with a fresh isomorphic charged service carrier: $S(U_T\\otimes I_H)S=I_T\\otimes U_H$. Prepare and clear the diagnostic fixture in that fresh service state; the sandwich restores the arbitrary old input and its external correlations exactly. Both full swaps, actual transport, non-ready sectors, detector archives and reference correlations are retained. Independent fresh prepared source calls with the same material coefficients provide the other allowed implementation. Each implementation charges its fresh carrier and preserves the old passive receiver's access dictionary.\n\n##### Q.0.7v.5.9. Explicit finite-duration and capture tails\n\nFor any smooth compact spectral vector $B(E)$, let\n\n$$\n\\widehat B(x)=(2\\pi)^{-1/2}\\int e^{\\mathrm{iEx}}B(E)\\,dE.\n$$\n\nIntegration by parts $k$ times, $k\\ge1$, gives\n\n$$\n\\|\\widehat B(x)\\|\\le\\frac{\\|B^{(k)}\\|_1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}|x|^k},\\qquad\n \\int_{|x|\\ge L}\\|\\widehat B(x)\\|^2dx\n \\le\\frac{\\|B^{(k)}\\|_1^2}{\\pi(2k-1)}L^{1-2k}.\n$$\n\nApply this to the finitely many free-lead incoming and outgoing coefficients $A,AR,AT$, including their actual reference-plane shifts. At large negative time, unwanted outgoing portions lie in such tails; at large positive time, the remaining incoming portion does. On the finite interaction interval use the same integration by parts in $E$ on $A(E)\\psi(x,E)e^{-iEt}$, with the supremum of its $L^1$ derivative over that finite interval. This bounds the cavity/barrier remainder by a finite constant times $|t|^{-k}$. The Jost/Volterra solutions and their energy derivatives give the constants without dropping resonances.\n\nEnergy/graph tails are obtained by the same calculation with $EA(E)$ in place of $A(E)$. To prepare a genuinely compact initial state, multiply the large-negative-time scattering packet by a registered smooth cutoff and normalize. Its generator error additionally includes the exact commutator $-i\\sigma_3\\chi'\\psi$; its cutoff derivative and normalization defect are charged. Unitary evolution then propagates the initial norm/graph errors. Physical finite-cell and matching-source errors are added afterward on this actual finite catalogue.\n\nConsequently all readout time windows and spatial capture supports are finite at any strictly positive tolerance. Their costs can be large. Choose horizon-to-barrier separation $D$ only after the required finite duration is fixed, so that all these observations occur before a return reaches the original horizon tube. This flight distance is not inserted into the internal loop clock.\n\nThe common schedule in §Q.0.7v.5.1 now combines the finite source, its actual reference and physical comparisons, and the confidence-aware instrument. Each independently adjustable physical error is chosen after the finite dimensions, frame gaps, source norms and consumed weights that multiply it. The intrinsic expanding-frame modular limit uses its separate fixed-test argument. The retained full carrier and conditional expectation satisfy Definition E.9.5d, while the characteristic overlap/reference and local heat/capacity record supplies the stated cover's Definition F.10.12a entries. The finite scattering data, reference-plane convention, geometrical clock dictionary, independently fixed structural phase, admitted finite domain and certified readout give the entries of Definition Q.0.7u. The construction uses finite material profiles, the complete retained outputs and source payers, and the proved state/dynamics comparisons. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q-0-7v-6-common-horizon-completion\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q07v6-common-horizon-recovery-page-and-transfer-realization\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.7v.6 (Common Horizon Recovery, Page and Transfer Realization).** The prescribed-background finite material source of Theorem Q.0.7v.5 carries the original common-code exterior-recovery, design, trace-coupled Page and closed-loop transfer records on one complete finite horizon dynamics. Its code is the original $q=4$, $n=22$, $k=1$ source of Construction E.9.5f.36, with every original completed radiation cut and its unchanged access dictionary. Write\n$$\n d=4^{22},\\qquad K=4,\\qquad \\eta=\\pi2^{-42},\\qquad\n \\mu^2=\\frac{4095(4d-1)}{4(d^2-1)}.\n$$\nThe source has finite exterior-recovery section error\n$$\n \\epsilon_j\\le32\\sqrt{2(\\mu+\\eta)}<\\frac15\n \\quad(j=19,20,21),\\qquad \\epsilon_{22}=0,\n \\tag{Q.0.7v.6.1}\n$$\nwith the original complete success/failure record and $p_{\\mathrm{fail}}<1/100$. Its moment and PageTV entries at all original completed cuts are\n$$\n \\begin{gathered}\n t_{\\mathrm{des}}=2,\\quad N_{t_j}=1,\\quad\n d_E(t_j)=4^j,\\quad d_L(t_j)=4^{22-j},\\quad\n \\gamma_2=1-4\\eta>0,\\quad \\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{emit}}=0,\\\\\n \\varepsilon_j^{\\mathrm{mom}}=4\\eta,\\qquad\n \\varepsilon_j^{\\mathrm{frame}}=(1-2/d^4)(4\\eta)^2,\\qquad\n \\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{Page}}(t_j)=\\eta,\n \\quad 1\\le j\\le22.\n \\end{gathered}\n \\tag{Q.0.7v.6.2}\n$$\nThe normalized frame-potential convention and physical times are those of Construction K.3d.4h. The original conditional Page entropy has radius\n$$\n r_j=\\eta\\ln(4^j-1)+h_2(\\eta)\n \\le(86\\ln2+1)2^{-40},\n \\tag{Q.0.7v.6.3}\n$$\nwith exact zero initial and final conditional code entropies and the original strict rise, turnover and fall. Raw key, background and apparatus entropies remain the separate terms of Equation (E.9.5f.36.A12).\n\nOn the same source, every mode accepted by the registered transfer protocol satisfies\n$$\n \\operatorname{dist}_{2\\pi}\\!\\left(\n \\frac{\\hbar\\omega}{k_BT_H},N\\ln2\\right)\n \\le\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}},\\qquad\n \\frac{\\hbar\\omega}{k_BT_H}=\\omega\\tau_H,\\qquad\n \\tau_H=\\frac{2\\pi c}{\\kappa_H},\n \\tag{Q.0.7v.6.4}\n$$\nwith Theorem Q.0.7v.5's finite mode/label domain, strictly positive comparison and phase budgets, and declared confidence. Its source-derived amplitudes, widths, greybody phases and dwell delay retain their separate entries.\n\n*Proof.* **One source and its retained algebra.** Apply Theorem Q.0.7v.5's ordered construction to the exact common-code source of Theorem E.9.5f.53a. Use the entire enlarged tested ordinary carrier $\\mathcal H_T$ of dimension $D$, its actual chronological unitary $U$, and its actual predeclared exterior/hidden partition. Section Q.0.7v.5.1 supplies\n$$\n \\mathcal A^{\\mathrm{ret}}=M_D,\\qquad\n \\pi_{\\mathrm{hor}}(X)=\\frac1{d_{\\mathrm{hid}}}\n \\operatorname{Tr}_{\\mathrm{hid}}(X)\\otimes I_{\\mathrm{hid}},\\qquad\n g_{\\mathrm{hor}}=\\frac1{72(2D^2-D)^2D^6}>0.\n \\tag{Q.0.7v.6.5}\n$$\nThe separating grammar has zero response-null kernel, $\\operatorname{Ad}_U$ is its retained automorphism, and the hidden kernel is $\\ker\\pi_{\\mathrm{hor}}$. The diagnostic checked margin is $g_{\\mathrm{hor}}/2$ at its registered confidence. Every source, field, key, archive, failure, clock and control factor assigned to this update is included before $D$ and the diagnostic reservations are computed. The KMS/reference comparisons and actual characteristic cover are the ones proved in Sections Q.0.7v.5.3–6. These populate Definition E.9.5d for the complete-history transfer update on the enlarged carrier.\n\nThe recovery records use their own actual prefixes, not this final update in place of a cut update. For each $j\\in\\{19,20,21,22\\}$ register the same full tested carrier with $W_j=U_j\\otimes I$, retained automorphism $\\operatorname{Ad}_{W_j}$ and the actual cut expectation $\\pi_j'$. Its response-null kernel is zero and its hidden kernel is $\\ker\\pi_j'$. Construction E.9.5f.53 applies to each of these four complete finite unitaries and to $U$: its kernel-separation proof uses only the full dimension and unitary norm preservation, so all five records have the analytic margin $g_{\\mathrm{hor}}$ in (Q.0.7v.6.5). The excluded candidate for each word $W$ is exactly $\\operatorname{Ad}_W\\circ\\Delta_a$ on the same predeclared nontrivial factor.\n\nTo give the five checked records and the transfer readout one confidence statement, fix $0<\\alpha<1$ in advance. In display (4) of Construction E.9.5f.53 use $\\alpha_0=\\min(\\alpha/10,1/16)$ in each of the five training/validation sample formulas, and assign transfer failure budget $\\alpha/2$. The same two Hoeffding bounds prove each diagnostic failure probability at most $\\alpha_0$; their union and the transfer failure cost at most $5\\alpha_0+\\alpha/2\\le\\alpha$. Arithmetic errors and the conservative checked margin are unchanged. This is a finite specification for every fixed $\\alpha>0$. Install five distinct fresh service/processor/record packs and sum Construction E.9.5f.53's actual preparation, storage, route, clock, fabrication and state-uniform action/work reservations, using each word's full-carrier matrices and duration. Every repetition of a cut prefix holds every remaining factor on all sectors of the full carrier. All service preparation, rest stresses and full varied-action neighborhoods are outside every consumed old and transfer test window, and all five diagnostic programs run afterward. Their full swaps restore the whole arbitrary source/reference input. These external service packs remain outside their own tested algebra, as required by Theorem Q.0.7v.5; no self-certification of arbitrary updates on the service is inferred.\n\n**Recovery and chronological preservation.** Through every original Page and recovery cut, all new quantum factors are held on every sector. Thus the old cut prefix $U_j$ is extended by $U_j\\otimes I$ on every sector. For $\\iota_j(A)=A\\otimes I$, normalized hidden partial trace gives\n$$\n \\pi_j'\\iota_j=\\iota_j\\pi_j.\n$$\nIndeed, tracing the additional hidden identity contributes its dimension, which cancels its new normalized-trace denominator. Let $\\mathcal T_j$ be the original finite norm-one test family of transported logical observables in (E.9.5f.36.A14), and let $\\mathcal C_j$ be its admitted recorded coarse domain. Register exactly\n$$\n \\mathcal T_j'=\\iota_j(\\mathcal T_j),\\qquad\n \\mathcal C_j'=\\iota_j(\\mathcal C_j),\\qquad\n s_j'(\\iota_j(C))=\\iota_j(s_j(C)).\n$$\nSince $\\operatorname{Ad}_{W_j}\\iota_j=\\iota_j\\operatorname{Ad}_{U_j}$, the preceding trace identity proves that $\\mathcal C_j'$ contains every required record $\\pi_j'(\\operatorname{Ad}_{W_j}(A))$ for $A\\in\\mathcal T_j'$. No recovery claim on the full enlarged algebra, or its unit ball, is made.\nInjectivity of $\\iota_j$ makes this well-defined. It is the old admitted section extended by the identity; the operational decoder ignores the added factors and keeps their actual records. Since $\\|X\\otimes I\\|=\\|X\\|$, the old section error is unchanged. Construction E.9.5f.36 gives the literal finite square (E.9.5f.36.A14) and section bound $2K\\overline\\epsilon_j$. Equation (E.9.5f.51.S15) gives $\\overline\\epsilon_{19}\\le4\\sqrt{2(\\mu+\\eta)}$; cuts $20$ and $21$ discard to that same logical channel, and cut $22$ uses the exact inverse. This proves (Q.0.7v.6.1), because $\\mu<1/65000$, $\\eta<2^{-40}$ and\n$$\n 2048(1/65000+2^{-40})<1/25.\n$$\nLikewise $p_{\\mathrm{fail}}\\le512(\\mu+\\eta)<1/100$. Every decoder is completed on invalid blocks, and every failure flag and archive is retained.\n\nLater scattering and diagnostic calls preserve the arbitrary old input jointly with every reference by the complete identity\n$$\n S(U_T\\otimes I_H)S=I_T\\otimes U_H\n$$\non the charged isomorphic service carrier. Induction also preserves this identity for adaptive calls, since their choices depend only on previous diagnostic records independent of the old input. This is the implementation selected in Section Q.0.7v.5.8; it neither resets old radiation nor grants the sender or passive receiver new access.\n\nThe old current, stress and clock records are preserved by a separate source argument. Section Q.0.7v.5.1 parks every new profile, support, rest stress, preparation, hold and full varied-action neighborhood outside the old compact test support, and deploys them only after its output windows. Their variations against every old test vanish. Quantum tensor independence alone is not used to infer zero apparatus stress. The new payers remain in the complete enlarged action.\n\n**Design, entropy and times.** The unchanged complete encoder induces exactly the conditional full-code law of Construction K.3d.4h,\n$$\n \\rho\\longmapsto\\sum_s p_s|s\\rangle\\langle s|_C\n       \\otimes U_s\\rho U_s^*,\n$$\nwith its actual algebraic key weights and hidden purifying archive. Therefore its proved moment norm, normalized frame potential and zero emission error give (Q.0.7v.6.2) on the original code. No full-$D$ Haar-mixing claim is used. The same bin/Haar coupling and physical cut dictionaries give the almost-sure half-trace radius $\\eta$ simultaneously at every original completed cut. Audenaert continuity on that original conditional code observable gives (Q.0.7v.6.3). The Page target's adjacent gaps exceed $4/5$, whereas\n$$\n 2(86\\ln2+1)2^{-40}<174\\,2^{-40}<4/5.\n$$\nConsequently every adjacent sign and the original turnover survive. The empty initial radiation and final pure conditional code have entropy zero. Each $t_j$ is the unchanged sum of the actual encoder, route, guard and hold durations in Construction K.3d.4h. Additional physical background entropies are retained in the actual raw output and are not assigned to this conditional Page observable.\n\n**Transfer and common clock.** Theorem Q.0.7v.5 uses the same enlarged retained carrier (Q.0.7v.6.5), not an independently chosen horizon channel. Its two finite smooth barriers and phase plate determine the finite scattering data, actual pulsed matrix elements, source action, reference, complete receiver and coherent readout. The fixed calibration $\\rho=c^2/\\kappa_H$ gives\n$$\n E_*=\\hbar\\kappa_H/c,\\qquad k_BT_H=E_*/(2\\pi),\n$$\nwhile the registered inner-face distance $\\pi$ gives $\\tau_H=2\\pi c/\\kappa_H$. These identities prove the thermal-coordinate equality in (Q.0.7v.6.4). For a populated accepted transfer output, nominate $N_0=1$ and $E_0=1+\\ln2/(2\\pi)$ in the interior of the frozen finite band before any comparison. More labels or centers may be added within the registered finite domain. Then $2\\pi E_0-N_0\\ln2=2\\pi$, so the nominated source-derived central phase is exactly zero modulo $2\\pi$. Choose the strictly positive source, packet and readout budgets with total confidence radius less than $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}}/2$. On their joint confidence event the reported interval is wholly inside the tolerance window, and this nominated center is accepted. This is a prescribed source/phase choice, not a fitted measured peak. The independently registered plate supplies $N\\ln2$ on that same loop. Its exact finite-width continuum multiplier is\n$$\n q(E)=G(E)e^{i(2\\pi E-N\\ln2)},\\qquad G(E)=-r'_1(E)r_2(E).\n$$\nThe actual finite-source comparison and interference inversion carry their packet, clock, source, capture and statistical errors into $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}}$. The accepted-phase rule of Proposition Q.0.19 gives (Q.0.7v.6.4). The full complex $G$, its phase and dwell delay remain distinct from the geometrical loop phase; no intensity maximum or reset heat is substituted for the independently registered structural contribution.\n\nAll consumed records are thus projections of one specified complete source history, with the proved cutwise tensor/expectation identities, later complete-channel preservation, same physical clock and full source-action support exclusion. The common positive-tolerance schedule of Theorem Q.0.7v.5 evaluates its constants after the source and test family are fixed and terminates with every payer retained. The finite intermediate recovery errors, completed-cut Page law and finite-domain transfer are exactly the asserted output types. Refining recovery with vanishing intermediate error, continuous partial-route Page values, global Einstein backreaction and complete evaporation have different predicates and are not used in this conjunction. ∎\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q019-conditional-landauer-phase-grid-signature\"></a>**Proposition Q.0.19 (Conditional Landauer Phase-Grid Signature).** On an accepted horizon closed-loop transfer record $\\mathfrak T_{\\mathrm{hor}}$, every peak accepted by its certified interference/readout protocol at an admitted pair $(\\omega,N)\\in\\mathcal D_{\\mathrm{loop}}$ obeys\n$$\n\\operatorname{dist}_{2\\pi}(x,N\\ln2)\n:=\n\\min_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}|x-N\\ln2-2\\pi k|\n\\le\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}},\n\\qquad x=\\frac{\\hbar\\omega}{k_BT_H},\\quad N\\ge1.\n\\tag{Q.0.19.1}\n$$\nExact congruence is the subbranch $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}}=0$. Independently of which labels and modes are realized, the nominal arithmetic grid obtained by extending the label to all positive integers is\n$$\n\\{N\\alpha_L\\bmod1:N\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}\\},\n\\qquad\n\\alpha_L=\\frac{\\ln2}{2\\pi}.\n\\tag{Q.0.19.2}\n$$\nThis nominal infinite orbit is dense and equidistributed in $[0,1)$, and its first $N_{\\max}$ labels have at most three adjacent circular gap lengths. A finite transfer record claims physical peaks only on its admitted domain; it does not realize the entire infinite orbit or every finite initial label block. At nonzero tolerance, accepted measured phases lie near their nominal labels and need not retain exact gap equalities. Even at zero tolerance, an arbitrary admitted subset is not necessarily an initial label block. Amplitudes, widths, greybody factors and nonresonant envelopes remain separate outputs.\n\n*Proof.* The accepted transfer record and its interference/readout certificate give (Q.0.19.1) on the admitted domain. The nominal label phases divided by $2\\pi$ form the displayed irrational rotation orbit. Lemma Q.0.7b gives irrationality, so Weyl equidistribution supplies the infinite-orbit statement. Proposition Q.0.7n gives the gap bound for the first $N_{\\max}$ nominal labels. Neither theorem enlarges the physical transfer domain or removes its nonzero phase tolerance. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q019a-beatty-staircase-of-lower-phase-approximants\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.19a (Beatty Staircase of Lower Phase Approximants).** For each $k\\ge1$, define\n$$\nN_k^+:=\\lfloor\\beta_Lk\\rfloor,\n\\qquad\n\\beta_L=\\frac{2\\pi}{\\ln2},\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\Delta_k^+:=2\\pi k-N_k^+\\ln2.\n$$\nThen\n$$\n0<\\Delta_k^+<\\ln2,\n$$\nand $N_{k+1}^+-N_k^+\\in\\{9,10\\}$ with limiting $10$-step frequency $\\beta_L-9$. These are arithmetic phase approximants. They are structural horizon-loop phase overheads only when an admitted loop satisfies Definition Q.0.7e and Proposition Q.0.7f for the same pair; physical entropy production additionally requires the eventwise reset calibration (Q.0.7e.0).\n\n*Proof.* Proposition Q.0.7j gives the Beatty staircase. Irrationality of $\\beta_L$ makes the lower mismatch strictly positive. The physical-status statement follows from Definition Q.0.7h. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q019b-convergent-hierarchy-of-smallest-phase-defects\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.19b (Convergent Hierarchy of Smallest Phase Defects).** *Let $(k_n,N_n)=(p_n,q_n)$ be the continued-fraction convergents of $\\alpha_L=\\ln2/(2\\pi)$. Then*\n$$\n\\delta_{N_n}:=\\min_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}|N_n\\ln2-2\\pi k|<\\frac{2\\pi}{N_n}.\n$$\n*The first low-defect pairs are*\n$$\n(k,N)\\in\\{(1,9),\\ (15,136),\\ (31,281),\\ (139,1260),\\ (170,1541),\\ldots\\}.\n$$\n\n*Proof.* This is Corollary Q.0.7l translated from $|N\\alpha_L-k|$ to $|N\\ln2-2\\pi k|$ by multiplication by $2\\pi$. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q020-conditional-phase-scales-on-the-transfer-branch\"></a>**Proposition Q.0.20 (Conditional Phase Scales on the Transfer Branch).** On an accepted horizon closed-loop transfer record $\\mathfrak T_{\\mathrm{hor}}$ that independently registers the phase step $\\ln2$, the grid scales are $(2\\pi,\\ln2)$. Hence the grid in $x=\\hbar\\omega/(k_BT_H)$ is independent of horizon mass, charge, spin, and matter content at the level of phase positions, up to the certified tolerance $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}}$. Amplitudes, widths, greybody factors, peak heights, and the nonresonant envelope remain separate outputs of $\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{scat}}$.\n\n*Proof.* The period $2\\pi$ is the single-valuedness period, and $\\ln2$ is an explicit phase-step entry of $\\mathfrak T_{\\mathrm{hor}}$. Neither varies with horizon parameters by the definition of that transfer branch. The phase step is not derived from SPAP or Theorem 31. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q020a-temperature-normalized-cross-horizon-collapse\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.20a (Temperature-Normalized Cross-Horizon Collapse).** *Let $H_1$ and $H_2$ be two horizons satisfying the same closed-loop transfer branch of Definition Q.0.7u with peak tolerance $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}}$, with Hawking temperatures $T_{H,1}$ and $T_{H,2}$. Define the reduced thermal phase map*\n$$\n\\Pi(\\omega,H)\n:=\n\\left[\n\\frac{\\hbar\\omega}{k_B T_H}\n\\right]_{2\\pi}\n\\in\\mathbb R/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z).\n$$\n*If two resonant transfer peaks carry the same cycle label $N$, then*\n$$\n\\operatorname{dist}_{2\\pi}\\!\\left(\\Pi(\\omega_N^{(1)},H_1),\\,\\Pi(\\omega_N^{(2)},H_2)\\right)\\le2\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}},\n$$\n*and on the exact-closure subbranch $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}}=0$ both reduced phases equal $[N\\ln2]_{2\\pi}$. For an unwrapped frequency comparison, additionally require a common lift $m\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}$ for the two actual registered frequencies such that*\n$$\nx_{N,m}:=N\\ln2+2\\pi m,\\qquad\n\\left|\\frac{\\hbar\\omega_{N,m}^{(r)}}{k_BT_{H,r}}-x_{N,m}\\right|\n\\le\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}}\\quad(r=1,2).\n$$\n*If $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}}<x_{N,m}$, then these frequencies obey*\n$$\n\\left|\\frac{\\omega_{N,m}^{(1)}}{\\omega_{N,m}^{(2)}}-\\frac{T_{H,1}}{T_{H,2}}\\right|\n\\le\n\\frac{T_{H,1}}{T_{H,2}}\\,\n\\frac{2\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}}}{x_{N,m}-\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}}},\n$$\n*with the exact ratio $\\omega_{N,m}^{(1)}/\\omega_{N,m}^{(2)}=T_{H,1}/T_{H,2}$ on the exact-closure subbranch. Thus the branch predicts a universal temperature-normalized phase ruler at tolerance $2\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}}$, while raw frequencies scale with horizon temperature.*\n\n*Proof.* Proposition Q.0.19 gives\n$$\n\\operatorname{dist}_{2\\pi}\\!\\left(\\frac{\\hbar\\omega_N^{(r)}}{k_B T_{H,r}},\\,N\\ln2\\right)\\le\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}}\n$$\nfor each horizon $H_r$, and the triangle inequality in $\\mathbb R/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)$ gives the reduced-phase bound; at $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}}=0$ both phases equal $[N\\ln2]_{2\\pi}$. For the lifted form write $\\hbar\\omega_{N,m}^{(r)}/(k_B T_{H,r})=x_{N,m}+e_r$ with $|e_r|\\le\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}}$. Then\n$$\n\\frac{\\omega_{N,m}^{(1)}}{\\omega_{N,m}^{(2)}}\n=\n\\frac{T_{H,1}}{T_{H,2}}\\cdot\\frac{x_{N,m}+e_1}{x_{N,m}+e_2},\n\\qquad\n\\left|\\frac{x_{N,m}+e_1}{x_{N,m}+e_2}-1\\right|\n\\le\n\\frac{|e_1-e_2|}{x_{N,m}-\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}}}\n\\le\n\\frac{2\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}}}{x_{N,m}-\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}}},\n$$\nand at $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}}=0$ the ratio is exactly $T_{H,1}/T_{H,2}$. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q020b-three-gap-spectral-fingerprint\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.20b (Three-Gap Spectral Fingerprint).** *For a finite Landauer-spectroscopy truncation*\n$$\n\\mathcal P_{N_{\\max}}\n:=\n\\{[N\\ln2]_{2\\pi}:1\\le N\\le N_{\\max}\\},\n$$\n*sort the points cyclically in $[0,2\\pi)$ and let $\\mathcal G_{N_{\\max}}$ be the set of adjacent circular gap lengths. Then*\n$$\n|\\mathcal G_{N_{\\max}}|\\le3.\n$$\n*Consequently, on the exact phase-matching subbranch $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}}=0$, a peak set realizing the first $N_{\\max}$ Landauer labels has at most three adjacent circular gap lengths after transforming frequencies to $x=\\hbar\\omega/(k_B T_H)$. At nonzero tolerance, the exact three-gap statement concerns the nominal grid $\\mathcal P_{N_{\\max}}$; measured phases satisfy the circular-distance bound of Proposition Q.0.19.*\n\n*Proof.* Divide the nominal phase points by $2\\pi$. The set becomes\n$$\n\\{N\\alpha_L\\bmod1:1\\le N\\le N_{\\max}\\},\n\\qquad\n\\alpha_L=\\frac{\\ln2}{2\\pi}.\n$$\nBy Lemma Q.0.7b, $\\alpha_L$ is irrational. The index range $1,\\ldots,N_{\\max}$ is a common circle translation of the range $0,\\ldots,N_{\\max}-1$ in Proposition Q.0.7n, so it has the same gap lengths. Multiplication by $2\\pi$ preserves their number. Proposition Q.0.19 identifies measured phases with the nominal grid on the zero-tolerance subbranch; at nonzero tolerance it supplies a proximity bound and does not preserve exact equality of gaps. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q020b1-certified-landauer-phase-grid-ledger\"></a>**Proposition Q.0.20b.1 (Certified Landauer Phase-Grid Ledger).** On the horizon closed-loop transfer branch of Definition Q.0.7u, set\n$$\n\\alpha_L:=\\frac{\\ln2}{2\\pi},\n\\qquad\nx_N:=[N\\ln2]_{2\\pi}=2\\pi\\{N\\alpha_L\\}.\n$$\nAssume an accepted finite certificate $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{grid}}$ accompanies the ledger. It contains rational outward enclosures for $\\ln2$, $\\pi$, and $\\alpha_L$; an interval-certified continued-fraction quotient transcript; exact integer recurrences for every displayed $(p_k,q_k)$; rational enclosures proving every displayed rounded error and discrepancy; and, for each finite gap row, a permutation whose interval-separated residues are in cyclic order together with exact gap multiplicities. Acceptance means that every interval operation is outward rounded, every asserted floor is constant on its input interval, every recurrence is an exact integer identity, and every displayed decimal interval rounds uniquely to the printed digits. The following finite ledger is recorded before any comparison with a horizon-transfer dataset and is conditional on acceptance of $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{grid}}$.\n\n1. **Grid ratio.**\n$$\n\\alpha_L=0.11031780007632579669822821605899884549134487436482\\ldots .\n$$\nLemma Q.0.7b makes $\\alpha_L$ transcendental and therefore irrational. Hence $N\\mapsto x_N$ is injective on $\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}$: if $x_N=x_M$ with $N\\ne M$, then $(N-M)\\ln2\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z$, contradicting irrationality of $\\alpha_L$.\n\n2. **Continued-fraction prefix.** Precision-doubling at 400 and 800 decimal digits gives the same first 40 partial quotients; the prefix used in the ledger is\n$$\n\\alpha_L=[0;9,15,2,4,1,1,1,1,2,2,3,1,1,1,1,3,4,1,1,1,1,24,1,2,1,\\ldots].\n$$\nIn particular $\\sum_{i=1}^{10}a_i=38$.\n\n3. **Convergents and near-recurrences.** For the convergents $p_k/q_k$ of $\\alpha_L$, the following entries are the best-approximation ladder used by Corollary Q.0.19b. Beyond the trivial $N=1$ entry, the listed denominators give the successive record-small phase defects over the displayed range.\n\n| $k$ | $p_k$ | $q_k$ | $\\lvert\\alpha_L-p_k/q_k\\rvert$ | $q_k\\lVert q_k\\alpha_L\\rVert$ | $\\lvert q_k\\ln2-2\\pi p_k\\rvert$ rad |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 1 | 1 | 9 | $7.93\\times10^{-4}$ | $0.0643$ | $4.486\\times10^{-2}$ |\n| 2 | 15 | 136 | $2.37\\times10^{-5}$ | $0.438$ | $2.024\\times10^{-2}$ |\n| 3 | 31 | 281 | $2.48\\times10^{-6}$ | $0.196$ | $4.387\\times10^{-3}$ |\n| 4 | 139 | 1260 | $3.40\\times10^{-7}$ | $0.539$ | $2.690\\times10^{-3}$ |\n| 5 | 170 | 1541 | $1.75\\times10^{-7}$ | $0.416$ | $1.697\\times10^{-3}$ |\n| 6 | 309 | 2801 | $5.64\\times10^{-8}$ | $0.443$ | $9.928\\times10^{-4}$ |\n| 7 | 479 | 4342 | $2.58\\times10^{-8}$ | $0.487$ | $7.041\\times10^{-4}$ |\n| 8 | 788 | 7143 | $6.43\\times10^{-9}$ | $0.328$ | $2.887\\times10^{-4}$ |\n| 9 | 2055 | 18628 | $1.08\\times10^{-9}$ | $0.376$ | $1.268\\times10^{-4}$ |\n| 10 | 4898 | 44399 | $1.26\\times10^{-10}$ | $0.248$ | $3.512\\times10^{-5}$ |\n| 11 | 16749 | 151825 | $2.25\\times10^{-11}$ | $0.518$ | $2.144\\times10^{-5}$ |\n| 12 | 21647 | 196224 | $1.11\\times10^{-11}$ | $0.427$ | $1.368\\times10^{-5}$ |\n| 13 | 38396 | 348049 | $3.55\\times10^{-12}$ | $0.430$ | $7.759\\times10^{-6}$ |\n| 14 | 60043 | 544273 | $1.73\\times10^{-12}$ | $0.513$ | $5.919\\times10^{-6}$ |\n| 15 | 98439 | 892322 | $3.28\\times10^{-13}$ | $0.261$ | $1.840\\times10^{-6}$ |\n\nThe finite table is not an asserted irrationality-measure theorem; it is the certified finite arithmetic record used by the branch.\n\n4. **Window-counting bound.** Let $D_N^*$ be the star discrepancy of $\\{\\{j\\alpha_L\\}:1\\le j\\le N\\}$. The standard continued-fraction discrepancy bound gives, for $q_k\\le N<q_{k+1}$,\n$$\nN D_N^*\\le C_0+\\sum_{i=1}^{k+1}a_i,\n\\qquad C_0\\le3.\n$$\nTherefore every $N\\in[q_9,q_{10})=[18628,44399)$ satisfies\n$$\nD_N^*\\le\\frac{41}{N}.\n$$\nFor any phase window of width $w$ radians, the count among the first $N$ grid points differs from $Nw/(2\\pi)$ by at most $2ND_N^*$. Verified instances are\n$$\nD_{137}^*=0.0129410421831,\n\\qquad\nD_{1000}^*=0.00294366579284,\n\\qquad\nD_{18628}^*=5.36815450825\\times10^{-5}.\n$$\n\n5. **Two-gap and three-gap rows.** The singleton grid at $N=q_0=1$ has one circular gap of length $1$. For $k\\ge1$, at $N=q_k$, direct cyclic sorting gives exactly two gaps:\n$$\n\\ell_1=\\|q_{k-1}\\alpha_L\\|,\n\\qquad\n\\ell_2=\\|q_{k-1}\\alpha_L\\|+\\|q_k\\alpha_L\\|,\n$$\nas fractions of the circle, with multiplicities $q_k-q_{k-1}$ and $q_{k-1}$.\n\n| $k$ | $q_k$ | gap lengths as fractions of the circle | multiplicities |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| 2 | 136 | $0.0071397993$, $0.0103606097$ | $(127,9)$ |\n| 9 | 18628 | $4.59451952\\times10^{-5}$, $6.61233982\\times10^{-5}$ | $(11485,7143)$ |\n| 10 | 44399 | $2.01782031\\times10^{-5}$, $2.57669921\\times10^{-5}$ | $(25771,18628)$ |\n\nFor generic finite budgets the three-distance theorem gives at most three gaps; the following rows are direct finite checks, with the largest equal to the sum of the two smaller gaps whenever three gaps occur.\n\n| $N$ | distinct gaps | lengths as fractions of the circle with multiplicities |\n|---:|---:|---|\n| 9 | 2 | $0.1103178\\times8$, $0.1174576\\times1$ |\n| 24 | 3 | $0.00713980\\times15$, $0.09603820\\times5$, $0.10317800\\times4$ |\n| 137 | 3 | $0.00322081\\times1$, $0.00713980\\times128$, $0.01036061\\times8$ |\n| 1000 | 3 | $0.00069818\\times719$, $0.00112627\\times21$, $0.00182445\\times260$ |\n| 4111 | 3 | $0.00015801\\times1310$, $0.00027008\\times2570$, $0.00042810\\times231$ |\n\nThe winding increments of the grid are encoded by the Sturmian word\n$$\ns_N=\\lfloor(N+1)\\alpha_L\\rfloor-\\lfloor N\\alpha_L\\rfloor\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n$$\nThe finite Beatty/convergent/three-gap data displayed here are certified by the continued-fraction record together with the remaining components of $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{grid}}$.\n\n*Proof.* The certificate enclosures for the two transcendental inputs can be checked using convergent series. The identity\n$$\n\\ln2=2\\sum_{m=0}^{\\infty}\\frac1{(2m+1)3^{2m+1}}\n$$\nhas, after the terms $m=0,\\ldots,M-1$, a positive remainder bounded by\n$$\n\\frac{2}{2M+1}\\frac{3^{-(2M+1)}}{1-3^{-2}}.\n$$\nMachin's identity\n$$\n\\pi=16\\arctan(1/5)-4\\arctan(1/239)\n$$\nis verified by the tangent addition formula and the fact that both sides lie in $(3,4)$; each arctangent is enclosed by its alternating series, whose error is smaller than the first omitted term. Rational interval division then encloses $\\alpha_L=\\ln2/(2\\pi)$ and verifies the printed prefix in item 1 by containment in its decimal-prefix interval.\n\nFor item 2, begin with the certified interval for $y_0=\\alpha_L$. At stage $j$, the certificate verifies that $y_j$ lies strictly inside one unit interval $(a_j,a_j+1)$ and forms an outward enclosure for\n$$\ny_{j+1}=\\frac1{y_j-a_j}.\n$$\nInduction therefore proves every recorded partial quotient. The exact recurrences\n$$\np_j=a_jp_{j-1}+p_{j-2},\n\\qquad\nq_j=a_jq_{j-1}+q_{j-2},\n$$\nwith $(p_{-1},p_0)=(1,0)$ and $(q_{-1},q_0)=(0,1)$ prove all integer entries in item 3. The certificate evaluates\n$$\ne_j=\\left|\\alpha_L-\\frac{p_j}{q_j}\\right|,\n\\qquad\nq_j\\lVert q_j\\alpha_L\\rVert=q_j|q_j\\alpha_L-p_j|,\n\\qquad\n|q_j\\ln2-2\\pi p_j|=2\\pi|q_j\\alpha_L-p_j|\n$$\nby outward interval arithmetic. Each resulting interval lies inside the rounding cell of the printed entry. Theorem Q.0.7k proves the record-minimum interpretation.\n\nFor item 4, the continued-fraction discrepancy theorem for an irrational rotation (Kuipers and Niederreiter 1974) applies because Lemma Q.0.7b proves irrationality and the exact recurrence verifies $q_k\\le N<q_{k+1}$. It yields\n$$\nND_N^*\\le3+\\sum_{i=1}^{k+1}a_i.\n$$\nThe certified quotients give $\\sum_{i=1}^{10}a_i=38$, hence $D_N^*\\le41/N$ on the displayed range. If $0\\le y_1\\le\\cdots\\le y_N<1$ are the certified sorted residues, then\n$$\nD_N^*\n=\\max_{1\\le i\\le N}\n\\max\\left\\{\\frac iN-y_i,\\ y_i-\\frac{i-1}{N}\\right\\}.\n$$\nThe supplied permutations and interval comparisons prove the three displayed finite values by this formula. An arbitrary circular arc is a difference of at most two anchored intervals, so its counting error is at most $2ND_N^*$.\n\nFor item 5, the three-gap theorem for irrational rotations (Sós 1958; Świerczkowski 1958) applies to $\\alpha_L$. At $N=q_k$, the Farey-neighbor gap-count formula (Taha 2018, Theorem 2), applied to the adjacent convergents $p_{k-1}/q_{k-1}$ and $p_k/q_k$, gives the two lengths\n$$\n\\lVert q_{k-1}\\alpha_L\\rVert,\n\\qquad\n\\lVert q_{k-1}\\alpha_L\\rVert+\\lVert q_k\\alpha_L\\rVert\n$$\nwith multiplicities $q_k-q_{k-1}$ and $q_{k-1}$; these sum to $q_k$. For every other displayed row, the certificate permutation places the intervals for $\\{j\\alpha_L\\}$ in strict cyclic order. Subtracting successive intervals, including the wraparound gap, proves the listed lengths, multiplicities, and their sum-to-one check. Finally, $0<\\alpha_L<1$ implies\n$$\n\\lfloor(N+1)\\alpha_L\\rfloor-\\lfloor N\\alpha_L\\rfloor\\in\\{0,1\\},\n$$\nwhich proves the binary winding-increment formula. No statement about amplitudes, widths, greybody factors, or nonresonant envelopes enters the certificate. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"remark-q020b2-status-split-for-the-horizon-transfer-branch\"></a>**Remark Q.0.20b.2 (Status Split for the Horizon Transfer Branch).** Proposition Q.0.20b.1 closes only the phase-grid arithmetic after an accepted transfer record $\\mathfrak T_{\\mathrm{hor}}$ is present. It fixes the positions, recurrence hierarchy, finite gap statistics, and window-counting bounds of the Landauer grid. It does not by itself derive the transfer map from retained horizon channel data, and it does not fix amplitudes, widths, greybody factors, nonresonant envelopes, or peak-identification tolerances.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q020b3-conditional-transfer-map-separation\"></a>**Theorem Q.0.20b.3 (Conditional Transfer-Map Separation).** Fix the retained horizon channel $\\mathfrak H_n^{\\mathrm{ret}}$, deterministic exterior recovery certificate $\\mathfrak S_{\\mathrm{hor},n}$, and Page/design scrambling certificates. Assume two finite scattering completions satisfy every prior constraint and agree on these records but give inequivalent retained phase-position outputs. Then those records do not determine the phase-position output. If one such completion violates\n$$\n\\operatorname{dist}_{2\\pi}(x,N\\ln2)\\le\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}}\n$$\non the same registered domain and tolerance, the records do not imply that transfer law. An accepted horizon transfer record $\\mathfrak T_{\\mathrm{hor}}$ supplies the additional scattering map, loop time, MPU-cycle phase, domain, and phase tolerance of Definition Q.0.7u.\n\n*Proof.* The assumed completions have the same prior datum and inequivalent retained outputs, so they satisfy the non-identifiability criterion of Theorem P.14.1f. If an admissible completion violates the displayed inequality, that inequality cannot be a consequence of the shared prior constraints. The existence and admissibility of the two completions are separate hypotheses; they do not follow solely from the absence of an explicit scattering-map entry. Definition Q.0.7u specifies the additional record on which the phase-grid conclusions are conditional. ∎\n<a id=\"corollary-q020c-landauer-horizon-spectroscopy-null-conditions\"></a>**Corollary Q.0.20c (Landauer Horizon Spectroscopy Null Conditions).** *Within an independently validated Definition Q.0.7u transfer branch, fix the admitted label domain, peak tolerance, and measurement-uncertainty protocol before comparison. The following statistically resolved observations falsify the Landauer phase-grid component of that branch:*\n\n1. *a resonance phase set whose reduced positions cannot be assigned admitted labels satisfying*\n$$\n\\operatorname{dist}_{2\\pi}(\\Pi(\\omega_N,H),N\\ln2)\\le\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}}\n$$\n*after accounting for the registered instrumental and greybody-model phase uncertainty;*\n\n2. *a cross-horizon comparison of the same registered labels whose reduced phases violate*\n$$\n\\operatorname{dist}_{2\\pi}(\\Pi(\\omega_N^{(1)},H_1),\\Pi(\\omega_N^{(2)},H_2))\\le2\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}};\n$$\n\n3. *on the exact phase-matching subbranch $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{peak}}=0$, a resolved block of the first $N_{\\max}$ labels whose adjacent reduced-phase gaps are incompatible with the three-gap bound after measurement uncertainty is included.*\n\n*Proof.* Item 1 contradicts Proposition Q.0.19 on its registered domain and tolerance. Item 2 contradicts the circular-distance inequality of Corollary Q.0.20a. Item 3 contradicts the zero-tolerance observed-grid consequence of Corollary Q.0.20b. Nonzero phase tolerance does not preserve the exact number of distinct measured gap lengths. Each contradiction is conditional on prior validation of Definition Q.0.7u; absent that branch, the null result rejects the transfer bridge rather than the SPAP/Landauer cost theorem itself. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"remark-q0191-numerical-phase-locations\"></a>**Remark Q.0.19.1 (Numerical Phase Locations).** Substituting $\\ln2\\approx0.6931472$ and $2\\pi\\approx6.2831853$, the first nine phase-grid locations are\n$$\n\\{N\\ln2\\bmod2\\pi\\}_{N=1}^{9}\n=\n\\{0.6931,\\,1.3863,\\,2.0794,\\,2.7726,\\,3.4657,\\,4.1589,\\,4.8520,\\,5.5452,\\,6.2383\\}.\n$$\nThe next point is $10\\ln2\\bmod2\\pi\\approx0.6483$. In sector $k=1$, the positive-overhead Beatty value is $N_1^+=9$ and $\\Delta_1^+=2\\pi-9\\ln2\\approx0.0449$.\n\n<a id=\"remark-q0192-status-boundary-for-landauer-horizon-spectroscopy\"></a>**Remark Q.0.19.2 (Status Boundary for Landauer Horizon Spectroscopy).** The framework-level prediction of Proposition Q.0.19 is the parameter-free phase grid on the closed-loop transfer branch. The grid fixes phase positions in $x$; standard greybody factors also carry angular-momentum, spin, polarization, dimension, and barrier-shape data and remain separate outputs of $\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{scat}}$.\n\n## Q.1 Foundational Relation and the Optimization Goal\n\nThis appendix relates the emergent Planck length $L_P$ to the MPU spacing $\\delta$ on the capacity-achieving, entropy-saturating, additive-ledger branch of Theorem E.6 and then evaluates the relation on a specified intersection of channel branches. Adopt the same-family saturation $C(\\mathcal E_N)=C_{\\max}$ and the independent calibration $G_{\\mathrm{op}}=G$, so that $L_P^2=G\\hbar/c^3$. Under these identifications, Equation E.9 gives\n$$\nG=\\frac{\\eta\\delta^2c^3}{4\\hbar\\chi C_{\\max}}.\n\\tag{Q.1}\n$$\nUsing $L_P^2=G\\hbar/c^3$ yields\n$$\nL_P^2=\\frac{\\eta\\delta^2}{4\\chi C_{\\max}},\n\\tag{Q.2}\n$$\nand hence\n$$\n\\frac{\\delta^2}{L_P^2}=\\frac{4\\chi C_{\\max}}{\\eta}.\n\\tag{Q.3}\n$$\n\n## Q.2 Conditional Boundary-Parameter Assignments\n\nEquation Q.3 is evaluated below using three independent entries: the residual-budget capacity assignment, the throughput-saturated channel-independence selection with the other variables held constant, and the ideal-packing selection with the other variables held constant. Their simultaneous use defines the combined branch.\n\n### <a id=\"q21-channel-capacity-c_max-2ln-2-on-the-residual-budget-branch\"></a>Q.2.1 Channel Capacity: $C_{\\max}^* = 2\\ln 2$ on the Residual-Budget Branch\n\nOn the separately assumed residual-budget branch of Appendix E, Equation E.14 assigns\n$$\nC_{\\max}^*=\\ln d_0-\\varepsilon_0.\n$$\nCombining the Theorem-Z.2 comparator value $d_0=8$ with the registered binary structural value $\\varepsilon_0=\\ln2$ from Definition 28, Definition J.1, and Theorem J.1 gives\n$$\n\\boxed{C_{\\max}^*=\\ln8-\\ln2=2\\ln2=2\\varepsilon_0}. \\tag{Q.10}\n$$\nThis is a conditional allocation identity, not a consequence of SPAP or of physical reset heat. Alternative resource-partition rules give different $C_{\\max}^*$ and therefore different values of $\\delta/L_P$ in Sections Q.2.4 and Q.5.\n\n<a id=\"remark-q101-bit-budget-characterization\"></a>**Remark Q.10.1 (Bit-Budget Characterization).** Since $N_{\\mathrm{vis}}^{\\min}=2^{K_0}=8$ and the minimal complex Hilbert carrier saturates $d_0=N_{\\mathrm{vis}}^{\\min}$ on the minimal branch, while the PCE reference uses structural $\\varepsilon_0=\\ln2$, the capacity-cost ratio, evaluated at $\\varepsilon=\\varepsilon_0$, has the structural form\n$$\n\\frac{C_{\\max}^*}{\\varepsilon} = \\frac{\\ln(d_0)-\\varepsilon}{\\varepsilon} = K_0 - 1.\n$$\nAt $K_0=3$ this gives $C_{\\max}^*/\\varepsilon=2$: under the residual-budget rule, one structural bit is reserved and $K_0-1=2$ structural bits remain as residual channel capacity. This counting identity does not imply a physical Landauer cost without the independent reset hypotheses stated above.\n\n### <a id=\"q22-correlation-factor-chi-1\"></a>Q.2.2 Correlation Factor: $\\chi^* = 1$\n\n<a id=\"lemma-q22-channel-independence-on-the-throughput-saturated-branch\"></a>**Lemma Q.2.2 (Channel Independence on the Throughput-Saturated Branch).** Hold $(\\delta,\\eta,C_{\\max})$ constant, assume the accepted optimization maximizes a utility strictly increasing in the saturated ND-RID throughput, and assume $\\chi=1$ is feasible. Then\n$$\n\\boxed{\\chi^*=1.}\n\\tag{Q.11}\n$$\n\n*Proof.* The effective link count is $N_{\\mathrm{eff}}=\\chi N_{\\mathrm{geom}}$ with $0<\\chi\\le1$. With the remaining variables constant, the throughput is strictly increasing in $\\chi$, so its feasible maximum is attained at $\\chi=1$. ∎\n\n### <a id=\"q23-packing-factor-eta-1\"></a>Q.2.3 Packing Factor: $\\eta^* = 1$\n\n<a id=\"lemma-q23-ideal-packing-on-the-throughput-saturated-branch\"></a>**Lemma Q.2.3 (Ideal Packing on the Throughput-Saturated Branch).** Hold $(\\delta,\\chi,C_{\\max})$ constant, assume the accepted optimization maximizes a utility strictly increasing in the saturated ND-RID throughput, and assume $\\eta=1$ is feasible. Then\n$$\n\\boxed{\\eta^*=1.}\n\\tag{Q.12}\n$$\n\n*Proof.* Since $\\sigma_{\\mathrm{geom}}=1/(\\eta\\delta^2)$ with $\\eta\\ge1$, the throughput is strictly decreasing in $\\eta$ while the remaining variables are constant. Its feasible maximum is therefore attained at $\\eta=1$. ∎\n\n### Q.2.4 Final Result\n\nSubstituting the three independently selected branch values $C_{\\max}^*=2\\ln2$, $\\chi^*=1$, and $\\eta^*=1$ into Equation (Q.3) yields the closed-form ratio reported in Equation (Q.18):\n$$\n\\frac{\\delta^2}{L_P^2}=8\\ln 2,\\qquad \\frac{\\delta}{L_P}=\\sqrt{8\\ln 2}\\approx 2.355.\n$$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q24a-scale-surface-and-coupled-objective-nonentailment\"></a>**Theorem Q.2.4a (Scale-Surface and Coupled-Objective Nonentailment).** Fix any $r>0$. On the declared parameter domain $C_{\\max}>0$, $0<\\chi\\le1$, and $\\eta\\ge1$, Equation Q.3 with $\\delta^2/L_P^2=r$ has the two-parameter solution surface\n$$\n\\mathcal F_r\n=\\left\\{\n\\left(\\frac{r\\eta}{4\\chi},\\chi,\\eta\\right):\n0<\\chi\\le1,\\ \\eta\\ge1\n\\right\\}.\n\\tag{Q.2.4a.1}\n$$\nIn particular, at $r=8\\ln2$, both\n$$\nz_0=(2\\ln2,1,1),\n\\qquad\nz_1=(4\\ln2,1,2)\n\\tag{Q.2.4a.2}\n$$\ngive the same spacing ratio. For $j\\in\\{0,1\\}$ the smooth objective completion on the ambient parameter domain\n$$\nJ_j(z)=\\|z-z_j\\|_2^2\n\\tag{Q.2.4a.3}\n$$\nis strongly convex, has Hessian $2I$, and has the unique stable minimizer $z_j$. Thus Equation Q.3 and its spacing value do not select a coupled objective, a unique optimizer, or its stochastic dynamics. Hard-imposing the three separate branch assignments selects $z_0$ by premise, not by a coupled optimization theorem.\n\n*Proof.* Solving $4\\chi C_{\\max}/\\eta=r$ for $C_{\\max}$ gives (Q.2.4a.1); substitution gives (Q.2.4a.2). The gradient and Hessian of (Q.2.4a.3) are $2(z-z_j)$ and $2I$, so $z_j$ is its unique global minimizer and is Lyapunov stable under the gradient flow $\\dot z=-\\nabla J_j(z)$. Since the two admissible completions share $r$ but select different minimizers, the scale relation cannot entail the missing coupled objective or optimizer. ∎\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q24b-monotone-product-box-optimizer-classification\"></a>**Proposition Q.2.4b (Monotone Product-Box Optimizer Classification).** Fix $C^*>0$ and the compact product box\n$$\nB:=[C_-,C^*]\\times[\\chi_-,1]\\times[1,\\eta_+],\n\\qquad\n0<C_-<C^*,\\quad0<\\chi_-<1<\\eta_+,\n\\tag{Q.2.4b.1}\n$$\nin the coordinates $z=(C_{\\max},\\chi,\\eta)$, and let $\\mathcal U$ be the class of continuous utilities on $B$ that are strictly increasing in $C_{\\max}$, strictly increasing in $\\chi$ and strictly decreasing in $\\eta$. Then:\n\n1. Every $U\\in\\mathcal U$ has the unique maximizer $z_*:=(C^*,1,1)$ on $B$. The Gibbs laws $\\pi_\\beta(dz)\\propto e^{\\beta U(z)}dz$ on $B$ converge weakly to $\\delta_{z_*}$ as $\\beta\\to\\infty$; for every neighborhood $O$ of $z_*$ there are constants $c_O,s_O>0$ with $\\pi_\\beta(B\\setminus O)\\le c_Oe^{-s_O\\beta}$. At $C^*=2\\ln2$, $z_*$ is the point $z_0$ of (Q.2.4a.2) and the spacing response $4\\chi C_{\\max}/\\eta$ equals $8\\ln2$.\n2. A coupling constraint can remove this selection. Assume $C_-<\\frac34C^*$ and $\\chi_-<\\frac12$, and let $B_\\flat:=\\{z\\in B:2C_{\\max}/C^*+\\chi\\le5/2\\}$. Then $z_*\\notin B_\\flat$, and the members $U_1:=C_{\\max}/C^*+\\chi-\\eta$ and $U_2:=3C_{\\max}/C^*+\\chi-\\eta$ of $\\mathcal U$ have on $B_\\flat$ the distinct unique maximizers $(\\frac34C^*,1,1)$ and $(C^*,\\frac12,1)$, with spacing responses $3C^*$ and $2C^*$.\n\n*Proof.* Item 1. For $z=(C_{\\max},\\chi,\\eta)\\in B$, coordinatewise monotonicity gives $U(z)\\le U(C^*,\\chi,\\eta)\\le U(C^*,1,\\eta)\\le U(C^*,1,1)$, with equality throughout only at $z_*$. Let $O$ be a neighborhood of $z_*$. Compactness and uniqueness give $m_O:=\\max_{B\\setminus O}U<U(z_*)$; put $s_O:=\\frac12(U(z_*)-m_O)$ and $O'_O:=\\{z\\in B:U(z)>U(z_*)-s_O\\}$, a nonempty relatively open subset of $B$ with positive volume $|O'_O|$. Then\n$$\n\\pi_\\beta(B\\setminus O)\n\\le\\frac{|B|\\,e^{\\beta m_O}}{|O'_O|\\,e^{\\beta(U(z_*)-s_O)}}\n=\\frac{|B|}{|O'_O|}e^{-\\beta s_O},\n$$\nwhich proves the tail bound and weak convergence. Substitution gives the spacing value. Item 2. Since $2+1>5/2$, $z_*\\notin B_\\flat$. Both utilities decrease strictly in $\\eta$ and the coupling constraint does not involve $\\eta$, so every maximizer has $\\eta=1$. In $(s,\\chi):=(C_{\\max}/C^*,\\chi)$ the remaining feasible set is the polygon with vertices $(s_-,\\chi_-)$, $(1,\\chi_-)$, $(1,\\frac12)$, $(\\frac34,1)$ and $(s_-,1)$, where $s_-:=C_-/C^*<\\frac34$. The linear function $s+\\chi$ takes the values $s_-+\\chi_-$, $1+\\chi_-$, $\\frac32$, $\\frac74$, $s_-+1$ at these vertices, and $3s+\\chi$ takes $3s_-+\\chi_-$, $3+\\chi_-$, $\\frac72$, $\\frac{13}4$, $3s_-+1$. The strict vertex maxima $\\frac74$ at $(\\frac34,1)$ and $\\frac72$ at $(1,\\frac12)$ are the unique maximizers of the two linear programs, because the maximizing set of a linear function on a polygon is a face spanned by maximizing vertices. The spacing responses are $4\\cdot1\\cdot\\frac34C^*=3C^*$ and $4\\cdot\\frac12\\cdot C^*=2C^*$. ∎\n\n<a id=\"definition-q24c-finite-reset-link-source-and-complete-invoice\"></a>**Definition Q.2.4c (Finite Reset-Link Source and Complete Invoice).** Fix the ideal externally driven source Q10-RESET-LINK-PERIODIC-v1 with the following exact preparation, intervention, ownership, physical-clock and positive-tariff contract.\n\n### 1. Frozen source and scope\n\nThe manufacturing grammar has exactly eight configurations $x=(q,k,g)$, $q,k,g\\in\\{1,2\\}$. These choices precede every output, cost comparison and proposed spacing prediction. $q$ is a data-factor width, $k$ a geometric row spacing, and $g$ a wired duplication multiplicity. They are not themselves declared to be Q10's coordinates: those are derived below. The source includes a complete finite eight-position controller; arbitrary extra couplings or channels are outside this named architecture. No all-hardware optimality is claimed. Configuration controls are classical and fixed during each communication episode. Coherent superposition of manufacturing settings and sender-controlled switching are not source operations. The nominated physical initial state is $X_{0}=000$, indexing $(q,k,g)=(1,1,1)$, and the specified adaptation law reaches every setting with positive probability. Other initial probability laws are analyzed mathematically; their physical preparation is not claimed without a separate invoice. Distributions over these eight actual settings do not add input ports. Their expected frame potential is the affine average of the conditional frame potentials, so no mixture can improve below the pure minimum, and a mixture attains it exactly when supported on the minimizing states. Physical $C_{\\max}$, $\\chi$ and $\\eta$ are reported conditional on the actual setting, not manufactured by averaging incompatible channel geometries.\n\nFor every positive integer $L$ use spatial geometry $T^2_{2L\\delta}\\times\\mathbb{R}$, with BOTH tangential coordinates periodic and the normal coordinate nonperiodic. The registered cut is $H=T^2\\times\\{0\\}$, the compact boundary of the source-side region $T^2\\times(-\\infty,0)$. There is no second normal-periodic cut. The source and receiver layers are at normal coordinates $-\\delta/2$ and $+\\delta/2$, respectively. The boundary has side lengths $2L\\delta$. The fixed resolution $\\delta>0$, clock period $\\tau>0$ and service prices are independent dimensional source parameters. The two layers lie on opposite sides of the boundary. There are $A=4L^2$ base surface cells. Positions $(i\\delta,j\\delta)$, $i,j\\in\\{0,\\dots,2L-1\\}$, fix the geometry. A configuration $k$ has one boundary-crossing link at each site with $j$ divisible by $k$. Its geometric link count is $N=A/k$. Pair adjacent $i$ positions when $g=2$; singleton groups when $g=1$. There are $M=A/(kg)$ groups. All links are finite-range, vertex degree is uniformly bounded, and the source-side pair wire has length $\\delta$. Inactive components are still priced stocks, but have no crossing interaction, signal port or edge in the active network. The geometric count concerns actual nonzero crossing edges, not the number of purchased spare components.\n\nEvery base cell contains a source-side pair of persistent fair classical bits $Z_i=(Z_{i1},Z_{i2})$, independent over $i$. This same environment and prediction task is used for every configuration. The next-time target is $Z$ itself. Every episode uses fresh independent source labels, prepared by $|+\\rangle$ states and source-side computational-basis measurement. Persistence means from the observation instant to the prediction instant WITHIN that episode, not reuse of an old hidden label in later adaptive frames. Past receiver transcripts therefore contain no information about a new episode’s unrevealed target bits. The complete preparation hardware and records are owned. All these bits, including ignored bits, remain on the source side and inaccessible to the predictor except through the specified boundary channel.\n\nEach group has exactly one admitted independently addressable 8-level input cell (three qubits); the remaining would-be inputs are not independent intervention ports. The group anchor is the least i site. Preparation loads the first q bits of its actual Z into the q data factors; the other factors are nuisance registers, not extra message ports. For channel capacity proofs arbitrary quantum inputs and reference systems on the one group input are admitted. No secret restriction to the fair encoding is used in the capacity upper bound.\n\n### 2. Source primitives and boundary\n\nThe finite driven source supplies the following independently declared primitives:\n\n1. The finite square-lattice source/receiver apparatus, three-qubit input, courier and receiver cells; finite mode menu; static unused stocks; nearest-neighbor configuration paths and source pair wires.\n2. The degenerate ready cells, their finite protection/retention service, and the fresh source-side reset and pinching auxiliaries. Their zero idle Hamiltonian does not make their purchase or protection free.\n3. The finite-duration Hadamard, CNOT, SWAP, reversible finite lookup, and receipt-writing ports; their exact matrices and supplied external waveforms specified in Section 5.\n4. The exact finite lazy/proposal/acceptance preparation menu and 56 acceptance angles specified in Section 6. These are calibrated native coefficients, not exact real numbers represented by an unpriced finite bit string.\n5. The local mode, forecast and proper-score lookup services, all their physical ROM cells, and their complete truth-table rules in Section 4.\n6. The finite externally driven timing schedule, fresh phase flags, execution receipts, and complete positive stock/service/retention tariff of Sections 7–8.\n7. The input/output intervention cut, source-only grading and baths, the complete receiver output algebra, and the independent-episode environment law below.\n\nThe source is an **open, externally driven finite native instrument**. It does not purport to compile all these ports into a particular Boolean gate library, derive their apparatus from a finite isolated Hamiltonian clock, or establish empirical manufacturability. The delivered coupling, not an inert ROM string alone, is part of each priced native work port. An additional microscopic supplier, driver response port, command, or bath access would require a new inventory and channel audit.\n\nAll source bath/reset/pinching registers, source copies of labels, the grader, and adaptation randomness are physically on the source side. There is no wire, optical output, receiver command, or receiver-accessible storage path from them to the receiver except the explicitly listed local crossing gates. They are not excluded by an accounting convention. Source-side local wires never cross the boundary.\n\nThe sole always-present configuration crossing is at lattice site (0,0), which belongs to every mode's active set. Before the unknown input boundary, its existing link sends the three classical mode bits to the receiver seed. This adds no geometric edge and is not a label-dependent channel output for fixed x. The mode is fixed before the admitted input. Admitting a sender's message as an extra mode-selection command would define a different channel and is not claimed here.\n\nThe rest of the lattice receives the mode via two nearest-neighbor snake paths, one in each layer. They have A−1 edges each; the three mode bits are copied along each path. Source and receiver tables then determine active and anchor masks locally from that mode and the station's fixed lattice address. There is no additional crossing edge at an inactive site. All data couplings are switched off outside their prescribed slots. A lattice site has only its path neighbors, its optional source pair neighbor, its local crossing neighbor, and a fixed finite number of internal ports. The intercell degree and edge range are uniformly bounded. Every native local lookup has bounded arity independent of L; a giant all-lattice scoring gate is not used.\n\n### 3. Literal cells, active routing and full receiver output\n\nEach period has a fresh package. The following are distinct physical two-level cells; readiness is included in their positive stock price. Source input and courier cells are grouped in threes. ROM and actuator stocks are separately counted in Section 8, not charged as these cells again.\n\n| Cells per package | Count | Side and purpose |\n|---|---:|---|\n| Z, persistent target bits | 2A | Source; same full task for every mode |\n| T, fair-preparation shadow | 2A | Source; retained purifier/record |\n| I, potential three-bit inputs | 3A | Source; independent intervention only at active anchors |\n| B, reset auxiliaries | 3A | Source; fresh ready SWAP destinations |\n| P, pinching pointers | 3A | Source |\n| Q, pinching dephasing shadows | 3A | Source |\n| E, potential couriers | 3A | Source; one cell at every possible crossing site |\n| Y, potential receiver cells | 3A | Receiver |\n| Local mode banks | 6A | Three bits per site per layer |\n| Local mask banks | 8A | Four fresh bits per site per layer |\n| Enabled-latch banks | 2A | One per site per layer |\n| Local forecast banks | 8A | Four bits per site per layer |\n| Local score banks | 2A | Source only |\n| Ready/completion flags | 2A | Receiver; fixed given successful schedule |\n| Dummy-loading bank | 2A | Source; fresh destinations only for arbitrary-input test mode |\n| Adaptation registers | 16 | Source; $X_{\\mathrm{old}}$(3), $X_{\\mathrm{new}}$(3), $J$(3), $J_{\\mathrm{shadow}}$(3), lazy(1), lazy_shadow(1), accept(1), accept_shadow(1) |\n| One-use operation receipts | $P_{\\mathrm{op}}=32A+12$ | One fresh cell for each dedicated possible operation port |\n| Phase flags | 2S | S fresh source and S fresh receiver flags |\n\nThus the exact non-ROM physical-cell count is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB_{\\mathrm{cell}} &= 52A+16+(32A+12)+2S = 84A+28+2S, \\\\\nS&=32A+32.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are additionally three initial source-side configuration cells shared by the finite $H$-period run, supplied ready in $X_{0}=000$, which denotes $(q,k,g)=(1,1,1)$. This is the physical initial law nominated here. Arbitrary alternative initial distributions are mathematical starting laws for the same kernel; supplying one physically would require its own preparation/record invoice. Every period receives the previous period's $X_{\\mathrm{new}}$ through three charged source-side copies into fresh $X_{\\mathrm{old}}$; previous states remain retained.\n\nAll A potential input/courier/receiver positions, every unused bit factor, disabled actuator, spare auxiliary and unused ROM row remain purchased and retained. Buying a spare does not turn its disabled crossing coupling into an active network edge.\n\nFor an active anchor $a$, data factors are $I_{a,1}$ through $I_{a,q}$. In task mode load $Z_{a,\\ell}$ into $I_{a,\\ell}$ by a CNOT, $q$ calls per anchor. For each nuisance factor $\\ell=q+1,\\dots,3$, apply $\\mathrm{SWAP}(I_{a,\\ell},B_{a,\\ell})$, exactly $(3-q)M$ calls. This is an actual reset to a supplied ready state and retains the removed input in $B$. It is not an erasure with an unpriced bath.\n\nFor each data factor apply $\\mathrm{CNOT}(I_{a,\\ell},P_{a,\\ell})$ and $\\mathrm{CNOT}(P_{a,\\ell},Q_{a,\\ell})$. The reduced $D$/$P$ instrument is computational-basis pinching with a retained outcome record; $Q$ is its explicitly retained source-side dilation shadow. No collapse postulate is used to delete a supplier record.\n\nFor **each** of the $g$ occupied sites $s$ in an anchor group, apply $\\mathrm{CNOT}(I_{a,\\ell},E_{s,\\ell})$. There are $qM$ onsite calls and $q(N-M)$ nearest-neighbor source-pair calls, exactly $qN$ courier-preparation calls in total. Then apply $\\mathrm{CNOT}(E_{s,\\ell},Y_{s,\\ell})$ at each site, exactly $qN$ local boundary-crossing calls. The pair copies happen wholly in the source layer. No anchor-to-two-distant-receivers gate is substituted for the $g$ separate crossing edges. $q$ data-factor interactions on one installed source/receiver cell pair are one geometric edge, not $q$ edges.\n\nFor arbitrary inputs and references, nuisance-reset auxiliaries and the entire source dilation are traced only when forming the receiver channel. For one group the Y part is exactly\n\n$$\n\\sum_j \\operatorname{Tr}[(|j\\rangle\\langle j|\\otimes I_R)\\rho]\\, |j,0\\rangle\\langle j,0|^{\\otimes g}.\n$$\n\nFor all groups the physically complete receiver output appends the forecast $F_x(j)$, every receiver mode/mask/enable/ready/phase flag, every receiver-side actuator receipt, fixed ROM/coefficient apparatus, and all inactive receiver cells. The latter outputs are fixed for $x$; the forecast is a deterministic function of the same label tuple $j$. In notation,\n\n$$\n\\Phi_x(\\rho) = \\sum_j \\operatorname{Tr}[\\Pi_j\\rho]\\, |Y_x(j),F_x(j)\\rangle\\langle Y_x(j),F_x(j)| \\otimes \\sigma_{\\mathrm{fixed},x}.\n$$\n\nThe reference extension uses the corresponding unnormalized conditional reference state in each $j$ block. The output has exactly $2^{qM}$ distinguishable data blocks; all its newly enumerated receiver outputs are included. Per group it still has $2^q$ blocks, hence capacity $q\\ln 2$, and duplicates do not add independently addressable inputs. A source reset or pinching output made accessible to the receiver would change this channel. No pricing argument is offered as a substitute for that physical exclusion.\n\n### 4. One unchanged environmental task and actual local scoring\n\nEach period prepares all 2A source Z bits by a Hadamard on ready Z and a CNOT from Z to its own fresh T shadow: 2A calls of each type. The admitted environment observable is the computational-basis classical algebra of Z. Its marginal is the product of 2A fair bits. Subsequent operations use Z only as control and preserve its value, so the next-time target is the same Z. T remains source-side. Equivalently, tracing the supplied shadow gives the exact finite fair classical preparation instrument.\n\nFor a run of H episodes, each episode uses a fresh independent Z with this same law, target dimension, horizon and scoring rule; within the episode it is persistent. The configuration kernel uses only registered prices and its own independent coins. Earlier receiver transcripts therefore do not reveal any new episode's ignored bits. This does not claim qM benefit on repeated prediction of one ever-accumulating, already learned realization of Z.\n\nAt site s, a local receiver forecast service receives the two potential Y data bits, the q−1 flag, and the anchor flag. It produces two two-bit forecast codes: 00 means probability 1/2, 01 means probability one on zero, and 10 means probability one on one. At an active anchor the first q target bits receive their observed deterministic forecasts; every other target bit receives 00. The source runs the identical local forecast rule on the retained I data and its own masks. On the task branch these source forecasts equal the actual receiver forecasts exactly. This source mirror adds no receiver information.\n\nThe source grader at each site reads that site's two Z bits and four forecast-code bits. It writes a two-bit score symbol in {0,1,2,INF}. A deterministic forecast disagreeing with Z, or reserved code 11, writes INF; otherwise it writes the number of unknown (00) forecasts. INF is a finite symbol representing extended logarithmic loss, not infinite stored energy. On the registered task it has probability zero. The sum of local score symbols, interpreted externally as an additive ledger, is the exact base-2 logarithmic loss. No uncharged all-lattice physical adder is claimed or needed.\n\nComplete finite lookup stock:\n\n- Mode lookup at each site in each layer: 3 input mode bits, 4 fresh mask outputs (active, anchor, second-data-enabled, pair-enabled), 8 rows × 4 bits. Total $64A$ ROM cells. Fixed lattice address is part of that site's delivered table, not an extra unknown command.\n- Forecast lookup in each layer at each site: 4 input bits, 4 fresh output bits, 16 rows × 4 bits. Total $128A$ ROM cells.\n- Score lookup at each source site: 6 input bits, 2 fresh output bits, 64 rows × 2 bits. Total $128A$ ROM cells.\n- Adaptation transition lookup: 8 input bits ($X_{\\mathrm{old}}$, $J$, lazy, accept), 3 fresh output bits, 256 rows × 3 bits. Total 768 ROM cells. Rule in Section 6.\n\nHence $B_{\\mathrm{ROM}}=320A+768$. Each truth table is delivered together with its actual reversible lookup work port $U_f\\colon|u,v\\rangle\\mapsto|u,v\\oplus f(u)\\rangle$. It has $U_f^2=I$ and retains its inputs. The table prices cover physical ROM readiness; the separate actuator stock price covers its coupling apparatus. They are different objects, not two purchases of the same cell.\n\nEvery one of the $2A$ target bits remains in the scoring task. Exactly $qM$ distinct bits are revealed. Conditional independence proves optimal mean loss $2A-qM$ and improvement $B=qM$ against every predictor using the complete receiver transcript. $\\gamma B$ is an independently priced prediction benefit. It is not source heat, an erasure rebate, or a theorem equating capacity to utility in other environments.\n\n### 5. Finite-duration gates, actual drive ports and work types\n\nSet $\\theta=\\tau/S$. Every scheduled slot has positive duration $\\theta$. On the declared working cells the idle Hamiltonian is zero, with strictly positive physical readiness and retention prices. For any finite involution $W$ above, let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(t)&=1-\\cos(2\\pi t/\\theta),\\qquad 0\\le t\\le \\theta, \\\\\nH_W(t)&=(\\pi\\hbar/(2\\theta)) f(t)(I-W).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis positive Hamiltonian vanishes at both endpoints, has peak norm at most $2\\pi\\hbar/\\theta$, and has exact endpoint unitary $W$ because $\\int f\\,dt=\\theta$. It implements the Hadamard, CNOT, SWAP and reversible lookup matrices, including their action on arbitrary references. It is an actual finite-time matrix coupling, not payment taken as proof that a desired map occurs.\n\nEach possible operation has a dedicated driver receipt cell. A used operation flips its receipt from zero to one with the same finite $X$-pulse construction; an unused port leaves it zero. The receipt is controlled only by the previously fixed mode/schedule, never by an unknown input label or a microscopic input-dependent work measurement. Source-side receipts remain source-side; every receiver-side receipt is included in $\\sigma_{\\mathrm{fixed},x}$. A native operation can run its target pulse and its independent receipt pulse in the same slot. The two phase flags for that slot are also set by finite $X$ pulses on fresh cells. No existing flag is overwritten, and no record is reset for reuse.\n\nThe acceptance rotation below has a positive generator bounded by $\\pi$ and the same envelope. Thus a conservative simultaneous driven-working-register peak bound for one operation, its receipt and two phase flags is\n\n$$\nE_{\\mathrm{peak}} \\le 8\\pi\\hbar/\\theta = 8\\pi\\hbar S/\\tau.\n$$\n\nA whole slot's operations have finite support. The configuration and data moves across lattice distances use only the declared nearest-neighbor or local edges. Increasing $L$ makes $S$ grow; the shorter $\\theta$ and growing peak reservation are openly part of this finite source family. There is no claim of a uniform speed, energy, or practical manufacturing bound as $L\\to\\infty$.\n\nAt slot endpoints, the signed work operator on the declared degenerate working registers is $U^*H_{\\mathrm{idle}}U-H_{\\mathrm{idle}}=0$. That statement is not the energy cost of a microscopic clock, driver, ready-cell factory or holding service. Those suppliers are the explicitly priced native open boundary. No equality between their resource tariffs and joules is claimed; no heat value is inferred from this zero endpoint work. The positive interaction peaks are retained in the energy-reservation ledger rather than subtracted as nonexistent. A more microscopic supplier model would need its own signed work/heat/stored-energy and output audit.\n\nThe exact external phase service and waveforms are constitutive source data. The finite schedule is not presented as an autonomous finite isolated clock or as an exact real waveform reconstructed from a free numerical program.\n\n### 6. Exact finite lazy-Metropolis implementation\n\nIndex configurations by $x=4(q-1)+2(k-1)+(g-1)$ in $\\{0,\\dots,7\\}$, with the three mode bits in that order. For a fixed $L$, positive primitive prices, $\\gamma$, $\\tau$ and $\\beta>0$, calculate the eight declared tariff potentials first. Put\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(x,j)&=(x+j) \\bmod 8,\\qquad j=1,\\dots,7, \\\\\n\\alpha_{xj}&=\\min\\{1,\\exp[-\\beta(V_y-V_x)]\\}, \\\\\n\\vartheta_{xj}&=\\arcsin(\\sqrt{\\alpha_{xj}}).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly these 56 angles are installed. Their exact calibration is an explicit new finite native menu for the selected parameters, not an arbitrary-real runtime oracle. The fixed common costs cancel from $V_y-V_x$, so the angles do not depend on $H$ or on which record happens to be produced. The bounded-angle service reservation is independent of their values; it does not use cheap prices for downhill moves.\n\nPrepare a lazy qubit in $|+\\rangle$, and prepare the three-bit proposal register in\n\n  $$\n  |u_7\\rangle=(1/\\sqrt{7})\\sum_{j=1}^7|j\\rangle.\n  $$\n\nA concrete proposal unitary is the involution $W_7=I-2|v\\rangle\\langle v|$, where $|v\\rangle=(|000\\rangle-|u_7\\rangle)/\\sqrt{2}$. It maps $|000\\rangle$ to $|u_7\\rangle$ exactly and is implemented by Section 5. Copy lazy and each proposal bit into their own source shadows. The reduced lazy/proposal registers are an independent fair bit and exactly uniform seven-way proposal. No rejection loop or unbounded fair-bit decoding is used.\n\nFor fixed classical $X_{\\mathrm{old}}$ and $J$, prepare the acceptance qubit by $U_A=\\exp(-iK)$,\n\n$$\nK = \\sum_{x=0}^7 \\sum_{j=1}^7 |x,j\\rangle\\langle x,j|\\otimes \\vartheta_{xj} \\sigma_y,\n$$\n\nwith the $j=0$ block zero. The positive pulse generator $K+(\\pi/2)I$ has spectrum in $[0,\\pi]$; its supplied envelope implements $U_{A}$ up to a common global phase. Conditional acceptance-one probability is $\\sin^2\\vartheta_{xj}=\\alpha_{xj}$. Copy the acceptance qubit into a fresh source shadow. All six random-register/shadow factors are retained; no measurement outcome is discarded.\n\nThe finite transition table writes into fresh $X_{\\mathrm{new}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\nf(x,j,l,b)=y(x,j)\\quad\\text{if }j\\in\\{1,\\dots,7\\},\\ l=1\\text{ and }b=1; \\\\\nf(x,j,l,b)=x\\quad\\text{otherwise.}\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nThus its complete table also defines the unused proposal-zero rows. Its reversible native implementation retains $X_{\\mathrm{old}}$, J, lazy and accept. The exact transition probabilities are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x,y) &= \\alpha_{xy}/14 \\quad \\text{for } y\\ne x, \\\\\nP(x,x) &= 1-\\sum_{y\\ne x} \\alpha_{xy}/14 \\ge 1/2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvery branch, including lazy idle and rejection, executes the same proposal, acceptance, shadow, transition and configuration-distribution slots. The acceptance coin is prepared even on lazy idle. There is no omitted rejection apparatus and no branch-dependent runtime. All 56 angle blocks and the unused $j=0$ block remain installed and paid. The per-package adaptation reservation is identical for every current state, accepted/rejected result and tariff value within the nominated bounded-angle menu.\n\nThe resulting finite chain is irreducible, aperiodic and reversible for $\\pi_{x}$ proportional to $\\exp(-\\beta V_x)$. Detailed balance follows from $\\exp(-\\beta V_x)\\alpha_{xy}=\\min(\\exp(-\\beta V_x),\\exp(-\\beta V_y))$. For finite $\\beta$ it does not permanently stay in a minimizer. As $\\beta\\to\\infty$ its invariant mass concentrates on the complete minimizing set, uniformly within exact ties.\n\n### 7. Exact slot census and all inactive reservations\n\nUse $S=32A+32$ slots of length $\\theta$. Predetermined station order is the fixed snake path and the fixed lattice-address order. Allocate one slot to each possible operation below, even when its mode disables it. A disabled operation leaves its target and its operation receipt unchanged; its clock/phase reservation still runs. Fixed operation counts are actual calls in every mode. Conditional counts below are the number of actual calls, not the number of purchased ports.\n\n| Operation class | Dedicated possible slots/ports | Actual calls in x |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Previous-state source copies into fresh $X_{\\mathrm{old}}$ | 3 | 3 |\n| Lazy preparation | 1 | 1 |\n| Uniform-seven proposal preparation | 1 | 1 |\n| Randomness shadow copies (1 lazy, 3 proposal, 1 accept) | 5 | 5 |\n| Controlled acceptance preparation | 1 | 1 |\n| Transition lookup into $X_{\\mathrm{new}}$ | 1 | 1 |\n| Source seed copies of $X_{\\mathrm{new}}$ | 3 | 3 |\n| Existing-link configuration seed crossing | 3 | 3 |\n| Source and receiver nearest-neighbor mode distribution | 6(A−1) | 6(A−1) |\n| Source and receiver mask lookups | 2A | 2A |\n| Source and receiver enabled-latch services | 2A | 2N |\n| Environment Hadamards and preparation shadows | 4A | 4A |\n| Task loading into data factors | 2A | qM |\n| Nuisance-factor SWAPs (possible factors 2,3) | 2A | (3−q)M |\n| Pinching pointer copies | 2A | qM |\n| Pinching shadow copies | 2A | qM |\n| Onsite source courier copies | 2A | qM |\n| Possible nearest-neighbor pair courier copies | $A$ | $q(N-M)$ |\n| Local data crossing copies | 2A | qN |\n| Source and receiver forecast lookups | 2A | 2A |\n| Source local score lookups | A | A |\n| Receiver ready/completion flag writes | 2A | 2A |\n| Pure padding slots | 20 | 20 |\n\nThe possible operation ports total $P_{\\mathrm{op}}=32A+12$; adding 20 pure padding slots gives $S$ exactly. The randomness stages occur in causal order: lazy/proposal preparation; corresponding shadows; acceptance preparation; acceptance shadow; transition; mode seeding/distribution. Then masks/latches precede all environment/data operations. Fair preparation precedes loading; reset precedes pinching; pinching precedes all couriers; all couriers precede all crossing copies; forecast and score follow. Ready/completion writes are scheduled at their named preparation/terminal boundaries. Reordering the independent fixed rows to this causal order does not change any count.\n\nAt every slot two separate phase work ports set fresh phase flags. Therefore $P_{\\mathrm{phase}}=2S$ and the total distinct actuator-stock count is\n\n$$\nP_{\\mathrm{port}}=P_{\\mathrm{op}}+2S=32A+12+2S.\n$$\n\nThe timing source supplies exactly S clock/phase intervals, with no runtime looping. Its phase flags, operation receipts and terminal flags exhaust its receiver-visible records. The predetermined command grammar is one execution of this finite schedule; there are no additional accepted runtime commands or unbounded retry tokens. In arbitrary-input channel mode, active anchors accept arbitrary three-qubit states at the nominated input cut, independently of supplier readiness; the qM paid loading reservations run on their designated dummy inputs rather than acting on the unknown data. The loading work ports and tariff remain charged, while the reset/pinching/copying map is unchanged. The separately inventoried 2A dummy-loading cells supply all qM needed destinations; no target, preparation shadow or already-used auxiliary is reused.\n\nThe 2A dummy-loading cells remain unused in task mode and add no communication port. Each active loading call in arbitrary-input mode uses the same Z control and its corresponding fresh dummy bit instead of I. Its routing selector is fixed before the accepted input. Both destinations and the native selector are included in the delivered loading actuator. This task/arbitrary-input selector is not a message input and is not optimized as a ninth configuration. The arbitrary unknown state is the state supplied at the mathematical input cut; its external preparation or reference system is not falsely supplied by this apparatus.\n\n### 8. Exact positive invoice, retention and finite-H accounting\n\nAll charges below are in a source resource unit $R$. $\\gamma$ is in $R$ per correctly revealed task bit, $\\tau$ in time, $V$ in $R$/time, and $\\beta$ in inverse $V$. Fix positive prices $r_{\\mathrm{cell}}$, $r_{\\mathrm{ROM}}$, $r_{\\mathrm{port}}$ for delivered physical ready cells, physical ROM cells, and native actuator work ports. The actuator price inclusively supplies its stated matrix coefficients and coupling, including selector and bounded pulse/receipt capabilities. It does not also charge its separately inventoried record or ROM cells. These are fixed manufacturing prices, independent of selected mode and observed outcomes.\n\nDefine\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB&=B_{\\mathrm{cell}}=84A+28+2S, \\\\\n\\mathrm{Rm}&=B_{\\mathrm{ROM}}=320A+768, \\\\\nP&=P_{\\mathrm{port}}=32A+12+2S, \\\\\nC_{\\mathrm{ready}}&=r_{\\mathrm{cell}} B+r_{\\mathrm{ROM}} \\mathrm{Rm}+r_{\\mathrm{port}} P.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvery actuator has one specified finite coupling and native driven-service boundary. No unlisted drive, routing compiler, copying memory, phase register, source preparation service or infinite random-bit generator is assumed.\n\nLet all of the following prices be independently positive. The exact fixed executed/reserved service bill per package is\n\n  $$\n  F(L)=3s_{\\mathrm{handoff}}+s_{\\mathrm{lazy}}+s_{\\mathrm{proposal}}+5s_{\\mathrm{random\\_record}}+s_{\\mathrm{accept}}+s_{\\mathrm{transition}}+3s_{\\mathrm{seed},S}+3s_{\\mathrm{seed},\\mathrm{cross}}+3(A-1)(s_{\\mathrm{distribute},S}+s_{\\mathrm{distribute},R})+A(s_{\\mathrm{mask},S}+s_{\\mathrm{mask},R})+2A(s_{\\mathrm{fair},H}+s_{\\mathrm{fair},\\mathrm{copy}})+A(s_{\\mathrm{forecast},S}+s_{\\mathrm{forecast},R}+s_{\\mathrm{score}})+2A\\,s_{\\mathrm{flag}}+2S\\,s_{\\mathrm{phase}}+S\\,s_{\\mathrm{clock}}.\n  $$\n\nThe S clock reservations include timing and all unused/padding intervals; they do not purchase any additional target-operation call. The 2S phase services purchase the two finite flag pulses in every interval. The target operation services in F and below are distinct supplier ports. No realized target operation is also billed as an extra dummy target operation. Fixed complete bounded-angle reservation $s_{\\mathrm{accept}}$ is charged for every update, independently of $\\alpha$ and the idle/accept/reject result. All unused physical angle branches are already included in $C_{\\mathrm{ready}}$.\n\nThe exact variable executed/reserved service bill is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nG_x&=(s_{\\mathrm{enable},S}+s_{\\mathrm{enable},R})N \\\\\n&\\quad +s_{\\mathrm{load}}\\, qM+s_{\\mathrm{reset}}(3-q)M+s_{\\mathrm{pin}}\\, qM+s_{\\mathrm{dephase}}\\, qM \\\\\n&\\quad +s_{\\mathrm{route}}[qM+q(N-M)]+s_{\\mathrm{cross}}\\, qN.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEach term refers to a row of Section 7. The N active source latches and N active receiver latches are different ports. The qN source courier and qN crossing services are different ports. The two pinching stages are different ports. No original input, reset output or supplier record is free. Service tariffs are reservation/delivery prices for these actual finite calls; they are not measured signed work values. The unused stock charges are in $C_{\\mathrm{ready}}$, while no fictitious realized gate is attributed to an inactive stock.\n\nTo retain **all** $H$ packages, including old receiver outputs, until the common final deadline $H\\tau$, use just-in-time package delivery at the start of each period and independent positive holding rates $h_{\\mathrm{cell}},h_{\\mathrm{ROM}},h_{\\mathrm{port}}$. Put\n\n$$\nK_{\\mathrm{hold}}=h_{\\mathrm{cell}} B+h_{\\mathrm{ROM}} \\mathrm{Rm}+h_{\\mathrm{port}} P.\n$$\n\nPackage $t$ is held for $(H-t+1)\\tau$. The complete physical holding invoice is therefore\n\n$$\nC_{\\mathrm{hold}}(H)=\\tau H(H+1) K_{\\mathrm{hold}}/2 + 3h_{\\mathrm{cell}} H\\tau.\n$$\n\nThe last term owns the three initial configuration cells. Their acquisition costs another $3r_{\\mathrm{cell}}$. No archived package is erased or recycled, and no old receiver output disappears from the complete retained transcript. At the stated final deadline the service claim ends; longer archival protection requires a new holding invoice. No single finite stock is claimed to implement infinitely many updates.\n\nFor any realized $H$-step configuration path $x_1,\\dots,x_H$, the complete tariff and proper-score benefit are exactly\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC_{\\mathrm{run}} &= 3r_{\\mathrm{cell}} + H[C_{\\mathrm{ready}}+F(L)] + C_{\\mathrm{hold}}(H) + \\sum_t G_{x_t}, \\\\\nB_{\\mathrm{run}} &= \\sum_t q_t M_t, \\\\\n\\tau \\sum_t V_{x_t} &= C_{\\mathrm{run}} - \\gamma B_{\\mathrm{run}},\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nusing the equal per-period allocation\n\n$$\nC_0(L,H)=C_{\\mathrm{ready}}+F(L)+\\tau(H+1)K_{\\mathrm{hold}}/2+3h_{\\mathrm{cell}}\\tau+3r_{\\mathrm{cell}}/H.\n$$\n\nThis allocation is an accounting identity for the complete finite run. The delivered operation/adaptation package tariff $C_{\\mathrm{ready}}+F$ is horizon-independent. **Full archival retention is not:** its explicitly displayed common premium grows with $H$. It would be false to call the entire all-records-held-forever source a horizon-independent fixed-cost apparatus. None of this changes differences $V_y-V_x$ or the minimizing set, because the full retention premium is common to all eight configurations. Thus all finite-$H$ sources implement the same nominated transition kernel; their consistent finite-dimensional path laws define a mathematical infinite chain without asserting a finite infinite-duration physical machine.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q24d-derived-feasible-set-pce-objective-and-complete-stochastic-classification\"></a>**Theorem Q.2.4d (Derived Feasible Set, PCE Objective and Complete Stochastic Classification).** The source of Definition Q.2.4c realizes the original coordinates\n\n$$\n(C_{\\max}, \\chi, \\eta)=(q \\ln 2,1/g,k),\\quad q,k,g \\in \\{1,2\\},\n$$\n\nand no other manufacturing modes. Its complete retained-output capacity and same-task prediction benefit, full source-tariff objective, all minimizer and tie regions, and paid lazy adaptation law have the following exact forms.\n\n*Proof.*\n\n### 2. Actual completed channel and full output boundary\n\nWrite the input space as $D_q\\otimes R_q$ with dimensions $d=2^q$ and $r=2^{3-q}$. A source-side swap moves $R_{q}$ into a supplied ready auxiliary and sets the cell's $R_{q}$ factor to $|0\\rangle$. A computational-basis measurement/pinching of $D_{q}$ uses an independent source-side record. Controlled finite permutations copy the resulting classical label $j$ into $q$ bits of each of the $g$ receiver-side 8-level cells; the other receiver bits remain $|0\\rangle$. This is copying a classical diagonal label, not cloning unknown quantum states. The group input and all reset/pinching auxiliaries stay source-side.\n\nThe full retained communication-output channel, including every response-active receiver flag, is\n\n  $$\n  \\Phi_{qg}(\\rho)=\\sum_{j=0}^{d-1}\\operatorname{Tr}[(|j\\rangle\\langle j|\\otimes I_r)\\rho]\\,|j,0\\rangle\\langle j,0|^{\\otimes g}\\otimes|\\text{fixed}\\rangle\\langle\\text{fixed}|_{\\mathrm{flags}}.\n  $$\n\nIts extension to references is the displayed quantum instrument with the unnormalized conditional reference state in each $j$ block. At the completed episode, append the actual receiver forecast record $f_x(j_1,\\dots,j_M)$, all intermediate receiver records, and timing/control transcripts. Every such record is a deterministic function of the same retained labels and public setting $x$, or a fixed padded value; there is no other label-dependent receiver output. Thus these records preserve the exact $d^{M}$ output alphabet and all capacity conclusions. The forecast is not incorrectly represented as a fixed flag. Every admitted intervention preserves the same diagonal output algebra. The hard-wired map factors through exactly one group input even if all source-side nuisance factors are varied. The immutable controller/phase/receipt ports expose no label-dependent side channel except $j$. All active receiver-side outputs are included above.\n\nThe reset and dephasing auxiliaries are physically retained and fully charged, but are NOT communication outputs: they have no crossing path or receiver access. Price alone does not exclude information. Opening a bath, sender, timing or controller output port would change $\\Phi$ and require recomputing capacity; in particular supplying the reset auxiliary as output can restore the missing capacity. This is the accepted AREA02 boundary distinction, not a claim that discarded information has ceased to exist.\n\nThe output support has dimension $d$, not $d^{g}$. For $n$ uses, including arbitrary entangled encodings, its dimension is $d^{n}$, giving Holevo information $\\le n\\ln d$. Orthogonal computational labels attain $n\\ln d$ with zero error. Therefore its regularized classical capacity is $\\ln d=q\\ln 2$. Each individual geometric link has the same marginal capacity, so $C_{\\max}=q\\ln 2$. Across $M$ independent group sources the response algebra has exactly $d^{M}$ distinct labels, with an explicit product achieving code. Across $N$ geometric links the equivalence relation is equality of every anchor label: duplicate positions supply no additional response distinction. Thus $N_{\\mathrm{eff}}=M$, $\\chi=M/N=1/g$. Two freely addressable channels merely sent equal data would instead have $\\chi=1$; that is explicitly not this source.\n\nThe physical geometry gives $\\sigma_{\\mathrm{geom}}=N/(A\\delta^{2})=1/(k\\delta^{2})$, hence $\\eta=k$ from the original E.3 definition. These identities are exact at every $L$; there is no boundary remainder on the periodic cross-section. The finite-size density sequence has the same constants. No continuum metric or gravitational calibration is inferred from that sequence.\n\nConsequently the complete feasible coordinate set is\n\n$$\nF=\\{(q \\ln 2,1/g,k): q,k,g\\in \\{1,2\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nAll eight records exist and are distinct in the retained coordinate quotient; the fixed three-bit manufacturing grammar has no additional configurations. F is a finite product set, derived from the actual admissible channels and geometry rather than postulated as a box.\n\n### 3. Same prediction task and benefit\n\nScore the next-time full fixed target $Z$ under base-2 logarithmic loss. Before reading any boundary signal its optimal loss is $2A$ bits. The channel reveals precisely the first $q$ bits at each of $M$ distinct anchors; all remaining source bits are independent fair bits conditional on the entire receiver transcript. The predictor assigns probability one to revealed values and one half to each unrevealed bit. Its mean loss is exactly $2A-qM$, with no approximation, and its improvement is $B=qM$ bits. Conditional entropy proves optimality against every predictor using the admitted receiver output. Repeated copies have no extra benefit, and inactive targets do not disappear from the task.\n\nThis provides an actual proper-scoring performance observable, not capacity assumed to equal utility. The capacity and predictive benefit happen to coincide in bits on this explicitly demonstrated source/code, with $B=MC_{\\max}/\\ln 2$. The power-equivalent benefit price $\\gamma>0$ per bit per period is registered independently, and is not extracted work or heat.\n\n### 1. Complete affine-value classifier, including reset work\n\nThe invoice is\n\n$$\n\\tau V(q,k,g)=C_0+(A/k)[a+bq+(cq+r(3-q)-\\gamma q)/g],\n$$\n\nwith $a,b,c,r,\\gamma>0$ independently registered and $q,k,g\\in\\{1,2\\}$. $C_0,\\tau,A$ are common positive source quantities. \n\nIn lexicographic $(q,k,g)$ order define $p=(a,b,c,r,\\gamma)$ and coefficient rows\n\n$$\nv_x=(1/k,\\ q/k,\\ q/(kg),\\ (3-q)/(kg),\\ -q/(kg)).\n$$\n\nThe full eight-state tariff classification is the following explicit rational polyhedral fan. For every nonempty subset S of the eight rows define the region\n\n  $$\n  R_S=\\{p>0:\\ (v_i-v_j)\\cdot p=0\\ \\text{for}\\ i,j\\in S;\\ (v_l-v_i)\\cdot p>0\\ \\text{for}\\ i\\in S,\\ l\\notin S\\}.\n  $$\n\nExactly on $R_{S}$ the complete minimizer set is $S$. Empty regions contribute no case. These 255 finite Boolean systems are a coverage-complete classification: for every positive $p$, its finite nonempty argmin $S$ satisfies exactly its displayed system, and no different $S$ can satisfy it. All boundary/tie strata are included, with no sampled tariff restriction. Every comparison hyperplane is explicitly $(v_i-v_j)\\cdot p=0$; duplications do not change the regions. This is a source-exhaustive decidable classifier rather than a restriction to generic unique optima. Exact rational tariffs can be evaluated using integer cross-multiplication; symbolic tariffs retain the displayed exact inequalities.\n\nThe strict source gap to nonminimizers is\n\n$$\n\\Delta V=(A/\\tau) \\min_{l\\notin S}(v_l-v_i)\\cdot p>0,\\quad i\\in S.\n$$\n\nIt is an exact minimum of finitely many positive linear forms on each nonempty $R_{S}$. If S is the whole set the gap convention is +∞. No term is fitted to a desired coordinate optimum.\n\nFor independently nominated native prices $a=2$, $b=2$, $c=3$, $r=1$ (each actual native primitive price is 1) and proper-score price $\\gamma=1/2$, the eight values excluding $C_{0}$ and divided by $A$ are, in lexicographic order:\n\n  17/2, 25/4, 17/4, 25/8, 12, 9, 6, 9/2.\n\nThus the unique minimizer is $(q,k,g)=(1,2,2)$, corresponding to $(C_{\\max},\\chi,\\eta)=(\\ln 2,1/2,2)$, and the exact gap is $(9A)/(8\\tau)$. The historical preferred point $(2\\ln 2,1,1)$ is not selected. The output of this bounded optimizer is a source result, not a calibration of Planck spacing.\n\nThe invoice may derive a,b,c,r as positive sums of more primitive tariffs rather than allowing every positive aggregate tuple. Then the actual admitted price class is the corresponding linear image; restrict every $R_{S}$ to that image. The classifier still covers it exactly. The stated primitive-price split realizes the concrete aggregate example.\n\n### 2. Frozen finite-state update\n\nLet E be the eight configurations, $\\beta>0$ an independently registered inverse resource-rate noise parameter. In one update frame, choose y uniformly from the seven states different from current x. Use an independent Bernoulli acceptance bit with probability\n\n$$\na_{xy}=\\min(1, \\exp(-\\beta[V(y)-V(x)])).\n$$\n\nThen move to y if accepted and otherwise remain x. To guarantee an explicit self loop at every state even when all proposals are accepted, perform this proposal only with probability 1/2; otherwise idle. Thus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{xy} &= (1/14)\\, a_{xy} \\quad \\text{for } y\\ne x, \\\\\nP_{xx} &= 1-\\sum_{y\\ne x}P_{xy}\\ge 1/2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe complete discrete kernel is stochastic and irreducible because all $a_{xy}>0$. Define $\\pi_x=\\exp(-\\beta V(x))/\\sum_z\\exp(-\\beta V(z))$. Then $\\pi_xP_{xy}=(1/14)\\min(\\pi_x,\\pi_y)=\\pi_yP_{yx}$. Therefore $\\pi$ is invariant and unique. An elementary uniform convergence bound follows: let $R=\\max V-\\min V$ and $\\varepsilon=e^{-\\beta R}/14$. Every entry of $P$ is at least $\\varepsilon$, so $P=8\\varepsilon U+(1-8\\varepsilon)Q$ for the uniform-row matrix $U$ and a stochastic $Q$. Consequently total-variation distances contract by at most $1-8\\varepsilon$ per step. This proves convergence from every initial state and stability of the unique invariant law. At positive $\\beta$ no claim of permanent convergence to a particular configuration is made.\n\nLet $m=|S|$ and gap $\\Delta V>0$. Then\n\n$$\n\\pi(E\\setminus S) \\le ((8-m)/m) \\exp(-\\beta\\Delta V).\n$$\n\nWithin S the stationary weights are exactly equal. Thus the low-noise limiting law is uniform on the complete minimizing set. The bound includes every tie identified above. These statements concern the adopted paid stochastic update source, not a universal law of physical adaptation.\n\n∎\n\n**Conditional Horizon-Cell Spectrum.** On the joint residual-budget and ideal-packing branch, the identities are\n\n$$\nC_{\\max}^*=\\ln8-\\ln2=\\ln4,\n\\qquad \\delta^2=4L_P^2\\ln4.\n$$\n\nSection Z.6 gives a four-dimensional syndrome factor whose maximum entropy is $\\ln4$. The canonical attractor has syndrome $00$ with certainty, so its actual syndrome entropy is zero. The four-state capacity and the entropy of a specified occupied state are distinct quantities. A horizon state with independent maximally mixed syndrome factors would have $S=N\\ln4$; these are the occupancy and independence premises for that entropy.\n\n**Area-spectrum hypothesis.** Assume an integer number of horizon cells, a fixed physical cell area $\\delta^2$, an additive area operator, and transitions changing that cell number by one. This supplies an area spacing\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\mathcal A=4L_P^2\\ln4.\n$$\n\nThe spacing identity alone supplies an area scale. The integer-cell and transition hypotheses give it a spectral interpretation. Its observable spectrum is specified by allowed transitions, matrix elements, degeneracies and line broadening.\n\nFor a Schwarzschild state with $\\mathcal A=16\\pi G^2M^2/c^4$ and $0<\\Delta\\mathcal A\\le\\mathcal A$, loss of one area increment gives the exact emitted energy\n\n$$\nE=Mc^2\\left[1-\\sqrt{1-\\Delta\\mathcal A/\\mathcal A}\\right].\n$$\n\nFor $\\Delta\\mathcal A/\\mathcal A\\ll1$,\n\n$$\nE=\\frac{c^6\\Delta\\mathcal A}{32\\pi G^2M}\n\\left[1+O(\\Delta\\mathcal A/\\mathcal A)\\right]\n=k_BT_H\\ln4\\left[1+O(\\Delta\\mathcal A/\\mathcal A)\\right].\n$$\n\nThe expansion follows from $1-\\sqrt{1-x}=x/2+O(x^2)$ and $k_BT_H=\\hbar c^3/(8\\pi GM)$. Emitted transition energies and ringdown frequencies use distinct observable maps.\n\nThe proposed relation\n\n$$\ne^{\\delta^2/(4L_P^2)}=(1+u^*)^M/2=d_0/a\n$$\n\nis retained on the conjunction of the spacing, binary active-rank, and saturation branches. It provides a check of shared normalizations.\n\n## Q.5 Final Result and Interpretation\n\nOn the intersection of the residual-capacity, throughput-saturated, channel-independence, and ideal-packing branches, the registered values are:\n* $C_{\\max}^* = 2\\ln 2$\n* $\\chi^* = 1$\n* $\\eta^* = 1$\n* $\\delta/L_P = \\sqrt{8\\ln 2} \\approx 2.355$\n\nSubstituting the residual-capacity, throughput-saturated, and ideal-packing branch values into the foundational relation (Q.3) yields:\n\n$$\\frac{\\delta^2}{L_P^2} = \\frac{4\\chi^* C_{\\max}^*}{\\eta^*}$$\n\nWith $\\chi^*=1$, $\\eta^*=1$, and $C_{\\max}^*=2\\ln2$ on those respective branches, this gives\n\n$$\\boxed{\\frac{\\delta^2}{L_P^2}=8\\ln2,\\qquad\\frac{\\delta}{L_P}=\\sqrt{8\\ln2}\\approx2.355}.\\tag{Q.18}$$\n\nThus the combined branch determines the dimensionless spatial ratio by the algebraic substitution\n$$\n\\frac{\\delta}{L_P}\n=\\sqrt{\\frac{4\\chi^*C_{\\max}^*}{\\eta^*}}\n=\\sqrt{\\frac{4\\cdot1\\cdot2\\ln2}{1}}\n=\\sqrt{8\\ln2}.\n$$\nIf the residual-capacity coefficient is replaced by $C_{\\max}=2\\lambda_C\\ln2$, the same relation gives $\\delta/L_P=\\sqrt{8\\lambda_C\\ln2}$. These identities establish conditional compatibility on the combined branch. Existence, uniqueness, and stability of a global PCE optimizer require a coupled PCE objective derived from the retained dynamics, which Theorem Q.2.4a shows Equation Q.3 does not select.\n\n## Q.6 Conditional Serialized-Frontier Calibration of the Temporal Scale\n\nEquation Q.18 fixes the spatial ratio $\\delta/L_P=\\sqrt{8\\ln2}$ only on its residual-budget, throughput-saturated, ideal-packing branch. A temporal ratio requires additional propagation and clock data.\n\n### Q.6.1 Serialized-Frontier Calibration\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q61-conditional-serialized-frontier-scale-calibration\"></a>**Proposition Q.6.1 (Conditional Serialized-Frontier Scale Calibration).** Assume:\n\n1. a separately registered positive lower edge-update duration $\\tau_{\\min}$;\n2. nonzero spacing $\\delta$, edge-by-edge serialization, and bounded weights as in Theorem 46;\n3. normalized uniform weights and one-link frontier attainment, so the attained speed is independently identified as\n   $$\n   c=\\frac{\\delta}{\\tau_{\\min}};\n   $$\n4. the Planck definitions, which give $c=L_P/t_P$.\n\nThen\n$$\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\delta}{L_P}=\\frac{\\tau_{\\min}}{t_P}}.\n$$\n\n*Proof.* Under assumption 3 and the Planck identity,\n$$\n\\frac{\\delta}{\\tau_{\\min}}=c=\\frac{L_P}{t_P}.\n$$\nCross multiplication gives the displayed ratio. Theorem 29 does not supply assumption 1, Theorem 46 does not supply assumption 3, and Lorentzian promotion remains governed by Corollary 46a and Appendix O. ∎\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q61a-one-link-response-clock-modulus\"></a>**Proposition Q.6.1a (One-Link Response-Clock Modulus).** Let two cells have spacing $\\delta>0$ and, for every rate $r>0$, let their continuous-time one-link Markov dynamics have generator\n$$\nL_r\n:=r\\begin{pmatrix}-1&1\\\\[2pt]1&-1\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\tag{Q.6.1a.1}\n$$\nAll members have the same adjacency, normalized uniform link weights, one-link jump ordering and spatial spacing. Starting in cell $0$, the probability of occupying cell $1$ at time $t$ is\n$$\np_{01}^{(r)}(t)=\\frac{1-e^{-2rt}}2.\n\\tag{Q.6.1a.2}\n$$\nFor a threshold $0<\\theta<1/2$ registered before comparison, the time at which this ensemble occupancy response first reaches $\\theta$ and its associated distance/time ratio are\n$$\n\\tau_\\theta(r)\n:=-\\frac{\\ln(1-2\\theta)}{2r},\n\\qquad\nv_\\theta(r):=\\frac{\\delta}{\\tau_\\theta(r)}.\n\\tag{Q.6.1a.3}\n$$\nThese are response-threshold quantities. The random first jump time $T_{\\mathrm{jump}}$ has $\\Pr(T_{\\mathrm{jump}}>t)=e^{-rt}$ and mean $1/r$, with no positive lower bound on individual waiting times. Thus $v_\\theta$ is not an attained pathwise frontier speed, and this Markov family does not supply Proposition Q.6.1's positive minimum edge-update duration. Rescaling $r\\mapsto\\lambda r$ for $\\lambda>0$ preserves the displayed spatial and dimensionless graph records but sends $\\tau_\\theta\\mapsto\\tau_\\theta/\\lambda$ and $v_\\theta\\mapsto\\lambda v_\\theta$. A dimensionful rate or clock calibration is necessary even to identify this response-threshold time from those structural records.\n\n*Proof.* The generator eigenvalues are $0$ and $-2r$, giving (Q.6.1a.2). This occupancy probability is strictly increasing from $0$ to $1/2$, so solving $p_{01}^{(r)}(t)=\\theta$ gives its unique threshold time (Q.6.1a.3). Before the first jump, the exit rate from cell $0$ is $r$, hence the survival law is $e^{-rt}$; it gives positive probability of a jump before every $t>0$. The rescaling identities follow by substitution. ∎\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q61b-renewal-link-clock-classification-of-the-attained-frontier\"></a>**Proposition Q.6.1b (Renewal Link-Clock Classification of the Attained Frontier).** Keep the spacing $\\delta>0$, normalized uniform weights and one-link ordering of Proposition Q.6.1a, and extend them to a serialized chain of cells $0,1,2,\\ldots$ in which the front advances one link per completed edge update. Let the successive update durations $D_1,D_2,\\ldots$ be independent with common law $\\mu$ on $(0,\\infty)$, put $T_n:=D_1+\\cdots+D_n$ for the arrival time at cell $n$, and let $\\tau_\\mu:=\\inf\\operatorname{supp}\\mu\\ge0$. Then:\n\n1. $T_n\\ge n\\tau_\\mu$ almost surely, and $\\Pr(T_n<n(\\tau_\\mu+\\epsilon))>0$ for every $n\\ge1$ and $\\epsilon>0$. Thus $\\tau_\\mu$ is the largest lower edge-update duration valid almost surely, and the registered lower time of Theorem 46 is available with $\\tau_{\\min}=\\tau_\\mu$ exactly when $\\tau_\\mu>0$.\n2. If $\\mathbb ED_1<\\infty$, then $n\\delta/T_n\\to\\delta/\\mathbb ED_1$ almost surely, and $\\mathbb ED_1\\ge\\tau_\\mu$ with equality exactly when $\\mu$ is the point mass $\\mu_{\\tau_\\mu}$ at $\\tau_\\mu$.\n3. The following are equivalent: (a) $\\tau_\\mu>0$ and $T_n=n\\tau_\\mu$ almost surely for every $n$, so that the uniform one-link frontier $c=\\delta/\\tau_\\mu$ of Proposition Q.6.1 is attained almost surely; (b) $\\mu=\\mu_\\tau$, the point mass at some $\\tau>0$; (c) $\\tau_\\mu>0$, $\\mathbb ED_1<\\infty$, and the almost-sure front speed $\\lim_nn\\delta/T_n$ equals $\\delta/\\tau_\\mu$.\n4. Exponential durations, the holding times of the Markov link dynamics of Proposition Q.6.1a, have $\\tau_\\mu=0$ and satisfy none of (a)--(c).\n\nWithin the renewal class, the deterministic clocks $\\mu_\\tau$, $\\tau>0$, are therefore exactly the link dynamics that supply premises 1 and 3 of Proposition Q.6.1. Every $\\tau>0$ occurs with the same spacing, adjacency and normalized weights, so the value of $\\tau$ is the free clock modulus of Proposition Q.6.1a.\n\n*Proof.* Each $D_j\\ge\\tau_\\mu$ almost surely and $\\Pr(D_j<\\tau_\\mu+\\epsilon)>0$ by the definition of the support; independence gives $\\Pr(T_n<n(\\tau_\\mu+\\epsilon))\\ge\\prod_{j\\le n}\\Pr(D_j<\\tau_\\mu+\\epsilon)>0$, so no number larger than $\\tau_\\mu$ bounds the duration almost surely. The strong law of large numbers gives $T_n/n\\to\\mathbb ED_1$ almost surely. Since $D_1-\\tau_\\mu\\ge0$ almost surely, $\\mathbb E(D_1-\\tau_\\mu)=0$ exactly when $D_1=\\tau_\\mu$ almost surely. In item 3, (a) at $n=1$ gives (b); (b) gives (a) and (c) directly; and (c) with item 2 gives $\\mathbb ED_1=\\tau_\\mu$, hence (b). The exponential law has support $[0,\\infty)$. The closing statement combines item 3 with the fact that $\\mu_\\tau$ changes no spatial or graph record. ∎\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q61c-deterministic-retained-link-clock-from-common-hard-rod-microdynamics\"></a>**Proposition Q.6.1c (Deterministic Retained-Link Clock from Common Hard-Rod Microdynamics).**\n\n## Complete finite-N deterministic mechanical link theorem\n\n### 1. Statement, including its quantifiers and declared data\n\nFor every integer $N\\ge 1$ and every independently specified $m,p_0,g,\\delta>0$ and $a\\ge 0$, there is an autonomous classical hard-core two-rail source with $2(N+2)$ rods, a binary input $b\\in\\{0,1\\}$, and counted vertices $0,\\dots,N$. Its source and receiver phase variables, collision flow, passive local observation, retained record and edge resource prices are specified below. For either input, and therefore for every probability mixture of the two inputs, the first source-sensitive complete local record at vertex $i$ is attained at\n\n$$\nT_i=i\\tau,\\qquad \\tau=mg/p_0>0.\n$$\n\nBefore $T_i$, every allowed observation of the full downstream local phase history has a source-independent distribution. At and after $T_i$ the actual receiving rod pair retains $b$ with zero error. Each of the $N$ counted edges takes exactly $\\tau$; their serialized contact events generate the clock. The construction has no separately supplied tick schedule or release timer. For the explicitly chosen receiver-material per delivered protocol nat representative, normalized weights are $w_e=1$ and\n\n$$\nd(i,j)=\\delta|i-j|,\\qquad c_{\\mathrm{link}}=\\delta/\\tau=\\delta p_0/(mg).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-arrival and attained-distance/time result within the declared source and response grammar. It is neither a universally fastest-device bound nor a claim that graph structure determines a dimensionful constant. The source phase space is continuous, its interactions singular, and its preparation exact. The claimed capacity ln 2 is that of the registered binary protocol, never unrestricted analog phase space.\n\n### 2. Complete source and autonomous flow\n\nUse two parallel, noninteracting one-dimensional rails $r=0,1$. Each has labeled equal-mass rods $i=0,\\dots,N+1$; the last pair is an uncounted guard. Each rod has mass $m$ and length $a$. Let $x_i=i(a+g)$. Both source preparations have\n\n$$\nq_{i,r}(0)=x_i,\\qquad p_{i,r}(0)=0,\n$$\n\nexcept $p_{0,b}(0)=p_0$. The independent inputs fix all lengths, masses, momenta and the source label before propagation. Timing is not modulated to convey another symbol. The all-zero-momentum null preparation is a separate diagnostic and is not a third admitted channel input.\n\nOn each rail impose $q_{i+1,r}-q_{i,r}\\ge a$. Between contacts,\n\n$$\ndq_{i,r}/dt=p_{i,r}/m,\\qquad dp_{i,r}/dt=0.\n$$\n\nAt an isolated approaching contact the adjacent momenta exchange. The autonomous Hamiltonian billiard has free Hamiltonian $H=\\sum_{i,r}p_{i,r}^{2}/(2m)$ in the nonoverlap chamber, with elastic reflection on its boundary. Its contact boundary is part of the source specification; no finite smooth contact potential is asserted.\n\nFor completeness, equal masses plus momentum and energy conservation imply $p+q=p'+q'$ and $p^{2}+q^{2}=p'^{2}+q'^{2}$. Therefore the two outgoing roots are the same unordered pair $\\{p,q\\}$. Of the identity and exchange alternatives, the approaching collision requires the exchange branch to produce nonapproaching relative motion. Here the incoming pair is $(p_0,0)$, so it becomes $(0,p_0)$. This physical update, not an assigned clock reading, defines each event.\n\nAt each contact use the outgoing, right-continuous momentum as the event state. Positions remain continuous. This convention is essential to attainment at the endpoint; a preimpact read would not give the same minimum for the displayed decoder. Only finitely many forward contacts occur, and their explicit solution below establishes existence and uniqueness for these source preparations without assuming a general many-body billiard theorem.\n\n### 3. Exact all-time solution and induction proof\n\nPut $v=p_0/m$ and $\\tau=g/v$. For each counted rod $0\\le i\\le N$, on rail $b$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nq_{i,b}(t)&=x_i+\\min\\{g,\\max\\{0,v(t-i\\tau)\\}\\}, \\\\\np_{i,b}(t)&=p_0 \\quad \\text{if } i\\tau\\le t<(i+1)\\tau, \\text{ and } 0 \\text{ otherwise}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe guard obeys\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nq_{n+1,b}(t)&=x_{n+1}+\\max\\{0,v(t-(N+1)\\tau)\\}, \\\\\np_{n+1,b}(t)&=p_0 \\quad \\text{if } t\\ge(N+1)\\tau, \\text{ and } 0 \\text{ otherwise}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvery inactive-rail rod remains at (xᵢ,0). All formulas are for $t\\ge 0$ and use the postimpact convention at equality.\n\nProof. At time 0 only rod 0 on rail $b$ moves, at velocity $v$. Its right end is separated by $g$ from rod 1's left end. For $0\\le t<g/v$ it encounters nothing; all other rods remain stationary. At $t=g/v$, $q_{0,b}=x_0+g=x_1-a$, so the two rods contact, and the elastic exchange transfers $p_{0}$ to rod 1. Rod 0 stops at $x_0+g$. Now rod 1 is at $x_{1}$ and faces exactly the same free gap $g$ to rod 2. Inductively, when rod $j$ receives momentum at $j\\tau$, it is the only moving rod, with the next receiver at $x_{j+1}$, and traverses $g$ before the next collision. Previous stopped rods cannot overtake it. Every other adjacent pair has no approaching relative velocity. At $(j+1)\\tau$ it stops at $x_j+g$ and transfers $p_{0}$ to $j+1$. Induction through $j=N$ establishes every counted and guard arrival. The guard thereafter has no obstacle and moves freely forever. No unlisted event or accumulating collision sequence is possible. ∎\n\nExactly one rod carries $p_{0}$ at every time. Thus the conserved total momentum is $p_{0}$ and the finite kinetic energy is $p_0^{2}/(2m)$. The source is still singular: finite kinetic energy says nothing about a finite force or finite stiffness bound.\n\n### 4. Full spatial support and what “local” means\n\nFor every counted vertex i fix the one-sided cell\n\n$$\nC_i=[x_i-a/2,x_{i+1}-a/2].\n$$\n\nThe complete physical support of either owned rod is $[q_{i,r}-a/2,q_{i,r}+a/2]$. Since $0\\le q_{i,r}-x_i\\le g$, that support lies in $C_i$ for all $t\\ge 0$. The upper bound is exact: $x_i+g+a/2=x_{i+1}-a/2$. A shared endpoint causes no earlier source dependence. Before $T_i$, the incoming rod $i-1$ has its right endpoint strictly below $x_i-a/2$; at $T_i$ it first touches that boundary.\n\nThe owned local phase variables are $z_i=(q_{i,0},p_{i,0},q_{i,1},p_{i,1})$. Passive classical observations may read arbitrary functions of those variables, or any time history/joint collection of downstream variables, with independent private randomness. They may not reach outside the declared downstream cells, launch extra carriers, alter momenta or gaps, or change the dynamics. This is a locality and operation registration, not a scheduled-read restriction. Observations at every time are permitted.\n\nThe locality restriction has content. An instrument observing an upstream collar of width $0<r<g$ encounters the incoming rod at $T_i-r/v$. A centered Voronoi assignment can likewise see an incoming rod before its contact. The theorem does not assert the same first-arrival time for those different support choices.\n\nThe guard is necessary for permanent records in fixed terminal cells. Without it, the last counted rod eventually exits any bounded terminal cell. Here counted rod $N$ transfers momentum to guard $N+1$ at $(N+1)\\tau$ and remains stopped inside $C_N$ forever. The outgoing guard travels on an explicitly unbounded free output ray. Therefore the all-time construction is finite-particle and finite-kinetic-energy, not a claim of bounded total spatial extent. At any specified finite observation horizon $H$ one may allocate only the finite output length required up to $H$, without asserting behavior after that horizon or installing an uncharged absorbing wall.\n\n### 5. Persistent decoder, no-early theorem and actual minimum\n\nDefine, for each counted pair and rail,\n\n$$\nA_{i,r}=1\\{p_{i,r}\\ge p_0/2 \\text{ or } q_{i,r}-x_i\\ge g/2\\}.\n$$\n\nThe record Rᵢ is ready (⊥) when both indicators vanish, and r when exactly Aᵢ,r is one. The double-active case is outside the admitted source histories and is not silently assigned a valid transmitted bit.\n\nFor $t<T_i$ both rods at node $i$ remain at their identical initial phase points. Indeed every rod $j\\ge i$, including the guard, is identical in the $b=0$ and $b=1$ histories for all such $t$. Consequently every deterministic passive response to these histories is identical, and adding independent randomness yields identical conditional outcome laws. Adaptivity cannot help: until $T_i$, identical prior observations give identical adaptive choices and equal subsequent local data. No phase observable, threshold crossing, random success tail, or downstream joint read can distinguish the input earlier.\n\nAt $t=T_i$, rail $b$ has momentum $p_{0}$ and the other has momentum zero, so $R_i=b$. For $T_i<t<T_{i+1}$ the same momentum term maintains the record. At $T_{i+1}$ the momentum leaves but the displacement is already $g$, so the displacement term keeps $R_i=b$ forever. The guard makes this also true at the terminal counted node. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\inf\\{t\\ge 0:R_i(t)\\ne\\bot\\}=\\min\\{t\\ge 0:R_i(t)\\ne\\bot\\}=T_i.\n$$\n\nThe infimum is genuinely attained in the registered postimpact grammar. This is a source-sensitive physical phase record, not a separately postulated latch or external archive. Its storage is paid for by retaining the displaced rods. The indicator has finite static readout margins on the exact states: independent momentum errors of magnitude strictly less than $p_0/2$ and displacement errors strictly less than $g/2$ preserve the decoded values. This does not preserve exact event times under dynamical preparation errors.\n\nA normalized passive classical instrument is explicit. If $\\mu$ is a probability measure supported on the admitted phase histories at a fixed time, set $I_r(\\mu)(B)=\\mu(B\\cap\\{R_i=r\\})$ for $r\\in\\{\\perp,0,1\\}$. These submeasures sum to $\\mu$ and yield the same result on repeated passive reads. The source-preparation/flow/record diagram is exactly the ready channel before $T_i$ and the identity binary channel from $T_i$ onward. Mixtures commute with this map. Exchanging source rails exchanges outputs. The null preparation gives no records. This formal instrument does not supply a free external digitizer, communication interface or detector with nonzero physical latency.\n\n### 6. Derived deterministic clock and finite-prefix compatibility\n\nThe event time comes from free flight $g$ at velocity $p_0/m$, not from another period. Thus, for every admitted history and every counted edge,\n\n$$\nD_i=T_i-T_{i-1}=mg/p_0=\\tau>0.\n$$\n\nThe event count is $\\min\\{N,\\lfloor t/\\tau\\rfloor\\}$. The variables $D_i$ have point-mass law $\\delta_\\tau$ under every input prior and are trivially independent. Every completed edge attains the lower duration $\\tau$; a larger proposed bound is violated by the first edge. Each active carrier comes from the same collision that establishes the preceding event and faces precisely the next untouched gap. Edge serialization is therefore a property of the solved flow.\n\nThe full continuous flight phase remains in the owned source. Coarse ready/completed symbols alone are not asserted to form an autonomous continuous-time Markov state; omitting phase while retaining timing would conceal the clock.\n\nIf the chain is extended with extra prepared rods, all old counted rods have exactly the same displayed phase histories for every $t\\ge 0$. After the old terminal transfers momentum, later collisions cannot catch it or send a reverse disturbance. Thus this is a compatible family of finite-$N$ statements with $O(N)$ material/archive inventory. It is not a finite device capable of infinitely many new updates. The binary bit is copied into distinct owned records rather than erased and renewed for free.\n\n### 7. Binary protocol capacity and the declared positive weighted path\n\nThe input alphabet contains precisely the two fixed phase preparations. For $t<T_i$ both transition columns of the receiver channel equal the ready output, so channel capacity is zero. For $t\\ge T_i$ the transition matrix is the identity on $\\{0,1\\}$. For any prior, $I(b;R_i)=H(b)\\le\\ln 2$, with equality for the fair prior. Thus completed one-use protocol capacity $C_e$ is exactly $\\ln 2$ nats. This is not an analog phase-space bound: allowing continuously variable momentum, launch timing, position, or other encodings changes the source class and may increase capacity without bound. A third distinguishable preparation already invalidates an unrestricted $\\ln 2$ claim.\n\nBefore cost comparison, choose owned receiving material per completed protocol nat as the resource representative. Allocate to directed edge $i-1\\to i$ its two previously ready receiving rods once; their mass is $M_e=2m$. They remain in its archive. The source pair and guard pair are separate overhead, and rails, isolation, exact preparation, geometry, calibration, passive-response convention and singular constraints remain declared resources. The functional below prices receiver material; it is not a complete monetary, manufacturing, entropy or total-apparatus cost.\n\n$$\n\\kappa_e=M_e/C_e=2m/(\\ln 2),\\qquad \\kappa^{*}=2m/(\\ln 2),\\qquad w_e=\\kappa_e/\\kappa^{*}=1.\n$$\n\nA separately prepared reflected experiment uses the same two-rod local allocation and scalar in the reverse direction, giving a symmetric representative. It is not the same already-spent chain magically reusable in both directions. On the path graph, a path from $i$ to $j$ has at least $|i-j|$ edges; the direct path has exactly that many. Hence Definition-35's infimum is attained and equals $d(i,j)=\\delta|i-j|$. The weights obey uniform positive bounds with $w_{\\min}=w_{\\max}=1$. Every forward subpath of the actual cascade takes $|i-j|\\tau$ and attains $d/(\\text{elapsed time})=\\delta/\\tau$; a new reverse or arbitrary-source experiment requires its own preparation.\n\nThe positive scalar is a chosen resource representative extracted from actual owned rods and the proved finite binary transfer budget. The choice of this functional is declared branch data, not a derivation that it is uniquely required. Uniform numbers alone do not identify it with a different preselected representative. The reset-specific entropy/capacity/refresh formula is never applied: there is no reset heat ledger, positive entropy-export derivation, refresh contraction, invented $d_{0}$, or Landauer saturation. One-use capacity divided by $\\tau$ is not a certified repeatable asymptotic bitrate without a separate preparation/renewal protocol.\n\n### 8. Calibration, physical interpretation and exact limitations\n\nThere are three lengths: free gap $g$, initial physical center spacing $a+g$, and propagation-cost edge length $\\delta$. They coincide only if separately chosen/calibrated. If $\\delta=a+g$, then $c_{\\mathrm{link}}=v(a+g)/g$; the rigid-rod ideal can propagate this front faster than the rod's translational velocity. If $a=0$ and $\\delta=g$, then $c_{\\mathrm{link}}=v$. Neither is a relativistic identification.\n\nHamilton's time coordinate is common to every degree of freedom. Independent $m,g,p_0$ calibration yields $\\tau=mg/p_0$ in that coordinate. Rescaling $p_{0}$ by $\\lambda$ rescales $\\tau$ by $1/\\lambda$ while preserving adjacency, $\\delta$ and normalized weights. Therefore the original clock-modulus nonentailment is preserved. For positive independent calibration intervals the enclosing duration interval is $[m_-g_-/p_+,m_+g_+/p_-]$. Exact theorem preparation is not exact laboratory calibration.\n\nThe theorem assumes equal masses, identical gaps, isolated rails, perfectly elastic singular collisions, exact initial rest/momentum, fixed local support and passive zero-latency phase extraction. Random momenta, thermal motion, unequal gaps or masses, nonelasticity or a real external readout can change the duration law. Finite static decoder margins are not dynamical timing robustness. No quantum-only or bounded finite-Hilbert realization is claimed. No universal material substrate, physical c, Planck identification, continuum characteristic symbol, Lorentzian cone equality, empirical hardware validation, or historical campaign acceptance is established.\n\n### 9. Why a soft contact does not inherit the attained full-phase front\n\nFor an isolated approaching pair, replace the hard boundary by $V_\\kappa(d)=\\kappa(a-d)_+^{2}/2$ and put $\\omega=\\sqrt{2\\kappa/m}$, $t_c=\\pi/\\omega$. The center of mass moves at $v/2$, while relative compression $y$ obeys $y''+\\omega^{2}y=0$, $y(0)=0$, $y'(0)=v$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(s)&=(v/\\omega)\\sin(\\omega s), \\\\\np_{\\mathrm{receiver}}(s)&=(p_0/2)(1-\\cos(\\omega s)),\\quad 0\\le s\\le t_c.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $s=t_c$ momentum exchanges and each rod advances $vt_c/2$. Under $vt_c/2<g$, the next receiver is still untouched when a collision completes. The first onset is $A_1=g/v$; after each full collision the remaining free gap is $g-vt_c/2$, giving $A_{n+1}-A_n=t_c+(g-vt_c/2)/v=\\tau+t_c/2$. Therefore\n\n$$\nA_n=n\\tau+(n-1)t_c/2,\\qquad B_n=A_n+t_c=n\\tau+(n+1)t_c/2.\n$$\n\nBoth converge to $n\\tau$ at each fixed finite $n$ as $\\kappa\\to\\infty$. For finite $\\kappa$ the receiver is unchanged at onset but $p_{\\mathrm{receiver}}(s)>0$ for every $0<s<t_c$, so an exact full-phase observer has source sensitivity arbitrarily soon afterward and no attained first sensitive time at $A_n$. A finite threshold gives a later crossing, but it changes which responses count and cannot erase an already allowed earlier analog response. Continuous momenta also cannot converge uniformly to the hard-core jump. This is a scoped falsifier of transferring the hard theorem to that soft family, not an impossibility theorem for every finite-force design.\n\n### 10. Exact finite-dimensional coherent-checkpoint obstruction\n\nFor any fixed finite-dimensional self-adjoint $H$, fixed initial state $\\rho$ and effect $0\\le F\\le I$,\n\n$$\np_F(t)=\\operatorname{Tr}[F \\exp(-itH/\\hbar)\\rho \\exp(itH/\\hbar)]\n$$\n\nis a finite linear combination of exponentials $\\exp[-it(E_a-E_b)/\\hbar]$, by spectral decomposition. It is real analytic. If it vanishes on an open interval $(0,\\tau)$, its analytic continuation is identically zero, so it cannot first become positive exactly at $\\tau$. Each matrix element of a fixed downstream reduced-state difference between two source states has the same property: equality on a nonempty open time interval implies equality for all times.\n\nConsequently an autonomous finite coherent checkpoint alone cannot provide this hard no-early-response step under a fixed unrestricted downstream response grammar. For example, $\\sin^{2}(Jt/\\hbar)>0$ at sufficiently small positive times despite perfect transfer at $\\pi\\hbar/(2J)$. This does not outlaw interventions, nonautonomous controls with owned dynamics, hybrid flow, field limits, or distinct admissible response grammars. It does not assert that relativistic quantum fields signal outside their causal cones.\n\n### Q.6.2 Temporal Scale on the Calibrated Branch\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q61-temporal-scale-on-the-serialized-frontier-branch\"></a>**Theorem Q.6.1 (Temporal Scale on the Serialized-Frontier Branch).** On the full branch of Proposition Q.6.1 and the spatial branch of Equation Q.18,\n$$\n\\boxed{\n\\tau_{\\min}=\\sqrt{8\\ln2}\\,t_P\\approx2.355\\,t_P.\n}\n$$\n\n*Proof.* Substitute $\\delta/L_P=\\sqrt{8\\ln2}$ into the conditional ratio of Proposition Q.6.1. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q61-conditional-unified-discretization-formula\"></a>**Corollary Q.6.1 (Conditional Unified Discretization Formula).** On the same joint branch,\n$$\n\\boxed{\n\\frac{\\delta}{L_P}\n=\n\\frac{\\tau_{\\min}}{t_P}\n=\n\\sqrt{8\\ln2}\\approx2.355.\n}\n$$\n\n### Q.6.3 Information-Theoretic Interpretation\n\n<a id=\"remark-q61-decomposition-of-the-discretization-scale\"></a>**Remark Q.6.1: Decomposition of the Discretization Scale.** The unified discretization factor $\\sqrt{d_0 \\cdot \\varepsilon}$ admits a transparent information-theoretic decomposition:\n\n- **Factor $d_0 = 8$:** Theorem 15 gives the finite operational-context floor $N_{\\mathrm{vis}}^{\\min}=2^{K_0}=8$; Theorem 23 gives the Hilbert-carrier lower bound $d_0\\ge N_{\\mathrm{vis}}^{\\min}$; and the minimal PCE branch used in the Appendix Z backbone selects $d_0 = 8$ (Theorem Z.2). This encodes the logical structure required for self-referential prediction.\n\n- **Factor $\\varepsilon_0=\\ln2$:** Definition 28 and Definition J.1 register the binary quotient, and Theorem J.1 supplies its structural log-cardinality. Lemma J.1 separately proves reset noninjectivity when its constant-ready-state architecture has a reachable pre-reset domain $A$ with $|A|>2$. Theorem 31 governs physical reset heat and is not used to define this factor.\n\nThe dimensionless value $\\sqrt{d_0\\varepsilon_0}=\\sqrt{8\\ln2}$ combines the selected carrier dimension with the structural binary reference on the stated saturation branch; it is not a universal thermodynamic cost of self-reference.\n\n<a id=\"remark-q62-complete-dependency-ledger\"></a>**Remark Q.6.2 (Complete Dependency Ledger).** The discretization formula uses two independently registered structural inputs:\n$$\n\\left.\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\mathrm{O1})\\text{--}(\\mathrm{O3})+(\\mathrm{FC})\n&\\xrightarrow{\\text{Thm 15}}K_0=3,\\ N_{\\mathrm{vis}}^{\\min}=8\n\\xrightarrow{\\text{Thm 23 + Hilbert distinguishability}}d_0\\ge8\n\\xrightarrow{\\text{Thm Z.2 comparator}}d_0=8,\\\\\n\\text{registered binary quotient}\n&\\xrightarrow{\\text{Def 28; Def J.1; Thm J.1}}\\varepsilon_0=\\ln2\n\\end{aligned}\n\\right\\}\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\frac{\\delta}{L_P}\n=\n\\sqrt{d_0\\varepsilon_0}\n=\n\\sqrt{8\\ln2}\n\\quad\\text{on Equation Q.18's branch.}\n$$\n\nThe two inputs are recorded separately below; neither is derived from the other.\n\n1. **The Hilbert space dimension** satisfies $d_0 \\ge N_{\\mathrm{vis}}^{\\min}=8$ by Theorem 23, with equality $d_0=8$ on the minimal PCE branch (Theorem Z.2).\n\n2. **The registered physical reset ledger** satisfies $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{reset}}\\ge H_q(P\\mid R)$ by Theorem 31, with a positive uniform floor only if $H_q(P\\mid R)\\ge h_{\\min}>0$. The structural reference $\\varepsilon_0=\\ln2$ instead comes from Definition 28, Definition J.1, and Theorem J.1; equality $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{reset}}=H_q(P\\mid R)$ requires the overhead-free implementation branch.\n\nThe mutual consistency constraint relates the minimal-branch values $d_0 = 8$ and $a = 2$ through Theorem Z.2. It does not require the general identity $a = e^\\varepsilon$.\n\n### Q.6.4 Experimental Predictions\n\n<a id=\"prediction-q61-no-leading-lorentz-violating-mdr-at-the-pce-attractor\"></a>**Prediction Q.6.1 (No Leading Lorentz-Violating MDR at the PCE Attractor).** Modified dispersion relations (MDRs) are a widely studied candidate signature in quantum-spacetime phenomenology; their presence and form depend on the propagation model and are not implied by a fundamental discretization scale alone (Amelino-Camelia 2013, Sections 2.2.1 and 3.1–3.2; Mattingly 2005, Sections 2–3). On the joint branch consisting of the serialized-frontier calibration of Proposition Q.6.1, the full Corollary 46a/Appendix O Lorentzian-promotion package, and cone sharing by the retained propagation sector with no appended preferred-frame operator, leading-order Lorentz-violating MDR terms written separately in powers of $E$ and $p$ are absent:\n$$\nE^2-p^2c^2=m^2c^4+\\text{Lorentz-invariant corrections}.\n$$\nOn that joint branch, the exact factor\n$$\n\\frac{\\delta}{L_P}=\\frac{\\tau_{\\min}}{t_P}=\\sqrt{8\\ln 2}\n$$\nfixes the invariant MPU cutoff\n$$\n\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{MPU}}:=\\frac{\\hbar c}{\\delta}=\\frac{E_P}{\\sqrt{8\\ln 2}}\\approx0.42466\\,E_P,\n$$\nbut it is not, by itself, an effective Lorentz-violation energy scale $E_{\\mathrm{QG}}$.\n\n*Derivation.* Proposition Q.6.1 calibrates the spatial and temporal scales only after the independent attained-frontier premise is accepted; it does not establish Lorentzian kinematics. The full Corollary 46a/Appendix O package supplies the Lorentzian principal symbol, while cone sharing and the absence of an appended preferred-frame operator exclude a sector-specific leading LIV term. Under those combined hypotheses the displayed dispersion relation is Lorentz invariant. If a non-attractor preferred-frame term is appended, its coefficient is instead governed by Prediction Q.6.2. Thus the null prediction belongs to the stated joint branch, not to Theorem 46 or Proposition Q.6.1 alone. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"prediction-q62-scale-ledger-bound-for-non-attractor-liv-branches\"></a>**Prediction Q.6.2 (Scale-Ledger Bound for Non-Attractor LIV Branches).** If an explicitly appended non-attractor propagation branch introduces a leading photon-speed correction\n$$\n\\frac{\\Delta v}{c}=\\xi_n\\left(\\frac{E}{\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{MPU}}}\\right)^n+O\\!\\left(\\left(\\frac{E}{\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{MPU}}}\\right)^{n+1}\\right),\n\\qquad n\\in\\{1,2\\},\n$$\nthen, for $\\xi_n\\ne0$, the energy scale in the photon-dispersion convention of Vasileiou et al. (2013) and LHAASO Collaboration (2024), whose leading speed term is $\\Delta v/c=-s(n+1)(E/E_{\\mathrm{QG},n})^n/2$ with $s=-\\operatorname{sgn}\\xi_n$, is\n$$\nE_{\\mathrm{QG},n}=\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{MPU}}\\left(\\frac{n+1}{2|\\xi_n|}\\right)^{1/n}.\n$$\nThus an observational lower bound $E_{\\mathrm{QG},n}>B_n$ in that convention and for the corresponding sign constrains the non-attractor branch coefficient by\n$$\n|\\xi_n|<\\frac{n+1}{2}\\left(\\frac{\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{MPU}}}{B_n}\\right)^n.\n$$\nWithin the deterministic photon-dispersion and source-emission models of the cited timing analyses, these bounds constrain the appended LIV branch. For $|\\xi_1|=1$, the scale $E_{\\mathrm{QG},1}=\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{MPU}}\\approx0.42466E_P$ is below their quoted linear lower limits above $E_P$. For $|\\xi_2|=1$, the corresponding scale is $E_{\\mathrm{QG},2}=\\sqrt{3/2}\\,\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{MPU}}\\approx0.52010E_P$, which is above their quoted quadratic lower limits and is not excluded by those limits. This comparison retains each analysis's sign, confidence level and source-systematic assumptions.\n\n*Derivation.* Equating the magnitudes of the leading speed coefficients gives $|\\xi_n|/\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{MPU}}^n=(n+1)/(2E_{\\mathrm{QG},n}^n)$, which yields the scale and coefficient bound. The sign identity follows from the same coefficient comparison. If $\\xi_n=0$, the order-$n$ scale is $E_{\\mathrm{QG},n}=\\infty$ by convention; any higher-order coefficients remain separate. The Lorentz-invariant attractor branch of Prediction Q.6.1 has no such leading LIV coefficient and is not excluded by finite lower limits on an appended branch's scale. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q62a-finite-rotational-mdr-class-and-coefficient-nonentailment\"></a>**Theorem Q.6.2a (Finite Rotational MDR Class and Coefficient Nonentailment).** Let $J\\ge2$ be an integer. Consider a real, translation-invariant, spatially rotation- and parity-invariant scalar quadratic propagation equation whose only time-derivative term is the canonically normalized $\\partial_t^2$, with no mixed time--space derivatives and a polynomial spatial symbol of order at most $2J$. Register the constant coefficient $\\mu^2\\ge0$, a positive quadratic coefficient $c^2$, and a scale $\\delta>0$. Its Fourier dispersion has the form\n$$\n\\omega^2(k)\n:=\\mu^2+c^2|k|^2\n+c^2\\sum_{j=2}^{J}b_j\\delta^{2j-2}|k|^{2j},\n\\tag{Q.6.2a.1}\n$$\nwith real $b_j$. Conversely, every such coefficient vector for which the right-hand side is nonnegative on the retained momentum window defines a second-order equation with no exponentially growing Fourier mode on that window. A zero-frequency mode can still have a solution $A+Bt$, so nonnegativity alone is not boundedness of all solutions. Neither the cutoff length $\\delta$ nor these symmetries determines any $b_j$.\n\nOn the massless branch, take one registered coefficient vector and let $b_j$ be its first nonzero entry. As $x:=\\delta|k|\\to0$ along the positive-frequency branch with $|k|>0$,\n$$\n\\frac{v_g(k)}c\n:=1+\\frac{2j-1}{2}b_j(\\delta|k|)^{2j-2}\n+O\\!\\left((\\delta|k|)^{2j}\\right)\n+O\\!\\left(b_j^2(\\delta|k|)^{4j-4}\\right).\n\\tag{Q.6.2a.2}\n$$\nThe remainder constants and the neighborhood where this expansion applies may depend on the full coefficient vector. Smallness of the first displayed correction alone does not control arbitrarily large higher coefficients. If every $b_j=0$, the massless positive-frequency branch has $v_g=c$. The choices $b_2=\\cdots=b_J=0$ and $b_2>0$, $b_3=\\cdots=b_J=0$, give distinct Fourier symbols with the same cutoff and structural symmetries and no exponentially growing modes. Their responses differ on any retained window containing nonzero momenta.\n\n*Proof.* Translation invariance diagonalizes the operator in Fourier space. Rotation and parity invariance make the real spatial polynomial a polynomial in $|k|^2$. The exact time-derivative premise contributes only $-\\omega^2$, giving (Q.6.2a.1) after registering the constant and quadratic terms. For a retained Fourier mode, the equation is $\\ddot f+\\omega^2(k)f=0$: positive $\\omega^2$ gives oscillations, zero gives $A+Bt$, and negative $\\omega^2$ gives exponential solutions. This proves the stated modal scope.\n\nFor $\\mu=0$, put $F(x)=1+\\sum_{\\ell=2}^Jb_\\ell x^{2\\ell-2}$. For the registered finite vector, $F(x)>0$ sufficiently near zero and $\\omega=c|k|\\sqrt{F(x)}$. Therefore\n$$\n\\frac{v_g}c=\\sqrt{F(x)}+\\frac{xF'(x)}{2\\sqrt{F(x)}}.\n$$\nTaylor expansion at $F(0)=1$ gives (Q.6.2a.2), with constants depending on the full polynomial. The zero and positive-$b_2$ examples prove coefficient nonuniqueness. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q62b-rank-six-anisotropy-floor-for-shell-symmetric-symbols\"></a>**Theorem Q.6.2b (Rank-Six Anisotropy Floor for Shell-Symmetric Symbols).** Fix $J\\ge1$. Let $\\ell$ be a real polynomial of order at most $2J$ on the retained momentum window in $\\mathbb R^4$ that is invariant under the group $\\Gamma^{+}$ of Proposition C.6h; this polynomial premise holds for a registered Taylor truncation of the symbol of a real translation-invariant operator on the $D_4$ lattice whose coupling function is constant on $\\Gamma^{+}$-orbits, in particular for equal-weight root-shell couplings, with finite support, or sufficient absolute moment bounds, and a controlled truncation remainder on the registered window as in Theorem Q.6.2a. Then every homogeneous component of $\\ell$ of degree at most $5$ is a real multiple of a power of $|k|^2$, and the degree-$6$ component lies in the span of $|k|^6$ and $T_6(k)$ of (C.6h.1). In the normalization of (Q.6.2a.1), in which the degree-$2j$ stratum carries the prefactor $\\delta^{2j-2}$ relative to the quadratic term, no coefficient choice within this class produces a rotation-noninvariant term at relative order $(\\delta|k|)^0$ or $(\\delta|k|)^2$: the lowest-order rotation-noninvariant contribution enters at relative order $(\\delta|k|)^4$, is proportional to the single anisotropic pattern of $T_6$, and its two displayed equal-weight shell moments are the Lemma C.6f defects $-1/d_0$ and $+1/M$. Along every fixed direction $\\hat k$ the restriction $\\ell(|k|\\hat k)$ is a polynomial in $|k|^2$ whose coefficients through $|k|^4$ are direction independent, with direction dependence first possible in the $|k|^6$ coefficient. On the joint branch of Prediction Q.6.1 extended by the registered premise that the retained propagation symbol is $\\Gamma^{+}$-invariant, rotation-noninvariant dispersion terms at relative orders $(\\delta|k|)^0$ and $(\\delta|k|)^2$ are therefore absent, the first admissible one is the $T_6$ stratum at relative order $(\\delta|k|)^4$. A propagation experiment tests this exclusion after its Lorentzian, polarization, and observable maps identify the measured coefficient with this scalar symbol. This supplies the anisotropic sector of the microscopic coefficient map on this branch: below rank six the anisotropic coefficients are not free parameters but vanish identically, while the isotropic coefficients remain nonentailed exactly as in Theorem Q.6.2a.\n\n*Proof.* For a finite-support real coupling function $c$ constant on $\\Gamma^{+}$-orbits of the $D_4$ lattice, the exact trigonometric symbol is $\\ell(k)=\\sum_xc(x)e^{ik\\cdot x}$; the product of the four sign changes lies in $\\Gamma^{+}$, so $c$ is even and $\\ell(k)=\\sum_xc(x)\\cos(k\\cdot x)$ is real, and for $g\\in\\Gamma^{+}$ the substitution $x=gy$, which permutes the lattice by Proposition C.6h item 1, gives $\\ell(gk)=\\sum_yc(gy)\\cos\\left(gk\\cdot gy\\right)=\\sum_yc(y)\\cos(k\\cdot y)=\\ell(k)$ by orthogonality and orbit constancy. Its registered Taylor truncation inherits this invariance term by term. The action of $\\Gamma^{+}$ on polynomials is linear and degree preserving, so every homogeneous component of an invariant polynomial is invariant, and Proposition C.6h items 2 and 3 give the stated component structure through degree $6$. The degree-$2$ and degree-$4$ strata are multiples of $|k|^2$ and $|k|^4$ and hence rotation invariant, so with the (Q.6.2a.1) prefactors the relative orders $(\\delta|k|)^0$ and $(\\delta|k|)^2$ carry no rotation-noninvariant term, and the degree-$6$ stratum at relative order $(\\delta|k|)^4$ is the first that can, its rotation-noninvariant direction being spanned by $T_6$ modulo $|k|^6$; its equal-weight shell moments are the Lemma C.6f defects because $\\frac1MT_6$ is the full contraction of the equal-weight sixth-moment tensor, by Proposition C.6h. Evenness makes the ray restriction $\\ell(|k|\\hat k)$ a polynomial in $|k|^2$; its coefficients through $|k|^4$ come from strata that are multiples of powers of $|k|^2$ and are direction independent, while the $|k|^6$ coefficient acquires the direction-dependent contribution proportional to $T_6(\\hat k)$, and $T_6$ is nonconstant on the unit sphere by the values $12$ and $18$ recorded in Proposition C.6h. On the extended joint branch the retained propagation symbol lies in the classified class, so the exclusion at relative orders $(\\delta|k|)^0$ and $(\\delta|k|)^2$ and the identification of the first admissible pattern follow from the classification, and an observation contradicting the exclusion contradicts membership in the class for every coefficient assignment, falsifying the branch. The classification constrains no isotropic coefficient, so the Theorem Q.6.2a nonentailment statement for them is unchanged, while the anisotropic entries of the microscopic coefficient map are supplied identically zero below rank six on this branch. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q62c-exact-d_4-shell-symbols-rank-six-amplitude-and-two-shell-cancellation\"></a>**Theorem Q.6.2c (Exact $D_4$ Shell Symbols, Rank-Six Amplitude and Two-Shell Cancellation).** Let $a>0$, let $aD_4$ be the scaled $D_4$ lattice with nearest-neighbour spacing $\\delta=\\sqrt2\\,a$, and for a finitely supported real coupling $w$ on $D_4\\setminus\\{0\\}$ that is constant on $\\Gamma^{+}$-orbits define\n$$\n\\ell_w(k):=\\sum_{y\\in D_4\\setminus\\{0\\}}w(y)\\bigl(1-\\cos(a\\,y\\cdot k)\\bigr),\n\\qquad k\\in\\mathbb R^4.\n\\tag{Q.6.2c.1}\n$$\nWrite $\\Xi_2:=\\Xi_{D_4}$ for the root shell and $\\Xi_4$ for the $24$ lattice vectors of squared norm $4$, namely $\\pm2e_i$ and $(\\pm1,\\pm1,\\pm1,\\pm1)$, and put $u:=(1,1,0,0)/\\sqrt2$.\n\n1. *Orbit-constant strata.* Every $\\Gamma^{+}$-orbit $O$ satisfies $\\sum_{y\\in O}(y\\cdot k)^2=\\frac14|O||y|^2|k|^2$, $\\sum_{y\\in O}(y\\cdot k)^4=\\frac18|O||y|^4|k|^4$ and $\\sum_{y\\in O}(y\\cdot k)^6=\\alpha_O|k|^6+\\beta_OT_6(k)$ with\n   $$\n   \\beta_O=\\frac16\\sum_{y\\in O}\\bigl[(y\\cdot u)^6-(y\\cdot e_1)^6\\bigr],\n   \\qquad\n   \\alpha_O+15\\beta_O=\\frac5{64}|O||y|^6.\n   $$\n   Consequently\n   $$\n   \\ell_w(k)=\\frac{a^2}2\\kappa_2|k|^2-\\frac{a^4}{24}\\kappa_4|k|^4+\\frac{a^6}{720}\\bigl(\\kappa_6|k|^6+\\beta_wT_6(k)\\bigr)+R_w(k),\n   \\tag{Q.6.2c.2}\n   $$\n   where $w_O$ is the value of $w$ on the orbit $O$, with $\\kappa_2=\\frac14\\sum_yw(y)|y|^2$, $\\kappa_4=\\frac18\\sum_yw(y)|y|^4$, $\\kappa_6=\\sum_Ow_O\\alpha_O$, the rank-six amplitude $\\beta_w=\\sum_Ow_O\\beta_O$, a linear functional of the orbit weights, and $|R_w(k)|\\le\\frac{a^8}{8!}\\sum_y|w(y)||y|^8|k|^8$.\n2. *Equal-weight root shell.* For $w=\\mathbf 1_{\\Xi_2}$, $\\ell_{\\Xi_2}(k)=24-4\\sum_{i<j}\\cos(ak_i)\\cos(ak_j)\\ge0$, with zeros exactly at $ak\\in2\\pi D_4^{*}$, and $(\\kappa_2,\\kappa_4,\\kappa_6,\\beta_w)=(12,12,0,1)$. With the quadratic stratum normalized,\n   $$\n   \\frac{\\ell_{\\Xi_2}(k)}{3\\delta^2}\n   =|k|^2-\\frac{\\delta^2}{24}|k|^4+\\frac{\\delta^4}{17280}T_6(k)+\\hat R(k),\n   \\qquad\n   |\\hat R(k)|\\le\\frac{\\delta^6|k|^8}{5040}.\n   \\tag{Q.6.2c.3}\n   $$\n   Along a unit direction $\\hat k$, $12\\le T_6(\\hat k)\\le18$, with the minimum attained exactly on the directions of $\\Xi_4$, the maximum exactly on the root directions, and spherical mean $15$. In the normalization of (Q.6.2a.1), the isotropic quartic coefficient is $b_2=-1/24$, the isotropic sextic coefficient is $15/17280=1/1152$, the rank-six anisotropy ranges over $[-3,3]/17280$ at relative order $(\\delta|k|)^4$, and the relative Taylor remainder is at most $(\\delta|k|)^6/5040$.\n3. *Two-shell cancellation.* $\\beta_{\\Xi_4}=-8$ and $\\alpha_{\\Xi_4}=240$. For the positive coupling $w=\\mathbf 1_{\\Xi_2}+\\frac18\\mathbf 1_{\\Xi_4}$, $\\beta_w=0$, $\\ell_w\\ge0$ with the same zero set, and\n   $$\n   \\frac{4\\,\\ell_w(k)}{15\\delta^2}\n   =|k|^2-\\frac{\\delta^2}{20}|k|^4+\\frac{\\delta^4}{720}|k|^6+\\hat R_w(k),\n   \\qquad\n   |\\hat R_w(k)|\\le\\frac{\\delta^6|k|^8}{2100}.\n   \\tag{Q.6.2c.4}\n   $$\n   Its degree-eight stratum is not a multiple of $|k|^8$, so its first rotation-noninvariant stratum occurs at relative order $(\\delta|k|)^6$.\n\nWithin the class of Theorem Q.6.2b, the lattice, the $\\Gamma^{+}$ symmetry and nonnegativity of the symbol therefore leave the rank-six amplitude undetermined: the normalized coefficient of $\\delta^4T_6(k)$ is $1/17280$ in (Q.6.2c.3) for the equal-weight root shell and $0$ in (Q.6.2c.4) for the positive two-shell coupling. The two-shell coupling enlarges the support of the fixed root shell, so it is compatible with Theorem C.6g.\n\n*Proof.* Item 1. For an orbit $O$, the power sums $\\sum_{y\\in O}(y\\cdot k)^{p}$ are $\\Gamma^{+}$-invariant homogeneous polynomials, because $\\Gamma^{+}$ is orthogonal and permutes $O$; odd power sums vanish because $-I\\in\\Gamma^{+}$. By Proposition C.6h they are multiples of $|k|^2$ and $|k|^4$ in degrees $2$ and $4$ and lie in $\\operatorname{span}\\{|k|^6,T_6\\}$ in degree $6$. On $\\mathbb R^4$, $\\Delta(y\\cdot k)^2=2|y|^2$, $\\Delta^2(y\\cdot k)^4=24|y|^4$, $\\Delta|k|^2=8$ and $\\Delta^2|k|^4=192$, which fix the degree-two and degree-four constants. The values $T_6(e_1)=12$ and $T_6(u)=18$ of Proposition C.6h give $\\beta_O$. The unit-sphere mean of $(y\\cdot\\hat k)^6$ is $15|y|^6/192$, so the mean of $T_6$ is $24\\cdot8\\cdot15/192=15$ and the mean of the orbit sum gives $\\alpha_O+15\\beta_O$. Expanding $1-\\cos x=\\frac{x^2}2-\\frac{x^4}{24}+\\frac{x^6}{720}-\\cdots$ with Lagrange remainder at most $x^8/8!$ termwise gives (Q.6.2c.2).\n\nItem 2. The identity $\\cos(A+B)+\\cos(A-B)=2\\cos A\\cos B$ applied to the four roots $\\pm e_i\\pm e_j$ of each coordinate pair gives the closed form. Every summand of (Q.6.2c.1) is nonnegative, and $\\ell_{\\Xi_2}(k)=0$ exactly when $a\\xi\\cdot k\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z$ for all roots, that is, $ak\\in2\\pi D_4^{*}$, because the roots generate $D_4$. Item 1 gives $\\sum_\\xi(\\xi\\cdot k)^2=12|k|^2$ and $\\sum_\\xi(\\xi\\cdot k)^4=12|k|^4$, in agreement with Lemma C.6f, and (C.6h.1) gives $\\sum_\\xi(\\xi\\cdot k)^6=T_6(k)$; substituting $a^2=\\delta^2/2$ and dividing by $3\\delta^2$ gives (Q.6.2c.3), with remainder constant $24\\cdot16/(16\\cdot8!\\cdot3)=1/5040$. On the unit sphere put $v_i=\\hat k_i^2$; then $T_6(\\hat k)=60\\sum_iv_i^2-48\\sum_iv_i^3$ on the simplex $\\sum_iv_i=1$, $v_i\\ge0$. At an extremum the Lagrange condition on the positive coordinates is $120v_i-144v_i^2=\\lambda$, so these coordinates take at most two values, which sum to $5/6$ when both occur. The resulting finitely many candidates give $12$ at $(1,0,0,0)$ and $(\\frac14,\\frac14,\\frac14,\\frac14)$, $18$ at $(\\frac12,\\frac12,0,0)$, $\\frac{44}3$ at $(\\frac13,\\frac13,\\frac13,0)$ and $(\\frac34,\\frac1{12},\\frac1{12},\\frac1{12})$, and $\\frac{46}3$ at $(\\frac23,\\frac16,\\frac16,0)$, which proves $12\\le T_6(\\hat k)\\le18$; since every extremizer is a candidate, the minimizing directions are $\\pm e_i$ and $\\frac12(\\pm1,\\pm1,\\pm1,\\pm1)$, those of $\\Xi_4$, and the maximizing directions are those of $\\Xi_2$. Division of the degree-six coefficient by the quadratic one gives the stated normalized coefficients.\n\nItem 3. The orbit $\\Gamma\\cdot2e_1\\cup\\Gamma\\cdot(1,1,1,1)$ is one $\\Gamma^{+}$-orbit because $r_v(2e_1)=(1,-1,-1,-1)$. At $e_1$ the sextic power sum of $\\Xi_4$ is $2\\cdot64+16=144$, and at $u$ it is $4\\cdot8+8\\cdot8=96$, so $\\beta_{\\Xi_4}=-8$ and $\\alpha_{\\Xi_4}=144+96=240$. Hence $T_6+\\frac18(240|k|^6-8T_6)=30|k|^6$. Item 1 gives the degree-two and degree-four sums $24|k|^2$ and $48|k|^4$ for $\\Xi_4$, so $\\kappa_2=15$, $\\kappa_4=18$ and $\\kappa_6=30$; dividing by $\\frac{15}4\\delta^2$ gives (Q.6.2c.4), with remainder constant $(384+\\frac18\\cdot24\\cdot256)\\cdot4/(16\\cdot8!\\cdot15)=1/2100$. Positivity and the zero set follow as in item 2, since $\\Xi_4\\subset D_4$. The octic power sums $\\sum_{\\Xi_2}(\\xi\\cdot k)^8+\\frac18\\sum_{\\Xi_4}(y\\cdot k)^8$ equal $12+66=78$ at $e_1$ and $33+24=57$ at $u$, two unit vectors, so the degree-eight stratum is not radial. The final assertions compare items 2 and 3. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q62d-complete-fixed-root-coupling-fiber-and-response-reconstruction\"></a>\n\n**Theorem Q.6.2d (Complete Fixed-Root Coupling Fiber and Response Reconstruction).** Let $a>0$, $\\delta=\\sqrt2\\,a$, and let $\\Xi_2$ be the $24$ roots in Theorem Q.6.2c. Consider every real even weighting $w(-\\xi)=w(\\xi)$ on this fixed support with $\\sum_\\xi w(\\xi)=1$. Define\n$$\nQ_w:=\\sum_{\\xi\\in\\Xi_2}w(\\xi)\\xi\\xi^{\\mathsf T},\\qquad\nM_{j,w}(k):=\\sum_{\\xi\\in\\Xi_2}w(\\xi)(\\xi\\cdot k)^j,\n\\qquad\nL_w(k):=\\sum_{\\xi\\in\\Xi_2}w(\\xi)\\bigl(1-\\cos(a\\xi\\cdot k)\\bigr).\n\\tag{Q.6.2d.1}\n$$\nThe corresponding real self-adjoint conservative scalar operator is the finite translation operator with Fourier symbol $L_w$. Nonnegative weights give a nonnegative quadratic form; item 2 also classifies signed weights whose full symbol is nonnegative. The signed domain is an operator-positivity class, whereas nonnegative edge-rate realizations use the simplex in item 1. All moment, reconstruction and uncertainty statements below concern this four-dimensional Euclidean scalar response.\n\n1. *Complete isotropic quadratic fiber.* The condition $Q_w=I_4/2$ holds exactly for the following two-parameter family. Partition the six coordinate pairs into\n   $$\n   \\mathcal M_1=\\{12,34\\},\\quad\n   \\mathcal M_2=\\{13,24\\},\\quad\n   \\mathcal M_3=\\{14,23\\}.\n   $$\n   There is a unique real triple $p=(p_1,p_2,p_3)$ with $\\sum_mp_m=1$ such that\n   $$\n   w(\\pm e_i\\pm e_j)=\\frac{p_m}{8}\\quad(ij\\in\\mathcal M_m).\n   \\tag{Q.6.2d.2}\n   $$\n   Nonnegative couplings are exactly the simplex $p_m\\ge0$. Strictly positive couplings are its interior. The equal-weight root shell is $p_*=(1/3,1/3,1/3)$.\n\n2. *Exact symbol and complete nonnegativity domain.* Put $c_i=\\cos(ak_i)$ and\n   $$\n   B_m(c):=\\sum_{ij\\in\\mathcal M_m}c_ic_j.\n   $$\n   Then\n   $$\n   L_p(k)=1-\\frac12\\sum_{m=1}^3p_mB_m(c),\\qquad\n   L_p(k)\\ge0\\ \\hbox{for every }k\\in\\mathbb R^4\n   \\iff p_m\\le1\\ \\hbox{for every }m.\n   \\tag{Q.6.2d.3}\n   $$\n   Thus the complete stable-symbol domain in this isotropic fiber is the triangle with vertices $(-1,1,1)$, $(1,-1,1)$ and $(1,1,-1)$; the positive-coupling simplex is a proper subset. If every $p_m<1$, the zero set is exactly $ak\\in2\\pi D_4^*$, including for signed weights in this interior. On its boundary the following interpolation formula gives the complete zero set:\n   $$\n   L_p(c)=\\sum_{s\\in\\{-1,1\\}^4}\\lambda_s(c)L_p(s),\\qquad\n   \\lambda_s(c):=\\prod_{i=1}^4\\frac{1+s_ic_i}{2}.\n   \\tag{Q.6.2d.4}\n   $$\n   Here $L_p(s)=0$ when all four signs agree, $L_p(s)=1$ when one or three signs are negative, and $L_p(s)=2(1-p_m)$ when the two negative indices form one pair of $\\mathcal M_m$. For a nonnegative symbol, $L_p(c)=0$ exactly when $\\lambda_s(c)=0$ for every vertex with $L_p(s)>0$.\n\n3. *Complete fourth-moment defect and its sharp size.* For $\\alpha_m:=p_m-1/3$ and\n   $$\n   H_m(k):=\\sum_{ij\\in\\mathcal M_m}k_i^2k_j^2,\n   \\qquad\n   J_m(k):=\\sum_{ij\\in\\mathcal M_m}(k_i^4k_j^2+k_i^2k_j^4),\n   $$\n   the exact moments are\n   $$\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   M_{2,p}(k)&=\\tfrac12|k|^2,\\\\\n   M_{4,p}(k)&=\\tfrac12|k|^4+3\\sum_m\\alpha_mH_m(k),\\\\\n   M_{6,p}(k)&=\\tfrac12\\sum_i k_i^6+\\tfrac{15}{2}\\sum_mp_mJ_m(k).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{Q.6.2d.5}\n   $$\n   In particular, fourth-moment isotropy holds exactly at $p=p_*$. Every other point of the isotropic quadratic fiber has its first nonradial even moment in degree four, while $p_*$ has its first such moment in degree six by Lemma C.6f. Set $D_{4,p}(u):=M_{4,p}(u)-1/2$ for $|u|=1$, and let $d\\sigma$ be normalized spherical measure. Its exact directional extrema and mean-square size are\n   $$\n   \\min_{|u|=1}D_{4,p}(u)=\\frac34\\min_m\\alpha_m,\n   \\quad\n   \\max_{|u|=1}D_{4,p}(u)=\\frac34\\max_m\\alpha_m,\n   \\quad\n   \\int_{S^3}D_{4,p}(u)^2\\,d\\sigma(u)=\\frac3{80}\\sum_m\\alpha_m^2.\n   \\tag{Q.6.2d.6}\n   $$\n   The normalized full symbol has the controlled expansion\n   $$\n   \\frac{8L_p(k)}{\\delta^2}\n   =|k|^2-\\frac{\\delta^2}{24}|k|^4\n   -\\frac{\\delta^2}{4}\\sum_m\\alpha_mH_m(k)\n   +\\frac{\\delta^4}{720}M_{6,p}(k)+R_p(k),\n   \\qquad\n   |R_p(k)|\\le\\frac{\\delta^6|k|^8}{5040}\\sum_m|p_m|.\n   \\tag{Q.6.2d.7}\n   $$\n   For nonnegative couplings the last sum is one. At $p_*$, (Q.6.2d.7) is exactly the normalized root-shell expansion (Q.6.2c.3).\n\n4. *Finite-window reconstruction and deterministic uncertainty.* Choose the three unit root directions $u_1=(e_1+e_2)/\\sqrt2$, $u_2=(e_1+e_3)/\\sqrt2$ and $u_3=(e_1+e_4)/\\sqrt2$. For any $r>0$ such that $A(r):=1-\\cos(\\delta r/2)>0$,\n   $$\n   M_{4,p}(u_m)=\\frac{1+3p_m}{4},\\qquad\n   L_p(ru_m)=A(r)-\\frac{p_m}{2}A(r)^2,\n   \\qquad\n   p_m=\\frac{2\\bigl(A(r)-L_p(ru_m)\\bigr)}{A(r)^2}.\n   \\tag{Q.6.2d.8}\n   $$\n   Thus three scalar Fourier responses at any such radius determine the entire root-supported operator. The compatible readings satisfy $\\sum_mL_p(ru_m)=3A(r)-A(r)^2/2$; two readings suffice if the quadratic normalization is known exactly. If readings $\\widetilde L_m$ have deterministic absolute errors at most $\\epsilon_m$, define\n   $$\n   I_m:=\\left[\n   \\frac{2(A-\\widetilde L_m-\\epsilon_m)}{A^2},\n   \\frac{2(A-\\widetilde L_m+\\epsilon_m)}{A^2}\n   \\right].\n   \\tag{Q.6.2d.9}\n   $$\n   The exact compatible coupling set is $\\{p:\\sum_mp_m=1,\\ p_m\\in I_m\\}$ intersected with $p_m\\ge0$ if nonnegative couplings are required, or with $p_m\\le1$ if full-symbol nonnegativity is required. These intervals are necessary and sufficient under the stated independent deterministic error bounds. The componentwise inverse error factor is exactly $2/A(r)^2$, asymptotic to $128/(\\delta r)^4$ as $r\\to0$. For any two normalized triples,\n   $$\n   \\sup_{k\\in\\mathbb R^4}|L_p(k)-L_{p'}(k)|\n   =2\\|p-p'\\|_\\infty.\n   \\tag{Q.6.2d.10}\n   $$\n   The reconstruction fixes $\\delta$ and the response normalization. Errors in those calibrations require their own propagated intervals.\n\n5. *Exact pullback of the local PCE tensor objective.* On every weighting in item 1, Theorem C.6e's tensor objective at $C_\\Sigma=2$ takes the same minimum value $-\\log\\det(I_4/2)=4\\log2$. Consequently its pullback to the declared nonnegative root-coupling class has the entire simplex as its minimizer set, although its minimizing tensor is unique. For example $p_*$ and $(1/2,1/3,1/6)$ have the same normalized quadratic tensor, objective, fixed root support and reciprocal zero set, but distinct symbols and distinct fourth moments. Fourth-moment isotropy singles out $p_*$ within this class; its physical admission or selection requires a coupling-level law in addition to the tensor objective. C.6e specifies feasible tensors and supplies no physical admission rule for every weighting in this pullback.\n\n*Proof.* For a coordinate pair $i<j$, let $x_{ij}$ be the common weight of $\\pm(e_i+e_j)$ and $y_{ij}$ the common weight of $\\pm(e_i-e_j)$. The off-diagonal entry $(Q_w)_{ij}$ is $2(x_{ij}-y_{ij})$, so isotropy gives $x_{ij}=y_{ij}=:b_{ij}$. The diagonal entries give $4\\sum_{j\\ne i}b_{ij}=1/2$, hence all four weighted degrees equal $1/8$. Subtracting pairs of these equations yields $b_{12}=b_{34}$, $b_{13}=b_{24}$ and $b_{14}=b_{23}$. The remaining equation is $b_{12}+b_{13}+b_{14}=1/8$. Setting these values equal to $p_1/8,p_2/8,p_3/8$ proves necessity, uniqueness and sufficiency in item 1; their signs give its simplex assertions. This argument exhausts the twelve independent even weights rather than assuming coordinate-pair symmetry.\n\nThe four roots on a pair have cosine sum $4c_ic_j$. Each matching carries eight roots, so (Q.6.2d.2) gives (Q.6.2d.3)'s symbol formula. It is affine separately in each $c_i$. Repeated one-dimensional interpolation therefore gives (Q.6.2d.4). Its coefficients are nonnegative and sum to one on $[-1,1]^4$. Direct substitution gives the stated vertex values. Each cosine variable ranges independently over $[-1,1]$, so nonnegativity of every vertex is necessary and sufficient for nonnegativity at every momentum. The vertex values impose exactly $p_m\\le1$. The three intersections of two of these boundary lines with $\\sum_mp_m=1$ give the stated stable triangle. The interpolation also proves the zero-set test.\n\nIf all $p_m<1$, the only zero vertices are $(1,1,1,1)$ and $(-1,-1,-1,-1)$. If some $c_i$ is strictly between $-1$ and $1$, the interpolation support contains two vertices differing only at that coordinate. At least one is a positive vertex, because the two zero vertices differ in all four coordinates. Thus every zero has all $c_i\\in\\{-1,1\\}$ and all signs equal. This says $ak\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z^4$ or $ak\\in\\pi(1,1,1,1)+2\\pi\\mathbb Z^4$, precisely $2\\pi D_4^*$. This proves item 2 including the signed case.\n\nSumming $(\\pm k_i\\pm k_j)^n$ over the four sign choices gives, for $n=4$, $4(k_i^4+6k_i^2k_j^2+k_j^4)$, and for $n=6$, $4(k_i^6+15k_i^4k_j^2+15k_i^2k_j^4+k_j^6)$. Multiplying by $p_m/8$ and using $\\sum_mp_m=1$ proves (Q.6.2d.5). Every coefficient of $k_i^4$ is $1/2$. If the fourth moment is radial, its radial coefficient must therefore be $1/2$. Its mixed coefficient on a pair of $\\mathcal M_m$ is $3p_m$ and the radial coefficient is one, forcing every $p_m=1/3$. Conversely these equalities give the radial fourth moment. The odd moments vanish by evenness; the second moment is radial throughout the fiber. Lemma C.6f supplies the nonradial sixth moment at its center.\n\nFor the extrema, put $v_i=u_i^2$ so $v_i\\ge0$ and $\\sum_iv_i=1$, and write $S(v)=\\sum_m\\alpha_m\\sum_{ij\\in\\mathcal M_m}v_iv_j$. The symmetric matrix representing $S$ has zero diagonal, off-diagonal entries $\\alpha_m/2$, and row sums zero. Its eigenvalues on the sum-zero subspace are $\\alpha_1,\\alpha_2,\\alpha_3$, using the three orthogonal sign vectors associated with the matchings. When $\\alpha\\ne0$, their sum zero forces both a positive and a negative eigenvalue, so there is no interior simplex maximum or minimum. On a triangular face, after permuting indices, its restriction is\n$$\n\\alpha_1v_1v_2+\\alpha_2v_1(1-v_1-v_2)+\\alpha_3v_2(1-v_1-v_2).\n$$\nThe Hessian determinant is $4\\alpha_2\\alpha_3-4\\alpha_1^2<0$: indeed $\\alpha_2\\alpha_3\\le\\alpha_1^2/4$ since $\\alpha_2+\\alpha_3=-\\alpha_1$, with strict negativity also when $\\alpha_1=0$ and $\\alpha\\ne0$. Thus no relative face interior is an extremum. On an edge associated with $\\mathcal M_m$, $S=\\alpha_m t(1-t)$, giving extrema $\\min_m\\alpha_m/4$ and $\\max_m\\alpha_m/4$. Multiplication by three proves the first two assertions in (Q.6.2d.6); $\\alpha=0$ is immediate.\n\nThe normalized spherical even-moment formula in four dimensions gives\n$$\n\\int H_m^2\\,d\\sigma=\\frac1{96},\\qquad\n\\int H_mH_n\\,d\\sigma=\\frac1{160}\\quad(m\\ne n).\n$$\nFor completeness, the required eighth moments are $\\int u_i^4u_j^4\\,d\\sigma=9/1920$, $\\int u_i^4u_j^2u_l^2\\,d\\sigma=3/1920$ for distinct indices, and $\\int u_1^2u_2^2u_3^2u_4^2\\,d\\sigma=1/1920$. These follow by pairing the eight coordinates and dividing by $4\\cdot6\\cdot8\\cdot10$. Expanding $9\\int(\\sum_m\\alpha_mH_m)^2d\\sigma$ and using $\\sum_m\\alpha_m=0$ gives $9(1/96-1/160)\\sum_m\\alpha_m^2=3\\sum_m\\alpha_m^2/80$.\n\nTaylor's theorem bounds the remainder in $1-\\cos x=x^2/2-x^4/24+x^6/720+\\text{remainder}$ by $|x|^8/8!$. Here $\\sum_\\xi|w(\\xi)|=\\sum_m|p_m|$ and $|\\xi\\cdot k|^8\\le16|k|^8$. Multiplication by $8/\\delta^2$ and $a^8=\\delta^8/16$ yields the bound in (Q.6.2d.7). Substitution of (Q.6.2d.5) gives its other terms. At $p_*$, $M_{6,p}=T_6/24$ by its definition, giving (Q.6.2c.3).\n\nOn $k=ru_m$ the two cosine coordinates belonging to the nominated pair equal $C=\\cos(\\delta r/2)$ and the other two equal one. Consequently $B_m=C^2+1$ and the other two $B$ values are $2C$. Substitution in (Q.6.2d.3) gives $L_p=1-C-p_m(1-C)^2/2$, proving the exact inversion. The fourth-moment probe formula follows either from (Q.6.2d.5) or from the same ray expansion. The inversion is affine in its reading, so the independent error intervals give exactly (Q.6.2d.9) and their intersection with the chosen domain. Since $A(r)=\\delta^2r^2/8+O(\\delta^4r^4)$, its inverse factor has the stated asymptotic.\n\nThe difference $L_p-L_{p'}$ is again multiaffine in $c$. At the vertices its values are zero or $-2(p_m-p'_m)$. Interpolation bounds its absolute value by $2\\|p-p'\\|_\\infty$, and a vertex associated with a maximizing component attains that value. Every such vertex is a cosine image of a momentum, proving (Q.6.2d.10). Finally, the constant tensor and its determinant establish item 5. The two displayed triples lie in the interior of the positive simplex and have all components below one. Item 2 gives their identical zero set; item 4 gives distinct responses, for example $L_{p_*}=4/3$ and $L_{(1/2,1/3,1/6)}=1$ at $ak=(\\pi,\\pi,0,0)$. This completes the proof. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q62e-parity-correct-gaussian-d4-transfer-and-conditional-source-causal-obstruction\"></a>**Proposition Q.6.2e (Parity-correct Gaussian D4 transfer and conditional-source causal obstruction).**\n\n**Hypotheses.** The coupling lies in the fixed-support scalar p-fiber of Theorem Q.6.2d. A Gaussian Euclidean action, a preferred coordinate time and its canonical positive-transfer continuation are added as assumptions; the tensor objective of Theorem C.6e is constant on the fiber (Theorem Q.6.2d, item 5).\n\n**1. Parity of the time slices.**\n\nWrite $D_4=\\{n\\in\\mathbb{Z}^4:\\sum n_i\\ \\text{even}\\}$, with physical lattice coordinates $an$. Designate coordinate 0 as time. Its $t=n$ slice has spatial parity $\\sum x_i\\equiv n\\bmod 2$. The spatial translation lattice is $D_3$, not $\\mathbb{Z}^3$. Pure time translation by $a$ and reflection $n\\to 1-n$ do NOT preserve $D_4$. Pure time translation by $2a$ and site reflection $n\\to -n$ do.\n\nSpatial momenta satisfy $k\\sim k+(\\pi/a)(1,1,1)$. Under that identification the temporal Fourier variable shifts by $\\pi$. Therefore the sign of a nearest-slice coefficient is not an obstruction to a positive *two-step* transfer, and reflection positivity on $D_4$ is tested with the site reflection $n\\to -n$.\n\n**2. Exact Gaussian covariance from the retained scalar symbol.**\n\nNumber the matching parameters so that $(01,23)$ has $p_1$, $(02,13)$ $p_2$, and $(03,12)$ $p_3$; $\\sum p=1$. Put $c_i=\\cos(ak_i)$, $i=1,2,3$, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA&=\\mu+1-(p_1 c_2 c_3+p_2 c_1 c_3+p_3 c_1 c_2)/2, \\\\\nB&=(p_1 c_1+p_2 c_2+p_3 c_3)/2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Gaussian action has multiplier $K(\\omega,k)=A-B\\cos\\omega$, namely exactly $\\mu+L_p(\\omega/a,k)$, not a fitted polynomial. Assume $p_{m}\\le 1$ (the exact full-symbol-positive domain) and $\\mu>0$. Then $A-|B|\\ge\\mu$ and $A>0$. Fourier inversion gives, with $D=\\sqrt{A^{2}-B^{2}}$,\n\n$$\nG_n(k)=r(k)^{|n|}/D(k),\\qquad r=B/(A+D),\\qquad |r|<1.\n$$\n\nAt $B=0$ this is read as $G_0=1/A$, $G_n=0$ for $n\\ne 0$. This formula follows by solving the covariance recurrence $AG_n-(B/2)(G_{n-1}+G_{n+1})=\\delta_{n0}$, including its normalization. Fourier measure is normalized consistently on the parity quotient; a doubled full-zone representation has the same result after the parity constraint is imposed.\n\nFor finite linear test functions supported at times $n\\ge 0$, reflection covariance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{nm} \\int dk\\, \\overline{f_n(k)}\\, G_{n+m}(k) f_m(k) \\\\\n&=\\int dk\\, D^{-1} |\\sum_n r^n f_n(k)|^2 \\ge 0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe allowed slice transforms obey $f_n(k+\\pi\\mathbf{1}/a)=(-1)^n f_n(k)$; $r$ changes sign under the same shift, so the integrand descends to the quotient. Gaussian reflection positivity for polynomial/Weyl observables follows from the positive reflected covariance by Gaussian Wick expansion (equivalently symmetric tensor powers). This establishes **site reflection positivity**.\n\nFor the following logarithmic Hamiltonian claim, restrict to infinite spatial volume with its ordinary $L^2$ momentum space. On the one-particle reconstruction, pure two-step temporal transfer is multiplication by $r^{2}$. It is positive and contractive. $B$ is a nonzero real-analytic trigonometric polynomial ($\\sum p=1$), so its zero set has spatial measure zero; multiplication by $r^{2}$ is injective on the ordinary $L^2$ one-particle space. Its logarithm defines a densely defined positive self-adjoint generator. The full free-field generator is its bosonic second quantization. This is a Gaussian transfer construction on the stated action and slicing in infinite spatial volume. On a finite torus it fails: a sampled $B=0$ momentum has $G_0=1/A>0$ but all nonzero-time covariances zero. Its nonzero reflection-plane OS vector is annihilated by the two-step transfer. No densely defined self-adjoint $H$ can have $\\exp(-\\Delta t\\,H/\\hbar)$ annihilate a nonzero vector. Removing that sector would discard a source-active contact mode. For example, the spatial quotient $D_3/4\\mathbb{Z}^3$ admits $k=(\\pi/(2a))(1,1,1)$, with $G_0=1/(1+\\mu)$ at equal weights. The logarithmic Hamiltonian is therefore asserted on infinite spatial $L^2$ only.\n\n**3. Physical time, source, and dispersion.**\n\nChoose an independent clock calibration $c>0$ so that the two-step elapsed physical time is $\\Delta t=2a/c$. The reconstructed one-particle energy and angular frequency are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE(k) &= \\hbar\\, \\Omega(k), \\\\\n\\Omega(k) &= (c/a) \\operatorname{arcosh}[A(k)/|B(k)|].\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe formula follows from $r^{2}=\\exp(-\\Delta t\\,\\Omega)$. Near the origin $B>0$ it agrees with the appropriate analytic-continuation pole. The $B<0$ sector has an alternating one-slice sign; ignoring it loses a branch of the Euclidean source. The two-step expression and energy are invariant under the true spatial reciprocal identification.\n\nA retained linear Euclidean source $J$ couples to the original scalar field: $Z[J]/Z[0]=\\exp(\\langle J,GJ\\rangle/2)$. Its full slice-to-canonical-source interface is not trivial. Away from $B=0$ put $s(k)=\\operatorname{sgn}B(k)$. Then $r^{|n-m|}=s^{n+m}\\exp[-a\\Omega|n-m|/c]$, so the original source at slice $n$ maps to $\\tilde{J}_n(k)=s(k)^nJ_n(k)$. This is invariant under the spatial reciprocal parity identification because both $s$ and the odd-slice $J_{n}$ change sign. Even slices use the canonical field; odd slices require the $s$ multiplier, generally spatially nonlocal, rather than the bare canonical field at time $na/c$. This sign multiplier is part of the source map. The $B=0$ source-active finite-volume contact sectors remain excluded as stated above.\n\nFix canonical field normalization by $\\langle\\Phi(t,k)\\Phi(0,-k)\\rangle=D(k)^{-1}\\exp[-i\\Omega(k)t]$. For the source-Hamiltonian convention $H_J=H-\\int J\\Phi$, its linear retarded response is $\\chi_R(t,k)=(2/[\\hbar D(k)])\\,\\theta(t)\\sin[\\Omega(k)t]$, since $\\chi_R=(i/\\hbar)\\theta(t)\\langle[\\Phi(t),\\Phi(0)]\\rangle$. Original odd-slice source/readout legs additionally carry the stated $s$ factors. This field carries one scalar polarization. Transverse polarizations, a gauge constraint and detector couplings for the selected equal-weight coupling are constructed in Theorem Q.6.2g.\n\nFor $\\mu>0$ the rest frequency is $\\Omega(0)=(c/a)\\operatorname{arcosh}(1+2\\mu)$. A rest energy $mc^{2}$ corresponds to $\\mu=[\\cosh(amc/\\hbar)-1]/2$ under the clock calibration.\n\nThe $\\mu\\downarrow 0$ formulas hold pointwise away from all zero-frequency/$D=0$ sectors. For the full stable domain $p_{m}\\le 1$, boundary parameters can have additional zero modes or zero sets; they are not exhausted by the constant mode. For example $p_1=1$ and $p_2+p_3=0$ gives $K(0,(0,\\pi/a,\\pi/a))=0$. The massless statements below are restricted to equal weights near the origin in infinite spatial volume.\n\n**4. Equal-weight physical spatial asymptotic, distinct from the Euclidean defect.**\n\nAt $p_1=p_2=p_3=1/3$ and $\\mu=0$, define $s_2=k_1^{2}+k_2^{2}+k_3^{2}$. On the low-momentum positive-$B$ branch,\n\n$$\n\\Omega(k)^{2}/c^{2}=s_2+(a^{4}/90)[s_2^{3}-(k_1^{6}+k_2^{6}+k_3^{6})]+O(a^{6}|k|^{8}).\n$$\n\nDerivation: with $q=(i\\Omega/c,k)$, $L(q)=a^{2}q^{2}/4-a^{4}(q^{2})^{2}/48+a^{6}M_6(q)/720+O(a^{8}|q|^{8})$. The quartic term vanishes to the needed order on the null cone. At $q^{2}=0$, $M_6(q)=-2\\sum_\\alpha q_\\alpha^{6}=2[s_2^{3}-\\sum_i k_i^{6}]$. Solving the leading quadratic equation gives the coefficient 1/90. The implicit analytic equation for $\\Omega^{2}$ has nonzero derivative at the origin after removing the overall $a^{2}$ factor, so the displayed local series exists. The remainder is a local asymptotic.\n\nThe leading sixth-degree correction vanishes on spatial coordinate axes and is largest for equal absolute spatial components at fixed $|k|$, since $\\sum_i k_i^{6}$ ranges from $s_2^{3}/9$ to $s_2^{3}$. The maximum bracket is $8s_2^{3}/9$. This is a three-dimensional statement on the chosen slice, distinct from the four-dimensional Euclidean defect of Theorem Q.6.2d. Equivalently $a^{4}/90=\\delta^{4}/360$. Along an axis the exact check is $\\cosh(a\\Omega/c)=(5-2\\cos ak)/(2+\\cos ak)$; its difference from $\\cosh(ak)$ begins $-(ak)^8/6048$, corroborating the absence of a sixth-order axial correction.\n\n**5. Ultraviolet obstruction of this canonical completion.**\n\nFor equal weights, $k^*=(\\pi/(2a),\\pi/(2a),\\pi/(2a))$ has $B=0$ and $A=1+\\mu>0$, with $\\nabla B=-(a/6)(1,1,1)\\ne 0$. Both representatives of this point of the $D_3$ reciprocal quotient have $B=0$.\n\nAs a regular $B=0$ surface is approached,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\Omega=(c/a)[\\log(2A/|B|)+O(B^{2}/A^{2})], \\\\\n\\nabla\\Omega=(c/a)[\\nabla A/A-\\nabla B/B+O(B)]\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nalong smooth transverse paths with $A$ bounded away from zero. Consequently the group velocity is unbounded. The generator's Fourier multiplier also has a logarithmic singularity, hence is not finite-range in spatial lattice coordinates. Thus this particular canonical continuous-time completion cannot have a finite global signal cone of relativistic type; in particular no finite universal velocity bound can be inferred from its infrared speed $c$. There is also a direct strict-cone proof: at any fixed positive real time the retarded multiplier $D^{-1}\\sin(\\Omega t)$ oscillates without a limit on approach to a regular $B=0$ surface. It has no continuous representative there. A spatial kernel supported in a finite-radius lattice ball would have a trigonometric-polynomial Fourier transform, necessarily continuous. Hence this retarded response has no exact finite-radius support at that time. The unbounded wavepacket velocities separately rule out a finite global group-velocity bound.\n\nThe obstruction concerns this canonical completion with all lattice momenta retained.\n\n**6. Exact conditioning and outstanding uncertainty joins.**\n\nAway from $B=0$ and $D=0$, the dimensionless $\\varepsilon=\\operatorname{arcosh}(A/|B|)$ obeys\n\n$$\nd\\varepsilon=[dA-(A/B)dB]/D.\n$$\n\nThus the local deterministic bound is $|d\\varepsilon|\\le(|dA|+|A/B|\\,|dB|)/D$. Derivatives of $A$ and $B$ are explicit linear functions of $dp$, $d\\mu$ and trigonometric functions of $a,k$. Correlated coefficient uncertainty is propagated by this signed Jacobian (or exact interval image of $\\operatorname{arcosh}(A/|B|)$), not by adding independent error bars without a covariance model. For $\\Omega=c\\varepsilon/a$,\n\n$$\nd\\Omega=(c/a)d\\varepsilon+(\\varepsilon/a)dc-(c\\varepsilon/a^{2})da,\n$$\n\nwhere $d\\varepsilon$ includes the dependence of $ak$ on $a$ and $k$. Conditioning diverges at the $B=0$ surfaces, and also at the massless zero mode in these coordinates. A finite bound requires a stated compact experimental momentum window with lower bounds on $|B|$ and $D$. Inside this model the exact symbol has no polynomial truncation error.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q62f-six-shell-scalar-isotropy-cone-and-gaussian-reflection-boundary\"></a>\n\n**Theorem Q.6.2f (Six-Shell Scalar Isotropy Cone and Gaussian Reflection Boundary).**\n\nLet $D_4=\\{x\\in\\mathbb{Z}^4:\\sum x_i\\ \\text{even}\\}$, let $X_R=\\{x\\in D_4:|x|^{2}=R\\}$, and restrict to the six squared radii $R=(2,4,6,8,10,12)$. Put $N_R=|X_R|=(24,24,96,24,144,96)$. Give each vector of $X_{R}$ the same nonnegative rate $w_{R}$, and define\n\n$$\nL_w(k)=\\sum_R w_R \\sum_{x\\in X_R} [1-\\cos(a\\, x\\cdot k)],\\quad a>0.\n$$\n\nNo probability or kinetic normalization is imposed unless explicitly stated. The zero vector of rates is excluded from minimal-support assertions. “Isotropic through order m” means every homogeneous Taylor term up to m is radial in four-dimensional **Euclidean** k. “Support size” below means number of used shells, not number of vectors.\n\n1. The full nonnegative cone isotropic through order eight has exactly four extreme rays, in the displayed radius order:\n\n   $$\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   v_1&=(224,48,8,1,0,0), \\\\\n   v_2&=(256,60,0,5,0,1), \\\\\n   v_3&=(0,2640,1560,405,112,0), \\\\\n   v_4&=(0,4020,0,1215,128,195).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   $$\n\n   Every such rate vector is a nonnegative linear combination of these rays. Exactly four shells are necessary and sufficient at minimum.\n\n2. The full real solution space isotropic through order ten is one-dimensional. Its nonnegative part is the ray\n\n   $$\n   w=t(2736,780,200,45,8,1),\\quad t\\ge 0.\n   $$\n\n   Every nonzero member uses all six shells. No nonzero member is isotropic at order twelve, hence this restricted support class has no nonzero nonnegative solution isotropic through order twelve.\n\n3. For $t=1$, $Q=\\sum_x w_x\\,xx^{\\mathsf T}=85680\\,I_4$, total rate mass is 105912, and $\\sum_x w_x|x|^{2}=342720$. The normalization $Q=I_4/2$ is $t=1/171360$, **not** $t=1/105912$. Under the former normalization the total mass is $1471/2380<1$; under probability normalization $Q=(1190/1471)I_4$, not $I_4/2$.\n\n4. For the order-ten rate vector at $t=1$, the massive scalar Gaussian precision $K_\\mu=\\mu+L_w$ with $\\mu=1$ fails site-reflection positivity, even on strictly positive even-time slices of the actual $D_4$ lattice. The failure has finite spatial-support tests in infinite spatial volume. It is invariant under the correct spatial parity alias and persists under simultaneous positive scaling of rates and the additive precision parameter. It therefore obstructs the extension of Proposition Q.6.2e's canonical positive two-step construction to this stencil.\n\nThe classification concerns shell-constant rates on these six shells.\n\n**Proof. I. Exact harmonic decomposition.**\n\nFor $P_{R,m}(k)=\\sum_{x\\in X_R}(x\\cdot k)^m$, let $h_6$ and $h_8$ be the top harmonic projections of $P_{2,6}$, $P_{2,8}$, respectively. With $s_2=|k|^{2}$, the degree-six polynomial is\n\n$$\nh_6=-3[\\sum_i k_i^6-5 \\sum_{i\\ne j} k_i^4 k_j^2+30 \\sum_{i<j<l}k_i^2k_j^2k_l^2].\n$$\n\nExplicitly,\n\n$$\nh_8=(3/2)\\sum_i k_i^8-14 \\sum_{i\\ne j}k_i^6k_j^2+49 \\sum_{i<j}k_i^4k_j^4-42 \\sum_i \\sum_{j<l;\\, j,l\\ne i}k_i^4k_j^2k_l^2+756 \\prod_i k_i^2.\n$$\n\nThe shell moments are W(F4)-invariant. Fischer decomposition together with the invariant ring of degrees 2,6,8,12 gives one invariant harmonic at degrees six and eight, none at degree ten, and two at degree twelve. In particular, for $m\\le 10$ only the radial part and the indicated h6/h8 descendants occur. The harmonic coefficients are\n\n| $R$ | $N_R$ | $a_R$ (degree six) | $b_R$ (degree eight) |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n|2|24|1|1|\n|4|24|−8|16|\n|6|96|12|−156|\n|8|24|64|256|\n|10|144|−210|870|\n|12|96|−96|−2496|\n\nFor a homogeneous polynomial P of degree m in dimension four its top harmonic is\n\n$$\nH_m(P)=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor} [(-1)^j (m-j)!/(4^j j!\\, m!)]\\, s_2^j \\Delta^jP.\n$$\n\nApplying $\\Delta$ proves this formula by pairwise cancellation. The complete shells are the signed coordinate permutations of the following representatives, with duplicates removed:\n\n| R | Representatives and orbit cardinalities |\n|---|---|\n|2|(1,1,0,0): 24|\n|4|(2,0,0,0): 8; (1,1,1,1): 16|\n|6|(2,1,1,0): 96|\n|8|(2,2,0,0): 24|\n|10|(3,1,0,0): 48; (2,2,1,1): 96|\n|12|(3,1,1,1): 64; (2,2,2,0): 32|\n\nThese lists exhaust all even-sum integer vectors of the indicated squared norms: each coordinate has magnitude at most three and the partitions of $R$ into four integer squares give exactly the listed cases. For any multi-index $\\alpha$ of total degree $m$, the coefficient of $k^\\alpha$ in $P_{R,m}$ is\n\n$$\n[m!/\\prod_i\\alpha_i!]\\sum_{\\xi\\in X_R}\\prod_i\\xi_i^{\\alpha_i}.\n$$\n\nThe sum vanishes if any $\\alpha_{i}$ is odd. Applying this coefficient formula to the displayed finite orbit lists and applying the harmonic projection gives every entry in the table. This is also a direct coefficient derivation of the polynomial identities below, independent of the invariant-ring description. More generally $\\Delta P_{R,m}=m(m-1)R\\,P_{R,m-2}$, and\n\n$$\n\\Delta(s_2^j h_l)=2j(2l+4+2j-2)s_2^{j-1}h_l.\n$$\n\nThus the **full**, not just top-harmonic, formulas are\n\n$$\nP_{R,6}=a_R h_6+(5/64)N_R R^3 s_2^3,\n$$\n\n$$\nP_{R,8}=b_R h_8+(7/4)R a_R s_2 h_6+(7/128)N_R R^4 s_2^4,\n$$\n\n$$\nP_{R,10}=(9/4)R b_R s_2 h_8+(35/16)R^2 a_R s_2^2 h_6+(21/512)N_R R^5 s_2^5.\n$$\n\nThe radial coefficients follow by spherical averaging: $\\mathbb{E}_{u\\in S^3}(x\\cdot u)^{2j}=|x|^{2j}(2j-1)!!/[4\\cdot 6\\cdots(2j+2)]$. Lower moments are $P_{R,2}=N_RRs_2/4$ and $P_{R,4}=N_RR^{2}s_2^{2}/8$. For completeness, the displayed finite-orbit coefficient formula yields the right-hand sides at every even multi-index of total degree six, eight and ten, on each of the six shells; direct differentiation gives $\\Delta h_6=\\Delta h_8=0$. Thus the finite statement does not require importing the invariant-ring classification. Sign symmetry kills all coefficients with an odd coordinate exponent.\n\nDistinct Fischer harmonic summands are linearly independent. Consequently the order-eight equations are exactly\n\n$$\n\\sum w_R a_R=0,\\quad \\sum w_R b_R=0,\\quad \\sum w_R R a_R=0.\n\\tag{A}\n$$\n\nThe order-ten equations add exactly\n\n$$\n\\sum w_R R b_R=0,\\quad \\sum w_R R^2 a_R=0.\n\\tag{B}\n$$\n\nThis establishes necessity and sufficiency, including all lower-harmonic descendant constraints.\n\n**II. Full cone and minimal support at order eight.**\n\nThe matrix of (A), with row order a,b,Ra, is\n\n[1, −8, 12, 64, −210, −96;\n 1, 16, −156, 256, 870, −2496;\n 2, −32, 72, 512, −2100, −1152].\n\nIts first three columns have determinant −1536; exact elimination gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw_2&=224 w_8-810 w_{10}-864 w_{12}, \\\\\nw_4&=48 w_8-150 w_{10}-180 w_{12}, \\\\\nw_6&=8 w_8-15 w_{10}-40 w_{12}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{C}\n$$\n\nA nonzero nonnegative solution has $w_8>0$: if $w_8=0$, the first equality forces $w_{10}=w_{12}=0$ and all other entries vanish. Set $y=w_{10}/w_8$ and $z=w_{12}/w_8$. The feasible section is\n\n$$\ny,z\\ge 0,\\quad 810y+864z\\le 224,\\quad 15y+40z\\le 8.\n$$\n\nThe $w_4$ inequality is redundant. Indeed $(55/324)$ times the first displayed upper bound plus $(5/6)$ times the second bounds $150y+180z$ by $3620/81<48$; hence $w_4/w_8\\ge268/81>0$.\n\nThe polygon's four vertices are\n\n$$\n(y,z)=(0,0),\\ (0,1/5),\\ (112/405,0),\\ (128/1215,13/81).\n$$\n\nSubstitution in (C) gives exactly v1–v4, each up to positive scale. A convex polygon is the convex hull of its vertices, so the original cone is exactly their positive hull; vertices give precisely the extreme rays. Each listed ray has four nonzero entries. Since nonnegative summation cannot cancel a positive entry, every nonzero cone element has support containing the support of at least one extreme ray; therefore fewer than four shells is impossible, and the four rays attain the minimum.\n\n**III. Order ten and the order-twelve obstruction.**\n\nAppending (B) and reducing gives the exact reduced row-echelon matrix\n\n[1,0,0,0,0,−2736;\n 0,1,0,0,0,−780;\n 0,0,1,0,0,−200;\n 0,0,0,1,0,−45;\n 0,0,0,0,1,−8].\n\nIts rank is five and its real nullspace is the line spanned by (2736,780,200,45,8,1); its nonnegative part is the stated positive ray. Thus all six shells are necessary, even before imposing nonnegativity on nonzero solutions.\n\nAt $t=1$, substitution of the rate vector into the proved moment formulas gives the radial coefficients\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{w,2}&=85680\\, s_2, \\\\\nP_{w,4}&=181440\\, s_2^2, \\\\\nP_{w,6}&=604800\\, s_2^3, \\\\\nP_{w,8}&=2661120\\, s_2^4, \\\\\nP_{w,10}&=14152320\\, s_2^5.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt degree twelve the two unit directions e1 and (e1+e2)/sqrt(2) have moments 89510400 and 86123520, differing by 3386880. Their norms are equal, so this disproves radiality; the spherical average is 86961600. As an independent structural check, the surviving descendant coefficient sums are\n\n$$\n\\sum w_R R^3 a_R=-230400,\\qquad \\sum w_R R^2 b_R=161280.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding degree-twelve descendants are $(77/32)s_2^{3}h_6$ and $(27/8)s_2^{2}h_8$ times those sums, alongside the degree-twelve top harmonic; their nonzero values already prevent radiality by Fischer uniqueness. Since every through-twelve solution must first solve (A),(B), the one-dimensional nullspace and this failure exclude every nonzero through-twelve solution within the six-shell class.\n\n**IV. Exact-symbol positivity, asymptotics and normalization.**\n\nAt $t=1$ the symbol has radial expansion through order ten\n\n$$\nL_w(k)=42840\\, a^2s_2-7560\\, a^4s_2^2+840\\, a^6s_2^3-66\\, a^8s_2^4+(39/10)a^{10}s_2^5+R_{12}(k).\n$$\n\nTaylor's theorem for cosine gives the global finite bound\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lvert R_{12}(k)\\rvert&\\le(a\\lvert k\\rvert)^{12}[\\sum_R N_Rw_RR^{6}]/12!\\\\\n&=(3904/693)(a\\lvert k\\rvert)^{12},\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nsince the numerator is 2698444800. This bounds the first omitted term together with the tail. The leading order-twelve difference between the two unit rays, evaluated at $k=\\rho u$, is $-(7/990)(a\\rho)^{12}$. Its higher remainder is $O((a\\rho)^{14})$; a uniform one-ray bound for the remainder after degree twelve is $(2368/9009)(a\\rho)^{14}$. All displayed expansions and bounds multiply by $t$ for a general nonnegative scaling.\n\nEach cosine summand is nonnegative, so the **exact** symbol is nonnegative for all $k$, despite the alternating signs of its truncated Taylor polynomial. For $t>0$ all six shell rates are positive. Because the root shell generates $D_4$, the exact zero set is precisely $ak\\in 2\\pi D_4^*$ (dual-lattice convention $D_4^*=\\{y:y\\cdot x\\in\\mathbb{Z}\\ \\text{for all}\\ x\\in D_4\\}$).\n\nThe rate and second-moment normalizations are different constraints. For any nonnegative stencil on shells with $R\\ge 2$, probability mass one implies $\\operatorname{tr}Q=\\sum_R N_Rw_RR\\ge 2$, with equality only if every positive rate is on $R=2$. Thus any strictly positive higher-shell mass makes probability normalization and $Q=I_4/2$ incompatible at fixed coordinates. For the order-ten ray, $Q=I_4/2$ gives total mass $1471/2380$. A zero-jump mass $909/2380$ completes a probability measure without changing the symbol; it enlarges the support by the zero displacement. Changing a rate clock or the lattice scale is a separate normalization.\n\n**V. Gaussian precision and the actual D4 parity quotient.**\n\nLet $D_4=\\{(n,x)\\in\\mathbb{Z}\\times\\mathbb{Z}^3:n+\\sum x_i\\ \\text{is even}\\}$, with physical coordinate spacing $a>0$. Let $X_{R}$ be its full shell of squared radius $R$. Use the scalar Gaussian precision\n\n$$\nK_\\mu(\\theta,k)=\\mu+\\sum_R w_R \\sum_{\\xi\\in X_R}[1-\\cos(\\xi_0 \\theta+a\\, \\xi_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\cdot k)],\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta=a\\omega$ is dimensionless temporal frequency. The action normalization is one-half times this positive quadratic form; the covariance multiplier is $K_\\mu^{-1}$. All rates are positive, so $K_\\mu\\ge\\mu$ on real momenta. We fix $\\mu=1$ in these raw rate units throughout the proof.\n\nEvery even-time slice has spatial lattice $D_3=\\{x\\in\\mathbb{Z}^3:\\sum x_i\\ \\text{even}\\}$. Pure time translation by $2a$ and site reflection $n\\mapsto -n$ preserve $D_4$; pure time translation by $a$ and the $\\mathbb{Z}^4$ link reflection $n\\mapsto 1-n$ do not. This proof uses only the former valid operations.\n\nChoose a representative for each $D_3$ spatial character. The alternative representative $k+(\\pi/a)(1,1,1)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nK_\\mu(\\theta,k+\\pi\\mathbf{1}/a)=K_\\mu(\\theta+\\pi,k),\n$$\n\nbecause $\\sum\\xi_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\equiv\\xi_0\\bmod 2$. With\n\n$$\nG_n(k)=(1/2\\pi)\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} \\exp(in\\theta)/K_\\mu(\\theta,k)\\, d\\theta,\n$$\n\nit follows that $G_n(k+\\pi\\mathbf{1}/a)=(-1)^nG_n(k)$. In particular **every even-time covariance is representative-independent**. The even-time tests used below are legitimate functions on the actual spatial reciprocal quotient. A full-zone parity representation or a normalized quotient representation gives the same sign; a possible common positive Fourier-measure normalization cannot remove the obstruction.\n\n**VI. Temporal polynomial and exact root brackets.**\n\nAt spatial momentum $k=0$ the total per-vector rates with a given absolute temporal jump are\n\n| Absolute temporal jump $j$ | Weighted count $A_j$ |\n|---:|---:|\n|0|43052|\n|1|55440|\n|2|7308|\n|3|112|\n\n$A_{j}$ includes both signs of the temporal coordinate. The $j=0$ vectors make no contribution to $K_\\mu(\\theta,0)$. Using $\\cos(2\\theta)=2x^{2}-1$ and $\\cos(3\\theta)=4x^{3}-3x$, $x=\\cos\\theta$,\n\n$$\nK_\\mu(x)=\\mu+55440(1-x)+7308(1-(2x^{2}-1))+112(1-(4x^{3}-3x))=\\mu+70168-55104x-14616x^{2}-448x^{3}.\n$$\n\nAt $\\mu=1$ its exact endpoint values are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nK(-29)&=302401,\\quad K(-27)=-279103, \\\\\nK(-6)&=-28615,\\quad K(-5)=36289, \\\\\nK(1)&=1,\\quad K(2)=-102087.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intermediate value theorem gives roots $r_1\\in(-29,-27)$, $r_2\\in(-6,-5)$, $r_3\\in(1,2)$. These are three distinct roots of a cubic and therefore exhaust its roots and are simple. All lie outside $[-1,1]$, as also required by $K(\\cos\\theta)\\ge 1$. Factoring\n\n$$\nK(x)=-448(x-r_1)(x-r_2)(x-r_3)\n$$\n\ngives $K'(r_1)<0$, $K'(r_2)>0$, $K'(r_3)<0$ exactly, without numerical root approximations.\n\n**VII. Signed covariance atoms.**\n\nFor each real root r outside [−1,1], define\n\n$$\nq(r)=r-\\operatorname{sgn}(r)\\sqrt{r^2-1},\\qquad |q(r)|<1.\n$$\n\nThe elementary Poisson/geometric-series identity is\n\n$$\n1/(r-\\cos\\theta)=[\\operatorname{sgn}(r)/\\sqrt{r^2-1}] \\sum_{n\\in \\mathbb{Z}} q(r)^{|n|} \\exp(in\\theta).\n$$\n\nIt follows either by summing the two geometric series or by multiplying through by $r-\\cos\\theta$. Partial fractions, valid because all roots are simple, give\n\n$$\n1/K(x)=\\sum_i [1/K'(r_i)]/(x-r_i).\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nG_n(0)&=\\sum_{i=1}^3 c_i q_i^{|n|}, \\\\\nq_i&=q(r_i), \\\\\nc_i&=-\\operatorname{sgn}(r_i)/[K'(r_i)\\sqrt{r_i^2-1}].\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe preceding exact root and derivative signs imply\n\n$$\nc_1<0,\\quad c_2>0,\\quad c_3>0.\n$$\n\nSet $\\lambda_i=q_i^{2}$. Since $|r_1|>|r_2|>|r_3|>1$ and $u-\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}$ is strictly decreasing on $u>1$,\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda_1<\\lambda_2<\\lambda_3<1.\n$$\n\nThe parity change of spatial representative sends $q_{i}$ to $-q_i$ but leaves $c_i$, $\\lambda_i$ and every $G_{2n}$ unchanged. In particular the negative atom is not the odd-slice sign of the nearest-slice case of Proposition Q.6.2e.\n\n**VIII. Failure on strictly positive even-time slices.**\n\nFor the times $0,2,4$, the reflected covariance matrix is $H_{ij}=G_{2(i+j)}(0)$, $i,j=0,1,2$. Writing $V_{ij}=\\lambda_j^i$ gives\n\n$$\nH=V \\operatorname{diag}(c_1,c_2,c_3) V^{\\mathsf T},\n$$\n\n$$\n\\det H=c_1c_2c_3 \\prod_{i<j}(\\lambda_j-\\lambda_i)^2<0.\n$$\n\nA negative determinant for this real symmetric matrix rules out positive semidefiniteness. More strongly, no reflection-plane/contact ambiguity is needed. On strictly positive times 2,4,6, choose real coefficients\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1=\\lambda_2\\lambda_3,\\quad \\alpha_2=-(\\lambda_2+\\lambda_3),\\quad \\alpha_3=1.\n$$\n\nTheir time polynomial is $p(\\lambda)=\\alpha_1\\lambda+\\alpha_2\\lambda^{2}+\\alpha_3\\lambda^{3}=\\lambda(\\lambda-\\lambda_2)(\\lambda-\\lambda_3)$. Consequently the reflected quadratic form is exactly\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{i,j=1}^3 \\alpha_i G_{2(i+j)}(0) \\alpha_j \\\\\n&=\\sum_j c_j p(\\lambda_j)^2 \\\\\n&=c_1 \\lambda_1^2(\\lambda_1-\\lambda_2)^2(\\lambda_1-\\lambda_3)^2<0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll these time slices exist on $D_4$ and are exchanged with their negative counterparts by the actual site reflection. Equivalently the strictly-positive-time Hankel determinant equals the preceding determinant times $\\prod_i\\lambda_i^{2}$ and remains negative.\n\nThe result excludes reflection positivity of the Gaussian measure already for a linear field observable.\n\n**IX. Finite spatial-support witnesses.**\n\nAt $k=0$ the preceding form is strictly negative. Because $K_1(\\theta,k)\\ge 1$ and the precision is a finite trigonometric polynomial, each fixed $G_n(k)$ is continuous in $k$ by dominated convergence. Keep the $\\alpha_{i}$ fixed at the $k=0$ values. Then\n\n$$\nF(k)=\\sum_{i,j=1}^3 \\alpha_i G_{2(i+j)}(k) \\alpha_j\n$$\n\nis strictly negative on an open neighborhood $U$ of zero in the $D_3$ reciprocal torus. Choose a nonzero spatial Fourier test $g$ supported in $U$, with the even-slice parity identification. Its reflected norm is $\\int|g(k)|^{2}F(k)\\,dk<0$. One may choose $U$ and $g$ inversion-symmetric to obtain real field tests.\n\nThis gives a valid infinite-volume $L^2$ source, not a single nonnormalizable plane wave. If the test algebra is restricted to finite spatial support, trigonometric polynomials for $D_3$ are dense in the reciprocal-torus $L^2$ space. Since the covariance is bounded ($K_1^{-1}\\le 1$), the reflected form is continuous under such approximation. A sufficiently close finite-support approximation therefore still has negative reflected norm. Alternatively, a finite periodic spatial quotient $D_3/(2M\\mathbb{Z}^3)$, $M\\ge 1$ an integer, contains the zero character as an actual finite-dimensional mode and directly admits the displayed constant-spatial test.\n\n**X. Scaling and the continuation boundary.**\n\nThe stencil's kinetic normalization $Q=I_4/2$ scales its raw rates by $t=1/171360$. To retain the same dimensionless Gaussian precision up to overall scale, also scale the additive mass parameter from $\\mu=1$ to $\\mu'=t$. Then\n\n$$\nK'=tK_1,\\qquad G'=t^{-1}G,\n$$\n\nso all reflected quadratic-form signs are unchanged. Scaling only the rates at fixed $\\mu=1$ is a different mass-to-rate model. The parameter $\\mu$ is an additive precision parameter.\n\nProposition Q.6.2e is a conditional Gaussian same-symbol construction on the fixed-root scalar $p$-fiber, where $K=A-B\\cos\\theta$ yields one covariance atom, whose even-step weight is nonnegative. The present longer-temporal-range precision has three atoms, one negative. Positive Euclidean Fourier multiplier and nonnegative jump rates are therefore insufficient for that canonical positive two-step reconstruction. The failure persists after restricting to strictly positive even slices and after the correct $D_4$ spatial alias quotient.\n\nThis proves the theorem. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q62g-stationary-selected-root-propagation-source\"></a>\n\n**Theorem Q.6.2g (Stationary-Selected Root Propagation Source).** Let $a>0$, let $D_4=\\{x\\in\\mathbb Z^4:\\sum_ix_i\\ \\text{even}\\}$ carry coordinate spacing $a$, let $\\Xi_2$ be its $24$ roots, and let $T_r$ be translation by $r$. Fix a cell gap $\\epsilon>0$, a length unit $u_L>0$ and a time unit $u_T>0$. The source of this theorem is an explicitly appended non-attractor propagation branch in the sense of Prediction Q.6.2. It consists of a code controller, a port array, a transverse gauge-field module whose matrices one fixed interpreter computes from the controller's stationary law, finitely many exchange ports, clock references and retained records, specified in items 1–6.\n\n1. *Stationary selection of the coupling.* The controller states are the codes $r\\in D_4\\setminus\\{0\\}$, with excitation energy $\\epsilon\\lVert r\\rVert^2$ carried by unary cells (cell $j$ of a coordinate has energy $(2j-1)\\epsilon$). At each step a root $s\\in\\Xi_2$ is drawn uniformly and recorded; the code moves to $r+s$ if $r+s\\ne0$ and $\\lVert r+s\\rVert^2\\le\\lVert r\\rVert^2$, and otherwise stays, and any released energy is transferred to a fresh ground-state register that is retained. This chain has the unique stationary probability\n$$\nq_r=\\tfrac1{24}\\quad(r\\in\\Xi_2),\\qquad q_r=0\\quad(r\\notin\\Xi_2)\n\\tag{Q.6.2g.1}\n$$\non all of $D_4\\setminus\\{0\\}$; every initial law converges to $q$, and the controller restricted to an energy ball $\\lVert r\\rVert^2\\le M$ has the same stationary law. If the initial law gives probability at most $\\delta_M$ to $\\lVert r\\rVert^2>M$, then after $m+3b$ steps\n$$\n\\operatorname{TV}\\bigl(\\operatorname{law}(r_{m+3b}),q\\bigr)\\le\\min\\Bigl\\{1,\\ \\delta_M+B(m,N)+\\bigl(\\tfrac{575}{576}\\bigr)^{b}\\Bigr\\},\\qquad N=\\max\\bigl\\{0,\\lfloor(M-2)/2\\rfloor\\bigr\\},\n\\tag{Q.6.2g.2}\n$$\nwhere $B(m,N)=\\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}\\binom mj24^{-j}(23/24)^{m-j}$ for $m\\ge N$, $B(m,0)=0$, and $B(m,N)=1$ for $m<N$.\n\n2. *Port form of the selected coupling.* The interpreter sets the port amplitudes from $q$, $W_qf=\\bigoplus_r\\sqrt{12q_r}\\,(T_r-I)f$. Then\n$$\nW_q^*W_q=L_q=24I-\\sum_{r\\in\\Xi_2}T_r,\\qquad\n\\ell_q(k)=24-4\\sum_{1\\le i<j\\le4}\\cos(ak_i)\\cos(ak_j)=24\\,L_{p_*}(k),\n\\tag{Q.6.2g.3}\n$$\nwhere $L_{p_*}$ is the equal-weight symbol of Theorem Q.6.2d.\n\n3. *Census of translation-invariant operators.* Let $m\\ge1$. A closed densely defined operator on $\\ell^2(D_4;\\mathbb C^m)$ whose graph is invariant under every translation is, after Fourier transform, multiplication by a measurable, almost everywhere finite matrix field $M(k)$ on the dual torus, with domain $\\{f:M\\hat f\\in L^2\\}$; the field is unique up to null sets, and every such field defines such an operator. The operator is bounded, self-adjoint, nonnegative, real or covariant under a lattice symmetry $g$ exactly when, almost everywhere, $M$ is essentially bounded, Hermitian, positive semidefinite, satisfies $M(-k)=\\overline{M(k)}$, or satisfies $M(gk)=U_gM(k)U_g^*$. The orbit-constant couplings of Theorem Q.6.2c are the scalar fields with $M(gk)=M(k)$ for every $g\\in\\Gamma^+$; every other field lies outside the orbit-constant class. With the fourth coordinate as Euclidean time, a bounded stationary matrix sequence with $C_{-n}=C_n=C_n^*$ at fixed spatial momentum satisfies $\\sum_{i,j\\ge0}v_i^*C_{i+j}v_j\\ge0$ for every finite vector list exactly when\n$$\nC_n=\\int_{[-1,1]}r^{|n|}\\,d\\mu(r)\n\\tag{Q.6.2g.4}\n$$\nfor a unique finite positive matrix measure $\\mu$; the inequalities with $C_{i+j+1}$ in place of $C_{i+j}$ hold in addition exactly when $\\mu$ is supported in $[0,1]$.\n\n4. *Transfer, gauge carrier and dispersion.* For spatial momentum $k\\in\\mathbb R^3$ put $c_i=\\cos(ak_i)$, $S=\\sum_ic_i$, $A=24-4\\sum_{i<j\\le3}c_ic_j$, $B=4S$ and $D=\\sqrt{A^2-B^2}$. Then $\\ell_q=A-B\\cos(ak_4)$ and $A\\ge|B|$, with equality exactly when $c_1=c_2=c_3=\\pm1$. Where $D>0$ and $B\\ne0$,\n$$\nE=a^{-1}\\operatorname{arcosh}(A/|B|),\\qquad\nC_n=\\frac{\\bigl(\\operatorname{sgn}(B)\\,e^{-aE}\\bigr)^{|n|}}{D},\\qquad\nZ=\\frac{2E}{D},\n\\tag{Q.6.2g.5}\n$$\nand $C_n$ is the Fourier inverse of $1/\\ell_q$ in $k_4$. Let $\\mathcal B$ be the spatial Brillouin torus of $D_3=\\{x\\in\\mathbb Z^3:\\sum_ix_i\\ \\text{even}\\}$ with normalized Haar measure, let $b_1=(1,1,0)$, $b_2=(1,0,1)$, $b_3=(0,1,1)$ be the columns of $B_{\\mathrm{sp}}$, and let $d(k)=a^{-1}B_{\\mathrm{sp}}^{-\\mathsf T}\\bigl(\\sin(ab_1\\cdot k),\\sin(ab_2\\cdot k),\\sin(ab_3\\cdot k)\\bigr)^{\\mathsf T}$. The gauge module has the action\n$$\nS_L=\\frac12\\int dt\\int_{\\mathcal B}\\Bigl(\\bigl\\lvert\\dot A-i\\,d\\,A_0\\bigr\\rvert^2-\\frac{E^2}{\\lvert d\\rvert^2}\\bigl\\lvert i\\,d\\times A\\bigr\\rvert^2\\Bigr)dk .\n\\tag{Q.6.2g.6}\n$$\nOutside the null set $\\{S=0\\}\\cup\\{D=0\\}\\cup\\{d=0\\}$, Gauss reduction leaves two transverse oscillators of frequency $E(k)$, and with $P_T=I-dd^{\\mathsf T}/\\lvert d\\rvert^2$ and $A_E=\\sqrt Z\\,A$ the vacuum Schwinger function is\n$$\n\\bigl\\langle\\mathcal T_E\\,A_E(k,2an)\\,A_E(-k,0)\\bigr\\rangle=P_T(k)\\,C_{2n}(k).\n\\tag{Q.6.2g.7}\n$$\nReal time is generated by $e^{-itH}$ with $H=d\\Gamma(E)$ on the transverse Fock space. The dispersion has the expansion\n$$\nE(k)^2=\\lvert k\\rvert^2+\\frac{a^4}{90}\\Bigl(\\lvert k\\rvert^6-\\sum_{i=1}^3k_i^6\\Bigr)+O\\bigl(a^6\\lvert k\\rvert^8\\bigr),\n\\tag{Q.6.2g.8}\n$$\nwith no term of order $a^2\\lvert k\\rvert^4$; the sextic term equals $a^4(k_1^2+k_2^2)(k_1^2+k_3^2)(k_2^2+k_3^2)/30$. Physical units are $x_{\\mathrm{phys}}=u_Lx$, $t_{\\mathrm{phys}}=u_Tt$, $\\nu=E/u_T$ and $H_{\\mathrm{phys}}=(\\hbar/u_T)H$, and the field and current gains are $\\sqrt{Z/(\\hbar u_T)}$.\n\n5. *Observables.* An exchange port $b$ of frequency $\\omega$, coupled to a normalized transverse wave packet $f$ by the driven Hamiltonian $H_c(t)=\\hbar\\sum_j\\bigl[\\gamma_j(t)\\,b^\\dagger a_j+\\overline{\\gamma_j(t)}\\,a_j^\\dagger b\\bigr]$ with $\\gamma_j(t)=g\\,\\overline{f_j}\\,e^{-i(\\omega-\\nu_j)t}$, generates exactly the interaction-picture exchange $\\hbar g\\,[b^\\dagger a(f)+a(f)^\\dagger b]$ and conserves the total excitation number. Pulses of area $\\pi/2$ swap a field mode with a fresh port, which prepares the vacuum, or a one-photon packet when the port is excited. On the one-excitation sector a detector pulse of area $\\theta$ registers a photon in $f$ with probability $\\sin^2\\theta\\,\\operatorname{Tr}[\\rho\\,a(f)^\\dagger a(f)]$, a polarization analyzer $f$ applied to a one-photon state $\\psi$ gives $\\lvert\\langle f,\\psi\\rangle\\rvert^2$, and the Ramsey readout against a reference port of frequency $\\omega$, with the phase of each pulse referenced to its own start time (or with impulsive pulses), gives\n$$\np_\\pm(T,\\phi)=\\tfrac12\\bigl[1\\pm\\cos\\bigl((\\nu_j-\\omega)T+\\phi\\bigr)\\bigr].\n\\tag{Q.6.2g.9}\n$$\nEvery finite adaptive protocol built from these operations, the clock references and the controller has a normalized history law $p(h\\mid\\theta)=\\operatorname{Tr}[K_h\\rho_0K_h^\\dagger]$.\n\n6. *One uncertainty law.* For one compact parameter set $\\Theta$ covering the source constants, the selector history, the units, the controls, the clock records and the readouts, the predictions form the single image $\\{(p(h\\mid\\theta))_h:\\theta\\in\\Theta\\}$. For two parameter records with initial trace distance $\\delta_\\rho$, per-step unitary discrepancies $\\delta_l$ and instrument discrepancies $\\epsilon_l$ (half the diamond distance),\n$$\n\\operatorname{TV}(p,p')\\le\\min\\Bigl\\{1,\\ \\delta_\\rho+\\sum_l(\\delta_l+\\epsilon_l)\\Bigr\\},\n\\tag{Q.6.2g.10}\n$$\nwithout any independence assumption. The selector discrepancy (Q.6.2g.2), the window and energy-mesh error $Q\\,T\\,\\Delta$ of a finite realization with excitation budget $Q$, duration $T$ and mesh $\\Delta$, the clock errors and the gate errors enter this one bound.\n\nFor $a=1$, $N=11$, $h=\\pi/11$ and the finite device whose field consists of the transverse modes at the six momenta $\\pm he_1$, $\\pm h(e_1+e_2)$, $\\pm h(e_1+e_2+e_3)$ of the spatial quotient $D_3/(2N\\mathbb Z^3)$, all of which have $S>0$, $D>0$ and $d\\ne0$, the readout (Q.6.2g.9) at $T=8$, $\\omega=1/4$ and $\\phi=0$ gives $p_+=0.979859711848672\\ldots$, $0.666390743130177\\ldots$ and $0.310944412632953\\ldots$ for the three directions.\n\n*Proof.* Item 1. Accepted moves never increase $\\lVert r\\rVert^2$. If $r\\notin\\Xi_2$ has two nonzero coordinates $r_i,r_j$, the root $s=-\\operatorname{sgn}(r_i)e_i-\\operatorname{sgn}(r_j)e_j$ gives $\\lVert r+s\\rVert^2-\\lVert r\\rVert^2=2-2(\\lvert r_i\\rvert+\\lvert r_j\\rvert)\\le-2$, and $r+s\\ne0$ because $r$ is not a root. If $r$ has one nonzero coordinate, parity gives $\\lvert r_i\\rvert\\ge2$, and $s=-\\operatorname{sgn}(r_i)e_i+e_j$ lowers $\\lVert r\\rVert^2$ by $2\\lvert r_i\\rvert-2\\ge2$. Hence every nonroot code has a strictly descending move of probability at least $1/24$, and at most $N$ strict descents occur from $\\lVert r\\rVert^2\\le M$. Coupling the descents with independent Bernoulli$(1/24)$ trials until absorption shows that the probability of not reaching $\\Xi_2$ within $m$ steps is at most $B(m,N)$. On $\\Xi_2$ an accepted move goes from $r$ to $r+s$ with $r\\cdot s=-1$, which is again a root; each root has eight such moves and stays with probability $16/24$. The resulting graph is symmetric and connected with diameter $3$, so the transition matrix on $\\Xi_2$ is symmetric, hence doubly stochastic, irreducible and aperiodic, and the uniform law is its unique stationary law. A path of length at most three, padded by holding steps, gives $P^3(x,y)\\ge24^{-3}$ for all roots $x,y$, a Doeblin minorization with constant $24\\cdot24^{-3}=1/576$ against the uniform law, whence the factor $(575/576)^b$ after $3b$ further steps (Levin and Peres 2017). A stationary law gives constant mass to the nonroot codes; almost-sure absorption makes that mass tend to zero, so the law is carried by $\\Xi_2$ and equals $q$. The same arguments apply inside every energy ball, which accepted moves never leave. One step with orthogonal records $\\lvert\\mathrm{tag}(r,s)\\rangle$ of energy $\\epsilon(\\lVert r\\rVert^2-\\lVert F(r,s)\\rVert^2)$, where $F(r,s)$ is the accepted or retained code, is the isometry $\\lvert r\\rangle\\mapsto24^{-1/2}\\sum_s\\lvert F(r,s)\\rangle\\lvert\\mathrm{tag}(r,s)\\rangle$; it conserves energy, extends to a unitary on each finite energy sector, and its controller marginal is the chain above.\n\nItem 2. $(T_r-I)^*(T_r-I)=2I-T_r-T_r^*$ and $\\Xi_2=-\\Xi_2$; summing with the weights $12q_r=\\frac12$ gives $L_q$. The four roots $\\pm e_i\\pm e_j$ contribute $4\\cos(ak_i)\\cos(ak_j)$ to $\\sum_r\\cos(ar\\cdot k)$, which gives $\\ell_q$, and comparison with (Q.6.2d.3) at $p_*$ gives $\\ell_q=24L_{p_*}$.\n\nItem 3. The Fourier transform carries translations to multiplication by characters. The orthogonal projection onto the closed graph commutes with them, hence with all bounded measurable multiplications, and is therefore a measurable field of projections on $\\mathbb C^m\\oplus\\mathbb C^m$. A vector $(0,v)\\ne0$ in the fibres on a set of positive measure would give a nonzero element $(0,g)$ of the graph, and a defect of the first projection on a set of positive measure would give a nonzero vector orthogonal to the domain; so almost every fibre is the graph of a matrix $M(k)$, and the domain is maximal. Conversely, the graph of a measurable field is closed and translation invariant, and its domain is dense because it contains the functions supported where $\\lVert M\\rVert$ is bounded. Boundedness, adjoints, positivity, reality and covariance are pointwise statements under the unitary Fourier transform (Reed and Simon 1980). For (Q.6.2g.4), positivity gives a semidefinite form on vector polynomials with $\\langle z^iv,z^jw\\rangle=v^*C_{i+j}w$. Multiplication by $z$ is a symmetric operator $X$ on its quotient, and boundedness of $(C_n)$ together with $\\lVert Xp\\rVert\\le\\lVert p\\rVert^{1-2^{-j}}\\lVert X^{2^j}p\\rVert^{2^{-j}}$ gives $\\lVert Xp\\rVert\\le\\lVert p\\rVert$. The closure of $X$ is a self-adjoint contraction, and its spectral measure compressed to the constant vectors is $\\mu$; uniqueness follows from density of polynomials in $C([-1,1])$, and the converse from $\\sum_{i,j}v_i^*C_{i+j}v_j=\\int p(r)^*\\,d\\mu(r)\\,p(r)$ with $p(r)=\\sum_iv_ir^i$. The inequalities with $C_{i+j+1}$ state $X\\ge0$, that is $\\operatorname{supp}\\mu\\subset[0,1]$.\n\nItem 4. With $k_4$ as time, $\\sum_{r\\in\\Xi_2}\\cos(ar\\cdot k)=4\\sum_{i<j\\le3}c_ic_j+4c_4S$, so $\\ell_q=A-B\\cos(ak_4)$. For $\\varepsilon=\\pm1$ the multiaffine function $6-\\sum_{i<j\\le3}c_ic_j-\\varepsilon S$ on $[-1,1]^3$ has nonnegative values at the eight vertices and vanishes only at $c=(\\varepsilon,\\varepsilon,\\varepsilon)$, which gives $A\\ge|B|$ and the equality case. For $D>0$ and $B\\ne0$ put $r=(A-D)/B=\\operatorname{sgn}(B)e^{-aE}$; then $\\lvert r\\rvert<1$, and $r^{|n|}/D$ solves $AC_n-\\frac B2(C_{n-1}+C_{n+1})=\\delta_{n0}$ and is summable, so it is the Fourier inverse of $(A-B\\cos\\theta)^{-1}$. The identities $d\\times d=0$ and $(i\\,d\\times)^2=\\lvert d\\rvert^2P_T$ make (Q.6.2g.6) invariant under $A\\mapsto A+i\\,d\\,\\chi$, $A_0\\mapsto A_0+\\dot\\chi$; varying $A_0$ gives the constraint $d\\cdot\\Pi=0$, and on the transverse quotient the Hamiltonian is $\\frac12(\\Pi_T^2+E^2A_T^2)$. Each $b_i$ lies in $D_3$, so $d$ is periodic on $\\mathcal B$; it is odd and $d(k)=k+O(a^2\\lvert k\\rvert^3)$. The oscillator Schwinger function $e^{-E\\lvert\\tau\\rvert}/(2E)$ multiplied by $Z$ is $e^{-E\\lvert\\tau\\rvert}/D$, which at $\\tau=2an$ equals $C_{2n}$, and the transverse projection gives (Q.6.2g.7). The sets $\\{S=0\\}$, $\\{D=0\\}$ and $\\{d=0\\}$ are zero sets of nonzero trigonometric polynomials, hence Haar-null; multiplication by $E$ is self-adjoint on $\\{f\\in L^2(\\mathcal B;\\mathbb C^3):P_Tf=f\\}$, and $H=d\\Gamma(E)$. For (Q.6.2g.8) put $s_2=\\lvert k\\rvert^2$; on the branch $S>0$ near $k=0$,\n$$\n\\frac AB=1+\\frac{a^2s_2}{2}+\\frac{a^4s_2^2}{24}+a^6\\Bigl(\\frac{s_2^3}{720}+\\frac{s_2^3-\\sum_ik_i^6}{180}\\Bigr)+O\\bigl(a^8\\lvert k\\rvert^8\\bigr),\n$$\nand comparison with $\\cosh(aE)=\\sum_n(aE)^{2n}/(2n)!$ order by order gives (Q.6.2g.8); the sextic form follows from $s_2^3-\\sum_ik_i^6=3(k_1^2+k_2^2)(k_1^2+k_3^2)(k_2^2+k_3^2)$. The unit map rescales the canonical pairs by $\\sqrt{\\hbar u_T}$ and $\\sqrt{\\hbar/u_T}$.\n\nItem 5. With $H_0$ the free Hamiltonian, $H_c(t)=e^{-iH_0t/\\hbar}\\,\\hbar g[b^\\dagger a(f)+a(f)^\\dagger b]\\,e^{iH_0t/\\hbar}$, so the interaction-picture generator is the exchange exactly, and both commute with $\\sum_ja_j^\\dagger a_j+\\sum_lb_l^\\dagger b_l$. On the one-excitation sector a pulse of area $\\theta$ maps $\\lvert f\\rangle\\mapsto\\cos\\theta\\lvert f\\rangle-i\\sin\\theta\\lvert b\\rangle$ and $\\lvert b\\rangle\\mapsto\\cos\\theta\\lvert b\\rangle-i\\sin\\theta\\lvert f\\rangle$ and fixes the modes orthogonal to $f$; this gives the preparation, counting and analyzer probabilities, and two pulses of area $\\pi/4$ separated by free evolution for time $T$ give (Q.6.2g.9). The instrument identities $\\sum_xM_x^\\dagger M_x=I$ and backward induction on the finite protocol tree give $\\sum_hK_h^\\dagger K_h=I$. The one-particle Hamiltonian of field and port, $\\bigl(\\begin{smallmatrix}E&gf\\\\ \\bar g f^*&\\omega\\end{smallmatrix}\\bigr)$, is nonnegative when $\\lvert g\\rvert^2\\lVert E^{-1/2}f\\rVert^2\\le\\omega$ by the Schur complement, so every admitted profile allows a nonzero coupling.\n\nItem 6. Replace one step at a time: unitary conjugation changes the trace distance by at most $\\lVert U-U'\\rVert$, an instrument changes it by at most half its diamond distance, and the final readout contracts trace distance; summing gives (Q.6.2g.10). Duhamel's formula bounds $\\lVert U-U'\\rVert$ by $\\hbar^{-1}\\int\\lVert H-H'\\rVert\\,dt$, and on excitation number at most $Q$ one has $\\lVert d\\Gamma(h)-d\\Gamma(h')\\rVert\\le Q\\lVert h-h'\\rVert$. For a finite realization choose a compact window $K$ on which $E$, $D$, $\\lvert S\\rvert$ and $\\lvert d\\rvert$ are bounded away from $0$ and $\\infty$, count the profile mass outside $K$ as initial-state error, and replace $E$ on $K$ by a cellwise constant $E_\\Delta$ with $\\lVert(E-E_\\Delta)1_K\\rVert\\le\\Delta$; then $\\lVert(e^{-itE}-e^{-itE_\\Delta})1_K\\rVert\\le\\lvert t\\rvert\\Delta$, and on excitation number at most $Q$ the free evolutions differ by at most $Q\\lvert t\\rvert\\Delta$ on the same Fock space. The approximating one-particle space is finite-dimensional and invariant under $E_\\Delta$, so every gate is a finite matrix with a finite two-level decomposition (Nielsen and Chuang 2010). The example values follow from (Q.6.2g.5) and (Q.6.2g.9) by direct evaluation. ∎\n\n### Q.6.5 Summary\n\nThe branch fixes a spatial resolution of a little more than two Planck lengths. The same numerical time scale follows under the propagation calibration; capacity and geometry have their separately stated branch inputs.\n\n#### Technical dependency ledger\n\n| Quantity | Symbol | Value | Origin |\n|:---------|:-------|:------|:-------|\n| Spatial discretization | $\\delta/L_P$ | $\\sqrt{8\\ln2}\\approx2.355$ on the residual-budget, throughput-saturated, ideal-packing branch | Eq. Q.18 together with Eq. Q.10 and Lemmas Q.2.2–Q.2.3 |\n| Temporal discretization | $\\tau_{\\min}/t_P$ | $\\sqrt{8\\ln2}\\approx2.355$ on the serialized-frontier calibration branch | Proposition Q.6.1 plus Eq. Q.18 |\n| Information budget | $C_{\\max}^*$ | $2\\ln2$ on the residual-capacity branch | Eq. Q.10 and its Appendix-E residual-budget premise |\n| Hilbert space dimension | $d_0$ | $8$ on the minimal Hilbert-carrier branch | Theorem 23; Theorem Z.2 |\n| Structural binary reference | $\\varepsilon_0$ | $\\ln2$ on the registered binary-support branch | Definition 28; Definition J.1; Theorem J.1; physical reset cost is separately ensemble-dependent under Theorem 31 |\n| Independence factor | $\\chi^*$ | $1$ on the throughput-saturated channel-independence branch | Lemma Q.2.2 |\n| Geometric inefficiency | $\\eta^*$ | $1$ on the ideal-packing branch | Lemma Q.2.3 |\n\nThe conditional dependency chain is\n$$\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{array}{c}\nK_0=3\\xrightarrow{\\text{Hilbert-carrier branch}}d_0=8,\\\\\n\\text{residual-capacity branch}\\xrightarrow{\\text{Eq. Q.10}}C_{\\max}^*=2\\ln2,\\\\\n\\text{throughput saturation}\\xrightarrow{\\text{Lemma Q.2.2}}\\chi^*=1,\\\\\n\\text{ideal packing}\\xrightarrow{\\text{Lemma Q.2.3}}\\eta^*=1\n\\end{array}\n}\n\\xrightarrow{\\text{Q.3}}\n\\frac{\\delta}{L_P}=\\sqrt{8\\ln2}.\n$$\n\n## Q.7 The Time-Quantum-to-Hubble Ratio\n\nOn the serialized-frontier calibration branch of Section Q.6, the registered edge-update duration satisfies $\\tau_{\\min}=\\sqrt{8\\ln2}\\,t_P$. Adjoining the de Sitter definition of $t_H$ then gives the following conditional time-scale ratio.\n\n### Q.7.1 The Hubble Time on the de Sitter Branch\n\n<a id=\"definition-q71-de-sitter-hubble-time\"></a>**Definition Q.7.1 (de Sitter Hubble Time).** On the cosmological branch with cosmological constant $\\Lambda>0$, the de Sitter Hubble time is\n$$\nt_H\\;:=\\;\\frac{1}{H_\\Lambda}\\;=\\;\\frac{1}{c}\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{\\Lambda}},\n$$\nwhere $H_\\Lambda=c\\sqrt{\\Lambda/3}$ is the de Sitter Hubble rate (Appendix E, Theorem E.9.3; Appendix H, Equation H.2).\n\n### Q.7.2 The Closed-Form Ratio\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q71-time-quantum-to-hubble-identity\"></a>**Theorem Q.7.1 (Time-Quantum-to-Hubble Identity).** On the full joint branch of Equation Q.18, Proposition Q.6.1, Theorem Q.6.1, and Definition Q.7.1, the ratio of the serialized-frontier update time to the de Sitter Hubble time is\n$$\n\\boxed{\\;\\frac{\\tau_{\\min}}{t_H}\\;=\\;\\sqrt{8\\ln 2}\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{\\Lambda L_P^2}{3}}\\;.}\n$$\n\n*Proof.* By Theorem Q.6.1, $\\tau_{\\min}=\\sqrt{8\\ln 2}\\,t_P$. By the Planck unit identity $L_P/t_P=c$,\n$$\n\\tau_{\\min}\\;=\\;\\sqrt{8\\ln 2}\\cdot\\frac{L_P}{c}.\n$$\nBy Definition Q.7.1, $t_H=\\sqrt{3/\\Lambda}/c$. Therefore\n$$\n\\frac{\\tau_{\\min}}{t_H}\\;=\\;\\sqrt{8\\ln 2}\\cdot\\frac{L_P/c}{\\sqrt{3/\\Lambda}/c}\\;=\\;\\sqrt{8\\ln 2}\\cdot L_P\\sqrt{\\frac{\\Lambda}{3}}\\;=\\;\\sqrt{8\\ln 2}\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{\\Lambda L_P^2}{3}}.\n$$\n∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q72-conditional-reciprocal-hubble-to-update-timescale-ratio\"></a>**Corollary Q.7.2 (Conditional Reciprocal Hubble-to-Update Timescale Ratio).** On the same joint branch, the reciprocal timescale ratio is\n$$\n\\frac{t_H}{\\tau_{\\min}}\\;=\\;\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8\\ln 2}}\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{\\Lambda L_P^2}}.\n$$\n\n*Proof.* Taking the reciprocal of Theorem Q.7.1 gives the formula. Interpreting it as an executed cycle count additionally requires continuous attained-rate operation for the entire Hubble interval. ∎\n\n<a id=\"proposition-q72a-executed-count-schedule-nonentailment\"></a>**Proposition Q.7.2a (Executed-Count Schedule Nonentailment).** Fix a de Sitter interval of duration $t_H\\ge\\tau>0$ and an attained cycle duration $\\tau$, and put $m=\\lfloor t_H/\\tau\\rfloor\\ge1$. The $m$ nonoverlapping slots\n$$\n[j\\tau,(j+1)\\tau),\n\\qquad j=0,\\ldots,m-1,\n\\tag{Q.7.2a.1}\n$$\nall share the same cosmology and clock. For every integer $n\\in\\{0,\\ldots,m\\}$, executing the cycle on any $n$ of these slots gives actual count $N_{\\mathrm{exec}}=n$. Therefore $(\\Lambda,t_H,\\tau)$ and the ratio $t_H/\\tau$ do not determine an executed count. A continuous-duty schedule fixed in advance selects $m$ completed cycles; even then the real ratio $t_H/\\tau$ is itself an integer count only when the endpoint divides exactly.\n\n*Proof.* Every subset of $n$ slots in (Q.7.2a.1) is a valid nonoverlapping schedule, so all integers from $0$ through $m$ occur while the three registered scale entries remain fixed. The continuous-duty assertion follows from the definition of the floor. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-q72b-continuous-duty-count-map-for-renewal-clocks\"></a>**Corollary Q.7.2b (Continuous-Duty Count Map for Renewal Clocks).** Run a renewal link clock of Proposition Q.6.1b with $\\tau_\\mu>0$ on continuous duty from time $0$, so that $N_{\\mathrm{exec}}(t):=\\max\\{n\\ge0:T_n\\le t\\}$, and let $t\\ge\\tau_\\mu$. Put $m:=\\lfloor t/\\tau_\\mu\\rfloor$ and $\\tau^+:=\\operatorname{ess\\,sup}D_1\\in(0,\\infty]$. Then $N_{\\mathrm{exec}}(t)\\le m$ almost surely, and\n$$\nN_{\\mathrm{exec}}(t)=m\\ \\text{almost surely}\n\\iff\nm\\,\\tau^+\\le t.\n\\tag{Q.7.2b.1}\n$$\nThe deterministic clock $\\mu_\\tau$ gives $N_{\\mathrm{exec}}(t)=\\lfloor t/\\tau\\rfloor$ for every $t$, and a clock with relative spread $(\\tau^+-\\tau_\\mu)/\\tau_\\mu\\ge1/m$ executes fewer than $m$ cycles with positive probability. If $\\mathbb ED_1<\\infty$, then $N_{\\mathrm{exec}}(t)/t\\to1/\\mathbb ED_1$ almost surely as $t\\to\\infty$. At the conditional ratio $t_H/\\tau_{\\min}\\approx4.345\\times10^{60}$ of Section Q.7.3, almost-sure equality of the executed count with $\\lfloor t_H/\\tau_{\\min}\\rfloor$ therefore requires a relative clock spread below $1/m\\approx2.30\\times10^{-61}$. A clock with larger spread whose duration law has an atom at $\\tau_\\mu$ still attains the count with positive probability, on the event that the first $m$ durations equal $\\tau_\\mu$, since then $T_m=m\\tau_\\mu\\le t$.\n\n*Proof.* Proposition Q.6.1b item 1 gives $T_{m+1}\\ge(m+1)\\tau_\\mu>t$ almost surely, so $N_{\\mathrm{exec}}(t)\\le m$, and $N_{\\mathrm{exec}}(t)=m$ exactly when $T_m\\le t$. If $m\\tau^+\\le t$, then $T_m\\le m\\tau^+\\le t$ almost surely. If $m\\tau^+>t$, choose a finite $\\tau'<\\tau^+$ with $m\\tau'>t$; each $D_j$ exceeds $\\tau'$ with positive probability, and independence gives $\\Pr(T_m>t)>0$. A relative spread at least $1/m$ gives $m\\tau^+\\ge(m+1)\\tau_\\mu>t$. The renewal strong law follows from $T_{N_{\\mathrm{exec}}(t)}\\le t<T_{N_{\\mathrm{exec}}(t)+1}$ and $T_n/n\\to\\mathbb ED_1$. The numerical tolerance is $1/m$ at the displayed ratio. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q72c-attained-observable-one-hubble-count\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-q72c-attained-observable-one-hubble-count-on-a-kinetic-de-sitter-source\"></a>**Theorem Q.7.2c (Attained, Observable One-Hubble Count on a Kinetic de Sitter Source).** Let $c,\\hbar,\\delta,R>0$ with $x=\\delta/R\\le1/100$, put $\\tau=\\delta/c$, $t_H=R/c$ and $m=\\lfloor R/\\delta\\rfloor$, given with a certified comparison at the floor boundary, and let $k\\ge4$ be an integer with $r_{\\mathrm{cal}}=k\\delta\\sqrt{3/(2\\pi)}<R/20$ and $N=6k^2$. The source consists of positive-energy null rays carrying two-dimensional internal factors (three bundled into an eight-dimensional carrier), stationary memories with degenerate internal registers, the boundary permutations named below at their registered intersections, the identity at every other intersection, and reflected radial support beams, on the prescribed metric\n$$\ng=-c^2dt^2+\\bigl\\lvert d\\mathbf r-(c/R)\\,\\mathbf r\\,dt\\bigr\\rvert^2 .\n\\tag{Q.7.2c.1}\n$$\n\n1. *Cosmological branch.* (Q.7.2c.1) is de Sitter space with $\\mathrm{Ric}=3g/R^2$ and $\\Lambda=3/R^2$. The central line $\\mathbf r=0$ is a geodesic with proper time $t$, and its Hubble interval is $[0,t_H]$, fixed by the geometry before any count.\n\n2. *Attained clock.* Put $A_*=R^2-\\delta^2/(4\\sinh^2(x/2))$ and $r_j^2=A_*+e^{jx}(R^2/4-A_*)$ for $j=0,\\dots,m+1$, and place stations $\\mathbf a_j$ with $\\lvert\\mathbf a_j\\rvert=r_j$, $\\lvert\\mathbf a_{j+1}-\\mathbf a_j\\rvert=\\delta$ and no three collinear. Then $R/2\\le r_j<0.84R$, and a null ray leaving $\\mathbf a_j$ at time $t_0$ in the direction of $\\mathbf a_{j+1}$ reaches it exactly at $t_0+\\tau$. The first response of station $j+1$ to a change of the label sent from station $j$ occurs exactly at delay $\\tau$.\n\n3. *Forward-locked duty and event records.* Bundles $W_j$, $j=1,\\dots,m+1$, on the null chords from $E_{j-1}$ to $E_j=(j\\tau,\\mathbf a_j)$ are fixed before the run. At $E_j$ the station loads $W_j$ into its core $(a,d,b)$ and applies, with the fresh outgoing bundle $(u,v,w)$, a private bit $e$ and a hit bit $h$,\n$$\nF:\\bigl((a,d,b),(u,v,w),e,h\\bigr)\\longmapsto\\bigl((u,v,e),(a,d,w),b,h\\oplus a\\bigr).\n\\tag{Q.7.2c.2}\n$$\n$F$ is a permutation of the $256$ basis states and preserves every four-momentum; on fresh stocks it sends $(a,d)$ onward, archives $b$ and writes $h=a$. With the label $(1,0,0)$ launched at $E_0$, every station $j\\le m+1$ records $h_j=1$, and a missing transfer leaves its hit $0$. Station $m+1$ receives after $t_H$.\n\n4. *Observable count.* Inward null readers meet the stations at $t_{\\mathrm{read}}=t_H+2\\tau$ and deliver the hits to a central bank before $t_{\\mathrm{read}}+1.84\\,t_H$. A reversible circuit of $m(5B+2)$ gates, $B=\\lceil\\log_2(m+2)\\rceil$, adds $h_1,\\dots,h_m$ into a $B$-bit register and returns its carries to zero, and the computational-basis measurement of the register gives\n$$\nN_{\\mathrm{exec}}(t_H)=\\sum_{j\\tau\\le t_H}h_j=\\lfloor t_H/\\tau\\rfloor .\n\\tag{Q.7.2c.3}\n$$\n\n5. *Capacity-area calibration on the same source.* The sphere of radius $r_{\\mathrm{cal}}$ has area $N\\delta^2$. An equal-area cubed-sphere partition divides it into $N$ cells of area $\\delta^2$ and uniformly bounded shape, and each cell is crossed by one channel (Q.7.2c.2) from a sender at radius $r_-$ inside to a receiver at radius $r_+$ outside, with $r_-^2=[2r_{\\mathrm{cal}}^2+(e^x-1)A_*]/(1+e^x)$, $r_+^2=2r_{\\mathrm{cal}}^2-r_-^2$, chord length $\\delta$ and flight time $\\tau$. The message-dependent output across the sphere is the tensor product of the $N$ channels, each of classical capacity $\\ln4$, so the capacity is $N\\ln4$ and the capacity per area is $\\ln4/\\delta^2$. With $G_{\\mathrm{op}}=\\delta^2c^3/(4\\hbar\\ln4)$ and, on the capacity-achieving, entropy-saturating, additive-ledger branch of Section Q.1, the independent calibration $G_{\\mathrm{op}}=G$,\n$$\n\\delta^2=8\\ln2\\,L_P^2,\\qquad \\tau=\\sqrt{8\\ln2}\\,t_P,\\qquad\nN_{\\mathrm{exec}}(t_H)=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8\\ln2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{\\Lambda L_P^2}}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{Q.7.2c.4}\n$$\n\n6. *Stress account.* A station of mass $M_j$ at radius $r_j$ needs the inward proper force $f_j=M_jc^2r_j/(R^2N_j)$, $N_j=\\sqrt{1-r_j^2/R^2}$, and an incident radial beam reflected at the station with power $J_j=cf_j/2$ supplies it. With $T^{\\mu\\nu}_{\\mathrm{ray}}=c\\int p^\\mu p^\\nu\\delta_g\\,d\\lambda$ and $T^{\\mu\\nu}_B=c\\int Mu^\\mu u^\\nu\\delta_g\\,d\\sigma$, the total stress obeys $\\nabla_\\nu(T_B+T_{\\mathrm{rad}})^{\\mu\\nu}=j_S^\\mu$, where $j_S$ is the current of ray endpoints at the initial and final slices.\n\n*Proof.* Item 1. With $\\mathbf q=e^{-ct/R}\\mathbf r$, $g=-c^2dt^2+e^{2ct/R}\\lvert d\\mathbf q\\rvert^2$, the flat Friedmann metric with $\\dot a/a=c/R$; its Ricci tensor is $3g/R^2$, so $\\Lambda=3/R^2$. The central line is a geodesic with proper time $t$.\n\nItem 2. The ray Hamiltonian $c\\lvert\\mathbf p\\rvert+(c/R)\\,\\mathbf r\\cdot\\mathbf p$ gives $\\dot{\\mathbf r}=c\\mathbf n+(c/R)\\mathbf r$ and $\\dot{\\mathbf p}=-(c/R)\\mathbf p$, so $\\mathbf r(t)=e^{c(t-t_0)/R}(\\mathbf a+R\\mathbf n)-R\\mathbf n$ with $\\lvert\\mathbf n\\rvert=1$. It reaches $\\mathbf b$ at $t_0+\\tau$ exactly when $\\lvert e^{-x}\\mathbf b-\\mathbf a\\rvert=R(1-e^{-x})$; with $\\lvert\\mathbf b-\\mathbf a\\rvert=\\delta$ this is equivalent to $\\lvert\\mathbf b\\rvert^2-A_*=e^x(\\lvert\\mathbf a\\rvert^2-A_*)$, the recurrence for $r_j$. Since $0\\le A_*\\le x^2R^2$ and $jx\\le1+x$, $r_j^2\\le e^{1.01}R^2/4<0.687R^2$; moreover $r_{j+1}-r_j\\le(e^x-1)r_j/2<0.425\\,\\delta$ and $r_j+r_{j+1}\\ge R>\\delta$, so each intersection circle of the two spheres is nondegenerate and a point avoiding the finitely many earlier lines exists. The squared increment is at least $xR^2(1/4-x^2)>\\delta^2$, so $\\mathbf a_j\\cdot(\\mathbf a_{j+1}-\\mathbf a_j)>0$ and consecutive chords lie in disjoint open radial annuli. Before $E_{j+1}$ the only route carrying the label to station $j+1$ is the bundle $W_{j+1}$, which has not met it; every other input of that station is fixed independently of the label; at $E_{j+1}$, (Q.7.2c.2) gives distinct hits for $a=0$ and $a=1$.\n\nItem 3. The inverse of $F$ reads $(a,d,w)$ from the second output, $(u,v,e)$ from the first, $b$ from the archive and $h$ as the last output plus $a$; internal registers are degenerate, so momenta are unchanged. Induction along the chain gives the hits.\n\nItem 4. An inward radial reader obeys $\\dot r=(c/R)r-c$, so from $r_j$ it reaches the centre after $(R/c)\\log(R/(R-r_j))<(R/c)\\log(1/0.16)<1.84\\,t_H$. In each increment step the carries $c_{k+1}=c_k\\wedge z_k$ are computed, $z_k\\mapsto z_k\\oplus c_k$ adds the hit, and the flipped Toffoli layer recomputes and erases the same carries because $c_{k+1}=c_k\\wedge\\neg z_k^{\\mathrm{new}}$; hence $z\\mapsto z+h_j \\bmod 2^B$ with $5B+2$ gates, and $m<2^B$ excludes overflow. The register value is $\\sum_{j\\le m}h_j=m=\\lfloor t_H/\\tau\\rfloor$.\n\nItem 5. $4\\pi r_{\\mathrm{cal}}^2=6k^2\\delta^2$ by the choice of $r_{\\mathrm{cal}}$. On a cube face the radial projection has area density $r_{\\mathrm{cal}}^2(1+u^2+v^2)^{-3/2}$ and face area $2\\pi r_{\\mathrm{cal}}^2/3$; an explicit equal-area chart of the face onto $[0,1]^2$ with bounded derivatives and inverse derivatives, divided into $k^2$ equal squares, gives cells of area $\\delta^2$ and diameter bounded by a fixed multiple of $\\delta$. The radii $r_\\pm$ satisfy $r_+^2-A_*=e^x(r_-^2-A_*)$ and $\\lvert r_+-r_-\\rvert<\\delta<r_++r_-$, so a chord of length $\\delta$ from radius $r_-$ to $r_+$ crosses the sphere once and has flight time $\\tau$ by item 2; a rotation places the crossing in the assigned cell. The channels act on disjoint factors, so the message-dependent output is their tensor product with an input-independent state; each factors through the four-dimensional input $(a,d)$, so by the Holevo bound its capacity is at most $\\ln4$, attained by the four orthogonal codes. Finally $G_{\\mathrm{op}}=G$ gives $\\delta^2=4\\hbar\\ln4\\,G/c^3=8\\ln2\\,L_P^2$, and $t_H/\\tau=R/\\delta$ with $R=\\sqrt{3/\\Lambda}$ gives (Q.7.2c.4).\n\nItem 6. A static site at radius $r$ has proper acceleration $c^2r/(R^2N_r)$ toward the centre. Reflection reverses the radial momentum flux $J/c$ of the beam, transferring $2J_j/c=f_j$. The divergence of a ray stress consists of its endpoint momenta; every boundary map preserves the momentum of each channel, so interior endpoint terms cancel, the support forces cancel between bodies and beams, and the initial and final endpoints form $j_S$. ∎\n\n### Q.7.3 Numerical Evaluation\n\nUsing the observed central value $\\Lambda L_P^2=2.86599\\times 10^{-122}$ from Equation V.5 (Appendix V), Theorem Q.7.1 gives\n$$\n\\frac{\\tau_{\\min}}{t_H}\\;=\\;\\sqrt{8\\ln 2}\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{2.86599\\times 10^{-122}}{3}}\\;\\approx\\;2.302\\times 10^{-61}.\n$$\nThe reciprocal conditional timescale ratio is\n$$\n\\frac{t_H}{\\tau_{\\min}}\\;\\approx\\;4.345\\times 10^{60}.\n$$\n\n### Q.7.4 Status of the Identity\n\n<a id=\"remark-q71-status-of-theorem-q71\"></a>**Remark Q.7.1 (Status of Theorem Q.7.1).** Theorem Q.7.1 is algebraic only after three independent branch packages are accepted: the spatial ratio of Equation Q.18, the serialized-frontier clock calibration of Proposition Q.6.1, and the de Sitter definition of $t_H$. It is not a consequence of $K_0$, $\\varepsilon_0$, $d_0$, and $\\Lambda L_P^2$ alone, and it does not independently close either the temporal or cosmological branch.\n\n<a id=\"remark-q72-cosmological-microscopic-bridge-scope\"></a>**Remark Q.7.2 (Cosmological--Microscopic Bridge Scope).** Theorem Q.7.1 packages the conditional serialized-frontier update scale and de Sitter Hubble scale. Its numerical ratio also depends on the residual-budget, ideal-packing, channel-independence, and clock/frontier calibration premises.\n";
